[[:db/cas 17592186052582 :answer/value "\\frac{5\\begin{vmatrix}x &}{3 \\mid y\\end{vmatrix}}" "\\frac{5|x |}{3 | y|}"] [:db/cas 17592186052585 :answer/value "-\\frac{5\\begin{vmatrix}x &}{3 \\mid y\\end{vmatrix}}" "-\\frac{5|x|}{3|y|}"] [:db/cas 17592186054346 :question/worked-solution "We fill in the table as follows:\n\n- First, we calculate the $y$-values on both lines for our different values of $x$.\n- Then, we calculate the error $\\mid y_2 - y_1 \\mid$ for each value of $x$.\n\nFor example, consider the third column of the table, which corresponds to $x=1.2$. Substituting $x=1.2$ into the expressions for $y_1$ and $y_2$, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny_{1} &= -4x + 2 \\\\\n&=-4(1.2) + 2 \\\\\n&=-2.8 \\\\\ny_{2} &= 2x - 5 \\\\\n&= 2(1.2) - 5 \\\\\n&=-2.6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we find the corresponding error:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{vmatrix}y_{1} - y_{2} & &= & -2.8 - (-2.6)\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\mid - 0.2 \\mid \\\\\n&= 0.2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe fill this information into the corresponding column:\n\n| $x$ | $0.8$ | $1.0$ | $1.2$ | $1.4$ | $1.6$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $y_1=-4x+2$ | $-1.2$ |  | $-2.8$ | $-3.6$ |  |\n| $y_2=2x-5$ | $-3.4$ |  | $-2.6$ | $-2.2$ |  |\n| $\\mid y_1-y_2 \\mid$ | $2.2$ |  | $0.2$ | $1.4$ |  |\n\nRepeating this procedure for each column in the table, we arrive at the following result.\n\n| $x$ | $0.8$ | $1.0$ | $1.2$ | $1.4$ | $1.6$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $y_1=-4x+2$ | $-1.2$ | $-2$ | $-2.8$ | $-3.6$ | $-4.4$ |\n| $y_2=2x-5$ | $-3.4$ | $-3$ | $-2.6$ | $-2.2$ | $-1.8$ |\n| $\\mid y_1-y_2 \\mid$ | $2.2$ | $1$ | $0.2$ | $1.4$ | $2.6$ |\n\nThe smallest error is $0.2$, which corresponds to $x=1.2$. Therefore,\n\n$$\nx\\approx 1.2\n$$\n\nis our approximate solution." "We fill in the table as follows:\n\n- First, we calculate the $y$-values on both lines for our different values of $x$.\n- Then, we calculate the error $\\mid y_2 - y_1 \\mid$ for each value of $x$.\n\nFor example, consider the third column of the table, which corresponds to $x=1.2$. Substituting $x=1.2$ into the expressions for $y_1$ and $y_2$, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny_{1} &= -4x + 2 \\\\\n&=-4(1.2) + 2 \\\\\n&=-2.8 \\\\\ny_{2} &= 2x - 5 \\\\\n&= 2(1.2) - 5 \\\\\n&=-2.6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we find the corresponding error:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{y}_{1}-{y}_{2}| & =|-2.8-(-2.6)| \\\\  & =|-0.2| \\\\  & =0.2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe fill this information into the corresponding column:\n\n| $x$ | $0.8$ | $1.0$ | $1.2$ | $1.4$ | $1.6$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $y_1=-4x+2$ | $-1.2$ |  | $-2.8$ | $-3.6$ |  |\n| $y_2=2x-5$ | $-3.4$ |  | $-2.6$ | $-2.2$ |  |\n| $\\mid y_1-y_2 \\mid$ | $2.2$ |  | $0.2$ | $1.4$ |  |\n\nRepeating this procedure for each column in the table, we arrive at the following result.\n\n| $x$ | $0.8$ | $1.0$ | $1.2$ | $1.4$ | $1.6$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $y_1=-4x+2$ | $-1.2$ | $-2$ | $-2.8$ | $-3.6$ | $-4.4$ |\n| $y_2=2x-5$ | $-3.4$ | $-3$ | $-2.6$ | $-2.2$ | $-1.8$ |\n| $\\mid y_1-y_2 \\mid$ | $2.2$ | $1$ | $0.2$ | $1.4$ | $2.6$ |\n\nThe smallest error is $0.2$, which corresponds to $x=1.2$. Therefore,\n\n$$\nx\\approx 1.2\n$$\n\nis our approximate solution."] [:db/cas 17592186054347 :question/worked-solution "We need to find the point of intersection of the two lines.\n\nFrom the graph, we can see that the solution should lie between $x=13$ and $x=14$. So, we can disregard all values of $x$ that are not between $13$ and $14$.\n\nNow, we evaluate the table entries for $x=13.2$ and $x=13.6$, as follows:\n\n- Substituting $x=13.2$ into the expressions for $y_1$ and $y_2$, we get the following:\n$$\n\\displaystyle y_{1}|= 0.43x + 6; = 0.43(13.2) + 6; = 11.676; y_{2}|= 0.65x + 3; = 0.65(13.2) + 3; = 11.58\n$$\nThen, we find the corresponding error:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{vmatrix}y_{1} - y_{2} & |= & 11.676 - 11.58\\end{vmatrix}; = \\mid 0.096 \\mid; 