\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} {\\color{blue}\\begin{array}{c} \\\\ 10\\end{array} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em}} & \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} {\\color{blue}\\begin{array}{c} \\\\ 7\\end{array} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em}} &  & \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} {\\color{blue}\\begin{array}{c} \\\\ 15\\end{array} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em}} \\\\  &  & \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\cancel{2} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\cancel{0} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\cancel{8} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} . \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\cancel{5} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\\\ \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} - \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} &  & \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} 1 \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} 2 \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} 4 \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} . \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} 7 \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\\\ \\hline  &  &  & \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} 8 \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} 3 \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} . \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} 8 \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em}\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the van will unload $83.8$ pounds of strawberries at the second store."] [:db/cas 17592186054506 :question/worked-solution "A quadratic equation has real solutions if it has two distinct real solutions ($\\mathcal{D}>0$) or one distinct real solution (\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{D}=0\n$$\n\n). Putting these conditions together, we must have\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{D} \\geq 0\n$$\n\nTo compute the discriminant, first note the following coefficients: $a=3$, $b=-6k$, and $c=12$. So, we require\n\n$$\nD\\begin{vmatrix}\\ge 0 \\\\ b^{2} - 4ac\\end{vmatrix}\\ge 0; (-6k)^{2} - 4(3)(12)\\begin{vmatrix}\\ge 0 \\\\ 36k^{2} - 144\\end{vmatrix}\\ge 0; 36k^{2}\\begin{vmatrix}\\ge 144 \\\\ k^{2}\\end{vmatrix}\\ge 4; \\sqrt{k^{2}} \\mid \\ge \\sqrt{4}; \\mid k \\mid \\mid \\ge 2\n$$\n\nIf\n\n$$\n\\mid k \\mid \\geq 2\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\nk \\leq -2\n$$\n\nor\n\n$$\nk \\geq 2\n$$\n\nUsing interval notation, then, our solution is\n$k \\in (-\\infty,-2] \\cup [2, \\infty)$." "A quadratic equation has real solutions if it has two distinct real solutions ($\\mathcal{D}>0$) or one distinct real solution (\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{D}=0\n$$\n\n). Putting these conditions together, we must have\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{D} \\geq 0\n$$\n\nTo compute the discriminant, first note the following coefficients: $a=3$, $b=-6k$, and $c=12$. So, we require\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathcal{D} & \\ge 0 \\\\ {b}^{2}-4ac & \\ge 0 \\\\ (-6k)^{2}-4(3)(12) & \\ge 0 \\\\ 36{k}^{2}-144 & \\ge 0 \\\\ 36{k}^{2} & \\ge 144 \\\\ {k}^{2} & \\ge 4 \\\\ \\sqrt{{k}^{2}} & \\ge \\sqrt{4} \\\\ |k| & \\ge 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf\n\n$$\n\\mid k \\mid \\geq 2\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\nk \\leq -2\n$$\n\nor\n\n$$\nk \\geq 2\n$$\n\nUsing interval notation, then, our solution is\n$k \\in (-\\infty,-2] \\cup [2, \\infty)$."] [:db/cas 17592186054507 :question/worked-solution "A quadratic equation has two distinct real solutions if its discriminant is positive: $\\mathcal{D}>0$.\n\nTo compute the discriminant, note the following coefficients: $a=3k$, $b=12$, and $c=k$.\n\nSo, we require\n\n$$\nD\\begin{vmatrix}> 0 \\\\ b^{2} - 4ac\\end{vmatrix}> 0; (12)^{2} - 4(3k)(k)\\begin{vmatrix}> 0 \\\\ 144 - 12k^{2}\\end{vmatrix}> 0; 144\\begin{vmatrix}> 12k^{2} \\\\ 12k^{2}\\end{vmatrix}< 144; k^{2}\\begin{vmatrix}< 12 \\\\ \\sqrt{k^{2}}\\end{vmatrix}< \\sqrt{12}; \\mid k \\mid \\mid < 2\\sqrt{3}\n$$\n\nIf $\\mid k \\mid < 2\\sqrt{3}$, then $-2\\sqrt{3} < k < 2\\sqrt{3}$. Using interval notation, then, our solution is $(-2\\sqrt{3},2\\sqrt{3})$.\n\nFinally, taking into account that\n\n$$\nk\\neq 0\n$$\n\nwe have $k\\in(-2\\sqrt{3}, 0) \\cup (0,2\\sqrt{3})$." "A quadratic equation has two distinct real solutions if its discriminant is positive: $\\mathcal{D}>0$.\n\nTo compute the discriminant, note the following coefficients: $a=3k$, $b=12$, and $c=k$.\n\nSo, we require\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathcal{D} & >0 \\\\ {b}^{2}-4ac & >0 \\\\ (12)^{2}-4(3k)(k) & >0 \\\\ 144-12{k}^{2} & >0 \\\\ 144 & >12{k}^{2} \\\\ 12{k}^{2} & <144 \\\\ {k}^{2} & <12 \\\\ \\sqrt{{k}^{2}} & <\\sqrt{12} \\\\ |k| & <2\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $\\mid k \\mid < 2\\sqrt{3}$, then $-2\\sqrt{3} < k < 2\\sqrt{3}$. Using interval notation, then, our solution is $(-2\\sqrt{3},2\\sqrt{3})$.\n\nFinally, taking into account that\n\n$$\nk\\neq 0\n$$\n\nwe have $k\\in(-2\\sqrt{3}, 0) \\cup (0,2\\sqrt{3})$."] [:db/cas 17592186054509 :question/worked-solution "A quadratic equation has two distinct real solutions if the discriminant is positive: $\\mathcal{D} > 0$.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the equation in standard quadratic form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx(kx + 10) &= -5 \\\\\nkx^{2} + 10x &= -5 \\\\\nkx^{2} + 10x + 5 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe coefficients of our quadratic equation are the following:\n\n$$\na = k, \\qquad b = 10, \\qquad c = 5\n$$\n\nSo, computing the discriminant, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nD &= b^{2} - 4ac \\\\\n&= (10)^{2} - 4(k)(5) \\\\\n&= 100 - 20k\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo have distinct real solutions, we need $\\mathcal{D} > 0{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}100 - 20k & > 0 \\\\ 100 & > 20k \\\\ 20k & < 100 \\\\ k & < \\frac{100}{20} \\\\ k & < 5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSince $k$ must be positive and an integer, we conclude that the greatest possible value is $k=4$." "A quadratic equation has two distinct real solutions if the discriminant is positive: $\\mathcal{D} > 0$.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the equation in standard quadratic form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx(kx + 10) &= -5 \\\\\nkx^{2} + 10x &= -5 \\\\\nkx^{2} + 10x + 5 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe coefficients of our quadratic equation are the following:\n\n$$\na = k, \\qquad b = 10, \\qquad c = 5\n$$\n\nSo, computing the discriminant, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nD &= b^{2} - 4ac \\\\\n&= (10)^{2} - 4(k)(5) \\\\\n&= 100 - 20k\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo have distinct real solutions, we need $\\mathcal{D} > 0{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}100-20k & >0 \\\\ 100 & >20k \\\\ 20k & <100 \\\\ k & <\\frac{100}{20} \\\\ k & <5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $k$ must be positive and an integer, we conclude that the greatest possible value is $k=4$."] [:db/cas 17592186054512 :question/problem "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 3}\\left[(2x-1)^3(x-2)^9\\right].\n$$" "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\rightarrow 3}\\left[(2x-1)^3(x-2)^9\\right].\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186054512 :question/worked-solution "Here, we apply the product rule and the power rule to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 3}\\left[(2x-1)^3(x-2)^9\\right]   &= \\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 3}(2x-1)^3\\cdot  \\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 3}(x-2)^9\\\\[3pt]\n                                                                                    &= \\left(\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 3}(2x-1)\\right)^3\\cdot  \\left(\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 3}(x-2)\\right)^9\\\\[3pt]\n                                                                                    &= (2\\cdot 3 -1)^3\\cdot (3-2)^9\\\\[3pt]\n                                                                                    &= 5^3 \\cdot 1^9\\\\[3pt]\n                                                                                    & = 125.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Here, we apply the product rule and the power rule to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\lim_{x\\rightarrow 3}\\left[(2x-1)^3(x-2)^9\\right]   &= \\lim_{x\\rightarrow 3}(2x-1)^3\\cdot  \\lim_{x\\rightarrow 3}(x-2)^9\\\\[3pt]\n                                                                                    &= \\left(\\lim_{x\\rightarrow 3}(2x-1)\\right)^3\\cdot  \\left(\\lim_{x\\rightarrow 3}(x-2)\\right)^9\\\\[3pt]\n                                                                                    &= (2\\cdot 3 -1)^3\\cdot (3-2)^9\\\\[3pt]\n                                                                                    &= 5^3 \\cdot 1^9\\\\[3pt]\n                                                                                    & = 125.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186054513 :question/problem "The figure below shows the graph of $f(x).$ Determine $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 2} \\left(\\dfrac{2-7x}{xf(x)+2}\\right)^3.\n$$\n\n![](images/50/5059a2cee34ad8547bf1af6239ab4a8356e715cd920707cf5f33d2de105a5c1d.png)" "The figure below shows the graph of $f(x).$ Determine $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\rightarrow 2} \\left(\\dfrac{2-7x}{xf(x)+2}\\right)^3.\n$$\n\n![](images/50/5059a2cee34ad8547bf1af6239ab4a8356e715cd920707cf5f33d2de105a5c1d.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186054513 :question/worked-solution "From the graph, we find $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 2} f(x)=3.\n$$ Therefore, using the power rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 2}{\\left(\\frac{2-7x}{xf(x)+2}\\right)}^{3} & ={({lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{2-7x}{xf(x)+2})}^{3} \\\\  & ={\\left(\\frac{2-7(2)}{(2\\cdot 3)+2}\\right)}^{3} \\\\  & ={\\left(\\frac{-12}{8}\\right)}^{3} \\\\  & ={\\left(\\frac{-3}{2}\\right)}^{3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{27}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "From the graph, we find $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\rightarrow 2} f(x)=3.\n$$ Therefore, using the power rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 2}{\\left(\\frac{2-7x}{xf(x)+2}\\right)}^{3} & ={(\\lim_{x\\to 2}\\frac{2-7x}{xf(x)+2})}^{3} \\\\  & ={\\left(\\frac{2-7(2)}{(2\\cdot 3)+2}\\right)}^{3} \\\\  & ={\\left(\\frac{-12}{8}\\right)}^{3} \\\\  & ={\\left(\\frac{-3}{2}\\right)}^{3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{27}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186054518 :question/problem "Find $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_\\limits{x \\to 1} \\dfrac{\\sqrt{x+8}}{x-2}.\n$$" "Find $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x \\to 1} \\dfrac{\\sqrt{x+8}}{x-2}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186054518 :question/worked-solution "Applying the quotient rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 1} \\dfrac{\\sqrt{x+8}}{x-2}\n=\\dfrac{\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 1} \\sqrt{x+8}}{\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 1}(x-2)}.\n$$\n\nWe calculate the limits of the numerator and denominator, separately.\n\nApplying the root rule in the numerator, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 1}\\sqrt{x+8} & =\\sqrt{{lim}_{x\\to 1}(x+8)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+8} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the denominator, we have\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 1}(x-2) & =1-2 \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{\\sqrt{x+8}}{x-2} & =\\frac{3}{(-1)} \\\\  & =-3\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Applying the quotient rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\rightarrow 1} \\dfrac{\\sqrt{x+8}}{x-2}\n=\\dfrac{\\lim_{x\\rightarrow 1} \\sqrt{x+8}}{\\lim_{x\\rightarrow 1}(x-2)}.\n$$\n\nWe calculate the limits of the numerator and denominator, separately.\n\nApplying the root rule in the numerator, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 1}\\sqrt{x+8} & =\\sqrt{\\lim_{x\\to 1}(x+8)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+8} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the denominator, we have\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 1}(x-2) & =1-2 \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 1}\\frac{\\sqrt{x+8}}{x-2} & =\\frac{3}{(-1)} \\\\  & =-3\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186054519 :question/problem "Find $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow -2}\\left(\\dfrac{f(x)}{x^2-1}\\right)^{2/3}\n$$ for the function $f(x)$ plotted below.\n\n![](images/58/583e67513466b35aafcae69c81d5703b45ffada4bdc08bb34de15ee6ce1d53e5.png)" "Find $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\rightarrow -2}\\left(\\dfrac{f(x)}{x^2-1}\\right)^{2/3}\n$$ for the function $f(x)$ plotted below.\n\n![](images/58/583e67513466b35aafcae69c81d5703b45ffada4bdc08bb34de15ee6ce1d53e5.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186054519 :question/worked-solution "According to the power rule and the quotient rules,\n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -2}{\\left(\\frac{f(x)}{{x}^{2}-1}\\right)}^{2/3} & ={\\left(\\frac{{lim}_{x\\to -2}f(x)}{{lim}_{x\\to -2}({x}^{2}-1)}\\right)}^{2/3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe find from the graph that the limit of the numerator is\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow -2} f(x)=1.\n$$\n\n We also calculate the limit of the denominator as\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -2}({x}^{2}-1) & =(-2)^{2}-1=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n So we get\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -2}{\\left(\\frac{f(x)}{{x}^{2}-1}\\right)}^{2/3} & ={\\left(\\frac{{lim}_{x\\to -2}f(x)}{{lim}_{x\\to -2}({x}^{2}-1)}\\right)}^{2/3} \\\\  & ={\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}^{2/3} \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{1}{9}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "According to the power rule and the quotient rules,\n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to -2}{\\left(\\frac{f(x)}{{x}^{2}-1}\\right)}^{2/3} & ={\\left(\\frac{\\lim_{x\\to -2}f(x)}{\\lim_{x\\to -2}({x}^{2}-1)}\\right)}^{2/3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe find from the graph that the limit of the numerator is\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\rightarrow -2} f(x)=1.\n$$\n\n We also calculate the limit of the denominator as\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to -2}({x}^{2}-1) & =(-2)^{2}-1=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n So we get\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to -2}{\\left(\\frac{f(x)}{{x}^{2}-1}\\right)}^{2/3} & ={\\left(\\frac{\\lim_{x\\to -2}f(x)}{\\lim_{x\\to -2}({x}^{2}-1)}\\right)}^{2/3} \\\\  & ={\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}^{2/3} \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{1}{9}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186054580 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_\\limits{x\\to \\pi/4}\\dfrac{\\sin^2{x}-\\cos^2{x}}{2\\sin{x}\\cos{2x}}=\n$$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to \\pi/4}\\dfrac{\\sin^2{x}-\\cos^2{x}}{2\\sin{x}\\cos{2x}}=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186054580 :question/worked-solution "If we attempt to evaluate this limit directly, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → π/4)\\frac{\\sin^{2} x - \\cos^{2} x}{2\\sin x\\cos 2x} &= (\\sin^{2} \\left(\\frac{π}{4}\\right) - \\cos^{2} \\left(\\frac{π}{4}\\right))/(2\\sin \\left(\\frac{π}{4}\\right)\\cos (2 \\cdot \\frac{π}{4})) \\\\\n&= (1 - 1)/(\\sqrt{2}\\cos \\left(\\frac{π}{2}\\right)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{0}{0}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we need to simplify the expression. The double angle formula for cosine will be useful in this case:\n\n$$\n\\cos{2x}=\\cos^2{x}-\\sin^2{x}\n$$\n\nUsing the double angle formula above, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → π/4)\\frac{\\sin^{2} x - \\cos^{2} x}{2\\sin x\\cos 2x} &= lim_(x → π/4)(\\sin^{2} x - \\cos^{2} x)/(2\\sin x(\\cos^{2} x - \\sin^{2} x)) \\\\\n&= lim_(x → π/4)(- (\\cos^{2} x - \\sin^{2} x))/(2\\sin x(\\cos^{2} x - \\sin^{2} x)) \\\\\n&= lim_(x → π/4)\\left(-\\frac{1}{2\\sin x}\\right) \\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\\n&=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" "If we attempt to evaluate this limit directly, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\lim_{x \\to  π/4}\\frac{\\sin^{2} x - \\cos^{2} x}{2\\sin x\\cos 2x} &= (\\sin^{2} \\left(\\frac{π}{4}\\right) - \\cos^{2} \\left(\\frac{π}{4}\\right))/(2\\sin \\left(\\frac{π}{4}\\right)\\cos (2 \\cdot \\frac{π}{4})) \\\\\n&= (1 - 1)/(\\sqrt{2}\\cos \\left(\\frac{π}{2}\\right)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{0}{0}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we need to simplify the expression. The double angle formula for cosine will be useful in this case:\n\n$$\n\\cos{2x}=\\cos^2{x}-\\sin^2{x}\n$$\n\nUsing the double angle formula above, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\lim_{x \\to  π/4}\\frac{\\sin^{2} x - \\cos^{2} x}{2\\sin x\\cos 2x} &= \\lim_{x \\to  π/4}(\\sin^{2} x - \\cos^{2} x)/(2\\sin x(\\cos^{2} x - \\sin^{2} x)) \\\\\n&= \\lim_{x \\to  π/4}(- (\\cos^{2} x - \\sin^{2} x))/(2\\sin x(\\cos^{2} x - \\sin^{2} x)) \\\\\n&= \\lim_{x \\to  π/4}\\left(-\\frac{1}{2\\sin x}\\right) \\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\\n&=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186054583 :question/worked-solution "The radius of convergence for the Taylor series of $f'(x)$ is the same as the Taylor series of $f(x).$ So, to find the radius of convergence of $f'(x),$ we need to compute $R.$\n\nTo calculate $R,$ we can use the ratio test. Computing the limit of the absolute value of the ratio of two consecutive terms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{(-3)^{n+1}\\,{x}^{n+1}}{(n+1)^{3}}\\cdot \\frac{{n}^{3}}{(-3)^{n}\\,{x}^{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{(-3)x\\cdot {n}^{3}}{(n+1)^{3}}| \\\\  & =3|x|{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{3}} \\\\  & =3|x|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe series converges when $L<1.$ So, it converges when\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3|x| & <1 \\\\ |x| & <\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of convergence is $R = \\dfrac 1 3.$" "The radius of convergence for the Taylor series of $f'(x)$ is the same as the Taylor series of $f(x).$ So, to find the radius of convergence of $f'(x),$ we need to compute $R.$\n\nTo calculate $R,$ we can use the ratio test. Computing the limit of the absolute value of the ratio of two consecutive terms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{(-3)^{n+1}\\,{x}^{n+1}}{(n+1)^{3}}\\cdot \\frac{{n}^{3}}{(-3)^{n}\\,{x}^{n}}| \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{(-3)x\\cdot {n}^{3}}{(n+1)^{3}}| \\\\  & =3|x|\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{3}} \\\\  & =3|x|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe series converges when $L<1.$ So, it converges when\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3|x| & <1 \\\\ |x| & <\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of convergence is $R = \\dfrac 1 3.$"] [:db/cas 17592186054584 :question/problem "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_\\limits{x\\to 0}\\dfrac{1-\\cos x}{3\\sin^2\\left(\\dfrac{x}{2}\\right)}.\n$$" "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to 0}\\dfrac{1-\\cos x}{3\\sin^2\\left(\\dfrac{x}{2}\\right)}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186054584 :question/worked-solution "If we attempt to evaluate this limit directly, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → 0)(1 - \\cos x)/(3\\sin^{2} \\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)) &= (1 - \\cos (0))/(3\\sin^{2} (0)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1 - 1}{3 \\cdot 0} \\\\\n&= \\frac{0}{0}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we need to simplify the expression. Again we will use the double angle formula for cosine, but in a different form:\n\n$$\n\\cos{x}=1-2\\sin^2\\left(\\dfrac{x}{2}\\right)\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → 0)(1 - \\cos x)/(3\\sin^{2} \\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)) &= lim_(x → 0)(1 - (1 - 2\\sin^{2} \\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)))/(3\\sin^{2} \\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)) \\\\\n&= lim_(x → 0)(2\\sin^{2} \\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right))/(3\\sin^{2} \\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)) \\\\\n&= lim_(x → 0)\\frac{2}{3} \\\\\n&= \\frac{2}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" "If we attempt to evaluate this limit directly, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\lim_{x \\to  0}(1 - \\cos x)/(3\\sin^{2} \\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)) &= (1 - \\cos (0))/(3\\sin^{2} (0)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1 - 1}{3 \\cdot 0} \\\\\n&= \\frac{0}{0}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we need to simplify the expression. Again we will use the double angle formula for cosine, but in a different form:\n\n$$\n\\cos{x}=1-2\\sin^2\\left(\\dfrac{x}{2}\\right)\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\lim_{x \\to  0}(1 - \\cos x)/(3\\sin^{2} \\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)) &= \\lim_{x \\to  0}(1 - (1 - 2\\sin^{2} \\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)))/(3\\sin^{2} \\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)) \\\\\n&= \\lim_{x \\to  0}(2\\sin^{2} \\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right))/(3\\sin^{2} \\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)) \\\\\n&= \\lim_{x \\to  0}\\frac{2}{3} \\\\\n&= \\frac{2}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186054585 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_\\limits{x\\to (-\\pi/2)}\\dfrac{\\cos^2x}{1+\\sin x}=\n$$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to (-\\pi/2)}\\dfrac{\\cos^2x}{1+\\sin x}=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186054585 :question/worked-solution "If we attempt to evaluate this limit directly, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → (- π/2))\\frac{\\cos^{2} x}{1 + \\sin x} &= (\\cos^{2} \\left(-\\frac{π}{2}\\right))/((1 + \\sin \\left(-\\frac{π}{2}\\right))) \\\\\n&= \\frac{0}{1 - 1} \\\\\n&= \\frac{0}{0}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we need to simplify the expression. In particular, we will use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n\\cos^2{x}=1-\\sin^2{x}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → (- π/2))\\frac{\\cos^{2} x}{1 + \\sin x} &= lim_(x → (- π/2))\\frac{\\cos^{2} x}{1 + \\sin x} \\\\\n&= lim_(x → (- π/2))\\frac{1 - \\sin^{2} x}{1 + \\sin x} \\\\\n&= lim_(x → (- π/2))((1 + \\sin x)(1 - \\sin x))/(1 + \\sin x) \\\\\n&= lim_(x → (- π/2))(1 - \\sin x) \\\\\n&= 1 - \\sin \\left(-\\frac{π}{2}\\right) \\\\\n&= 1 - (-1) \\\\\n&= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" "If we attempt to evaluate this limit directly, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\lim_{x \\to  (- π/2)}\\frac{\\cos^{2} x}{1 + \\sin x} &= (\\cos^{2} \\left(-\\frac{π}{2}\\right))/((1 + \\sin \\left(-\\frac{π}{2}\\right))) \\\\\n&= \\frac{0}{1 - 1} \\\\\n&= \\frac{0}{0}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we need to simplify the expression. In particular, we will use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n\\cos^2{x}=1-\\sin^2{x}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\lim_{x \\to  (- π/2)}\\frac{\\cos^{2} x}{1 + \\sin x} &= \\lim_{x \\to  (- π/2)}\\frac{\\cos^{2} x}{1 + \\sin x} \\\\\n&= \\lim_{x \\to  (- π/2)}\\frac{1 - \\sin^{2} x}{1 + \\sin x} \\\\\n&= \\lim_{x \\to  (- π/2)}((1 + \\sin x)(1 - \\sin x))/(1 + \\sin x) \\\\\n&= \\lim_{x \\to  (- π/2)}(1 - \\sin x) \\\\\n&= 1 - \\sin \\left(-\\frac{π}{2}\\right) \\\\\n&= 1 - (-1) \\\\\n&= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186054586 :question/problem "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_\\limits{x\\to 0}\\dfrac{\\sec x-1}{7\\tan^2x}.\n$$" "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to 0}\\dfrac{\\sec x-1}{7\\tan^2x}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186054586 :question/worked-solution "If we attempt to evaluate this limit directly, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → 0)\\frac{\\sec x - 1}{7\\tan^{2} x} &= (\\sec (0) - 1)/(7\\tan^{2} (0)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1 - 1}{7 \\cdot 0} \\\\\n&= \\frac{0}{0}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we need to simplify the expression. In particular, we will use the Pythagorean identity for tangent and secant:\n\n$$\n\\tan^2{x}=\\sec^2{x}-1\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → 0)\\frac{\\sec x - 1}{7\\tan^{2} x} &= lim_(x → 0)(\\sec x - 1)/(7(\\sec^{2} x - 1)) \\\\\n&= lim_(x → 0)(\\sec x - 1)/(7(\\sec x - 1)(\\sec x + 1)) \\\\\n&= lim_(x → 0)(1)/(7(\\sec x + 1)) \\\\\n&= (1)/(7(\\sec (0) + 1)) \\\\\n&= (1)/(7(1 + 1)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{14}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" "If we attempt to evaluate this limit directly, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\lim_{x \\to  0}\\frac{\\sec x - 1}{7\\tan^{2} x} &= (\\sec (0) - 1)/(7\\tan^{2} (0)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1 - 1}{7 \\cdot 0} \\\\\n&= \\frac{0}{0}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we need to simplify the expression. In particular, we will use the Pythagorean identity for tangent and secant:\n\n$$\n\\tan^2{x}=\\sec^2{x}-1\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\lim_{x \\to  0}\\frac{\\sec x - 1}{7\\tan^{2} x} &= \\lim_{x \\to  0}(\\sec x - 1)/(7(\\sec^{2} x - 1)) \\\\\n&= \\lim_{x \\to  0}(\\sec x - 1)/(7(\\sec x - 1)(\\sec x + 1)) \\\\\n&= \\lim_{x \\to  0}(1)/(7(\\sec x + 1)) \\\\\n&= (1)/(7(\\sec (0) + 1)) \\\\\n&= (1)/(7(1 + 1)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{14}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186055053 :question/problem "Find $lim_(x → 4)\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{x^{3} - 10}{x^{2} - 3x - 2}}$." "Find $\\lim_{x \\to  4}\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{x^{3} - 10}{x^{2} - 3x - 2}}$."] [:db/cas 17592186055104 :question/problem "If $y = 0$, then $2\\begin{vmatrix}1 - 3^{y} & -\\end{vmatrix}2^{y} - 4|=$ {{field-1}}." "If $y = 0$, then $2|1-{3}^{y}|-|{2}^{y}-4|=$ {{field-1}}."] [:db/cas 17592186055114 :question/problem "If $p = 1$, then $\\begin{vmatrix}p & ^{4} - 4\\end{vmatrix}p - \\left.3\\right|^{2} + 6 =$ {{field-1}}." "If $p = 1$, then $|p{|}^{4}-4|p-3{|}^{2}+6=$ {{field-1}}."] [:db/cas 17592186055232 :question/problem "Find $lim_(z → 3)((z - 5)^{5})/((z + 3)^{2})$." "Find $\\lim_{z \\to  3}((z - 5)^{5})/((z + 3)^{2})$."] [:db/cas 17592186055233 :question/worked-solution "From the graph, we get that ${lim}_{x\\to \\,-1}f(x)=0.$ \n\nFrom the product rule, we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,-1}(5+7x)^{3}(f(x)-x)^{4}={lim}_{x\\to \\,-1}(5+7x)^{3}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\,-1}(f(x)-x)^{4}.\n$$\n\nWe calculate the limits of each factor, separately.\n\nApplying the power rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,-1}(5+7x)^{3} & ={({lim}_{x\\to \\,-1}(5+7x))}^{3} \\\\  & =(5+7\\cdot (-1))^{3} \\\\  & =(-2)^{3} \\\\  & =-8,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,-1}(f(x)-x)^{4} & ={({lim}_{x\\to \\,-1}(f(x)-x))}^{4} \\\\  & =(0-(-1))^{4} \\\\  & ={1}^{4} \\\\  & =1\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,-1}(5+7x)^{3}(f(x)-x)^{4} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,-1}(5+7x)^{3}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\,-1}(f(x)-x)^{4} \\\\  & =(-8)\\cdot (1) \\\\  & =-8\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "From the graph, we get that $\\lim_{x\\to \\,-1}f(x)=0.$ \n\nFrom the product rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,-1}(5+7x)^{3}(f(x)-x)^{4}=\\lim_{x\\to \\,-1}(5+7x)^{3}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\,-1}(f(x)-x)^{4}.\n$$\n\nWe calculate the limits of each factor, separately.\n\nApplying the power rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\,-1}(5+7x)^{3} & ={(\\lim_{x\\to \\,-1}(5+7x))}^{3} \\\\  & =(5+7\\cdot (-1))^{3} \\\\  & =(-2)^{3} \\\\  & =-8,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\,-1}(f(x)-x)^{4} & ={(\\lim_{x\\to \\,-1}(f(x)-x))}^{4} \\\\  & =(0-(-1))^{4} \\\\  & ={1}^{4} \\\\  & =1\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\,-1}(5+7x)^{3}(f(x)-x)^{4} & =\\lim_{x\\to \\,-1}(5+7x)^{3}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\,-1}(f(x)-x)^{4} \\\\  & =(-8)\\cdot (1) \\\\  & =-8\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186055234 :question/problem "![](images/4a/4a0bb4656cbc3c4c3055635b2f9aa63357ed6422c7e641f3c8982d101cf62c94.png)\n\nFind $lim_(x → 1/2)(2(f(x))^{4})/((6x - 1)^{3})$ for the function $f(x)$ plotted above." "![](images/4a/4a0bb4656cbc3c4c3055635b2f9aa63357ed6422c7e641f3c8982d101cf62c94.png)\n\nFind $\\lim_{x \\to  1/2}(2(f(x))^{4})/((6x - 1)^{3})$ for the function $f(x)$ plotted above."] [:db/cas 17592186055235 :question/problem "![](images/c1/c19de6e0431131e0f6dd048e3c1b6544505d0479d98e423944191da690e2ae40.png)\n\nFind $lim_(x → - 1)\\sqrt{10 - 3f(x)}$ for the function $f(x)$ plotted above." "![](images/c1/c19de6e0431131e0f6dd048e3c1b6544505d0479d98e423944191da690e2ae40.png)\n\nFind $\\lim_{x \\to  - 1}\\sqrt{10 - 3f(x)}$ for the function $f(x)$ plotted above."] [:db/cas 17592186055236 :question/problem "![](images/10/10b0662032c021bb027a3ed54de3d03fbc2606f27ea5eaf8c50725dcb14b0854.png)\n\nFind $lim_(x → 0)\\sqrt[3]{6 + 4f(x)}$ for the function $f(x)$ plotted above." "![](images/10/10b0662032c021bb027a3ed54de3d03fbc2606f27ea5eaf8c50725dcb14b0854.png)\n\nFind $\\lim_{x \\to  0}\\sqrt[3]{6 + 4f(x)}$ for the function $f(x)$ plotted above."] [:db/cas 17592186055297 :question/problem "Find $lim_(x → 1)\\frac{2\\sqrt{x + 3}}{5x - 1}$." "Find $\\lim_{x \\to  1}\\frac{2\\sqrt{x + 3}}{5x - 1}$."] [:db/cas 17592186055312 :question/problem "Evaluate $lim_(x → 0)(7 - 7\\cos x)/(8\\sin^{2} \\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right))$." "Evaluate $\\lim_{x \\to  0}(7 - 7\\cos x)/(8\\sin^{2} \\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right))$."] [:db/cas 17592186055313 :question/problem "What is $lim_(x → π/2)\\frac{4\\cos^{2} x}{1 - \\sin x}$?" "What is $\\lim_{x \\to  π/2}\\frac{4\\cos^{2} x}{1 - \\sin x}$?"] [:db/cas 17592186055314 :question/problem "Evaluate $lim_(x → 0)\\frac{\\cos 2x - 1}{10\\sin^{2} x}$." "Evaluate $\\lim_{x \\to  0}\\frac{\\cos 2x - 1}{10\\sin^{2} x}$."] [:db/cas 17592186055315 :question/problem "Evaluate $lim_(x → π/2)\\frac{1 + \\cos 2x}{12\\cos^{2} x}$." "Evaluate $\\lim_{x \\to  π/2}\\frac{1 + \\cos 2x}{12\\cos^{2} x}$."] [:db/cas 17592186055316 :question/problem "Evaluate $lim_(x → (- π/4))(2 + 2\\cos (4x))/(\\cos^{2} (2x))$." "Evaluate $\\lim_{x \\to  (- π/4)}(2 + 2\\cos (4x))/(\\cos^{2} (2x))$."] [:db/cas 17592186055318 :question/problem "Evaluate $lim_(x → 0)(5\\sec (2x) - 5)/(12\\tan^{2} (2x))$." "Evaluate $\\lim_{x \\to  0}(5\\sec (2x) - 5)/(12\\tan^{2} (2x))$."] [:db/cas 17592186055319 :question/problem "What is $lim_(x → (- π))\\frac{\\cos x + 1}{\\sin^{2} x}$?" "What is $\\lim_{x \\to  (- π)}\\frac{\\cos x + 1}{\\sin^{2} x}$?"] [:db/cas 17592186055320 :question/problem "Evaluate $lim_(x → (- π/4))(4 + 4\\csc (2x))/(5\\cot^{2} (2x))$." "Evaluate $\\lim_{x \\to  (- π/4)}(4 + 4\\csc (2x))/(5\\cot^{2} (2x))$."] [:db/cas 17592186055358 :question/worked-solution "The radius of convergence for the Taylor series of ${f}^{′}(x)$ is the same as the Taylor series of $f(x).$ So, to find the radius of convergence of ${f}^{′}(x),$ we need to compute $R.$ \n\nTo calculate $R,$ we can use the ratio test. Computing the limit of the absolute value of the ratio of two consecutive terms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{(-1)^{n}{x}^{n+1}}{(n+1)!}\\cdot \\frac{n!}{(-1)^{n-1}{x}^{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{(-1)x\\cdot n!}{(n+1)!}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{|x|}{n+1} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe series converges when $L<1.$  Since $0<1$ for all $x,$ we conclude that \n$R=\\infty .$" "The radius of convergence for the Taylor series of ${f}^{′}(x)$ is the same as the Taylor series of $f(x).$ So, to find the radius of convergence of ${f}^{′}(x),$ we need to compute $R.$ \n\nTo calculate $R,$ we can use the ratio test. Computing the limit of the absolute value of the ratio of two consecutive terms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{(-1)^{n}{x}^{n+1}}{(n+1)!}\\cdot \\frac{n!}{(-1)^{n-1}{x}^{n}}| \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{(-1)x\\cdot n!}{(n+1)!}| \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{|x|}{n+1} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe series converges when $L<1.$  Since $0<1$ for all $x,$ we conclude that \n$R=\\infty .$"] [:db/cas 17592186055372 :question/problem "Find $lim_(y → 3)(5 - y)^{2}(2y - 5)^{11}$." "Find $\\lim_{y \\to  3}(5 - y)^{2}(2y - 5)^{11}$."] [:db/cas 17592186055505 :question/problem "Suppose that $X$ and $Y$ are sets. If $\\mid X|= 3$ and $\\mid Y|= 4$, then $\\begin{vmatrix}P(X) & +\\end{vmatrix}P(Y)|=$" "Suppose that $X$ and $Y$ are sets. If $\\mid X|= 3$ and $\\mid Y|= 4$, then $|\\mathcal{P}(X)|+|\\mathcal{P}(Y)|=$"] [:db/cas 17592186055938 :tutorial/content "For a quadratic equation\n\n$$\nax^2+bx+c=0\n$$\n\nthe discriminant\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{D} = b^2 - 4ac\n$$\n\ntells us about the equation's solutions:\n\n- If $\\mathcal{D} > 0$, there are *two distinct real solutions.*\n- If $\\mathcal{D} = 0$, there is *one distinct real solution.*\n- If $\\mathcal{D} < 0$, there are *no real solutions.*\n\nIf we are given a quadratic equation with an unknown parameter, then we can use the criterion $D > 0$ to determine the values of the unknown parameter such that the quadratic equation has two *distinct* real solutions.\n\nFor example, suppose we have the quadratic equation\n\n$$\nx^2+kx+9=0\n$$\n\nLet's find all the values of $k$ such that this equation has two distinct real solutions.\n\nA quadratic equation has two distinct real solutions if its discriminant is positive: $\\mathcal{D}>0$. To compute the discriminant, we first note that the coefficients of the equation are\n\n$$\na=1,\\quad b=k,\\quad c=9\n$$\n\nSo, we require\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}D & > 0 \\\\ b^{2} - 4ac & > 0 \\\\ (k)^{2} - 4(1)(9) & > 0 \\\\ k^{2} - 36 & > 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSolving this inequality for the variable $k$, we get the following:\n$$\n\\displaystyle k^{2}\\begin{vmatrix}> 36 \\\\ \\sqrt{k^{2}}\\end{vmatrix}> \\sqrt{36}; \\mid k \\mid \\mid > 6\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are\n\n$$\nk > 6\\quad\\textrm{or}\\quad k < -6\n$$\n\nUsing interval notation, we can write our solution as\n\n$$\nk \\in (-\\infty,-6) \\cup (6,\\infty)\n$$\n\nAny value of $k$ inside this interval will give a quadratic equation with *distinct* real solutions." "For a quadratic equation\n\n$$\nax^2+bx+c=0\n$$\n\nthe discriminant\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{D} = b^2 - 4ac\n$$\n\ntells us about the equation's solutions:\n\n- If $\\mathcal{D} > 0$, there are *two distinct real solutions.*\n- If $\\mathcal{D} = 0$, there is *one distinct real solution.*\n- If $\\mathcal{D} < 0$, there are *no real solutions.*\n\nIf we are given a quadratic equation with an unknown parameter, then we can use the criterion $D > 0$ to determine the values of the unknown parameter such that the quadratic equation has two *distinct* real solutions.\n\nFor example, suppose we have the quadratic equation\n\n$$\nx^2+kx+9=0\n$$\n\nLet's find all the values of $k$ such that this equation has two distinct real solutions.\n\nA quadratic equation has two distinct real solutions if its discriminant is positive: $\\mathcal{D}>0$. To compute the discriminant, we first note that the coefficients of the equation are\n\n$$\na=1,\\quad b=k,\\quad c=9\n$$\n\nSo, we require\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathcal{D} & >0 \\\\ {b}^{2}-4ac & >0 \\\\ (k)^{2}-4(1)(9) & >0 \\\\ {k}^{2}-36 & >0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this inequality for the variable $k$, we get the following:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{k}^{2} & >36 \\\\ \\sqrt{{k}^{2}} & >\\sqrt{36} \\\\ |k| & >6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are\n\n$$\nk > 6\\quad\\textrm{or}\\quad k < -6\n$$\n\nUsing interval notation, we can write our solution as\n\n$$\nk \\in (-\\infty,-6) \\cup (6,\\infty)\n$$\n\nAny value of $k$ inside this interval will give a quadratic equation with *distinct* real solutions."] [:db/cas 17592186055939 :tutorial/content "Recall that for a quadratic equation\n\n$$\nax^2 + bx + c = 0\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\na\\neq 0\n$$\n\nthe discriminant is defined as\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{D} = b^2 - 4ac\n$$\n\nThe discriminant tells us about the nature of the equation's solutions:\n\n- If $\\mathcal{D} > 0$, we have *two distinct real solutions*.\n- If $\\mathcal{D} = 0$, we have *one repeated real solution* (sometimes called a “double root”).\n- If $\\mathcal{D} < 0$, we have *no real solutions.*\n\nCombining the first two conditions, we can say that a quadratic equation *has real solutions* if the discriminant is *non-negative*:\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{D} \\geq 0\n$$\n\nIf a quadratic equation contains an unknown parameter and we know the equation has real solutions, we can use the condition\n\n$$\n\\mathcal D \\geq 0\n$$\n\nto place conditions on the unknown parameter.\n\nTo demonstrate, let's consider the equation\n\n$$\nx^2 - 2x + k = 0\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is a real parameter. We want to determine *all* the values of $k$ for which the equation has real solutions.\n\nTo compute the discriminant, we first note that the coefficients are\n\n$$\na = 1, \\qquad b = -2, \\qquad c = k\n$$\n\nSo, computing the discriminant, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nD &= b^{2} - 4ac \\\\\n&= (-2)^{2} - 4(1)(k) \\\\\n&= 4 - 4k\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo have real solutions, we need\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{D} \\geq 0\n$$\n\nThis gives the inequality\n\n$$\n4 - 4k \\geq 0\n$$\n\nSolving this inequality for the variable $k$, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}4 - 4k & \\ge 0 \\\\ 4 & \\ge 4k \\\\ 4k & \\le 4 \\\\ k & \\le \\frac{4}{4} = 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our solution is\n\n$$\nk\\leq 1\n$$\n\nWe can also express this solution using interval notation:\n\n$$\nk \\in (-\\infty, 1 ]\n$$\n\nAny value of $k$ in this interval will give a quadratic equation with real solutions.\n\nSometimes, these kinds of problems involve solving a simple quadratic inequality. Let's see an example." "Recall that for a quadratic equation\n\n$$\nax^2 + bx + c = 0\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\na\\neq 0\n$$\n\nthe discriminant is defined as\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{D} = b^2 - 4ac\n$$\n\nThe discriminant tells us about the nature of the equation's solutions:\n\n- If $\\mathcal{D} > 0$, we have *two distinct real solutions*.\n- If $\\mathcal{D} = 0$, we have *one repeated real solution* (sometimes called a “double root”).\n- If $\\mathcal{D} < 0$, we have *no real solutions.*\n\nCombining the first two conditions, we can say that a quadratic equation *has real solutions* if the discriminant is *non-negative*:\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{D} \\geq 0\n$$\n\nIf a quadratic equation contains an unknown parameter and we know the equation has real solutions, we can use the condition\n\n$$\n\\mathcal D \\geq 0\n$$\n\nto place conditions on the unknown parameter.\n\nTo demonstrate, let's consider the equation\n\n$$\nx^2 - 2x + k = 0\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is a real parameter. We want to determine *all* the values of $k$ for which the equation has real solutions.\n\nTo compute the discriminant, we first note that the coefficients are\n\n$$\na = 1, \\qquad b = -2, \\qquad c = k\n$$\n\nSo, computing the discriminant, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nD &= b^{2} - 4ac \\\\\n&= (-2)^{2} - 4(1)(k) \\\\\n&= 4 - 4k\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo have real solutions, we need\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{D} \\geq 0\n$$\n\nThis gives the inequality\n\n$$\n4 - 4k \\geq 0\n$$\n\nSolving this inequality for the variable $k$, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4-4k & \\ge 0 \\\\ 4 & \\ge 4k \\\\ 4k & \\le 4 \\\\ k & \\le \\frac{4}{4}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our solution is\n\n$$\nk\\leq 1\n$$\n\nWe can also express this solution using interval notation:\n\n$$\nk \\in (-\\infty, 1 ]\n$$\n\nAny value of $k$ in this interval will give a quadratic equation with real solutions.\n\nSometimes, these kinds of problems involve solving a simple quadratic inequality. Let's see an example."] [:db/cas 17592186055953 :tutorial/content "Let's consider the following system of equations.\n\n$$\n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}y = x + 1 \\\\ y = 4 x - 3\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\n\nThe graph corresponding to the system is shown below:\n\n![](images/15/1545b0d9b78f93f18867c20dc40375ba3f3837ea3087a9a9f203cdafb7c2e859.png)\n\nWe can see from the graph that the $x$-value that satisfies the system lies somewhere between $1.0$ and $1.5$.\n\nWe can get a better approximation by setting up a table of values, like the one shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $1.0$ | $1.1$ | $1.2$ | $1.3$ | $1.4$ | $1.5$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $y_1=x+1$ |  |  |  |  |  |  |\n| $y_2=4x-3$ |  |  |  |  |  |  |\n| $\\mid y_1-y_2 \\mid$ |  |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nNow, we fill in the table as follows:\n\n- First, we calculate the $y$-values on both lines for our different values of $x$.\n- Then, we calculate $\\mid y_2 - y_1 \\mid$ for each value of $x$. We call this the **error.**\n\nFor example, consider the first column of the table, which corresponds to $x=1.0$. Substituting $x=1.0$ into the expressions for $y_1$ and $y_2$, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny_{1} &= x + 1 \\\\\n&= 1.0 + 1 \\\\\n&= 2.0 \\\\\ny_{2} &= 4x - 3 \\\\\n&= 4(1.0) - 3 \\\\\n&= 1.0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we find the corresponding error:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{vmatrix}y_{1} - y_{2} & &= & 2.0 - 1.0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\mid 1.0 \\mid \\\\\n&= 1.0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe fill this information into the first column:\n\n| $x$ | $1.0$ | $1.1$ | $1.2$ | $1.3$ | $1.4$ | $1.5$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $y_1=x+1$ | $2.0$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n| $y_2=4x-3$ | $1.0$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n| $\\mid y_1-y_2 \\mid$ | $1.0$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nRepeating this procedure for each column in the table, we arrive at the following result.\n\n| $x$ | $1.0$ | $1.1$ | $1.2$ | $1.3$ | $1.4$ | $1.5$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $y_1=x+1$ | $2.0$ | $2.1$ | $2.2$ | $2.3$ | $2.4$ | $2.5$ |\n| $y_2=4x-3$ | $1.0$ | $1.4$ | $1.8$ | $2.2$ | $2.6$ | $3.0$ |\n| $\\mid y_1-y_2 \\mid$ | $1.0$ | $0.7$ | $0.4$ | $0.1$ | $0.2$ | $0.5$ |\n\nFinally, we look for the smallest error. In the bottom row of the table, we see that $0.1$ is the smallest error. This corresponds to $x=1.3$.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nx\\approx1.3\n$$\n\nis our approximate solution." "Let's consider the following system of equations.\n\n$$\n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}y = x + 1 \\\\ y = 4 x - 3\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\n\nThe graph corresponding to the system is shown below:\n\n![](images/15/1545b0d9b78f93f18867c20dc40375ba3f3837ea3087a9a9f203cdafb7c2e859.png)\n\nWe can see from the graph that the $x$-value that satisfies the system lies somewhere between $1.0$ and $1.5$.\n\nWe can get a better approximation by setting up a table of values, like the one shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $1.0$ | $1.1$ | $1.2$ | $1.3$ | $1.4$ | $1.5$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $y_1=x+1$ |  |  |  |  |  |  |\n| $y_2=4x-3$ |  |  |  |  |  |  |\n| $\\mid y_1-y_2 \\mid$ |  |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nNow, we fill in the table as follows:\n\n- First, we calculate the $y$-values on both lines for our different values of $x$.\n- Then, we calculate $\\mid y_2 - y_1 \\mid$ for each value of $x$. We call this the **error.**\n\nFor example, consider the first column of the table, which corresponds to $x=1.0$. Substituting $x=1.0$ into the expressions for $y_1$ and $y_2$, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny_{1} &= x + 1 \\\\\n&= 1.0 + 1 \\\\\n&= 2.0 \\\\\ny_{2} &= 4x - 3 \\\\\n&= 4(1.0) - 3 \\\\\n&= 1.0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we find the corresponding error:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{y}_{1}-{y}_{2}| & =|2.0-1.0| \\\\  & =|1.0| \\\\  & =1.0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe fill this information into the first column:\n\n| $x$ | $1.0$ | $1.1$ | $1.2$ | $1.3$ | $1.4$ | $1.5$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $y_1=x+1$ | $2.0$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n| $y_2=4x-3$ | $1.0$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n| $\\mid y_1-y_2 \\mid$ | $1.0$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nRepeating this procedure for each column in the table, we arrive at the following result.\n\n| $x$ | $1.0$ | $1.1$ | $1.2$ | $1.3$ | $1.4$ | $1.5$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $y_1=x+1$ | $2.0$ | $2.1$ | $2.2$ | $2.3$ | $2.4$ | $2.5$ |\n| $y_2=4x-3$ | $1.0$ | $1.4$ | $1.8$ | $2.2$ | $2.6$ | $3.0$ |\n| $\\mid y_1-y_2 \\mid$ | $1.0$ | $0.7$ | $0.4$ | $0.1$ | $0.2$ | $0.5$ |\n\nFinally, we look for the smallest error. In the bottom row of the table, we see that $0.1$ is the smallest error. This corresponds to $x=1.3$.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nx\\approx1.3\n$$\n\nis our approximate solution."] [:db/cas 17592186055954 :tutorial/content "The **power rule** states that the limit of a power is the power of the limit. More precisely, if $n$ is any positive integer and $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow a}f(x)=L,\n$$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to a}(f(x))^{n}={({lim}_{x\\to a}f(x))}^{n}={L}^{n}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor example, with the power rule, we can evaluate a limit like \n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 2}(3x-4)^{8}\n$$\n\n by evaluating the limit of the expression within parentheses and then taking the power of the result.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 2}(3x-4)^{8} & ={({lim}_{x\\to 2}(3x-4))}^{8} \\\\  & =(3\\cdot 2-4)^{8} \\\\  & ={2}^{8} \\\\  & =256\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The **power rule** states that the limit of a power is the power of the limit. More precisely, if $n$ is any positive integer and $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\rightarrow a}f(x)=L,\n$$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to a}(f(x))^{n}={(\\lim_{x\\to a}f(x))}^{n}={L}^{n}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor example, with the power rule, we can evaluate a limit like \n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\rightarrow 2}(3x-4)^{8}\n$$\n\n by evaluating the limit of the expression within parentheses and then taking the power of the result.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 2}(3x-4)^{8} & ={(\\lim_{x\\to 2}(3x-4))}^{8} \\\\  & =(3\\cdot 2-4)^{8} \\\\  & ={2}^{8} \\\\  & =256\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186055955 :tutorial/content "The **root rule** states that the limit of a root is the root of the limit (provided that the root exists).\n\nMore precisely, if $n$ is any positive integer and $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow a}f(x)=L,\n$$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to a}\\sqrt[n]{f(x)}=\\sqrt[n]{{lim}_{x\\to a}f(x)}=\\sqrt[n]{L}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor example, with the root rule, we can evaluate a limit like \n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 2}\\sqrt{3x-2}\n$$\n\n by evaluating the limit of the expression within the root and then taking the root of the result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 2}\\sqrt{3x-2} & =\\sqrt{{lim}_{x\\to 2}(3x-2)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3\\cdot 2-2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The **root rule** states that the limit of a root is the root of the limit (provided that the root exists).\n\nMore precisely, if $n$ is any positive integer and $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\rightarrow a}f(x)=L,\n$$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to a}\\sqrt[n]{f(x)}=\\sqrt[n]{\\lim_{x\\to a}f(x)}=\\sqrt[n]{L}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor example, with the root rule, we can evaluate a limit like \n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\rightarrow 2}\\sqrt{3x-2}\n$$\n\n by evaluating the limit of the expression within the root and then taking the root of the result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 2}\\sqrt{3x-2} & =\\sqrt{\\lim_{x\\to 2}(3x-2)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3\\cdot 2-2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186055956 :tutorial/content "If we combine the power rule and the root rule, then we get the following useful generalization: if $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow a}f(x)=L,\n$$ then for any fraction $p/q,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow a}\\left(f(x)\\right)^{p/q} =  \\left(\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow a} f(x)\\right)^{p/q} = L^{p/q}\n$$\n\nprovided that $L^{p/q}$ exists.\n\nFor example, we can evaluate a limit like \n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{y\\rightarrow 64} y^{-1/6}\n$$\n\n by evaluating the limit of $y$ and then exponentiating the result to the fractional exponent:\n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{y\\to 64}{y}^{-1/6} & ={({lim}_{y\\to 64}y)}^{-1/6} \\\\  & =(64)^{-1/6} \\\\  & ={({2}^{6})}^{-1/6} \\\\  & ={2}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "If we combine the power rule and the root rule, then we get the following useful generalization: if $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\rightarrow a}f(x)=L,\n$$ then for any fraction $p/q,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\rightarrow a}\\left(f(x)\\right)^{p/q} =  \\left(\\lim_{x\\rightarrow a} f(x)\\right)^{p/q} = L^{p/q}\n$$\n\nprovided that $L^{p/q}$ exists.\n\nFor example, we can evaluate a limit like \n\n$$\n\\lim_{y\\rightarrow 64} y^{-1/6}\n$$\n\n by evaluating the limit of $y$ and then exponentiating the result to the fractional exponent:\n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{y\\to 64}{y}^{-1/6} & ={(\\lim_{y\\to 64}y)}^{-1/6} \\\\  & =(64)^{-1/6} \\\\  & ={({2}^{6})}^{-1/6} \\\\  & ={2}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186055961 :tutorial/content "Suppose that we want to calculate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\to \\pi/2}\\dfrac{\\sin{2x}}{2\\cos{x}}\n$$\n\nIf we attempt to evaluate this limit by directly substituting\n\n$$\nx=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\n$$\n\nwe get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → π/2)\\frac{\\sin 2x}{2\\cos x} &= (\\sin (2 \\cdot \\frac{π}{2}))/(2\\cos \\left(\\frac{π}{2}\\right)) \\\\\n&= (\\sin (π))/(2\\cos \\left(\\frac{π}{2}\\right)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{0}{0}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, if we simplify the fraction before attempting to evaluate the limit, we obtain a more clear result.\n\nTo simplify the fraction in this particular case, the double angle formula for sine will be useful:\n\n$$\n\\sin{2x}=2\\sin{x}\\cos{x}\n$$\n\nUsing the double angle formula above, we have\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle lim_{x \\to  \\pi  / 2} {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\sin   2 x}{2 \\cos   x}} & \\displaystyle  = lim_{x \\to  \\pi  / 2} {\\displaystyle \\frac{2 \\sin   x \\cos   x}{2 \\cos   x}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = lim_{x \\to  \\pi  / 2} {\\displaystyle \\frac{2 \\sin   x \\cancel{\\cos   x}}{2 \\cancel{\\cos   x}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = lim_{x \\to  \\pi  / 2} \\sin   x \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = \\sin   ( {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\pi }{2}} ) \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = 1.\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nIn other cases, we may need to use a different trigonometric identity." "Suppose that we want to calculate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\pi/2}\\dfrac{\\sin{2x}}{2\\cos{x}}\n$$\n\nIf we attempt to evaluate this limit by directly substituting\n\n$$\nx=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\n$$\n\nwe get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\lim_{x \\to  π/2}\\frac{\\sin 2x}{2\\cos x} &= (\\sin (2 \\cdot \\frac{π}{2}))/(2\\cos \\left(\\frac{π}{2}\\right)) \\\\\n&= (\\sin (π))/(2\\cos \\left(\\frac{π}{2}\\right)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{0}{0}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, if we simplify the fraction before attempting to evaluate the limit, we obtain a more clear result.\n\nTo simplify the fraction in this particular case, the double angle formula for sine will be useful:\n\n$$\n\\sin{2x}=2\\sin{x}\\cos{x}\n$$\n\nUsing the double angle formula above, we have\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle lim_{x \\to  \\pi  / 2} {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\sin   2 x}{2 \\cos   x}} & \\displaystyle  = \\lim_{x \\to  \\pi  / 2} {\\displaystyle \\frac{2 \\sin   x \\cos   x}{2 \\cos   x}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = \\lim_{x \\to  \\pi  / 2} {\\displaystyle \\frac{2 \\sin   x \\cancel{\\cos   x}}{2 \\cancel{\\cos   x}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = \\lim_{x \\to  \\pi  / 2} \\sin   x \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = \\sin   ( {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\pi }{2}} ) \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = 1.\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nIn other cases, we may need to use a different trigonometric identity."] [:db/cas 17592186056063 :tutorial/content "We can use the same method to multiply decimals even when they are greater than $1$. For example, to compute\n\n$$\n4.7 \\times 0.8\n$$\n\nwe first ignore the decimal points and multiply as if both numbers were whole numbers. We ignore any leading ${\\color{red}{0}}$'s during the multiplication:\n\n$$\n5; 4\\begin{vmatrix}.7 \\\\ \\cdot\\end{vmatrix}0\\begin{vmatrix}.8 \\\\ 3\\end{vmatrix}7 \\mid 6\n$$\n\nWe now count the total number of decimal places in the two factors.\n\nThere is $\\color{blue}1$ decimal place in $4.7$ and there is $\\color{blue}1$ decimal place in $0.8$, so their product will have\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}1} + {\\color{blue}1} = 2\n$$\n\ndecimal places.\n\nWe take our value of $376$ and insert a decimal point to make a number with $2$ decimal places:\n\n$$\n3\\,\\overset{\\color{red}\\downarrow}{\\color{red}\\bbox[2px, lightgray]{.}}\\!\\!\\!\\underbrace{76}_{\\large{ {2} \\text{ digits}}}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n4.7 \\times 0.8 = 3.76 \\,\n$$" "We can use the same method to multiply decimals even when they are greater than $1$. For example, to compute\n\n$$\n4.7 \\times 0.8\n$$\n\nwe first ignore the decimal points and multiply as if both numbers were whole numbers. We ignore any leading ${\\color{red}{0}}$'s during the multiplication:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{array}{cccc} &  & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\begin{aligned} \\\\ {\\color{blue}{5}}\\end{aligned}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} &  \\\\  &  & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}4\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}.7\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} \\\\ \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\times \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} &  & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}{\\color{red}{0\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}}} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}.8\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} \\\\ \\hline  & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}3\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}7\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}6\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\end{array}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now count the total number of decimal places in the two factors.\n\nThere is $\\color{blue}1$ decimal place in $4.7$ and there is $\\color{blue}1$ decimal place in $0.8$, so their product will have\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}1} + {\\color{blue}1} = 2\n$$\n\ndecimal places.\n\nWe take our value of $376$ and insert a decimal point to make a number with $2$ decimal places:\n\n$$\n3\\,\\overset{\\color{red}\\downarrow}{\\color{red}\\bbox[2px, lightgray]{.}}\\!\\!\\!\\underbrace{76}_{\\large{ {2} \\text{ digits}}}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n4.7 \\times 0.8 = 3.76 \\,\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186056065 :tutorial/content "Decimal subtraction works in a similar way to whole number subtraction.\n\nAs an example, let's find the value of $0.8 - 0.5$.\n\nFirst, we write one number above the other, lining up the decimal points and placing a decimal point below the others in our answer:\n\n$$\n0\\begin{vmatrix}. & 8 \\\\ - & 0\\end{vmatrix}. \\mid 5;\n$$\n\nNext, we proceed by subtracting the two numbers just as we would with whole numbers:\n\n$$\n0\\begin{vmatrix}. & 8 \\\\ - & 0\\end{vmatrix}.\\begin{vmatrix}5 \\\\ 0\\end{vmatrix}. \\mid 3\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n$$\n0.8 - 0.5= 0.3\n$$\n\nThis is just like $8-5=3$, but with the decimal point in a different position." "Decimal subtraction works in a similar way to whole number subtraction.\n\nAs an example, let's find the value of $0.8 - 0.5$.\n\nFirst, we write one number above the other, lining up the decimal points and placing a decimal point below the others in our answer:\n\n$$\n0\\begin{vmatrix}. & 8 \\\\ - & 0\\end{vmatrix}. \\mid 5;\n$$\n\nNext, we proceed by subtracting the two numbers just as we would with whole numbers:\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{ccccc} &  & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}0\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}.\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}8\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} \\\\ \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}-\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} &  & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}0\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}.\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}5\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} \\\\ \\hline  &  & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}0\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}.\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}3\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\end{array}\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n$$\n0.8 - 0.5= 0.3\n$$\n\nThis is just like $8-5=3$, but with the decimal point in a different position."] [:db/cas 17592186056069 :tutorial/content "Let's think about how to factor the following trinomial:\n\n$$\nx^2 + 7x + 12\n$$\n\nThis trinomial is neither a perfect square nor a difference of squares. In such cases, the factoring process requires a little trial and error.\n\nWe can factor this trinomial in four steps:\n\n- **Step 1:** Find the factor pairs of the constant term $(12)$:The factor pairs of $12$ are\n$(1, 12), (2, 6), (3, 4)$.\n- **Step 2:** Determine which of these factor pairs add up to the coefficient of the linear term $(7)$:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}1 + 12|= 13 & \\cdot \\\\ 2 + 6|= 8 & \\cdot \\\\ 3 + 4|= 7 & ✓\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n- **Step 3:** Using the factor pair identified in the previous step, write the linear term as $7x=3x+4x$:\n$x^{2} + 7x + 12|=; x^{2} + \\underbrace{3x + 4x}_{7x} + 12$\n- **Step 4:** Factor the first two terms and the last two terms separately and then factor the expression completely:\n$$\n\\displaystyle x^{2} + 3x + 4x + 12|=; (x^{2} + 3x) + (4x + 12)|=; x(x + 3) + 4(x + 3)|=; (x + 4)(x + 3)\n$$\n\nAnd we're done!\n\nBefore we continue, note the following:\n\n- The process carried out in step 4 is sometimes called **factoring by grouping.** This is because we group two sets of terms and factor them individually before factoring the entire expression.\n- We can check that our answer is correct by multiplying out the factors:\n$$\n\\displaystyle (x + 4)(x + 3)|= x(x + 3) + 4(x + 3); = x^{2} + 3x + 4x + 12; = x^{2} + 7x + 12✓\n$$" "Let's think about how to factor the following trinomial:\n\n$$\nx^2 + 7x + 12\n$$\n\nThis trinomial is neither a perfect square nor a difference of squares. In such cases, the factoring process requires a little trial and error.\n\nWe can factor this trinomial in four steps:\n\n- **Step 1:** Find the factor pairs of the constant term $(12)$:The factor pairs of $12$ are\n$(1, 12), (2, 6), (3, 4)$.\n- **Step 2:** Determine which of these factor pairs add up to the coefficient of the linear term $(7)$:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1+12 & =13 & {\\color{red}{\\times }} \\\\ 2+6 & =8 & {\\color{red}{\\times }} \\\\ 3+4 & =7 & {\\color{green}{✓}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- **Step 3:** Using the factor pair identified in the previous step, write the linear term as $7x=3x+4x$:\n$x^{2} + 7x + 12|=; x^{2} + \\underbrace{3x + 4x}_{7x} + 12$\n- **Step 4:** Factor the first two terms and the last two terms separately and then factor the expression completely:\n$$\n\\displaystyle x^{2} + 3x + 4x + 12|=; (x^{2} + 3x) + (4x + 12)|=; x(x + 3) + 4(x + 3)|=; (x + 4)(x + 3)\n$$\n\nAnd we're done!\n\nBefore we continue, note the following:\n\n- The process carried out in step 4 is sometimes called **factoring by grouping.** This is because we group two sets of terms and factor them individually before factoring the entire expression.\n- We can check that our answer is correct by multiplying out the factors:\n$$\n\\displaystyle (x + 4)(x + 3)|= x(x + 3) + 4(x + 3); = x^{2} + 3x + 4x + 12; = x^{2} + 7x + 12✓\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186059533 :question/worked-solution "Note that our vector lies in the $4$th quadrant. Using basic trigonometry, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{x} &= \\mid a\\begin{vmatrix}\\cos θ \\\\ = 12\\cos (-31^{∘}) \\\\ \\approx 12 \\cdot 0.857 \\\\ \\approx 10.3 \\\\ a_{y}|=\\end{vmatrix}a \\mid \\sin θ \\\\\n&= 12\\sin (-31^{∘}) \\\\\n&\\approx 12 \\cdot (-0.515) \\\\\n&\\approx - 6.2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\mathbf{a} = \\langle a_x, a_y \\rangle = \\langle 10.3, -6.2 \\rangle \n\t\t= {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}10.3 \\\\ - 6.2\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$" "Note that our vector lies in the $4$th quadrant. Using basic trigonometry, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{x} & =|\\mathbf{a}|\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  \\\\  & =12\\operatorname{cos}(-{31}^{∘}) \\\\  & \\approx 12\\cdot 0.857 \\\\  & \\approx 10.3 \\\\ {a}_{y} & =|\\mathbf{a}|\\operatorname{sin}\\theta  \\\\  & =12\\operatorname{sin}(-{31}^{∘}) \\\\  & \\approx 12\\cdot (-0.515) \\\\  & \\approx -6.2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\mathbf{a} = \\langle a_x, a_y \\rangle = \\langle 10.3, -6.2 \\rangle \n\t\t= {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}10.3 \\\\ - 6.2\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186059534 :question/worked-solution "Note that our vector lies in the $2$nd quadrant.\n\nFirst, to find the angle that out vector makes with the positive $x$-axis, we add\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\theta = \\dfrac{\\pi}{3} + \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} = \\dfrac{5\\pi}{6}\n$$\n\nUsing our formulas, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{x} &= \\mid a\\begin{vmatrix}\\cos θ \\\\ = 60\\cos \\left(\\frac{5π}{6}\\right) \\\\ = 60 \\cdot \\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right) \\\\ =-30\\sqrt{3} \\\\ a_{y}|=\\end{vmatrix}a \\mid \\sin θ \\\\\n&= 60\\sin \\left(\\frac{5π}{6}\\right) \\\\\n&= 60 \\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\\n&= 30\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf{a} &=\\langle a_x, a_y \\rangle \\\\\n&=\\langle -30\\sqrt{3}, 30 \\rangle \\\\\n&={\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}- 30 \\sqrt{3} \\\\ 30\\end{array} \\right]}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Note that our vector lies in the $2$nd quadrant.\n\nFirst, to find the angle that out vector makes with the positive $x$-axis, we add\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\theta = \\dfrac{\\pi}{3} + \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} = \\dfrac{5\\pi}{6}\n$$\n\nUsing our formulas, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{x} & =|\\mathbf{a}|\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  \\\\  & =60\\operatorname{cos}\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{6}\\right) \\\\  & =60\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-30\\sqrt{3} \\\\ {a}_{y} & =|\\mathbf{a}|\\operatorname{sin}\\theta  \\\\  & =60\\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{6}\\right) \\\\  & =60\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =30\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf{a} &=\\langle a_x, a_y \\rangle \\\\\n&=\\langle -30\\sqrt{3}, 30 \\rangle \\\\\n&={\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}- 30 \\sqrt{3} \\\\ 30\\end{array} \\right]}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186059539 :question/worked-solution "Using the formula for the dot product, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na \\cdot b &= \\begin{vmatrix}a & \\cdot & b\\end{vmatrix}\\cdot \\cos θ \\\\\n&= 45 \\cdot 12 \\cdot \\cos 77^{∘} \\\\\n&\\approx 45 \\cdot 12 \\cdot 0.224951 \\\\\n&\\approx 121.474\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Using the formula for the dot product, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot \\mathbf{b} & =|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{a}\\hspace{0.167em}|\\cdot |\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{b}\\hspace{0.167em}|\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}\\theta  \\\\  & =45\\cdot 12\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}{77}^{∘} \\\\  & \\approx 45\\cdot 12\\cdot 0.224\\hspace{0.167em}951 \\\\  & \\approx 121.474\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186059540 :question/worked-solution "If $\\mathbf{a}$ makes an angle of $127^\\circ$ with the positive $x$-axis and $\\mathbf{b}$ makes an angle of $-23^\\circ$ with the positive $x$-axis, then the angle between the vectors is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nθ &= \\mid 127^{∘} - (-23^{∘})|= 150^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/e1/e16c58888810038f7ef5b5794e74d87fe8af6b273f0d527be94181cb79f5477f.png)\n\nNow, using the formula for the dot product, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na \\cdot b &= \\begin{vmatrix}a & \\cdot & b\\end{vmatrix}\\cdot \\cos θ \\\\\n&= 4 \\cdot 3 \\cdot \\cos 150^{∘} \\\\\n&= 4 \\cdot 3 \\cdot \\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right) \\\\\n&=-6\\sqrt{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" "If $\\mathbf{a}$ makes an angle of $127^\\circ$ with the positive $x$-axis and $\\mathbf{b}$ makes an angle of $-23^\\circ$ with the positive $x$-axis, then the angle between the vectors is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nθ &= \\mid 127^{∘} - (-23^{∘})|= 150^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/e1/e16c58888810038f7ef5b5794e74d87fe8af6b273f0d527be94181cb79f5477f.png)\n\nNow, using the formula for the dot product, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot \\mathbf{b} & =|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{a}\\hspace{0.167em}|\\cdot |\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{b}\\hspace{0.167em}|\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}\\theta  \\\\  & =4\\cdot 3\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}{150}^{∘} \\\\  & =4\\cdot 3\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-6\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186059542 :question/worked-solution "Since the angle between the vectors is\n\n$$\n\\theta = \\dfrac\\pi 2\n$$\n\nthe vectors are perpendicular and thus\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a} \\cdot \\mathbf{b} = 0\n$$\n\nWe can confirm this. Using the formula for the dot product, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na \\cdot b &= \\begin{vmatrix}a & \\cdot & b\\end{vmatrix}\\cdot \\cos θ \\\\\n&= 13\\sqrt{17} \\cdot 146 \\cdot \\cos \\left(\\frac{π}{2}\\right) \\\\\n&= 13\\sqrt{17} \\cdot 146 \\cdot 0 \\\\\n&= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Since the angle between the vectors is\n\n$$\n\\theta = \\dfrac\\pi 2\n$$\n\nthe vectors are perpendicular and thus\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a} \\cdot \\mathbf{b} = 0\n$$\n\nWe can confirm this. Using the formula for the dot product, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot \\mathbf{b} & =|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{a}\\hspace{0.167em}|\\cdot |\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{b}\\hspace{0.167em}|\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}\\theta  \\\\  & =13\\sqrt{17}\\cdot 146\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) \\\\  & =13\\sqrt{17}\\cdot 146\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186060894 :tutorial/content "To understand why the derivative of sine is cosine, we need to use the definition of the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}} {\\textrm{d}x} (\\sin x) =  \\lim_{h\\rightarrow 0}\\dfrac{\\sin(x+h) - \\sin x}{h}\n$$\n\nNow, the so-called **addition formula for sine** states that\n\n$$\n\\sin(x+h) = \\sin(x)\\cos(h)+\\cos (x)  \\sin(h).\n$$\n\nBy using the addition formula for sine, we can rewrite our expression for the derivative as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(h)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(h)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}{h} \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(h)-1)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(h)}{h} \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\frac{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(h)-1)}{h}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(h)}{h}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\underbrace{{lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(h)-1)}{h}}_{0}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\underbrace{{lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(h)}{h}}_{1} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the last step, we used two important results for trigonometric limits:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{h\\rightarrow 0} \\dfrac{1 - \\cos(h)}{h}=0 \\qquad \\text{and} \\qquad \\lim_{h\\rightarrow 0} \\dfrac{\\sin(h)}{h}=1.\n$$\n\nDeriving these results requires separate discussions, which we'll leave to future topics." "To understand why the derivative of sine is cosine, we need to use the definition of the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}} {\\textrm{d}x} (\\sin x) =  \\lim_{h\\rightarrow 0}\\dfrac{\\sin(x+h) - \\sin x}{h}\n$$\n\nNow, the so-called **addition formula for sine** states that\n\n$$\n\\sin(x+h) = \\sin(x)\\cos(h)+\\cos (x)  \\sin(h).\n$$\n\nBy using the addition formula for sine, we can rewrite our expression for the derivative as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) & =\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(h)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(h)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}{h} \\\\  & =\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(h)-1)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(h)}{h} \\\\  & =\\lim_{h\\to 0}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\frac{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(h)-1)}{h}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(h)}{h}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\underbrace{\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\frac{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(h)-1)}{h}}_{0}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\underbrace{\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(h)}{h}}_{1} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the last step, we used two important results for trigonometric limits:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{h\\rightarrow 0} \\dfrac{1 - \\cos(h)}{h}=0 \\qquad \\text{and} \\qquad \\lim_{h\\rightarrow 0} \\dfrac{\\sin(h)}{h}=1.\n$$\n\nDeriving these results requires separate discussions, which we'll leave to future topics."] [:db/cas 17592186060895 :tutorial/content "We can follow the same method for the cosine and use the definition of the derivative to show that $(\\cos x)'=-\\sin x.$ First, we start with the definition of the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}} {\\textrm{d}x} (\\cos x) =  \\lim_{h\\rightarrow 0}\\dfrac{\\cos(x+h) - \\cos x}{h}\n$$\n\nNow, the so-called **addition formula for cosine** states that\n\n$$\n\\cos(x+h) = \\cos(x)\\cos(h)- \\sin(x)\\sin(h).\n$$\n\nUsing the addition formula for cosine, we can rewrite our expression for the derivative as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(h)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(h)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)}{h} \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(h)-1)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(h)}{h} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\underbrace{{lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(h)-1}{h}}_{0}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\underbrace{{lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(h)}{h}}_{1} \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOnce again, in the last step, we used the special limits for sine and cosine." "We can follow the same method for the cosine and use the definition of the derivative to show that $(\\cos x)'=-\\sin x.$ First, we start with the definition of the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}} {\\textrm{d}x} (\\cos x) =  \\lim_{h\\rightarrow 0}\\dfrac{\\cos(x+h) - \\cos x}{h}\n$$\n\nNow, the so-called **addition formula for cosine** states that\n\n$$\n\\cos(x+h) = \\cos(x)\\cos(h)- \\sin(x)\\sin(h).\n$$\n\nUsing the addition formula for cosine, we can rewrite our expression for the derivative as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) & =\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(h)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(h)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)}{h} \\\\  & =\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(h)-1)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(h)}{h} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\underbrace{\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(h)-1}{h}}_{0}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\underbrace{\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(h)}{h}}_{1} \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOnce again, in the last step, we used the special limits for sine and cosine."] [:db/cas 17592186060912 :tutorial/content "Consider the vector\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a}=\\langle 3,2 \\rangle\n$$\n\nshown below.\n\n![](images/22/22a560689c3872d945fdbea036ad5963a4c78f075c33637e116da9b04d49afc0.png)\n\nThe angle ${\\color{blue}\\theta}$ is the angle that the vector $\\mathbf{a}$ makes with the **positive $x$-axis**.\n\nNow, from $\\triangle OXA$, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\tan θ &= \\frac{\\begin{vmatrix}XA &}{\\mid OX\\end{vmatrix}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{a_{y}}{a_{x}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{2}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Consider the vector\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a}=\\langle 3,2 \\rangle\n$$\n\nshown below.\n\n![](images/22/22a560689c3872d945fdbea036ad5963a4c78f075c33637e116da9b04d49afc0.png)\n\nThe angle ${\\color{blue}\\theta}$ is the angle that the vector $\\mathbf{a}$ makes with the **positive $x$-axis**.\n\nNow, from $\\triangle OXA$, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}\\theta  & =\\frac{|XA|}{|OX|} \\\\  & =\\frac{{a}_{y}}{{a}_{x}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186060914 :tutorial/content "If a vector $\\mathbf{a}$ makes the angle $\\theta$ with the positive $x$-axis then\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}a_{x} = & a & \\cos θ \\\\ a_{y} = & a & \\sin θ\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a} = \\langle a_x, a_y \\rangle = \\langle \\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{a} & \\cos\\theta, & \\mathbf{a}\\end{vmatrix} \\sin\\theta \\rangle\n$$" "If a vector $\\mathbf{a}$ makes the angle $\\theta$ with the positive $x$-axis then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {a}_{x}=|\\mathbf{a}|\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  \\\\  & {a}_{y}=|\\mathbf{a}|\\operatorname{sin}\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a} = \\langle a_x, a_y \\rangle = \\langle |\\mathbf{a}| \\cos\\theta, |\\mathbf{a}| \\sin\\theta \\rangle .\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186060917 :tutorial/content "The **dot product** (or **scalar product**) of two vectors $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a} \\cdot \\mathbf{b} = \\begin{vmatrix}\\,\\mathbf{a}\\, & \\cdot & \\,\\mathbf{b}\\,\\end{vmatrix} \\cdot \\cos\\theta\n$$\n\nwhere $\\theta$ is the angle formed between the vectors when their tails are placed at the same point.\n\n![](images/87/87395f5fe6657cec5dd98eed9c3c535a5f6dc9f259d4558c86a4d618c904e5c1.png)\n\nFor example, suppose we know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mid \\mathbf{a} &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mid \\mathbf{b} &= 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\theta = \\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}\n$$\n\nWe can now define the **dot product** (or **scalar product**) of $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na \\cdot b &= \\begin{vmatrix}a & \\cdot & b\\end{vmatrix}\\cdot \\cos θ \\\\\n&= 2 \\cdot 4 \\cdot \\cos \\left(\\frac{2π}{3}\\right) \\\\\n&= 2 \\cdot 4 \\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\\\\n&=-4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The **dot product** (or **scalar product**) of two vectors $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a} \\cdot \\mathbf{b} = |\\,\\mathbf{a}\\,| \\cdot |\\,\\mathbf{b}\\,| \\cdot \\cos\\theta,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\theta$ is the angle formed between the vectors when their tails are placed at the same point.\n\n![](images/87/87395f5fe6657cec5dd98eed9c3c535a5f6dc9f259d4558c86a4d618c904e5c1.png)\n\nFor example, suppose we know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mid \\mathbf{a} &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mid \\mathbf{b} &= 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\theta = \\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}\n$$\n\nWe can now define the **dot product** (or **scalar product**) of $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot \\mathbf{b} & =|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{a}\\hspace{0.167em}|\\cdot |\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{b}\\hspace{0.167em}|\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\cdot 4\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}\\left(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\right) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 4\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186060919 :tutorial/content "Two nonzero vectors $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ are **perpendicular** if the angle between them is\n\n$$\n\\theta=90^\\circ\n$$\n\nWe can denote that two vectors are perpendicular using the symbol $\\perp$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a} \\perp \\mathbf{b}\n$$\n\nIn general, the dot product of two perpendicular vectors is $0$. If $\\mathbf{a} \\perp \\mathbf{b}$, and\n\n$$\n\\theta = 90^\\circ\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na \\cdot b &= \\begin{vmatrix}a & \\cdot & b & \\cdot \\cos 90^{∘} \\\\ = & a & \\cdot & b\\end{vmatrix}\\cdot 0 \\\\\n&= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe converse is also true. If $\\mathbf a$ and $\\mathbf b$ are nonzero vectors, then\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a} \\perp \\mathbf{b} \\quad\\Leftrightarrow\\quad \\mathbf{a} \\cdot \\mathbf{b} = 0\n$$" "Two nonzero vectors $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ are **perpendicular** if the angle between them is\n\n$$\n\\theta=90^\\circ\n$$\n\nWe can denote that two vectors are perpendicular using the symbol $\\perp$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a} \\perp \\mathbf{b}\n$$\n\nIn general, the dot product of two perpendicular vectors is $0$. If $\\mathbf{a} \\perp \\mathbf{b}$, and\n\n$$\n\\theta = 90^\\circ\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot \\mathbf{b} & =|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{a}\\hspace{0.167em}|\\cdot |\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{b}\\hspace{0.167em}|\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}{90}^{∘} \\\\  & =|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{a}\\hspace{0.167em}|\\cdot |\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{b}\\hspace{0.167em}|\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe converse is also true. If $\\mathbf a$ and $\\mathbf b$ are nonzero vectors, then\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a} \\perp \\mathbf{b} \\quad\\Leftrightarrow\\quad \\mathbf{a} \\cdot \\mathbf{b} = 0\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186061199 :answer/value "\\frac{\\begin{vmatrix}p &}{9 \\mid q\\end{vmatrix}}" "\\frac{|p |}{9 | q|}"] [:db/cas 17592186062901 :answer/value "$lim_(x → 3^{+})f(x)$ does not exist" "$\\lim_{x \\to  3^{+}}f(x)$ does not exist"] [:db/cas 17592186062902 :answer/value "$lim_(x → 3)f(x)$ does not exist" "$\\lim_{x \\to  3}f(x)$ does not exist"] [:db/cas 17592186062904 :answer/value "lim_(x → 3)f(x) \\ne f(3)" "\\lim_{x \\to  3}f(x) \\ne f(3)"] [:db/cas 17592186063386 :answer/value "lim_(x → - 1^{-})f(x) \\ne lim_(x → - 1^{+})f(x)" "\\lim_{x \\to  - 1^{-}}f(x) \\ne lim_(x → - 1^{+})f(x)"] [:db/cas 17592186063387 :answer/value "lim_(x → - 1)f(x) \\ne f(-1)" "\\lim_{x \\to  - 1}f(x) \\ne f(-1)"] [:db/cas 17592186063389 :answer/value "$lim_(x → - 1^{+})f(x)$ does not exist" "$\\lim_{x \\to  - 1^{+}}f(x)$ does not exist"] [:db/cas 17592186063911 :question/problem "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_\\limits{x \\to 0} \\dfrac{\\sec x -1}{x}.\n$$" "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x \\to 0} \\dfrac{\\sec x -1}{x}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186063911 :question/worked-solution "Notice that as $x \\to 0$, both numerator and denominator approach $0$.\n\nSo, if we attempt to evaluate the limit directly, we get\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x \\to 0}\\dfrac{\\sec x -1}{x} = \\dfrac{\\sec 0-1}{0} = \\dfrac 00\n$$\n\nwhich is an indeterminate form.\n\nInstead, let's recall the following special limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x \\to 0} \\dfrac{1-\\cos x}{x} = 0\n$$\n\nNow, multiplying both the numerator and denominator by $\\cos x$, we can rewrite the expression and evaluate the limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → 0)\\frac{\\sec x - 1}{x} &= lim_(x → 0)((\\sec x - 1)\\cos x)/(x\\cos x) \\\\\n&= lim_(x → 0)((\\frac{1}{\\cos x} - 1)\\cos x)/(x\\cos x) \\\\\n&= lim_(x → 0)\\frac{1 - \\cos x}{x\\cos x} \\\\\n&= lim_(x → 0)\\frac{1 - \\cos x}{x} \\cdot lim_(x → 0)\\frac{1}{\\cos x} \\\\\n&= 0 \\cdot (1)/(\\cos (0)) \\\\\n&= 0 \\cdot 1 \\\\\n&= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Notice that as $x \\to 0$, both numerator and denominator approach $0$.\n\nSo, if we attempt to evaluate the limit directly, we get\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x \\to 0}\\dfrac{\\sec x -1}{x} = \\dfrac{\\sec 0-1}{0} = \\dfrac 00\n$$\n\nwhich is an indeterminate form.\n\nInstead, let's recall the following special limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x \\to 0} \\dfrac{1-\\cos x}{x} = 0\n$$\n\nNow, multiplying both the numerator and denominator by $\\cos x$, we can rewrite the expression and evaluate the limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\lim_{x \\to  0}\\frac{\\sec x - 1}{x} &= \\lim_{x \\to  0}((\\sec x - 1)\\cos x)/(x\\cos x) \\\\\n&= \\lim_{x \\to  0}((\\frac{1}{\\cos x} - 1)\\cos x)/(x\\cos x) \\\\\n&= \\lim_{x \\to  0}\\frac{1 - \\cos x}{x\\cos x} \\\\\n&= \\lim_{x \\to  0}\\frac{1 - \\cos x}{x} \\cdot lim_(x → 0)\\frac{1}{\\cos x} \\\\\n&= 0 \\cdot (1)/(\\cos (0)) \\\\\n&= 0 \\cdot 1 \\\\\n&= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186063916 :question/worked-solution "To test for continuity of $f(x)$ at a point $x=c$, we need to check three conditions:\n\n- Does $f(c)$ exist?\n\n- Does $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to c}f(x)$ exist?\n\n- Does $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to c}f(x) = f(c)?\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Using the expression for the first branch of the function, we obtain that \n\n$$\nf(1)= 1+1= 2.\n$$\n\nSo, $f(x)$ is defined at $x=1.$ $\\:\\:{\\color{green}\\checkmark}$\n\n- Statement II is false. By calculating the one-sided limits, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}(1+x) \\\\  & =1+1 \\\\  & =2 \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}(1-x) \\\\  & =1-1 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result:\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{x\\to 1^-} f(x) \\neq \\lim\\limits_{x\\to 1^+} f(x)\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit of $f(x)$ at $x=1$ does not exist. $\\:\\:{\\color{red}\\times}$\n\n- Statement III is false since the limit of $f(x)$ at $x=1$ does not exist. $\\:\\:{\\color{red}\\times}$\n\n- Statement IV is false. Since not all statements I-III are true, the function $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=1,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/c1/c1ac7fdbbedfa59db5b49a3554cf1fb62b6d1fcafb346536f76599acdd82dd63.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "To test for continuity of $f(x)$ at a point $x=c$, we need to check three conditions:\n\n- Does $f(c)$ exist?\n\n- Does $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to c}f(x)$ exist?\n\n- Does $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to c}f(x) = f(c)?\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Using the expression for the first branch of the function, we obtain that \n\n$$\nf(1)= 1+1= 2.\n$$\n\nSo, $f(x)$ is defined at $x=1.$ $\\:\\:{\\color{green}\\checkmark}$\n\n- Statement II is false. By calculating the one-sided limits, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{-}}(1+x) \\\\  & =1+1 \\\\  & =2 \\\\ \\lim_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{+}}(1-x) \\\\  & =1-1 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result:\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{x\\to 1^-} f(x) \\neq \\lim\\limits_{x\\to 1^+} f(x)\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit of $f(x)$ at $x=1$ does not exist. $\\:\\:{\\color{red}\\times}$\n\n- Statement III is false since the limit of $f(x)$ at $x=1$ does not exist. $\\:\\:{\\color{red}\\times}$\n\n- Statement IV is false. Since not all statements I-III are true, the function $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=1,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/c1/c1ac7fdbbedfa59db5b49a3554cf1fb62b6d1fcafb346536f76599acdd82dd63.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""] [:db/cas 17592186063917 :question/worked-solution "Let's check the conditions necessary for $f(x)$ to be continuous at $x=2.$\n\n- $f(2) = 6,$ so $f(2)$ exists. ${\\color{green}\\checkmark}$\n\n- We calculate the one-sided limits and find that they coincide:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}(3x+2) \\\\  & =3(2)+2 \\\\  & =8, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}({x}^{3}) \\\\  & ={2}^{3} \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the overall limit exists and $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_\\limits{x\\to 2} f(x)=8.\\, \\, {\\color{green}\\checkmark}\n$$ $\\ \\\\[1ex]$\n\n- The limit of the function is not equal to the function value: $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_\\limits{x\\to 2} f(x)=  8,\n$$ while $f(2) = 6.$ ${\\color{red}\\times}$\n\nTherefore, the function $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=2$ because $\\lim_\\limits{x\\to 2} f(x) \\neq f(2).$" "Let's check the conditions necessary for $f(x)$ to be continuous at $x=2.$\n\n- $f(2) = 6,$ so $f(2)$ exists. ${\\color{green}\\checkmark}$\n\n- We calculate the one-sided limits and find that they coincide:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{-}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{-}}(3x+2) \\\\  & =3(2)+2 \\\\  & =8, \\\\ \\lim_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{+}}({x}^{3}) \\\\  & ={2}^{3} \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the overall limit exists and $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to 2} f(x)=8.\\, \\, {\\color{green}\\checkmark}\n$$ $\\ \\\\[1ex]$\n\n- The limit of the function is not equal to the function value: $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to 2} f(x)=  8,\n$$ while $f(2) = 6.$ ${\\color{red}\\times}$\n\nTherefore, the function $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=2$ because $\\lim_{x\\to 2} f(x) \\neq f(2).$"] [:db/cas 17592186063933 :question/worked-solution "Let's use the definition\n\n$$\nf'(a) = \\lim_{h\\to 0}\\dfrac {f(a+h) - f(a)} {h}\n$$\n\nIn our problem, we have $a=5$, and so by the definition we have\n\n$$\nf^{′}(5) = lim_(h → 0)(f(5 + h) - f(5))/(h)\n$$\n\nApplying this to\n\n$$\nf(x)=3x^2\n$$\n\n, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{′}(5) &= lim_(h → 0)((3(5 + h)^{2} - 3(5)^{2})/(h)) \\\\\n&= lim_(h → 0)\\left(\\frac{75 + 30h + 3h^{2} - 75}{h}\\right) \\\\\n&= lim_(h → 0)\\left(\\frac{30h + 3h^{2}}{h}\\right) \\\\\n&= lim_(h → 0)(30 + 3h) \\\\\n&= 30 + 3(0) \\\\\n&= 30\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let's use the definition\n\n$$\nf'(a) = \\lim_{h\\to 0}\\dfrac {f(a+h) - f(a)} {h}\n$$\n\nIn our problem, we have $a=5$, and so by the definition we have\n\n$$\nf^{′}(5) = \\lim_{h \\to  0}(f(5 + h) - f(5))/(h)\n$$\n\nApplying this to\n\n$$\nf(x)=3x^2\n$$\n\n, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{′}(5) &= \\lim_{h \\to  0}((3(5 + h)^{2} - 3(5)^{2})/(h)) \\\\\n&= \\lim_{h \\to  0}\\left(\\frac{75 + 30h + 3h^{2} - 75}{h}\\right) \\\\\n&= \\lim_{h \\to  0}\\left(\\frac{30h + 3h^{2}}{h}\\right) \\\\\n&= \\lim_{h \\to  0}(30 + 3h) \\\\\n&= 30 + 3(0) \\\\\n&= 30\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186063934 :question/worked-solution "Let\n\n$$\nf(x)=2x-x^2\n$$\n\nThe slope of the tangent line is equal to $f'(2)$.\n\nLet's use the equivalent definition for $f'(a)$, given by\n\n$$\nf'(a) = \\lim_{x\\to a}\\dfrac {f(x) - f(a)} {x-a}\n$$\n\nUsing the above definition with $a=2$, we have\n\n$$\nf'(2) = \\lim_{x\\to 2}\\dfrac {f(x) - f(2)} {x-2}\n$$\n\nApplying this to\n\n$$\nf(x)=2x-x^2\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{′}(2) &= lim_(x → 2)(([2x - x^{2}] - [2(2) - (2)^{2}])/(x - 2)) \\\\\n&= lim_(x → 2)\\left(\\frac{-x^{2} + 2x}{x - 2}\\right) \\\\\n&= lim_(x → 2)((-x(x - 2))/(x - 2)) \\\\\n&= lim_(x → 2)(-x) \\\\\n&=-2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the slope of the tangent line to the curve at $x=2$ is $-2$." "Let\n\n$$\nf(x)=2x-x^2\n$$\n\nThe slope of the tangent line is equal to $f'(2)$.\n\nLet's use the equivalent definition for $f'(a)$, given by\n\n$$\nf'(a) = \\lim_{x\\to a}\\dfrac {f(x) - f(a)} {x-a}\n$$\n\nUsing the above definition with $a=2$, we have\n\n$$\nf'(2) = \\lim_{x\\to 2}\\dfrac {f(x) - f(2)} {x-2}\n$$\n\nApplying this to\n\n$$\nf(x)=2x-x^2\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{′}(2) &= \\lim_{x \\to  2}(([2x - x^{2}] - [2(2) - (2)^{2}])/(x - 2)) \\\\\n&= \\lim_{x \\to  2}\\left(\\frac{-x^{2} + 2x}{x - 2}\\right) \\\\\n&= \\lim_{x \\to  2}((-x(x - 2))/(x - 2)) \\\\\n&= \\lim_{x \\to  2}(-x) \\\\\n&=-2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the slope of the tangent line to the curve at $x=2$ is $-2$."] [:db/cas 17592186063994 :question/worked-solution "First, we line up the decimal points:\n\n$$\n0\\begin{vmatrix}. & 9 & 4 \\\\ + & 8 &.\\end{vmatrix}3 \\mid 2;\n$$\n\nNext, we proceed by adding the two numbers just as we would with whole numbers:\n\n$$\n1; 0\\begin{vmatrix}. & 9 & 4 \\\\ + & 8 &.\\end{vmatrix}3\\begin{vmatrix}2 \\\\ 9\\end{vmatrix}. \\mid 2 \\mid 6\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n0.94 + 8.32 = 9.26\n$$" "First, we line up the decimal points:\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{cccccc} &  & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}0\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}.\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}9\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}4\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} \\\\ \\hspace{-0.167em