}{log}_{3}⁡(x+c) \\\\  & ={log}_{3}⁡(0+c) \\\\  & ={log}_{3}⁡(c).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for $h(x)$ to be continuous at $x=0,$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 0}h(x) & =h(0) \\\\ {log}_{3}⁡(c) & =2 \\\\ c & ={3}^{2} \\\\ c & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting $c=9$ removes the point discontinuity."] [:db/cas 17592186064118 :question/worked-solution "From the definition of $f(x),$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,1}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,1}({x}^{2}+2kx+1) \\\\  & ={1}^{2}+2k(1)+1 \\\\  & =2+2k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for $f(x)$ to be continuous at $x=1,$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 1}f(x) & =f(1) \\\\ 2+2k & =4 \\\\ 2k & =2 \\\\ k & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting $k=1$ removes the point discontinuity." "From the definition of $f(x),$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\,1}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to \\,1}({x}^{2}+2kx+1) \\\\  & ={1}^{2}+2k(1)+1 \\\\  & =2+2k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for $f(x)$ to be continuous at $x=1,$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 1}f(x) & =f(1) \\\\ 2+2k & =4 \\\\ 2k & =2 \\\\ k & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting $k=1$ removes the point discontinuity."] [:db/cas 17592186064142 :question/problem "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_\\limits{x \\to 0} \\dfrac{1-\\cos{3x}}{x}.\n$$" "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x \\to 0} \\dfrac{1-\\cos{3x}}{x}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186064142 :question/worked-solution "Notice that as $x \\to 0$, both numerator and denominator approach $0$.\n\nSo, if we attempt to evaluate the limit directly, we get\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x \\to 0}\\dfrac{1-\\cos{3x}}{x} = \\dfrac{1-\\cos(3\\cdot 0)}{0} = \\dfrac 00\n$$\n\nwhich is an indeterminate form.\n\nInstead, let's recall the following special limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{\\theta \\to 0} \\dfrac{1-\\cos \\theta}{\\theta} = 0\n$$\n\nRewriting the given limit using the algebra of limits and applying our special limit, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → 0)\\frac{1 - \\cos 3x}{x} &= lim_(x → 0)(3 \\cdot \\frac{1 - \\cos 3x}{3 \\cdot x}) \\\\\n&= 3 \\cdot lim_(x → 0)\\frac{1 - \\cos 3x}{3x}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $\\theta=3x$. Then, since $\\theta \\to 0$ as $x \\to 0$, we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x \\to 0} \\dfrac{1-\\cos {\\color{blue}3x}}{\\color{blue}3x} = \\lim_\\limits{\\theta \\to 0} \\dfrac{1-\\cos {\\color{blue}\\theta}}{\\color{blue}\\theta} = 0\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n3\\cdot\\lim_\\limits{x \\to 0} \\dfrac{1-\\cos{\\color{black}3x}}{\\color{black}3x}= 3\\cdot 0 = 0\n$$" "Notice that as $x \\to 0$, both numerator and denominator approach $0$.\n\nSo, if we attempt to evaluate the limit directly, we get\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x \\to 0}\\dfrac{1-\\cos{3x}}{x} = \\dfrac{1-\\cos(3\\cdot 0)}{0} = \\dfrac 00\n$$\n\nwhich is an indeterminate form.\n\nInstead, let's recall the following special limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0} \\dfrac{1-\\cos \\theta}{\\theta} = 0\n$$\n\nRewriting the given limit using the algebra of limits and applying our special limit, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\lim_{x \\to  0}\\frac{1 - \\cos 3x}{x} &= \\lim_{x \\to  0}(3 \\cdot \\frac{1 - \\cos 3x}{3 \\cdot x}) \\\\\n&= 3 \\cdot lim_(x → 0)\\frac{1 - \\cos 3x}{3x}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $\\theta=3x$. Then, since $\\theta \\to 0$ as $x \\to 0$, we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x \\to 0} \\dfrac{1-\\cos {\\color{blue}3x}}{\\color{blue}3x} = \\lim_{\\theta \\to 0} \\dfrac{1-\\cos {\\color{blue}\\theta}}{\\color{blue}\\theta} = 0\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n3\\cdot\\lim_{x \\to 0} \\dfrac{1-\\cos{\\color{black}3x}}{\\color{black}3x}= 3\\cdot 0 = 0\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186064149 :question/problem "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_\\limits{y \\to 0} \\dfrac{\\cos^2 y-1}{y}.\n$$" "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{y \\to 0} \\dfrac{\\cos^2 y-1}{y}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186064149 :question/worked-solution "Notice that as $y \\to 0$, both numerator and denominator approach $0$.\n\nSo, if we attempt to evaluate the limit directly, we get\n\n$$\nlim_(y → 0)\\frac{\\cos^{2} y - 1}{y} = (\\cos^{2} (0) - 1)/(0) = \\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nwhich is an indeterminate form.\n\nInstead, let's recall the following special limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{y \\to 0} \\dfrac{1-\\cos y}{y} = 0\n$$\n\nRewriting the given limit using the algebra of limits and applying our special limit, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(y → 0)\\frac{\\cos^{2} y - 1}{y} &= lim_(y → 0)((\\cos y - 1)(\\cos y + 1))/(y) \\\\\n&= lim_(y → 0)\\frac{\\cos y - 1}{y} \\cdot lim_(y → 0)(\\cos y + 1) \\\\\n&=-lim_(y → 0)\\frac{1 - \\cos y}{y} \\cdot lim_(y → 0)(\\cos y + 1) \\\\\n&=-0 \\cdot (\\cos 0 + 1) \\\\\n&= 0 \\cdot 2 \\\\\n&= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Notice that as $y \\to 0$, both numerator and denominator approach $0$.\n\nSo, if we attempt to evaluate the limit directly, we get\n\n$$\n\\lim_{y \\to  0}\\frac{\\cos^{2} y - 1}{y} = (\\cos^{2} (0) - 1)/(0) = \\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nwhich is an indeterminate form.\n\nInstead, let's recall the following special limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{y \\to 0} \\dfrac{1-\\cos y}{y} = 0\n$$\n\nRewriting the given limit using the algebra of limits and applying our special limit, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\lim_{y \\to  0}\\frac{\\cos^{2} y - 1}{y} &= \\lim_{y \\to  0}((\\cos y - 1)(\\cos y + 1))/(y) \\\\\n&= \\lim_{y \\to  0}\\frac{\\cos y - 1}{y} \\cdot lim_(y → 0)(\\cos y + 1) \\\\\n&=-\\lim_{y \\to  0}\\frac{1 - \\cos y}{y} \\cdot lim_(y → 0)(\\cos y + 1) \\\\\n&=-0 \\cdot (\\cos 0 + 1) \\\\\n&= 0 \\cdot 2 \\\\\n&= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186064512 :question/worked-solution "To test for continuity of $f(x)$ at a point $x=c$, we need to check three conditions:\n\n- Does $f(c)$ exist?\n\n- Does ${lim}_{x\\to c}f(x)$ exist?\n\n- Does ${lim}_{x\\to c}f(x)=f(c)?$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement “$f(x)$ is defined at $x=1$” is true. Using the expression for the second branch of the function, we obtain that \n\n$$\nf(1)=2-(1)=1.\n$$\n\nSo, $f(x)$ is defined at $x=1.$ $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- Statement ${lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x)$ is true. By calculating the one-sided limits, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}(2x-1) \\\\  & =2(1)-1 \\\\  & =1 \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}(2-x) \\\\  & =2-(1) \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x)\n$$\n\n \nTherefore, the limit of $f(x)$ at $x=1$ exists:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 1}f(x)=1\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\n- Statement ${lim}_{x\\to 1}f(x)=f(1)$ is true. Indeed, we have that ${lim}_{x\\to 1}f(x)=1=f(1).$  $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- Statement “$f(x)$ is continuous at $x=1$” is true. Since all statements “$f(x)$ is defined at $x=1$”; ${lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x)$; ${lim}_{x\\to 1}f(x)=f(1)$ are true, according to the continuity test, the function $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"All the statements are true.\"" "To test for continuity of $f(x)$ at a point $x=c$, we need to check three conditions:\n\n- Does $f(c)$ exist?\n\n- Does $\\lim_{x\\to c}f(x)$ exist?\n\n- Does $\\lim_{x\\to c}f(x)=f(c)?$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement “$f(x)$ is defined at $x=1$” is true. Using the expression for the second branch of the function, we obtain that \n\n$$\nf(1)=2-(1)=1.\n$$\n\nSo, $f(x)$ is defined at $x=1.$ $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- Statement $\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x)=\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x)$ is true. By calculating the one-sided limits, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{-}}(2x-1) \\\\  & =2(1)-1 \\\\  & =1 \\\\ \\lim_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{+}}(2-x) \\\\  & =2-(1) \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x)=\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x)\n$$\n\n \nTherefore, the limit of $f(x)$ at $x=1$ exists:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 1}f(x)=1\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\n- Statement $\\lim_{x\\to 1}f(x)=f(1)$ is true. Indeed, we have that $\\lim_{x\\to 1}f(x)=1=f(1).$  $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- Statement “$f(x)$ is continuous at $x=1$” is true. Since all statements “$f(x)$ is defined at $x=1$”; $\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x)=\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x)$; $\\lim_{x\\to 1}f(x)=f(1)$ are true, according to the continuity test, the function $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"All the statements are true.\""] [:db/cas 17592186064513 :question/problem "Consider the following function:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle f ( x ) = \\left\\{  \\begin{array}{ll}2 x + 3 , & x < - 2 \\\\ - 1 , & x = - 2 \\\\ - {x}^{2} + 1 , & x > - 2\\end{array} \\right.\n$$\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding $f(x)$?\n\n1. $f(x)$ is defined at $x =-2$\n2. $lim_(x → (-2))f(x)$ exists\n3. $lim_(x → (-2))f(x) = f(-2)$\n4. $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x =-2$" "Consider the following function:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle f ( x ) = \\left\\{  \\begin{array}{ll}2 x + 3 , & x < - 2 \\\\ - 1 , & x = - 2 \\\\ - {x}^{2} + 1 , & x > - 2\\end{array} \\right.\n$$\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding $f(x)$?\n\n1. $f(x)$ is defined at $x =-2$\n2. $\\lim_{x \\to  (-2)}f(x)$ exists\n3. $\\lim_{x \\to  (-2)}f(x) = f(-2)$\n4. $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x =-2$"] [:db/cas 17592186064753 :question/worked-solution "We're told that $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=-2.$ According to the definition of continuity, this means that ${lim}_{x\\to (-2)}f(x)$ exists and\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (-2)}f(x)=f(-2)=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-2)}({(f(x))}^{2}+4) & =({2}^{2}+4)=8.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We're told that $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=-2.$ According to the definition of continuity, this means that $\\lim_{x\\to (-2)}f(x)$ exists and\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (-2)}f(x)=f(-2)=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (-2)}({(f(x))}^{2}+4) & =({2}^{2}+4)=8.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186064754 :question/problem "If $f(x)$ is continuous at $x = 3$ and $f(3) = 1$, then what is $lim_(x → 3^{+})\\sqrt{x^{2} - f(x)}$?" "If $f(x)$ is continuous at $x = 3$ and $f(3) = 1$, then what is $\\lim_{x \\to  3^{+}}\\sqrt{x^{2} - f(x)}$?"] [:db/cas 17592186064789 :question/problem "Evaluate $lim_(x → 0)(1 - \\cos^{2} x)/(x(1 + \\cos x))$." "Evaluate $\\lim_{x \\to  0}(1 - \\cos^{2} x)/(x(1 + \\cos x))$."] [:db/cas 17592186064790 :question/problem "What is $lim_(x → 0)\\frac{1 - \\cos x}{x\\cos x}$?" "What is $\\lim_{x \\to  0}\\frac{1 - \\cos x}{x\\cos x}$?"] [:db/cas 17592186064791 :question/problem "What is $lim_(x → 0)\\frac{\\cos x - 1 + \\sin^{2} x}{x}$?" "What is $\\lim_{x \\to  0}\\frac{\\cos x - 1 + \\sin^{2} x}{x}$?"] [:db/cas 17592186064792 :question/problem "Calculate $lim_(t → 0)\\frac{1 - \\sec t}{t\\sec t}$." "Calculate $\\lim_{t \\to  0}\\frac{1 - \\sec t}{t\\sec t}$."] [:db/cas 17592186064793 :question/problem "Evaluate $lim_(x → 1)(1 - \\cos (x - 1))/(1 - x)$." "Evaluate $\\lim_{x \\to  1}(1 - \\cos (x - 1))/(1 - x)$."] [:db/cas 17592186064807 :question/worked-solution "Let's check the conditions necessary for $f(x)$ to be continuous at $x=3.$\n\n- $f(3)=0,$ so $f(3)$ exists. $✓$\n\n- We calculate the one-sided limits and find that they coincide:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}{x}^{2} \\\\  & ={3}^{2} \\\\  & =9, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to {3}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {3}^{+}}(2x+3) \\\\  & =2(3)+3 \\\\  & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the overall limit exists and ${lim}_{x\\to 3}f(x)=9.\\,\\,✓$ $\\,\\,\\,$\n\n- The limit of the function is not equal to the function value: ${lim}_{x\\to 3}f(x)=9,$ while $f(3)=0.$ $\\times $\n\nTherefore, the function $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=3$ because ${lim}_{x\\to 3}f(x)\\ne f(3).$" "Let's check the conditions necessary for $f(x)$ to be continuous at $x=3.$\n\n- $f(3)=0,$ so $f(3)$ exists. $✓$\n\n- We calculate the one-sided limits and find that they coincide:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {3}^{-}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to {3}^{-}}{x}^{2} \\\\  & ={3}^{2} \\\\  & =9, \\\\ \\lim_{x\\to {3}^{+}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to {3}^{+}}(2x+3) \\\\  & =2(3)+3 \\\\  & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the overall limit exists and $\\lim_{x\\to 3}f(x)=9.\\,\\,✓$ $\\,\\,\\,$\n\n- The limit of the function is not equal to the function value: $\\lim_{x\\to 3}f(x)=9,$ while $f(3)=0.$ $\\times $\n\nTherefore, the function $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=3$ because $\\lim_{x\\to 3}f(x)\\ne f(3).$"] [:db/cas 17592186064887 :question/problem "What is $lim_(x → 0)\\frac{1 - \\cos^{2} 2x}{3x}$?" "What is $\\lim_{x \\to  0}\\frac{1 - \\cos^{2} 2x}{3x}$?"] [:db/cas 17592186064888 :question/worked-solution "Let's check the conditions necessary for $f(x)$ to be continuous at $x=2.$\n\n- $f(-1)=(-1)^{2}+1=2,$ so $f(-1)$ exists. $✓$\n\n- We calculate the one-sided limits and check if they coincide:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}({x}^{3}+x) \\\\  & =(-1)^{3}+(-1) \\\\  & =-2, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}({x}^{2}+1) \\\\  & =(-1)^{2}+1 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the one-sided limits exist but are not equal. $\\,\\,\\times $ $\\,\\,\\,$\n\nTherefore, the function $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=-1$ because ${lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}f(x)\\ne {lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}f(x).$" "Let's check the conditions necessary for $f(x)$ to be continuous at $x=2.$\n\n- $f(-1)=(-1)^{2}+1=2,$ so $f(-1)$ exists. $✓$\n\n- We calculate the one-sided limits and check if they coincide:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}({x}^{3}+x) \\\\  & =(-1)^{3}+(-1) \\\\  & =-2, \\\\ \\lim_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}({x}^{2}+1) \\\\  & =(-1)^{2}+1 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the one-sided limits exist but are not equal. $\\,\\,\\times $ $\\,\\,\\,$\n\nTherefore, the function $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=-1$ because $\\lim_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}f(x)\\ne {lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}f(x).$"] [:db/cas 17592186065352 :tutorial/content "Consider the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x \\to 0} \\dfrac{1- \\cos x}{x}\n$$\n\nNotice that direct substitution of $x=0$ leads to the indeterminate form\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → 0)\\frac{1 - \\cos x}{x} &= \\frac{1 - \\cos 0}{0} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1 - 1}{0} \\\\\n&= \\frac{0}{0}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, if we plot the graph of\n\n$$\ny=\\dfrac{1-\\cos x}{x}\n$$\n\nwe get the following picture.\n\n![](images/8c/8c3e6395017a2d1206f10debb5e6feb0719556ed4e053c7dfe1fbb0c80bd61aa.png)\n\nWhile\n\n$$\ny = \\dfrac{1 - \\cos{x}}{x}\n$$\n\nis undefined at $x=0$, it does appear from the graph that\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x \\to 0} \\dfrac{1-\\cos x}{x}=0\n$$\n\nThis is indeed the case. Moreover, it's possible to prove this result rigorously. However, we will assume that it is true and use it to calculate other limits." "Consider the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x \\to 0} \\dfrac{1- \\cos x}{x}\n$$\n\nNotice that direct substitution of $x=0$ leads to the indeterminate form\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\lim_{x \\to  0}\\frac{1 - \\cos x}{x} &= \\frac{1 - \\cos 0}{0} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1 - 1}{0} \\\\\n&= \\frac{0}{0}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, if we plot the graph of\n\n$$\ny=\\dfrac{1-\\cos x}{x}\n$$\n\nwe get the following picture.\n\n![](images/8c/8c3e6395017a2d1206f10debb5e6feb0719556ed4e053c7dfe1fbb0c80bd61aa.png)\n\nWhile\n\n$$\ny = \\dfrac{1 - \\cos{x}}{x}\n$$\n\nis undefined at $x=0$, it does appear from the graph that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x \\to 0} \\dfrac{1-\\cos x}{x}=0\n$$\n\nThis is indeed the case. Moreover, it's possible to prove this result rigorously. However, we will assume that it is true and use it to calculate other limits."] [:db/cas 17592186065353 :tutorial/content "We can prove the result\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_\\limits{x \\to 0} \\dfrac{1-\\cos x}{x}=0\n$$\n\nusing the limit\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x \\to 0} \\dfrac{\\sin x}{x}=1\n$$\n\nFirst, we multiply the numerator and the denominator of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1-\\cos x}{x}\n$$\n\nby $1+\\cos x$, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{1 - \\cos x}{x} &= ((1 - \\cos x)(1 + \\cos x))/(x(1 + \\cos x)) \\\\\n&= (1 - \\cos^{2} x)/(x(1 + \\cos x)) \\\\\n&= (\\sin^{2} x)/(x(1 + \\cos x))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now take the limit as $x\\to 0$, and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → 0)\\frac{1 - \\cos x}{x} &= lim_(x → 0)(\\sin^{2} x)/(x(1 + \\cos x)) \\\\\n&= lim_(x → 0)(\\frac{\\sin x}{x} \\cdot \\frac{\\sin x}{1 + \\cos x}) \\\\\n&= (lim_(x → 0)\\frac{\\sin x}{x}) \\cdot (lim_(x → 0)\\frac{\\sin x}{1 + \\cos x}) \\\\\n&= 1 \\cdot \\frac{\\sin 0}{1 + \\cos 0} \\\\\n&= \\frac{0}{1 + 1} \\\\\n&= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We can prove the result\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x \\to 0} \\dfrac{1-\\cos x}{x}=0\n$$\n\nusing the limit\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x \\to 0} \\dfrac{\\sin x}{x}=1\n$$\n\nFirst, we multiply the numerator and the denominator of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1-\\cos x}{x}\n$$\n\nby $1+\\cos x$, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{1 - \\cos x}{x} &= ((1 - \\cos x)(1 + \\cos x))/(x(1 + \\cos x)) \\\\\n&= (1 - \\cos^{2} x)/(x(1 + \\cos x)) \\\\\n&= (\\sin^{2} x)/(x(1 + \\cos x))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now take the limit as $x\\to 0$, and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\lim_{x \\to  0}\\frac{1 - \\cos x}{x} &= \\lim_{x \\to  0}(\\sin^{2} x)/(x(1 + \\cos x)) \\\\\n&= \\lim_{x \\to  0}(\\frac{\\sin x}{x} \\cdot \\frac{\\sin x}{1 + \\cos x}) \\\\\n&= (\\lim_{x \\to  0}\\frac{\\sin x}{x}) \\cdot (\\lim_{x \\to  0}\\frac{\\sin x}{1 + \\cos x}) \\\\\n&= 1 \\cdot \\frac{\\sin 0}{1 + \\cos 0} \\\\\n&= \\frac{0}{1 + 1} \\\\\n&= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186065373 :tutorial/content "We can use our special limit with cosine to evaluate other limits, such as\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x \\to 3} \\dfrac{1-\\cos(x-3)}{2x-6}\n$$\n\nFor this limit, notice that as $x\\to3$, both the numerator and denominator approach $0$. So, if we attempt to evaluate the limit directly, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac00,\n$$\n\nwhich is an indeterminate form.\n\nInstead, we rewrite the limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → 3)(1 - \\cos (x - 3))/(2x - 6) &= lim_(x → 3)(1 - \\cos (x - 3))/(2(x - 3)) \\\\\n&= lim_(x → 3)(\\frac{1}{2} \\cdot (1 - \\cos (x - 3))/(x - 3)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot lim_(x → 3)(1 - \\cos (x - 3))/((x - 3))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, this limit looks very similar to our special limit for cosine. So, if we make the substitution\n\n$$\n\\theta = x-3\n$$\n\nthen, since $\\theta \\to 0$ as $x \\to 3$, we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x \\to 3} \\dfrac{1-\\cos{\\color{blue}(x-3)}}{\\color{blue}(x-3)} = \\lim_\\limits{\\theta \\to 0} \\: \\dfrac{1-\\cos {\\color{blue}\\theta}}{\\color{blue}\\theta} = 0\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → 3)(1 - \\cos (x - 3))/(2x - 6) &= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot lim_(x → 3)(1 - \\cos (x - 3))/((x - 3)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 0 \\\\\n&= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We can use our special limit with cosine to evaluate other limits, such as\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x \\to 3} \\dfrac{1-\\cos(x-3)}{2x-6}\n$$\n\nFor this limit, notice that as $x\\to3$, both the numerator and denominator approach $0$. So, if we attempt to evaluate the limit directly, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac00,\n$$\n\nwhich is an indeterminate form.\n\nInstead, we rewrite the limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\lim_{x \\to  3}(1 - \\cos (x - 3))/(2x - 6) &= \\lim_{x \\to  3}(1 - \\cos (x - 3))/(2(x - 3)) \\\\\n&= \\lim_{x \\to  3}(\\frac{1}{2} \\cdot (1 - \\cos (x - 3))/(x - 3)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot lim_(x → 3)(1 - \\cos (x - 3))/((x - 3))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, this limit looks very similar to our special limit for cosine. So, if we make the substitution\n\n$$\n\\theta = x-3\n$$\n\nthen, since $\\theta \\to 0$ as $x \\to 3$, we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x \\to 3} \\dfrac{1-\\cos{\\color{blue}(x-3)}}{\\color{blue}(x-3)} = \\lim_{\\theta \\to 0} \\: \\dfrac{1-\\cos {\\color{blue}\\theta}}{\\color{blue}\\theta} = 0\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\lim_{x \\to  3}(1 - \\cos (x - 3))/(2x - 6) &= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot lim_(x → 3)(1 - \\cos (x - 3))/((x - 3)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 0 \\\\\n&= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186065378 :tutorial/content "Let's build some more intuition for why velocity is defined as the derivative of position.\n\nIf a car is moving at a constant velocity, and it travels a distance of $\\Delta x=100\\,\\textrm{m}$ in a time interval $\\Delta t=10\\,\\textrm{s},$ then we know that its velocity is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{Δx}{Δt} \\\\  & =\\frac{100}{10} \\\\  & =10\\,\\text{m/s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBut what if the velocity itself changes with time? Perhaps the car accelerates for a couple of seconds in the beginning, thereby increasing its velocity. In that case, the formula above only gives us the average velocity. \n\nHowever, if we take a smaller time interval, we can get more precise information about the velocity at a given time $t.$ To get the instantaneous velocity of the car, we need to take the limit as $\\Delta t\\to 0.$ When we do this, we get the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & ={lim}_{Δt\\to 0}\\frac{Δx}{Δt} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe instantaneous velocity is the number that you read on the speedometer of cars." "Let's build some more intuition for why velocity is defined as the derivative of position.\n\nIf a car is moving at a constant velocity, and it travels a distance of $\\Delta x=100\\,\\textrm{m}$ in a time interval $\\Delta t=10\\,\\textrm{s},$ then we know that its velocity is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{Δx}{Δt} \\\\  & =\\frac{100}{10} \\\\  & =10\\,\\text{m/s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBut what if the velocity itself changes with time? Perhaps the car accelerates for a couple of seconds in the beginning, thereby increasing its velocity. In that case, the formula above only gives us the average velocity. \n\nHowever, if we take a smaller time interval, we can get more precise information about the velocity at a given time $t.$ To get the instantaneous velocity of the car, we need to take the limit as $\\Delta t\\to 0.$ When we do this, we get the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\lim_{Δt\\to 0}\\frac{Δx}{Δt} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe instantaneous velocity is the number that you read on the speedometer of cars."] [:db/cas 17592186065391 :tutorial/content "Consider the function $f(x)$ below.\n$$\nf(x)=\n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{ll}3 x + a , & x \\ne  4 , \\\\ 13 , & x = 4\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\n\nThe function $f(x)$ seems to have a point discontinuity at $x=4.$ However, by selecting a particular value for $a,$ we can remove the point discontinuity and make the function continuous.\n\nWe can find this value of $a$ by forcing the limit of the function to be equal to the value of the function at $x=4\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{x\\to 4} f(x) = f(4)\n$$\n\nRemember that $\\lim\\limits_{x\\to 4} f(x)$ represents the value that $f(x)$ approaches as $x$ gets closer and closer, but not equal, to $4.$ So, when evaluating $\\lim\\limits_{x\\to 4} f(x),$ we need to use the function definition for $x \\neq 4.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 4}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to 4}(3x+a) \\\\  & =3(4)+a \\\\  & =12+a\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt $x=4,$ the function is defined as $f(x)=13.$ So $f(4)=13,$ and we can solve the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 4}f(x) & =f(4) \\\\ 12+a & =13 \\\\ a & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, to make $f(x)$ continuous at $x=4,$ we need to choose $a=1.$" "Consider the function $f(x)$ below.\n$$\nf(x)=\n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{ll}3 x + a , & x \\ne  4 , \\\\ 13 , & x = 4\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\n\nThe function $f(x)$ seems to have a point discontinuity at $x=4.$ However, by selecting a particular value for $a,$ we can remove the point discontinuity and make the function continuous.\n\nWe can find this value of $a$ by forcing the limit of the function to be equal to the value of the function at $x=4\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{x\\to 4} f(x) = f(4)\n$$\n\nRemember that $\\lim\\limits_{x\\to 4} f(x)$ represents the value that $f(x)$ approaches as $x$ gets closer and closer, but not equal, to $4.$ So, when evaluating $\\lim\\limits_{x\\to 4} f(x),$ we need to use the function definition for $x \\neq 4.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 4}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to 4}(3x+a) \\\\  & =3(4)+a \\\\  & =12+a\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt $x=4,$ the function is defined as $f(x)=13.$ So $f(4)=13,$ and we can solve the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 4}f(x) & =f(4) \\\\ 12+a & =13 \\\\ a & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, to make $f(x)$ continuous at $x=4,$ we need to choose $a=1.$"] [:db/cas 17592186065402 :tutorial/content "Remember that to test for continuity of $f(x)$ at a point $x=c$, we need to check three conditions:\n\n1\\. Does $f(c)$ exist?\n\n2\\. Does $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to c}f(x)$ exist?\n\n3\\. Does $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to c}f(x) = f(c)?\n$$\n\nLet's use these three conditions to show that the following function is continuous at $x=1.$\n\n$$\nf(x) =\n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{ll}1 + x , & x \\le  1 \\\\ 3 - x & x > 1\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\n\nWe check all three conditions in turn.\n\n1\\. Using the expression for the first branch of the function, we obtain that \n\n  $$\nf(1)= 1+1= 2.\n$$\n\n  So, $f(x)$ is defined at $x=1.$ $\\:\\:{\\color{green}\\checkmark}$\n\n2\\. By calculating the one-sided limits, we get the following:\n\n  $$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}(1+x) \\\\  & =1+1 \\\\  & =2 \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}(3-x) \\\\  & =3-1 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  As a result:\n\n  $$\n\\lim\\limits_{x\\to 1^-} f(x) = \\lim\\limits_{x\\to 1^+} f(x)\n$$\n\n  Therefore, the limit of $f(x)$ at $x=1$ exists: \n\n  $$\n\\lim\\limits_{x \\to 1} f(x) = 2 \\:\\:{\\color{green}\\checkmark}\n$$\n\n3\\. We have that $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim\\limits_{x \\to 1} f(x) = 2 = f(1).\n$$  $\\:\\: {\\color{green}\\checkmark}$\n\nTherefore, the function $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=1.$ The graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/c5/c55aa89d279d94b7561f6c24ebdb37bafcf46dbc20edf4f93d515aca7ae4cde6.png)" "Remember that to test for continuity of $f(x)$ at a point $x=c$, we need to check three conditions:\n\n1\\. Does $f(c)$ exist?\n\n2\\. Does $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to c}f(x)$ exist?\n\n3\\. Does $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to c}f(x) = f(c)?\n$$\n\nLet's use these three conditions to show that the following function is continuous at $x=1.$\n\n$$\nf(x) =\n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{ll}1 + x , & x \\le  1 \\\\ 3 - x & x > 1\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\n\nWe check all three conditions in turn.\n\n1\\. Using the expression for the first branch of the function, we obtain that \n\n  $$\nf(1)= 1+1= 2.\n$$\n\n  So, $f(x)$ is defined at $x=1.$ $\\:\\:{\\color{green}\\checkmark}$\n\n2\\. By calculating the one-sided limits, we get the following:\n\n  $$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{-}}(1+x) \\\\  & =1+1 \\\\  & =2 \\\\ \\lim_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{+}}(3-x) \\\\  & =3-1 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  As a result:\n\n  $$\n\\lim\\limits_{x\\to 1^-} f(x) = \\lim\\limits_{x\\to 1^+} f(x)\n$$\n\n  Therefore, the limit of $f(x)$ at $x=1$ exists: \n\n  $$\n\\lim\\limits_{x \\to 1} f(x) = 2 \\:\\:{\\color{green}\\checkmark}\n$$\n\n3\\. We have that $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim\\limits_{x \\to 1} f(x) = 2 = f(1).\n$$  $\\:\\: {\\color{green}\\checkmark}$\n\nTherefore, the function $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=1.$ The graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/c5/c55aa89d279d94b7561f6c24ebdb37bafcf46dbc20edf4f93d515aca7ae4cde6.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186065443 :tutorial/content "Adding decimals is no harder than adding whole numbers.\n\nFor example, let's find $0.4 + 2.6$.\n\nFirst, we write one number above the other, lining up the decimal points and placing a decimal point below the others in our answer:\n\n$$\n0\\begin{vmatrix}. & 4 \\\\ + & 2\\end{vmatrix}. \\mid 6;\n$$\n\nNext, we proceed by adding the two numbers just as we would with whole numbers. After each addition, we write the ones place and carry the tens place of the result.\n\n$$\n1; 0\\begin{vmatrix}. & 4 \\\\ + & 2\\end{vmatrix}.\\begin{vmatrix}6 \\\\ 3\\end{vmatrix}. \\mid 0\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n0.4 + 2.6 = 3\n$$" "Adding decimals is no harder than adding whole numbers.\n\nFor example, let's find $0.4 + 2.6$.\n\nFirst, we write one number above the other, lining up the decimal points and placing a decimal point below the others in our answer:\n\n$$\n0\\begin{vmatrix}. & 4 \\\\ + & 2\\end{vmatrix}. \\mid 6;\n$$\n\nNext, we proceed by adding the two numbers just as we would with whole numbers. After each addition, we write the ones place and carry the tens place of the result.\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{ccccc} &  & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}{\\color{blue}{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}}} &  &  \\\\  &  & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}0\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}.\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}4\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} \\\\ \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}+\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} &  & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}2\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}.\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}6\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} \\\\ \\hline  &  & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}3\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}.\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}0\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\end{array}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n0.4 + 2.6 = 3\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186065560 :answer/value "-5\\ln\\begin{vmatrix}x + 2 & + \\frac{2}{x + 2} - 2\\ln & x - 3\\end{vmatrix}+ K" "-5\\ln|x + 2 | + \\frac{2}{x + 2} - 2\\ln | x - 3|+ K"] [:db/cas 17592186065561 :answer/value "2\\ln\\begin{vmatrix}x + 2 & -\\frac{2}{x - 3} + 5\\ln & x - 3\\end{vmatrix}+ K" "2\\ln|x + 2 | -\\frac{2}{x - 3} + 5\\ln | x - 3|+ K"] [:db/cas 17592186065562 :answer/value "-2\\ln\\begin{vmatrix}x - 3 & + \\frac{3}{x - 3} + 2\\ln & x + 2\\end{vmatrix}+ K" "-2\\ln|x - 3 | + \\frac{3}{x - 3} + 2\\ln | x + 2|+ K"] [:db/cas 17592186065563 :answer/value "-2\\ln\\begin{vmatrix}x + 2 & + \\frac{5}{x + 2} + 2\\ln & x - 3\\end{vmatrix}+ K" "-2\\ln|x + 2 | + \\frac{5}{x + 2} + 2\\ln | x - 3|+ K"] [:db/cas 17592186065564 :answer/value "5\\ln\\begin{vmatrix}x + 2 & + \\frac{2}{x - 3} + 2\\ln & x - 3\\end{vmatrix}+ K" "5\\ln|x + 2 | + \\frac{2}{x - 3} + 2\\ln | x - 3|+ K"] [:db/cas 17592186066016 :answer/value "lim_(x → - \\frac{π}{2}^{-})f(x) = \\frac{π}{2}, lim_(x → - \\frac{π}{2}^{+})f(x) = π" "\\lim_{x \\to  - \\frac{π}{2}^{-}}f(x) = \\frac{π}{2}, \\lim_{x \\to  - \\frac{π}{2}^{+}}f(x) = π"] [:db/cas 17592186066017 :answer/value "lim_(x → - \\frac{π}{2}^{-})f(x) = π, lim_(x → - \\frac{π}