+ 9\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements is true?" "Consider the following derivation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} &  & (x+3)^{2}-6x & = \\\\ {\\color{gray}{\\text{Step 1:}}} &  & ({x}^{2}+2\\cdot x\\cdot 3+9)-6x & = \\\\ {\\color{gray}{\\text{Step 2:}}} &  & ({x}^{2}+6x+9)-6x & = \\\\ {\\color{gray}{\\text{Step 3:}}} &  & {x}^{2}+(6x-6x)+9 & = \\\\ {\\color{gray}{\\text{Step 4:}}} &  & {x}^{2}+9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements is true?"] [:db/cas 17592186129456 :question/problem "Consider the following derivation:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle 5x - (x - 5)^{2}|=; Step 1:\\begin{vmatrix}5x - (x^{2} - 2 \\cdot x \\cdot 5 + 25)|= \\\\ Step 2:\\end{vmatrix}5x - (x^{2} + 5x + 25)|=; Step 3:\\begin{vmatrix}-x^{2} + (5x - 5x) - 25|= \\\\ Step 4:\\end{vmatrix}- x^{2} - 25\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements is true?" "Consider the following derivation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} &  & 5x-(x-5)^{2} & = \\\\ {\\color{gray}{\\text{Step 1:}}} &  & 5x-({x}^{2}-2\\cdot x\\cdot 5+25) & = \\\\ {\\color{gray}{\\text{Step 2:}}} &  & 5x-({x}^{2}+5x+25) & = \\\\ {\\color{gray}{\\text{Step 3:}}} &  & -{x}^{2}+(5x-5x)-25 & = \\\\ {\\color{gray}{\\text{Step 4:}}} &  & -{x}^{2}-25\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements is true?"] [:db/cas 17592186129553 :question/problem "Find $lim_(x → 8^{+})(\\sqrt[3]{x} + 1)$." "Find $\\lim_{x \\to  8^{+}}(\\sqrt[3]{x} + 1)$."] [:db/cas 17592186129595 :question/problem "Find $lim_(x → - ∞)\\sqrt{5 + 4x}$." "Find $\\lim_{x \\to  - \\infty}\\sqrt{5 + 4x}$."] [:db/cas 17592186129723 :question/problem "Calculate $lim_(x → 4^{-})\\sqrt{x - 4}$." "Calculate $\\lim_{x \\to  4^{-}}\\sqrt{x - 4}$."] [:db/cas 17592186129788 :question/problem "What is $lim_(x → ∞)\\sqrt{x}$?" "What is $\\lim_{x \\to  \\infty}\\sqrt{x}$?"] [:db/cas 17592186129789 :question/problem "What is $lim_(x → - ∞)\\sqrt[3]{x}$?" "What is $\\lim_{x \\to  - \\infty}\\sqrt[3]{x}$?"] [:db/cas 17592186129790 :question/problem "Calculate $lim_(x → 3)\\sqrt[3]{2x - 6}$." "Calculate $\\lim_{x \\to  3}\\sqrt[3]{2x - 6}$."] [:db/cas 17592186129791 :question/problem "What is $lim_(x → - ∞)\\sqrt[3]{5x + 3}$?" "What is $\\lim_{x \\to  - \\infty}\\sqrt[3]{5x + 3}$?"] [:db/cas 17592186129794 :question/problem "Given the functions $f(x)$, $g(x)$ and $h(x)$ below, which of them has a jump discontinuity at $x =-3$?\n\n$f(x)|= {8 + x,\\begin{vmatrix}x < - 3 \\\\ 2 - x,\\end{vmatrix}x \\ge - 3,; g(x)|= {7 + x,\\begin{vmatrix}x < - 3 \\\\ 3 - x,\\end{vmatrix}x \\ge - 3,; h(x)|= \\ln (x + 3)$." "Given the functions $f(x)$, $g(x)$ and $h(x)$ below, which of them has a jump discontinuity at $x =-3$?\n\n$\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\begin{cases}8+x, & x<-3 \\\\ 2-x, & x\\ge -3,\\end{cases} \\\\ g(x) & =\\begin{cases}7+x, & x<-3 \\\\ 3-x, & x\\ge -3,\\end{cases} \\\\ h(x) & =\\operatorname{ln}(x+3).\\end{aligned}$."] [:db/cas 17592186129795 :question/problem "Given the functions $f(x), g(x)$ and $h(x)$ below, which of them has a point discontinuity at $x = 0$?\n$f(x)|= {e^{x},\\begin{vmatrix}x \\le 0 \\\\ 1,\\end{vmatrix}x > 0; g(x)|= {2x + 3,\\begin{vmatrix}x < 0 \\\\ 1,\\end{vmatrix}x = 0; - 3x + 3,\\begin{vmatrix}x > 0 \\\\ h(x)|= {x^{2} + 4\\begin{vmatrix}x < 0 \\\\ 4,\\end{vmatrix}x = 0; 4(x + 1),\\end{vmatrix}x > 0$" "Given the functions $f(x), g(x)$ and $h(x)$ below, which of them has a point discontinuity at $x = 0$?\n$\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\begin{cases}{e}^{x}, & x\\le 0 \\\\ 1, & x>0\\end{cases} \\\\  \\\\ g(x) & =\\begin{cases}2x+3, & x<0 \\\\ 1, & x=0 \\\\ -3x+3, & x>0\\end{cases} \\\\  \\\\ h(x) & =\\begin{cases}{x}^{2}+4 & x<0 \\\\ 4, & x=0 \\\\ 4(x+1), & x>0\\end{cases}\\end{aligned}$"] [:db/cas 17592186129797 :question/problem "Given the functions $f(x), g(x)$ and $h(x)$ below, which of them has a jump discontinuity at $x = 2$?\n\n$f(x)|= {\\frac{2}{x - 2},\\begin{vmatrix}x < 2 \\\\ x + 1,\\end{vmatrix}x \\ge 2,; g(x)|= {2 - x^{2},\\begin{vmatrix}x \\le 2 \\\\ x + 1,\\end{vmatrix}x > 2,; h(x)|= {x^{2} - 3x,\\begin{vmatrix}x < 2 \\\\ -3,\\end{vmatrix}x \\ge 2$," "Given the functions $f(x), g(x)$ and $h(x)$ below, which of them has a jump discontinuity at $x = 2$?\n\n$\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\begin{cases}\\frac{2}{x-2}, & x<2 \\\\ x+1, & x\\ge 2,\\end{cases} \\\\ g(x) & =\\begin{cases}2-{x}^{2}, & x\\le 2 \\\\ x+1, & x>2,\\end{cases} \\\\ h(x) & =\\begin{cases}{x}^{2}-3x, & x<2 \\\\ -3, & x\\ge 2,\\end{cases}\\end{aligned}$,"] [:db/cas 17592186129818 :question/problem "Calculate $lim_(x → 4)\\sqrt{6x + 1}$." "Calculate $\\lim_{x \\to  4}\\sqrt{6x + 1}$."] [:db/cas 17592186129836 :question/problem "Given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(x) & ={\\begin{aligned}5x-5, & x<1 \\\\ 0, & x=1 \\\\ {x}^{3}-1, & x>1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." "Given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(x) & =\\begin{cases}5x-5, & x<1 \\\\ 0, & x=1 \\\\ {x}^{3}-1, & x>1\\end{cases}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186129836 :question/worked-solution "Let's check our continuity conditions:\n\n- $g(1)=0$ exists $\\,✓$\n\n- The left and right-sided limits are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}g(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}(5x-5)=5(1)-5=0, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}g(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}({x}^{3}-1)={1}^{3}-1=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the left and right-sided limits are equal, we conclude that statement ${lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}g(x)={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}g(x)$ is true and\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 1}g(x)=0.\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\n- The value of the limit is ${lim}_{x\\to 1}g(x)=0,$ and the value of the function is $g(1)=0.$ So, statement ${lim}_{x\\to 1}g(x)=g(1)$ is true and\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 1}g(x)=g(1)\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\nSo, the function is continuous at $x=0,$ and statement “$g(x)$ has a removable discontinuity at $x=1$” is false.\n\nIn conclusion, only statements ${lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}g(x)={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}g(x)$ and ${lim}_{x\\to 1}g(x)=g(1)$ are true." "Let's check our continuity conditions:\n\n- $g(1)=0$ exists $\\,✓$\n\n- The left and right-sided limits are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}g(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}(5x-5)=5(1)-5=0, \\\\ \\lim_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}g(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}({x}^{3}-1)={1}^{3}-1=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the left and right-sided limits are equal, we conclude that statement $\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{+}}g(x)=\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{-}}g(x)$ is true and\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 1}g(x)=0.\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\n- The value of the limit is $\\lim_{x\\to 1}g(x)=0,$ and the value of the function is $g(1)=0.$ So, statement $\\lim_{x\\to 1}g(x)=g(1)$ is true and\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 1}g(x)=g(1)\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\nSo, the function is continuous at $x=0,$ and statement “$g(x)$ has a removable discontinuity at $x=1$” is false.\n\nIn conclusion, only statements $\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{+}}g(x)=\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{-}}g(x)$ and $\\lim_{x\\to 1}g(x)=g(1)$ are true."] [:db/cas 17592186130368 :tutorial/content "We can use the sign table method to solve rational inequalities with zero on one side. For example, the inequality\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x+1}{x+2} > 0\n$$\n\ncan be solved using the sign table method.\n\nSometimes, rational inequalities require some rearrangement before we can apply the sign table method.\n\nAs an example, let's consider the inequality\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x+1}{x+2} > x\n$$\n\nTo solve this inequality, we first subtract $x$ from both sides of the inequality to get zero on one side:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x+1}{x+2} - x > 0\n$$\n\nThen, we put all of the terms on the left-hand side over a common denominator and simplify.\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{x + 1}{x + 2} - x & > 0 \\\\ \\frac{x + 1}{x + 2} - (x(x + 2))/(x + 2) & > 0 \\\\ (x + 1 - x(x + 2))/(x + 2) & > 0 \\\\ \\frac{x + 1 - x^{2} - 2x}{x + 2} & > 0 \\\\ \\frac{1 - x - x^{2}}{x + 2} & > 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this inequality using the sign table method." "We can use the sign table method to solve rational inequalities with zero on one side. For example, the inequality\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x+1}{x+2} > 0\n$$\n\ncan be solved using the sign table method.\n\nSometimes, rational inequalities require some rearrangement before we can apply the sign table method.\n\nAs an example, let's consider the inequality\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x+1}{x+2} > x\n$$\n\nTo solve this inequality, we first subtract $x$ from both sides of the inequality to get zero on one side:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x+1}{x+2} - x > 0\n$$\n\nThen, we put all of the terms on the left-hand side over a common denominator and simplify.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x+1}{x+2}-x & >0 \\\\ \\frac{x+1}{x+2}-\\frac{x(x+2)}{x+2} & >0 \\\\ \\frac{x+1-x(x+2)}{x+2} & >0 \\\\ \\frac{x+1-{x}^{2}-2x}{x+2} & >0 \\\\ \\frac{1-x-{x}^{2}}{x+2} & >0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this inequality using the sign table method."] [:db/cas 17592186130382 :tutorial/content "Earlier, we claimed that function $f(x)$ is differentiable at $x_0$ *if the function admits a unique tangent line* at $x_0.$ Let's now justify this claim.\n\nRecall that a function $f(x)$ is differentiable at $x=x_0$ with derivative $f'(x_0)$ if and only if the following limit exists:\n\n$$\nf'(x_0) = \\lim_{x\\to x_0}\\dfrac {f(x) - f(x_0)} {x-x_0}\n$$\n\nWe can rewrite this limit as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {x}_{0}}\\left(\\frac{f(x)-f({x}_{0})}{x-{x}_{0}}\\right)-{f}^{′}({x}_{0}) & =0 \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to {x}_{0}}(\\frac{f(x)-f({x}_{0})}{x-{x}_{0}}-{f}^{′}({x}_{0})) & =0 \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to {x}_{0}}\\frac{f(x)-f({x}_{0})-(x-{x}_{0}){f}^{′}({x}_{0})}{x-{x}_{0}} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for the above limit to hold true, the numerator must approach zero. So, near $x_0,$ we must have\n\n$$\nf(x) - f(x_0) - (x-x_0)f'(x_0) \\approx 0,\n$$\n\nwhich can be rewritten as\n\n$$\nf(x) - f(x_0) \\approx (x-x_0)f'(x_0).\n$$\n\nThis corresponds to the following equation of the tangent line at $x_0\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\ny -y_0 = (x-x_0)\\dfrac{\\textrm d f}{\\textrm d x}(x_0)\n$$\n\nFrom here, we can say that a function $f(x)$ is differentiable at $x_0$ *if the function admits a unique tangent line* at $x_0.$\n\nWe'll now use a similar idea to define the differentiability of a multivariable function at a point $P$ in terms of tangent planes." "Earlier, we claimed that function $f(x)$ is differentiable at $x_0$ *if the function admits a unique tangent line* at $x_0.$ Let's now justify this claim.\n\nRecall that a function $f(x)$ is differentiable at $x=x_0$ with derivative $f'(x_0)$ if and only if the following limit exists:\n\n$$\nf'(x_0) = \\lim_{x\\to x_0}\\dfrac {f(x) - f(x_0)} {x-x_0}\n$$\n\nWe can rewrite this limit as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {x}_{0}}\\left(\\frac{f(x)-f({x}_{0})}{x-{x}_{0}}\\right)-{f}^{′}({x}_{0}) & =0 \\\\ \\lim_{x\\to {x}_{0}}(\\frac{f(x)-f({x}_{0})}{x-{x}_{0}}-{f}^{′}({x}_{0})) & =0 \\\\ \\lim_{x\\to {x}_{0}}\\frac{f(x)-f({x}_{0})-(x-{x}_{0}){f}^{′}({x}_{0})}{x-{x}_{0}} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for the above limit to hold true, the numerator must approach zero. So, near $x_0,$ we must have\n\n$$\nf(x) - f(x_0) - (x-x_0)f'(x_0) \\approx 0,\n$$\n\nwhich can be rewritten as\n\n$$\nf(x) - f(x_0) \\approx (x-x_0)f'(x_0).\n$$\n\nThis corresponds to the following equation of the tangent line at $x_0\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\ny -y_0 = (x-x_0)\\dfrac{\\textrm d f}{\\textrm d x}(x_0)\n$$\n\nFrom here, we can say that a function $f(x)$ is differentiable at $x_0$ *if the function admits a unique tangent line* at $x_0.$\n\nWe'll now use a similar idea to define the differentiability of a multivariable function at a point $P$ in terms of tangent planes."] [:db/cas 17592186130448 :tutorial/content "Let's compute the distance between the following complex numbers:\n\n$$\nz_{1} = 1 + \\textrm{i}, z_{2} = 2 + 3\\textrm{i}\n$$\n\nComputing the difference, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nz_{1} - z_{2} &= (1 + \\textrm{i}) - (2 + 3\\textrm{i}) \\\\\n&=-1 - 2\\textrm{i}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the distance between the numbers is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{vmatrix}z_{1} - z_{2} & &= & -1 - 2\\textrm{i}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{(-1)^{2} + (-2)^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let's compute the distance between the following complex numbers:\n\n$$\nz_{1} = 1 + \\textrm{i}, z_{2} = 2 + 3\\textrm{i}\n$$\n\nComputing the difference, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nz_{1} - z_{2} &= (1 + \\textrm{i}) - (2 + 3\\textrm{i}) \\\\\n&=-1 - 2\\textrm{i}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the distance between the numbers is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{z}_{1}-{z}_{2}| & =|-1-2\\text{i}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+(-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186133543 :question/worked-solution "Applying a stretch of factor $k$ and invariant $x$-axis to a point means that the $y$-coordinate of the point should be multiplied by $k$.\n\nThe following function represents this transformation:\n\n$$\n(x,y) \\mapsto \\left(x,ky\\right)\n$$\n\nSince the points $P(1,6)$ and $Q(9,8)$ are mapped to the points $P'(1,3)$ and $Q'(9,4)$, respectively, by the stretch, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}(1, 6) & ↦ (1, k \\cdot 6) = (1, 3), \\\\ (9, 8) & ↦ (9, k \\cdot 8) = (9, 4)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(1, 6k) &= (1, 3), \\\\\n(9, 8k) &= (9, 4)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the $y$-coordinates, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}6k = 3 & ⇒k = \\frac{1}{2}, \\\\ 8k = 4 & ⇒k = \\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" "Applying a stretch of factor $k$ and invariant $x$-axis to a point means that the $y$-coordinate of the point should be multiplied by $k$.\n\nThe following function represents this transformation:\n\n$$\n(x,y) \\mapsto \\left(x,ky\\right)\n$$\n\nSince the points $P(1,6)$ and $Q(9,8)$ are mapped to the points $P'(1,3)$ and $Q'(9,4)$, respectively, by the stretch, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(1,6) & ↦(1,k\\cdot 6)=(1,3), \\\\ (9,8) & ↦(9,k\\cdot 8)=(9,4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(1, 6k) &= (1, 3), \\\\\n(9, 8k) &= (9, 4)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the $y$-coordinates, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6k=3 & \\hspace{1em}\\Rightarrow \\hspace{1em}k=\\frac{1}{2}, \\\\ 8k=4 & \\hspace{1em}\\Rightarrow \\hspace{1em}k=\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186134221 :question/problem "Consider the **orthonormal** set $U$ below. By adding a new vector $x = [x_{1}, x_{2}, x_{3}]^{T}$, extend the set so that it forms an **orthonormal** basis of $R^{3}$. What is the value of $\\begin{vmatrix}x_{1} & + & x_{2} & + & x_{3}\\end{vmatrix}$?\n$$\n\\displaystyle U = {\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ 0 \\\\ -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}, \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\end{bmatrix}}\n$$" "Consider the **orthonormal** set $U$ below. By adding a new vector $x = [x_{1}, x_{2}, x_{3}]^{T}$, extend the set so that it forms an **orthonormal** basis of $R^{3}$. What is the value of $|{x}_{1}|+|{x}_{2}|+|{x}_{3}|?$?\n$$\n\\displaystyle U = {\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ 0 \\\\ -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}, \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\end{bmatrix}}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186135094 :tutorial/content "A fair coin is tossed, then a fair die is rolled. What is the probability of the coin landing on heads and the die landing on $5$?\n\nLet's call $H$ the event \"the coin lands on heads,\" and $F$ is the event \"the die lands on $5$\". Notice that $H$ and $F$ are independent events since the outcome of one event doesn't influence the other. Let's use a tree diagram to compute the required probability.\n\nThe probabilities on the first level of the tree correspond to the event $H$:\n\n$$\nP(H) = \\dfrac12, \\qquad P(H') = \\dfrac12\n$$\n\nLet's add these values to our diagram.\n\n![](images/43/431ab148431d5a89d1c60bccebf71310e086f96009c7d39c438445e374f32ef4.png)\n\nNext, we find the probabilities on the second level. Since $H$ and $F$ are independent, these correspond to the probability of $F$ occurring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(F\\begin{vmatrix}H) &= P(F \\mid H^{′}) = P(F) = \\frac{1}{6} \\\\ P(F^{′} \\mid H)|= P(F^{′}\\end{vmatrix}H^{′}) = P(F^{′}) = \\frac{5}{6}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add these to our diagram:\n\n![](images/10/10af4ec660a95ddedbaa706fe2e2b35a37735b5708926d3b4042ef319542e32c.png)\n\nFinally, to compute the event that the coin lands on heads and the die lands on five, we multiply across the branches:\n\n![](images/07/07bb6d137efaa12079c2a68566fcce0876d586d0eaae9da911c68fc1422df172.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP(H\\cap F) = \\dfrac12\\cdot\\dfrac16 = \\dfrac{1}{12}\n$$" "A fair coin is tossed, then a fair die is rolled. What is the probability of the coin landing on heads and the die landing on $5$?\n\nLet's call $H$ the event \"the coin lands on heads,\" and $F$ is the event \"the die lands on $5$\". Notice that $H$ and $F$ are independent events since the outcome of one event doesn't influence the other. Let's use a tree diagram to compute the required probability.\n\nThe probabilities on the first level of the tree correspond to the event $H$:\n\n$$\nP(H) = \\dfrac12, \\qquad P(H') = \\dfrac12\n$$\n\nLet's add these values to our diagram.\n\n![](images/43/431ab148431d5a89d1c60bccebf71310e086f96009c7d39c438445e374f32ef4.png)\n\nNext, we find the probabilities on the second level. Since $H$ and $F$ are independent, these correspond to the probability of $F$ occurring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(F|H) & =P(F|{H}^{'})=P(F)={\\color{blue}{\\frac{1}{6}}} \\\\ P({F}^{'}|H) & =P({F}^{'}|{H}^{'})=P({F}^{'})={\\color{red}{\\frac{5}{6}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add these to our diagram:\n\n![](images/10/10af4ec660a95ddedbaa706fe2e2b35a37735b5708926d3b4042ef319542e32c.png)\n\nFinally, to compute the event that the coin lands on heads and the die lands on five, we multiply across the branches:\n\n![](images/07/07bb6d137efaa12079c2a68566fcce0876d586d0eaae9da911c68fc1422df172.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP(H\\cap F) = \\dfrac12\\cdot\\dfrac16 = \\dfrac{1}{12}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186135189 :tutorial/content "A **multiple** of $\\color{blue}2$ is any number that can be written as\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}2} \\times \\boxed{ {\\phantom{A}}}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\boxed{ {\\phantom{A}}}$ is a whole number.\n\nFor example, let's take a look at the first few entries of the ${\\color{blue}{2}}\\times$ multiplication table:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\cdot 1 &= & 2 \\\\ 2 \\cdot 2|= & 4 \\\\ 2 \\cdot 3|= & 6 \\\\ 2 \\cdot 4|= & 8 \\\\ 2 \\cdot 5|= & 10\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe multiples of $\\color{blue}2$ are given by the following products:\n${\\color{red}2}, \\quad {\\color{red}4}, \\quad {\\color{red}6}, \\quad {\\color{red}8}, \\quad {\\color{red}10}, \\quad \\ldots$\n\nWe can picture all of the multiples of $\\color{blue}2$ using a number line:\n\n![](images/42/4211337d656766e5964015789e7508386aa59e81284268b3a8722e7442bed400.png)" "A **multiple** of $\\color{blue}2$ is any number that can be written as\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}2} \\times \\boxed{ {\\phantom{A}}}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\boxed{ {\\phantom{A}}}$ is a whole number.\n\nFor example, let's take a look at the first few entries of the ${\\color{blue}{2}}\\times$ multiplication table:\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{rcl}{\\color{blue}{2}}\\times 1 & = & {\\color{red}{2}} \\\\ {\\color{blue}{2}}\\times 2 & = & {\\color{red}{4}} \\\\ {\\color{blue}{2}}\\times 3 & = & {\\color{red}{6}} \\\\ {\\color{blue}{2}}\\times 4 & = & {\\color{red}{8}} \\\\ {\\color{blue}{2}}\\times 5 & = & {\\color{red}{10}}\\end{array}\n$$\n\nThe multiples of $\\color{blue}2$ are given by the following products:\n${\\color{red}2}, \\quad {\\color{red}4}, \\quad {\\color{red}6}, \\quad {\\color{red}8}, \\quad {\\color{red}10}, \\quad \\ldots$\n\nWe can picture all of the multiples of $\\color{blue}2$ using a number line:\n\n![](images/42/4211337d656766e5964015789e7508386aa59e81284268b3a8722e7442bed400.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186138092 :question/worked-solution "To estimate the total amount that Michael paid, we need to estimate the product $13 \\times 17$.\n\nWe round both factors to the nearest ten:\n$$\n\\displaystyle 13\\begin{vmatrix}\\cdot & 17 \\\\ ↓ & ↓ \\\\ 10 & \\cdot\\end{vmatrix}20\n$$\nSo, we now need to find the value of $10 \\times 20$.\n\nFirst, we figure out the non-zero parts and the blocks of zeros for both numbers:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}10 & ⟶10 \\\\ 20 & ⟶20\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe can see that there are $2$ zeros in total.\n\nNext, we multiply the non-zero parts:\n\n$$\n\\bbox[2px, lightgray]{1} \\times \\bbox[2px, lightgray]{2} = \\color{red}{2}\n$$\n\nFinally, we join the above with the block of zeros:\n${\\color{red}2}{\\color{blue}00}$\n\nTherefore, Michael paid approximately $\\$200$ for the T-shirts." "To estimate the total amount that Michael paid, we need to estimate the product $13 \\times 17$.\n\nWe round both factors to the nearest ten:\n$$\n\\begin{array}{ccc}13 & \\times  & 17 \\\\ ↓ &  & ↓ \\\\ {\\color{blue}{10}} & \\times  & {\\color{blue}{20}}\\end{array}\n$$\nSo, we now need to find the value of $10 \\times 20$.\n\nFirst, we figure out the non-zero parts and the blocks of zeros for both numbers:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}10 & ⟶10 \\\\ 20 & ⟶20\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe can see that there are $2$ zeros in total.\n\nNext, we multiply the non-zero parts:\n\n$$\n\\bbox[2px, lightgray]{1} \\times \\bbox[2px, lightgray]{2} = \\color{red}{2}\n$$\n\nFinally, we join the above with the block of zeros:\n${\\color{red}2}{\\color{blue}00}$\n\nTherefore, Michael paid approximately $\\$200$ for the T-shirts."] [:db/cas 17592186138093 :question/worked-solution "To estimate the total number of tools produced, we need to estimate the product $694 \\times 38$.\n\nWe round both factors to the nearest ten, as required:\n$$\n\\displaystyle 694\\begin{vmatrix}\\cdot & 38 \\\\ ↓ & ↓ \\\\ 690 & \\cdot\\end{vmatrix}40\n$$\nHence, we need to find the value of $690 \\times 40$.\n\nFirst, we figure out non-zero parts and blocks of zeros for both numbers:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}690 & ⟶690 \\\\ 40 & ⟶40\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe can see that there are $2$ zeros in total.\n\nNext, we multiply the non-zero parts. We can do this using an area model:\n\n![](images/fa/faa4a2b25fbbee9445b34bbbc9054e140bd48a6ffc296f71d37afdbaf4a35837.png)\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n69\\times 4 = 240+36 = {\\color{red}{276}}\n$$\n\nFinally, we join the above with the block of zeros:\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}276}{\\color{blue}00} = 27,600\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tools factory produced approximately $27,600$ tools." "To estimate the total number of tools produced, we need to estimate the product $694 \\times 38$.\n\nWe round both factors to the nearest ten, as required:\n$$\n\\begin{array}{ccc}694 & \\times  & 38 \\\\ ↓ &  & ↓ \\\\ {\\color{blue}{690}} & \\times  & {\\color{blue}{40}}\\end{array}\n$$\nHence, we need to find the value of $690 \\times 40$.\n\nFirst, we figure out non-zero parts and blocks of zeros for both numbers:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}690 & ⟶690 \\\\ 40 & ⟶40\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe can see that there are $2$ zeros in total.\n\nNext, we multiply the non-zero parts. We can do this using an area model:\n\n![](images/fa/faa4a2b25fbbee9445b34bbbc9054e140bd48a6ffc296f71d37afdbaf4a35837.png)\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n69\\times 4 = 240+36 = {\\color{red}{276}}\n$$\n\nFinally, we join the above with the block of zeros:\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}276}{\\color{blue}00} = 27,600\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tools factory produced approximately $27,600$ tools."] [:db/cas 17592186138094 :question/worked-solution "We round the first factor to the nearest ten and the second factor to the nearest hundred:\n$$\n\\displaystyle 23\\begin{vmatrix}\\cdot & 785 \\\\ ↓ & ↓ \\\\ 20 & \\cdot\\end{vmatrix}800\n$$\nHence, we need to find the value of $20 \\times 800$.\n\nFirst, we figure out the non-zero parts and the blocks of zeros for both numbers:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}20 & ⟶20 \\\\ 800 & ⟶800\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe can see that there are $3$ zeros in total.\n\nNext, we multiply the non-zero parts:\n\n$$\n\\bbox[2px, lightgray]{2} \\times \\bbox[2px, lightgray]{8} = \\color{red}{16}\n$$\n\nFinally, we join the above with the block of zeros:\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}16}{\\color{blue}000} = 16,000\n$$\n\nTherefore, $23 \\times 785$ is approximately $16,000$." "We round the first factor to the nearest ten and the second factor to the nearest hundred:\n$$\n\\begin{array}{ccc}23 & \\times  & 785 \\\\ ↓ &  & ↓ \\\\ {\\color{blue}{20}} & \\times  & {\\color{blue}{800}}\\end{array}\n$$\nHence, we need to find the value of $20 \\times 800$.\n\nFirst, we figure out the non-zero parts and the blocks of zeros for both numbers:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}20 & ⟶20 \\\\ 800 & ⟶800\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe can see that there are $3$ zeros in total.\n\nNext, we multiply the non-zero parts:\n\n$$\n\\bbox[2px, lightgray]{2} \\times \\bbox[2px, lightgray]{8} = \\color{red}{16}\n$$\n\nFinally, we join the above with the block of zeros:\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}16}{\\color{blue}000} = 16,000\n$$\n\nTherefore, $23 \\times 785$ is approximately $16,000$."] [:db/cas 17592186138095 :question/worked-solution "We round the first factor to the nearest thousand and the second factor to the nearest ten:\n$$\n\\displaystyle 3, 586\\begin{vmatrix}\\cdot & 43 \\\\ ↓ & ↓ \\\\ 4, 000 & \\cdot\\end{vmatrix}40\n$$\nHence, we need to find the value of $4,000 \\times 40$.\n\nFirst, we figure out the non-zero parts and the blocks of zeros for both numbers:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}4, 000 & ⟶4000 \\\\ 40 & ⟶40\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe can see that there are $4$ zeros in total.\n\nNext, we multiply the non-zero parts:\n\n$$\n\\bbox[2px, lightgray]{4} \\times \\bbox[2px, lightgray]{4} = \\color{red}{16}\n$$\n\nFinally, we join the above with the block of zeros:\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}16}{\\color{blue}0000} = 160,000\n$$\n\nSo, the estimate is $160,000$." "We round the first factor to the nearest thousand and the second factor to the nearest ten:\n$$\n\\begin{array}{ccc}3,586 & \\times  & 43 \\\\ ↓ &  & ↓ \\\\ {\\color{blue}{4,000}} & \\times  & {\\color{blue}{40}}\\end{array}\n$$\nHence, we need to find the value of $4,000 \\times 40$.\n\nFirst, we figure out the non-zero parts and the blocks of zeros for both numbers:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}4, 000 & ⟶4000 \\\\ 40 & ⟶40\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe can see that there are $4$ zeros in total.\n\nNext, we multiply the non-zero parts:\n\n$$\n\\bbox[2px, lightgray]{4} \\times \\bbox[2px, lightgray]{4} = \\color{red}{16}\n$$\n\nFinally, we join the above with the block of zeros:\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}16}{\\color{blue}0000} = 160,000\n$$\n\nSo, the estimate is $160,000$."] [:db/cas 17592186138142 :question/worked-solution "We round both factors to the nearest whole number:\n$$\n\\displaystyle 7.7\\begin{vmatrix}\\cdot & 5.2 \\\\ ↓ & ↓ \\\\ 8 & \\cdot\\end{vmatrix}5\n$$\n\nSo, we need to find the value of $8 \\times 5$. Computing the product, we get:\n\n$$\n8 \\times 5 = 40\n$$\n\nTherefore, $7.7 \\times 5.2$ is approximately $40$." "We round both factors to the nearest whole number:\n$$\n\\begin{array}{ccc}7.7 & \\times  & 5.2 \\\\ ↓ &  & ↓ \\\\ {\\color{blue}{8}} & \\times  & {\\color{blue}{5}}\\end{array}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to find the value of $8 \\times 5$. Computing the product, we get:\n\n$$\n8 \\times 5 = 40\n$$\n\nTherefore, $7.7 \\times 5.2$ is approximately $40$."] [:db/cas 17592186138143 :question/worked-solution "We round both factors to the nearest whole number:\n$$\n\\displaystyle 6.15\\begin{vmatrix}\\cdot & 8.64 \\\\ ↓ & ↓ \\\\ 6 & \\cdot\\end{vmatrix}9\n$$\n\nSo, we need to find the value of $6 \\times 9$. Computing the product, we get:\n\n$$\n6 \\times 9 = 54\n$$\n\nTherefore, $6.15 \\times 8.64$ is approximately $54$." "We round both factors to the nearest whole number:\n$$\n\\begin{array}{ccc}6.15 & \\times  & 8.64 \\\\ ↓ &  & ↓ \\\\ {\\color{blue}{6}} & \\times  & {\\color{blue}{9}}\\end{array}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to find the value of $6 \\times 9$. Computing the product, we get:\n\n$$\n6 \\times 9 = 54\n$$\n\nTherefore, $6.15 \\times 8.64$ is approximately $54$."] [:db/cas 17592186138154 :question/worked-solution "There is $\\color{blue}1$ decimal place in $15.4$ and there are $\\color{blue}2$ decimal places in $20.79$.\n\nIn order to add the two numbers, we need them to have the same number of decimal places. So, we rewrite the first number so that it has $\\color{blue}2$ decimal places:\n\n$$\n15.4=15.4{\\color{red}0}\n$$\n\nNext, we line up the decimal points:\n\n$$\n1\\begin{vmatrix}5 &. & 4 & 0 \\\\ + & 2 & 0 &.\\end{vmatrix}7 \\mid 9;\n$$\n\nFinally, we proceed by adding the two numbers just as we would with whole numbers:\n\n$$\n1; 1\\begin{vmatrix}5 &. & 4 & 0 \\\\ + & 2 & 0 &.\\end{vmatrix}7\\begin{vmatrix}9 \\\\ 3\\end{vmatrix}6\\begin{vmatrix}. & 1\\end{vmatrix}9\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n15.4 + 20.79 = 36.19\n$$" "There is $\\color{blue}1$ decimal place in $15.4$ and there are $\\color{blue}2$ decimal places in $20.79$.\n\nIn order to add the two numbers, we need them to have the same number of decimal places. So, we rewrite the first number so that it has $\\color{blue}2$ decimal places:\n\n$$\n15.4=15.4{\\color{red}0}\n$$\n\nNext, we line up the decimal points:\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{ccccccc} &  & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}1\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}5\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}.\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}4\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}0\\hspace