ace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}6\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\end{array}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we insert the decimal point in the result to have the same number of decimal places as the decimal factor.\n\nThere are $\\color{blue}2$ decimal places in $12.69$, so the product will also have ${\\color{blue}{2}}$ decimal places. We take our value of $5076$ and insert a decimal point to make a number with $\\color{blue}2$ decimal places:\n\n$$\n50\\,\\overset{\\color{red}\\downarrow}{\\color{red}\\bbox[2px, lightgray]{.}}\\!\\!\\!\\underbrace{76}_{\\large\\text{\\color{blue}2 digits}}\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n12.69 \\times 4 = 50.76 \\,\n$$\n\nTherefore, Bart spent $\\$50.76$ to buy the tickets."] [:db/cas 17592186138212 :question/problem "From left to right, what are the missing digits in the following multiplication problem?\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle 4\\begin{vmatrix}7 & 9 \\\\ \\cdot & 6 \\\\ 2 & \\phantom{i}\\end{vmatrix}\\phantom{i} \\mid 4\n$$" "From left to right, what are the missing digits in the following multiplication problem?\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{ccccc} &  & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}4\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}7\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}9\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} \\\\ \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\times \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} &  &  &  & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}6\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} \\\\ \\hline  & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}2\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167