167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}7\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}9\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} \\\\ \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\times \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} &  &  &  & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}6\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} \\\\ \\hline  & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}{\\color{red}{2\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}}} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\enclose{box}{\\text{[math]\\color{red}8[/math]}}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\enclose{box}{\\text{7}}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}4\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\end{array} & \\hspace{2em}\\hspace{2em} & \\begin{aligned}4\\times 6=24\\hspace{1em}\\text{and}\\hspace{1em}{\\color{blue}{4}}+24=28 \\\\ \\text{Carry:}\\hspace{0.222em}{\\color{blue}{-}} \\\\ \\text{Write:}\\hspace{0.222em}{\\color{red}{28}}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing digits, from left-to-right, are $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}8}$ and $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}7}$."] [:db/cas 17592186138237 :question/worked-solution "First, we round both numbers to the nearest whole number:\n$$\n\\displaystyle 15.123\\begin{vmatrix}+ & 8.8 \\\\ ↓ & ↓ \\\\ 15 & +\\end{vmatrix}9\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the sum:\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}15} + {\\color{blue}9} = 24\n$$\n\nTherefore, $15.123 + 8.8$ is about $24$." "First, we round both numbers to the nearest whole number:\n$$\n\\begin{array}{ccc}15.123 & + & 8.8 \\\\ ↓ &  & ↓ \\\\ {\\color{blue}{15}} & + & {\\color{blue}{9}}\\end{array}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the sum:\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}15} + {\\color{blue}9} = 24\n$$\n\nTherefore, $15.123 + 8.8$ is about $24$."] [:db/cas 17592186138238 :question/worked-solution "To estimate the initial length of the wire, we need to estimate the sum $8.15+ 7.75$.\n\nFirst, we round both numbers to the nearest whole number:\n$$\n\\displaystyle 8.15\\begin{vmatrix}+ & 7.75 \\\\ ↓ & ↓ \\\\ 8 & +\\end{vmatrix}8\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the sum:\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}8} + {\\color{blue}8} = 16\n$$\n\nTherefore, the length of the wire was approximately $16$ meters before Joe cut it." "To estimate the initial length of the wire, we need to estimate the sum $8.15+ 7.75$.\n\nFirst, we round both numbers to the nearest whole number:\n$$\n\\begin{array}{ccc}8.15 & + & 7.75 \\\\ ↓ &  & ↓ \\\\ {\\color{blue}{8}} & + & {\\color{blue}{8}}\\end{array}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the sum:\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}8} + {\\color{blue}8} = 16\n$$\n\nTherefore, the length of the wire was approximately $16$ meters before Joe cut it."] [:db/cas 17592186138239 :question/worked-solution "First, we round both numbers to the nearest whole number:\n$$\n\\displaystyle 55.1\\begin{vmatrix}- & 24.76 \\\\ ↓ & ↓ \\\\ 55 & -\\end{vmatrix}25\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the difference:\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}55} - {\\color{blue}25} = 30\n$$\n\nTherefore, $55.1 - 24.76$ is approximately $30$." "First, we round both numbers to the nearest whole number:\n$$\n\\begin{array}{ccc}55.1 & - & 24.76 \\\\ ↓ &  & ↓ \\\\ {\\color{blue}{55}} & - & {\\color{blue}{25}}\\end{array}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the difference:\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}55} - {\\color{blue}25} = 30\n$$\n\nTherefore, $55.1 - 24.76$ is approximately $30$."] [:db/cas 17592186138240 :question/worked-solution "To estimate the total amount of rain that city $B$ received, we need to estimate the difference $14.7 - 5.745$.\n\nFirst, we round both numbers to the nearest whole number:\n$$\n\\displaystyle 14.7\\begin{vmatrix}- & 5.745 \\\\ ↓ & ↓ \\\\ 15 & -\\end{vmatrix}6\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the difference:\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}15} - {\\color{blue}6} = 9\n$$\n\nTherefore, city $B$ received approximately $9$ inches of rainfall during the week." "To estimate the total amount of rain that city $B$ received, we need to estimate the difference $14.7 - 5.745$.\n\nFirst, we round both numbers to the nearest whole number:\n$$\n\\begin{array}{ccc}14.7 & - & 5.745 \\\\ ↓ &  & ↓ \\\\ {\\color{blue}{15}} & - & {\\color{blue}{6}}\\end{array}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the difference:\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}15} - {\\color{blue}6} = 9\n$$\n\nTherefore, city $B$ received approximately $9$ inches of rainfall during the week."] [:db/cas 17592186138263 :question/worked-solution "Here, we need to estimate the value of $997 \\div 19$.\n\nFirst, we round both numbers to the nearest ten:\n$$\n\\displaystyle 997\\begin{vmatrix}\\div & 19 \\\\ ↓ & ↓ \\\\ 1, 000 & \\div\\end{vmatrix}20\n$$\n\nSo we need to find the value of $1,000 \\div 20$, which can be written as the following fraction:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1,000}{20}\n$$\n\nBy dividing the numerator and denominator by $10$, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1,000 \\div 10}{\\, \\, \\, \\, \\,\\, \\, \\, 20 \\div 10} = \\dfrac{100}{2}\n$$\n\nwhich is the same as\n\n$$\n100 \\div 2 = 50\n$$\n\nTherefore, $997 \\div 19$ is about $50$.\n\nSo, it will take about $50$ minutes to fill the pool." "Here, we need to estimate the value of $997 \\div 19$.\n\nFirst, we round both numbers to the nearest ten:\n$$\n\\begin{array}{ccc}997 & ÷ & 19 \\\\ ↓ &  & ↓ \\\\ {\\color{blue}{1,000}} & ÷ & {\\color{blue}{20}}\\end{array}\n$$\n\nSo we need to find the value of $1,000 \\div 20$, which can be written as the following fraction:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1,000}{20}\n$$\n\nBy dividing the numerator and denominator by $10$, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1,000 \\div 10}{\\, \\, \\, \\, \\,\\, \\, \\, 20 \\div 10} = \\dfrac{100}{2}\n$$\n\nwhich is the same as\n\n$$\n100 \\div 2 = 50\n$$\n\nTherefore, $997 \\div 19$ is about $50$.\n\nSo, it will take about $50$ minutes to fill the pool."] [:db/cas 17592186138265 :question/worked-solution "First, we round both numbers to the nearest ten:\n$$\n\\displaystyle 727\\begin{vmatrix}\\div & 22 \\\\ ↓ & ↓ \\\\ 730 & \\div\\end{vmatrix}20\n$$\n\nSo we need to find the value of $730 \\div 20$, which we write as the following fraction:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{730}{20}\n$$\n\nDividing the numerator and denominator by $10$, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{730 \\div 10}{20 \\div 10} = \\dfrac{73}{2}\n$$\n\nwhich is the same as $73 \\div 2$.\n\nComputing the quotient, we obtain:\n\n| $\\,\\,3 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 6 {\\,. \\,} 5$$2$$\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{7 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 3 {\\,. \\,} 0}$$-\\!\\!\\!$$6$$1$$3$$-\\!\\!\\!$$1$$2$$1$$0$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$1$$0$$0$ |\n| --- |\n\nTherefore, $727 \\div 22$ is approximately $36.5 \\,$." "First, we round both numbers to the nearest ten:\n$$\n\\begin{array}{ccc}727 & ÷ & 22 \\\\ ↓ &  & ↓ \\\\ {\\color{blue}{730}} & ÷ & {\\color{blue}{20}}\\end{array}\n$$\n\nSo we need to find the value of $730 \\div 20$, which we write as the following fraction:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{730}{20}\n$$\n\nDividing the numerator and denominator by $10$, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{730 \\div 10}{20 \\div 10} = \\dfrac{73}{2}\n$$\n\nwhich is the same as $73 \\div 2$.\n\nComputing the quotient, we obtain:\n\n| $\\,\\,3 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 6 {\\,. \\,} 5$$2$$\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{7 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 3 {\\,. \\,} 0}$$-\\!\\!\\!$$6$$1$$3$$-\\!\\!\\!$$1$$2$$1$$0$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$1$$0$$0$ |\n| --- |\n\nTherefore, $727 \\div 22$ is approximately $36.5 \\,$."] [:db/cas 17592186138266 :question/worked-solution "We round both numbers to the nearest hundred:\n$$\n\\displaystyle 142, 773\\begin{vmatrix}\\div & 398 \\\\ ↓ & ↓ \\\\ 142, 800 & \\div\\end{vmatrix}400\n$$\n\nSo we need to find the value of $142,800 \\div 400$, which we can write as the following fraction:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{142,800}{400}\n$$\n\nDividing the numerator and denominator by $100$, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{142,800 \\div 100}{400 \\div 100} = \\dfrac{1,428}{4}\n$$\n\nwhich is the same as $1,428 \\div 4$.\n\nComputing the quotient, we obtain:\n\n| $\\,\\, {\\:\\phantom{\\mid} {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 3 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 5 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 7}$$4$$\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{1 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 4 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 2 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 8}$$-\\!\\!\\!$$1$$2$$2$$2$$-\\!\\!\\!$$2$$0$$2$$8$$-\\!\\!\\!$$2$$8$$0$ |\n| --- |\n\nTherefore, $142,773 \\div 398$ is approximately $357$." "We round both numbers to the nearest hundred:\n$$\n\\begin{array}{ccc}142,773 & ÷ & 398 \\\\ ↓ &  & ↓ \\\\ {\\color{blue}{142,800}} & ÷ & {\\color{blue}{400}}\\end{array}\n$$\n\nSo we need to find the value of $142,800 \\div 400$, which we can write as the following fraction:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{142,800}{400}\n$$\n\nDividing the numerator and denominator by $100$, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{142,800 \\div 100}{400 \\div 100} = \\dfrac{1,428}{4}\n$$\n\nwhich is the same as $1,428 \\div 4$.\n\nComputing the quotient, we obtain:\n\n| $\\,\\, {\\:\\phantom{\\mid} {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 3 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 5 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 7}$$4$$\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{1 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 4 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 2 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 8}$$-\\!\\!\\!$$1$$2$$2$$2$$-\\!\\!\\!$$2$$0$$2$$8$$-\\!\\!\\!$$2$$8$$0$ |\n| --- |\n\nTherefore, $142,773 \\div 398$ is approximately $357$."] [:db/cas 17592186138276 :question/worked-solution "First, we round both numbers to the nearest whole number:\n$$\n\\displaystyle 539.5\\begin{vmatrix}\\div & 18.2 \\\\ ↓ & ↓ \\\\ 540 & \\div\\end{vmatrix}18\n$$\nSo, we need to find the value of $540 \\div 18$. Using long division, we get:\n\n| $3$$0$$18$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{5 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 4 {\\;\\phantom{\\mid}} 0}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$5$$4$$0$$0$ |\n| --- |\n\nThat is,\n\n$$\n540 \\div 18 = 30\n$$\n\nTherefore, $539.5 \\div 18.2$ is approximately $30$." "First, we round both numbers to the nearest whole number:\n$$\n\\begin{array}{ccc}539.5 & ÷ & 18.2 \\\\ ↓ &  & ↓ \\\\ {\\color{blue}{540}} & ÷ & {\\color{blue}{18}}\\end{array}\n$$\nSo, we need to find the value of $540 \\div 18$. Using long division, we get:\n\n| $3$$0$$18$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{5 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 4 {\\;\\phantom{\\mid}} 0}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$5$$4$$0$$0$ |\n| --- |\n\nThat is,\n\n$$\n540 \\div 18 = 30\n$$\n\nTherefore, $539.5 \\div 18.2$ is approximately $30$."] [:db/cas 17592186138320 :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the modulus of $z_3{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{vmatrix}z_3 &= & z_1 & \\cdot\\end{vmatrix}z_2|=4 \\cdot 1.5 = 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the diagram, the arguments of $z_1$ and $z_2$ are equal to\n\n$$\n\\theta_1=\\dfrac{3\\pi}{4}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\theta_2=\\dfrac{5\\pi}{4}\n$$\n\nrespectively. So, the argument of $z_3$ equals\n\n$$\n\\theta_3 = \\theta_1 + \\theta_2 = \\dfrac{3\\pi}{4} + \\dfrac{5\\pi}{4} = 2\\pi\n$$\n\nThe diagram below shows the resulting complex number.\n\n![](images/97/977f8d80c5a7bcf7b84c9125b83f63cc9e3cdc3efd1dd33a33c2050b4a4b2878.png)" "First, we calculate the modulus of $z_3{:}$\n\n$$\n|z_3|=|z_1|\\cdot|z_2|=4 \\cdot 1.5 = 6.\n$$\n\nFrom the diagram, the arguments of $z_1$ and $z_2$ are equal to\n\n$$\n\\theta_1=\\dfrac{3\\pi}{4}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\theta_2=\\dfrac{5\\pi}{4}\n$$\n\nrespectively. So, the argument of $z_3$ equals\n\n$$\n\\theta_3 = \\theta_1 + \\theta_2 = \\dfrac{3\\pi}{4} + \\dfrac{5\\pi}{4} = 2\\pi\n$$\n\nThe diagram below shows the resulting complex number.\n\n![](images/97/977f8d80c5a7bcf7b84c9125b83f63cc9e3cdc3efd1dd33a33c2050b4a4b2878.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186139617 :tutorial/content "We can find a good estimate for the sum of two decimals by first rounding each decimal to the nearest whole number.\n\nTo illustrate, let's find an estimate for the value of $16.6+1.3$.\n\nFirst, we round both numbers to the nearest whole number:\n$$\n\\displaystyle 16.6\\begin{vmatrix}+ & 1.3 \\\\ ↓ & ↓ \\\\ 17 & +\\end{vmatrix}1\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the sum:\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}17} + {\\color{blue}1} = 18\n$$\n\nTherefore, $16.6+1.3$ is about $18$.\n\nThe exact answer is $17.9$, so our estimate is pretty close." "We can find a good estimate for the sum of two decimals by first rounding each decimal to the nearest whole number.\n\nTo illustrate, let's find an estimate for the value of $16.6+1.3$.\n\nFirst, we round both numbers to the nearest whole number:\n$$\n\\begin{array}{ccc}16.6 & + & 1.3 \\\\ ↓ &  & ↓ \\\\ {\\color{blue}{17}} & + & {\\color{blue}{1}}\\end{array}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the sum:\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}17} + {\\color{blue}1} = 18\n$$\n\nTherefore, $16.6+1.3$ is about $18$.\n\nThe exact answer is $17.9$, so our estimate is pretty close."] [:db/cas 17592186139618 :tutorial/content "We also use rounding to estimate the difference of two decimals. To illustrate, let's find an estimate of $25.85- 12.32$.\n\nFirst, we round both numbers to the nearest whole number:\n$$\n\\displaystyle 25.85\\begin{vmatrix}- & 12.32 \\\\ ↓ & ↓ \\\\ 26 & -\\end{vmatrix}12\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the difference:\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}26} - {\\color{blue}12} = 14\n$$\n\nTherefore, $25.85-12.32$ is about $14$." "We also use rounding to estimate the difference of two decimals. To illustrate, let's find an estimate of $25.85- 12.32$.\n\nFirst, we round both numbers to the nearest whole number:\n$$\n\\begin{array}{ccc}25.85 & - & 12.32 \\\\ ↓ &  & ↓ \\\\ {\\color{blue}{26}} & - & {\\color{blue}{12}}\\end{array}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the difference:\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}26} - {\\color{blue}12} = 14\n$$\n\nTherefore, $25.85-12.32$ is about $14$."] [:db/cas 17592186139632 :tutorial/content "To estimate the result of dividing two decimals, we can round both decimals before we divide.\n\nFor example, let's estimate the value of $60.1 \\div 15.4$ by rounding each decimal to the nearest whole number.\n\nFirst, we round both decimals to the nearest whole number:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}60.1 & ÷ & 15.4 \\\\ ↓ &  & ↓ \\\\ 60 & ÷ & 15\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to find the value of $60 \\div 15.$ Computing the quotient, we get:\n\n$$\n60 \\div 15 = 4\n$$\n\nTherefore, $60.1 \\div 15.4$ is approximately $4.$" "To estimate the result of dividing two decimals, we can round both decimals before we divide.\n\nFor example, let's estimate the value of $60.1 \\div 15.4$ by rounding each decimal to the nearest whole number.\n\nFirst, we round both decimals to the nearest whole number:\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{ccc}60.1 & ÷ & 15.4 \\\\ ↓ &  & ↓ \\\\ 60 & ÷ & 15\\end{array}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to find the value of $60 \\div 15.$ Computing the quotient, we get:\n\n$$\n60 \\div 15 = 4\n$$\n\nTherefore, $60.1 \\div 15.4$ is approximately $4.$"] [:db/cas 17592186139673 :tutorial/content "When multiplying two numbers, it's often faster and more convenient to estimate the result.\n\nLet's estimate the product $18\\times 63$ by rounding each factor to the nearest ten.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle 18\\begin{vmatrix}\\cdot & 63 \\\\ ↓ & ↓ \\\\ 20 & \\cdot\\end{vmatrix}60\n$$\nWe now need to find the value of $20 \\times 60$, which is much easier!\n\nFirst, we figure out the non-zero parts and the blocks of zeros for both numbers:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}20 & ⟶20 \\\\ 60 & ⟶60\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe can see that there are $2$ zeros in total.\n\nNext, we multiply the non-zero parts:\n\n$$\n\\bbox[2px, lightgray]{2} \\times \\bbox[2px, lightgray]{6} = {\\color{red}{12}}\n$$\n\nFinally, we join the above with the block of zeros:\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}12}{\\color{blue}00} = 1,200\n$$\n\nTherefore, $18 \\times 63$ is approximately $1,200$.\n\nThe actual answer is $1,134$, so our estimate is pretty good!" "When multiplying two numbers, it's often faster and more convenient to estimate the result.\n\nLet's estimate the product $18\\times 63$ by rounding each factor to the nearest ten.\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{ccc}18 & \\times  & 63 \\\\ ↓ &  & ↓ \\\\ {\\color{blue}{20}} & \\times  & {\\color{blue}{60}}\\end{array}\n$$\nWe now need to find the value of $20 \\times 60$, which is much easier!\n\nFirst, we figure out the non-zero parts and the blocks of zeros for both numbers:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}20 & ⟶20 \\\\ 60 & ⟶60\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe can see that there are $2$ zeros in total.\n\nNext, we multiply the non-zero parts:\n\n$$\n\\bbox[2px, lightgray]{2} \\times \\bbox[2px, lightgray]{6} = {\\color{red}{12}}\n$$\n\nFinally, we join the above with the block of zeros:\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}12}{\\color{blue}00} = 1,200\n$$\n\nTherefore, $18 \\times 63$ is approximately $1,200$.\n\nThe actual answer is $1,134$, so our estimate is pretty good!"] [:db/cas 17592186139674 :tutorial/content "In the last few examples, we rounded a three-digit number to the nearest ten, but the resulting multiplication was still a bit tricky. We can make the estimation easier by rounding the factors to different place values.\n\nFor example, let's estimate $684 \\times 38$ by rounding the *first* factor to the nearest *hundred*, and the *second* factor to the nearest *ten*:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle 684\\begin{vmatrix}\\cdot & 38 \\\\ ↓ & ↓ \\\\ 700 & \\cdot\\end{vmatrix}40\n$$\nHence, we need to find the value of $700 \\times 40$.\n\nFirst, we figure out the non-zero parts and the blocks of zeros for both numbers:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}700 & ⟶700 \\\\ 40 & ⟶40\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe can see that there are $3$ zeros in total.\n\nNext, we multiply the non-zero parts:\n\n$$\n\\bbox[2px, lightgray]{7} \\times \\bbox[2px, lightgray]{4} = \\color{red}{28}\n$$\n\nFinally, we join the above with the block of zeros:\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}28}{\\color{blue}000} = 28,000\n$$\n\nTherefore, $684\\times 38$ is approximately $28,000$.\n\nThis estimation is less accurate than if we rounded both numbers to the nearest $10$, but it is much easier to calculate." "In the last few examples, we rounded a three-digit number to the nearest ten, but the resulting multiplication was still a bit tricky. We can make the estimation easier by rounding the factors to different place values.\n\nFor example, let's estimate $684 \\times 38$ by rounding the *first* factor to the nearest *hundred*, and the *second* factor to the nearest *ten*:\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{ccc}684 & \\times  & 38 \\\\ ↓ &  & ↓ \\\\ {\\color{blue}{700}} & \\times  & {\\color{blue}{40}}\\end{array}\n$$\nHence, we need to find the value of $700 \\times 40$.\n\nFirst, we figure out the non-zero parts and the blocks of zeros for both numbers:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}700 & ⟶700 \\\\ 40 & ⟶40\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe can see that there are $3$ zeros in total.\n\nNext, we multiply the non-zero parts:\n\n$$\n\\bbox[2px, lightgray]{7} \\times \\bbox[2px, lightgray]{4} = \\color{red}{28}\n$$\n\nFinally, we join the above with the block of zeros:\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}28}{\\color{blue}000} = 28,000\n$$\n\nTherefore, $684\\times 38$ is approximately $28,000$.\n\nThis estimation is less accurate than if we rounded both numbers to the nearest $10$, but it is much easier to calculate."] [:db/cas 17592186139704 :tutorial/content "We can estimate decimal multiplication by rounding each decimal to the nearest whole number, and then multiplying the resulting numbers.\n\nFor example, to estimate the product $7.3 \\times 4.9$, we first round both factors to the nearest whole number:\n$$\n\\displaystyle 7.3\\begin{vmatrix}\\cdot & 4.9 \\\\ ↓ & ↓ \\\\ 7 & \\cdot\\end{vmatrix}5\n$$\n\nThe estimate is $7 \\times 5$. Computing the product, we get:\n\n$$\n7 \\times 5 = 35\n$$\n\nTherefore, $7.3 \\times 4.9$ is approximately $35$." "We can estimate decimal multiplication by rounding each decimal to the nearest whole number, and then multiplying the resulting numbers.\n\nFor example, to estimate the product $7.3 \\times 4.9$, we first round both factors to the nearest whole number:\n$$\n\\begin{array}{ccc}7.3 & \\times  & 4.9 \\\\ ↓ &  & ↓ \\\\ {\\color{blue}{7}} & \\times  & {\\color{blue}{5}}\\end{array}\n$$\n\nThe estimate is $7 \\times 5$. Computing the product, we get:\n\n$$\n7 \\times 5 = 35\n$$\n\nTherefore, $7.3 \\times 4.9$ is approximately $35$."] [:db/cas 17592186139709 :tutorial/content "Suppose we want to compute the sum of two numbers with different numbers of decimal places, such as\n\n$$\n19+5.6\n$$\n\nHere, there are no decimal places in $19$, but there is $\\color{blue}1$ decimal place in $5.6$.\n\nIn order to add the two numbers, we need the two numbers to have the same number of decimal places. We can achieve this by attaching an extra zero behind the decimal place in the first number, so that it has $\\color{blue}1$ decimal place:\n\n$$\n19\\,\\overset{\\color{red}\\downarrow}{\\color{red}\\bbox[2px, lightgray]{.}}\\!\\!\\underbrace{0}_{\\large{ { {\\color{blue}1}} \\text{ zero}}}\\!\\!\n$$\n\nRemember that whenever we attach an extra zero behind the decimal place, the number remains unchanged. So the number $19.0$ is equivalent to the original number $19$.\n\nNow the addition problem reads\n\n$$\n19.0 + 5.6\n$$\n\nand we can add the decimal numbers using the usual method. First, we line up the decimal points:\n\n$$\n1\\begin{vmatrix}9 &. & 0 \\\\ + & 5 &.\\end{vmatrix}6;\n$$\n\nThen, we proceed by adding the two numbers just as we would with whole numbers:\n\n$$\n1; 1\\begin{vmatrix}9 &. & 0 \\\\ + & 5 &.\\end{vmatrix}6; 2\\begin{vmatrix}4 &.\\end{vmatrix}6\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n19 + 5.6 = 24.6\n$$" "Suppose we want to compute the sum of two numbers with different numbers of decimal places, such as\n\n$$\n19+5.6\n$$\n\nHere, there are no decimal places in $19$, but there is $\\color{blue}1$ decimal place in $5.6$.\n\nIn order to add the two numbers, we need the two numbers to have the same number of decimal places. We can achieve this by attaching an extra zero behind the decimal place in the first number, so that it has $\\color{blue}1$ decimal place:\n\n$$\n19\\,\\overset{\\color{red}\\downarrow}{\\color{red}\\bbox[2px, lightgray]{.}}\\!\\!\\underbrace{0}_{\\large{ { {\\color{blue}1}} \\text{ zero}}}\\!\\!\n$$\n\nRemember that whenever we attach an extra zero behind the decimal place, the number remains unchanged. So the number $19.0$ is equivalent to the original number $19$.\n\nNow the addition problem reads\n\n$$\n19.0 + 5.6\n$$\n\nand we can add the decimal numbers using the usual method. First, we line up the decimal points:\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{cccccc} &  & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}1\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}9\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}.\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}0\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} \\\\ \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}+\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} &  &  & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}5\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}.\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}6\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} \\\\ \\hline  &  &  &  & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}.\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\end{array}\n$$\n\nThen, we proceed by adding the two numbers just as we would with whole numbers:\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{cccccc} &  & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}{\\color{blue}{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}}} &  &  &  \\\\  &  & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}1\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}9\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}.\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}0\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} \\\\ \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}+\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} &  &  & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}5\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}.\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}6\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} \\\\ \\hline  &  & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}2\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}4\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}.\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}6\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\end{array}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n19 + 5.6 = 24.6\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186139711 :tutorial/content "Sometimes, we will need to place more than one zero to the right of a decimal point.\n\nFor instance, to find the value of $78.9 + 45.962$, notice that there is $\\color{blue}1$ decimal place in $78.9$ and there are $\\color{blue}3$ decimal places in $45.962$.\n\nTo add the two numbers, we need them to have the same number of decimal places. So, we rewrite the first number so that it has $\\color{blue}3$ decimal places:\n\n$$\n78.9=78.9{\\color{red}00}\n$$\n\nNext, we line up the decimal points:\n\n$$\n7\\begin{vmatrix}8 &. & 9 & 0 & 0 \\\\ + & 4 & 5 &. & 9\\end{vmatrix}6 \\mid 2;\n$$\n\nFinally, we proceed by adding the two numbers just as we would with whole numbers:\n\n$$\n1\\begin{vmatrix}1 \\\\ 7\\end{vmatrix}8\\begin{vmatrix}. & 9 & 0 & 0 \\\\ + & 4 & 5 &.\\end{vmatrix}9\\begin{vmatrix}6 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}4\\begin{vmatrix}. & 8 & 6\\end{vmatrix}2\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n78.9 + 45.962 = 124.862\n$$" "Sometimes, we will need to place more than one zero to the right of a decimal point.\n\nFor instance, to find the value of $78.9 + 45.962$, notice that there is $\\color{blue}1$ decimal place in $78.9$ and there are $\\color{blue}3$ decimal places in $45.962$.\n\nTo add the two numbers, we need them to have the same number of decimal places. So, we rewrite the first number so that it has $\\color{blue}3$ decimal places:\n\n$$\n78.9=78.9{\\color{red}00}\n$$\n\nNext, we line up the decimal points:\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{cccccccc} &  & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}7\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{