} &  & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}{\\color{blue}{\\begin{aligned}\\end{aligned}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}}} &  \\\\  &  & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}3\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}.\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}5\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} \\\\ \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}+\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} &  & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}5\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}.\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}5\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} \\\\ \\hline  & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}9\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}.\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}0\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\end{array}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n3.68 + 5.42 \\approx 9\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186142588 :question/worked-solution "To estimate the amount of fruit juice that Maria prepared, we have to find an approximation for the sum\n$0.73 + 4.18$.\n\nFirst, we round both numbers to the nearest benchmark:\n$$\n\\displaystyle 0.73\\begin{vmatrix}+ & 4.18 \\\\ ↓ & ↓ \\\\ 0.5 & +\\end{vmatrix}4.0\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the sum:\n\n$$\n0\\begin{vmatrix}. & 5 \\\\ + & 4\\end{vmatrix}.\\begin{vmatrix}0 \\\\ 4\\end{vmatrix}. \\mid 5\n$$\n\nTherefore, Maria prepared approximately $4.5$ liters of fruit juice." "To estimate the amount of fruit juice that Maria prepared, we have to find an approximation for the sum\n$0.73 + 4.18$.\n\nFirst, we round both numbers to the nearest benchmark:\n$$\n\\begin{array}{ccc}0.73 & + & 4.18 \\\\ ↓ &  & ↓ \\\\ {\\color{blue}{0.5}} & + & {\\color{blue}{4.0}}\\end{array}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the sum:\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{cccccc} &  & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}0\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}.\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}5\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} &  \\\\ \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}+\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} &  & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}4\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}.\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}0\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} &  \\\\ \\hline  &  & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}4\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}.\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}5\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\end{array}\n$$\n\nTherefore, Maria prepared approximately $4.5$ liters of fruit juice."] [:db/cas 17592186144028 :tutorial/content "Sometimes when multiplying the ones, the result is two digits long.\n\nIn this case, we write down the digit in the ones place, and we **carry** the digit in the tens place. We then add the carried digit to the result of the multiplication of the tens.\n\nFor example, let's multiply $26$ by $6$.\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rlr}\\displaystyle \\begin{array}{cccc}\\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} & \\displaystyle  \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} 2 \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} & \\displaystyle \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} 6 \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\\\ \\displaystyle \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\times  \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} & \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} 6 \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\\\ \\hline \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} & \\displaystyle \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} & \\displaystyle \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em}\\end{array} & \\displaystyle \\hspace{2em} \\hspace{2em} & \\displaystyle \\hspace{2em} \\phantom{14 \\hspace{1em} \\text{and} \\hspace{1em} {\\color{blue}3} + 14 = 170000}\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nFirst, we multiply the ones. We have\n\n$$\n6\\times6= {\\color{blue}3}{\\color{red}6}\n$$\n\n, so we write the ones place value, $\\color{red}6$, and carry the tens place value, ${\\color{blue}3}$:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rlrl}\\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle \\begin{array}{cccc}\\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\underset{ {\\color{blue}3}}{} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} & \\displaystyle  \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} 2 \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} & \\displaystyle \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\bbox[2px,lightgray]{6} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\\\ \\displaystyle \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\times  \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} & \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\bbox[2px,lightgray]{6} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\\\ \\hline \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} & \\displaystyle \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} {\\color{red}6 \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em}}\\end{array} & \\displaystyle \\hspace{2em} \\hspace{2em} & \\displaystyle \\begin{array}{c}\\displaystyle \\bbox[2px,lightgray]{6} \\times  \\bbox[2px,lightgray]{6} = 36 \\phantom{00000000000000000} \\\\ \\displaystyle \\text{Carry:} \\hspace{0.222em} {\\color{blue}3} \\\\ \\displaystyle \\text{Write:} \\hspace{0.222em} {\\color{red}6}\\end{array}\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nFinally, we multiply the tens. We have\n\n$$\n2\\times 6 = 12\n$$\n\n, to which we add the carry of ${\\color{blue}3}$:\n\n$$\n_(3); 2\\begin{vmatrix}6 \\\\ \\cdot\\end{vmatrix}6; 1\\begin{vmatrix}5 & 6\\end{vmatrix}2 \\cdot 6 = 12and3 + 12 = 15; Carry:-; Write:15\n$$\n\nWhen we reach the tens multiplication, we do not carry anything. Instead, we write down the complete result.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n26 \\times 6 = 156\n$$" "Sometimes when multiplying the ones, the result is two digits long.\n\nIn this case, we write down the digit in the ones place, and we **carry** the digit in the tens place. We then add the carried digit to the result of the multiplication of the tens.\n\nFor example, let's multiply $26$ by $6$.\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rlr}\\displaystyle \\begin{array}{cccc}\\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} & \\displaystyle  \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} 2 \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} & \\displaystyle \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} 6 \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\\\ \\displaystyle \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\times  \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} & \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} 6 \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\\\ \\hline \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} & \\displaystyle \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} & \\displaystyle \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em}\\end{array} & \\displaystyle \\hspace{2em} \\hspace{2em} & \\displaystyle \\hspace{2em} \\phantom{14 \\hspace{1em} \\text{and} \\hspace{1em} {\\color{blue}3} + 14 = 170000}\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nFirst, we multiply the ones. We have\n\n$$\n6\\times6= {\\color{blue}3}{\\color{red}6}\n$$\n\n, so we write the ones place value, $\\color{red}6$, and carry the tens place value, ${\\color{blue}3}$:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rlrl}\\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle \\begin{array}{cccc}\\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\underset{ {\\color{blue}3}}{} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} & \\displaystyle  \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} 2 \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} & \\displaystyle \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\bbox[2px,lightgray]{6} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\\\ \\displaystyle \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\times  \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} & \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\bbox[2px,lightgray]{6} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\\\ \\hline \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} & \\displaystyle \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} {\\color{red}6 \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em}}\\end{array} & \\displaystyle \\hspace{2em} \\hspace{2em} & \\displaystyle \\begin{array}{c}\\displaystyle \\bbox[2px,lightgray]{6} \\times  \\bbox[2px,lightgray]{6} = 36 \\phantom{00000000000000000} \\\\ \\displaystyle \\text{Carry:} \\hspace{0.222em} {\\color{blue}3} \\\\ \\displaystyle \\text{Write:} \\hspace{0.222em} {\\color{red}6}\\end{array}\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nFinally, we multiply the tens. We have\n\n$$\n2\\times 6 = 12\n$$\n\n, to which we add the carry of ${\\color{blue}3}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{array}{cccc} &  & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}{}_{{\\color{blue}{3}}}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} &  \\\\  &  & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}2\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}6\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} \\\\ \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\times \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} &  &  & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}6\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} \\\\ \\hline  & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}{\\color{red}{1\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}}} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}{\\color{red}{5\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}}} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}6\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\end{array} & \\hspace{2em}\\hspace{2em} & \\begin{aligned}2\\times 6=12\\hspace{1em}\\text{and}\\hspace{1em}{\\color{blue}{3}}+12=15 \\\\ \\text{Carry:}\\hspace{0.222em}{\\color{blue}{-}} \\\\ \\text{Write:}\\hspace{0.222em}{\\color{red}{15}}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen we reach the tens multiplication, we do not carry anything. Instead, we write down the complete result.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n26 \\times 6 = 156\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186144030 :tutorial/content "We can extend our understanding of place value to multiply three-digit numbers by one-digit numbers.\n\nAs an example, let's consider the following multiplication problem:\n\n$$\n145 \\times {\\color{red}{4}}\n$$\n\nFirst, let's write the three-digit number $(145)$ in expanded form:\n\n$$\n145 = {\\color{blue}{100}} + {\\color{blue}{40}} + {\\color{blue}{5}}\n$$\n\nWe now multiply all numbers in our expanded form by ${\\color{red}{4}}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n100 \\cdot 4 &= 400 \\\\\n40 \\cdot 4 &= 160 \\\\\n5 \\cdot 4 &= 20\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now add these results:\n\n$$\n4\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ + & 1\\end{vmatrix}6\\begin{vmatrix}0 \\\\ +\\end{vmatrix}2\\begin{vmatrix}0 \\\\ 5\\end{vmatrix}8 \\mid 0\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n145 \\times 4 = 580\n$$" "We can extend our understanding of place value to multiply three-digit numbers by one-digit numbers.\n\nAs an example, let's consider the following multiplication problem:\n\n$$\n145 \\times {\\color{red}{4}}\n$$\n\nFirst, let's write the three-digit number $(145)$ in expanded form:\n\n$$\n145 = {\\color{blue}{100}} + {\\color{blue}{40}} + {\\color{blue}{5}}\n$$\n\nWe now multiply all numbers in our expanded form by ${\\color{red}{4}}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n100 \\cdot 4 &= 400 \\\\\n40 \\cdot 4 &= 160 \\\\\n5 \\cdot 4 &= 20\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now add these results:\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{ccccc} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}4\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}0\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}0\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} \\\\ \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}+\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} &  & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}1\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace