aystyle  & \\displaystyle 1 \\hspace{0.167em} 1 \\enclose{longdiv}{1 \\hspace{0.167em} 1 \\hspace{0.167em} 1 \\hspace{0.167em} 1} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle \\phantom{1 \\hspace{0.167em} 1} \\hspace{0.222em} \\hspace{0.222em} \\underset{―}{1 \\hspace{0.167em} 1} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle \\phantom{1 \\hspace{0.167em} 1} \\hspace{0.222em} \\hspace{0.222em} \\phantom{1 \\hspace{0.167em}} {\\color{red}0}\\end{array}}\n$$\n- Bring down ${\\color{purple}1}$. Notice that $(11)_2$ goes ${\\color{blue}0}$ times into $(1)_2$. So, we write ${\\color{blue}0}$ in the quotient:\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle \\phantom{1 \\hspace{0.167em} 1} \\hspace{0.222em} \\hspace{0.222em} \\phantom{1 \\hspace{0.167em}} 1 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\color{blue}0} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle 1 \\hspace{0.167em} 1 \\enclose{longdiv}{1 \\hspace{0.167em} 1 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\color{purple}1} \\hspace{0.167em} 1} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle \\phantom{1 \\hspace{0.167em} 1} \\hspace{0.222em} \\hspace{0.222em} \\underset{―}{1 \\hspace{0.167em} 1} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle \\phantom{1 \\hspace{0.167em} 1} \\hspace{0.222em} \\hspace{0.222em} \\phantom{1 \\hspace{0.167em}} 0 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\color{purple}1}\\end{array}}\n$$\n- Bring down ${\\color{purple}1}$. Notice that $(11)_2$ goes ${\\color{blue}1}$ times into $(11)_2$. So, we write ${\\color{blue}1}$ in the quotient and $(11)_2 - (11)_2 = ({\\color{red}0})_2{:}$\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle \\phantom{1 \\hspace{0.167em} 1} \\hspace{0.222em} \\hspace{0.222em} \\phantom{1 \\hspace{0.167em}} 1 \\hspace{0.167em} 0 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\color{blue}1} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle 1 \\hspace{0.167em} 1 \\enclose{longdiv}{1 \\hspace{0.167em} 1 \\hspace{0.167em} 1 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\color{purple}1}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle \\phantom{1 \\hspace{0.167em} 1} \\hspace{0.222em} \\hspace{0.222em} \\underset{―}{1 \\hspace{0.167em} 1} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle \\phantom{1 \\hspace{0.167em} 1} \\hspace{0.222em} \\hspace{0.222em} \\phantom{1 \\hspace{0.167em}} 0 \\hspace{0.167em} 1 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\color{purple}1} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle \\phantom{1 \\hspace{0.167em} 1} \\hspace{0.222em} \\hspace{0.222em} \\underset{―}{\\phantom{1 \\hspace{0.222em} 0 \\hspace{0.222em}} 1 \\hspace{0.167em} 1} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle \\phantom{1 \\hspace{0.167em} 1} \\hspace{0.222em} \\hspace{0.222em} \\phantom{1 \\hspace{0.167em} 0 \\hspace{0.167em} 1 \\hspace{0.167em}} \\hspace{0.167em} {\\color{red}0}\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n(1\\,111)_2 \\div (11)_2 = (101)_2\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186180782 :tutorial/content "Suppose we wish to solve the following inequality for the variable $x$:\n\n$$\n5x + 4 < 8k\n$$\n\nWe solve this inequality using the addition and multiplication principles, treating $k$ like any other number.\n\nFirst, we apply the addition principle, subtracting $4$ from both sides of the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}5x + 4 & < 8k \\\\ 5x + 4 - 4 & < 8k - 4 \\\\ 5x & < 8k - 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the multiplication principle, dividing both sides of the inequality by $5{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}5x & < 8k - 4 \\\\ \\frac{5x}{5} & < \\frac{8k - 4}{5} \\\\ x & < \\frac{8k - 4}{5}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to our inequality is\n\n$$\nx < \\dfrac{8k-4}{5}\n$$" "Suppose we wish to solve the following inequality for the variable $x$:\n\n$$\n5x + 4 < 8k\n$$\n\nWe solve this inequality using the addition and multiplication principles, treating $k$ like any other number.\n\nFirst, we apply the addition principle, subtracting $4$ from both sides of the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x+4 & <8k \\\\ 5x+4-4 & <8k-4 \\\\ 5x & <8k-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the multiplication principle, dividing both sides of the inequality by $5{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x & <8k-4 \\\\ \\frac{5x}{5} & <\\frac{8k-4}{5} \\\\ x & <\\frac{8k-4}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to our inequality is\n\n$$\nx < \\dfrac{8k-4}{5}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186183921 :question/problem "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_\\limits{x \\to 0} \\dfrac{\\arccos(x) -\\pi}{x-\\pi}.\n$$" "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x \\to 0} \\dfrac{\\arccos(x) -\\pi}{x-\\pi}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186183921 :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch the graph of $y=\\arccos(x){:}$\n\n![](images/9d/9dcc5a116c2a564547283783a7df7b1cd3a9ab3b76ac24dc2695c4cb367796d5.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\arccos(x)$ is $x \\in [-1,1].$  \n\nNow, substituting $x=0$ directly into the limit, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(x)-\\pi }{x-\\pi }\\right) & =\\frac{\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(0)-\\pi }{0-\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\pi )}{(-\\pi )} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)}{(-\\pi )} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let's sketch the graph of $y=\\arccos(x){:}$\n\n![](images/9d/9dcc5a116c2a564547283783a7df7b1cd3a9ab3b76ac24dc2695c4cb367796d5.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\arccos(x)$ is $x \\in [-1,1].$  \n\nNow, substituting $x=0$ directly into the limit, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(x)-\\pi }{x-\\pi }\\right) & =\\frac{\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(0)-\\pi }{0-\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\pi )}{(-\\pi )} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)}{(-\\pi )} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186183922 :question/problem "What is the value of $\\lim_\\limits{x \\to \\, \\infty} \\arccos(x-1)?$" "What is the value of $\\lim_{x \\to \\, \\infty} \\arccos(x-1)?$"] [:db/cas 17592186183922 :question/worked-solution "First, let's sketch the graph of $y = \\arccos(x-1){:}$\n\n![](images/4c/4ccf7e79e19f49027f615a075e655139e6926b3ba30fe35d63078fd7917d8869.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\arccos(x-1)$ is $x \\in [0,2].$ This means that $\\arccos(x-1)$ is undefined outside this interval.\n\nTherefore, $\\lim_\\limits{x \\to \\, \\infty} \\arccos(x-1)$ is undefined." "First, let's sketch the graph of $y = \\arccos(x-1){:}$\n\n![](images/4c/4ccf7e79e19f49027f615a075e655139e6926b3ba30fe35d63078fd7917d8869.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\arccos(x-1)$ is $x \\in [0,2].$ This means that $\\arccos(x-1)$ is undefined outside this interval.\n\nTherefore, $\\lim_{x \\to \\, \\infty} \\arccos(x-1)$ is undefined."] [:db/cas 17592186183923 :question/problem "Compute the value of $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_\\limits{x \\to \\, \\infty}\\cos\\left( \\dfrac{2}{3}\\arctan \\left(2x+1\\right)\\right).\n$$" "Compute the value of $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x \\to \\, \\infty}\\cos\\left( \\dfrac{2}{3}\\arctan \\left(2x+1\\right)\\right).\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186183923 :question/worked-solution "First, let's sketch the graph of  $y =\\arctan \\left(2x+1\\right){:}$  \n\n![](images/06/06d01e3eb47f69e5172e0ba3c99708a05c79b12a48764fe29c50e94f93b73dd1.png)\n\nThe domain of   $\\arctan \\left(2x+1 \\right)$ is $(-\\infty,\\infty).$   \n\nAs $x$ increases to $\\infty,$ the graph tends to $y={\\color{blue}\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,\\infty }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2x+1)) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, let's sketch the graph of  $y =\\arctan \\left(2x+1\\right){:}$  \n\n![](images/06/06d01e3eb47f69e5172e0ba3c99708a05c79b12a48764fe29c50e94f93b73dd1.png)\n\nThe domain of   $\\arctan \\left(2x+1 \\right)$ is $(-\\infty,\\infty).$   \n\nAs $x$ increases to $\\infty,$ the graph tends to $y={\\color{blue}\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\,\\infty }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2x+1)) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186183932 :question/worked-solution "We round the dividend to the nearest ten:\n\n$$\n448\\begin{vmatrix}\\div & 5 \\\\ ↓ & ↓ \\\\ 450 & \\div\\end{vmatrix}5\n$$\n\nHence, we need to find the value of $450 \\div 5$.\n\nRemember that $450$ is the same as\n\n$$\n450 = 45 \\times 10\n$$\n\nSo, we can solve this division problem using place value strategies as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}450 \\div 5 &= 45 \\cdot 10 \\div 5 \\\\ &= 45 \\div 5 \\cdot 10 \\\\ &= (45 \\div 5) \\cdot 10 \\\\ &= 9 \\cdot 10 \\\\ &= 90\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n448 \\div 5 \\approx 90\n$$" "We round the dividend to the nearest ten:\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{ccc}448 & ÷ & 5 \\\\ ↓ &  & ↓ \\\\ {\\color{blue}{450}} & ÷ & 5\\end{array}\n$$\n\nHence, we need to find the value of $450 \\div 5$.\n\nRemember that $450$ is the same as\n\n$$\n450 = 45 \\times 10\n$$\n\nSo, we can solve this division problem using place value strategies as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}450 \\div 5 &= 45 \\cdot 10 \\div 5 \\\\ &= 45 \\div 5 \\cdot 10 \\\\ &= (45 \\div 5) \\cdot 10 \\\\ &= 9 \\cdot 10 \\\\ &= 90\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n448 \\div 5 \\approx 90\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186183933 :question/worked-solution "We round the dividend to the nearest hundred:\n\n$$\n7, 971\\begin{vmatrix}\\div & 10 \\\\ ↓ & ↓ \\\\ 8, 000 & \\div\\end{vmatrix}10\n$$\n\nHence, we need to find the value of $8,000 \\div 10$.\n\nSince the divisor equals $10$, we solve this simplified division problem as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle 8 , 000 \\div  10 & \\displaystyle  = \\\\ \\displaystyle 8 , 00 \\cancel{0} &  \\\\ \\displaystyle 800 & \\displaystyle \\end{array}}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n7,971 \\div 10 \\approx 800\n$$" "We round the dividend to the nearest hundred:\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{ccc}7,971 & ÷ & 10 \\\\ ↓ &  & ↓ \\\\ {\\color{blue}{8,000}} & ÷ & 10\\end{array}\n$$\n\nHence, we need to find the value of $8,000 \\div 10$.\n\nSince the divisor equals $10$, we solve this simplified division problem as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle 8 , 000 \\div  10 & \\displaystyle  = \\\\ \\displaystyle 8 , 00 \\cancel{0} &  \\\\ \\displaystyle 800 & \\displaystyle \\end{array}}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n7,971 \\div 10 \\approx 800\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186183934 :question/worked-solution "To find the number of bags needed to pack the potatoes, we need to divide $27,825$ by $7$.\n\nFirst, we round the dividend to the nearest thousand:\n\n$$\n27, 825\\begin{vmatrix}\\div & 7 \\\\ ↓ & ↓ \\\\ 28, 000 & \\div\\end{vmatrix}7\n$$\n\nHence, we need to find the value of $28,000 \\div 7$.\n\nRemember that $28,000$ is the same as\n\n$$\n28,000 = 28 \\times 1,000\n$$\n\nSo, we can solve this division problem using place value strategies as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}28, 000 \\div 7 &= 28 \\cdot 1, 000 \\div 7 \\\\ &= 28 \\div 7 \\cdot 1, 000 \\\\ &= (28 \\div 7) \\cdot 1, 000 \\\\ &= 4 \\cdot 1, 000 \\\\ &= 4, 000\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n27,825 \\div 7 \\approx 4,000\n$$\n\nTherefore, the farmer needs about $4,000$ bags." "To find the number of bags needed to pack the potatoes, we need to divide $27,825$ by $7$.\n\nFirst, we round the dividend to the nearest thousand:\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{ccc}27,825 & ÷ & 7 \\\\ ↓ &  & ↓ \\\\ {\\color{blue}{28,000}} & ÷ & 7\\end{array}\n$$\n\nHence, we need to find the value of $28,000 \\div 7$.\n\nRemember that $28,000$ is the same as\n\n$$\n28,000 = 28 \\times 1,000\n$$\n\nSo, we can solve this division problem using place value strategies as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}28, 000 \\div 7 &= 28 \\cdot 1, 000 \\div 7 \\\\ &= 28 \\div 7 \\cdot 1, 000 \\\\ &= (28 \\div 7) \\cdot 1, 000 \\\\ &= 4 \\cdot 1, 000 \\\\ &= 4, 000\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n27,825 \\div 7 \\approx 4,000\n$$\n\nTherefore, the farmer needs about $4,000$ bags."] [:db/cas 17592186183958 :question/worked-solution "To find the roots of the function, we must solve the equation\n\n$$\nf(x) = 0\n$$\n\nIn this case, our equation is\n\n$$\n4 \\mid x-1 \\mid -16 = 0\n$$\n\nFirst, we isolate the absolute value on the left side of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4 \\mid x - 1 \\mid &= 16 \\\\\n\\mid x - 1 \\mid &= 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEither the positive or negative of the expression inside the absolute value must be equal to the right-hand side of the equation. So, we set up the two corresponding equations and solve them independently:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx - 1 &= 4\\begin{vmatrix}- (x - 1)|= 4 \\\\ x|= 5\\end{vmatrix}- x + 1|= 4 \\\\\n- x &= 3 \\\\\nx &= -3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the roots are $x=-3$ and $x=5$." "To find the roots of the function, we must solve the equation\n\n$$\nf(x) = 0\n$$\n\nIn this case, our equation is\n\n$$\n4 \\mid x-1 \\mid -16 = 0\n$$\n\nFirst, we isolate the absolute value on the left side of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4 \\mid x - 1 \\mid &= 16 \\\\\n\\mid x - 1 \\mid &= 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEither the positive or negative of the expression inside the absolute value must be equal to the right-hand side of the equation. So, we set up the two corresponding equations and solve them independently:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-1 & =4 & \\hspace{2em} & \\hspace{2em} & -(x-1) & =4 \\\\ x & =5 &  &  & -x+1 & =4 \\\\  &  &  &  & -x & =3 \\\\  &  &  &  & x & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the roots are $x=-3$ and $x=5$."] [:db/cas 17592186183959 :question/worked-solution "To find the roots of the function, we must solve the equation\n\n$$\nf(x) = 0\n$$\n\nIn this case, our equation is\n\n$$\n-3 \\mid x+2 \\mid +9 = 0\n$$\n\nFirst, we isolate the absolute value on the left side of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-3 \\mid x + 2 \\mid &= -9 \\\\\n\\mid x + 2 \\mid &= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEither the positive or negative of the expression inside the absolute value must be equal to the right-hand side of the equation. So, we set up the two corresponding equations and solve them independently:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx + 2 &= 3\\begin{vmatrix}- (x + 2)|= 3 \\\\ x|= 1\\end{vmatrix}- x - 2|= 3 \\\\\n- x &= 5 \\\\\nx &= -5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the roots are $x=-5$ and $x=1$." "To find the roots of the function, we must solve the equation\n\n$$\nf(x) = 0\n$$\n\nIn this case, our equation is\n\n$$\n-3 \\mid x+2 \\mid +9 = 0\n$$\n\nFirst, we isolate the absolute value on the left side of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-3 \\mid x + 2 \\mid &= -9 \\\\\n\\mid x + 2 \\mid &= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEither the positive or negative of the expression inside the absolute value must be equal to the right-hand side of the equation. So, we set up the two corresponding equations and solve them independently:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+2 & =3 & \\hspace{2em} & \\hspace{2em} & -(x+2) & =3 \\\\ x & =1 &  &  & -x-2 & =3 \\\\  &  &  &  & -x & =5 \\\\  &  &  &  & x & =-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the roots are $x=-5$ and $x=1$."] [:db/cas 17592186183971 :question/worked-solution "Recall the following equivalent formulas:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}A⇒B & ≡A^(―)∨B \\\\ A⇔B & ≡(A∧B)∨(A^(―)∧B^(―)) \\\\ A⊕B & ≡(A∧B^(―))∨(A^(―)∧B)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's find an equivalent disjunctive normal form to the given formula.\n\n- Eliminate $\\Rightarrow$ and $\\oplus$ from the expression:\n$$\n\\displaystyle x_{1}⊕(x_{2}⇒x_{3})\\begin{vmatrix}≡ \\\\ x_{1}⊕(x_{2}^(―)∨x_{3})\\end{vmatrix}≡; (x_{1}∧(x_{2}^(―)∨x_{3}^(―)))∨(x_{1}^(―)∧(x_{2}^(―)∨x_{3}))\n$$\n- Use the laws of logic to simplify the expression:\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle ( {x}_{1} \\land  ( \\overline{\\overline{ {x}_{2}} \\lor  {x}_{3}} ) ) \\lor  ( \\overline{ {x}_{1}} \\land  ( \\overline{ {x}_{2}} \\lor  {x}_{3} ) ) & \\displaystyle  \\equiv  \\\\ \\displaystyle ( {x}_{1} \\land  ( \\overline{\\overline{ {x}_{2}}} \\land  \\overline{ {x}_{3}} ) ) \\lor  ( \\overline{ {x}_{1}} \\land  ( \\overline{ {x}_{2}} \\lor  {x}_{3} ) ) & \\displaystyle  \\equiv  \\\\ \\displaystyle ( {x}_{1} \\land  {x}_{2} \\land  \\overline{ {x}_{3}} ) \\lor  ( \\overline{ {x}_{1}} \\land  ( \\overline{ {x}_{2}} \\lor  {x}_{3} ) ) & \\displaystyle  \\equiv  \\\\ \\displaystyle ( {x}_{1} \\land  \\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{ {\\color{blue}{x}_{2}}} \\land  \\overline{ {x}_{3}} ) \\lor  ( \\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{ {\\color{blue}\\overline{ {x}_{1}}}} \\land  \\overline{ {x}_{2}} ) \\lor  ( \\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{ {\\color{blue}\\overline{ {x}_{1}}}} \\land  {x}_{3} ) & \\end{array}}\n$$\n\nThe final expression is in disjunctive normal form." "Recall the following equivalent formulas:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}A⇒B & ≡A^(―)∨B \\\\ A⇔B & ≡(A∧B)∨(A^(―)∧B^(―)) \\\\ A⊕B & ≡(A∧B^(―))∨(A^(―)∧B)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's find an equivalent disjunctive normal form to the given formula.\n\n- Eliminate $\\Rightarrow$ and $\\oplus$ from the expression:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}⊕({x}_{2}\\Rightarrow {x}_{3}) & ≡ \\\\ {x}_{1}⊕(\\overset{―}{{x}_{2}}∨{x}_{3}) & ≡ \\\\ ({x}_{1}∧(\\overset{―}{\\overset{―}{{x}_{2}}∨{x}_{3}}))∨(\\overset{―}{{x}_{1}}∧(\\overset{―}{{x}_{2}}∨{x}_{3}))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- Use the laws of logic to simplify the expression:\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle ( {x}_{1} \\land  ( \\overline{\\overline{ {x}_{2}} \\lor  {x}_{3}} ) ) \\lor  ( \\overline{ {x}_{1}} \\land  ( \\overline{ {x}_{2}} \\lor  {x}_{3} ) ) & \\displaystyle  \\equiv  \\\\ \\displaystyle ( {x}_{1} \\land  ( \\overline{\\overline{ {x}_{2}}} \\land  \\overline{ {x}_{3}} ) ) \\lor  ( \\overline{ {x}_{1}} \\land  ( \\overline{ {x}_{2}} \\lor  {x}_{3} ) ) & \\displaystyle  \\equiv  \\\\ \\displaystyle ( {x}_{1} \\land  {x}_{2} \\land  \\overline{ {x}_{3}} ) \\lor  ( \\overline{ {x}_{1}} \\land  ( \\overline{ {x}_{2}} \\lor  {x}_{3} ) ) & \\displaystyle  \\equiv  \\\\ \\displaystyle ( {x}_{1} \\land  \\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{ {\\color{blue}{x}_{2}}} \\land  \\overline{ {x}_{3}} ) \\lor  ( \\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{ {\\color{blue}\\overline{ {x}_{1}}}} \\land  \\overline{ {x}_{2}} ) \\lor  ( \\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{ {\\color{blue}\\overline{ {x}_{1}}}} \\land  {x}_{3} ) & \\end{array}}\n$$\n\nThe final expression is in disjunctive normal form."] [:db/cas 17592186184033 :question/worked-solution "Recall the following logical equivalences:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}A⇒B & ≡A^(―)∨B \\\\ A⇔B & ≡(A^(―)∨B)∧(A∨B^(―)) \\\\ A⊕B & ≡(A∨B)∧(A^(―)∨B^(―))\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's find an equivalent disjunctive normal form for the given formula.\n\n- First, we eliminate $\\Rightarrow$ and $\\oplus$ from the expression:\n$$\n\\displaystyle x_{1}⇒(x_{1}^(―)⊕x_{2})\\begin{vmatrix}≡ \\\\ x_{1}^(―)∨(x_{1}^(―)⊕x_{2})\\end{vmatrix}≡; x_{1}^(―)∨((x_{1}^(―)∨x_{2})∧(x_{1}^(―)^(―)∨x_{2}^(―)))\n$$\n- Finally, we use the laws of logic to simplify the expression:\n$$\n\\displaystyle x_{1}^(―)∨((x_{1}^(―)∨x_{2})∧(x_{1}^(―)^(―)∨x_{2}^(―)))\\begin{vmatrix}≡ \\\\ x_{1}^(―)∨((x_{1}^(―)∨x_{2})∧(x_{1}∨x_{2}^(―)))\\end{vmatrix}≡; (x_{1}^(―)∨x_{1}^(―)∨x_{2})∧(x_{1}^(―)∨x_{1}∨x_{2}^(―))\\begin{vmatrix}≡ \\\\ (x_{1}^(―)∨x_{2})∧(t∨x_{2}^(―))\\end{vmatrix}≡; (x_{1}^(―)∨x_{2})∧t \\mid ≡; x_{1}^(―)∨x_{2}\n$$\n\nOur final expression is in conjunctive normal form." "Recall the following logical equivalences:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}A⇒B & ≡A^(―)∨B \\\\ A⇔B & ≡(A^(―)∨B)∧(A∨B^(―)) \\\\ A⊕B & ≡(A∨B)∧(A^(―)∨B^(―))\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's find an equivalent disjunctive normal form for the given formula.\n\n- First, we eliminate $\\Rightarrow$ and $\\oplus$ from the expression:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}\\Rightarrow (\\overset{―}{{x}_{1}}⊕{x}_{2}) & ≡ \\\\ \\overset{―}{{x}_{1}}∨(\\overset{―}{{x}_{1}}⊕{x}_{2}) & ≡ \\\\ \\overset{―}{{x}_{1}}∨((\\overset{―}{{x}_{1}}∨{x}_{2})∧(\\overset{―}{\\overset{―}{{x}_{1}}}∨\\overset{―}{{x}_{2}})) & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n- Finally, we use the laws of logic to simplify the expression:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{{x}_{1}}∨((\\overset{―}{{x}_{1}}∨{x}_{2})∧(\\overset{―}{\\overset{―}{{x}_{1}}}∨\\overset{―}{{x}_{2}})) & ≡ \\\\ \\overset{―}{{x}_{1}}∨((\\overset{―}{{x}_{1}}∨{x}_{2})∧({x}_{1}∨\\overset{―}{{x}_{2}})) & ≡ \\\\ (\\overset{―}{{x}_{1}}∨\\overset{―}{{x}_{1}}∨{x}_{2})∧(\\overset{―}{{x}_{1}}∨{x}_{1}∨\\overset{―}{{x}_{2}}) & ≡ \\\\ (\\overset{―}{{x}_{1}}∨{x}_{2})∧(\\mathrm{t}∨\\overset{―}{{x}_{2}}) & ≡ \\\\ (\\overset{―}{{x}_{1}}∨{x}_{2})∧\\mathrm{t} & ≡ \\\\ \\overset{―}{{x}_{1}}∨{x}_{2} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOur final expression is in conjunctive normal form."] [:db/cas 17592186184125 :question/worked-solution "We're required to determine the left and right continuity properties of $h(x)$ at $x = 1.$\n\nLet's start by computing $h(1){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(1) & ={e}^{1-1} \\\\  & ={e}^{0} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. The function is left-continuous at $x=1$ since the left-sided limit is equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}h(x) & ={e}^{1-1} \\\\  & ={e}^{0} \\\\  & =1 \\\\  & =h(1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. The function is *not* right-continuous at $x=1$ since the right-sided limit is *not* equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}h(x) & =1-1 \\\\  & =0 \\\\  & \\ne h(1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStatement III is false. For the function to be continuous at $x=1,$ it must be both left-continuous and right-continuous at this point. However, we have already determined that the function is not right-continuous at $x=1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "We're required to determine the left and right continuity properties of $h(x)$ at $x = 1.$\n\nLet's start by computing $h(1){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(1) & ={e}^{1-1} \\\\  & ={e}^{0} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. The function is left-continuous at $x=1$ since the left-sided limit is equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{-}}h(x) & ={e}^{1-1} \\\\  & ={e}^{0} \\\\  & =1 \\\\  & =h(1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. The function is *not* right-continuous at $x=1$ since the right-sided limit is *not* equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{+}}h(x) & =1-1 \\\\  & =0 \\\\  & \\ne h(1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStatement III is false. For the function to be continuous at $x=1,$ it must be both left-continuous and right-continuous at this point. However, we have already determined that the function is not right-continuous at $x=1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""] [:db/cas 17592186184129 :question/worked-solution "We're required to determine the left and right continuity properties of $f(x)$ at $x = 0.$\n\nFirst, we note that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0)=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. The function is left-continuous at $x = 0$ since the left-sided limit is equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x) & =2-0 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. The function is right-continuous at $x = 0$ since the right-sided limit is equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x) & =\\frac{4}{0+2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{2} \\\\  & =2 \\\\  & =f(0)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. The function is both left and right-continuous at $x=0,$ which implies that the function is continuous at $x=0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" "We're required to determine the left and right continuity properties of $f(x)$ at $x = 0.$\n\nFirst, we note that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0)=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. The function is left-continuous at $x = 0$ since the left-sided limit is equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x) & =2-0 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. The function is right-continuous at $x = 0$ since the right-sided limit is equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x) & =\\frac{4}{0+2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{2} \\\\  & =2 \\\\  & =f(0)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. The function is both left and right-continuous at $x=0,$ which implies that the function is continuous at $x=0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""] [:db/cas 17592186184514 :question/problem "Evaluate $lim_(x → - 1)(8x\\arctan (x))$." "Evaluate $\\lim_{x \\to  - 1}(8x\\arctan (x))$."] [:db/cas 17592186184515 :question/problem "What is the value of $lim_(x → 2)((2\\arcsin (x) - π)^{2})/(2x)$?" "What is the value of $\\lim_{x \\to  2}((2\\arcsin (x) - π)^{2})/(2x)$?"] [:db/cas 17592186184517 :question/problem "What is $lim_(x → ∞)4\\arctan (1 - x)$?" "What is $\\lim_{x \\to  \\infty}4\\arctan (1 - x)$?"] [:db/cas 17592186184518 :question/problem "What is $lim_(x → - ∞)\\sin (2\\arctan (x))$?" "What is $\\lim_{x \\to  - \\infty}\\sin (2\\arctan (x))$?"] [:db/cas 17592186184519 :question/worked-solution "![](images/9d/9dcc5a116c2a564547283783a7df7b1cd3a9ab3b76ac24dc2695c4cb367796d5.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(x)$ is $x\\in [-1,1].$  \n\nNow, substituting $x=-\\frac{1}{2}$ directly into the limit, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,-1/2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(x)) & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arccos}⁡\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{2\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "![](images/9d/9dcc5a116c2a564547283783a7df7b1cd3a9ab3b76ac24dc2695c4cb367796d5.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(x)$ is $x\\in [-1,1].$  \n\nNow, substituting $x=-\\frac{1}{2}$ directly into the limit, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\,-1/2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(x)) & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arccos}⁡\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{2\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186184655 :question/problem "Let $f$ and $g$ be Boolean functions of three variables defined as\n$$\n\\displaystyle f(x_{1}, x_{2}, x_{3})|=\\begin{vmatrix}x_{1} - x_{2} & \\cdot & x_{3} - x_{1}\\end{vmatrix},; g(x_{1}, x_{2}, x_{3})|= max{x_{1} \\cdot x_{2}, x_{3}}.\n$$\nComplete the following table corresponding to the function $g(x_{1}, x_{2}, f(x_{1}, x_{2}, x_{3}))$.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x_{1}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x_{2}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x_{3}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$g(x_{1}, x_{2}, f(x_{1}, x_{2}, x_{3}))$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">{{field-1}}</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">{{field-4}}</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n" "Let $f$ and $g$ be Boolean functions of three variables defined as\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f({x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3}) & =|{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}|\\cdot |{x}_{3}-{x}_{1}|, \\\\ g({x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3}) & =max\\{{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2},{x}_{3}\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\nComplete the following table corresponding to the function $g(x_{1}, x_{2}, f(x_{1}, x_{2}, x_{3}))$.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x_{1}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x_{2}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x_{3}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$g(x_{1}, x_{2}, f(x_{1}, x_{2}, x_{3}))$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">{{field-1}}</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">{{field-4}}</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n"] [:db/cas 17592186185265 :tutorial/content "In a previous lesson, we discussed that there are two main types of automata: *acceptors* and *transducers*. Recall:\n\n- An acceptor is a machine that receives a string as input and then either accepts or rejects the string.\n- A transducer is a machine that receives a string as input and then returns another string as output.\n\nIn this lesson, we will focus on transducers.\n\nOne example of a transducer is the **Mealy machine**, which processes an input string and produces a corresponding output string.\n\nMore formally, a Mealy machine is a six-tuple of the form\n\n$$\nM=(Q, \\Sigma, \\Gamma, \\delta, \\omega, q_0)\n$$\n\nwhere:\n\n- $Q$ is a nonempty finite set of **states**. These are all the possible configurations the machine can be in.\n- $\\Sigma$ is a nonempty finite set of **input symbols**. The machine can process this \"alphabet\" of symbols as inputs.\n- $\\Gamma$ is a nonempty finite set of **output symbols**. The machine can write this \"alphabet\" of symbols as outputs.\n- $\\delta$ is a **transition function** of the form $\\delta: Q \\times \\Sigma \\to Q$. It takes a state and an input symbol and returns a state. This function determines how the machine moves from one state to another based on a given input symbol.\n- $\\omega$ is an **output function** of the form $\\omega: Q \\times \\Sigma \\to \\Gamma$. It takes a state and an input symbol and returns an output symbol. This function determines what symbol to write based on a given state and input symbol.\n- $q_0 \\in Q$ is a **start state** (or **initial state**). This is the state in which the machine begins.\n\nWe can visualize a Mealy machine as a directed graph where\n\n- the vertices represent the states,\n- the arcs, labeled with input and output symbols, represent transitions from one state to another, and the corresponding output symbol, and\n- the start state is labeled by the input arrow $\\to$.\n\nAn example of a Mealy machine is shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/86/8662f40b6786d26d2cc19f7bd83d01c4d8d26412d193d258a29bb5c316f9d61f.png)\n\nIn this case, the machine has the following components:\n\n- The set of states $Q = \\{p,q,r\\}$ (represented by the vertices).\n- The set of input symbols $\\Sigma = \\{0,1\\}$ (represented by the first part of the arcs' labels).\n- The set of output symbols $\\Gamma = \\{0,1\\}$ (represented by the second part of the arcs' labels).\n- The transition and output functions $\\delta$ and $\\omega$ with the following transitions and outputs defined by the arcs:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}δ(p, 0)|= r, & δ(p, 1)|= q, & ω(p, 0)|= 1, & ω(p, 1)|= 0, \\\\ δ(q, 0)|= p, & δ(q, 1)|= r, & ω(q, 0)|= 0, & ω(q, 1)|= 0, \\\\ δ(r, 0)|= q, & δ(r, 1)|= r & ω(r, 0)|= 1, & ω(r, 1)|= 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nFor instance, the arrow from $p$ to $q$, labeled $1/0$, means that $\\delta(p,1) = q$ and $\\omega(p,1) = 0$.![](images/fc/fc0954d37cff4ded578c385b9ed8a680d78f0a28281c7480aff22c1863049892.png)\n- The start state $p$ (that is labeled by the input arrow $\\to$), as shown below.![](images/59/5920974c2cfda5c10dfa01f7c7dc20d6e2911d122aa957844a7286319002f0da.png)" "In a previous lesson, we discussed that there are two main types of automata: *acceptors* and *transducers*. Recall:\n\n- An acceptor is a machine that receives a string as input and then either accepts or rejects the string.\n- A transducer is a machine that receives a string as input and then returns another string as output.\n\nIn this lesson, we will focus on transducers.\n\nOne example of a transducer is the **Mealy machine**, which processes an input string and produces a corresponding output string.\n\nMore formally, a Mealy machine is a six-tuple of the form\n\n$$\nM=(Q, \\Sigma, \\Gamma, \\delta, \\omega, q_0)\n$$\n\nwhere:\n\n- $Q$ is a nonempty finite set of **states**. These are all the possible configurations the machine can be in.\n- $\\Sigma$ is a nonempty finite set of **input symbols**. The machine can process this \"alphabet\" of symbols as inputs.\n- $\\Gamma$ is a nonempty finite set of **output symbols**. The machine can write this \"alphabet\" of symbols as outputs.\n- $\\delta$ is a **transition function** of the form $\\delta: Q \\times \\Sigma \\to Q$. It takes a state and an input symbol and returns a state. This function determines how the machine moves from one state to another based on a given input symbol.\n- $\\omega$ is an **output function** of the form $\\omega: Q \\times \\Sigma \\to \\Gamma$. It takes a state and an input symbol and returns an output symbol. This function determines what symbol to write based on a given state and input symbol.\n- $q_0 \\in Q$ is a **start state** (or **initial state**). This is the state in which the machine begins.\n\nWe can visualize a Mealy machine as a directed graph where\n\n- the vertices represent the states,\n- the arcs, labeled with input and output symbols, represent transitions from one state to another, and the corresponding output symbol, and\n- the start state is labeled by the input arrow $\\to$.\n\nAn example of a Mealy machine is shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/86/8662f40b6786d26d2cc19f7bd83d01c4d8d26412d193d258a29bb5c316f9d61f.png)\n\nIn this case, the machine has the following components:\n\n- The set of states $Q = \\{p,q,r\\}$ (represented by the vertices).\n- The set of input symbols $\\Sigma = \\{0,1\\}$ (represented by the first part of the arcs' labels).\n- The set of output symbols $\\Gamma = \\{0,1\\}$ (represented by the second part of the arcs' labels).\n- The transition and output functions $\\delta$ and $\\omega$ with the following transitions and outputs defined by the arcs:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\delta (p,0) & =r, & \\delta (p,1) & =q, &  &  & \\omega (p,0) & =1, & \\omega (p,1) & =0, \\\\ \\delta (q,0) & =p, & \\delta (q,1) & =r, &  &  & \\omega (q,0) & =0, & \\omega (q,1) & =0, \\\\ \\delta (r,0) & =q, & \\delta (r,1) & =r &  &  & \\omega (r,0) & =1, & \\omega (r,1) & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\nFor instance, the arrow from $p$ to $q$, labeled $1/0$, means that $\\delta(p,1) = q$ and $\\omega(p,1) = 0$.![](images/fc/fc0954d37cff4ded578c385b9ed8a680d78f0a28281c7480aff22c1863049892.png)\n- The start state $p$ (that is labeled by the input arrow $\\to$), as shown below.![](images/59/5920974c2cfda5c10dfa01f7c7dc20d6e2911d122aa957844a7286319002f0da.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186185266 :tutorial/content "Recall that a transducer is a machine that receives a string as input and then returns another string as output. In a previous lesson, we studied Mealy machines. In this lesson, we'll look at another type of transducer called a **Moore machine.**\n\nA Moore machine is a six-tuple of the form\n\n$$\nM = (Q, \\Sigma, \\Gamma, \\delta, \\omega, q_0)\n$$\n\nwhere:\n\n- $Q$ is a nonempty finite set of **states**. These are all the possible configurations the machine can be in.\n- $\\Sigma$ is a nonempty finite set of **input symbols**. The machine can process this \"alphabet\" of symbols as inputs.\n- $\\Gamma$ is a nonempty finite set of **output symbols**. The machine can write this \"alphabet\" of symbols as outputs.\n- $\\delta$ is a **transition function** of the form $\\delta: Q \\times \\Sigma \\to Q$. It takes a state and an input symbol and returns a state. This function determines how the machine moves from one state to another based on a given input symbol.\n- $\\omega$ is an **output function** of the form $\\omega: Q \\to \\Gamma$. It takes a state and returns an output symbol. This function determines what symbol to write based on a given state.\n- $q_0 \\in Q$ is a **start state** (or **initial state**). This is the state in which the machine begins.\n\nWe can visualize a Moore machine as a directed graph where\n\n- vertices represent the states and their corresponding output symbol,\n- the arcs, labeled with input symbols, represent transitions from one state to another, and\n- the start state is labeled by the input arrow $\\to$.\n\nAn example of a Moore machine is shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/d6/d65203077e6ec7a3d0ce9df8c4c10111126f7c8850154816233896c9f0a9698a.png)\n\nIn this case, the machine has the following components:\n\n- The set of states $Q = \\{p,q\\}$ (represented by the vertices).\n- The set of input symbols $\\Sigma = \\{0,1\\}$ (represented by the arcs' labels).\n- The set of output symbols $\\Omega = \\{a,b\\}$ (represented by the vertices' labels).\n- The transition function $\\delta$, defined by the arcs with the following transitions:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}δ(p, 0)|= q, & δ(p, 1)|= p, \\\\ δ(q, 0)|= q, & δ(q, 1)|= p\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nFor instance, the arrow from $p$ to $q$, labeled $0$, means that $\\delta(p,0) = q$.![](images/0c/0c80de9044b66d31f00b0fe7970e8dd460719219ac1ea1bfe8782413f0bd6b4e.png)\n- The output function $\\omega$ is defined for each vertex (state) with the following outputs:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\omega(p) = b, \\qquad \\omega(q) = a\n$$\nFor instance, the node labeled $q\\mid a$ means that $\\omega(q) = a$.![](images/ba/bab06afc6eba39cfd9a0be14542b5922a96ed848aee55b30871ab3d5e5fc291d.png)\n- The start state $p$ (that is labeled by the input arrow $\\to$), as shown below.![](images/e3/e3c6fdd49e9984d3c8bc75550767a5f05e382685150ed6a081d43cb620c9f93d.png)" "Recall that a transducer is a machine that receives a string as input and then returns another string as output. In a previous lesson, we studied Mealy machines. In this lesson, we'll look at another type of transducer called a **Moore machine.**\n\nA Moore machine is a six-tuple of the form\n\n$$\nM = (Q, \\Sigma, \\Gamma, \\delta, \\omega, q_0)\n$$\n\nwhere:\n\n- $Q$ is a nonempty finite set of **states**. These are all the possible configurations the machine can be in.\n- $\\Sigma$ is a nonempty finite set of **input symbols**. The machine can process this \"alphabet\" of symbols as inputs.\n- $\\Gamma$ is a nonempty finite set of **output symbols**. The machine can write this \"alphabet\" of symbols as outputs.\n- $\\delta$ is a **transition function** of the form $\\delta: Q \\times \\Sigma \\to Q$. It takes a state and an input symbol and returns a state. This function determines how the machine moves from one state to another based on a given input symbol.\n- $\\omega$ is an **output function** of the form $\\omega: Q \\to \\Gamma$. It takes a state and returns an output symbol. This function determines what symbol to write based on a given state.\n- $q_0 \\in Q$ is a **start state** (or **initial state**). This is the state in which the machine begins.\n\nWe can visualize a Moore machine as a directed graph where\n\n- vertices represent the states and their corresponding output symbol,\n- the arcs, labeled with input symbols, represent transitions from one state to another, and\n- the start state is labeled by the input arrow $\\to$.\n\nAn example of a Moore machine is shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/d6/d65203077e6ec7a3d0ce9df8c4c10111126f7c8850154816233896c9f0a9698a.png)\n\nIn this case, the machine has the following components:\n\n- The set of states $Q = \\{p,q\\}$ (represented by the vertices).\n- The set of input symbols $\\Sigma = \\{0,1\\}$ (represented by the arcs' labels).\n- The set of output symbols $\\Omega = \\{a,b\\}$ (represented by the vertices' labels).\n- The transition function $\\delta$, defined by the arcs with the following transitions:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\delta (p,0) & =q, & \\delta (p,1) & =p, \\\\ \\delta (q,0) & =q, & \\delta (q,1) & =p\\end{aligned}\n$$\nFor instance, the arrow from $p$ to $q$, labeled $0$, means that $\\delta(p,0) = q$.![](images/0c/0c80de9044b66d31f00b0fe7970e8dd460719219ac1ea1bfe8782413f0bd6b4e.png)\n- The output function $\\omega$ is defined for each vertex (state) with the following outputs:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\omega(p) = b, \\qquad \\omega(q) = a\n$$\nFor instance, the node labeled $q\\mid a$ means that $\\omega(q) = a$.![](images/ba/bab06afc6eba39cfd9a0be14542b5922a96ed848aee55b30871ab3d5e5fc291d.png)\n- The start state $p$ (that is labeled by the input arrow $\\to$), as shown below.![](images/e3/e3c6fdd49e9984d3c8bc75550767a5f05e382685150ed6a081d43cb620c9f93d.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186185343 :tutorial/content "Let's consider the following piecewise function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x)={\\begin{aligned}{2}^{x} & \\,x<0 \\\\ \\sqrt{x+1} & \\,x\\ge 0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the function has two \"pieces,\" one for $x < 0$ and one for $x\\geq 0.$ We wish to examine whether the function is continuous at $x=0.$\n\nIf we plot the function, it appears as though the function *is* continuous at this point.\n\n![](images/81/813b611a99d031631dae2d98ebac47e61c1121e33bb0c8bcb6c23358dc0d9f15.png)\n\nHowever, to be sure that the function is continuous at $x=0,$ we need to check that the function is both left-continuous and right-continuous at $x=0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 0^-}f(x) =  \\lim_{x\\to 0^+}f(x) =f(0).\n$$\n\nFirst, we note that\n\n$$\nf(0) = \\sqrt{0+1} = 1.\n$$\n\nNow, let's check the two limits:\n\n- Approaching from the left, we have $x < 0,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=2^x.$ We get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}{2}^{x} \\\\  & ={2}^{0} \\\\  & =1 \\\\  & =f(0).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Approaching from the right, we have $x>0,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=\\sqrt {x+1}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\sqrt{x+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{0+1} \\\\  & =1 \\\\  & =f(0).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the function is both left-continuous and right-continuous at $x=0,$ we conclude that it is continuous at $x=0.$" "Let's consider the following piecewise function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x)=\\begin{cases}{2}^{x} & \\hspace{1em}x<0 \\\\ \\sqrt{x+1} & \\hspace{1em}x\\ge 0\\end{cases}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the function has two \"pieces,\" one for $x < 0$ and one for $x\\geq 0.$ We wish to examine whether the function is continuous at $x=0.$\n\nIf we plot the function, it appears as though the function *is* continuous at this point.\n\n![](images/81/813b611a99d031631dae2d98ebac47e61c1121e33bb0c8bcb6c23358dc0d9f15.png)\n\nHowever, to be sure that the function is continuous at $x=0,$ we need to check that the function is both left-continuous and right-continuous at $x=0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 0^-}f(x) =  \\lim_{x\\to 0^+}f(x) =f(0).\n$$\n\nFirst, we note that\n\n$$\nf(0) = \\sqrt{0+1} = 1.\n$$\n\nNow, let's check the two limits:\n\n- Approaching from the left, we have $x < 0,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=2^x.$ We get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{-}}{2}^{x} \\\\  & ={2}^{0} \\\\  & =1 \\\\  & =f(0).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Approaching from the right, we have $x>0,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=\\sqrt {x+1}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\sqrt{x+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{0+1} \\\\  & =1 \\\\  & =f(0).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the function is both left-continuous and right-continuous at $x=0,$ we conclude that it is continuous at $x=0.$"] [:db/cas 17592186185359 :tutorial/content "In this lesson, we will learn how to deal with limits involving inverse trigonometric functions. \n\nLet's begin by recalling the graph of $y=\\arcsin(x).$\n\n![](images/df/df22eea4331c47da3047381ca672fd80ebf75118054316fe88c0e2d96dae0d34.png)\n\nUsing the graph, we note the following:\n\n- The domain of $\\arcsin(x)$ is $x \\in [-1,1].$\n\n- For every value $a \\in (-1,1),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\to a} \\arcsin(x) = \\arcsin(a).\n$$\n\n \nIn other words, we can evaluate the limit using direct substitution. For example,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 1/2}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(x) & =\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For any value $a$ that does *not* lie in the domain of $\\arcsin(x),$ the limit\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\to a} \\arcsin(x)\n$$\n\n \ndoes *not* exist.\n\n- At the point $x=-1,$ the limit does *not* exist because the left-sided limit does not exist. However, the right-sided limit *does* exist and is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(x) & =\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Similarly, at the point $x=1,$ the limit does *not* exist because the right-sided limit does not exist. However, the left-sided limit *does* exist and is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(x) & =\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(1) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe limit properties of $y=\\arccos(x)$ are similar. Let's discuss them briefly." "In this lesson, we will learn how to deal with limits involving inverse trigonometric functions. \n\nLet's begin by recalling the graph of $y=\\arcsin(x).$\n\n![](images/df/df22eea4331c47da3047381ca672fd80ebf75118054316fe88c0e2d96dae0d34.png)\n\nUsing the graph, we note the following:\n\n- The domain of $\\arcsin(x)$ is $x \\in [-1,1].$\n\n- For every value $a \\in (-1,1),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to a} \\arcsin(x) = \\arcsin(a).\n$$\n\n \nIn other words, we can evaluate the limit using direct substitution. For example,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 1/2}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(x) & =\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For any value $a$ that does *not* lie in the domain of $\\arcsin(x),$ the limit\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to a} \\arcsin(x)\n$$\n\n \ndoes *not* exist.\n\n- At the point $x=-1,$ the limit does *not* exist because the left-sided limit does not exist. However, the right-sided limit *does* exist and is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(x) & =\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Similarly, at the point $x=1,$ the limit does *not* exist because the right-sided limit does not exist. However, the left-sided limit *does* exist and is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{-}}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(x) & =\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(1) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe limit properties of $y=\\arccos(x)$ are similar. Let's discuss them briefly."] [:db/cas 17592186185360 :tutorial/content "We can deduce limits at infinity of transformed inverse trigonometric functions by sketching their graphs.\n\nFor example, let's consider the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x \\to \\, \\infty} \\dfrac{2}{\\pi}\\arctan \\left(x-\\pi\\right)\n$$\n\nTo evaluate this limit, we first sketch the graph of  $y =\\arctan \\left(x-\\pi\\right){:}$  \n\n![](images/51/51a0b9a210147b3791ce20a2f65320e20618826671ea6c7c31a27f8647595079.png)\n\nThe domain of   $\\arctan \\left(x-\\pi\\right)$ is $(-\\infty,\\infty).$   Moreover, from the graph, we see that \n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{x\\to \\infty} \\arctan \\left(x-\\pi\\right)=\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} }  .\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the algebra of limits, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,\\infty }\\frac{2}{\\pi }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x-\\pi ) & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\,\\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x-\\pi ) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }\\cdot \\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLimits at infinity involving the inverse sine and cosine functions usually do not exist. Let's see an example." "We can deduce limits at infinity of transformed inverse trigonometric functions by sketching their graphs.\n\nFor example, let's consider the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x \\to \\, \\infty} \\dfrac{2}{\\pi}\\arctan \\left(x-\\pi\\right)\n$$\n\nTo evaluate this limit, we first sketch the graph of  $y =\\arctan \\left(x-\\pi\\right){:}$  \n\n![](images/51/51a0b9a210147b3791ce20a2f65320e20618826671ea6c7c31a27f8647595079.png)\n\nThe domain of   $\\arctan \\left(x-\\pi\\right)$ is $(-\\infty,\\infty).$   Moreover, from the graph, we see that \n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{x\\to \\infty} \\arctan \\left(x-\\pi\\right)=\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} }  .\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the algebra of limits, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\,\\infty }\\frac{2}{\\pi }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x-\\pi ) & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\,\\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x-\\pi ) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }\\cdot \\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLimits at infinity involving the inverse sine and cosine functions usually do not exist. Let's see an example."] [:db/cas 17592186185362 :tutorial/content "The graph of $y=\\arctan x$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/fc/fc06a4e63f38a315dd3944f65f871cca2dbdddee26ec268e857f79e5f4ab8848.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we note the following:\n\n- The domain is $x \\in (-\\infty, \\infty).$\n\n- For every value $a,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\to a} \\arctan(x) = \\arctan(a).\n$$\n\n- The limit as $x\\to-\\infty$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x) & =-\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The limit as $x\\to\\infty$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x) & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can use the algebra of limits to evaluate expressions involving inverse trigonometric functions. Let's see some examples." "The graph of $y=\\arctan x$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/fc/fc06a4e63f38a315dd3944f65f871cca2dbdddee26ec268e857f79e5f4ab8848.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we note the following:\n\n- The domain is $x \\in (-\\infty, \\infty).$\n\n- For every value $a,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to a} \\arctan(x) = \\arctan(a).\n$$\n\n- The limit as $x\\to-\\infty$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x) & =-\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The limit as $x\\to\\infty$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x) & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can use the algebra of limits to evaluate expressions involving inverse trigonometric functions. Let's see some examples."] [:db/cas 17592186185363 :tutorial/content "Let's recall the graph of $y=\\arccos(x).$\n\n![](images/9d/9dcc5a116c2a564547283783a7df7b1cd3a9ab3b76ac24dc2695c4cb367796d5.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we note the following:\n\n- The domain is $x \\in [-1,1].$\n\n- For every value $a \\in (-1,1),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\to a} \\arccos(x) = \\arccos(a).\n$$\n\n- For any value $a$ that does *not* lie in the domain of $\\arccos(x),$ the limit\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\to a} \\arccos(x)\n$$\n\n \ndoes *not* exist.\n\n- The right-sided limit at $x=-1$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(x) & =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(-1) \\\\  & =\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Similarly, the left-sided limit at $x=1$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(x) & =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(1) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's deduce the limit properties of $y = \\arctan x.$" "Let's recall the graph of $y=\\arccos(x).$\n\n![](images/9d/9dcc5a116c2a564547283783a7df7b1cd3a9ab3b76ac24dc2695c4cb367796d5.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we note the following:\n\n- The domain is $x \\in [-1,1].$\n\n- For every value $a \\in (-1,1),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to a} \\arccos(x) = \\arccos(a).\n$$\n\n- For any value $a$ that does *not* lie in the domain of $\\arccos(x),$ the limit\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to a} \\arccos(x)\n$$\n\n \ndoes *not* exist.\n\n- The right-sided limit at $x=-1$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(x) & =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(-1) \\\\  & =\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Similarly, the left-sided limit at $x=1$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{-}}\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(x) & =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(1) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's deduce the limit properties of $y = \\arctan x.$"] [:db/cas 17592186185370 :tutorial/content "We can use rounding to *estimate* the result of a division problem.\n\nFor example, let's estimate the quotient of\n\n$$\n478 \\div 10\n$$\n\nBy rounding the dividend to the nearest ten, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n478\\begin{vmatrix}\\div & 10 \\\\ ↓ & ↓ \\\\ 480 & \\div\\end{vmatrix}10\n$$\n\nSo, to estimate the quotient of our original division problem, we need to find the value of\n\n$$\n480 \\div 10\n$$\n\nWe can solve this simplified problem as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle 480 \\div  10 & \\displaystyle  = \\\\ \\displaystyle 48 \\cancel{0} & \\displaystyle  = \\\\ \\displaystyle 48 & \\displaystyle \\end{array}}\n$$\n\nSo, we conclude that\n\n$$\n478 \\div 10 \\approx 48\n$$\n\nRemember that the symbol \"\n\n$$\n\\approx\n$$\n\n\" means \"is approximately equal to.\"" "We can use rounding to *estimate* the result of a division problem.\n\nFor example, let's estimate the quotient of\n\n$$\n478 \\div 10\n$$\n\nBy rounding the dividend to the nearest ten, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{ccc}478 & ÷ & 10 \\\\ ↓ &  & ↓ \\\\ {\\color{blue}{480}} & ÷ & 10\\end{array}\n$$\n\nSo, to estimate the quotient of our original division problem, we need to find the value of\n\n$$\n480 \\div 10\n$$\n\nWe can solve this simplified problem as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle 480 \\div  10 & \\displaystyle  = \\\\ \\displaystyle 48 \\cancel{0} & \\displaystyle  = \\\\ \\displaystyle 48 & \\displaystyle \\end{array}}\n$$\n\nSo, we conclude that\n\n$$\n478 \\div 10 \\approx 48\n$$\n\nRemember that the symbol \"\n\n$$\n\\approx\n$$\n\n\" means \"is approximately equal to.\""] [:db/cas 17592186185382 :tutorial/content "Recall that to find the **roots** of a function $f(x)$, we must solve the equation\n\n$$\nf(x) = 0\n$$\n\nFor instance, let's calculate the roots of\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\mid x-3 \\mid -6\n$$\n\nTo find the roots of $f(x)$, we need to solve the following absolute value equation:\n\n$$\n\\mid x-3 \\mid -6 = 0\n$$\n\nFirst, we isolate the absolute value on the left side of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mid x - 3 \\mid &= 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEither the positive or negative of the expression inside the absolute value must be equal to the right-hand side of the equation. So, we set up the two corresponding equations and solve them independently:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx - 3 &= 6\\begin{vmatrix}- (x - 3)|= 6 \\\\ x|= 9\\end{vmatrix}- x + 3|= 6 \\\\\n- x &= 3 \\\\\nx &= -3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the roots are $x=-3$ and $x=9$." "Recall that to find the **roots** of a function $f(x)$, we must solve the equation\n\n$$\nf(x) = 0\n$$\n\nFor instance, let's calculate the roots of\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\mid x-3 \\mid -6\n$$\n\nTo find the roots of $f(x)$, we need to solve the following absolute value equation:\n\n$$\n\\mid x-3 \\mid -6 = 0\n$$\n\nFirst, we isolate the absolute value on the left side of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mid x - 3 \\mid &= 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEither the positive or negative of the expression inside the absolute value must be equal to the right-hand side of the equation. So, we set up the two corresponding equations and solve them independently:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-3 & =6 & \\hspace{2em} & \\hspace{2em} & -(x-3) & =6 \\\\ x & =9 &  &  & -x+3 & =6 \\\\  &  &  &  & -x & =3 \\\\  &  &  &  & x & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the roots are $x=-3$ and $x=9$."] [:db/cas 17592186185410 :tutorial/content "In mathematics, there are two main types of \"machines\" known as **automata**. These structures represent the first step toward formally defining the concept of an **algorithm**.\n\nThe first type of automaton is called an **acceptor.** It processes a string of symbols and determines whether to accept or reject it. The second is called a **transducer,** which takes an input string and transforms it into another string. In this lesson, we will focus on acceptors.\n\nA deterministic finite automaton (DFA) is a specific type of acceptor. It has a finite number of states and processes symbols from an input alphabet, transitioning between states based on predefined rules determined by the input symbols.\n\nMore formally, a DFA is a five-tuple of the form\n\n$$\nA=(Q, \\Sigma, \\delta, q_0, F)\n$$\n\nwhere the components are as follows:\n\n- $Q$ is a nonempty finite set of **states**. These are all the possible configurations the automaton can be in.\n- $\\Sigma$ is a nonempty finite set of **input symbols**. This is the \"alphabet\" of symbols that the DFA can process as inputs.\n- $\\delta$ is a **transition function** of the form $\\delta: Q \\times \\Sigma \\to Q$. It takes a state and an input symbol and returns a state. This function determines how the DFA moves from one state to another based on a given input symbol.\n- $q_0 \\in Q$ is a **start state** (or **initial state**). This is the state in which the automaton begins.\n- $F \\subseteq Q$ is a set of **final** (or **accepting**) **states**.\n\nWe can visualize a DFA as a directed graph, where\n\n- vertices represent the states,\n- arcs, labeled with input symbols, represent transitions from one state to another,\n- the start state is labeled by the input arrow $\\to$, and\n- the final states are circled by a double line.\n\nFor example, a deterministic finite automaton is given in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/fa/fa046f67b6f665c6d5ba78b7f368e64d1fd4b68058b8531b5525382bff7724fe.png)\n\nIn this case, the automaton has the following components:\n\n- The set of states $Q = \\{p,q\\}$ (represented by the vertices).\n- The set of input symbols $\\Sigma = \\{0,1,2\\}$ (represented by the arcs' labels).\n- The transition function $\\delta$ with the following transitions defined by the arcs:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}δ(p, 0)|= p, & δ(p, 1)|= q, & δ(p, 2)|= p, \\\\ δ(q, 0)|= p, & δ(q, 1)|= q, & δ(q, 2)|= p\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nFor instance, the arrow from $p$ to $q$, labeled $1$, means that $\\delta(p,1)=q$.![](images/04/04bb859e26e9e2c77cb1e9b5f8a8c31a52badf157d04537665a3bec5138620fa.png)\n- The start state $p$ (that is labeled by the input arrow $\\to$), as shown below.![](images/59/5920974c2cfda5c10dfa01f7c7dc20d6e2911d122aa957844a7286319002f0da.png)\n- The set of final states $F = \\{q\\}$ (circled by the double line), as shown below.![](images/23/23fd3b4957b3099a5f52335148c17a3bccef4c52eeb66136d36a1293e8b0c35f.png)" "In mathematics, there are two main types of \"machines\" known as **automata**. These structures represent the first step toward formally defining the concept of an **algorithm**.\n\nThe first type of automaton is called an **acceptor.** It processes a string of symbols and determines whether to accept or reject it. The second is called a **transducer,** which takes an input string and transforms it into another string. In this lesson, we will focus on acceptors.\n\nA deterministic finite automaton (DFA) is a specific type of acceptor. It has a finite number of states and processes symbols from an input alphabet, transitioning between states based on predefined rules determined by the input symbols.\n\nMore formally, a DFA is a five-tuple of the form\n\n$$\nA=(Q, \\Sigma, \\delta, q_0, F)\n$$\n\nwhere the components are as follows:\n\n- $Q$ is a nonempty finite set of **states**. These are all the possible configurations the automaton can be in.\n- $\\Sigma$ is a nonempty finite set of **input symbols**. This is the \"alphabet\" of symbols that the DFA can process as inputs.\n- $\\delta$ is a **transition function** of the form $\\delta: Q \\times \\Sigma \\to Q$. It takes a state and an input symbol and returns a state. This function determines how the DFA moves from one state to another based on a given input symbol.\n- $q_0 \\in Q$ is a **start state** (or **initial state**). This is the state in which the automaton begins.\n- $F \\subseteq Q$ is a set of **final** (or **accepting**) **states**.\n\nWe can visualize a DFA as a directed graph, where\n\n- vertices represent the states,\n- arcs, labeled with input symbols, represent transitions from one state to another,\n- the start state is labeled by the input arrow $\\to$, and\n- the final states are circled by a double line.\n\nFor example, a deterministic finite automaton is given in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/fa/fa046f67b6f665c6d5ba78b7f368e64d1fd4b68058b8531b5525382bff7724fe.png)\n\nIn this case, the automaton has the following components:\n\n- The set of states $Q = \\{p,q\\}$ (represented by the vertices).\n- The set of input symbols $\\Sigma = \\{0,1,2\\}$ (represented by the arcs' labels).\n- The transition function $\\delta$ with the following transitions defined by the arcs:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\delta (p,0) & =p, & \\delta (p,1) & =q, & \\delta (p,2) & =p, \\\\ \\delta (q,0) & =p, & \\delta (q,1) & =q, & \\delta (q,2) & =p\\end{aligned}\n$$\nFor instance, the arrow from $p$ to $q$, labeled $1$, means that $\\delta(p,1)=q$.![](images/04/04bb859e26e9e2c77cb1e9b5f8a8c31a52badf157d04537665a3bec5138620fa.png)\n- The start state $p$ (that is labeled by the input arrow $\\to$), as shown below.![](images/59/5920974c2cfda5c10dfa01f7c7dc20d6e2911d122aa957844a7286319002f0da.png)\n- The set of final states $F = \\{q\\}$ (circled by the double line), as shown below.![](images/23/23fd3b4957b3099a5f52335148c17a3bccef4c52eeb66136d36a1293e8b0c35f.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186187991 :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of our series in turn:\n\n- The first series\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty} \\dfrac{(-1)^n}{n^{p+1}}\n$$\nis an alternating $p$-series and converges for\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}p + 1 & > 0 \\\\ p & > - 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n- The second series\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty} (p+1)^n\n$$\nis a geometric series with common ratio $r = p+1$. For convergence, we require $\\mid r \\mid < 1$. So, this series converges for\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\mid p + 1 \\mid \\mid < 1; - 1 < p + 1\\begin{vmatrix}< 1 \\\\ -2 < p\\end{vmatrix}< 0.\n$$\n\nFor both series to converge, we need both of the following inequalities to be satisfied:\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}p > - 1 \\\\ - 2 < p < 0\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\n\nTherefore, both series converge provided that $-1 < p < 0$." "Let's examine each of our series in turn:\n\n- The first series\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty} \\dfrac{(-1)^n}{n^{p+1}}\n$$\nis an alternating $p$-series and converges for\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p+1 & >0 \\\\ p & >-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- The second series\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty} (p+1)^n\n$$\nis a geometric series with common ratio $r = p+1$. For convergence, we require $\\mid r \\mid < 1$. So, this series converges for\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|p+1| & <1 \\\\ -1<p+1 & <1 \\\\ -2<p & <0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor both series to converge, we need both of the following inequalities to be satisfied:\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}p > - 1 \\\\ - 2 < p < 0\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\n\nTherefore, both series converge provided that $-1 < p < 0$."] [:db/cas 17592186188002 :question/worked-solution "Two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ with a joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x, y) = f_X(x) \\cdot f_Y (y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where $f_X(x)$ and $f_Y (y)$ are the marginal probability density functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Computing the marginal density function for $Y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{4}x{e}^{-y/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{-y/2}{[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-y/2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhere $y \\in \\left[0,\\infty\\right).$\n\n- Statement II is true. Computing the marginal density function for $X,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{4}x{e}^{-y/2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}x{lim}_{b\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{b}{e}^{-y/2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}x{lim}_{b\\to \\infty }{[-2{e}^{-y/2}]}_{0}^{b} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}x{lim}_{b\\to \\infty }[-2{e}^{-b/2}+2] \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhere $x \\in \\left[0,2\\right].$ So, when $x \\in \\left[0,2\\right]$ and $y\\in \\left[0,\\infty\\right),$ we obtain that \n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\dfrac{1}{4}xe^{-y/2}}_{\\large f(x,y)} = \\underbrace{\\Big(\\dfrac{x}{2}\\Big)}_{\\large f_X(x)} \\cdot \\underbrace{\\Big(\\dfrac{1}{2}e^{-y/2}\\Big)}_{\\large f_Y(y)}.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, our random variables $X$ and $Y$ are independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" "Two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ with a joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x, y) = f_X(x) \\cdot f_Y (y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where $f_X(x)$ and $f_Y (y)$ are the marginal probability density functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Computing the marginal density function for $Y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{4}x{e}^{-y/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{-y/2}{[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-y/2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhere $y \\in \\left[0,\\infty\\right).$\n\n- Statement II is true. Computing the marginal density function for $X,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{4}x{e}^{-y/2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}x\\lim_{b\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{b}{e}^{-y/2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}x\\lim_{b\\to \\infty }{[-2{e}^{-y/2}]}_{0}^{b} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}x\\lim_{b\\to \\infty }[-2{e}^{-b/2}+2] \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhere $x \\in \\left[0,2\\right].$ So, when $x \\in \\left[0,2\\right]$ and $y\\in \\left[0,\\infty\\right),$ we obtain that \n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\dfrac{1}{4}xe^{-y/2}}_{\\large f(x,y)} = \\underbrace{\\Big(\\dfrac{x}{2}\\Big)}_{\\large f_X(x)} \\cdot \\underbrace{\\Big(\\dfrac{1}{2}e^{-y/2}\\Big)}_{\\large f_Y(y)}.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, our random variables $X$ and $Y$ are independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""] [:db/cas 17592186188003 :question/worked-solution "The marginal density function for $Y$ when $y \\geq 1$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{{x}^{3}{y}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{{y}^{3}}{lim}_{b\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{b}\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{{y}^{3}}{lim}_{b\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{2{x}^{2}}]}_{1}^{b} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{{y}^{3}}{lim}_{b\\to \\infty }(\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{2{b}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{{y}^{3}}(\\frac{1}{2}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{y}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the full expression for $f_Y(y)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf_Y(y) = \n\t{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{ll}{\\displaystyle \\frac{2}{ {y}^{3}}} , & y \\ge  1 , \\\\ 0 , & \\text{otherwise} .\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\n\nFinally, the  expected value of $Y$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y] & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }y\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }y\\cdot \\frac{2}{{y}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2{lim}_{b\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{b}\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2{lim}_{b\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{y}]}_{1}^{b} \\\\  & =2{lim}_{b\\to \\infty }(1-\\frac{1}{b}) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The marginal density function for $Y$ when $y \\geq 1$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{{x}^{3}{y}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{{y}^{3}}\\lim_{b\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{b}\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{{y}^{3}}\\lim_{b\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{2{x}^{2}}]}_{1}^{b} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{{y}^{3}}\\lim_{b\\to \\infty }(\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{2{b}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{{y}^{3}}(\\frac{1}{2}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{y}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the full expression for $f_Y(y)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf_Y(y) = \n\t{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{ll}{\\displaystyle \\frac{2}{ {y}^{3}}} , & y \\ge  1 , \\\\ 0 , & \\text{otherwise} .\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\n\nFinally, the  expected value of $Y$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y] & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }y\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }y\\cdot \\frac{2}{{y}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\lim_{b\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{b}\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\lim_{b\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{y}]}_{1}^{b} \\\\  & =2\\lim_{b\\to \\infty }(1-\\frac{1}{b}) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186188004 :question/worked-solution "When a polar curve is differentiable at a point $\\theta_0$, there is a horizontal tangent at $\\theta_0$ if\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = \\dfrac{y'(\\theta_0)}{x'(\\theta_0)} = 0\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\ny'(\\theta_0) = 0 \\qquad \\textrm{and}\\qquad x'(\\theta_0) \\neq 0.\n$$\n\nHowever, if the curve is continuous at $\\theta_0$ yet the derivative $y'(x)$ takes the indeterminate form $\\dfrac00$ at this point, we say that the curve has a horizontal tangent at this point if\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{\\theta \\to \\theta_0} \\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = 0.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're told that $x'\\left(\\dfrac{3\\pi}2\\right)=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Substituting $\\theta = \\dfrac{3\\pi}2$ into the expression for $y'(\\theta),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\right) & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\right)+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{3\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\right)+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\pi ) \\\\  & =0+0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Computing the limit as $\\theta\\to \\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}$ using L'Hopital's rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to 3\\pi /2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 3\\pi /2}\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )} \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 3\\pi /2}\\left(\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\right) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 3\\pi /2}\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\right) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 3\\pi /2}\\left(\\frac{{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta )}^{′}}{{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{′}}\\right) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 3\\pi /2}\\left(\\frac{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }{-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\right)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{3\\pi }{2})}{-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{3\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\right)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\pi )}{-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\pi )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1+2\\cdot (-1)}{-2\\cdot 0-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-2}{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{-1}{0} \\\\  & \\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the limit in part II does *not* equal to $0,$ there curve does *not* have a horizontal tangent at $\\theta=\\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}.$ In fact, it is a vertical tangent.\n\n![](images/1b/1bff05165761ba775dfae73cd4d41c28f5a939b8caca602d516636a6fb05ae75.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"\n\n**Note**: We'll discuss vertical tangents in more detail later in this lesson." "When a polar curve is differentiable at a point $\\theta_0$, there is a horizontal tangent at $\\theta_0$ if\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = \\dfrac{y'(\\theta_0)}{x'(\\theta_0)} = 0\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\ny'(\\theta_0) = 0 \\qquad \\textrm{and}\\qquad x'(\\theta_0) \\neq 0.\n$$\n\nHowever, if the curve is continuous at $\\theta_0$ yet the derivative $y'(x)$ takes the indeterminate form $\\dfrac00$ at this point, we say that the curve has a horizontal tangent at this point if\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{\\theta \\to \\theta_0} \\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = 0.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're told that $x'\\left(\\dfrac{3\\pi}2\\right)=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Substituting $\\theta = \\dfrac{3\\pi}2$ into the expression for $y'(\\theta),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\right) & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\right)+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{3\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\right)+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\pi ) \\\\  & =0+0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Computing the limit as $\\theta\\to \\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}$ using L'Hopital's rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\theta \\to 3\\pi /2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\lim_{\\theta \\to 3\\pi /2}\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\theta \\to 3\\pi /2}\\left(\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\right) \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\theta \\to 3\\pi /2}\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\right) \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\theta \\to 3\\pi /2}\\left(\\frac{{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta )}^{′}}{{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{′}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\theta \\to 3\\pi /2}\\left(\\frac{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }{-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\right)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{3\\pi }{2})}{-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{3\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\right)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\pi )}{-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\pi )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1+2\\cdot (-1)}{-2\\cdot 0-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-2}{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{-1}{0} \\\\  & \\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the limit in part II does *not* equal to $0,$ there curve does *not* have a horizontal tangent at $\\theta=\\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}.$ In fact, it is a vertical tangent.\n\n![](images/1b/1bff05165761ba775dfae73cd4d41c28f5a939b8caca602d516636a6fb05ae75.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"\n\n**Note**: We'll discuss vertical tangents in more detail later in this lesson."] [:db/cas 17592186188023 :question/worked-solution "For a given polar curve, there is a vertical tangent at $\\theta = \\theta_0$ if\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = \\dfrac{y'(\\theta_0)}{x'(\\theta_0)}\n$$\n\nis infinite. This means that\n\n$$\ny'(\\theta_0) \\neq 0 \\qquad \\textrm{and}\\qquad x'(\\theta_0) = 0.\n$$\n\nHowever, if the curve is continuous at $\\theta_0$ yet the derivative takes the indeterminate form $\\dfrac00$ at this point, we say that the curve has a vertical tangent at this point if\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{\\theta \\to \\theta_0^{-}} \\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = \\pm\\infty \\qquad\\textrm{and}\\qquad \\lim\\limits_{\\theta \\to \\theta_0^{+}} \\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = \\pm\\infty.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're told that $y'\\left(\\dfrac\\pi2\\right)=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Substituting $\\theta = \\dfrac\\pi2$ into the expression for $x'(\\theta),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) & =-\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)+2\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)) \\\\  & =0\\cdot (0+2\\cdot 0\\cdot 1) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Computing the limit as $\\theta\\to\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right)^+,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{+}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{+}}\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )} \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{+}}\\left(\\frac{-\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta (1+{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}{-\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta (\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta )}\\right) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{+}}\\left(\\frac{-\\cancel{cot}\\theta \\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta (1+{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}{-\\cancel{cot}\\theta (\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta )}\\right) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{+}}\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta (1+{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }\\right) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{+}}\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the limit in the numerator, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{+}}(\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) \\\\  & =1+1 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the second hint, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{+}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{+}}\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{0}^{-}} \\\\  & =-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Using the calculations from part I, and the first hint, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{0}^{+}} \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the limits of our derivative are both infinite, there is a vertical tangent to the curve at $\\theta=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}.$ \n\n![](images/2d/2dc0665a0f979fd247d010a7b6d5891a758eff7e5357b881011ddf0d3f2f2002.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" "For a given polar curve, there is a vertical tangent at $\\theta = \\theta_0$ if\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = \\dfrac{y'(\\theta_0)}{x'(\\theta_0)}\n$$\n\nis infinite. This means that\n\n$$\ny'(\\theta_0) \\neq 0 \\qquad \\textrm{and}\\qquad x'(\\theta_0) = 0.\n$$\n\nHowever, if the curve is continuous at $\\theta_0$ yet the derivative takes the indeterminate form $\\dfrac00$ at this point, we say that the curve has a vertical tangent at this point if\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{\\theta \\to \\theta_0^{-}} \\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = \\pm\\infty \\qquad\\textrm{and}\\qquad \\lim\\limits_{\\theta \\to \\theta_0^{+}} \\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = \\pm\\infty.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're told that $y'\\left(\\dfrac\\pi2\\right)=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Substituting $\\theta = \\dfrac\\pi2$ into the expression for $x'(\\theta),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) & =-\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)+2\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)) \\\\  & =0\\cdot (0+2\\cdot 0\\cdot 1) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Computing the limit as $\\theta\\to\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right)^+,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{+}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\lim_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{+}}\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{+}}\\left(\\frac{-\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta (1+{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}{-\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta (\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta )}\\right) \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{+}}\\left(\\frac{-\\cancel{cot}\\theta \\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta (1+{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}{-\\cancel{cot}\\theta (\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta )}\\right) \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{+}}\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta (1+{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }\\right) \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{+}}\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the limit in the numerator, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{+}}(\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) \\\\  & =1+1 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the second hint, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{+}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\lim_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{+}}\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{0}^{-}} \\\\  & =-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Using the calculations from part I, and the first hint, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\lim_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{0}^{+}} \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the limits of our derivative are both infinite, there is a vertical tangent to the curve at $\\theta=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}.$ \n\n![](images/2d/2dc0665a0f979fd247d010a7b6d5891a758eff7e5357b881011ddf0d3f2f2002.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""] [:db/cas 17592186188093 :question/worked-solution "To estimate how many trees will be in the park when the workers are finished, we need to find an approximation for the sum\n\n$$\n243+158\n$$\n\nFirst, we round both numbers to the nearest hundred:\n\n$$\n243\\begin{vmatrix}+ & 158 \\\\ ↓ & ↓ \\\\ 200 & +\\end{vmatrix}200\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the sum ${\\color{blue}{200}} + {\\color{blue}{200}}$:\n$$\n\\displaystyle 2\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ + & 2\\end{vmatrix}0\\begin{vmatrix}0 \\\\ 4\\end{vmatrix}0 \\mid 0\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n243 + 158 \\approx 400\n$$\n\nTherefore, there will be approximately $400$ trees in the park when the workers are finished." "To estimate how many trees will be in the park when the workers are finished, we need to find an approximation for the sum\n\n$$\n243+158\n$$\n\nFirst, we round both numbers to the nearest hundred:\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{ccc}243 & + & 158 \\\\ ↓ &  & ↓ \\\\ {\\color{blue}{200}} & + & {\\color{blue}{200}}\\end{array}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the sum ${\\color{blue}{200}} + {\\color{blue}{200}}$:\n$$\n\\begin{array}{ccccc} &  & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}2\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}0\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}0\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} \\\\ \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}+\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}