m} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} &  & \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} 1 \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} 2 \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} 2 \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} 7 \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} 9 \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} 0 \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} 0 \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\\\ \\hline  &  &  &  & \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} 1 \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} 7 \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} 9 \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} 0 \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} 0 \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em}\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, Mike earns $\\$17,900$ more per year."] [:db/cas 17592186188166 :question/worked-solution "First, we line up our numbers (*ones* over *ones*, *tens* over *tens*, and *hundreds* over *hundreds*):\n\n$$\n5\\begin{vmatrix}7 & 8 \\\\ - & 2\\end{vmatrix}4 \\mid 6\n$$\n\nNext, we proceed by subtracting the numbers in each place value (from right to left):\n\n**Step 1.** Subtracting the ones,\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}8} - {\\color{blue}6} = {\\color{blue}2}{:}\n$$\n\n$$\n5\\begin{vmatrix}7 & 8 \\\\ - & 2\\end{vmatrix}4 \\mid 6; 2\n$$\n\n**Step 2.** Subtracting the tens,\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}7} - {\\color{blue}4} = {\\color{blue}3}{:}\n$$\n\n$$\n5\\begin{vmatrix}7 & 8 \\\\ - & 2\\end{vmatrix}4\\begin{vmatrix}6 \\\\ 3\\end{vmatrix}2\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Subtracting the hundreds,\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}5} - {\\color{blue}2} = {\\color{blue}3}{:}\n$$\n\n$$\n5\\begin{vmatrix}7 & 8 \\\\ - & 2\\end{vmatrix}4\\begin{vmatrix}6 \\\\ 3\\end{vmatrix}3 \\mid 2\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n578 - 246 = 332\n$$" "First, we line up our numbers (*ones* over *ones*, *tens* over *tens*, and *hundreds* over *hundreds*):\n\n$$\n5\\begin{vmatrix}7 & 8 \\\\ - & 2\\end{vmatrix}4 \\mid 6\n$$\n\nNext, we proceed by subtracting the numbers in each place value (from right to left):\n\n**Step 1.** Subtracting the ones,\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}8} - {\\color{blue}6} = {\\color{blue}2}{:}\n$$\n\n$$\n5\\begin{vmatrix}7 & 8 \\\\ - & 2\\end{vmatrix}4 \\mid 6; 2\n$$\n\n**Step 2.** Subtracting the tens,\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}7} - {\\color{blue}4} = {\\color{blue}3}{:}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{cccc} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}5\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}{\\color{blue}{7}}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}8\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} \\\\ \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}-\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}2\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}{\\color{blue}{4}}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}6\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} \\\\ \\hline  &  & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}{\\color{blue}{3}}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}2\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\end{array}\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Subtracting the hundreds,\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}5} - {\\color{blue}2} = {\\color{blue}3}{:}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{cccc} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}{\\color{blue}{5}}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}7\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}8\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} \\\\ \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}-\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}{\\color{blue}{2}}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}4\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}6\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} \\\\ \\hline  & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}{\\color{blue}{3}}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}3\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}2\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\end{array}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n578 - 246 = 332\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186188551 :question/problem "Consider the polar curve $r = \\sin^{2} θ$ for $θ ∈ (-\\frac{π}{2}, \\frac{π}{2})$. Given that\n$$\n\\displaystyle x^{′}(θ) = 2\\sin θ\\cos^{2} θ - \\sin^{3} θ, y^{′}(θ) = 3\\sin^{2} θ\\cos θ,\n$$\nand $y^{′}(θ) = 0$ at $θ = 0$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $x^{′}(0) = 0$\n2. $lim_(θ → 0)\\frac{dy}{dx} = 0$\n3. The curve has a horizontal tangent at $θ = 0$\n\n*Hint: It is not necessary to use L'Hopital's rule to evaluate the limit in part II.*\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." "Consider the polar curve $r = \\sin^{2} θ$ for $θ ∈ (-\\frac{π}{2}, \\frac{π}{2})$. Given that\n$$\n\\displaystyle x^{′}(θ) = 2\\sin θ\\cos^{2} θ - \\sin^{3} θ, y^{′}(θ) = 3\\sin^{2} θ\\cos θ,\n$$\nand $y^{′}(θ) = 0$ at $θ = 0$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $x^{′}(0) = 0$\n2. $\\lim_{θ \\to  0}\\frac{dy}{dx} = 0$\n3. The curve has a horizontal tangent at $θ = 0$\n\n*Hint: It is not necessary to use L'Hopital's rule to evaluate the limit in part II.*\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question."] [:db/cas 17592186188566 :question/worked-solution "When a polar curve is differentiable at a point ${\\theta }_{0}$, there is a horizontal tangent at ${\\theta }_{0}$ if\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})}{{x}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})}=0\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{x}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nHowever, if the curve is continuous at ${\\theta }_{0}$ yet the derivative ${y}^{′}(x)$ takes the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}$ at this point, we say that the curve has a horizontal tangent at this point if\n\n$$\n{lim}_{\\theta \\to {\\theta }_{0}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=0.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're told that ${x}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Substituting $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ into the expression for ${y}^{′}(\\theta ),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi ) \\\\  & =0-0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Computing the limit as $\\theta \\to \\frac{\\pi }{2}$ using L'Hopital's rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi /2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi /2}\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )} \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi /2}\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )}{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}\\right) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi /2}\\left(\\frac{{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ))}^{′}}{{(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ))}^{′}}\\right) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi /2}\\left(\\frac{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}{-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2})}{-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )}{-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )} \\\\  & =\\frac{-1-2\\cdot (-1)}{0+2\\cdot 0} \\\\  & =\\frac{-1+2}{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{0} \\\\  & \\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the limit in part II does *not* equal to $0,$ there curve does *not* have a horizontal tangent at $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ In fact, it is a vertical tangent.\n\n![](images/2c/2c1b42bf3a8c1b5f8755e02a162e1554904bbc432285cb8ba8c7b06181e1cc03.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "When a polar curve is differentiable at a point ${\\theta }_{0}$, there is a horizontal tangent at ${\\theta }_{0}$ if\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})}{{x}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})}=0\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{x}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nHowever, if the curve is continuous at ${\\theta }_{0}$ yet the derivative ${y}^{′}(x)$ takes the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}$ at this point, we say that the curve has a horizontal tangent at this point if\n\n$$\n\\lim_{\\theta \\to {\\theta }_{0}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=0.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're told that ${x}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Substituting $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ into the expression for ${y}^{′}(\\theta ),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi ) \\\\  & =0-0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Computing the limit as $\\theta \\to \\frac{\\pi }{2}$ using L'Hopital's rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\theta \\to \\pi /2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\lim_{\\theta \\to \\pi /2}\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\theta \\to \\pi /2}\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )}{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}\\right) \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\theta \\to \\pi /2}\\left(\\frac{{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ))}^{′}}{{(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ))}^{′}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\theta \\to \\pi /2}\\left(\\frac{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}{-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2})}{-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )}{-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )} \\\\  & =\\frac{-1-2\\cdot (-1)}{0+2\\cdot 0} \\\\  & =\\frac{-1+2}{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{0} \\\\  & \\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the limit in part II does *not* equal to $0,$ there curve does *not* have a horizontal tangent at $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ In fact, it is a vertical tangent.\n\n![](images/2c/2c1b42bf3a8c1b5f8755e02a162e1554904bbc432285cb8ba8c7b06181e1cc03.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""] [:db/cas 17592186188567 :question/problem "Consider the polar curve $r=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -1$ for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).$ Given that \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(\\theta )=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ),\\,{y}^{′}(\\theta )=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ),\n$$\n\nand ${x}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=0,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${y}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=0$\n\nII. ${lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\infty $\n\nIII. The curve has a vertical tangent at $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the following results.*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\to {0}^{-}\\,\\text{as}\\,\\theta \\to {\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)}^{-} \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\to {0}^{+}\\,\\text{as}\\,\\theta \\to {\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)}^{+}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Consider the polar curve $r=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -1$ for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).$ Given that \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(\\theta )=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ),\\,{y}^{′}(\\theta )=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ),\n$$\n\nand ${x}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=0,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${y}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=0$\n\nII. $\\lim_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\infty $\n\nIII. The curve has a vertical tangent at $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the following results.*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\to {0}^{-}\\,\\text{as}\\,\\theta \\to {\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)}^{-} \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\to {0}^{+}\\,\\text{as}\\,\\theta \\to {\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)}^{+}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186188567 :question/worked-solution "For a given polar curve, there is a vertical tangent at $\\theta ={\\theta }_{0}$ if\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})}{{x}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})}\n$$\n\nis infinite. This means that\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})\\ne 0\\,\\text{and}\\,{x}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})=0.\n$$\n\nHowever, if the curve is continuous at ${\\theta }_{0}$ yet the derivative takes the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}$ at this point, we say that the curve has a vertical tangent at this point if\n\n$$\n{lim}_{\\theta \\to {\\theta }_{0}^{-}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\pm \\infty \\,\\text{and}\\,{lim}_{\\theta \\to {\\theta }_{0}^{+}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\pm \\infty .\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're told that ${x}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Substituting $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ into the expression for ${y}^{′}(\\theta ),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi ) \\\\  & =-0+0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Computing the limit as $\\theta \\to {\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)}^{-}$ using L'Hopital's rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )} \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}\\left(\\frac{-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}\\right) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}\\left(\\frac{{(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ))}^{′}}{{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ))}^{′}}\\right) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the limit in the numerator, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi ) \\\\  & =1+2\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =1-2 \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the first hint, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{-1}{{0}^{-}} \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Using the calculations from part II, and the second hint, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{+}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{+}}\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{-1}{{0}^{+}} \\\\  & =-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the limits of our derivative are both infinite, there is a vertical tangent to the curve at $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\n![](images/51/515b4cbcb39ad80d4401adffd450aed9e222663c469f1cf1cf36cbd900b5eb4b.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" "For a given polar curve, there is a vertical tangent at $\\theta ={\\theta }_{0}$ if\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})}{{x}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})}\n$$\n\nis infinite. This means that\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})\\ne 0\\,\\text{and}\\,{x}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})=0.\n$$\n\nHowever, if the curve is continuous at ${\\theta }_{0}$ yet the derivative takes the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}$ at this point, we say that the curve has a vertical tangent at this point if\n\n$$\n\\lim_{\\theta \\to {\\theta }_{0}^{-}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\pm \\infty \\,\\text{and}\\,\\lim_{\\theta \\to {\\theta }_{0}^{+}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\pm \\infty .\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're told that ${x}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Substituting $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ into the expression for ${y}^{′}(\\theta ),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi ) \\\\  & =-0+0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Computing the limit as $\\theta \\to {\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)}^{-}$ using L'Hopital's rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\lim_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}\\left(\\frac{-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}\\right) \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}\\left(\\frac{{(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ))}^{′}}{{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ))}^{′}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the limit in the numerator, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi ) \\\\  & =1+2\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =1-2 \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the first hint, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\lim_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{-1}{{0}^{-}} \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Using the calculations from part II, and the second hint, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{+}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\lim_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{+}}\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{-1}{{0}^{+}} \\\\  & =-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the limits of our derivative are both infinite, there is a vertical tangent to the curve at $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\n![](images/51/515b4cbcb39ad80d4401adffd450aed9e222663c469f1cf1cf36cbd900b5eb4b.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""] [:db/cas 17592186188613 :question/problem "Consider the polar curve $r = θ^{2}\\cos θ$ for $θ ∈ (- π, π)$. Given that\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle x^{′}(θ) = 2θ\\cos^{2} (θ) - θ^{2}\\sin (2θ), y^{′}(θ) = θ\\sin (2θ) + θ^{2}\\cos (2θ),\n$$\nand $x^{′}(0) = 0$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $y^{′}(0) = 0$\n2. $lim_(θ → 0^{-})\\frac{dy}{dx} = ∞$\n3. The curve has a vertical tangent at $θ = 0$\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." "Consider the polar curve $r = θ^{2}\\cos θ$ for $θ ∈ (- π, π)$. Given that\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle x^{′}(θ) = 2θ\\cos^{2} (θ) - θ^{2}\\sin (2θ), y^{′}(θ) = θ\\sin (2θ) + θ^{2}\\cos (2θ),\n$$\nand $x^{′}(0) = 0$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $y^{′}(0) = 0$\n2. $\\lim_{θ \\to  0^{-}}\\frac{dy}{dx} = ∞$\n3. The curve has a vertical tangent at $θ = 0$\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question."] [:db/cas 17592186188634 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle {f}_{X | Y} ( x \\hspace{0.167em} | \\hspace{0.167em} 3 ) = \\left\\{  \\begin{array}{ll}{\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{3}} , & x = 12 \\\\ {\\displaystyle \\frac{2}{3}} , & x = 18 \\\\ 0 , & \\text{otherwise}\\end{array} \\right.\n$$\n\nSuppose $X$ and $Y$ are discrete random variables. Find the conditional variance $Var\\begin{bmatrix}X & Y = 3\\end{bmatrix}$ given that the conditional probability mass function $f_{X\\mid Y}(x \\mid 3)$ is shown above and the corresponding conditional expected value is $μ_{X \\mid 3} = 16$.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." "$$\n\\displaystyle {f}_{X | Y} ( x \\hspace{0.167em} | \\hspace{0.167em} 3 ) = \\left\\{  \\begin{array}{ll}{\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{3}} , & x = 12 \\\\ {\\displaystyle \\frac{2}{3}} , & x = 18 \\\\ 0 , & \\text{otherwise}\\end{array} \\right.\n$$\n\nSuppose $X$ and $Y$ are discrete random variables. Find the conditional variance $\\text{Var}[X\\hspace{0.167em}|\\hspace{0.167em}Y=3]$ given that the conditional probability mass function $f_{X\\mid Y}(x \\mid 3)$ is shown above and the corresponding conditional expected value is $μ_{X \\mid 3} = 16$.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question."] [:db/cas 17592186188655 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle {f}_{X | Y} ( x \\hspace{0.167em} | \\hspace{0.167em} 2 ) = \\left\\{  \\begin{array}{ll}{\\displaystyle \\frac{x}{4}} , & \\hspace{1em} 1 \\le  x \\le  3 \\\\ 0 , & \\hspace{1em} \\text{otherwise}\\end{array} \\right.\n$$\n\nLet $X$ and $Y$ be continuous random variables.\nFind the conditional variance $Var\\begin{bmatrix}X & Y = 2\\end{bmatrix}$ given that the conditional probability density function $f_{X\\mid Y}(x \\mid 2)$ is shown above and the corresponding expected value is $μ_{X \\mid 2} = \\frac{13}{6}$.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." "$$\n\\displaystyle {f}_{X | Y} ( x \\hspace{0.167em} | \\hspace{0.167em} 2 ) = \\left\\{  \\begin{array}{ll}{\\displaystyle \\frac{x}{4}} , & \\hspace{1em} 1 \\le  x \\le  3 \\\\ 0 , & \\hspace{1em} \\text{otherwise}\\end{array} \\right.\n$$\n\nLet $X$ and $Y$ be continuous random variables.\nFind the conditional variance $\\text{Var}[X\\hspace{0.167em}|\\hspace{0.167em}Y=2]$ given that the conditional probability density function $f_{X\\mid Y}(x \\mid 2)$ is shown above and the corresponding expected value is $μ_{X \\mid 2} = \\frac{13}{6}$.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question."] [:db/cas 17592186188719 :question/problem "$$\n\\begin{array}{r}\\displaystyle f ( x , y ) = \\left\\{  \\begin{array}{ll}\\displaystyle {\\displaystyle \\frac{24 y}{7 ( 1 + x )}} , & \\displaystyle 0 \\le  x \\le  1 , \\hspace{0.278em} {x}^{2} \\le  y \\le  1 \\\\ \\displaystyle 0 , & \\displaystyle \\text{otherwise}\\end{array} \\right.  \\hspace{1em} {f}_{X} ( x ) = \\left\\{  \\begin{array}{ll}\\displaystyle {\\displaystyle \\frac{12 ( 1 - {x}^{4} )}{7 ( 1 + x )}} , & \\displaystyle 0 \\le  x \\le  1 \\\\ \\displaystyle 0 , & \\displaystyle \\text{otherwise}\\end{array} \\right. \\end{array}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ and $Y$ be continuous random variables. Their joint probability density function and the marginal probability density function of $X$ are shown above. Find the conditional variance $Var\\begin{bmatrix}Y & X = 0\\end{bmatrix}$ given that the corresponding expected value is $μ_{Y \\mid 0} = \\frac{2}{3}$.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." "$$\n\\begin{array}{r}\\displaystyle f ( x , y ) = \\left\\{  \\begin{array}{ll}\\displaystyle {\\displaystyle \\frac{24 y}{7 ( 1 + x )}} , & \\displaystyle 0 \\le  x \\le  1 , \\hspace{0.278em} {x}^{2} \\le  y \\le  1 \\\\ \\displaystyle 0 , & \\displaystyle \\text{otherwise}\\end{array} \\right.  \\hspace{1em} {f}_{X} ( x ) = \\left\\{  \\begin{array}{ll}\\displaystyle {\\displaystyle \\frac{12 ( 1 - {x}^{4} )}{7 ( 1 + x )}} , & \\displaystyle 0 \\le  x \\le  1 \\\\ \\displaystyle 0 , & \\displaystyle \\text{otherwise}\\end{array} \\right. \\end{array}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ and $Y$ be continuous random variables. Their joint probability density function and the marginal probability density function of $X$ are shown above. Find the conditional variance $\\text{Var}[Y\\hspace{0.167em}|\\hspace{0.167em}X=0]$ given that the corresponding expected value is $μ_{Y \\mid 0} = \\frac{2}{3}$.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question."] [:db/cas 17592186188721 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle {f}_{Y | X} ( y \\hspace{0.167em} | \\hspace{0.167em} 2 ) = \\left\\{  \\begin{array}{ll}{\\displaystyle \\frac{3}{2}} {y}^{2} , & \\hspace{1em} - 1 \\le  y \\le  1 \\\\ 0 , & \\hspace{1em} \\text{otherwise}\\end{array} \\right.\n$$\n\nLet $X$ and $Y$ be continuous random variables.\nFind the conditional variance $Var\\begin{bmatrix}Y & X = 2\\end{bmatrix}$ given that the conditional probability density function $f_{Y\\mid X}(y \\mid 2)$ is shown above and the corresponding expected value is $μ_{Y \\mid 2} = 0$.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." "$$\n\\displaystyle {f}_{Y | X} ( y \\hspace{0.167em} | \\hspace{0.167em} 2 ) = \\left\\{  \\begin{array}{ll}{\\displaystyle \\frac{3}{2}} {y}^{2} , & \\hspace{1em} - 1 \\le  y \\le  1 \\\\ 0 , & \\hspace{1em} \\text{otherwise}\\end{array} \\right.\n$$\n\nLet $X$ and $Y$ be continuous random variables.\nFind the conditional variance $\\text{Var}[Y\\hspace{0.167em}|\\hspace{0.167em}X=2]$ given that the conditional probability density function $f_{Y\\mid X}(y \\mid 2)$ is shown above and the corresponding expected value is $μ_{Y \\mid 2} = 0$.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question."] [:db/cas 17592186189374 :tutorial/content "So, it seems that the curve $r = 1-\\sin\\theta$ has a vertical tangent at $\\theta = \\dfrac\\pi 2.$ However, to be sure of this, we need to check that the tangent lines' slopes grow to infinity as we approach $\\theta = \\dfrac \\pi 2.$ In other words, we need to check that\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{\\theta \\to (\\pi/2)^{-}} \\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = \\pm\\infty \\qquad\\textrm{and}\\qquad \\lim\\limits_{\\theta \\to (\\pi/2)^{+}} \\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = \\pm\\infty.\n$$\n\nLet's now verify that this is true:\n\nUsing techniques discussed in previous lessons, it's straightforward to show that\n\n$$\nx'(\\theta)=-\\sin\\theta-\\cos(2\\theta), \\qquad \ny'(\\theta)=\\cos\\theta - \\sin2\\theta.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi /2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi /2}\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}\\right) \\\\  & =-{lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi /2}\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe already know that substituting $\\theta = \\dfrac\\pi 2$ gives the indeterminate form $\\dfrac00,$ so evaluating this limit by direct substitution won't work. However, we can evaluate this limit using L'Hopital's rule.\n\n- Applying L'Hopital's rule to evaluate the left-sided limit, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-{lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ))}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}\\right) \\\\  & =-{lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}\\left(\\frac{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ))^{′}}{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ))^{′}}\\right) \\\\  & =-{lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}\\left(\\frac{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )}\\right) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the limit in the numerator, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi ) \\\\  & =1-2 \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the limit in the denominator, it can be shown that\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{\\theta \\to (\\pi/2)^-} \\left(\\cos\\theta - 2\\sin(2\\theta)\\right) = 0^-,\n$$\n\nwhere the notation $0^-$ means that the denominator approaches zero from the negative numbers.  \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{-1}{{0}^{-}} \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Using similar methods, it can be shown that the right-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{+}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{+}}\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{-1}{{0}^{+}} \\\\  & =-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the limits of our derivative are both infinite, we conclude that there is a vertical tangent to the curve at $\\theta=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}.$ \n\n![](images/ea/ea0a03e575d962bd8d69505e20166dcc60c77cfe933f30762bb81296ccf7a797.png)" "So, it seems that the curve $r = 1-\\sin\\theta$ has a vertical tangent at $\\theta = \\dfrac\\pi 2.$ However, to be sure of this, we need to check that the tangent lines' slopes grow to infinity as we approach $\\theta = \\dfrac \\pi 2.$ In other words, we need to check that\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{\\theta \\to (\\pi/2)^{-}} \\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = \\pm\\infty \\qquad\\textrm{and}\\qquad \\lim\\limits_{\\theta \\to (\\pi/2)^{+}} \\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = \\pm\\infty.\n$$\n\nLet's now verify that this is true:\n\nUsing techniques discussed in previous lessons, it's straightforward to show that\n\n$$\nx'(\\theta)=-\\sin\\theta-\\cos(2\\theta), \\qquad \ny'(\\theta)=\\cos\\theta - \\sin2\\theta.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\theta \\to \\pi /2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\lim_{\\theta \\to \\pi /2}\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\lim_{\\theta \\to \\pi /2}\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe already know that substituting $\\theta = \\dfrac\\pi 2$ gives the indeterminate form $\\dfrac00,$ so evaluating this limit by direct substitution won't work. However, we can evaluate this limit using L'Hopital's rule.\n\n- Applying L'Hopital's rule to evaluate the left-sided limit, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\lim_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ))}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\lim_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}\\left(\\frac{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ))^{′}}{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ))^{′}}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\lim_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}\\left(\\frac{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )}\\right) \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the limit in the numerator, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi ) \\\\  & =1-2 \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the limit in the denominator, it can be shown that\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{\\theta \\to (\\pi/2)^-} \\left(\\cos\\theta - 2\\sin(2\\theta)\\right) = 0^-,\n$$\n\nwhere the notation $0^-$ means that the denominator approaches zero from the negative numbers.  \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\lim_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{-1}{{0}^{-}} \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Using similar methods, it can be shown that the right-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{+}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\lim_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{+}}\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{-1}{{0}^{+}} \\\\  & =-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the limits of our derivative are both infinite, we conclude that there is a vertical tangent to the curve at $\\theta=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}.$ \n\n![](images/ea/ea0a03e575d962bd8d69505e20166dcc60c77cfe933f30762bb81296ccf7a797.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186189381 :tutorial/content "The first property to consider is how to recover the marginal CDFs of $X$ and $Y$ from the joint CDF. \n\nSuppose the joint CDF of two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{ll}( 1 - {e}^{- x} ) ( 1 - {e}^{- 2 y} ) , & x \\ge  0 , \\hspace{0.278em} y \\ge  0 \\\\ 0 , & \\text{otherwise} .\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\n\nLet's calculate $F_Y(y),$ the marginal CDF of $Y$ using the following property:\n\n$$\nF_Y(y) = F(\\infty,y) = \\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\infty} F(x,y)\n$$\n\nTherefore, for $y \\geq 0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}_{Y}(y) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }[(1-{e}^{-x})(1-{e}^{-2y})] \\\\  & =(1-{e}^{-2y}){lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(1-\\frac{1}{{e}^{x}}) \\\\  & =(1-{e}^{-2y}){lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(1-0) \\\\  & =1-{e}^{-2y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the full expression for the marginal CDF is as follows:\n\n$$\nF_Y(y)=\n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{ll}1 - {e}^{- 2 y} , & \\hspace{1em} y \\ge  0 \\\\ 0 , & \\hspace{1em} \\text{otherwise}\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\n\nLet's see another example." "The first property to consider is how to recover the marginal CDFs of $X$ and $Y$ from the joint CDF. \n\nSuppose the joint CDF of two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{ll}( 1 - {e}^{- x} ) ( 1 - {e}^{- 2 y} ) , & x \\ge  0 , \\hspace{0.278em} y \\ge  0 \\\\ 0 , & \\text{otherwise} .\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\n\nLet's calculate $F_Y(y),$ the marginal CDF of $Y$ using the following property:\n\n$$\nF_Y(y) = F(\\infty,y) = \\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\infty} F(x,y)\n$$\n\nTherefore, for $y \\geq 0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}_{Y}(y) & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }[(1-{e}^{-x})(1-{e}^{-2y})] \\\\  & =(1-{e}^{-2y})\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }(1-\\frac{1}{{e}^{x}}) \\\\  & =(1-{e}^{-2y})\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }(1-0) \\\\  & =1-{e}^{-2y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the full expression for the marginal CDF is as follows:\n\n$$\nF_Y(y)=\n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{ll}1 - {e}^{- 2 y} , & \\hspace{1em} y \\ge  0 \\\\ 0 , & \\hspace{1em} \\text{otherwise}\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\n\nLet's see another example."] [:db/cas 17592186189391 :tutorial/content "So, it seems that the curve $r = 1+\\cos\\theta$ has a horizontal tangent at $\\theta = \\pi.$ However, to be sure of this, we need to check that the tangent lines flatten out as we approach $\\theta = \\pi.$ In other words, we need to check that\n\n \n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{\\theta \\to \\pi} \\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = 0.\n$$\n\nLet's now verify that this is true:\n\nUsing techniques discussed in previous lessons, it's straightforward to show that\n\n$$\nx'(\\theta)=-\\sin\\theta(2\\cos\\theta+1), \\qquad \ny'(\\theta)=2\\cos^2\\theta +\\cos\\theta - 1.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi }\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi }\\left(\\frac{2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -1}{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +1)}\\right) \\\\  & =-{lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi }\\left(\\frac{2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +1)}\\right) \\\\  & =-{lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi }\\left(\\frac{2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -1}{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\right) \\\\  & =-{lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi }\\left(\\frac{2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe already know that substituting $\\theta = \\pi$ gives the indeterminate form $\\dfrac00,$ so evaluating this limit by direct substitution won't work. However, we can evaluate this limit using L'Hopital's rule.\n\nApplying L'Hopital's rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi }\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-{lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi }\\left(\\frac{2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\right) \\\\  & =-{lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi }\\left(\\frac{(2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -1)^{′}}{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{′}}\\right) \\\\  & =-{lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi }\\left(\\frac{-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\right) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi }\\left(\\frac{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi }{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\pi +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\cdot (-1)\\cdot (0)+0}{2\\cdot 1+(-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{1} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{\\theta \\to \\pi} \\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = 0\n$$\n\nwe conclude that our curve does indeed have a horizontal tangent at $\\theta = \\pi.$\n\n![](images/f1/f172dbe1e7fa97fa924feed4ee193a620b1224a985a4e406ef4e46afbdc0cb2a.png)" "So, it seems that the curve $r = 1+\\cos\\theta$ has a horizontal tangent at $\\theta = \\pi.$ However, to be sure of this, we need to check that the tangent lines flatten out as we approach $\\theta = \\pi.$ In other words, we need to check that\n\n \n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{\\theta \\to \\pi} \\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = 0.\n$$\n\nLet's now verify that this is true:\n\nUsing techniques discussed in previous lessons, it's straightforward to show that\n\n$$\nx'(\\theta)=-\\sin\\theta(2\\cos\\theta+1), \\qquad \ny'(\\theta)=2\\cos^2\\theta +\\cos\\theta - 1.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\theta \\to \\pi }\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\lim_{\\theta \\to \\pi }\\left(\\frac{2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -1}{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +1)}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\lim_{\\theta \\to \\pi }\\left(\\frac{2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +1)}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\lim_{\\theta \\to \\pi }\\left(\\frac{2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -1}{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\right) \\\\  & =-\\lim_{\\theta \\to \\pi }\\left(\\frac{2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe already know that substituting $\\theta = \\pi$ gives the indeterminate form $\\dfrac00,$ so evaluating this limit by direct substitution won't work. However, we can evaluate this limit using L'Hopital's rule.\n\nApplying L'Hopital's rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\theta \\to \\pi }\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\lim_{\\theta \\to \\pi }\\left(\\frac{2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\right) \\\\  & =-\\lim_{\\theta \\to \\pi }\\left(\\frac{(2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -1)^{′}}{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{′}}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\lim_{\\theta \\to \\pi }\\left(\\frac{-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\right) \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\theta \\to \\pi }\\left(\\frac{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi }{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\pi +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\cdot (-1)\\cdot (0)+0}{2\\cdot 1+(-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{1} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{\\theta \\to \\pi} \\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = 0\n$$\n\nwe conclude that our curve does indeed have a horizontal tangent at $\\theta = \\pi.$\n\n![](images/f1/f172dbe1e7fa97fa924feed4ee193a620b1224a985a4e406ef4e46afbdc0cb2a.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186189446 :tutorial/content "Adding large numbers without using a calculator takes some effort. However, in some circumstances, it is sufficient to find an **estimate** for a particular sum. Computing an estimate is usually faster and easier than solving the original problem.\n\nTo estimate the answer to an addition problem, we round the numbers *first* and *then* add them.\n\nTo illustrate this, let's find an estimate for the value of\n\n$$\n382+567\n$$\n\nLet's start by rounding both numbers to the nearest ten:\n\n$$\n382\\begin{vmatrix}+ & 567 \\\\ ↓ & ↓ \\\\ 380 & +\\end{vmatrix}570\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the sum ${\\color{blue}{380}} + {\\color{blue}{570}}$:\n\n$$\n1; 3\\begin{vmatrix}8 & 0 \\\\ + & 5\\end{vmatrix}7\\begin{vmatrix}0 \\\\ 9\\end{vmatrix}5 \\mid 0\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n382+567 \\approx 950\n$$\n\nThe symbol\n\n$$\n\\approx\n$$\n\nmeans \"is approximately equal to.\"\n\nThe estimate we've obtained is pretty close to the actual value of the sum. In fact,\n\n$$\n382+567=949\n$$\n\nIn this example, we rounded to the nearest ten. In the next example, we'll estimate a sum by rounding both numbers to the nearest hundred." "Adding large numbers without using a calculator takes some effort. However, in some circumstances, it is sufficient to find an **estimate** for a particular sum. Computing an estimate is usually faster and easier than solving the original problem.\n\nTo estimate the answer to an addition problem, we round the numbers *first* and *then* add them.\n\nTo illustrate this, let's find an estimate for the value of\n\n$$\n382+567\n$$\n\nLet's start by rounding both numbers to the nearest ten:\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{ccc}382 & + & 567 \\\\ ↓ &  & ↓ \\\\ {\\color{blue}{380}} & + & {\\color{blue}{570}}\\end{array}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the sum ${\\color{blue}{380}} + {\\color{blue}{570}}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{ccccc} &  & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}{\\color{blue}{1\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}}} &  &  \\\\  &  & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}3\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}8\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}0\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} \\\\ \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}+\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} &  & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}5\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}7\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}0\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} \\\\ \\hline  &  & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}9\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}5\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}0\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\end{array}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n382+567 \\approx 950\n$$\n\nThe symbol\n\n$$\n\\approx\n$$\n\nmeans \"is approximately equal to.\"\n\nThe estimate we've obtained is pretty close to the actual value of the sum. In fact,\n\n$$\n382+567=949\n$$\n\nIn this example, we rounded to the nearest ten. In the next example, we'll estimate a sum by rounding both numbers to the nearest hundred."] [:db/cas 17592186189448 :tutorial/content "Let's take a look at the following addition problem:\n\n$$\n132 + 46\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're adding a two-digit number to a three-digit number. To do this, we use the standard algorithm.\n\nThe first step is to write the numbers in the usual way, where ones are aligned over ones, tens over tens, and hundreds over hundreds:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ + & 4 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNext, we proceed by adding the numbers in each place value (from right to left) starting from the ones:\n\n**Step 1.** Adding the ones, we get\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}2} + {\\color{blue}6} = {\\color{blue}8}{:}\n$$\n\n$$\n1\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ + & 4\\end{vmatrix}6; 8\n$$\n\n**Step 2.** Adding the tens, we get\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}3} + {\\color{blue}4} = {\\color{blue}7}{:}\n$$\n\n$$\n1\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ + & 4\\end{vmatrix}6; 7 \\mid 8\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Notice that $46$ has no hundred digit. Therefore, we place a $\\color{red}0$ in its hundreds place:\n\n$$\n1\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ + & 0\\end{vmatrix}4\\begin{vmatrix}6 \\\\ 7\\end{vmatrix}8\n$$\n\nThen, adding the hundreds, we get\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}1} + {\\color{blue}0} = {\\color{blue}1}{:}\n$$\n\n$$\n1\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ + & 0\\end{vmatrix}4\\begin{vmatrix}6 \\\\ 1\\end{vmatrix}7 \\mid 8\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n132 + 46 = 178\n$$" "Let's take a look at the following addition problem:\n\n$$\n132 + 46\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're adding a two-digit number to a three-digit number. To do this, we use the standard algorithm.\n\nThe first step is to write the numbers in the usual way, where ones are aligned over ones, tens over tens, and hundreds over hundreds:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ + & 4 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNext, we proceed by adding the numbers in each place value (from right to left) starting from the ones:\n\n**Step 1.** Adding the ones, we get\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}2} + {\\color{blue}6} = {\\color{blue}8}{:}\n$$\n\n$$\n1\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ + & 4\\end{vmatrix}6; 8\n$$\n\n**Step 2.** Adding the tens, we get\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}3} + {\\color{blue}4} = {\\color{blue}7}{:}\n$$\n\n$$\n1\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ + & 4\\end{vmatrix}6; 7 \\mid 8\n$$\n\n**St