{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\end{array}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n4,745,136 + 25,985 = 4,771,121\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186189459 :tutorial/content "Drawing place value charts each time we want to add a pair of numbers isn't very convenient. For this reason, we usually follow a simplified process called the **standard algorithm**.\n\nLet's once again consider the following sum:\n\n$$\n24+35\n$$\n\nFirst, we line up our numbers as follows: (ones over ones, and tens over tens):\n\n$$\n2\\begin{vmatrix}4 \\\\ +\\end{vmatrix}3 \\mid 5\n$$\n\nNotice that we've also drawn a horizontal line. This is where we'll write our answer.\n\nNext, we proceed by adding the numbers in each place value (from right to left):\n\n**Step 1.** Adding the ones, we get\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}4} + {\\color{blue}5} = {\\color{blue}9}{:}\n$$\n\n$$\n2\\begin{vmatrix}4 \\\\ +\\end{vmatrix}3 \\mid 5; 9\n$$\n\n**Step 2.** Adding the tens, we get\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}2} + {\\color{blue}3} = {\\color{blue}5}{:}\n$$\n\n$$\n2\\begin{vmatrix}4 \\\\ +\\end{vmatrix}3\\begin{vmatrix}5 \\\\ 5\\end{vmatrix}9\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n24 + 35 = 59\n$$" "Drawing place value charts each time we want to add a pair of numbers isn't very convenient. For this reason, we usually follow a simplified process called the **standard algorithm**.\n\nLet's once again consider the following sum:\n\n$$\n24+35\n$$\n\nFirst, we line up our numbers as follows: (ones over ones, and tens over tens):\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{cccc} &  & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}2\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}4\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} \\\\ \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}+\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} &  & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}3\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}5\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} \\\\ \\hline  &  & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\end{array}\n$$\n\nNotice that we've also drawn a horizontal line. This is where we'll write our answer.\n\nNext, we proceed by adding the numbers in each place value (from right to left):\n\n**Step 1.** Adding the ones, we get\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}4} + {\\color{blue}5} = {\\color{blue}9}{:}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{cccc} &  & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}2\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}{\\color{blue}{4}}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} \\\\ \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}+\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} &  & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}3\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}{\\color{blue}{5}}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} \\\\ \\hline  &  & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}{\\color{blue}{9}}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\end{array}\n$$\n\n**Step 2.** Adding the tens, we get\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}2} + {\\color{blue}3} = {\\color{blue}5}{:}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{cccc} &  & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}{\\color{blue}{2}}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}4\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} \\\\ \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}+\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} &  & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}{\\color{blue}{3}}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}5\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} \\\\ \\hline  &  & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}{\\color{blue}{5}}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}9\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\end{array}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n24 + 35 = 59\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186189461 :tutorial/content "Once again, we consider the following sum:\n\n$$\n37+26\n$$\n\nTo use the standard algorithm, we first line up our numbers (ones over ones, and tens over tens):\n\n$$\n3\\begin{vmatrix}7 \\\\ +\\end{vmatrix}2 \\mid 6\n$$\n\nNext, we proceed by adding the numbers in each place value (from right to left):\n\n**Step 1.** Adding the ones, we get\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}7} + {\\color{blue}6} = {\\color{red}1}{\\color{blue}3}\n$$\n\nWe write ${\\color{blue}3}$ below the *ones* and carry ${\\color{red}1}$ to the *tens*:\n\n$$\n1; 3\\begin{vmatrix}7 \\\\ +\\end{vmatrix}2 \\mid 6; 3\n$$\n\n**Step 2.** Adding the tens, we get\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}1} + {\\color{blue}3} + {\\color{blue}2} = {\\color{blue}6}{:}\n$$\n\n$$\n1; 3\\begin{vmatrix}7 \\\\ +\\end{vmatrix}2\\begin{vmatrix}6 \\\\ 6\\end{vmatrix}3\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n37 + 26 = 63\n$$" "Once again, we consider the following sum:\n\n$$\n37+26\n$$\n\nTo use the standard algorithm, we first line up our numbers (ones over ones, and tens over tens):\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{cccc} &  & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}3\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}7\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} \\\\ \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}+\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} &  & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}2\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}6\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} \\\\ \\hline  &  & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\end{array}\n$$\n\nNext, we proceed by adding the numbers in each place value (from right to left):\n\n**Step 1.** Adding the ones, we get\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}7} + {\\color{blue}6} = {\\color{red}1}{\\color{blue}3}\n$$\n\nWe write ${\\color{blue}3}$ below the *ones* and carry ${\\color{red}1}$ to the *tens*:\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{cccc} &  & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}{\\color{red}{1}}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} &  \\\\  &  & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}3\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}{\\color{blue}{7}}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} \\\\ \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}+\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} &  & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}2\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}{\\color{blue}{6}}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} \\\\ \\hline  &  & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}{\\color{blue}{3}}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\end{array}\n$$\n\n**Step 2.** Adding the tens, we get\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}1} + {\\color{blue}3} + {\\color{blue}2} = {\\color{blue}6}{:}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{cccc} &  & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}{\\color{blue}{1}}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} &  \\\\  &  & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}{\\color{blue}{3}}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}7\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} \\\\ \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}+\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} &  & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}{\\color{blue}{2}}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}6\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} \\\\ \\hline  &  & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}{\\color{blue}{6}}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}3\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\end{array}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n37 + 26 = 63\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186189462 :tutorial/content "Subtracting large numbers without using a calculator takes some effort. However, in some circumstances, it is sufficient to find an **estimate** for a particular difference. Computing an estimate is usually faster and easier than solving the original problem.\n\nTo estimate the answer to a subtraction problem, we round the numbers *first* and *then* subtract them.\n\nTo illustrate this, let's find an estimate for the value of\n\n$$\n481 - 73\n$$\n\nLet's start by rounding both numbers to the nearest ten:\n\n$$\n481\\begin{vmatrix}- & 73 \\\\ ↓ & ↓ \\\\ 480 & -\\end{vmatrix}70\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the difference ${\\color{blue}{480}} - {\\color{blue}{70}}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 8 & 0 \\\\ - & 7 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n481 - 73 \\approx 410\n$$\n\nThe symbol\n\n$$\n\\approx\n$$\n\nmeans \"is approximately equal to.\"\n\nThe estimate we've obtained is pretty close to the actual value of the difference. In fact,\n\n$$\n481 - 73=408\n$$\n\nIn this example, we rounded to the nearest ten. In the next example, we'll estimate a difference by rounding both numbers to the nearest hundred." "Subtracting large numbers without using a calculator takes some effort. However, in some circumstances, it is sufficient to find an **estimate** for a particular difference. Computing an estimate is usually faster and easier than solving the original problem.\n\nTo estimate the answer to a subtraction problem, we round the numbers *first* and *then* subtract them.\n\nTo illustrate this, let's find an estimate for the value of\n\n$$\n481 - 73\n$$\n\nLet's start by rounding both numbers to the nearest ten:\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{ccc}481 & - & 73 \\\\ ↓ &  & ↓ \\\\ {\\color{blue}{480}} & - & {\\color{blue}{70}}\\end{array}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the difference ${\\color{blue}{480}} - {\\color{blue}{70}}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 8 & 0 \\\\ - & 7 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n481 - 73 \\approx 410\n$$\n\nThe symbol\n\n$$\n\\approx\n$$\n\nmeans \"is approximately equal to.\"\n\nThe estimate we've obtained is pretty close to the actual value of the difference. In fact,\n\n$$\n481 - 73=408\n$$\n\nIn this example, we rounded to the nearest ten. In the next example, we'll estimate a difference by rounding both numbers to the nearest hundred."] [:db/cas 17592186189469 :tutorial/content "We can use the standard algorithm to add multiple multi-digit whole numbers.\n\nFor example, let's use the standard algorithm to compute the following sum:\n\n$$\n801 + 523 + 198\n$$\n\nFirst, we line up our numbers:\n\n$$\n8\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ + & 5\\end{vmatrix}2\\begin{vmatrix}3 \\\\ +\\end{vmatrix}1 \\mid 9 \\mid 8\n$$\n\nNext, we add the numbers in each place value (from right to left). However, this time, we need to sum three numbers in each place value, as well as any carried digits:\n\n$$\n1\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 8 & 0\\end{vmatrix}1; +\\begin{vmatrix}5 & 2 & 3 \\\\ + & 1 & 9\\end{vmatrix}8; 1\\begin{vmatrix}5 & 2\\end{vmatrix}2\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n801 + 523 + 198 = 1,522\n$$" "We can use the standard algorithm to add multiple multi-digit whole numbers.\n\nFor example, let's use the standard algorithm to compute the following sum:\n\n$$\n801 + 523 + 198\n$$\n\nFirst, we line up our numbers:\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{ccccc} &  & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-