frac{3^{n + 1} + 1}{3^{n} - 2} - 3 \\mid < ε$.\n\nSolving this inequality for $n$, we get\n$>$ {{field-1}} {{field-2}}\n\nTherefore, we choose $N$\n{{field-3}} {{field-4}}." "Using the formal definition of a limit, prove that\n\n$\\lim_{n \\to  \\infty}\\frac{3^{n + 1} + 1}{3^{n} - 2} = 3$.\n\nWe need to show that for any real $ε > 0$, there exists a natural number $N$ such that, for every natural number $n \\ge N$, we have\n\n$\\mid \\frac{3^{n + 1} + 1}{3^{n} - 2} - 3 \\mid < ε$.\n\nSolving this inequality for $n$, we get\n$>$ {{field-1}} {{field-2}}\n\nTherefore, we choose $N$\n{{field-3}} {{field-4}}."] [:db/cas 17592186199548 :tutorial/content "Suppose that $a_n$ is a sequence defined for\n\n$$\nn\\geq 1\n$$\n\nWe know that the *limit* of a sequence (if it exists) is the unique number $L$ such that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n \\to \\infty} a_n = L\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this statement means that the distance between $a_n$ and $L$ can be as small as we want, provided that $n$ is large enough.\n\nWhile intuition is important, none of this is very precise. What does \"the distance between $a_n$ and $L$ can be as small as we want\" mean? And what about \"provided that $n$ is large enough\"? Can we quantify these ideas more precisely?\n\nWe need a precise mathematical definition of what's meant by *the limit of a sequence.* This lesson aims to construct a suitable definition in the case where $L=0$.\n\nWe start with a definition.\n\n> *A **null sequence** is a sequence that converges to zero.*\n\nFor example, the sequence\n\n$$\na_n = \\dfrac{1}{1+n^2}, \\qquad n\\geq 1\n$$\n\nis a null sequence since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(n → ∞)a_{n} &= lim_(n → ∞)\\left(\\frac{1}{1 + n^{2}}\\right) = 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the sequence\n\n$$\nb_n = n+1, \\qquad n\\geq 1\n$$\n\nis *not* a null sequence since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(n → ∞)b_{n} &= lim_(n → ∞)(n + 1) \\\\\n&= ∞ \\\\\n&\\ne 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Suppose that $a_n$ is a sequence defined for\n\n$$\nn\\geq 1\n$$\n\nWe know that the *limit* of a sequence (if it exists) is the unique number $L$ such that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n \\to \\infty} a_n = L\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this statement means that the distance between $a_n$ and $L$ can be as small as we want, provided that $n$ is large enough.\n\nWhile intuition is important, none of this is very precise. What does \"the distance between $a_n$ and $L$ can be as small as we want\" mean? And what about \"provided that $n$ is large enough\"? Can we quantify these ideas more precisely?\n\nWe need a precise mathematical definition of what's meant by *the limit of a sequence.* This lesson aims to construct a suitable definition in the case where $L=0$.\n\nWe start with a definition.\n\n> *A **null sequence** is a sequence that converges to zero.*\n\nFor example, the sequence\n\n$$\na_n = \\dfrac{1}{1+n^2}, \\qquad n\\geq 1\n$$\n\nis a null sequence since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\lim_{n \\to  \\infty}a_{n} &= \\lim_{n \\to  \\infty}\\left(\\frac{1}{1 + n^{2}}\\right) = 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the sequence\n\n$$\nb_n = n+1, \\qquad n\\geq 1\n$$\n\nis *not* a null sequence since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\lim_{n \\to  \\infty}b_{n} &= \\lim_{n \\to  \\infty}(n + 1) \\\\\n&= ∞ \\\\\n&\\ne 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186199554 :tutorial/content "A set $A$ is called **countably infinite** (or **denumerable**) if it has the same cardinality as the set of natural numbers.\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}A & = & \\mathbb{N}\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\nIn other words, $A$ is countably infinite if there exists a bijection between $A$ and the set of natural numbers $\\mathbb{N}$. Elements of a countably infinite set can be listed as a *sequence* indexed by natural numbers:\n\n$$\na_1, \\: a_2, \\: \\ldots, \\: a_n, \\: \\ldots\n$$\n\nA set is **countable** if it is either finite or countably infinite.\n\nThe cardinality of the natural numbers (i.e., all countably infinite sets) is denoted by $\\aleph_0$, which reads as \"aleph-zero\" or \"aleph-null.\"\n\n$$\n\\mid \\mathbb{N} \\mid = \\aleph_0\n$$\n\nAleph-null is the smallest infinite cardinality in the following sense:\n\n> *Any subset of a countably infinite set is either finite or countably infinite.*\n\nConsequently, any infinite subset of a countably infinite set is countably infinite.\n\nFinally, a set is called **uncountable** if it's infinite but not countable (i.e., there is no bijection between $\\mathbb{N}$ and this set). We will see examples of uncountable sets in future lessons." "A set $A$ is called **countably infinite** (or **denumerable**) if it has the same cardinality as the set of natural numbers.\n\n$$\n|A| = |\\mathbb{N}|\n$$\n\nIn other words, $A$ is countably infinite if there exists a bijection between $A$ and the set of natural numbers $\\mathbb{N}$. Elements of a countably infinite set can be listed as a *sequence* indexed by natural numbers:\n\n$$\na_1, \\: a_2, \\: \\ldots, \\: a_n, \\: \\ldots\n$$\n\nA set is **countable** if it is either finite or countably infinite.\n\nThe cardinality of the natural numbers (i.e., all countably infinite sets) is denoted by $\\aleph_0$, which reads as \"aleph-zero\" or \"aleph-null.\"\n\n$$\n\\mid \\mathbb{N} \\mid = \\aleph_0\n$$\n\nAleph-null is the smallest infinite cardinality in the following sense:\n\n> *Any subset of a countably infinite set is either finite or countably infinite.*\n\nConsequently, any infinite subset of a countably infinite set is countably infinite.\n\nFinally, a set is called **uncountable** if it's infinite but not countable (i.e., there is no bijection between $\\mathbb{N}$ and this set). We will see examples of uncountable sets in future lessons."] [:db/cas 17592186199563 :tutorial/content "Consider the following sequence:\n\n$$\na_n = 2\\sqrt{n}, \\qquad n \\geq 1\n$$\n\nNote that this sequence is divergent:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to\\infty}\\left(2\\sqrt n\\right) = \\infty\n$$\n\nGiven that $M > 0$, let's find a natural number $N(M)$ such that for all\n\n$$\nn \\geq N\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\na_n > M\n$$\n\nFirst, we solve the inequality $a_n > M$ for $n{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2\\sqrt{n} & > M \\\\ \\sqrt{n} & > \\frac{M}{2} \\\\ n & > \\left(\\frac{M}{2}\\right)^{2} \\\\ n & > \\frac{M^{2}}{4}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSo, we let $N$ be the smallest natural number such that\n\n$$\nN > \\dfrac{M^2}{4}\n$$\n\nAnd that's it! Given any $M >0$, no matter how large, $a_n$ is guaranteed to be larger than $M$ provided that\n\n$$\nn\\geq N\n$$\n\nwhere $N$ is the smallest natural number larger than\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{M^2}{4}\n$$" "Consider the following sequence:\n\n$$\na_n = 2\\sqrt{n}, \\qquad n \\geq 1\n$$\n\nNote that this sequence is divergent:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to\\infty}\\left(2\\sqrt n\\right) = \\infty\n$$\n\nGiven that $M > 0$, let's find a natural number $N(M)$ such that for all\n\n$$\nn \\geq N\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\na_n > M\n$$\n\nFirst, we solve the inequality $a_n > M$ for $n{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{n} & >M \\\\ \\sqrt{n} & >\\frac{M}{2} \\\\ n & >{\\left(\\frac{M}{2}\\right)}^{2} \\\\ n & >\\frac{{M}^{2}}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we let $N$ be the smallest natural number such that\n\n$$\nN > \\dfrac{M^2}{4}\n$$\n\nAnd that's it! Given any $M >0$, no matter how large, $a_n$ is guaranteed to be larger than $M$ provided that\n\n$$\nn\\geq N\n$$\n\nwhere $N$ is the smallest natural number larger than\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{M^2}{4}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186199566 :tutorial/content "Recall that the power set of a finite $X$, denoted $\\mathcal{P}(X)$, contains every subset of $S$, including $X$ itself and the empty set.\n\nWe've already seen that if $X$ is *finite*, the cardinality of $\\mathcal{P}(X)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\big\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathcal{P}(X)\\big & = 2^{\\mid X\\end{vmatrix}}\n$$\n\nWe know from previous lessons that the cardinality of the set of natural numbers is denoted $\\aleph_0$, so\n\n$$\n\\big \\mid \\mathbb N\\big \\mid = \\aleph_0\n$$\n\nThe power set of $\\mathbb N$, denoted $\\mathcal P(\\mathbb N)$, is an infinite set that contains every subset of the natural numbers. Taking inspiration from the above result, we will define the cardinality of the power set of the natural numbers as follows:\n\n$$\n\\big \\mid \\mathcal P(\\mathbb N)\\big \\mid = 2^{\\aleph_0}\n$$\n\nLoosely speaking, $\\mathcal P (\\mathbb N)$ is \"larger\" than $\\mathbb N$. Can we quantify this more precisely? Indeed, we can. In fact, the cardinality of $\\mathcal P(\\mathbb{N})$ is deeply connected to that of $(0,1)$, as given by the following result:\n\n> *The power set of $\\mathbb{N}$ has the same cardinality as the interval $(0,1)$, i.e.,*\n> $\\big\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathcal{P}(\\mathbb{N}) \\big & = 2^{\\aleph_0} = \\big & (0,1) \\big\\end{vmatrix} = \\mathfrak{c}$.\n\nThis means that there is a bijection that maps every real number $a$ from $(0,1)$ to a subset $A$ of natural numbers. We'll prove this result later in the lesson.\n\n![](images/33/33fc45904c0eb6ef0da7cfd0947577ca5f2885c9bb1f1a79ff80b16c8ee9b5ad.png)\n\nMore generally, for *any* countably infinite set $X$, we have\n\n$$\n\\big \\mid \\mathcal{P}(X) \\big \\mid = \\mathfrak{c}\n$$\n\nFor example,\n\n$$\n\\big\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathcal{P}(\\mathbb N) \\big & = \\big & \\mathcal{P}(\\mathbb Z) \\big & = \\big & \\mathcal{P}(\\mathbb Q) \\big\\end{vmatrix} = \\mathfrak{c}\n$$" "Recall that the power set of a finite $X$, denoted $\\mathcal{P}(X)$, contains every subset of $S$, including $X$ itself and the empty set.\n\nWe've already seen that if $X$ is *finite*, the cardinality of $\\mathcal{P}(X)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\big|\\mathcal{P}(X)\\big| = 2^{|X|}.\n$$\n\nWe know from previous lessons that the cardinality of the set of natural numbers is denoted $\\aleph_0$, so\n\n$$\n\\big \\mid \\mathbb N\\big \\mid = \\aleph_0\n$$\n\nThe power set of $\\mathbb N$, denoted $\\mathcal P(\\mathbb N)$, is an infinite set that contains every subset of the natural numbers. Taking inspiration from the above result, we will define the cardinality of the power set of the natural numbers as follows:\n\n$$\n\\big \\mid \\mathcal P(\\mathbb N)\\big \\mid = 2^{\\aleph_0}\n$$\n\nLoosely speaking, $\\mathcal P (\\mathbb N)$ is \"larger\" than $\\mathbb N$. Can we quantify this more precisely? Indeed, we can. In fact, the cardinality of $\\mathcal P(\\mathbb{N})$ is deeply connected to that of $(0,1)$, as given by the following result:\n\n> *The power set of $\\mathbb{N}$ has the same cardinality as the interval $(0,1)$, i.e.,*\n> $\\big| \\mathcal{P}(\\mathbb{N}) \\big| = 2^{\\aleph_0} = \\big| (0,1) \\big| = \\mathfrak{c}.$.\n\nThis means that there is a bijection that maps every real number $a$ from $(0,1)$ to a subset $A$ of natural numbers. We'll prove this result later in the lesson.\n\n![](images/33/33fc45904c0eb6ef0da7cfd0947577ca5f2885c9bb1f1a79ff80b16c8ee9b5ad.png)\n\nMore generally, for *any* countably infinite set $X$, we have\n\n$$\n\\big \\mid \\mathcal{P}(X) \\big \\mid = \\mathfrak{c}\n$$\n\nFor example,\n\n$$\n\\big| \\mathcal{P}(\\mathbb N) \\big| = \\big| \\mathcal{P}(\\mathbb Z) \\big| = \\big| \\mathcal{P}(\\mathbb Q) \\big| = \\mathfrak{c}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186199570 :tutorial/content "So, is the set of all infinite binary sequences countable?\n\nTo answer this, consider the set $\\{0,1 \\}^{\\mathbb{N}}$ of all infinite binary sequences over $\\{0,1 \\}$. Then, we have the following theorem:\n\n> *The set of all infinite binary sequences has the cardinality of the continuum:* $\\big\\begin{vmatrix}\\{0,1 \\}^{\\mathbb{N}} \\big & = \\big & \\mathcal{P}(\\mathbb{N}) \\big\\end{vmatrix} = \\mathfrak{c}$\n\nThis result implies that the set of all infinite binary sequences is *uncountable!*\n\nTo prove this, consider the function\n\n$$\nf: \\mathcal P(\\mathbb N)\\to \\{0,1 \\}^{\\mathbb{N}}\n$$\n\nthat, given $A \\subseteq \\mathbb{N}$, maps $A$ to the sequence $(s_1, s_2, s_3, \\ldots) \\in \\{0,1 \\}^{\\mathbb{N}}$, where\n\n$$\ns_n = \n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{ll}1 , & \\text{if} \\hspace{0.222em} n \\in  A \\\\ 0 , & \\text{if} \\hspace{0.222em} n \\notin  A .\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\n\nFor example, if\n\n$$\nA = \\{2, 4, 5 \\}\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\nf(A) = (0, 1, 0, 1, 1, 0, \\ldots)\n$$\n\nwhere all terms after the $5$th equal zero.\n\nThe function $f$ is a bijection from $\\mathcal{P}(\\mathbb{N})$ onto $\\{0,1 \\}^{\\mathbb{N}}$. To show this, we need to show that $f$ is injective and surjective:\n\n- First, we prove $f$ is injective. Assume that the subsets $A\\subseteq \\mathbb N$ and $B\\subseteq \\mathbb N$ are distinct. Then, one of them should contain an element that the other doesn't contain. Without loss of generality, assume that $s \\in A$ while $s \\notin B$ for some natural number $s$. Then, the sequence $f(A)$ has $1$ as its $s$th term whereas $f(B)$ has $0$. In other words, distinct subsets give distinct sequences. So, $f$ is injective.\n- On the other hand, $f$ is surjective since for any sequence from $\\{0,1 \\}^{\\mathbb{N}}$, we can reconstruct the corresponding subset in $\\mathbb{N}$. For example, the sequence $(0, 1, 1, 1, 0, 0, \\ldots)$, where all terms after the $4$th equal zero, corresponds to $\\{2,3,4\\}$. In other words, each sequence in $\\{0,1\\}^\\mathbb{N}$ is paired with an element of $\\mathcal P (\\mathbb N)$. So, $f$ is surjective.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\big\\begin{vmatrix}\\{0,1 \\}^{\\mathbb{N}} \\big & = \\big & \\mathcal{P}(\\mathbb{N}) \\big\\end{vmatrix} = \\mathfrak{c}\n$$" "So, is the set of all infinite binary sequences countable?\n\nTo answer this, consider the set $\\{0,1 \\}^{\\mathbb{N}}$ of all infinite binary sequences over $\\{0,1 \\}$. Then, we have the following theorem:\n\n> *The set of all infinite binary sequences has the cardinality of the continuum:* $\\big| \\{ 0,1 \\}^{\\mathbb{N}} \\big| = \\big| \\mathcal{P}(\\mathbb{N}) \\big| = \\mathfrak{c}$\n\nThis result implies that the set of all infinite binary sequences is *uncountable!*\n\nTo prove this, consider the function\n\n$$\nf: \\mathcal P(\\mathbb N)\\to \\{0,1 \\}^{\\mathbb{N}}\n$$\n\nthat, given $A \\subseteq \\mathbb{N}$, maps $A$ to the sequence $(s_1, s_2, s_3, \\ldots) \\in \\{0,1 \\}^{\\mathbb{N}}$, where\n\n$$\ns_n = \n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{ll}1 , & \\text{if} \\hspace{0.222em} n \\in  A \\\\ 0 , & \\text{if} \\hspace{0.222em} n \\notin  A .\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\n\nFor example, if\n\n$$\nA = \\{2, 4, 5 \\}\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\nf(A) = (0, 1, 0, 1, 1, 0, \\ldots)\n$$\n\nwhere all terms after the $5$th equal zero.\n\nThe function $f$ is a bijection from $\\mathcal{P}(\\mathbb{N})$ onto $\\{0,1 \\}^{\\mathbb{N}}$. To show this, we need to show that $f$ is injective and surjective:\n\n- First, we prove $f$ is injective. Assume that the subsets $A\\subseteq \\mathbb N$ and $B\\subseteq \\mathbb N$ are distinct. Then, one of them should contain an element that the other doesn't contain. Without loss of generality, assume that $s \\in A$ while $s \\notin B$ for some natural number $s$. Then, the sequence $f(A)$ has $1$ as its $s$th term whereas $f(B)$ has $0$. In other words, distinct subsets give distinct sequences. So, $f$ is injective.\n- On the other hand, $f$ is surjective since for any sequence from $\\{0,1 \\}^{\\mathbb{N}}$, we can reconstruct the corresponding subset in $\\mathbb{N}$. For example, the sequence $(0, 1, 1, 1, 0, 0, \\ldots)$, where all terms after the $4$th equal zero, corresponds to $\\{2,3,4\\}$. In other words, each sequence in $\\{0,1\\}^\\mathbb{N}$ is paired with an element of $\\mathcal P (\\mathbb N)$. So, $f$ is surjective.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\big| \\{ 0,1 \\}^{\\mathbb{N}} \\big| = \\big| \\mathcal{P}(\\mathbb{N}) \\big| = \\mathfrak{c}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186199574 :tutorial/content "Let's now prove the theorem stated in the introduction:\n\n> *The power set of $\\mathbb{N}$ has the same cardinality as the interval $(0,1)$, i.e., $\\big\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathcal{P}(\\mathbb{N}) \\big & = & (0,1)\\end{vmatrix} = \\mathfrak{c}$.*\n\nFirst, consider the function $f: \\mathcal{P}(\\mathbb N) \\to (0,1)$ that, given $A \\subseteq \\mathbb{N}$, maps it to the decimal number\n\n$$\n0.a_1 a_2 a_3 \\ldots \\in (0,1)\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\na_n = \n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{ll}1 , & \\text{if} \\hspace{0.222em} n \\in  A , \\\\ 2 , & \\text{if} \\hspace{0.222em} n \\notin  A .\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\n\nFor example, if\n\n$$\nA = \\{2, 4, 5 \\}\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\nf(A) = 0.21211\\overline{2}\n$$\n\nThe function $f$ is injective since\n\n$$\nf(A) \\neq f(B)\n$$\n\nwhenever\n\n$$\nA \\neq B\n$$\n\nIndeed, if two subsets $A$ and $B$ are distinct, then one of them should contain an element that the other doesn't contain. Without loss of generality, assume that $s \\in A$ while $s \\notin B$ for some natural number $s$. Then, the sequence $f(A)$ has $1$ as its $s$th term whereas $f(B)$ has $2$. In other words, distinct subsets give distinct sequences. So, we get\n\n$$\n\\big\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathcal{P}(\\mathbb{N}) \\big & \\leq \\big & (0,1) \\big\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, consider the function $g: (0,1) \\to \\mathcal{P}(\\mathbb N)$ that, given $0.a_1 a_2 a_3 \\ldots \\in (0,1)$, maps it to the set\n\n$$\n\\{10 a_1, 10^2 a_2, 10^3 a_3, \\ldots \\}\n$$\n\n**Watch out!** To avoid ambiguity, we use only those decimal representations that do not have repeating $9$'s at the end. For example,\n\n$$\n0.5=0.4\\overline{9}\n$$\n\nbut we consider only the representation $0.5$.\n\nThe function $g$ is injective since\n\n$$\ng(x) \\neq g(y)\n$$\n\nwhenever\n\n$$\nx \\neq y\n$$\n\nIndeed, if\n\n$$\nx=0.x_1x_2x_3\\ldots\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\ny=0.y_1y_2y_3\\ldots\n$$\n\nthen $g(x)=g(y)$ would mean that\n\n$$\n\\{10 x_1, 10^2 x_2, 10^3 x_3, \\ldots \\} = \\{10 y_1, 10^2 y_2, 10^3 y_3, \\ldots \\}\n$$\n\nSince the elements listed in each set must be distinct (the ascending powers of $10$ guarantee this), this equality is true if and only if $x_i=y_i$ for all $i \\in \\mathbb{N}$, i.e., $x=y$. So, we get\n\n$$\n\\big\\begin{vmatrix}(0,1) \\big & \\leq \\big & \\mathcal{P}(\\mathbb{N}) \\big\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\nFinally, according to the Cantor-Bernstein-Schröder theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\big\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathcal{P}(\\mathbb{N}) \\big & = \\big & (0,1) \\big\\end{vmatrix} = \\mathfrak{c}\n$$\n\n**Note:** We did not find a concrete bijection between the underlying sets, but we showed that it exists, which is enough!" "Let's now prove the theorem stated in the introduction:\n\n> *The power set of $\\mathbb{N}$ has the same cardinality as the interval $(0,1)$, i.e., $\\big| \\mathcal{P}(\\mathbb{N}) \\big| = |(0,1)| = \\mathfrak{c}.$.*\n\nFirst, consider the function $f: \\mathcal{P}(\\mathbb N) \\to (0,1)$ that, given $A \\subseteq \\mathbb{N}$, maps it to the decimal number\n\n$$\n0.a_1 a_2 a_3 \\ldots \\in (0,1)\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\na_n = \n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{ll}1 , & \\text{if} \\hspace{0.222em} n \\in  A , \\\\ 2 , & \\text{if} \\hspace{0.222em} n \\notin  A .\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\n\nFor example, if\n\n$$\nA = \\{2, 4, 5 \\}\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\nf(A) = 0.21211\\overline{2}\n$$\n\nThe function $f$ is injective since\n\n$$\nf(A) \\neq f(B)\n$$\n\nwhenever\n\n$$\nA \\neq B\n$$\n\nIndeed, if two subsets $A$ and $B$ are distinct, then one of them should contain an element that the other doesn't contain. Without loss of generality, assume that $s \\in A$ while $s \\notin B$ for some natural number $s$. Then, the sequence $f(A)$ has $1$ as its $s$th term whereas $f(B)$ has $2$. In other words, distinct subsets give distinct sequences. So, we get\n\n$$\n\\big| \\mathcal{P}(\\mathbb{N}) \\big| \\leq \\big| (0,1) \\big|.\n$$\n\nNow, consider the function $g: (0,1) \\to \\mathcal{P}(\\mathbb N)$ that, given $0.a_1 a_2 a_3 \\ldots \\in (0,1)$, maps it to the set\n\n$$\n\\{10 a_1, 10^2 a_2, 10^3 a_3, \\ldots \\}\n$$\n\n**Watch out!** To avoid ambiguity, we use only those decimal representations that do not have repeating $9$'s at the end. For example,\n\n$$\n0.5=0.4\\overline{9}\n$$\n\nbut we consider only the representation $0.5$.\n\nThe function $g$ is injective since\n\n$$\ng(x) \\neq g(y)\n$$\n\nwhenever\n\n$$\nx \\neq y\n$$\n\nIndeed, if\n\n$$\nx=0.x_1x_2x_3\\ldots\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\ny=0.y_1y_2y_3\\ldots\n$$\n\nthen $g(x)=g(y)$ would mean that\n\n$$\n\\{10 x_1, 10^2 x_2, 10^3 x_3, \\ldots \\} = \\{10 y_1, 10^2 y_2, 10^3 y_3, \\ldots \\}\n$$\n\nSince the elements listed in each set must be distinct (the ascending powers of $10$ guarantee this), this equality is true if and only if $x_i=y_i$ for all $i \\in \\mathbb{N}$, i.e., $x=y$. So, we get\n\n$$\n\\big| (0,1) \\big| \\leq \\big| \\mathcal{P}(\\mathbb{N}) \\big|.\n$$\n\nFinally, according to the Cantor-Bernstein-Schröder theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\big| \\mathcal{P}(\\mathbb{N}) \\big| = \\big| (0,1) \\big| = \\mathfrak{c}.\n$$\n\n**Note:** We did not find a concrete bijection between the underlying sets, but we showed that it exists, which is enough!"] [:db/cas 17592186199587 :tutorial/content "We can use modular congruences to prove statements about divisibility. Congruences often provide a more straightforward way of establishing the truth of a divisibility statement compared to other methods.\n\nFor example, let's prove the following proposition:\n\n> *$5^n - 1$ is divisible by $4$ for any integer $n \\geq 1$.*\n\nIn this case, we need to show the following:\n\n$$\nn \\geq 1 \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad 4 \\mid (5^n- 1)\n$$\n\nWe start by checking that the statement makes sense in the given domain of $n{:}$\n\n> *Notice that the expression $5^n -1$ gives us an integer value when $n \\geq 1$.*\n\nRecall that $n \\mid a$ is equivalent to $a \\equiv 0 \\: (\\textrm{mod}\\:n)$. So, instead of proving divisibility by $4$, we can show that our expression is congruent to $0$ modulo $4{:}$\n\n> *According to the definition of modular congruence,*\n> $4 \\mid (5^n-1) \\quad\\Leftrightarrow\\quad 5^n -1 \\equiv 0 \\: (\\textrm{mod}\\:4)$.\n\nNow, we proceed as follows:\n\n> *Working in modulo $4$, we have the following:*\n> $5^{n} - 1 \\mid ≡(1)^{n} - 1\\begin{vmatrix}(mod4) \\\\ ≡1 - 1\\end{vmatrix}(mod4); ≡0 \\mid (mod4)$\n\nFinally, we make our conclusion:\n\n> *Therefore, $4 \\mid (5^n -1)$, as required.*\n\nFor clarity, let's restate the proposition and full proof." "We can use modular congruences to prove statements about divisibility. Congruences often provide a more straightforward way of establishing the truth of a divisibility statement compared to other methods.\n\nFor example, let's prove the following proposition:\n\n> *$5^n - 1$ is divisible by $4$ for any integer $n \\geq 1$.*\n\nIn this case, we need to show the following:\n\n$$\nn \\geq 1 \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad 4 \\mid (5^n- 1)\n$$\n\nWe start by checking that the statement makes sense in the given domain of $n{:}$\n\n> *Notice that the expression $5^n -1$ gives us an integer value when $n \\geq 1$.*\n\nRecall that $n \\mid a$ is equivalent to $a \\equiv 0 \\: (\\textrm{mod}\\:n)$. So, instead of proving divisibility by $4$, we can show that our expression is congruent to $0$ modulo $4{:}$\n\n> *According to the definition of modular congruence,*\n> $4 \\mid (5^n-1) \\quad\\Leftrightarrow\\quad 5^n -1 \\equiv 0 \\: (\\textrm{mod}\\:4)$.\n\nNow, we proceed as follows:\n\n> *Working in modulo $4$, we have the following:*\n> $\\begin{aligned}{5}^{n}-1 & ≡(1)^{n}-1 & (\\text{mod}\\hspace{0.222em}4) \\\\  & ≡1-1 & (\\text{mod}\\hspace{0.222em}4) \\\\  & ≡0 & (\\text{mod}\\hspace{0.222em}4)\\end{aligned}$\n\nFinally, we make our conclusion:\n\n> *Therefore, $4 \\mid (5^n -1)$, as required.*\n\nFor clarity, let's restate the proposition and full proof."] [:db/cas 17592186199596 :tutorial/content "In previous lessons, we saw how to define the limit of a null sequence (i.e., a sequence whose limit equals zero) and how to construct a formal proof that a given sequence is null.\n\nIn this lesson, we'll extend our understanding to include sequences that converge a finite (not necessarily zero) limit. If such a limit exists, it's usually denoted as $L$.\n\nConsider the following sequence:\n\n$$\na_n = \\dfrac{n+1}{n}, \\qquad n \\geq 1\n$$\n\nIt's easy to see that the limit of this sequence is\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}{L}}={\\color{red}{1}}{:}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(n → ∞)a_{n} &= lim_(n → ∞)\\left(\\frac{n + 1}{n}\\right) \\\\\n&= lim_(n → ∞)(\\frac{n}{n} + \\frac{1}{n}) \\\\\n&= lim_(n → ∞)(1 + \\frac{1}{n}) \\\\\n&= 1 + 0 \\\\\n&= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that for any $\\varepsilon > 0$, there exists a natural number $N(\\varepsilon)$ such that the distance between $a_n$ and\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}{L}} = {\\color{red}{1}}\n$$\n\nis *smaller than* $\\varepsilon$ for all\n\n$$\nn\\geq N\n$$\n\nIn other words,\n\n$$\n\\forall n\\geq N,\\qquad \\mid a_n - {\\color{red}{1}} \\mid < \\varepsilon\n$$\n\nLet's draw a few sketches to illustrate this idea.\n\n- If we select $\\varepsilon = 0.5$, then $\\mid a_n-{\\color{red}{1}} \\mid < \\varepsilon$ for all $n \\geq 3$.\n\n![](images/be/bee8ffa92b56e2e8dedc214322481b13a3ab8cd4b73d84a5c1300d10dbd43d4f.png)\n\n- If $\\varepsilon = 0.3$, then $\\mid a_n-{\\color{red}{1}} \\mid < \\varepsilon$ for all $n \\geq 4$.\n\n![](images/d2/d27e06c19457ab1c89386cde995c043c6d6d55169ec8fdc32509690bf1880767.png)\n\n- If $\\varepsilon = 0.1$, then $\\mid a_n-{\\color{red}{1}} \\mid < \\varepsilon$ for all $n \\geq 11$.\n\n![](images/56/564c5ddf12f0171c5d55da0d669f9f7bff2726707c72e473fa9e8eb6704bd937.png)\n\nThe key idea is this: By making $\\varepsilon$ smaller and smaller, we can *always* find an index $N$ where the distance between $a_n$ and $\\color{red}L$ is smaller than $\\varepsilon$ for all\n\n$$\nn\\geq N\n$$" "In previous lessons, we saw how to define the limit of a null sequence (i.e., a sequence whose limit equals zero) and how to construct a formal proof that a given sequence is null.\n\nIn this lesson, we'll extend our understanding to include sequences that converge a finite (not necessarily zero) limit. If such a limit exists, it's usually denoted as $L$.\n\nConsider the following sequence:\n\n$$\na_n = \\dfrac{n+1}{n}, \\qquad n \\geq 1\n$$\n\nIt's easy to see that the limit of this sequence is\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}{L}}={\\color{red}{1}}{:}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\lim_{n \\to  \\infty}a_{n} &= \\lim_{n \\to  \\infty}\\left(\\frac{n + 1}{n}\\right) \\\\\n&= \\lim_{n \\to  \\infty}(\\frac{n}{n} + \\frac{1}{n}) \\\\\n&= \\lim_{n \\to  \\infty}(1 + \\frac{1}{n}) \\\\\n&= 1 + 0 \\\\\n&= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that for any $\\varepsilon > 0$, there exists a natural number $N(\\varepsilon)$ such that the distance between $a_n$ and\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}{L}} = {\\color{red}{1}}\n$$\n\nis *smaller than* $\\varepsilon$ for all\n\n$$\nn\\geq N\n$$\n\nIn other words,\n\n$$\n\\forall n\\geq N,\\qquad \\mid a_n - {\\color{red}{1}} \\mid < \\varepsilon\n$$\n\nLet's draw a few sketches to illustrate this idea.\n\n- If we select $\\varepsilon = 0.5$, then $\\mid a_n-{\\color{red}{1}} \\mid < \\varepsilon$ for all $n \\geq 3$.\n\n![](images/be/bee8ffa92b56e2e8dedc214322481b13a3ab8cd4b73d84a5c1300d10dbd43d4f.png)\n\n- If $\\varepsilon = 0.3$, then $\\mid a_n-{\\color{red}{1}} \\mid < \\varepsilon$ for all $n \\geq 4$.\n\n![](images/d2/d27e06c19457ab1c89386cde995c043c6d6d55169ec8fdc32509690bf1880767.png)\n\n- If $\\varepsilon = 0.1$, then $\\mid a_n-{\\color{red}{1}} \\mid < \\varepsilon$ for all $n \\geq 11$.\n\n![](images/56/564c5ddf12f0171c5d55da0d669f9f7bff2726707c72e473fa9e8eb6704bd937.png)\n\nThe key idea is this: By making $\\varepsilon$ smaller and smaller, we can *always* find an index $N$ where the distance between $a_n$ and $\\color{red}L$ is smaller than $\\varepsilon$ for all\n\n$$\nn\\geq N\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186199597 :tutorial/content "We can use benchmarks to estimate decimal subtraction.\n\nFor example, suppose we want to estimate the following difference using benchmarks with decimal parts of $0.0$ or $0.5{:}$\n\n$$\n12.53 - 7.17\n$$\n\nWe first round both numbers to the nearest benchmark:\n\n$$\n12.53\\begin{vmatrix}- & 7.17 \\\\ ↓ & ↓ \\\\ 12.5 & -\\end{vmatrix}7.0\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the difference:\n\n$$\n1\\begin{vmatrix}2 &. & 5 \\\\ - & 7 &.\\end{vmatrix}0; 5 \\mid. \\mid 5\n$$\n\nTherefore, $12.53 - 7.17$ is approximately $5.5$, which we can write as follows:\n\n$$\n12.53 - 7.17 \\approx 5.5\n$$" "We can use benchmarks to estimate decimal subtraction.\n\nFor example, suppose we want to estimate the following difference using benchmarks with decimal parts of $0.0$ or $0.5{:}$\n\n$$\n12.53 - 7.17\n$$\n\nWe first round both numbers to the nearest benchmark:\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{ccc}12.53 & - & 7.17 \\\\ ↓ &  & ↓ \\\\ {\\color{blue}{12.5}} & - & {\\color{blue}{7.0}}\\end{array}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the difference:\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{cccccc} &  & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}1\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}2\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}.\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}5\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} \\\\ \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}-\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} &  &  & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}7\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}.\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}0\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} \\\\ \\hline  &  & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}5\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}.\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}5\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\end{array}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $12.53 - 7.17$ is approximately $5.5$, which we can write as follows:\n\n$$\n12.53 - 7.17 \\approx 5.5\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186199606 :tutorial/content "Consider the following null sequence:\n\n$$\na_n = \\dfrac{1}{n^2}, \\qquad n \\geq 1\n$$\n\nGiven that $\\varepsilon > 0$, let's find a natural number $N(\\varepsilon)$ such that for all\n\n$$\nn \\geq N\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\mid a_n \\mid < \\varepsilon\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that our sequence is positive for any natural\n\n$$\nn \\geq 1\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, we have\n\n$$\n\\mid a_n \\mid = \\left| \\dfrac{1}{n^2} \\right| = \\dfrac{1}{n^2}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the inequality $\\mid a_n \\mid < \\varepsilon$ for $n{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{n^{2}} & < ε \\\\ 1 & < εn^{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{ε} & < n^{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{ε}} & < n \\\\ n & > \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{ε}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSo, we let $N$ be the smallest natural number such that\n\n$$\nN > \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{\\varepsilon}}\n$$\n\nAnd that's it! Given any $\\varepsilon > 0$, no matter how small, $\\mid a_n \\mid$ is guaranteed to be smaller than $\\varepsilon$ provided that\n\n$$\nn\\geq N\n$$\n\nwhere $N$ is the smallest natural number greater than\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt\\varepsilon}\n$$" "Consider the following null sequence:\n\n$$\na_n = \\dfrac{1}{n^2}, \\qquad n \\geq 1\n$$\n\nGiven that $\\varepsilon > 0$, let's find a natural number $N(\\varepsilon)$ such that for all\n\n$$\nn \\geq N\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\mid a_n \\mid < \\varepsilon\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that our sequence is positive for any natural\n\n$$\nn \\geq 1\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, we have\n\n$$\n\\mid a_n \\mid = \\left| \\dfrac{1}{n^2} \\right| = \\dfrac{1}{n^2}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the inequality $\\mid a_n \\mid < \\varepsilon$ for $n{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}} & <ε \\\\ 1 & <ε{n}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{ε} & <{n}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{ε}} & <n \\\\ n & >\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{ε}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we let $N$ be the smallest natural number such that\n\n$$\nN > \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{\\varepsilon}}\n$$\n\nAnd that's it! Given any $\\varepsilon > 0$, no matter how small, $\\mid a_n \\mid$ is guaranteed to be smaller than $\\varepsilon$ provided that\n\n$$\nn\\geq N\n$$\n\nwhere $N$ is the smallest natural number greater than\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt\\varepsilon}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186199624 :tutorial/content "Proposition:\n\n> *$5^n - 1$ is divisible by $4$ for any integer $n \\geq 1$.*\n\nProof:\n\n> *Notice that the expression $5^n -1$ gives us an integer value when $n \\geq 1$.*\n> *According to the definition of modular congruence,*\n> $4 \\mid (5^n-1) \\quad\\Leftrightarrow\\quad 5^n -1 \\equiv 0 \\: (\\textrm{mod}\\:4)$.\n> *Working in modulo $4$, we have the following:*\n> $5^{n} - 1 \\mid ≡(1)^{n} - 1\\begin{vmatrix}(mod4) \\\\ ≡1 - 1\\end{vmatrix}(mod4); ≡0 \\mid (mod4)$\n> *Therefore, $4 \\mid (5^n -1)$, as required.*" "Proposition:\n\n> *$5^n - 1$ is divisible by $4$ for any integer $n \\geq 1$.*\n\nProof:\n\n> *Notice that the expression $5^n -1$ gives us an integer value when $n \\geq 1$.*\n> *According to the definition of modular congruence,*\n> $4 \\mid (5^n-1) \\quad\\Leftrightarrow\\quad 5^n -1 \\equiv 0 \\: (\\textrm{mod}\\:4)$.\n> *Working in modulo $4$, we have the following:*\n> $\\begin{aligned}{5}^{n}-1 & ≡(1)^{n}-1 & (\\text{mod}\\hspace{0.222em}4) \\\\  & ≡1-1 & (\\text{mod}\\hspace{0.222em}4) \\\\  & ≡0 & (\\text{mod}\\hspace{0.222em}4)\\end{aligned}$\n> *Therefore, $4 \\mid (5^n -1)$, as required.*"] [:db/cas 17592186199735 :answer/value "\\begin{vmatrix}A|= & B\\end{vmatrix}" "|A|= | B|"] [:db/cas 17592186199736 :answer/value "\\begin{vmatrix}A & \\ne & B\\end{vmatrix}" "|A | \\ne | B|"] [:db/cas 17592186199751 :answer/value "\\begin{vmatrix}A & \\ne & B\\end{vmatrix}" "|A | \\ne | B|"] [:db/cas 17592186199752 :answer/value "\\begin{vmatrix}A|= & B\\end{vmatrix}" "|A|= | B|"] [:db/cas 17592186199762 :answer/value "\\begin{vmatrix}A & \\ne & B\\end{vmatrix}" "|A | \\ne | B|"] [:db/cas 17592186199763 :answer/value "\\begin{vmatrix}A|= & B\\end{vmatrix}" "|A|= | B|"] [:db/cas 17592186199783 :answer/value "\\begin{vmatrix}A & \\ne & B\\end{vmatrix}" "|A | \\ne | B|"] [:db/cas 17592186199784 :answer/value "\\begin{vmatrix}A|= & B\\end{vmatrix}" "|A|= | B|"] [:db/cas 17592186199795 :answer/value "\\begin{vmatrix}A & \\ne & B\\end{vmatrix}" "|A | \\ne | B|"] [:db/cas 17592186199796 :answer/value "\\begin{vmatrix}A|= & B\\end{vmatrix}" "|A|= | B|"] [:db/cas 17592186199802 :answer/value "\\begin{vmatrix}A|= & B\\end{vmatrix}" "|A|= | B|"] [:db/cas 17592186199803 :answer/value "\\begin{vmatrix}A & \\ne & B\\end{vmatrix}" "|A | \\ne | B|"] [:db/cas 17592186199823 :answer/value "\\begin{vmatrix}A|= & B\\end{vmatrix}" "|A|= | B|"] [:db/cas 17592186199824 :answer/value "\\begin{vmatrix}A & \\ne & B\\end{vmatrix}" "|A | \\ne | B|"] [:db/cas 17592186199831 :answer/value "\\begin{vmatrix}A & \\ne & B\\end{vmatrix}" "|A | \\ne | B|"] [:db/cas 17592186199832 :answer/value "\\begin{vmatrix}A|= & B\\end{vmatrix}" "|A|= | B|"] [:db/cas 17592186203666 :question/worked-solution "Sets $A$ and $B$ have the *same cardinality* if there exists a bijection between $A$ and $B$.\n\nRecall that the function $y=\\ln x$ bijectively (i.e., both injectively and surjectively) maps the interval $(0,\\infty)$ onto the interval\n\n$$\n(-\\infty,\\infty) = \\mathbb{R}\n$$\n\n![](images/84/84f0bcbc610b1f80dc592cde2f3e976d197c8f6e9de22f1586de0de73cc06843.png)\n\nHowever, we need a function that maps $(8,\\infty)$ onto $\\mathbb R$ instead of $(0,\\infty)$ onto $\\mathbb{R}$. To construct one, we shift our logarithm function by $8$ units to the right. This can be done by subtracting $8$ from the argument.\n\nSo, we consider the function $f: A \\to B$ that maps the underlying sets as follows:\n\n$$\nf: x \\to \\ln(x-8)\n$$\n\nNotice that $f$ bijectively maps $(8,\\infty)$ onto $\\mathbb{R}$.\n\n![](images/32/3275f2495414672116b41d4e4f1df61e173b4fd3dce6034e7c85a9cc254acaf2.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}A & = & B\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\ni.e., the sets have the same cardinality." "Sets $A$ and $B$ have the *same cardinality* if there exists a bijection between $A$ and $B$.\n\nRecall that the function $y=\\ln x$ bijectively (i.e., both injectively and surjectively) maps the interval $(0,\\infty)$ onto the interval\n\n$$\n(-\\infty,\\infty) = \\mathbb{R}\n$$\n\n![](images/84/84f0bcbc610b1f80dc592cde2f3e976d197c8f6e9de22f1586de0de73cc06843.png)\n\nHowever, we need a function that maps $(8,\\infty)$ onto $\\mathbb R$ instead of $(0,\\infty)$ onto $\\mathbb{R}$. To construct one, we shift our logarithm function by $8$ units to the right. This can be done by subtracting $8$ from the argument.\n\nSo, we consider the function $f: A \\to B$ that maps the underlying sets as follows:\n\n$$\nf: x \\to \\ln(x-8)\n$$\n\nNotice that $f$ bijectively maps $(8,\\infty)$ onto $\\mathbb{R}$.\n\n![](images/32/3275f2495414672116b41d4e4f1df61e173b4fd3dce6034e7c85a9cc254acaf2.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n|A| = |B|,\n$$\n\ni.e., the sets have the same cardinality."] [:db/cas 17592186203667 :question/worked-solution "Sets $A$ and $B$ have the *same cardinality* if there exists a bijection between $A$ and $B$.\n\nRecall that the function $y = \\tan x$ bijectively (i.e., both injectively and surjectively) maps the interval\n\n$$\n\\left(-\\dfrac{\\pi}2,\\dfrac{\\pi}2\\right)\n$$\n\nonto the interval\n\n$$\n(-\\infty,\\infty) = \\mathbb{R}\n$$\n\n![](images/0c/0c30abace6b86aa63ce3da8bafa2bb4748271fdfa3d320ee0f2c7f18fbeec998.png)\n\nHowever, we need a function that maps from $(-3,3)$ onto $\\mathbb R$ instead of\n\n$$\n\\left(-\\dfrac{\\pi}2,\\dfrac{\\pi}2\\right)\n$$\n\nonto $\\mathbb R$. To construct one, we can expand our tangent curve by a factor of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{6}{\\pi}\n$$\n\nalong the $x$-axis. This can be done by multiplying the argument of the function by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\n$$\n\nSo, we consider the function $f: A \\to B$ that maps the underlying sets as follows:\n\n$$\nf: x \\to \\tan \\left(\\dfrac{\\pi x}{6}\\right)\n$$\n\nNotice that $f$ bijectively maps $(-3,3)$ onto $\\mathbb{R}$.\n\n![](images/1e/1e8c3a10fc9688fcc009178d9777201d367e82ad0d9c410cc24fa925f5091e96.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}A & = & B\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\ni.e., the sets have the same cardinality." "Sets $A$ and $B$ have the *same cardinality* if there exists a bijection between $A$ and $B$.\n\nRecall that the function $y = \\tan x$ bijectively (i.e., both injectively and surjectively) maps the interval\n\n$$\n\\left(-\\dfrac{\\pi}2,\\dfrac{\\pi}2\\right)\n$$\n\nonto the interval\n\n$$\n(-\\infty,\\infty) = \\mathbb{R}\n$$\n\n![](images/0c/0c30abace6b86aa63ce3da8bafa2bb4748271fdfa3d320ee0f2c7f18fbeec998.png)\n\nHowever, we need a function that maps from $(-3,3)$ onto $\\mathbb R$ instead of\n\n$$\n\\left(-\\dfrac{\\pi}2,\\dfrac{\\pi}2\\right)\n$$\n\nonto $\\mathbb R$. To construct one, we can expand our tangent curve by a factor of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{6}{\\pi}\n$$\n\nalong the $x$-axis. This can be done by multiplying the argument of the function by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\n$$\n\nSo, we consider the function $f: A \\to B$ that maps the underlying sets as follows:\n\n$$\nf: x \\to \\tan \\left(\\dfrac{\\pi x}{6}\\right)\n$$\n\nNotice that $f$ bijectively maps $(-3,3)$ onto $\\mathbb{R}$.\n\n![](images/1e/1e8c3a10fc9688fcc009178d9777201d367e82ad0d9c410cc24fa925f5091e96.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n|A| = |B|,\n$$\n\ni.e., the sets have the same cardinality."] [:db/cas 17592186203693 :question/worked-solution "For a relation $R$ defined over the sets $X$ and $Y$, and a relation $S$ defined over the sets $Y$ and $Z$, the *composition* $S \\circ R$ is given by\n\n$$\nS \\circ R = \\big\\{(x,z) \\in X \\times Z \\:: \\: \\exists y \\in Y, \\: x \\: R \\: y \\: \\land \\: y \\: S \\: z \\big\\}\n$$\n\nSince our relations are defined on relatively small sets, we can visualize them using the following mapping diagrams, where we match the elements of the two inner sets by the dashed lines:\n\n![](images/98/9819ec3233b22095898b87eb0f54de8adb8271a27e5a5d606fcce7b1c986a646.png)\n\nAn ordered pair belongs to the composition $S \\circ R$ if there is a path starting from the first component (on the left-hand side) and ending at the second component (on the right-hand side).\n\nIn our example, these paths are the following:\n\n$$\na⟶0- - -0⟶\\cdot; a⟶1- - -1⟶4\\begin{vmatrix}Therefore,(a, 4) ∈ S∘R \\\\ a⟶1- - -1⟶7\\end{vmatrix}Therefore,(a, 7) ∈ S∘R; a⟶1- - -1⟶10\\begin{vmatrix}Therefore,(a, 10) ∈ S∘R \\\\ c⟶0- - -0⟶\\cdot \\\\ d⟶1- - -1⟶4\\end{vmatrix}Therefore,(d, 4) ∈ S∘R; d⟶1- - -1⟶7\\begin{vmatrix}Therefore,(d, 7) ∈ S∘R \\\\ d⟶1- - -1⟶10\\end{vmatrix}Therefore,(d, 10) ∈ S∘R; e⟶0- - -0⟶\\cdot; e⟶1- - -1⟶4\\begin{vmatrix}Therefore,(e, 4) ∈ S∘R \\\\ e⟶1- - -1⟶7\\end{vmatrix}Therefore,(e, 7) ∈ S∘R; e⟶1- - -1⟶10 \\mid Therefore,(e, 10) ∈ S∘R\n$$\n\nSo, nine complete paths start on the left-hand side and end on the right-hand side. They correspond to the following ordered pairs:\n\n$$\nS \\circ R = \\big\\{(a,4), (a,7), (a,10), (d,4), (d,7), (d,10), (e,4), (e,7), (e,10)\\big\\}\n$$" "For a relation $R$ defined over the sets $X$ and $Y$, and a relation $S$ defined over the sets $Y$ and $Z$, the *composition* $S \\circ R$ is given by\n\n$$\nS \\circ R = \\big\\{(x,z) \\in X \\times Z \\:: \\: \\exists y \\in Y, \\: x \\: R \\: y \\: \\land \\: y \\: S \\: z \\big\\}\n$$\n\nSince our relations are defined on relatively small sets, we can visualize them using the following mapping diagrams, where we match the elements of the two inner sets by the dashed lines:\n\n![](images/98/9819ec3233b22095898b87eb0f54de8adb8271a27e5a5d606fcce7b1c986a646.png)\n\nAn ordered pair belongs to the composition $S \\circ R$ if there is a path starting from the first component (on the left-hand side) and ending at the second component (on the right-hand side).\n\nIn our example, these paths are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & a⟶0\\hspace{0.222em}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{- - -}\\hspace{0.222em}\\hspace{0.222em}0⟶{\\color{red}{\\times }} \\\\  & a⟶1\\hspace{0.222em}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{- - -}\\hspace{0.222em}\\hspace{0.222em}1⟶4\\hspace{2em}\\hspace{2em} &  & \\text{Therefore, }(a,4)\\in S∘R \\\\  & a⟶1\\hspace{0.222em}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{- - -}\\hspace{0.222em}\\hspace{0.222em}1⟶7\\hspace{2em}\\hspace{2em} &  & \\text{Therefore, }(a,7)\\in S∘R \\\\  & a⟶1\\hspace{0.222em}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{- - -}\\hspace{0.222em}\\hspace{0.222em}1⟶10\\hspace{2em}\\hspace{2em} &  & \\text{Therefore, }(a,10)\\in S∘R \\\\  & c⟶0\\hspace{0.222em}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{- - -}\\hspace{0.222em}\\hspace{0.222em}0⟶{\\color{red}{\\times }} \\\\  & d⟶1\\hspace{0.222em}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{- - -}\\hspace{0.222em}\\hspace{0.222em}1⟶4\\hspace{2em}\\hspace{2em} &  & \\text{Therefore, }(d,4)\\in S∘R \\\\  & d⟶1\\hspace{0.222em}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{- - -}\\hspace{0.222em}\\hspace{0.222em}1⟶7\\hspace{2em}\\hspace{2em} &  & \\text{Therefore, }(d,7)\\in S∘R \\\\  & d⟶1\\hspace{0.222em}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{- - -}\\hspace{0.222em}\\hspace{0.222em}1⟶10\\hspace{2em}\\hspace{2em} &  & \\text{Therefore, }(d,10)\\in S∘R \\\\  & e⟶0\\hspace{0.222em}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{- - -}\\hspace{0.222em}\\hspace{0.222em}0⟶{\\color{red}{\\times }} \\\\  & e⟶1\\hspace{0.222em}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{- - -}\\hspace{0.222em}\\hspace{0.222em}1⟶4\\hspace{2em}\\hspace{2em} &  & \\text{Therefore, }(e,4)\\in S∘R \\\\  & e⟶1\\hspace{0.222em}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{- - -}\\hspace{0.222em}\\hspace{0.222em}1⟶7\\hspace{2em}\\hspace{2em} &  & \\text{Therefore, }(e,7)\\in S∘R \\\\  & e⟶1\\hspace{0.222em}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{- - -}\\hspace{0.222em}\\hspace{0.222em}1⟶10\\hspace{2em}\\hspace{2em} &  & \\text{Therefore, }(e,10)\\in S∘R\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, nine complete paths start on the left-hand side and end on the right-hand side. They correspond to the following ordered pairs:\n\n$$\nS \\circ R = \\big\\{(a,4), (a,7), (a,10), (d,4), (d,7), (d,10), (e,4), (e,7), (e,10)\\big\\}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186203721 :question/worked-solution "Sets $A$ and $B$ have the *same cardinality* if there exists a bijection of $A$ onto $B$.\n\nRecall that the function $y=x$ bijectively (i.e., both injectively and surjectively) maps the interval $(0,2)$ onto the interval $(0,2)$.\n\n![](images/8e/8ea2ae439c91dd7a6239b188ae90573511fcb533289d52ebfcd41b79f8b03242.png)\n\nHowever, we need a function that maps $(0,2)$ of length $2$ onto $(1,9)$ of length $8$. To construct one, we proceed as follows:\n\n- First, we stretch our function by a factor of $4$ along the $y$-axis, i.e, multiply the function by $4$.\n- Next, we shift the result $1$ units up, i.e., add $1$ to the result of the previous transformation.\n\nSo, we consider the function $f: (0,2) \\to (1,9)$ that maps the underlying sets as follows:\n\n$$\nf: x \\to 4x + 1\n$$\n\nNotice that $f$ bijectively maps $(0,2)$ onto $(1,9)$.\n\n![](images/cc/ccaf0cd63033f73a091a1d14188bc979c4090b1f94e61842917fd214552fb7ad.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}A & = & B\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\ni.e., the sets have the same cardinality." "Sets $A$ and $B$ have the *same cardinality* if there exists a bijection of $A$ onto $B$.\n\nRecall that the function $y=x$ bijectively (i.e., both injectively and surjectively) maps the interval $(0,2)$ onto the interval $(0,2)$.\n\n![](images/8e/8ea2ae439c91dd7a6239b188ae90573511fcb533289d52ebfcd41b79f8b03242.png)\n\nHowever, we need a function that maps $(0,2)$ of length $2$ onto $(1,9)$ of length $8$. To construct one, we proceed as follows:\n\n- First, we stretch our function by a factor of $4$ along the $y$-axis, i.e, multiply the function by $4$.\n- Next, we shift the result $1$ units up, i.e., add $1$ to the result of the previous transformation.\n\nSo, we consider the function $f: (0,2) \\to (1,9)$ that maps the underlying sets as follows:\n\n$$\nf: x \\to 4x + 1\n$$\n\nNotice that $f$ bijectively maps $(0,2)$ onto $(1,9)$.\n\n![](images/cc/ccaf0cd63033f73a091a1d14188bc979c4090b1f94e61842917fd214552fb7ad.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n|A| = |B|,\n$$\n\ni.e., the sets have the same cardinality."] [:db/cas 17592186203722 :question/worked-solution "Sets $A$ and $B$ have the *same cardinality* if there exists a bijection between $A$ and $B$.\n\nRecall that the function $y=e^x$ bijectively (i.e., both injectively and surjectively) maps the interval\n\n$$\n(-\\infty,\\infty) = \\mathbb{R}\n$$\n\nonto the interval $(0,\\infty)$.\n\n![](images/c0/c02ce2e0276f70672c2ee3873a48f68c42f53ea02450c03385d71a7c5257ed38.png)\n\nHowever, we need a function that maps $\\mathbb R$ onto $(-4,\\infty)$ instead of $(0,\\infty)$. To construct one, we shift our exponential graph by $4$ downward. This can be done by subtracting $4$ from the function.\n\nSo, we consider the function $f: B \\to A$ that maps the underlying sets as follows:\n\n$$\nf: x \\to e^{x}-4\n$$\n\nNotice that $f$ bijectively maps $\\mathbb{R}$ onto $(-4,\\infty)$.\n\n![](images/29/29d1bf44e638b8290eab5a08e14d7c16544d43152a5116f6fcb195698ddf47b4.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}A & = & B\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\ni.e., the sets have the same cardinality." "Sets $A$ and $B$ have the *same cardinality* if there exists a bijection between $A$ and $B$.\n\nRecall that the function $y=e^x$ bijectively (i.e., both injectively and surjectively) maps the interval\n\n$$\n(-\\infty,\\infty) = \\mathbb{R}\n$$\n\nonto the interval $(0,\\infty)$.\n\n![](images/c0/c02ce2e0276f70672c2ee3873a48f68c42f53ea02450c03385d71a7c5257ed38.png)\n\nHowever, we need a function that maps $\\mathbb R$ onto $(-4,\\infty)$ instead of $(0,\\infty)$. To construct one, we shift our exponential graph by $4$ downward. This can be done by subtracting $4$ from the function.\n\nSo, we consider the function $f: B \\to A$ that maps the underlying sets as follows:\n\n$$\nf: x \\to e^{x}-4\n$$\n\nNotice that $f$ bijectively maps $\\mathbb{R}$ onto $(-4,\\infty)$.\n\n![](images/29/29d1bf44e638b8290eab5a08e14d7c16544d43152a5116f6fcb195698ddf47b4.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n|A| = |B|,\n$$\n\ni.e., the sets have the same cardinality."] [:db/cas 17592186203732 :question/worked-solution "The connector \"if and only if\" means we need to prove an equivalence.\n\n$$\nP\\Leftrightarrow Q\n$$\n\nHowever, instead of proving an equivalence directly, we can instead prove two conditional statements.\n\n$$\nP\\Leftrightarrow Q \\equiv (P\\Rightarrow Q) \\land (Q\\Rightarrow P)\n$$\n\nSo, we start our proof as follows:\n\n> We need to show the following:\n> $\\textrm{n is even} \\quad\\Leftrightarrow\\quad \\textrm{{n}^{2} is even}$\n> This is the same as proving two implications.\n\nFirst, we consider the \"left to right\" implication.\n\n> **Case $\\Rightarrow{:}$** We need to show that\n> $\\textrm{n is even} \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad \\textrm{{n}^{2} is even}.$\n> If $n$ is even, then $n \\equiv 0 \\: (\\textrm{mod}\\:2)$. Therefore,\n> $n^{2} \\mid ≡0^{2}\\begin{vmatrix}(mod2) \\\\ ≡0\\end{vmatrix}(mod2)$\n> which means that $n^2$ is even. So, the first implication is proved.\n\nNext, we consider the \"right to left\" implication.\n\n> **Case $\\Leftarrow{:}$** We need to show that\n> $$\n{ { {n}^{2}} \\textrm{ is even}} \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad { {n} \\textrm{ is even}}.\n$$\n> Or, in terms of congruences,\n> $n^2 \\equiv 0 \\:\\: (\\textrm{mod}\\:2) \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad n \\equiv 0 \\:\\: (\\textrm{mod}\\:2)$.\n\nNow, the final statement can be re-written using negations as follows:\n\n> Since we're working modulo $2$ (where the only remainders are $0$ and $1$), this statement is equivalent to\n> $n^2 \\not\\equiv 1 \\:\\: (\\textrm{mod}\\:2) \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad n \\not\\equiv 1 \\:\\: (\\textrm{mod}\\:2)$.\n\nProving the negation statement can be a difficult task, but we can avoid that by using the contrapositive:\n\n> The contrapositive of the last statement is\n> $n \\equiv 1 \\:\\: (\\textrm{mod}\\:2) \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad n^2 \\equiv 1 \\:\\: (\\textrm{mod}\\:2)$.\n\nNext, we'll prove the contrapositive, as shown below.\n\n> Now, if $n \\equiv 1 \\: (\\textrm{mod}\\:2)$, then\n> $n^{2} \\mid ≡1^{2}\\begin{vmatrix}(mod2) \\\\ ≡1\\end{vmatrix}(mod2)$\n> as required. So, the second implication is proved.\n\nFinally, we write down the conclusion:\n\n> Therefore, $n$ is even if and only if $n^2$ is even." "The connector \"if and only if\" means we need to prove an equivalence.\n\n$$\nP\\Leftrightarrow Q\n$$\n\nHowever, instead of proving an equivalence directly, we can instead prove two conditional statements.\n\n$$\nP\\Leftrightarrow Q \\equiv (P\\Rightarrow Q) \\land (Q\\Rightarrow P)\n$$\n\nSo, we start our proof as follows:\n\n> We need to show the following:\n> $\\textrm{n is even} \\quad\\Leftrightarrow\\quad \\textrm{{n}^{2} is even}$\n> This is the same as proving two implications.\n\nFirst, we consider the \"left to right\" implication.\n\n> **Case $\\Rightarrow{:}$** We need to show that\n> $\\textrm{n is even} \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad \\textrm{{n}^{2} is even}.$\n> If $n$ is even, then $n \\equiv 0 \\: (\\textrm{mod}\\:2)$. Therefore,\n> $\\begin{aligned}{n}^{2} & ≡{0}^{2} & (\\text{mod}\\hspace{0.222em}2) \\\\  & ≡0 & (\\text{mod}\\hspace{0.222em}2)\\end{aligned}$\n> which means that $n^2$ is even. So, the first implication is proved.\n\nNext, we consider the \"right to left\" implication.\n\n> **Case $\\Leftarrow{:}$** We need to show that\n> $$\n{ { {n}^{2}} \\textrm{ is even}} \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad { {n} \\textrm{ is even}}.\n$$\n> Or, in terms of congruences,\n> $n^2 \\equiv 0 \\:\\: (\\textrm{mod}\\:2) \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad n \\equiv 0 \\:\\: (\\textrm{mod}\\:2)$.\n\nNow, the final statement can be re-written using negations as follows:\n\n> Since we're working modulo $2$ (where the only remainders are $0$ and $1$), this statement is equivalent to\n> $n^2 \\not\\equiv 1 \\:\\: (\\textrm{mod}\\:2) \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad n \\not\\equiv 1 \\:\\: (\\textrm{mod}\\:2)$.\n\nProving the negation statement can be a difficult task, but we can avoid that by using the contrapositive:\n\n> The contrapositive of the last statement is\n> $n \\equiv 1 \\:\\: (\\textrm{mod}\\:2) \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad n^2 \\equiv 1 \\:\\: (\\textrm{mod}\\:2)$.\n\nNext, we'll prove the contrapositive, as shown below.\n\n> Now, if $n \\equiv 1 \\: (\\textrm{mod}\\:2)$, then\n> $\\begin{aligned}{n}^{2} & ≡{1}^{2} & (\\text{mod}\\hspace{0.222em}2) \\\\  & ≡1 & (\\text{mod}\\hspace{0.222em}2)\\end{aligned}$\n> as required. So, the second implication is proved.\n\nFinally, we write down the conclusion:\n\n> Therefore, $n$ is even if and only if $n^2$ is even."] [:db/cas 17592186205257 :tutorial/content "Recall that two sets $A$ and $B$ have the *same cardinality* if there exists a bijection of $A$ onto $B$. If no such bijection exists, the sets are said to have *unequal cardinalities*.\n\nIn this lesson, we'll discuss how bijections can be used to determine whether two real intervals have the same cardinality.\n\nTo begin, consider the intervals $A$ and $B$, defined below:\n\n$$\nA= (0,1), \\qquad B= (0,2)\n$$\n\nIn terms of their *lengths*, the second interval is twice as long as the first. But what about their cardinalities?\n\nSince the length of $B$ is twice that of $A$, our intuition might tell us that $B$ should have twice the cardinality of $A$. However, this is *not* the case! You might be surprised to learn that $A$ and $B$ have the same cardinality!\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}A & = & B\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\nTo show why this is true, we need to construct a bijection that maps $A$ onto $B$.\n\nFirst, notice that the function $y=x$ bijectively (i.e., both injectively and surjectively) maps the interval $(0,1)$ onto the interval $(0,1)$.\n\n![](images/1a/1a1318e558e58556557556364fee6bdd53b1844a1e661a31e52138978841db37.png)\n\nHowever, we need a function that maps $(0,1)$ onto $(0,2)$ instead of onto $(0,1)$. To construct one, we can stretch our function by a factor of $2$ along the $y$-axis. This can be done by multiplying the function by $2$.\n\nSo, we consider the function $f: A \\to B$ that maps the underlying sets as follows:\n$f: x \\to 2x$\n\nNotice that $f$ bijectively maps $(0,1)$ onto $(0,2)$.\n\n![](images/81/81fca77700d1982091a68d32d548adbf1b2f4e734d7335f67d1e8684bc691a1c.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}A & = & B\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\ni.e., the sets have the same cardinality." "Recall that two sets $A$ and $B$ have the *same cardinality* if there exists a bijection of $A$ onto $B$. If no such bijection exists, the sets are said to have *unequal cardinalities*.\n\nIn this lesson, we'll discuss how bijections can be used to determine whether two real intervals have the same cardinality.\n\nTo begin, consider the intervals $A$ and $B$, defined below:\n\n$$\nA= (0,1), \\qquad B= (0,2)\n$$\n\nIn terms of their *lengths*, the second interval is twice as long as the first. But what about their cardinalities?\n\nSince the length of $B$ is twice that of $A$, our intuition might tell us that $B$ should have twice the cardinality of $A$. However, this is *not* the case! You might be surprised to learn that $A$ and $B$ have the same cardinality!\n\n$$\n|A| = |B|\n$$\n\nTo show why this is true, we need to construct a bijection that maps $A$ onto $B$.\n\nFirst, notice that the function $y=x$ bijectively (i.e., both injectively and surjectively) maps the interval $(0,1)$ onto the interval $(0,1)$.\n\n![](images/1a/1a1318e558e58556557556364fee6bdd53b1844a1e661a31e52138978841db37.png)\n\nHowever, we need a function that maps $(0,1)$ onto $(0,2)$ instead of onto $(0,1)$. To construct one, we can stretch our function by a factor of $2$ along the $y$-axis. This can be done by multiplying the function by $2$.\n\nSo, we consider the function $f: A \\to B$ that maps the underlying sets as follows:\n$f: x \\to 2x$\n\nNotice that $f$ bijectively maps $(0,1)$ onto $(0,2)$.\n\n![](images/81/81fca77700d1982091a68d32d548adbf1b2f4e734d7335f67d1e8684bc691a1c.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n|A| = |B|,\n$$\n\ni.e., the sets have the same cardinality."] [:db/cas 17592186205258 :tutorial/content "As we've seen, transformations of the function\n\n$$\nf(x) = x\n$$\n\nhelp to construct bijections that map finite intervals to finite intervals. Let's now consider some functions we can use to map infinite intervals to infinite intervals.\n\n- The function $y=e^x$ bijectively (i.e., both injectively and surjectively) maps the interval $(-\\infty,\\infty) = \\mathbb{R}$ onto the interval $(0,\\infty)$.![](images/c0/c02ce2e0276f70672c2ee3873a48f68c42f53ea02450c03385d71a7c5257ed38.png)\n\n- On the other hand, $y=\\ln x$ bijectively maps the interval $(0,\\infty)$ onto the interval $(-\\infty,\\infty) = \\mathbb{R}$.![](images/84/84f0bcbc610b1f80dc592cde2f3e976d197c8f6e9de22f1586de0de73cc06843.png)\n\nIn particular, this means that\n\n$$\n\\big\\begin{vmatrix}(0,\\infty) \\big & = \\big & (-\\infty,\\infty) \\big & = & \\mathbb R\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\nSimilar to what we've seen previously, we can perform transformations on these elementary functions to construct bijections that map between different intervals. Let's see some examples." "As we've seen, transformations of the function\n\n$$\nf(x) = x\n$$\n\nhelp to construct bijections that map finite intervals to finite intervals. Let's now consider some functions we can use to map infinite intervals to infinite intervals.\n\n- The function $y=e^x$ bijectively (i.e., both injectively and surjectively) maps the interval $(-\\infty,\\infty) = \\mathbb{R}$ onto the interval $(0,\\infty)$.![](images/c0/c02ce2e0276f70672c2ee3873a48f68c42f53ea02450c03385d71a7c5257ed38.png)\n\n- On the other hand, $y=\\ln x$ bijectively maps the interval $(0,\\infty)$ onto the interval $(-\\infty,\\infty) = \\mathbb{R}$.![](images/84/84f0bcbc610b1f80dc592cde2f3e976d197c8f6e9de22f1586de0de73cc06843.png)\n\nIn particular, this means that\n\n$$\n\\big| (0,\\infty) \\big| = \\big| (-\\infty,\\infty) \\big| = |\\mathbb R|.\n$$\n\nSimilar to what we've seen previously, we can perform transformations on these elementary functions to construct bijections that map between different intervals. Let's see some examples."] [:db/cas 17592186205259 :tutorial/content "Finally, let's consider functions we can use to map an (open) segment to the real line and vice-versa.\n\n- The function $y = \\tan x$ bijectively (i.e., both injectively and surjectively) maps the interval $\\left(-\\dfrac{\\pi}2,\\dfrac{\\pi}2\\right)$ onto the interval $(-\\infty,\\infty) = \\mathbb{R}$.![](images/0c/0c30abace6b86aa63ce3da8bafa2bb4748271fdfa3d320ee0f2c7f18fbeec998.png)\n- Similarly, the function $y = \\cot x$ bijectively maps the interval $\\left(0, \\pi\\right)$ onto the interval $(-\\infty,\\infty) = \\mathbb{R}$.![](images/8f/8f464060d171517b98dcf7cfd345420e534c349c2a98e83d2300f63f42a4132c.png)\n- Finally, the function $y = \\arctan x$ bijectively maps the interval $\\left(-\\infty, \\infty \\right)$ onto the interval $\\left(-\\dfrac\\pi2,\\dfrac\\pi2\\right)$.![](images/db/db7c994eeb92c6b2debd80129d65d20cf14dc039f9a3a3a46235f8c4054c720d.png)\n\nIn particular, this means that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\bigg\\begin{vmatrix}\\left(-\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}, \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\right) \\bigg & =\\big & (0,\\pi)\\big &= \\big & (-\\infty, \\infty) \\big & = \\big & \\mathbb R\\big\\end{vmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Finally, let's consider functions we can use to map an (open) segment to the real line and vice-versa.\n\n- The function $y = \\tan x$ bijectively (i.e., both injectively and surjectively) maps the interval $\\left(-\\dfrac{\\pi}2,\\dfrac{\\pi}2\\right)$ onto the interval $(-\\infty,\\infty) = \\mathbb{R}$.![](images/0c/0c30abace6b86aa63ce3da8bafa2bb4748271fdfa3d320ee0f2c7f18fbeec998.png)\n- Similarly, the function $y = \\cot x$ bijectively maps the interval $\\left(0, \\pi\\right)$ onto the interval $(-\\infty,\\infty) = \\mathbb{R}$.![](images/8f/8f464060d171517b98dcf7cfd345420e534c349c2a98e83d2300f63f42a4132c.png)\n- Finally, the function $y = \\arctan x$ bijectively maps the interval $\\left(-\\infty, \\infty \\right)$ onto the interval $\\left(-\\dfrac\\pi2,\\dfrac\\pi2\\right)$.![](images/db/db7c994eeb92c6b2debd80129d65d20cf14dc039f9a3a3a46235f8c4054c720d.png)\n\nIn particular, this means that\n\n$$\n\\bigg| \\left( -\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}, \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\right) \\bigg| =\\big|(0,\\pi)\\big|= \\big| (-\\infty, \\infty) \\big| = \\big|\\mathbb R\\big|.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186208318 :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the given equation as\n$c^2 = 13^2$.\n\nNext, we take the square root of both sides of the equation and solve the resulting absolute value equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nc^{2} &= 13^{2} \\\\\n\\sqrt{c^{2}} &= \\sqrt{13^{2}} \\\\\n\\begin{vmatrix}c & &= & 13\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n\\mid c \\mid &= 13 \\\\\nc &= ± 13\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the **negative** solution to the given equation is\n\n$$\nc = \\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{-13}\n$$" "First, we rewrite the given equation as\n$c^2 = 13^2$.\n\nNext, we take the square root of both sides of the equation and solve the resulting absolute value equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}^{2} & ={13}^{2} \\\\ \\sqrt{{c}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{{13}^{2}} \\\\ |c| & =|13| \\\\ |c| & =13 \\\\ c & =\\pm 13\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the **negative** solution to the given equation is\n\n$$\nc = \\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{-13}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186208391 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle 3\\begin{vmatrix}4 - a & -7 & 4 - a\\end{vmatrix} = -60\n$$\nThe positive solution of the equation given above is $a =$" "$$\n3|4 - a| - 7|4 - a| = -60\n$$\nThe positive solution of the equation given above is $a =$"] [:db/cas 17592186208391 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the absolute value terms contain the same expression. So, by combining these like terms, the equation becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3\\begin{vmatrix}4 - a & -7 & 4 - a\\end{vmatrix} &= -60 \\\\\n- 4 \\mid 4 - a \\mid &= -60\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, dividing both sides by $-4$, we get\n\n$$\n\\mid 4 - a \\mid = 15\n$$\n\nNext, we consider both possible cases for the absolute value.\n\n- If $\\mid 4 - a \\mid = 4 - a$, then\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\mid 4 - a \\mid |= 15; 4 - a|= 15; - a|= 11; a|=-11.\n$$\n- If $\\mid 4 - a \\mid = -(4 - a)$, then\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\mid 4 - a \\mid |= 15; - (4 - a)|= 15; - 4 + a|= 15; a|= 19.\n$$\n\nSince the question asks for the positive solution, the correct answer is\n\n$$\na=\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{19}\n$$" "First, notice that the absolute value terms contain the same expression. So, by combining these like terms, the equation becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3|4-a|-7|4-a| & =-60 \\\\ -4|4-a| & =-60.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, dividing both sides by $-4$, we get\n\n$$\n\\mid 4 - a \\mid = 15\n$$\n\nNext, we consider both possible cases for the absolute value.\n\n- If $\\mid 4 - a \\mid = 4 - a$, then\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\mid 4 - a \\mid |= 15; 4 - a|= 15; - a|= 11; a|=-11.\n$$\n- If $\\mid 4 - a \\mid = -(4 - a)$, then\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\mid 4 - a \\mid |= 15; - (4 - a)|= 15; - 4 + a|= 15; a|= 19.\n$$\n\nSince the question asks for the positive solution, the correct answer is\n\n$$\na=\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{19}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186208402 :question/worked-solution "We are given the following system:\n\n$$\n{y = 49; y = x^{2} - 2x + 1\n$$\n\nSubstituting $y = 49$ into the second equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} - 2x + 1 &= 49 \\\\\nx^{2} - 2x + 1 - 49 &= 0 \\\\\nx^{2} - 2x - 48 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe solve this quadratic equation by factoring. We begin by finding two numbers that multiply to $-48$ and add to $-2$. These are $-8$ and $6{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} - 2x - 48 &= 0 \\\\\nx^{2}\\underbrace{-8x + 6x}_{-2x} - 48 &= 0 \\\\\nx(x - 8) + 6(x - 8) &= 0 \\\\\n(x - 8)(x + 6) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the zero product rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x - 8 = 0 & orx + 6 = 0 \\\\ x = 8 & orx =-6\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, among the given options, the possible value of $x$ is $8$." "We are given the following system:\n\n$$\n{y = 49; y = x^{2} - 2x + 1\n$$\n\nSubstituting $y = 49$ into the second equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} - 2x + 1 &= 49 \\\\\nx^{2} - 2x + 1 - 49 &= 0 \\\\\nx^{2} - 2x - 48 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe solve this quadratic equation by factoring. We begin by finding two numbers that multiply to $-48$ and add to $-2$. These are $-8$ and $6{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} - 2x - 48 &= 0 \\\\\nx^{2}\\underbrace{-8x + 6x}_{-2x} - 48 &= 0 \\\\\nx(x - 8) + 6(x - 8) &= 0 \\\\\n(x - 8)(x + 6) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the zero product rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-8=0\\hspace{2em} & \\text{or}\\hspace{2em}x+6=0 \\\\ x=8\\hspace{2em} & \\text{or}\\hspace{2em}x=-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, among the given options, the possible value of $x$ is $8$."] [:db/cas 17592186208427 :question/worked-solution "We want to find all possible values of $y$ such that the point $(x, y)$ satisfies both inequalities.\n\nSince $x < -2$, the largest possible value of $5x - 3$, which serves as the upper bound for $y$, occurs as $x$ is near $-2$.\n\nTo identify this boundary value, we substitute $x = -2$ into the expression:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}y & < 5x - 3 \\\\ y & < 5(-2) - 3 \\\\ y & < - 13\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSo, the values of $y$ that satisfy the system are $y < -13$." "We want to find all possible values of $y$ such that the point $(x, y)$ satisfies both inequalities.\n\nSince $x < -2$, the largest possible value of $5x - 3$, which serves as the upper bound for $y$, occurs as $x$ is near $-2$.\n\nTo identify this boundary value, we substitute $x = -2$ into the expression:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & <5x-3 \\\\ y & <5(-2)-3 \\\\ y & <-13\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the values of $y$ that satisfy the system are $y < -13$."] [:db/cas 17592186208428 :question/worked-solution "We substitute $y = 10$ into each inequality to determine which $x$-values satisfy the system of inequalities.\n\n- Substituting $y = 10$ into the first inequality, we obtain\n$10 \\leq 12. \\quad \\color{green}\\checkmark$\n- Substituting $y = 10$ into the second inequality $y > \\dfrac{x}{2} + 2$ and solving for $x$, we get:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}10 & > \\frac{x}{2} + 2 \\\\ 8 & > \\frac{x}{2} \\\\ x & < 16\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, among the answer choices, only $x = 15$ satisfies both inequalities." "We substitute $y = 10$ into each inequality to determine which $x$-values satisfy the system of inequalities.\n\n- Substituting $y = 10$ into the first inequality, we obtain\n$10 \\leq 12. \\quad \\color{green}\\checkmark$\n- Substituting $y = 10$ into the second inequality $y > \\dfrac{x}{2} + 2$ and solving for $x$, we get:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10 & >\\frac{x}{2}+2 \\\\ 8 & >\\frac{x}{2} \\\\ x & <16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, among the answer choices, only $x = 15$ satisfies both inequalities."] [:db/cas 17592186208429 :question/worked-solution "We substitute each ordered pair into the inequalities to check which one satisfies both conditions.\n\n- For $(2, 3)$:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}y > 2x - 1 & ⇒3 > 2(2) - 1 = 3\\cdot \\\\ x + y < 4 & ⇒2 + 3 = 5 < 4\\cdot\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n- For $(1, 1)$:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}y > 2x - 1 & ⇒1 > 2(1) - 1 = 1\\cdot \\\\ x + y < 4 & ⇒1 + 1 = 2 < 4✓\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n- For $(2, 2)$:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}y > 2x - 1 & ⇒2 > 2(2) - 1 = 3\\cdot \\\\ x + y < 4 & ⇒2 + 2 = 4 < 4\\cdot\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n- For $(1, 2)$:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}y > 2x - 1 & ⇒2 > 2(1) - 1 = 1✓ \\\\ x + y < 4 & ⇒1 + 2 = 3 < 4✓\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, only the pair $(1, 2)$ satisfies both inequalities." "We substitute each ordered pair into the inequalities to check which one satisfies both conditions.\n\n- For $(2, 3)$:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & y>2x-1 &  & \\hspace{1em}\\Rightarrow \\hspace{1em}3>2(2)-1=3\\hspace{1em}{\\color{red}{\\times }} \\\\  & x+y<4 &  & \\hspace{1em}\\Rightarrow \\hspace{1em}2+3=5<4\\hspace{1em}{\\color{red}{\\times }}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- For $(1, 1)$:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & y>2x-1 &  & \\hspace{1em}\\Rightarrow \\hspace{1em}1>2(1)-1=1\\hspace{1em}{\\color{red}{\\times }} \\\\  & x+y<4 &  & \\hspace{1em}\\Rightarrow \\hspace{1em}1+1=2<4\\hspace{1em}{\\color{green}{✓}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- For $(2, 2)$:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & y>2x-1 &  & \\hspace{1em}\\Rightarrow \\hspace{1em}2>2(2)-1=3\\hspace{1em}{\\color{red}{\\times }} \\\\  & x+y<4 &  & \\hspace{1em}\\Rightarrow \\hspace{1em}2+2=4<4\\hspace{1em}{\\color{red}{\\times }}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For $(1, 2)$:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & y>2x-1 &  & \\hspace{1em}\\Rightarrow \\hspace{1em}2>2(1)-1=1\\hspace{1em}{\\color{green}{✓}} \\\\  & x+y<4 &  & \\hspace{1em}\\Rightarrow \\hspace{1em}1+2=3<4\\hspace{1em}{\\color{green}{✓}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, only the pair $(1, 2)$ satisfies both inequalities."] [:db/cas 17592186208432 :question/worked-solution "We are told that, according to the triangle inequality theorem, the sum of any two sides of a triangle must be greater than the third side.\n\nApplying the triangle inequality to the three combinations of side lengths, we obtain the following three inequalities:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}7 + 10 & > x \\\\ 7 + x & > 10 \\\\ 10 + x & > 7\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, we simplify each inequality:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}x & < 17 \\\\ x & > 3 \\\\ x & > - 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNext, since side lengths are positive, the valid lower bound is $x > 3$. So, we have\n$3 < x < 17$.\n\nTherefore, the correct inequality is $3 < x < 17$." "We are told that, according to the triangle inequality theorem, the sum of any two sides of a triangle must be greater than the third side.\n\nApplying the triangle inequality to the three combinations of side lengths, we obtain the following three inequalities:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7+10 & >x \\\\ 7+x & >10 \\\\ 10+x & >7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we simplify each inequality:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & <17 \\\\ x & >3 \\\\ x & >-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, since side lengths are positive, the valid lower bound is $x > 3$. So, we have\n$3 < x < 17$.\n\nTherefore, the correct inequality is $3 < x < 17$."] [:db/cas 17592186208448 :question/worked-solution "In $\\triangle ABC$, the sum of the measures of the interior angles is always $180^\\circ$.\n\nThus, the sum of the measures of $\\angle A$ and $\\angle B$ must be *less than* $180^\\circ$, as there must be room for $\\angle C$. This means\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}m∠A + m∠B & < 180^{∘} \\\\ 67^{∘} + m∠B & < 180^{∘} \\\\ m∠B & < 113^{∘}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, among the choices, only $109^\\circ$ could be the measure of $\\angle B$." "In $\\triangle ABC$, the sum of the measures of the interior angles is always $180^\\circ$.\n\nThus, the sum of the measures of $\\angle A$ and $\\angle B$ must be *less than* $180^\\circ$, as there must be room for $\\angle C$. This means\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m\\mathrm{∠}A+m\\mathrm{∠}B & <{180}^{∘} \\\\ {67}^{∘}+m\\mathrm{∠}B & <{180}^{∘} \\\\ m\\mathrm{∠}B & <{113}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, among the choices, only $109^\\circ$ could be the measure of $\\angle B$."] [:db/cas 17592186208532 :question/worked-solution "We are asked to determine which table contains only points that satisfy the system of inequalities $v > u + 1$ and $u > 8$. To do this, we check that each point satisfies both conditions.\n\nThe correct table is the following:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$u$</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$v$</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$9$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$11$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$10$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$12$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$12$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$15$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nNotice that for the points $(9, 11)$, $(10, 12)$, and $(12, 15)$, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}11 & > 9 + 1 = 10✓ \\\\ 12 & > 10 + 1 = 11✓ \\\\ 15 & > 12 + 1 = 13✓\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nand\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}9 & > 8✓ \\\\ 10 & > 8✓ \\\\ 12 & > 8✓\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nBoth inequalities are satisfied for all points." "We are asked to determine which table contains only points that satisfy the system of inequalities $v > u + 1$ and $u > 8$. To do this, we check that each point satisfies both conditions.\n\nThe correct table is the following:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$u$</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$v$</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$9$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$11$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$10$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$12$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$12$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$15$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nNotice that for the points $(9, 11)$, $(10, 12)$, and $(12, 15)$, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}11 & >9+1=10\\hspace{1em}{\\color{green}{✓}} \\\\ 12 & >10+1=11\\hspace{1em}{\\color{green}{✓}} \\\\ 15 & >12+1=13\\hspace{1em}{\\color{green}{✓}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9 & >8\\hspace{1em}{\\color{green}{✓}} \\\\ 10 & >8\\hspace{1em}{\\color{green}{✓}} \\\\ 12 & >8\\hspace{1em}{\\color{green}{✓}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBoth inequalities are satisfied for all points."] [:db/cas 17592186209211 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle 3\\begin{vmatrix}2 - x & + 2\\end{vmatrix}2 - x|= 20\n$$\nThe positive solution of the equation given above is $x =$ {{field-1}}." "$$\n3|2-x|+2|2-x|=20\n$$\nThe positive solution of the equation given above is $x =$ {{field-1}}."] [:db/cas 17592186209351 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle 3\\begin{vmatrix}4 - a & -7\\end{vmatrix}4 - a|=-60\n$$\nThe positive solution of the equation given above is $a =$ {{field-1}}." "$$\n3|4-a|-7|4-a|=-60\n$$\nThe positive solution of the equation given above is $a =$ {{field-1}}."] [:db/cas 17592186212995 :question/worked-solution "For our equation to have infinitely many solutions, both sides of our equation must be equivalent expressions. So, we must match our coefficients and constants:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{5}{6}ax + (-4) &= \\frac{5}{2}x + (8b)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis yields the two equations\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{5}{6}a &= \\frac{5}{2} & and - 4|= 8b \\\\ a|= \\frac{6}{5} \\cdot \\frac{5}{2} & andb|=-\\frac{4}{8} \\\\ a|= 3 & andb|=-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{a}{b}=\\displaystyle{\\dfrac{3}{-1/2}=3\\cdot(-2)=-6}\n$$" "For our equation to have infinitely many solutions, both sides of our equation must be equivalent expressions. So, we must match our coefficients and constants:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{5}{6}ax + (-4) &= \\frac{5}{2}x + (8b)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis yields the two equations\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{6}a & =\\frac{5}{2}\\hspace{2em} & \\text{and}\\hspace{2em}-4 & =8b \\\\ a & =\\frac{6}{5}\\cdot \\frac{5}{2}\\hspace{2em} & \\text{and}\\hspace{2em}b & =-\\frac{4}{8} \\\\ a & =3\\hspace{2em} & \\text{and}\\hspace{2em}b & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{a}{b}=\\displaystyle{\\dfrac{3}{-1/2}=3\\cdot(-2)=-6}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186216808 :question/worked-solution "A quadratic equation has no real solutions if the discriminant is negative: $\\mathcal{D} < 0$.\n\nTo compute the discriminant, note the following coefficients: $a = k$, $b = 6$ and $c = k$.\n\nSo, we require\n\n$$\nD\\begin{vmatrix}< 0 \\\\ b^{2} - 4ac\\end{vmatrix}< 0; (6)^{2} - 4(k)(k)\\begin{vmatrix}< 0 \\\\ 36 - 4k^{2}\\end{vmatrix}< 0; 36\\begin{vmatrix}< 4k^{2} \\\\ k^{2}\\end{vmatrix}> 9; \\sqrt{k^{2}} \\mid > \\sqrt{9}; \\mid k \\mid \\mid > 3\n$$\n\nIf $\\mid k \\mid > 3$, then $k \\lt -3$ or $k \\gt 3$. Using interval notation, then, our solution is $k \\in (-\\infty, -3) \\cup (3, \\infty)$." "A quadratic equation has no real solutions if the discriminant is negative: $\\mathcal{D} < 0$.\n\nTo compute the discriminant, note the following coefficients: $a = k$, $b = 6$ and $c = k$.\n\nSo, we require\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathcal{D} & <0 \\\\ {b}^{2}-4ac & <0 \\\\ (6)^{2}-4(k)(k) & <0 \\\\ 36-4{k}^{2} & <0 \\\\ 36 & <4{k}^{2} \\\\ {k}^{2} & >9 \\\\ \\sqrt{{k}^{2}} & >\\sqrt{9} \\\\ |k| & >3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $\\mid k \\mid > 3$, then $k \\lt -3$ or $k \\gt 3$. Using interval notation, then, our solution is $k \\in (-\\infty, -3) \\cup (3, \\infty)$."] [:db/cas 17592186216810 :question/worked-solution "A quadratic equation has no real solutions if the discriminant is negative: $\\mathcal{D} < 0$.\n\nFirst, rewrite the equation in standard quadratic form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx(px - 24) &= -9 \\\\\npx^{2} - 24x &= -9 \\\\\npx^{2} - 24x + 9 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe coefficients of our quadratic equation are the following:\n\n$$\na = p, \\qquad b = -24, \\qquad c = 9\n$$\n\nSo, computing the discriminant, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nD &= b^{2} - 4ac \\\\\n&= (-24)^{2} - 4(p)(9) \\\\\n&= 576 - 36p\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo have no real solutions, we need $\\mathcal{D} < 0{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}576 - 36p & < 0 \\\\ -36p & < - 576 \\\\ p & > \\frac{-576}{-36} \\\\ p & > 16\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSince $p$ must be an integer, we conclude that the least possible value is $17$." "A quadratic equation has no real solutions if the discriminant is negative: $\\mathcal{D} < 0$.\n\nFirst, rewrite the equation in standard quadratic form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx(px - 24) &= -9 \\\\\npx^{2} - 24x &= -9 \\\\\npx^{2} - 24x + 9 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe coefficients of our quadratic equation are the following:\n\n$$\na = p, \\qquad b = -24, \\qquad c = 9\n$$\n\nSo, computing the discriminant, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nD &= b^{2} - 4ac \\\\\n&= (-24)^{2} - 4(p)(9) \\\\\n&= 576 - 36p\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo have no real solutions, we need $\\mathcal{D} < 0{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}576-36p & <0 \\\\ -36p & <-576 \\\\ p & >\\frac{-576}{-36} \\\\ p & >16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $p$ must be an integer, we conclude that the least possible value is $17$."] [:db/cas 17592186216813 :question/worked-solution "The line $y = m$ intersects the parabola\n\n$$\ny = 4x^2 - 8x + 7\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n4x^2 - 8x + 7 = m\n$$\n\nA quadratic equation has two distinct real roots if the discriminant is positive: $\\mathcal{D} > 0$.\n\nFirst, rewrite the equation in standard quadratic form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4x^{2} - 8x + 7 &= m \\\\\n4x^{2} - 8x + 7 - m &= 0 \\\\\n4x^{2} - 8x + (7 - m) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe coefficients of our quadratic equation are the following:\n\n$$\na = 4, \\qquad b = -8, \\qquad c = 7 - m\n$$\n\nSo, computing the discriminant, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nD &= b^{2} - 4ac \\\\\n&= (-8)^{2} - 4(4)(7 - m) \\\\\n&= 64 - 112 + 16m \\\\\n&=-48 + 16m\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor two distinct real solutions, we need $\\mathcal{D} > 0{:}$\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}-48 + 16m & > 0 \\\\ 16m & > 48 \\\\ m & > 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSince $m$ must be an integer, we conclude that the least possible value is\n$4$." "The line $y = m$ intersects the parabola\n\n$$\ny = 4x^2 - 8x + 7\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n4x^2 - 8x + 7 = m\n$$\n\nA quadratic equation has two distinct real roots if the discriminant is positive: $\\mathcal{D} > 0$.\n\nFirst, rewrite the equation in standard quadratic form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4x^{2} - 8x + 7 &= m \\\\\n4x^{2} - 8x + 7 - m &= 0 \\\\\n4x^{2} - 8x + (7 - m) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe coefficients of our quadratic equation are the following:\n\n$$\na = 4, \\qquad b = -8, \\qquad c = 7 - m\n$$\n\nSo, computing the discriminant, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nD &= b^{2} - 4ac \\\\\n&= (-8)^{2} - 4(4)(7 - m) \\\\\n&= 64 - 112 + 16m \\\\\n&=-48 + 16m\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor two distinct real solutions, we need $\\mathcal{D} > 0{:}$\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-48+16m & >0 \\\\ 16m & >48 \\\\ m & >3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $m$ must be an integer, we conclude that the least possible value is\n$4$."] [:db/cas 17592186216817 :question/worked-solution "The line $y=k$ intersects the parabola\n\n$$\ny = -3x^2 - 6x + 9\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n-3x^2 - 6x + 9 = k\n$$\n\nA quadratic equation has no real roots if the discriminant is negative: $\\mathcal{D} < 0$.\n\nFirst, rewrite the equation in standard quadratic form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-3x^{2} - 6x + 9 &= k \\\\\n- 3x^{2} - 6x + 9 - k &= 0 \\\\\n- 3x^{2} - 6x + (9 - k) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe coefficients of our quadratic equation are the following:\n\n$$\na = -3, \\qquad b = -6, \\qquad c = 9-k\n$$\n\nSo, computing the discriminant, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nD &= b^{2} - 4ac \\\\\n&= (-6)^{2} - 4(-3)(9 - k) \\\\\n&= 36 + 12(9 - k) \\\\\n&= 36 + 108 - 12k \\\\\n&= 144 - 12k\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor no real solutions, we need $\\mathcal{D} < 0{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}144 - 12k & < 0 \\\\ -12k & < - 144 \\\\ 12k & > 144 \\\\ k & > \\frac{144}{12} \\\\ k & > 12\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSince $k$ must be an integer, we conclude that the smallest possible value is $13$." "The line $y=k$ intersects the parabola\n\n$$\ny = -3x^2 - 6x + 9\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n-3x^2 - 6x + 9 = k\n$$\n\nA quadratic equation has no real roots if the discriminant is negative: $\\mathcal{D} < 0$.\n\nFirst, rewrite the equation in standard quadratic form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-3x^{2} - 6x + 9 &= k \\\\\n- 3x^{2} - 6x + 9 - k &= 0 \\\\\n- 3x^{2} - 6x + (9 - k) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe coefficients of our quadratic equation are the following:\n\n$$\na = -3, \\qquad b = -6, \\qquad c = 9-k\n$$\n\nSo, computing the discriminant, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nD &= b^{2} - 4ac \\\\\n&= (-6)^{2} - 4(-3)(9 - k) \\\\\n&= 36 + 12(9 - k) \\\\\n&= 36 + 108 - 12k \\\\\n&= 144 - 12k\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor no real solutions, we need $\\mathcal{D} < 0{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}144-12k & <0 \\\\ -12k & <-144 \\\\ 12k & >144 \\\\ k & >\\frac{144}{12} \\\\ k & >12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $k$ must be an integer, we conclude that the smallest possible value is $13$."] [:db/cas 17592186216822 :question/worked-solution "First, recall that the parabola is symmetric about the vertical line passing through the vertex. Because $g(3) = g(9)$, the vertex is midway between $x_1 = 3$ and $x_2 = 9$.\n\nSo, the $x$-coordinate of the vertex is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nh &= \\frac{x_{1} + x_{2}}{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{3 + 9}{2} \\\\\n&= 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince at the vertex, $g(h) = k$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ng(6) &= a(6)^{2} - 12(6) + c \\\\\nk &= 36a - 72 + c\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that the parabola has vertex below the $x$-axis, $k < 0$, we have\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}36a - 72 + c & < 0 \\\\ 36a + c & < 72\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, we check each statement:\n\n- Statement I is false because $c$ does not always have to be negative. For example, if the parabola opens upward with $a = 1 > 0$, we get\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}36(1) + c & < 72 \\\\ c & < 36\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\nwhich could be positive.\n- Statement II is true. Since the parabola opens upward, we must have $a > 0$.\n- Statement III is false because $c$ does not always have to be positive. For example, if the parabola opens upward with $a = 3 > 0$, we get\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}36(3) + c & < 72 \\\\ c & < - 36\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\nwhich is negative.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"." "First, recall that the parabola is symmetric about the vertical line passing through the vertex. Because $g(3) = g(9)$, the vertex is midway between $x_1 = 3$ and $x_2 = 9$.\n\nSo, the $x$-coordinate of the vertex is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nh &= \\frac{x_{1} + x_{2}}{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{3 + 9}{2} \\\\\n&= 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince at the vertex, $g(h) = k$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ng(6) &= a(6)^{2} - 12(6) + c \\\\\nk &= 36a - 72 + c\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that the parabola has vertex below the $x$-axis, $k < 0$, we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}36a-72+c & <0 \\\\ 36a+c & <72.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we check each statement:\n\n- Statement I is false because $c$ does not always have to be negative. For example, if the parabola opens upward with $a = 1 > 0$, we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}36(1)+c & <72 \\\\ c & <36,\\end{aligned}\n$$\nwhich could be positive.\n- Statement II is true. Since the parabola opens upward, we must have $a > 0$.\n- Statement III is false because $c$ does not always have to be positive. For example, if the parabola opens upward with $a = 3 > 0$, we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}36(3)+c & <72 \\\\ c & <-36,\\end{aligned}\n$$\nwhich is negative.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"."] [:db/cas 17592186216849 :question/worked-solution "The line $y=k-3x$ intersects the parabola\n\n$$\ny=3x^2 - 12x + 8\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n3x^2 - 12x + 8 = k - 3x\n$$\n\nA quadratic equation has two distinct real roots if the discriminant is positive: $\\mathcal{D} > 0$.\n\nFirst, rewrite the equation in standard quadratic form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3x^{2} - 12x + 8 &= k - 3x \\\\\n3x^{2} - 12x + 8 + 3x - k &= 0 \\\\\n3x^{2} - 9x + (8 - k) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe coefficients of our quadratic equation are the following:\n\n$$\na = 3, \\qquad b = -9, \\qquad c = 8-k\n$$\n\nSo, computing the discriminant, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nD &= b^{2} - 4ac \\\\\n&= (-9)^{2} - 4(3)(8 - k) \\\\\n&= 81 - 96 + 12k \\\\\n&=-15 + 12k\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor two distinct real solutions, we need $\\mathcal{D} > 0{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-15 + 12k & > 0 \\\\ 12k & > 15 \\\\ k & > \\frac{15}{12} \\\\ k & > 1.25\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSince $k$ must be an integer, we conclude that the least possible value is $2$." "The line $y=k-3x$ intersects the parabola\n\n$$\ny=3x^2 - 12x + 8\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n3x^2 - 12x + 8 = k - 3x\n$$\n\nA quadratic equation has two distinct real roots if the discriminant is positive: $\\mathcal{D} > 0$.\n\nFirst, rewrite the equation in standard quadratic form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3x^{2} - 12x + 8 &= k - 3x \\\\\n3x^{2} - 12x + 8 + 3x - k &= 0 \\\\\n3x^{2} - 9x + (8 - k) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe coefficients of our quadratic equation are the following:\n\n$$\na = 3, \\qquad b = -9, \\qquad c = 8-k\n$$\n\nSo, computing the discriminant, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nD &= b^{2} - 4ac \\\\\n&= (-9)^{2} - 4(3)(8 - k) \\\\\n&= 81 - 96 + 12k \\\\\n&=-15 + 12k\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor two distinct real solutions, we need $\\mathcal{D} > 0{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-15+12k & >0 \\\\ 12k & >15 \\\\ k & >\\frac{15}{12} \\\\ k & >1.25\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $k$ must be an integer, we conclude that the least possible value is $2$."] [:db/cas 17592186216850 :question/worked-solution "The line\n\n$$\ny=x+k\n$$\n\nintersects the parabola\n\n$$\ny = 2x^2 - 4x + 8\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n2x^2 - 4x + 8 = x+k\n$$\n\nA quadratic equation has no real roots if the discriminant is negative: $\\mathcal{D} < 0$.\n\nFirst, rewrite the equation in standard quadratic form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2x^{2} - 4x + 8 &= x + k \\\\\n2x^{2} - 4x + 8 - x - k &= 0 \\\\\n2x^{2} - 5x + (8 - k) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe coefficients of our quadratic equation are the following:\n\n$$\na = 2, \\qquad b = -5, \\qquad c = 8-k\n$$\n\nSo, computing the discriminant, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nD &= b^{2} - 4ac \\\\\n&= (-5)^{2} - 4(2)(8 - k) \\\\\n&= 25 - 8(8 - k) \\\\\n&= 25 - 64 + 8k \\\\\n&= 8k - 39\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor no real solutions, we need $\\mathcal{D} < 0{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}8k - 39 & < 0 \\\\ 8k & < 39 \\\\ k & < \\frac{39}{8} \\\\ k & < 4.875\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSince $k$ must be an integer, we conclude that the greatest possible value is $4$." "The line\n\n$$\ny=x+k\n$$\n\nintersects the parabola\n\n$$\ny = 2x^2 - 4x + 8\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n2x^2 - 4x + 8 = x+k\n$$\n\nA quadratic equation has no real roots if the discriminant is negative: $\\mathcal{D} < 0$.\n\nFirst, rewrite the equation in standard quadratic form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2x^{2} - 4x + 8 &= x + k \\\\\n2x^{2} - 4x + 8 - x - k &= 0 \\\\\n2x^{2} - 5x + (8 - k) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe coefficients of our quadratic equation are the following:\n\n$$\na = 2, \\qquad b = -5, \\qquad c = 8-k\n$$\n\nSo, computing the discriminant, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nD &= b^{2} - 4ac \\\\\n&= (-5)^{2} - 4(2)(8 - k) \\\\\n&= 25 - 8(8 - k) \\\\\n&= 25 - 64 + 8k \\\\\n&= 8k - 39\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor no real solutions, we need $\\mathcal{D} < 0{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8k-39 & <0 \\\\ 8k & <39 \\\\ k & <\\frac{39}{8} \\\\ k & <4.875\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $k$ must be an integer, we conclude that the greatest possible value is $4$."] [:db/cas 17592186216888 :question/worked-solution "To compute the discriminant, note the following coefficients: $a=2$, $b=-2k$ and $c=3k-4$.\n\nSo, we require\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}D & < 0 \\\\ b^{2} - 4ac & < 0 \\\\ (-2k)^{2} - 4(2)(3k - 4) & < 0 \\\\ 4k^{2} - 8(3k - 4) & < 0 \\\\ 4k^{2} - 24k + 32 & < 0 \\\\ k^{2} - 6k + 8 & < 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, let's consider the parabola\n\n$$\ny=k^2 - 6k + 8\n$$\n\nand find its solutions. We obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nk^{2} - 6k + 8 &= 0 \\\\\nk^{2} - 2k - 4k + 8 &= 0 \\\\\nk(k - 2) - 4(k - 2) &= 0 \\\\\n(k - 2)(k - 4) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the solutions are $k=2$ and $k=4$. Since the leading coefficient is positive, the parabola opens upward. Therefore, we get the following graph:\n\n![](images/32/32dd90a9598bbe7d98af1fb3f4c13c830c8de166b679e211c92ff8c073b92452.png)\n\nWe want\n\n$$\ny =k^2 - 6k + 8 < 0\n$$\n\nmeaning that the graph lies below the $k$-axis. Based on the graph, the corresponding interval is $(2,4)$.\n\nTherefore, the given equation has no real solutions when $k \\in (2, 4)$." "To compute the discriminant, note the following coefficients: $a=2$, $b=-2k$ and $c=3k-4$.\n\nSo, we require\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathcal{D} & <0 \\\\ {b}^{2}-4ac & <0 \\\\ (-2k)^{2}-4(2)(3k-4) & <0 \\\\ 4{k}^{2}-8(3k-4) & <0 \\\\ 4{k}^{2}-24k+32 & <0 \\\\ {k}^{2}-6k+8 & <0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's consider the parabola\n\n$$\ny=k^2 - 6k + 8\n$$\n\nand find its solutions. We obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nk^{2} - 6k + 8 &= 0 \\\\\nk^{2} - 2k - 4k + 8 &= 0 \\\\\nk(k - 2) - 4(k - 2) &= 0 \\\\\n(k - 2)(k - 4) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the solutions are $k=2$ and $k=4$. Since the leading coefficient is positive, the parabola opens upward. Therefore, we get the following graph:\n\n![](images/32/32dd90a9598bbe7d98af1fb3f4c13c830c8de166b679e211c92ff8c073b92452.png)\n\nWe want\n\n$$\ny =k^2 - 6k + 8 < 0\n$$\n\nmeaning that the graph lies below the $k$-axis. Based on the graph, the corresponding interval is $(2,4)$.\n\nTherefore, the given equation has no real solutions when $k \\in (2, 4)$."] [:db/cas 17592186216918 :question/worked-solution "![](images/77/774314ec80771e07338c2217da497b8a83b1795356756188875f7070a5275cb5.png)\n\nThe perpendicular bisector of the chord must pass through the circle's center. Therefore, the perpendicular bisectors of two different chords of the same circle must intersect at the center.\n\nSo, to find the center of the circle, we need to find where the perpendicular bisectors $l_1$ and $l_2$ intersect.\n\nThe equations of the lines are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n{y &= -x \\\\\ny &= 2x + 12\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to each other, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-x &= 2x + 12 \\\\\n- 3x &= 12 \\\\\nx &= -4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $x=-4$ into the first equation, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= -x = - (-4) = 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the circle's center is at $(-4,4)$." "![](images/77/774314ec80771e07338c2217da497b8a83b1795356756188875f7070a5275cb5.png)\n\nThe perpendicular bisector of the chord must pass through the circle's center. Therefore, the perpendicular bisectors of two different chords of the same circle must intersect at the center.\n\nSo, to find the center of the circle, we need to find where the perpendicular bisectors $l_1$ and $l_2$ intersect.\n\nThe equations of the lines are\n\n$$\n\\begin{cases}\\begin{aligned}y & =-x \\\\ y & =2x+12.\\end{aligned}\\end{cases}\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to each other, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-x &= 2x + 12 \\\\\n- 3x &= 12 \\\\\nx &= -4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $x=-4$ into the first equation, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= -x = - (-4) = 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the circle's center is at $(-4,4)$."] [:db/cas 17592186216982 :question/worked-solution "Let's compute the mean absolute deviation for Team B's scores.\n\nSince we are given Team B's mean (${\\color{blue}5}$), we subtract it from each number in the data set, find the absolute value of the results, and add all these together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}7 - 5 & + & 5 - 5 & + & 8 - 5 & + & 4 - 5 & + & 1 - 5\\end{vmatrix} &= \\begin{vmatrix}2 & + & 0 & + & 3 & + & -1 & + & -4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &= 2 + 0 + 3 + 1 + 4 \\\\ &= 10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to calculate the mean absolute deviation (MAD), we divide the sum obtained above (${\\color{red}10}$) by the total number of data points ($5$):\n\n$$\n\\text{MAD} = \\dfrac{\\color{red}10}{5} = 2\n$$\n\nSo, Team B's scores have a mean absolute deviation of $2$ home runs.\n\nLet's now examine the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is false.\n- Statement II is true, while statement III is false. Since $1.5 < 2$, Team $A$'s home run scores had a smaller spread than Team $B$'s.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" "Let's compute the mean absolute deviation for Team B's scores.\n\nSince we are given Team B's mean (${\\color{blue}5}$), we subtract it from each number in the data set, find the absolute value of the results, and add all these together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|7-{\\color{blue}{5}}|+|5-{\\color{blue}{5}}|+|8-{\\color{blue}{5}}|+|4-{\\color{blue}{5}}|+|1-{\\color{blue}{5}}| &= |\\hspace{0.167em}2\\hspace{0.167em}|+|\\hspace{0.167em}0\\hspace{0.167em}|+|\\hspace{0.167em}3\\hspace{0.167em}|+|-1|+|-4| \\\\ &= 2+0+3+1+4 \\\\ &= {\\color{red}{10}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to calculate the mean absolute deviation (MAD), we divide the sum obtained above (${\\color{red}10}$) by the total number of data points ($5$):\n\n$$\n\\text{MAD} = \\dfrac{\\color{red}10}{5} = 2\n$$\n\nSo, Team B's scores have a mean absolute deviation of $2$ home runs.\n\nLet's now examine the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is false.\n- Statement II is true, while statement III is false. Since $1.5 < 2$, Team $A$'s home run scores had a smaller spread than Team $B$'s.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""] [:db/cas 17592186218232 :tutorial/content "Recall that for a quadratic equation\n\n$$\nax^2 + bx + c = 0\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\na\\neq 0\n$$\n\nthe discriminant is defined as\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{D} = b^2 - 4ac\n$$\n\nA quadratic equation has no real solutions if the discriminant is negative: $\\mathcal{D} < 0$.\n\nIf a quadratic equation contains an unknown parameter and we know the equation has no real solutions, we can use the condition $\\mathcal D < 0$ to place conditions on the unknown parameter.\n\nTo demonstrate, let's consider the equation\n\n$$\n2x^2-5kx+10 = 0\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is a real parameter. We wish to find *all* the values of $k$ for which the quadratic equation has no real solutions.\n\nTo compute the discriminant, we first note that the coefficients are\n\n$$\na=2,\\quad b=-5k,\\quad c=10\n$$\n\nSo, we require\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}D & < 0 \\\\ b^{2} - 4ac & < 0 \\\\ (-5k)^{2} - 4(2)(10) & < 0 \\\\ 25k^{2} - 80 & < 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis is a quadratic inequality in the variable $k$. Solving this equation for $k$, we get the following:\n\n$$\n25k^{2} - 80\\begin{vmatrix}< 0 \\\\ 25k^{2}\\end{vmatrix}< 80; k^{2}\\begin{vmatrix}< \\frac{16}{5} \\\\ \\sqrt{k^{2}}\\end{vmatrix}< \\sqrt{\\frac{16}{5}}; \\mid k \\mid \\mid < \\frac{4}{\\sqrt{5}}; \\mid k \\mid \\mid < \\frac{4\\sqrt{5}}{5}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our solution is\n\n$$\n-\\dfrac{4\\sqrt{5}}{5} < k < \\dfrac{4\\sqrt{5}}{5}\n$$\n\nWe can also express this solution using interval notation:\n\n$$\nk \\in \\left(-\\dfrac{4\\sqrt{5}}{5},\\dfrac{4\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\right)\n$$\n\nAny value of $k$ in this interval will give a quadratic equation with no real solutions." "Recall that for a quadratic equation\n\n$$\nax^2 + bx + c = 0\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\na\\neq 0\n$$\n\nthe discriminant is defined as\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{D} = b^2 - 4ac\n$$\n\nA quadratic equation has no real solutions if the discriminant is negative: $\\mathcal{D} < 0$.\n\nIf a quadratic equation contains an unknown parameter and we know the equation has no real solutions, we can use the condition $\\mathcal D < 0$ to place conditions on the unknown parameter.\n\nTo demonstrate, let's consider the equation\n\n$$\n2x^2-5kx+10 = 0\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is a real parameter. We wish to find *all* the values of $k$ for which the quadratic equation has no real solutions.\n\nTo compute the discriminant, we first note that the coefficients are\n\n$$\na=2,\\quad b=-5k,\\quad c=10\n$$\n\nSo, we require\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathcal{D} & <0 \\\\ {b}^{2}-4ac & <0 \\\\ (-5k)^{2}-4(2)(10) & <0 \\\\ 25{k}^{2}-80 & <0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a quadratic inequality in the variable $k$. Solving this equation for $k$, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}25{k}^{2}-80 & <0 \\\\ 25{k}^{2} & <80 \\\\ {k}^{2} & <\\frac{16}{5} \\\\ \\sqrt{{k}^{2}} & <\\sqrt{\\frac{16}{5}} \\\\ |k| & <\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ |k| & <\\frac{4\\sqrt{5}}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our solution is\n\n$$\n-\\dfrac{4\\sqrt{5}}{5} < k < \\dfrac{4\\sqrt{5}}{5}\n$$\n\nWe can also express this solution using interval notation:\n\n$$\nk \\in \\left(-\\dfrac{4\\sqrt{5}}{5},\\dfrac{4\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\right)\n$$\n\nAny value of $k$ in this interval will give a quadratic equation with no real solutions."] [:db/cas 17592186218246 :tutorial/content "In this lesson, we’ll learn how to use the discriminant to determine the values of unknown parameters that make a quadratic system have either two solutions (two intersection points) or no solutions (no intersection points).\n\nLet's consider the following quadratic system:\n\n$$\n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}y = 4 {x}^{2} - 8 x + 5 \\\\ y = m\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\n\nIn the system above, $m$ is an integer constant. Let's find the least possible value of $m$ such that the graphs of these functions intersect at two distinct points in the $xy$-plane.\n\nThe line $y=m$ intersects the parabola\n\n$$\ny=4x^2 - 8x + 5\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n4x^2 - 8x + 5 = m\n$$\n\nA quadratic equation has two distinct real roots if the discriminant is positive: $\\mathcal{D} > 0$.\n\nFirst, rewrite the equation in standard quadratic form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4x^{2} - 8x + 5 &= m \\\\\n4x^{2} - 8x + 5 - m &= 0 \\\\\n4x^{2} - 8x + (5 - m) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe coefficients of our quadratic equation are the following:\n\n$$\na = 4, \\qquad b = -8, \\qquad c = 5-m\n$$\n\nSo, computing the discriminant, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nD &= b^{2} - 4ac \\\\\n&= (-8)^{2} - 4(4)(5 - m) \\\\\n&= 64 - 80 + 16m \\\\\n&=-16 + 16m\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor two distinct real solutions, we need $\\mathcal{D} > 0{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-16 + 16m & > 0 \\\\ 16m & > 16 \\\\ m & > 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSince $m$ must be an integer, we conclude that the least possible value is $2$." "In this lesson, we’ll learn how to use the discriminant to determine the values of unknown parameters that make a quadratic system have either two solutions (two intersection points) or no solutions (no intersection points).\n\nLet's consider the following quadratic system:\n\n$$\n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}y = 4 {x}^{2} - 8 x + 5 \\\\ y = m\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\n\nIn the system above, $m$ is an integer constant. Let's find the least possible value of $m$ such that the graphs of these functions intersect at two distinct points in the $xy$-plane.\n\nThe line $y=m$ intersects the parabola\n\n$$\ny=4x^2 - 8x + 5\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n4x^2 - 8x + 5 = m\n$$\n\nA quadratic equation has two distinct real roots if the discriminant is positive: $\\mathcal{D} > 0$.\n\nFirst, rewrite the equation in standard quadratic form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4x^{2} - 8x + 5 &= m \\\\\n4x^{2} - 8x + 5 - m &= 0 \\\\\n4x^{2} - 8x + (5 - m) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe coefficients of our quadratic equation are the following:\n\n$$\na = 4, \\qquad b = -8, \\qquad c = 5-m\n$$\n\nSo, computing the discriminant, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nD &= b^{2} - 4ac \\\\\n&= (-8)^{2} - 4(4)(5 - m) \\\\\n&= 64 - 80 + 16m \\\\\n&=-16 + 16m\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor two distinct real solutions, we need $\\mathcal{D} > 0{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-16+16m & >0 \\\\ 16m & >16 \\\\ m & >1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $m$ must be an integer, we conclude that the least possible value is $2$."] [:db/cas 17592186218247 :tutorial/content "Now, let’s see an example where there are no intersection points.\n\nIf the graph of the equation\n\n$$\ny = x^2 - 4x + 7\n$$\n\ndoesn't intersect the line $y=k$ in the $xy$-plane, where $k$ is an integer constant, let's find the greatest possible value of $k$.\n\nThe line $y=k$ intersects the parabola\n\n$$\ny = x^2 - 4x + 7\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nx^2 - 4x + 7 = k\n$$\n\nA quadratic equation has no real roots if the discriminant is negative: $\\mathcal{D} < 0$.\n\nFirst, rewrite the equation in standard quadratic form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} - 4x + 7 &= k \\\\\nx^{2} - 4x + 7 - k &= 0 \\\\\nx^{2} - 4x + (7 - k) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe coefficients of our quadratic equation are the following:\n\n$$\na = 1, \\qquad b = -4, \\qquad c = 7-k\n$$\n\nSo, computing the discriminant, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nD &= (-4)^{2} - 4(1)(7 - k) \\\\\n&= 16 - 4(7 - k) \\\\\n&= 16 - 28 + 4k \\\\\n&= 4k - 12\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor no real solutions, we need $\\mathcal{D} < 0{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}4k - 12 & < 0 \\\\ 4k & < 12 \\\\ k & < \\frac{12}{4} \\\\ k & < 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSince $k$ must be an integer, we conclude that the greatest possible value is $2$." "Now, let’s see an example where there are no intersection points.\n\nIf the graph of the equation\n\n$$\ny = x^2 - 4x + 7\n$$\n\ndoesn't intersect the line $y=k$ in the $xy$-plane, where $k$ is an integer constant, let's find the greatest possible value of $k$.\n\nThe line $y=k$ intersects the parabola\n\n$$\ny = x^2 - 4x + 7\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nx^2 - 4x + 7 = k\n$$\n\nA quadratic equation has no real roots if the discriminant is negative: $\\mathcal{D} < 0$.\n\nFirst, rewrite the equation in standard quadratic form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} - 4x + 7 &= k \\\\\nx^{2} - 4x + 7 - k &= 0 \\\\\nx^{2} - 4x + (7 - k) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe coefficients of our quadratic equation are the following:\n\n$$\na = 1, \\qquad b = -4, \\qquad c = 7-k\n$$\n\nSo, computing the discriminant, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nD &= (-4)^{2} - 4(1)(7 - k) \\\\\n&= 16 - 4(7 - k) \\\\\n&= 16 - 28 + 4k \\\\\n&= 4k - 12\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor no real solutions, we need $\\mathcal{D} < 0{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4k-12 & <0 \\\\ 4k & <12 \\\\ k & <\\frac{12}{4} \\\\ k & <3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $k$ must be an integer, we conclude that the greatest possible value is $2$."] [:db/cas 17592186218248 :tutorial/content "In this lesson, we'll learn how to represent geometric properties of 2D shapes using algebraic functions.\nThis allows us to translate real-world measurements into mathematical (algebraic) models that we can analyze.\n\nWe'll begin by learning to interpret algebraic expressions representing geometric properties.\n\nFor example, suppose we are given that the area $A$, in square centimeters, of a rectangular solar panel can be represented by the expression\n\n$$\nA = w(w + 14)\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is the width, in centimeters, of the panel. What expression represents the length, in centimeters, of the panel in this expression?\n\nThe area of a rectangle is given by\n\n$$\nA = \\text{width} \\cdot \\text{length}\n$$\n\nHere, we have\n\n$$\nA = w \\cdot (w + 14)\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is the width.\n\n$$\nA =\\begin{vmatrix}width & \\cdot\\end{vmatrix}length; ↕\\begin{vmatrix}↕ \\\\ A =\\end{vmatrix}w \\mid \\cdot \\mid (w + 14)\n$$\n\nTherefore, comparing the two expressions, we conclude that the length must be\n\n$$\nw + 14\n$$" "In this lesson, we'll learn how to represent geometric properties of 2D shapes using algebraic functions.\nThis allows us to translate real-world measurements into mathematical (algebraic) models that we can analyze.\n\nWe'll begin by learning to interpret algebraic expressions representing geometric properties.\n\nFor example, suppose we are given that the area $A$, in square centimeters, of a rectangular solar panel can be represented by the expression\n\n$$\nA = w(w + 14)\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is the width, in centimeters, of the panel. What expression represents the length, in centimeters, of the panel in this expression?\n\nThe area of a rectangle is given by\n\n$$\nA = \\text{width} \\cdot \\text{length}\n$$\n\nHere, we have\n\n$$\nA = w \\cdot (w + 14)\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is the width.\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{rccc}A\\hspace{1em}= & {\\color{blue}{\\text{width}}} & \\cdot  & {\\color{red}{\\text{length}}} \\\\  & ↕ &  & ↕ \\\\ A\\hspace{1em}= & {\\color{blue}{w}} & \\cdot  & {\\color{red}{(w+14)}}\\end{array}\n$$\n\nTherefore, comparing the two expressions, we conclude that the length must be\n\n$$\nw + 14\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186218267 :tutorial/content "In previous lessons, we learned how to determine conditions on unknown parameters in quadratic equations to force a particular number of solutions. As we saw, this process often involves solving a quadratic inequality.\n\nIn this lesson, we'll consider cases where the quadratic inequality that arises from these problems must be solved using a graphical method.\n\nFor example, for which values of $k$ does the equation\n\n$$\n3x^2-6kx+k=0\n$$\n\nhave two distinct real solutions?\n\nRecall that a quadratic equation has two distinct real solutions if its discriminant is positive: $\\mathcal{D}>0$.\n\nTo compute the discriminant, we first note the equation's coefficients:\n\n$$\na=3,\\quad b=-6k,\\quad c=k\n$$\n\nSo, we require\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}D & > 0 \\\\ b^{2} - 4ac & > 0 \\\\ (-6k)^{2} - 4(3)(k) & > 0 \\\\ 36k^{2} - 12k & > 0 \\\\ 3k^{2} - k & > 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, let's consider the parabola $y=3k^2-k$ and find its solutions. We obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3k^{2} - k &= 0 \\\\\nk(3k - 1) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the solutions are\n\n$$\nk=0\\quad \\text{and}\\quad k=\\dfrac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nSince the leading coefficient is positive, the parabola opens upward. Therefore, we get the following graph:\n\n![](images/4d/4deda3a2118d71d65e47b9ee7d2419df2f0c258652b650a3e7288879cdedd170.png)\n\nWe want\n\n$$\ny = 3k^2 - k > 0\n$$\n\nmeaning that the graph lies above the $k$-axis. Based on the graph, the corresponding intervals are $(-\\infty,0)$ or\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{1}{3},\\infty\\right)\n$$\n\nTherefore, our equation has two distinct real solutions when\n\n$$\nk \\in (-\\infty,0) \\cup \\left(\\dfrac{1}{3},\\infty\\right)\n$$" "In previous lessons, we learned how to determine conditions on unknown parameters in quadratic equations to force a particular number of solutions. As we saw, this process often involves solving a quadratic inequality.\n\nIn this lesson, we'll consider cases where the quadratic inequality that arises from these problems must be solved using a graphical method.\n\nFor example, for which values of $k$ does the equation\n\n$$\n3x^2-6kx+k=0\n$$\n\nhave two distinct real solutions?\n\nRecall that a quadratic equation has two distinct real solutions if its discriminant is positive: $\\mathcal{D}>0$.\n\nTo compute the discriminant, we first note the equation's coefficients:\n\n$$\na=3,\\quad b=-6k,\\quad c=k\n$$\n\nSo, we require\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathcal{D} & >0 \\\\ {b}^{2}-4ac & >0 \\\\ (-6k)^{2}-4(3)(k) & >0 \\\\ 36{k}^{2}-12k & >0 \\\\ 3{k}^{2}-k & >0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's consider the parabola $y=3k^2-k$ and find its solutions. We obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3k^{2} - k &= 0 \\\\\nk(3k - 1) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the solutions are\n\n$$\nk=0\\quad \\text{and}\\quad k=\\dfrac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nSince the leading coefficient is positive, the parabola opens upward. Therefore, we get the following graph:\n\n![](images/4d/4deda3a2118d71d65e47b9ee7d2419df2f0c258652b650a3e7288879cdedd170.png)\n\nWe want\n\n$$\ny = 3k^2 - k > 0\n$$\n\nmeaning that the graph lies above the $k$-axis. Based on the graph, the corresponding intervals are $(-\\infty,0)$ or\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{1}{3},\\infty\\right)\n$$\n\nTherefore, our equation has two distinct real solutions when\n\n$$\nk \\in (-\\infty,0) \\cup \\left(\\dfrac{1}{3},\\infty\\right)\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186218277 :tutorial/content "Let's now consider cases where we transform higher-order polynomials.\n\nSuppose we have the function\n\n$$\nf(x) = (x+4)(x-6)^2\n$$\n\nand we are told that\n\n$$\nf(12-k) = 0\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is a constant. What are the possible values of $k$?\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is written in factored form, so we can immediately deduce its zeros:\n\n- From the factor $(x+4)$, we get $x=-4$.\n- From the factor $(x-6)^2$, we get $x=6$.\n\nNotice that $x=6$ is a *double root* because the factor $(x-6)$ is squared. So the zeros of $f$ are $x=-4$ and $x=6$.\n\nThe equation\n\n$$\nf(12-k)=0\n$$\n\nmeans that the input $12-k$ must be equal to one of the zeros of $f$.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}12 - k =-4 & ⇒k = 16, \\\\ 12 - k = 6 & ⇒k = 6\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSo the possible values of $k$ are $6$ and $16$." "Let's now consider cases where we transform higher-order polynomials.\n\nSuppose we have the function\n\n$$\nf(x) = (x+4)(x-6)^2\n$$\n\nand we are told that\n\n$$\nf(12-k) = 0\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is a constant. What are the possible values of $k$?\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is written in factored form, so we can immediately deduce its zeros:\n\n- From the factor $(x+4)$, we get $x=-4$.\n- From the factor $(x-6)^2$, we get $x=6$.\n\nNotice that $x=6$ is a *double root* because the factor $(x-6)$ is squared. So the zeros of $f$ are $x=-4$ and $x=6$.\n\nThe equation\n\n$$\nf(12-k)=0\n$$\n\nmeans that the input $12-k$ must be equal to one of the zeros of $f$.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12-k=-4\\hspace{1em} & \\Rightarrow \\hspace{1em}k=16, \\\\ 12-k=6\\hspace{1em} & \\Rightarrow \\hspace{1em}k=6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the possible values of $k$ are $6$ and $16$."] [:db/cas 17592186220990 :question/worked-solution "To find the fewest number of classes that makes the unlimited pass worthwhile, we assume each class costs the highest price of $\\$9.50$.\n\nLet $x$ represent the number of classes attended.\n\nWithout the pass, the total cost is $\\$9.50$ per class:\n$9.50x$\n\nWith the pass, the cost is a flat $\\$135$.\n\nWe want the pass to cost less than paying individually. This gives the inequality:\n\n$$\n135 < 9.50x\n$$\n\nNow, solve the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}135 & < 9.50x \\\\ \\frac{135}{9.50} & < x \\\\ 14.210… & < x\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSince $x$ must be a whole number, the smallest value that satisfies the inequality is $15$." "To find the fewest number of classes that makes the unlimited pass worthwhile, we assume each class costs the highest price of $\\$9.50$.\n\nLet $x$ represent the number of classes attended.\n\nWithout the pass, the total cost is $\\$9.50$ per class:\n$9.50x$\n\nWith the pass, the cost is a flat $\\$135$.\n\nWe want the pass to cost less than paying individually. This gives the inequality:\n\n$$\n135 < 9.50x\n$$\n\nNow, solve the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}135 & <9.50x \\\\ \\frac{135}{9.50} & <x \\\\ 14.210… & <x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $x$ must be a whole number, the smallest value that satisfies the inequality is $15$."] [:db/cas 17592186221036 :question/worked-solution "To find the slope of the line $4x - y = 1$, we rewrite the equation in slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n-y = -4x + 1 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad y = 4x - 1\n$$\n\nSo, the slope is $4$.\n\nNow, a line is perpendicular to another line when its slope is the negative reciprocal of that other line's slope. That means the second line must have slope\n\n$$\n-\\dfrac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nFrom the line equation\n\n$$\nmx + ny = 1\n$$\n\nit follows that the slope of the second line is\n\n$$\n-\\dfrac{m}{n}\n$$\n\nSo we set\n\n$$\n-\\dfrac{m}{n} = -\\dfrac{1}{4} \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad \\dfrac{m}{n} = \\dfrac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nNow, let's utilise this information to check which pair of lines is perpendicular:\n\n- First, we consider the following lines:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}4x + y = 1 & ⇒y =-4x + 1 \\\\ mx + 2ny = 1 & ⇒y =-\\frac{m}{2n}x + \\frac{1}{2n}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nThe slope of the first line is $-4$.\nThe slope of the second line is\n$$\n\\displaystyle -\\frac{m}{2n}|=-\\frac{1}{2} \\cdot \\frac{m}{n}; =-\\frac{1}{2} \\cdot \\frac{1}{4}; =-\\frac{1}{8}.\n$$\nSince the product of slopes does not equal $-1$, this pair of lines is NOT perpendicular.\n- Next, we consider the following lines:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}4x + y = 1 & ⇒y =-4x + 1 \\\\ mx - ny = 1 & ⇒y = \\frac{m}{n}x - \\frac{1}{n}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nThe slope of the first line is $-4$.\nThe slope of the second line is\n$\\dfrac m n = \\dfrac 1 4$.\nSince the product of the slopes equals $-1$, this pair of lines is perpendicular.\n- Finally, we consider the following lines:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}8x - 2y = 1 & ⇒y = 4x - \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 2mx + ny = 1 & ⇒y =-\\frac{2m}{n}x + \\frac{1}{n}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nThe slope of the first line is $4$.\nThe slope of the second line is\n$$\n\\displaystyle -\\frac{2m}{n}|=-2 \\cdot \\frac{m}{n}; =-2 \\cdot \\frac{1}{4}; =-\\frac{1}{2}\n$$\nSince the product of slopes does not equal $-1$, this pair of lines is NOT perpendicular.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" "To find the slope of the line $4x - y = 1$, we rewrite the equation in slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n-y = -4x + 1 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad y = 4x - 1\n$$\n\nSo, the slope is $4$.\n\nNow, a line is perpendicular to another line when its slope is the negative reciprocal of that other line's slope. That means the second line must have slope\n\n$$\n-\\dfrac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nFrom the line equation\n\n$$\nmx + ny = 1\n$$\n\nit follows that the slope of the second line is\n\n$$\n-\\dfrac{m}{n}\n$$\n\nSo we set\n\n$$\n-\\dfrac{m}{n} = -\\dfrac{1}{4} \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad \\dfrac{m}{n} = \\dfrac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nNow, let's utilise this information to check which pair of lines is perpendicular:\n\n- First, we consider the following lines:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x+y=1\\hspace{2em} & \\Rightarrow \\hspace{2em}y=-4x+1 \\\\ mx+2ny=1\\hspace{2em} & \\Rightarrow \\hspace{2em}y=-\\frac{m}{2n}x+\\frac{1}{2n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\nThe slope of the first line is $-4$.\nThe slope of the second line is\n$$\n\\displaystyle -\\frac{m}{2n}|=-\\frac{1}{2} \\cdot \\frac{m}{n}; =-\\frac{1}{2} \\cdot \\frac{1}{4}; =-\\frac{1}{8}.\n$$\nSince the product of slopes does not equal $-1$, this pair of lines is NOT perpendicular.\n- Next, we consider the following lines:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x+y=1\\hspace{2em} & \\Rightarrow \\hspace{2em}y=-4x+1 \\\\ mx-ny=1\\hspace{2em} & \\Rightarrow \\hspace{2em}y=\\frac{m}{n}x-\\frac{1}{n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\nThe slope of the first line is $-4$.\nThe slope of the second line is\n$\\dfrac m n = \\dfrac 1 4$.\nSince the product of the slopes equals $-1$, this pair of lines is perpendicular.\n- Finally, we consider the following lines:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8x-2y=1\\hspace{2em} & \\Rightarrow \\hspace{2em}y=4x-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 2mx+ny=1\\hspace{2em} & \\Rightarrow \\hspace{2em}y=-\\frac{2m}{n}x+\\frac{1}{n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\nThe slope of the first line is $4$.\nThe slope of the second line is\n$$\n\\displaystyle -\\frac{2m}{n}|=-2 \\cdot \\frac{m}{n}; =-2 \\cdot \\frac{1}{4}; =-\\frac{1}{2}\n$$\nSince the product of slopes does not equal $-1$, this pair of lines is NOT perpendicular.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""] [:db/cas 17592186221072 :question/worked-solution "Since lines $p$ and $q$ are parallel, and the line $t$ acts as a transversal, the angle labeled $a^\\circ$ and the angle adjacent to $b^\\circ$ outside the parallel lines are corresponding angles. Hence, they are congruent.\n\n![](images/ed/edbb26b99c0bafe9a3281749e2d70045c46c7e916bfba5066b9f5f57eaaadbfb.png)\n\nThe angle labeled $a^\\circ$ and the adjacent angle form a linear pair, creating a straight angle at the point of intersection of $t$ and $q$. The measure of a straight angle is $180^\\circ$, so we can write\n\n$$\nb + a = 180\n$$\n\nSo, statement II is false.\n\nNext, we are told that $a > 105$. Substituting this into the equation, we get\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}a & > 105 \\\\ b + a & > b + 105 \\\\ 180 & > b + 105 \\\\ 75 & > b \\\\ b & < 75\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, statement I is true while statement III is false.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"." "Since lines $p$ and $q$ are parallel, and the line $t$ acts as a transversal, the angle labeled $a^\\circ$ and the angle adjacent to $b^\\circ$ outside the parallel lines are corresponding angles. Hence, they are congruent.\n\n![](images/ed/edbb26b99c0bafe9a3281749e2d70045c46c7e916bfba5066b9f5f57eaaadbfb.png)\n\nThe angle labeled $a^\\circ$ and the adjacent angle form a linear pair, creating a straight angle at the point of intersection of $t$ and $q$. The measure of a straight angle is $180^\\circ$, so we can write\n\n$$\nb + a = 180\n$$\n\nSo, statement II is false.\n\nNext, we are told that $a > 105$. Substituting this into the equation, we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & >105 \\\\ b+a & >b+105 \\\\ 180 & >b+105 \\\\ 75 & >b \\\\ b & <75.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, statement I is true while statement III is false.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"."] [:db/cas 17592186221081 :question/worked-solution "We simplify the inequality by dividing *all terms* by the greatest common factor of $5{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{25p + 10q}{5} & > \\frac{15}{5} \\\\ 5p + 2q & > 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSo, the inequality $25p + 10q > 15$ is equivalent to\n$5p + 2q > 3$." "We simplify the inequality by dividing *all terms* by the greatest common factor of $5{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{25p+10q}{5} & >\\frac{15}{5} \\\\ 5p+2q & >3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the inequality $25p + 10q > 15$ is equivalent to\n$5p + 2q > 3$."] [:db/cas 17592186221087 :question/worked-solution "Let $s$ represent the number of $25$-book boxes and $t$ represent the number of $60$-book boxes.\n\nWe are given two constraints:\n\n- The total number of boxes must be at most $20{:}$\n$s + t \\leq 20$\n- The total number of books must be at least $500{:}$\n$25s + 60t \\geq 500$\n\nWe want to *maximize* the number of $25$-book boxes, $s$, subject to these constraints.\n\nNow, we assume $t = 0$ to check the highest possible $s$ that satisfies both conditions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}25s + 60t & \\ge 500 \\\\ 25s + 60(0) & \\ge 500 \\\\ 25s & \\ge 500 \\\\ s & \\ge \\frac{500}{25} = 20\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSo, the largest possible value is $s = 20$.\n\nWe check if this value satisfies the total number of books condition:\n\n$$\ns + t=20 + 0=20\\leq 20 \\quad{\\color{green}\\checkmark}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum number of $25$-book boxes is $20$." "Let $s$ represent the number of $25$-book boxes and $t$ represent the number of $60$-book boxes.\n\nWe are given two constraints:\n\n- The total number of boxes must be at most $20{:}$\n$s + t \\leq 20$\n- The total number of books must be at least $500{:}$\n$25s + 60t \\geq 500$\n\nWe want to *maximize* the number of $25$-book boxes, $s$, subject to these constraints.\n\nNow, we assume $t = 0$ to check the highest possible $s$ that satisfies both conditions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}25s+60t & \\ge 500 \\\\ 25s+60(0) & \\ge 500 \\\\ 25s & \\ge 500 \\\\ s & \\ge \\frac{500}{25}=20\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the largest possible value is $s = 20$.\n\nWe check if this value satisfies the total number of books condition:\n\n$$\ns + t=20 + 0=20\\leq 20 \\quad{\\color{green}\\checkmark}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum number of $25$-book boxes is $20$."] [:db/cas 17592186221943 :question/problem "Given that\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}L{\\sin ωt}|= \\frac{ω}{s^{2} + ω^{2}}, & s > 0, \\\\ L{\\cos ωt}|= \\frac{s}{s^{2} + ω^{2}}, & s > 0\\end{b