 of the second kind because the function under the integral is unbounded as we approach the *upper* integration limit from any point that lies in the interval $x\\in (0,4):$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{1}{16-{x}^{2}}\\to \\infty \\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to {4}^{-}\n$$\n\nWe can attempt to evaluate this integral by integrating $f(x)$ over the interval $x\\in [0,b]$ and taking the limit as $b\\to {4}^{-}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{4}\\frac{1}{16-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{b\\to {4}^{-}}({\\int }_{0}^{b}\\frac{1}{16-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x)\\end{aligned}\n$$" "This is an improper integral of the second kind because the function under the integral is unbounded as we approach the *upper* integration limit from any point that lies in the interval $x\\in (0,4):$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{1}{16-{x}^{2}}\\to \\infty \\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to {4}^{-}\n$$\n\nWe can attempt to evaluate this integral by integrating $f(x)$ over the interval $x\\in [0,b]$ and taking the limit as $b\\to {4}^{-}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{4}\\frac{1}{16-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\lim_{b\\to {4}^{-}}({\\int }_{0}^{b}\\frac{1}{16-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x)\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186272217 :question/worked-solution "This is an improper integral of the second kind because the function under the integral is unbounded as we approach the *lower* integration limit from any point that lies in the interval $x\\in (0,2):$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{1}{x(x+1)}\\to \\infty \\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to {0}^{+}\n$$\n\nWe can attempt to evaluate this integral by integrating $f(x)$ over the interval $x\\in [a,2]$ and taking the limit as $a\\to {0}^{+}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{x(x+1)}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to {0}^{+}}({\\int }_{a}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{x(x+1)}\\,)\\end{aligned}\n$$" "This is an improper integral of the second kind because the function under the integral is unbounded as we approach the *lower* integration limit from any point that lies in the interval $x\\in (0,2):$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{1}{x(x+1)}\\to \\infty \\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to {0}^{+}\n$$\n\nWe can attempt to evaluate this integral by integrating $f(x)$ over the interval $x\\in [a,2]$ and taking the limit as $a\\to {0}^{+}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{x(x+1)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\lim_{a\\to {0}^{+}}({\\int }_{a}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{x(x+1)}\\,)\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186272236 :question/worked-solution "This is an improper integral of the second kind because the function under the integral is unbounded as we approach the *upper* integration limit from any point that lies in the interval $x\\in (-1,2):$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{1}{4-{x}^{2}}\\to \\infty \\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to {2}^{-}\n$$\n\nWe can attempt to evaluate this integral by integrating $f(x)$ over the interval $x\\in [-1,b]$ and taking the limit as $b\\to {2}^{-}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{4-{x}^{2}} & ={lim}_{b\\to {2}^{-}}({\\int }_{-1}^{b}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{4-{x}^{2}})\\end{aligned}\n$$" "This is an improper integral of the second kind because the function under the integral is unbounded as we approach the *upper* integration limit from any point that lies in the interval $x\\in (-1,2):$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{1}{4-{x}^{2}}\\to \\infty \\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to {2}^{-}\n$$\n\nWe can attempt to evaluate this integral by integrating $f(x)$ over the interval $x\\in [-1,b]$ and taking the limit as $b\\to {2}^{-}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{4-{x}^{2}} & =\\lim_{b\\to {2}^{-}}({\\int }_{-1}^{b}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{4-{x}^{2}})\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186272278 :question/worked-solution "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial:\n\n- The constant term is $-16,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1,\\,\\pm 2,\\,\\pm 4,\\,\\pm 8$ and $\\pm 16.$\n\n- The leading coefficient is $4,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1,\\,\\pm 2$ and $\\pm 4.$\n\nFirst, we list out all the possibilities for the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm \\frac{1}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{4}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{2}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{2}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{2}{4}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{4}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{4}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{4}{4}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{8}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{8}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{8}{4}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{16}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{16}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{16}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we reduce the fractions to lowest terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm 1, & \\pm \\frac{1}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{4}, \\\\ \\pm 2, & \\pm 1, & \\pm \\frac{1}{2}, \\\\ \\pm 4, & \\pm 2, & \\pm 1, \\\\ \\pm 8, & \\pm 4, & \\pm 2, \\\\ \\pm 16, & \\pm 8, & \\pm 4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that some potential roots are duplicates (shown above in red). Removing these duplicates, and listing those remaining in order from least to greatest in absolute value, we have\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{1}{4},\\,\\pm \\frac{1}{2}\\,\\pm 1,\\,\\pm 2,\\,\\pm 4,\\,\\pm 8,\\,\\pm 16.\n$$\n\nThis makes a total of $14$ potential roots. (Remember that each $\\pm $ sign accounts for $2$ roots, and $2\\cdot 7=14.$)" "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial:\n\n- The constant term is $-16,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1,\\,\\pm 2,\\,\\pm 4,\\,\\pm 8$ and $\\pm 16.$\n\n- The leading coefficient is $4,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1,\\,\\pm 2$ and $\\pm 4.$\n\nFirst, we list out all the possibilities for the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{ccc}\\pm \\frac{1}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{4}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{2}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{2}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{2}{4}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{4}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{4}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{4}{4}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{8}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{8}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{8}{4}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{16}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{16}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{16}{4}.\\end{array}\n$$\n\nNext, we reduce the fractions to lowest terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{ccc}\\pm 1, & \\pm \\frac{1}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{4}, \\\\ \\pm 2, & \\pm 1, & \\pm \\frac{1}{2}, \\\\ \\pm 4, & \\pm 2, & \\pm 1, \\\\ \\pm 8, & \\pm 4, & \\pm 2, \\\\ \\pm 16, & \\pm 8, & \\pm 4.\\end{array}\n$$\n\nNotice that some potential roots are duplicates (shown above in red). Removing these duplicates, and listing those remaining in order from least to greatest in absolute value, we have\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{1}{4},\\,\\pm \\frac{1}{2}\\,\\pm 1,\\,\\pm 2,\\,\\pm 4,\\,\\pm 8,\\,\\pm 16.\n$$\n\nThis makes a total of $14$ potential roots. (Remember that each $\\pm $ sign accounts for $2$ roots, and $2\\cdot 7=14.$)"] [:db/cas 17592186272279 :question/worked-solution "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial:\n\n- The constant term is $5,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1$ and $\\pm 5.$\n\n- The leading coefficient is $6,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1,\\,\\pm 2,\\,\\pm 3$ and $\\pm 6.$\n\nFirst, we list out all the possibilities for the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm \\frac{1}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{3}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{6}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{5}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{5}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{5}{3}, & \\pm \\frac{5}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we reduce the fractions to lowest terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm 1, & \\pm \\frac{1}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{3}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{6}, \\\\ \\pm 5, & \\pm \\frac{5}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{5}{3}, & \\pm \\frac{5}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that none of these potential roots are duplicates. Listing those potential roots in order from least to greatest in absolute value, we have\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{1}{6},\\,\\pm \\frac{1}{3},\\,\\pm \\frac{1}{2},\\,\\pm \\frac{5}{6},\\,\\pm 1,\\,\\pm \\frac{5}{3},\\,\\pm \\frac{5}{2},\\,\\pm 5.\n$$\n\nThis makes a total of $16$ potential roots. (Remember that each $\\pm $ sign accounts for $2$ roots, and $2\\cdot 8=16.$)" "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial:\n\n- The constant term is $5,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1$ and $\\pm 5.$\n\n- The leading coefficient is $6,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1,\\,\\pm 2,\\,\\pm 3$ and $\\pm 6.$\n\nFirst, we list out all the possibilities for the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{cccc}\\pm \\frac{1}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{3}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{6}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{5}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{5}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{5}{3}, & \\pm \\frac{5}{6}.\\end{array}\n$$\n\nNext, we reduce the fractions to lowest terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{cccc}\\pm 1, & \\pm \\frac{1}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{3}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{6}, \\\\ \\pm 5, & \\pm \\frac{5}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{5}{3}, & \\pm \\frac{5}{6}.\\end{array}\n$$\n\nNotice that none of these potential roots are duplicates. Listing those potential roots in order from least to greatest in absolute value, we have\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{1}{6},\\,\\pm \\frac{1}{3},\\,\\pm \\frac{1}{2},\\,\\pm \\frac{5}{6},\\,\\pm 1,\\,\\pm \\frac{5}{3},\\,\\pm \\frac{5}{2},\\,\\pm 5.\n$$\n\nThis makes a total of $16$ potential roots. (Remember that each $\\pm $ sign accounts for $2$ roots, and $2\\cdot 8=16.$)"] [:db/cas 17592186272341 :question/worked-solution "This is an improper integral of the second kind because the function under the integral is unbounded as we approach the *lower* integration limit from any point that lies in the interval $x\\in (1,4):$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}\\to \\infty \\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to {1}^{+}\n$$\n\nWe can attempt to evaluate this integral by integrating $f(x)$ over the interval $x\\in [a,4]$ and taking the limit as $a\\to {1}^{+}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to {1}^{+}}({\\int }_{a}^{4}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x)\\end{aligned}\n$$" "This is an improper integral of the second kind because the function under the integral is unbounded as we approach the *lower* integration limit from any point that lies in the interval $x\\in (1,4):$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}\\to \\infty \\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to {1}^{+}\n$$\n\nWe can attempt to evaluate this integral by integrating $f(x)$ over the interval $x\\in [a,4]$ and taking the limit as $a\\to {1}^{+}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\lim_{a\\to {1}^{+}}({\\int }_{a}^{4}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x)\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186272342 :question/worked-solution "This is an improper integral of the second kind because the function under the integral is unbounded as we approach the *upper* integration limit from any point that lies in the interval $x\\in (-1,5):$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{25-{x}^{2}}}\\to \\infty \\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to {5}^{-}\n$$\n\nWe can attempt to evaluate this integral by integrating $f(x)$ over the interval $x\\in [-1,b]$ and taking the limit as $b\\to {5}^{-}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{5}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{25-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{b\\to {5}^{-}}({\\int }_{-1}^{b}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{25-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x)\\end{aligned}\n$$" "This is an improper integral of the second kind because the function under the integral is unbounded as we approach the *upper* integration limit from any point that lies in the interval $x\\in (-1,5):$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{25-{x}^{2}}}\\to \\infty \\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to {5}^{-}\n$$\n\nWe can attempt to evaluate this integral by integrating $f(x)$ over the interval $x\\in [-1,b]$ and taking the limit as $b\\to {5}^{-}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{5}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{25-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\lim_{b\\to {5}^{-}}({\\int }_{-1}^{b}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{25-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x)\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186275245 :question/worked-solution "We're required to determine the left and right continuity properties of $f(x)$ at $x=4.$\n\nLet's start by computing $f(4):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(4) & =4-2 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement “$f(x)$ is left-continuous at $x=4$” is true. The function is left-continuous at $x=4,$ since the left-sided limit is equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {4}^{-}}f(x) & =4-2 \\\\  & =2 \\\\  & =f(4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “$f(x)$ is right-continuous at $x=4$” is true. The function is right-continuous at $x=4,$ since the right-sided limit is equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {4}^{+}}f(x) & =\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =2 \\\\  & =f(4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “$f(x)$ is continuous at $x=4$” is true. The function is both left and right-continuous at $x=4,$ which implies that the function is continuous at $x=4.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$f(x)$ is left-continuous at $x=4$”; “$f(x)$ is right-continuous at $x=4$”; “$f(x)$ is continuous at $x=4$”." "We're required to determine the left and right continuity properties of $f(x)$ at $x=4.$\n\nLet's start by computing $f(4):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(4) & =4-2 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement “$f(x)$ is left-continuous at $x=4$” is true. The function is left-continuous at $x=4,$ since the left-sided limit is equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {4}^{-}}f(x) & =4-2 \\\\  & =2 \\\\  & =f(4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “$f(x)$ is right-continuous at $x=4$” is true. The function is right-continuous at $x=4,$ since the right-sided limit is equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {4}^{+}}f(x) & =\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =2 \\\\  & =f(4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “$f(x)$ is continuous at $x=4$” is true. The function is both left and right-continuous at $x=4,$ which implies that the function is continuous at $x=4.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$f(x)$ is left-continuous at $x=4$”; “$f(x)$ is right-continuous at $x=4$”; “$f(x)$ is continuous at $x=4$”."] [:db/cas 17592186275246 :question/worked-solution "We're required to determine the left and right continuity properties of $h(x)$ at $x=-1.$\n\nLet's start by computing $h(-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(-1) & =-\\sqrt{-(-1)} \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{1} \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement “$h(x)$ is left-continuous at $x=-1$” is true. The function is left-continuous at $x=-1$ since the left-sided limit is equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}h(x) & =-\\sqrt{-(-1)} \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{1} \\\\  & =-1 \\\\  & =h(-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “$h(x)$ is right-continuous at $x=-1$” is false. The function is *not* right-continuous at $x=-1$ since the right-sided limit is *not* equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}h(x) & =1-2(-1) \\\\  & =1+2 \\\\  & =3 \\\\  & \\ne h(-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “$h(x)$ is continuous at $x=-1$” is false. For the function to be continuous at $x=-1,$ it must be both left-continuous and right-continuous. However, we have already determined that the function is not right-continuous at $x=-1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$h(x)$ is left-continuous at $x=-1$” only." "We're required to determine the left and right continuity properties of $h(x)$ at $x=-1.$\n\nLet's start by computing $h(-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(-1) & =-\\sqrt{-(-1)} \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{1} \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement “$h(x)$ is left-continuous at $x=-1$” is true. The function is left-continuous at $x=-1$ since the left-sided limit is equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}h(x) & =-\\sqrt{-(-1)} \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{1} \\\\  & =-1 \\\\  & =h(-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “$h(x)$ is right-continuous at $x=-1$” is false. The function is *not* right-continuous at $x=-1$ since the right-sided limit is *not* equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}h(x) & =1-2(-1) \\\\  & =1+2 \\\\  & =3 \\\\  & \\ne h(-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “$h(x)$ is continuous at $x=-1$” is false. For the function to be continuous at $x=-1,$ it must be both left-continuous and right-continuous. However, we have already determined that the function is not right-continuous at $x=-1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$h(x)$ is left-continuous at $x=-1$” only."] [:db/cas 17592186275265 :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u=2x+4.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}u=\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty $ when $x\\to \\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=2x+4$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $4$ | $\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{(2x+4)^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}}(\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}u\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{(2x+4)^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{3}{2}{\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }{u}^{-3/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{4}^{a}{u}^{-3/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-2{u}^{-1/2}]}_{4}^{a} \\\\  & =-3\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{u}}]}_{4}^{a} \\\\  & =-3\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{a}}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{4}}] \\\\  & =-3\\cdot (0-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we substitute $u=2x+4.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}u=\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty $ when $x\\to \\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=2x+4$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $4$ | $\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{(2x+4)^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}}(\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}u\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{(2x+4)^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{3}{2}{\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }{u}^{-3/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{4}^{a}{u}^{-3/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{[-2{u}^{-1/2}]}_{4}^{a} \\\\  & =-3\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{u}}]}_{4}^{a} \\\\  & =-3\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{a}}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{4}}] \\\\  & =-3\\cdot (0-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186275266 :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u={x}^{3}+1.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=3{x}^{2}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u={x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty $ when $x\\to \\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u={x}^{3}+1$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{x}^{2}}{\\sqrt{({x}^{3}+1)^{5}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{u}^{5}}}\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{3}+1}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{u}^{5}}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{u}^{-5/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}{u}^{-5/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{2}{3}{u}^{-3/2}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{9}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{9}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{a}^{3}}}-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{9}(0-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we substitute $u={x}^{3}+1.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=3{x}^{2}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u={x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty $ when $x\\to \\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u={x}^{3}+1$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{x}^{2}}{\\sqrt{({x}^{3}+1)^{5}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{u}^{5}}}\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{3}+1}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{u}^{5}}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{u}^{-5/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}{u}^{-5/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{2}{3}{u}^{-3/2}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{9}\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{9}\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{a}^{3}}}-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{9}(0-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186275267 :question/worked-solution "We proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-2}\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =4{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{-2}{x}^{-2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[-{x}^{-1}]}_{a}^{-2} \\\\  & =-4\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{1}{x}]}_{a}^{-2} \\\\  & =-4\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\frac{1}{(-2)}-\\frac{1}{a}] \\\\  & =-4\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2}-0) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-2}\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =4\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{-2}{x}^{-2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{[-{x}^{-1}]}_{a}^{-2} \\\\  & =-4\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{1}{x}]}_{a}^{-2} \\\\  & =-4\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\frac{1}{(-2)}-\\frac{1}{a}] \\\\  & =-4\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2}-0) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186275270 :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)+C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{10}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =10\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =10\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)]_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =10\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(a)-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1)] \\\\  & =10\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(a)]-\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x,$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x]=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x]=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n![](images/27/27ec65761e5119be666e69b67b2fa2fcb7b4f3665ddc41faddd6c136d5c955b1.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{10}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =10\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(a)]-\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =10\\cdot (\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)+C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{10}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =10\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =10\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)]_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =10\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(a)-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1)] \\\\  & =10\\cdot (\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(a)]-\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x,$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x]=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x]=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n![](images/27/27ec65761e5119be666e69b67b2fa2fcb7b4f3665ddc41faddd6c136d5c955b1.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{10}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =10\\cdot (\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(a)]-\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =10\\cdot (\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186275271 :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1+{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(z)+C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\sqrt{3}}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{1+{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z & =4\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{-\\sqrt{3}}^{a}\\frac{1}{1+{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =4\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(z)]_{-\\sqrt{3}}^{a} \\\\  & =4\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(a)-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-\\sqrt{3})] \\\\  & =4\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(a)-\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)] \\\\  & =4\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(a)]+\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x,$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x]=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x]=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n![](images/27/27ec65761e5119be666e69b67b2fa2fcb7b4f3665ddc41faddd6c136d5c955b1.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\sqrt{3}}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{1+{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z & =4\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(a)]+\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =4\\cdot (\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{10\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1+{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(z)+C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\sqrt{3}}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{1+{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z & =4\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{-\\sqrt{3}}^{a}\\frac{1}{1+{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =4\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(z)]_{-\\sqrt{3}}^{a} \\\\  & =4\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(a)-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-\\sqrt{3})] \\\\  & =4\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(a)-\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)] \\\\  & =4\\cdot (\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(a)]+\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x,$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x]=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x]=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n![](images/27/27ec65761e5119be666e69b67b2fa2fcb7b4f3665ddc41faddd6c136d5c955b1.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\sqrt{3}}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{1+{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z & =4\\cdot (\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(a)]+\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =4\\cdot (\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{10\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186275272 :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1+{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(z)+C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{8}{1+{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z & =8\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{-1}\\frac{1}{1+{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =8\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(z)]_{a}^{-1} \\\\  & =8\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-1)-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(a)] \\\\  & =8\\cdot (-\\frac{\\pi }{4}-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(a)])\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x,$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x]=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x]=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n![](images/27/27ec65761e5119be666e69b67b2fa2fcb7b4f3665ddc41faddd6c136d5c955b1.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{8}{1+{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z & =8\\cdot (-\\frac{\\pi }{4}-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(a)]) \\\\  & =8\\cdot (-\\frac{\\pi }{4}-\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)) \\\\  & =8\\cdot (\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1+{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(z)+C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{8}{1+{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z & =8\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{-1}\\frac{1}{1+{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =8\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(z)]_{a}^{-1} \\\\  & =8\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-1)-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(a)] \\\\  & =8\\cdot (-\\frac{\\pi }{4}-\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(a)])\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x,$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x]=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x]=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n![](images/27/27ec65761e5119be666e69b67b2fa2fcb7b4f3665ddc41faddd6c136d5c955b1.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{8}{1+{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z & =8\\cdot (-\\frac{\\pi }{4}-\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(a)]) \\\\  & =8\\cdot (-\\frac{\\pi }{4}-\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)) \\\\  & =8\\cdot (\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186275273 :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1+(kx)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{k}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(kx)+C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1/(2\\sqrt{3})}\\frac{6}{1+36{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =6\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{1/(2\\sqrt{3})}\\frac{1}{1+36{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =6\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{1/(2\\sqrt{3})}\\frac{1}{1+(6x)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =6\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(6x)]}_{a}^{1/(2\\sqrt{3})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{3})-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(6a)] \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(6a)].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(6x),$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(6x)]=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(6x)]=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n![](images/fa/faa31c8261818c5c978d984a9b90a13e58794b02ff6f47e868ad6d5a06b7f71a.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1/(2\\sqrt{3})}\\frac{6}{1+36{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(6a)] \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}-\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1+(kx)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{k}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(kx)+C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1/(2\\sqrt{3})}\\frac{6}{1+36{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =6\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{1/(2\\sqrt{3})}\\frac{1}{1+36{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =6\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{1/(2\\sqrt{3})}\\frac{1}{1+(6x)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =6\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(6x)]}_{a}^{1/(2\\sqrt{3})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{3})-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(6a)] \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}-\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(6a)].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(6x),$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(6x)]=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(6x)]=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n![](images/fa/faa31c8261818c5c978d984a9b90a13e58794b02ff6f47e868ad6d5a06b7f71a.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1/(2\\sqrt{3})}\\frac{6}{1+36{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}-\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(6a)] \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}-\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186275274 :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1+(kx)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{k}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(kx)+C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-\\sqrt{3}/2}\\frac{6}{1+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =6\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{-\\sqrt{3}/2}\\frac{1}{1+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =6\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{-\\sqrt{3}/2}\\frac{1}{1+(2x)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =6\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2x)]}_{a}^{-\\sqrt{3}/2} \\\\  & =3\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-\\sqrt{3})-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2a)] \\\\  & =3\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(-\\frac{\\pi }{3}-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2a)) \\\\  & =-\\pi -3\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2a)].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2x),$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2x)]=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2x)]=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n![](images/38/382ca84f05af04af9c9b2c837d549a09b695574781f12bc67b8438b95b5f5d75.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-\\sqrt{3}/2}\\frac{1}{1+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\pi -3\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2a)] \\\\  & =-\\pi -3\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\pi +\\frac{3\\pi }{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1+(kx)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{k}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(kx)+C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-\\sqrt{3}/2}\\frac{6}{1+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =6\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{-\\sqrt{3}/2}\\frac{1}{1+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =6\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{-\\sqrt{3}/2}\\frac{1}{1+(2x)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =6\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2x)]}_{a}^{-\\sqrt{3}/2} \\\\  & =3\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-\\sqrt{3})-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2a)] \\\\  & =3\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(-\\frac{\\pi }{3}-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2a)) \\\\  & =-\\pi -3\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2a)].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2x),$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2x)]=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2x)]=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n![](images/38/382ca84f05af04af9c9b2c837d549a09b695574781f12bc67b8438b95b5f5d75.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-\\sqrt{3}/2}\\frac{1}{1+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\pi -3\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2a)] \\\\  & =-\\pi -3\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\pi +\\frac{3\\pi }{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186275275 :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{{k}^{2}+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{k}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{k}\\right)+C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}x & =2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{2}^{a}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{2}^{a}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{2}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)]}_{2}^{a} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{2}\\right)-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡1) \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{2}\\right)]-\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right),$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)]=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\n$$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)]=\\frac{\\pi }{2}\n$$\n\n![](images/04/042ad2450306552d9d5a7e4219d15547ad7104601b7ce6350a64193981de0de7.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{2}\\right)]-\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{{k}^{2}+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{k}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{k}\\right)+C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}x & =2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{2}^{a}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{2}^{a}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{2}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)]}_{2}^{a} \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{2}\\right)-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡1) \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{2}\\right)]-\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right),$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)]=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\n$$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)]=\\frac{\\pi }{2}\n$$\n\n![](images/04/042ad2450306552d9d5a7e4219d15547ad7104601b7ce6350a64193981de0de7.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{2}\\right)]-\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186275276 :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{{k}^{2}+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{k}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{k}\\right)+C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{5}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{z}^{2}+25}\\,\\text{d}z & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{5}^{a}\\frac{1}{{z}^{2}+25}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{5}^{a}\\frac{1}{{z}^{2}+{5}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{z}{5}\\right)]}_{5}^{a} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{5}\\right)-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{5}\\right)-\\frac{\\pi }{4}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{5}\\right),$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{5}\\right)]=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\n$$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{5}\\right)]=\\frac{\\pi }{2}\n$$\n\n![](images/f8/f8325b1ea152bd40aaa909202bfce59e621952486041c657c0e27b9192ecec03.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{5}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{z}^{2}+25}\\,\\text{d}z & =\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{5}\\right)-\\frac{\\pi }{4}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot (\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{20}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{{k}^{2}+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{k}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{k}\\right)+C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{5}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{z}^{2}+25}\\,\\text{d}z & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{5}^{a}\\frac{1}{{z}^{2}+25}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{5}^{a}\\frac{1}{{z}^{2}+{5}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{z}{5}\\right)]}_{5}^{a} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{5}\\right)-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{5}\\right)-\\frac{\\pi }{4}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{5}\\right),$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{5}\\right)]=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\n$$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{5}\\right)]=\\frac{\\pi }{2}\n$$\n\n![](images/f8/f8325b1ea152bd40aaa909202bfce59e621952486041c657c0e27b9192ecec03.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{5}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{z}^{2}+25}\\,\\text{d}z & =\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{5}\\right)-\\frac{\\pi }{4}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot (\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{20}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186275278 :question/worked-solution "We proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}{2}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{1}{2}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{{2}^{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}]}_{a}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[{2}^{x}]}_{a}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }({2}^{1}-{2}^{a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot (2-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{2}^{a})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y={2}^{x}$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{2}^{x}=0$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{2}^{x}=\\infty $\n\n![](images/b9/b9a29cbd8a029e0f26e3da6a042ada0f8a809053233979bb4ff73db3dcf0f10d.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}{2}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot (2-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{2}^{a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot (2-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}{2}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{1}{2}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{{2}^{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}]}_{a}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[{2}^{x}]}_{a}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }({2}^{1}-{2}^{a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot (2-\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{2}^{a})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y={2}^{x}$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }{2}^{x}=0$\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }{2}^{x}=\\infty $\n\n![](images/b9/b9a29cbd8a029e0f26e3da6a042ada0f8a809053233979bb4ff73db3dcf0f10d.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}{2}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot (2-\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{2}^{a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot (2-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186275279 :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int {b}^{\\,px+q}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{p\\operatorname{ln}⁡(b)}{b}^{\\,px+q}+C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}{2}^{3x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}{2}^{3x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{{2}^{3x}}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}]}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[{2}^{3x}]}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot (1-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{2}^{3a})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y={2}^{3x}$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{2}^{3x}=0$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{2}^{3x}=\\infty $\n\n![](images/bd/bdf8e7dad9550977ad6bd207b43cd75e91226ed84c2807dac3d023470d9e02ce.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}{2}^{3x} & =\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot (1-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{2}^{3a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot (1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int {b}^{\\,px+q}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{p\\operatorname{ln}⁡(b)}{b}^{\\,px+q}+C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}{2}^{3x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}{2}^{3x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{{2}^{3x}}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}]}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[{2}^{3x}]}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot (1-\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{2}^{3a})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y={2}^{3x}$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }{2}^{3x}=0$\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }{2}^{3x}=\\infty $\n\n![](images/bd/bdf8e7dad9550977ad6bd207b43cd75e91226ed84c2807dac3d023470d9e02ce.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}{2}^{3x} & =\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot (1-\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{2}^{3a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot (1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186275281 :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u=\\sqrt{x}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}}\\,⟹\\,2\\text{d}u=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty $ when $x\\to \\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=\\sqrt{x}$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{3}^{-\\sqrt{x}}}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x & =2{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{3}^{-u}\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{3}^{-\\sqrt{x}}}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x & =2{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{3}^{-u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{3}^{-u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{{3}^{-u}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}]}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[{3}^{-u}]}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{3}^{a}}-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\frac{1}{{3}^{x}},$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{{3}^{x}}=\\infty $\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{3}^{x}}=0$\n\n![](images/ff/ff41c6fa5858f45e76a4d4fec0f0a8080300b69fcb632df60141d93c66f3cdd9.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{3}^{-\\sqrt{x}}}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{3}^{a}}-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\cdot (0-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we substitute $u=\\sqrt{x}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}}\\,⟹\\,2\\text{d}u=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty $ when $x\\to \\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=\\sqrt{x}$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{3}^{-\\sqrt{x}}}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x & =2{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{3}^{-u}\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{3}^{-\\sqrt{x}}}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x & =2{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{3}^{-u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{3}^{-u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{{3}^{-u}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}]}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[{3}^{-u}]}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\cdot (\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{3}^{a}}-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\frac{1}{{3}^{x}},$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{{3}^{x}}=\\infty $\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{3}^{x}}=0$\n\n![](images/ff/ff41c6fa5858f45e76a4d4fec0f0a8080300b69fcb632df60141d93c66f3cdd9.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{3}^{-\\sqrt{x}}}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\cdot (\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{3}^{a}}-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\cdot (0-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186275282 :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u=\\sqrt{1-x}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{1-x}}\\,⟹\\,-\\text{d}u=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{1-x}}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty $ when $x\\to -\\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=\\sqrt{1-x}$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $-\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{{e}^{-\\sqrt{1-x}}}{2\\sqrt{1-x}}\\,\\text{d}x=-{\\int }_{\\infty }^{1}{e}^{-u}\\,\\text{d}u={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{e}^{-u}\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{{e}^{-\\sqrt{1-x}}}{2\\sqrt{1-x}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}{e}^{-u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[-{e}^{-u}]_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(-\\frac{1}{{e}^{a}}+\\frac{1}{e}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{e}-{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{e}^{a}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\frac{1}{{e}^{x}}$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{{e}^{x}}=\\infty $\n\n- ${lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{e}^{x}}=0$\n\n![](images/4e/4ef3194e946aec805866202a5e0ad521291d71b1fabc2df68d6cb1a4d93da42b.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{{e}^{-\\sqrt{1-x}}}{2\\sqrt{1-x}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{e}-{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{e}^{a}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{e}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{e}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we substitute $u=\\sqrt{1-x}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{1-x}}\\,⟹\\,-\\text{d}u=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{1-x}}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty $ when $x\\to -\\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=\\sqrt{1-x}$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $-\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{{e}^{-\\sqrt{1-x}}}{2\\sqrt{1-x}}\\,\\text{d}x=-{\\int }_{\\infty }^{1}{e}^{-u}\\,\\text{d}u={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{e}^{-u}\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{{e}^{-\\sqrt{1-x}}}{2\\sqrt{1-x}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}{e}^{-u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }[-{e}^{-u}]_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(-\\frac{1}{{e}^{a}}+\\frac{1}{e}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{e}-\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{e}^{a}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\frac{1}{{e}^{x}}$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{{e}^{x}}=\\infty $\n\n- $\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{e}^{x}}=0$\n\n![](images/4e/4ef3194e946aec805866202a5e0ad521291d71b1fabc2df68d6cb1a4d93da42b.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{{e}^{-\\sqrt{1-x}}}{2\\sqrt{1-x}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{e}-\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{e}^{a}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{e}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{e}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186275283 :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=\\frac{1}{x}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty $ when $x\\to \\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{x\\cdot {5}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{5}^{u}}\\,\\text{d}u={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{5}^{-u}\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{x\\cdot {5}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{5}^{-u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{5}^{-u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{{5}^{-u}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}]}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[{5}^{-u}]}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{5}^{a}}-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\frac{1}{{5}^{x}},$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{{5}^{x}}=\\infty $\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{5}^{x}}=0$\n\n![](images/3c/3cb8903ed53a713c5e23e4dbad4801788440954ac3a5f7e08ab1bd71cc9c631f.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{x\\cdot {5}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{5}^{a}}-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}\\cdot (0-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we substitute $u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=\\frac{1}{x}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty $ when $x\\to \\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{x\\cdot {5}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{5}^{u}}\\,\\text{d}u={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{5}^{-u}\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{x\\cdot {5}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{5}^{-u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{5}^{-u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{{5}^{-u}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}]}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[{5}^{-u}]}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}\\cdot (\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{5}^{a}}-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\frac{1}{{5}^{x}},$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{{5}^{x}}=\\infty $\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{5}^{x}}=0$\n\n![](images/3c/3cb8903ed53a713c5e23e4dbad4801788440954ac3a5f7e08ab1bd71cc9c631f.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{x\\cdot {5}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}\\cdot (\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{5}^{a}}-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}\\cdot (0-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186275284 :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u=2+{x}^{3}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=3{x}^{2}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u={x}^{2}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to -\\infty $ when $x\\to -\\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=2+{x}^{3}$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $-\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $3$ | $-\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}{x}^{2}{e}^{2+{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{3}{e}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}{x}^{2}{e}^{2+{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{3}{e}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[{e}^{u}]_{a}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }({e}^{3}-{e}^{a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot ({e}^{3}-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{e}^{a})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, recall the end behavior of the function $y={e}^{x},$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{e}^{x}=0$\n\n- ${lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{x}=\\infty $\n\n![](images/3d/3df1853e93a5ab2a1c1ca4114d450b29a69223bd576b059d9aef1b17c68fe5b7.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}{x}^{2}{e}^{2+{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot ({e}^{3}-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{e}^{a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot ({e}^{3}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{3}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we substitute $u=2+{x}^{3}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=3{x}^{2}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u={x}^{2}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to -\\infty $ when $x\\to -\\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=2+{x}^{3}$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $-\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $3$ | $-\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}{x}^{2}{e}^{2+{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{3}{e}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}{x}^{2}{e}^{2+{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{3}{e}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[{e}^{u}]_{a}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }({e}^{3}-{e}^{a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot ({e}^{3}-\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{e}^{a})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, recall the end behavior of the function $y={e}^{x},$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{e}^{x}=0$\n\n- $\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{x}=\\infty $\n\n![](images/3d/3df1853e93a5ab2a1c1ca4114d450b29a69223bd576b059d9aef1b17c68fe5b7.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}{x}^{2}{e}^{2+{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot ({e}^{3}-\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{e}^{a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot ({e}^{3}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{3}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186275285 :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u=1-{x}^{2}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-2x\\,⟹\\,-\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u=x\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to -\\infty $ when $x\\to \\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=1-{x}^{2}$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $-\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{2}^{1-{x}^{2}}x\\,\\text{d}x=-\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{-\\infty }{2}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}{2}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{2}^{1-{x}^{2}}x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}{2}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{{2}^{u}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}]}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[{2}^{u}]_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(1-{2}^{a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}(1-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{2}^{a})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, recall the end behavior of the function $y={2}^{a},$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{2}^{a}=0$\n\n- ${lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{2}^{a}=\\infty $\n\n![](images/15/15f110a3a228573a5ec349664615293be38fff8c4bd4f14a8ec1b11b997cb818.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{2}^{1-{x}^{2}}x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\cdot (1-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{2}^{a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\cdot (1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we substitute $u=1-{x}^{2}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-2x\\,⟹\\,-\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u=x\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to -\\infty $ when $x\\to \\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=1-{x}^{2}$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $-\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{2}^{1-{x}^{2}}x\\,\\text{d}x=-\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{-\\infty }{2}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}{2}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{2}^{1-{x}^{2}}x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}{2}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{{2}^{u}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}]}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }[{2}^{u}]_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }(1-{2}^{a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}(1-\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{2}^{a})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, recall the end behavior of the function $y={2}^{a},$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{2}^{a}=0$\n\n- $\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{2}^{a}=\\infty $\n\n![](images/15/15f110a3a228573a5ec349664615293be38fff8c4bd4f14a8ec1b11b997cb818.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{2}^{1-{x}^{2}}x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\cdot (1-\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{2}^{a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\cdot (1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186275287 :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u=\\sqrt{x}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}}\\,⟹\\,2\\,\\text{d}u=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty $ when $x\\to \\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=\\sqrt{x}$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}(x+1)}\\,\\text{d}x & =2{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}(x+1)}\\,\\text{d}x & =2{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(u)]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(a)-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1)] \\\\  & =2\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(a)]-\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x),$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)]=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)]=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n![](images/46/46e6cf2950c99831a7445a862fb36a6a7b8f9233a82e49c7cf5da4a383ec4756.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}(x+1)}\\,\\text{d}x & =2\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(a)]-\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =2\\cdot (\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we substitute $u=\\sqrt{x}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}}\\,⟹\\,2\\,\\text{d}u=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty $ when $x\\to \\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=\\sqrt{x}$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}(x+1)}\\,\\text{d}x & =2{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}(x+1)}\\,\\text{d}x & =2{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(u)]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(a)-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1)] \\\\  & =2\\cdot (\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(a)]-\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x),$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)]=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)]=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n![](images/46/46e6cf2950c99831a7445a862fb36a6a7b8f9233a82e49c7cf5da4a383ec4756.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}(x+1)}\\,\\text{d}x & =2\\cdot (\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(a)]-\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =2\\cdot (\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186275288 :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u={x}^{4}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=4{x}^{3}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{d}u={x}^{3}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty $ when $x\\to -\\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u={x}^{4}$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $-\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{{x}^{3}}{1+9{x}^{8}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{\\infty }^{0}\\frac{1}{1+9{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{1+9{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{{x}^{3}}{1+9{x}^{8}}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{1+9{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}\\frac{1}{1+9{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3u)]}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{12}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3u)]_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{12}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3a)-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(0)] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{12}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3a)]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3x),$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3x)]=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3x)]=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n![](images/94/94ad95c4f0477ae2dd29538e6cfcbbd52cbd7eda5b1c29246c1b4967566a7341.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{{x}^{3}}{1+9{x}^{8}}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\frac{1}{12}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3a)] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{12}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\pi }{24}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we substitute $u={x}^{4}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=4{x}^{3}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{d}u={x}^{3}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty $ when $x\\to -\\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u={x}^{4}$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $-\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{{x}^{3}}{1+9{x}^{8}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{\\infty }^{0}\\frac{1}{1+9{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{1+9{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{{x}^{3}}{1+9{x}^{8}}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{1+9{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}\\frac{1}{1+9{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3u)]}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{12}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3u)]_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{12}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3a)-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(0)] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{12}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3a)]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3x),$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3x)]=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3x)]=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n![](images/94/94ad95c4f0477ae2dd29538e6cfcbbd52cbd7eda5b1c29246c1b4967566a7341.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{{x}^{3}}{1+9{x}^{8}}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\frac{1}{12}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3a)] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{12}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\pi }{24}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186275289 :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u={t}^{3}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t}=3{t}^{2}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=3{t}^{2}\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to -\\infty $ when $t\\to -\\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u={t}^{3}$ is as follows:\n\n| $t$ | $0$ | $-\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $-\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{3{t}^{2}}{1+25{t}^{6}}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{1}{1+25{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{3{t}^{2}}{1+25{t}^{6}}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{1}{1+25{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{1}{1+25{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot \\operatorname{arctan}⁡(5u)]}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(5u)]_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(0)-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(5a)] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(5a)]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(5x),$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(5x)]=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(5x)]=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n![](images/06/06f99111a041ff564ece3019e7dba5331bb4f225d224dff07bb92d83f4e33eb2.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{3{t}^{2}}{1+25{t}^{6}}\\,\\text{d}t & =-\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(5a)] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we substitute $u={t}^{3}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t}=3{t}^{2}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=3{t}^{2}\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to -\\infty $ when $t\\to -\\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u={t}^{3}$ is as follows:\n\n| $t$ | $0$ | $-\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $-\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{3{t}^{2}}{1+25{t}^{6}}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{1}{1+25{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{3{t}^{2}}{1+25{t}^{6}}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{1}{1+25{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{1}{1+25{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot \\operatorname{arctan}⁡(5u)]}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(5u)]_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(0)-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(5a)] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(5a)]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(5x),$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(5x)]=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(5x)]=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n![](images/06/06f99111a041ff564ece3019e7dba5331bb4f225d224dff07bb92d83f4e33eb2.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{3{t}^{2}}{1+25{t}^{6}}\\,\\text{d}t & =-\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(5a)] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186275290 :question/worked-solution "We proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{{\\theta }^{6}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}\\frac{2}{{\\theta }^{6}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}2{\\theta }^{-6}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[(-\\frac{2}{5}{\\theta }^{-5})]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{5}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{1}{{\\theta }^{5}}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{5}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\frac{1}{{a}^{5}}-\\frac{1}{{1}^{5}}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{5}(0-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{{\\theta }^{6}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}\\frac{2}{{\\theta }^{6}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}2{\\theta }^{-6}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{[(-\\frac{2}{5}{\\theta }^{-5})]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{5}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{1}{{\\theta }^{5}}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{5}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\frac{1}{{a}^{5}}-\\frac{1}{{1}^{5}}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{5}(0-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186275291 :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(x):$ \n\n![](images/df/df22eea4331c47da3047381ca672fd80ebf75118054316fe88c0e2d96dae0d34.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(x)$ is $x\\in [-1,1].$  \n\nNow, substituting $x=0$ directly into the limit, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,0}(2x\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(x)) & =2\\cdot 0\\cdot \\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(0) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 0\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let's sketch the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(x):$ \n\n![](images/df/df22eea4331c47da3047381ca672fd80ebf75118054316fe88c0e2d96dae0d34.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(x)$ is $x\\in [-1,1].$  \n\nNow, substituting $x=0$ directly into the limit, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\,0}(2x\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(x)) & =2\\cdot 0\\cdot \\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(0) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 0\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186275292 :question/worked-solution "We proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{3}^{\\infty }\\frac{5}{{x}^{2}}\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{3}^{a}\\frac{5}{{x}^{2}}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{3}^{a}5{x}^{-2}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[(-5{x}^{-1})]}_{3}^{a} \\\\  & =-5\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{1}{x}]}_{3}^{a} \\\\  & =-5\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\frac{1}{a}-\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\  & =-5\\cdot (0-\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{3}^{\\infty }\\frac{5}{{x}^{2}}\\text{d}x & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{3}^{a}\\frac{5}{{x}^{2}}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{3}^{a}5{x}^{-2}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{[(-5{x}^{-1})]}_{3}^{a} \\\\  & =-5\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{1}{x}]}_{3}^{a} \\\\  & =-5\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\frac{1}{a}-\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\  & =-5\\cdot (0-\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186275294 :question/worked-solution "We proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{8}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{\\sqrt[3]{{z}^{4}}}\\,\\text{d}z & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{8}^{a}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt[3]{{z}^{4}}}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =2{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{8}^{a}{z}^{-4/3}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =2{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-3{z}^{-1/3}]}_{8}^{a} \\\\  & =-6\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{z}}]}_{8}^{a} \\\\  & =-6\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{a}}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{8}}) \\\\  & =-6(0-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{8}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{\\sqrt[3]{{z}^{4}}}\\,\\text{d}z & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{8}^{a}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt[3]{{z}^{4}}}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =2\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{8}^{a}{z}^{-4/3}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =2\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{[-3{z}^{-1/3}]}_{8}^{a} \\\\  & =-6\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{z}}]}_{8}^{a} \\\\  & =-6\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{a}}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{8}}) \\\\  & =-6(0-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186275295 :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u=z+2.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}z}=1\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=\\text{d}z.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty $ when $z\\to \\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=z+2$ is as follows:\n\n| $z$ | $-1$ | $\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-1}^{\\infty }\\frac{6}{(z+2)^{4}}\\,\\text{d}z={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{6}{{u}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}u\n$$\n\nFinally, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{\\infty }\\frac{6}{(z+2)^{4}}\\,\\text{d}z & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{6}{{u}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =6{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{u}^{-4}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =6{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}{u}^{-4}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =6{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{3}{u}^{-3}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{1}{{u}^{3}}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\frac{1}{{a}^{3}}-\\frac{1}{{1}^{3}}] \\\\  & =-2\\cdot (0-1) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we substitute $u=z+2.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}z}=1\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=\\text{d}z.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty $ when $z\\to \\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=z+2$ is as follows:\n\n| $z$ | $-1$ | $\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-1}^{\\infty }\\frac{6}{(z+2)^{4}}\\,\\text{d}z={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{6}{{u}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}u\n$$\n\nFinally, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{\\infty }\\frac{6}{(z+2)^{4}}\\,\\text{d}z & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{6}{{u}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =6{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{u}^{-4}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =6\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}{u}^{-4}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =6\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{3}{u}^{-3}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{1}{{u}^{3}}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\frac{1}{{a}^{3}}-\\frac{1}{{1}^{3}}] \\\\  & =-2\\cdot (0-1) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186275296 :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u={z}^{4}+1.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}z}=4{z}^{3}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{4}\\text{d}u={z}^{3}\\,\\text{d}z.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty $ when $z\\to \\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u={z}^{4}+1$ is as follows:\n\n| $z$ | $1$ | $\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $2$ | $\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{8{z}^{3}}{{({z}^{4}+1)}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}z & ={\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }\\frac{8}{{u}^{4}}(\\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{u}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{8{z}^{3}}{{({z}^{4}+1)}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}z & =2{\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{u}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }{u}^{-4}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{2}^{a}{u}^{-4}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{3}{u}^{-3}]}_{2}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{1}{{u}^{3}}]}_{2}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\frac{1}{{a}^{3}}-\\frac{1}{8}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}(0-\\frac{1}{8}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we substitute $u={z}^{4}+1.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}z}=4{z}^{3}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{4}\\text{d}u={z}^{3}\\,\\text{d}z.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty $ when $z\\to \\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u={z}^{4}+1$ is as follows:\n\n| $z$ | $1$ | $\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $2$ | $\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{8{z}^{3}}{{({z}^{4}+1)}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}z & ={\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }\\frac{8}{{u}^{4}}(\\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{u}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{8{z}^{3}}{{({z}^{4}+1)}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}z & =2{\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{u}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }{u}^{-4}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{2}^{a}{u}^{-4}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{3}{u}^{-3}]}_{2}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{1}{{u}^{3}}]}_{2}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }(\\frac{1}{{a}^{3}}-\\frac{1}{8}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}(0-\\frac{1}{8}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186275297 :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(x):$ \n\n![](images/9d/9dcc5a116c2a564547283783a7df7b1cd3a9ab3b76ac24dc2695c4cb367796d5.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(x)$ is $x\\in [-1,1].$   \n\nNow, substituting $x=\\sqrt{3}/2$ directly into the limit, we have  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,\\sqrt{3}/2}(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}+3\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(x)) & =\\frac{3\\pi }{2}+3\\cdot \\operatorname{arccos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{2}+3\\cdot \\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right) \\\\  & =2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let's sketch the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(x):$ \n\n![](images/9d/9dcc5a116c2a564547283783a7df7b1cd3a9ab3b76ac24dc2695c4cb367796d5.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(x)$ is $x\\in [-1,1].$   \n\nNow, substituting $x=\\sqrt{3}/2$ directly into the limit, we have  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\,\\sqrt{3}/2}(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}+3\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(x)) & =\\frac{3\\pi }{2}+3\\cdot \\operatorname{arccos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{2}+3\\cdot \\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right) \\\\  & =2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186275300 :question/worked-solution "We proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{2}{3}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{2}{3}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{{3}^{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)}]}_{a}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[{3}^{x}]}_{a}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }({3}^{2}-{3}^{a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)}\\cdot (9-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{3}^{a})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y={3}^{x}$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{3}^{x}=0$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{3}^{x}=\\infty $\n\n![](images/ab/abae6131aceb7348a82ea01a544b28a62d8835b7857da14a6339b4ae3b88a1fd.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}{3}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)}\\cdot (9-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{3}^{a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)}\\cdot (9-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{2}{3}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{2}{3}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{{3}^{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)}]}_{a}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[{3}^{x}]}_{a}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }({3}^{2}-{3}^{a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)}\\cdot (9-\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{3}^{a})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y={3}^{x}$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }{3}^{x}=0$\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }{3}^{x}=\\infty $\n\n![](images/ab/abae6131ac