24}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186275304 :question/worked-solution "We proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{3}\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{3}{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[{e}^{x}]}_{a}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }({e}^{3}-{e}^{a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot ({e}^{3}-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{e}^{a})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y={e}^{x}$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{e}^{x}=0$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{e}^{x}=\\infty $\n\n![](images/11/115e3198226940a76b770f5942bb06e6496bd234d6cb5fee2d33f19038931bd3.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{3}\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot ({e}^{3}-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{e}^{a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot ({e}^{3}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{3}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{3}\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{3}{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[{e}^{x}]}_{a}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }({e}^{3}-{e}^{a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot ({e}^{3}-\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{e}^{a})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y={e}^{x}$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }{e}^{x}=0$\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }{e}^{x}=\\infty $\n\n![](images/11/115e3198226940a76b770f5942bb06e6496bd234d6cb5fee2d33f19038931bd3.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{3}\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot ({e}^{3}-\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{e}^{a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot ({e}^{3}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{3}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186275305 :question/worked-solution "We proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{2}3{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{3}3{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[{e}^{x}]}_{a}^{2} \\\\  & =3\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }({e}^{2}-{e}^{a}) \\\\  & =3\\cdot ({e}^{2}-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{e}^{a})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y={e}^{x}$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{e}^{x}=0$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{e}^{x}=\\infty $\n\n![](images/11/115e3198226940a76b770f5942bb06e6496bd234d6cb5fee2d33f19038931bd3.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{2}3{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =3\\cdot ({e}^{2}-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{e}^{a}) \\\\  & =3({e}^{2}-0) \\\\  & =3{e}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{2}3{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{3}3{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[{e}^{x}]}_{a}^{2} \\\\  & =3\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }({e}^{2}-{e}^{a}) \\\\  & =3\\cdot ({e}^{2}-\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{e}^{a})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y={e}^{x}$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }{e}^{x}=0$\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }{e}^{x}=\\infty $\n\n![](images/11/115e3198226940a76b770f5942bb06e6496bd234d6cb5fee2d33f19038931bd3.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{2}3{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =3\\cdot ({e}^{2}-\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{e}^{a}) \\\\  & =3({e}^{2}-0) \\\\  & =3{e}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186275306 :question/worked-solution "We proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{4}5{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{4}5{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =5\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[{e}^{x}]}_{a}^{4} \\\\  & =5\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }({e}^{4}-{e}^{a}) \\\\  & =5\\cdot ({e}^{4}-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{e}^{a})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y={e}^{x}$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{e}^{x}=0$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{e}^{x}=\\infty $\n\n![](images/11/115e3198226940a76b770f5942bb06e6496bd234d6cb5fee2d33f19038931bd3.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{4}5{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =5\\cdot ({e}^{4}-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{e}^{a}) \\\\  & =5({e}^{4}-0) \\\\  & =5{e}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{4}5{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{4}5{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =5\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[{e}^{x}]}_{a}^{4} \\\\  & =5\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }({e}^{4}-{e}^{a}) \\\\  & =5\\cdot ({e}^{4}-\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{e}^{a})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y={e}^{x}$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }{e}^{x}=0$\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }{e}^{x}=\\infty $\n\n![](images/11/115e3198226940a76b770f5942bb06e6496bd234d6cb5fee2d33f19038931bd3.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{4}5{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =5\\cdot ({e}^{4}-\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{e}^{a}) \\\\  & =5({e}^{4}-0) \\\\  & =5{e}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186275307 :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int {e}^{px+q}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{p}{e}^{px+q}+C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{\\infty }{e}^{-2x-1}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{-1}^{a}{e}^{-2x-1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-2x-1}]}_{-1}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{-1}}{2}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[{e}^{-2x}]}_{-1}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2e}({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{e}^{2a}}-{e}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\frac{1}{{e}^{2x}},$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{{e}^{2x}}=\\infty $\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{e}^{2x}}=0$\n\n![](images/00/0064a1c234adc9cf5d28d16696bef8a72384a8cc9f85ecc865c5d4ba36dc0d49.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{\\infty }{e}^{-2x-1}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\frac{1}{2e}({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{e}^{2a}}-{e}^{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2e}(0-{e}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{e}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int {e}^{px+q}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{p}{e}^{px+q}+C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{\\infty }{e}^{-2x-1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{-1}^{a}{e}^{-2x-1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-2x-1}]}_{-1}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{-1}}{2}\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{[{e}^{-2x}]}_{-1}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2e}(\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{e}^{2a}}-{e}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\frac{1}{{e}^{2x}},$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{{e}^{2x}}=\\infty $\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{e}^{2x}}=0$\n\n![](images/00/0064a1c234adc9cf5d28d16696bef8a72384a8cc9f85ecc865c5d4ba36dc0d49.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{\\infty }{e}^{-2x-1}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\frac{1}{2e}(\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{e}^{2a}}-{e}^{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2e}(0-{e}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{e}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186275308 :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x):$\n\n![](images/ca/ca5e8035505f20619b6707d6d1f2d0b7b2f125a004a0dbd9d448840b0e4ffd99.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)$ is $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$  \n\nAs $x$ increases to $\\infty ,$ the graph tends to $y=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,\\infty }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi ) \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let's sketch the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x):$\n\n![](images/ca/ca5e8035505f20619b6707d6d1f2d0b7b2f125a004a0dbd9d448840b0e4ffd99.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)$ is $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$  \n\nAs $x$ increases to $\\infty ,$ the graph tends to $y=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\,\\infty }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi ) \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186275309 :question/worked-solution "To test for continuity of $f(x)$ at a point $x=c$, we need to check three conditions:\n\n- Does $f(c)$ exist?\n\n- Does ${lim}_{x\\to c}f(x)$ exist?\n\n- Does ${lim}_{x\\to c}f(x)=f(c)?$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement “$f(x)$ is defined at $x=0$” is true. Using the expression for the middle branch of the function, we obtain that \n\n$$\nf(0)=0.\n$$\n\nSo, $f(x)$ is defined at $x=0.$ $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- Statement “${lim}_{x\\to \\,0}f(x)$ exists” is true. By calculating the one-sided limits, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}(x+1) \\\\  & =(0)+1 \\\\  & =1 \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}({e}^{-x}) \\\\  & ={e}^{0} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x)\n$$\n\n \nTherefore, the limit of $f(x)$ at $x=0$ exists:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 0}f(x)=1\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\n- Statement ${lim}_{x\\to \\,0}f(x)=f(0)$ is false. Indeed, we have that ${lim}_{x\\to 0}f(x)=1\\ne f(0).$  $\\,\\,\\times $\n\n- Statement “$f(x)$ is continuous at $x=0$” is false. Since not all statements “$f(x)$ is defined at $x=0$”; “${lim}_{x\\to \\,0}f(x)$ exists”; ${lim}_{x\\to \\,0}f(x)=f(0)$ are true, the function $f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$f(x)$ is defined at $x=0$”; “${lim}_{x\\to \\,0}f(x)$ exists” only." "To test for continuity of $f(x)$ at a point $x=c$, we need to check three conditions:\n\n- Does $f(c)$ exist?\n\n- Does $\\lim_{x\\to c}f(x)$ exist?\n\n- Does $\\lim_{x\\to c}f(x)=f(c)?$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement “$f(x)$ is defined at $x=0$” is true. Using the expression for the middle branch of the function, we obtain that \n\n$$\nf(0)=0.\n$$\n\nSo, $f(x)$ is defined at $x=0.$ $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- Statement “$\\lim_{x\\to \\,0}f(x)$ exists” is true. By calculating the one-sided limits, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{-}}(x+1) \\\\  & =(0)+1 \\\\  & =1 \\\\ \\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}({e}^{-x}) \\\\  & ={e}^{0} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x)=\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x)\n$$\n\n \nTherefore, the limit of $f(x)$ at $x=0$ exists:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 0}f(x)=1\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\n- Statement $\\lim_{x\\to \\,0}f(x)=f(0)$ is false. Indeed, we have that $\\lim_{x\\to 0}f(x)=1\\ne f(0).$  $\\,\\,\\times $\n\n- Statement “$f(x)$ is continuous at $x=0$” is false. Since not all statements “$f(x)$ is defined at $x=0$”; “$\\lim_{x\\to \\,0}f(x)$ exists”; $\\lim_{x\\to \\,0}f(x)=f(0)$ are true, the function $f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$f(x)$ is defined at $x=0$”; “$\\lim_{x\\to \\,0}f(x)$ exists” only."] [:db/cas 17592186275310 :question/worked-solution "To test for continuity of $f(x)$ at a point $x=c$, we need to check three conditions:\n\n- Does $f(c)$ exist?\n\n- Does ${lim}_{x\\to c}f(x)$ exist?\n\n- Does ${lim}_{x\\to c}f(x)=f(c)?$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement “$f(x)$ is defined at $x=-1$” is true. Using the expression for the first branch of the function, we obtain that \n\n$$\nf(-1)=\\sqrt{3-(-1)}=2.\n$$\n\nSo, $f(x)$ is defined at $x=-1.$ $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- Statement “${lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)}f(x)$ exists” is false. By calculating the one-sided limits, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {(-1)}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {(-1)}^{-}}(\\sqrt{3-x}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3-(-1)} \\\\  & =2 \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to {(-1)}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {(-1)}^{+}}(x+1) \\\\  & =(-1)+1 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {(-1)}^{-}}f(x)\\ne {lim}_{x\\to {(-1)}^{+}}f(x)\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit of $f(x)$ at $x=-1$ does not exist. $\\,\\,\\times $\n\n- Statement ${lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)}f(x)=f(-1)$ is false since the limit of $f(x)$ at $x=-1$ does not exist. $\\,\\,\\times $\n\n- Statement “$f(x)$ is continuous at $x=-1$” is false. Since not all statements “$f(x)$ is defined at $x=-1$”; “${lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)}f(x)$ exists”; ${lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)}f(x)=f(-1)$ are true, the function $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=-1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$f(x)$ is defined at $x=-1$” only." "To test for continuity of $f(x)$ at a point $x=c$, we need to check three conditions:\n\n- Does $f(c)$ exist?\n\n- Does $\\lim_{x\\to c}f(x)$ exist?\n\n- Does $\\lim_{x\\to c}f(x)=f(c)?$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement “$f(x)$ is defined at $x=-1$” is true. Using the expression for the first branch of the function, we obtain that \n\n$$\nf(-1)=\\sqrt{3-(-1)}=2.\n$$\n\nSo, $f(x)$ is defined at $x=-1.$ $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- Statement “$\\lim_{x\\to \\,(-1)}f(x)$ exists” is false. By calculating the one-sided limits, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {(-1)}^{-}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to {(-1)}^{-}}(\\sqrt{3-x}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3-(-1)} \\\\  & =2 \\\\ \\lim_{x\\to {(-1)}^{+}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to {(-1)}^{+}}(x+1) \\\\  & =(-1)+1 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {(-1)}^{-}}f(x)\\ne {lim}_{x\\to {(-1)}^{+}}f(x)\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit of $f(x)$ at $x=-1$ does not exist. $\\,\\,\\times $\n\n- Statement $\\lim_{x\\to \\,(-1)}f(x)=f(-1)$ is false since the limit of $f(x)$ at $x=-1$ does not exist. $\\,\\,\\times $\n\n- Statement “$f(x)$ is continuous at $x=-1$” is false. Since not all statements “$f(x)$ is defined at $x=-1$”; “$\\lim_{x\\to \\,(-1)}f(x)$ exists”; $\\lim_{x\\to \\,(-1)}f(x)=f(-1)$ are true, the function $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=-1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$f(x)$ is defined at $x=-1$” only."] [:db/cas 17592186275311 :question/worked-solution "To test for continuity of $f(x)$ at a point $x=c$, we need to check three conditions:\n\n- Does $f(c)$ exist?\n\n- Does ${lim}_{x\\to c}f(x)$ exist?\n\n- Does ${lim}_{x\\to c}f(x)=f(c)?$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement “$f(x)$ is defined at $x=0$” is true. Using the expression for the middle branch of the function, we obtain that \n\n$$\nf(0)=1.\n$$\n\nSo, $f(x)$ is defined at $x=1.$ $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- Statement ${lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x)$ is true. By calculating the one-sided limits, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}(2x) \\\\  & =2(0) \\\\  & =0 \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}(3x) \\\\  & =3(0) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x)\n$$\n\n \nTherefore, the limit of $f(x)$ at $x=0$ exists:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 0}f(x)=0\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\n- Statement ${lim}_{x\\to 0}f(x)=f(0)$ is false. Indeed, we have that ${lim}_{x\\to 0}f(x)=0\\ne f(0).$  $\\,\\,\\times $\n\n- Statement “$f(x)$ is continuous at $x=0$” is false. Since not all statements “$f(x)$ is defined at $x=0$”; ${lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x)$; ${lim}_{x\\to 0}f(x)=f(0)$ are true, according to the continuity test, the function $f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$f(x)$ is defined at $x=0$”; ${lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x)$ only." "To test for continuity of $f(x)$ at a point $x=c$, we need to check three conditions:\n\n- Does $f(c)$ exist?\n\n- Does $\\lim_{x\\to c}f(x)$ exist?\n\n- Does $\\lim_{x\\to c}f(x)=f(c)?$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement “$f(x)$ is defined at $x=0$” is true. Using the expression for the middle branch of the function, we obtain that \n\n$$\nf(0)=1.\n$$\n\nSo, $f(x)$ is defined at $x=1.$ $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- Statement $\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x)=\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x)$ is true. By calculating the one-sided limits, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{-}}(2x) \\\\  & =2(0) \\\\  & =0 \\\\ \\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}(3x) \\\\  & =3(0) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x)=\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x)\n$$\n\n \nTherefore, the limit of $f(x)$ at $x=0$ exists:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 0}f(x)=0\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\n- Statement $\\lim_{x\\to 0}f(x)=f(0)$ is false. Indeed, we have that $\\lim_{x\\to 0}f(x)=0\\ne f(0).$  $\\,\\,\\times $\n\n- Statement “$f(x)$ is continuous at $x=0$” is false. Since not all statements “$f(x)$ is defined at $x=0$”; $\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x)=\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x)$; $\\lim_{x\\to 0}f(x)=f(0)$ are true, according to the continuity test, the function $f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$f(x)$ is defined at $x=0$”; $\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x)=\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x)$ only."] [:db/cas 17592186275312 :question/worked-solution "To test for continuity of $f(x)$ at a point $x=c$, we need to check three conditions:\n\n- Does $f(c)$ exist?\n\n- Does ${lim}_{x\\to c}f(x)$ exist?\n\n- Does ${lim}_{x\\to c}f(x)=f(c)?$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement “$f(x)$ is defined at $x=2$” is true. Using the expression for the first branch of the function, we obtain that \n\n$$\nf(2)=-4-2=-6.\n$$\n\nSo, $f(x)$ is defined at $x=2.$ $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- Statement ${lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x)$ is true. By calculating the one-sided limits, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}(-4-x) \\\\  & =-4-2 \\\\  & =-6 \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}(-3x) \\\\  & =-3(2) \\\\  & =-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x)\n$$\n\n \nTherefore, the limit of $f(x)$ at $x=2$ exists:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 2}f(x)=-6\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\n- Statement ${lim}_{x\\to 2}f(x)=f(2)$ is true. Indeed, we have that ${lim}_{x\\to 2}f(x)=-6=f(2).$  $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- Statement “$f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=2$” is false. Since all statements “$f(x)$ is defined at $x=2$”; ${lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x)$; ${lim}_{x\\to 2}f(x)=f(2)$ are true, according to the continuity test, the function $f(x)$ is continuous 