lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(3{x}^{2}-x) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }x(3x-1) \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f\\ne O(1)$ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\n- Statement “$f=O(x{e}^{x})$ as $x\\to \\infty $” is true. Let $g(x)=x{e}^{x}.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{3{x}^{2}-x}{x{e}^{x}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{x(3x-1)}{x{e}^{x}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{3x-1}{{e}^{x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince now direct evaluation leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty },$ we can evaluate the limit using L'Hopital's rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{3x-1}{{e}^{x}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(3x-1)^{′}}{({e}^{x})^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{3}{{e}^{x}} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f=O(x{e}^{x})$ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\n- Statement “$f=O({x}^{3})$ as $x\\to \\infty $” is true. Let $g(x)={x}^{3}.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{3{x}^{2}-x}{{x}^{3}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{3}{x}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f=O({x}^{8})$ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$f=O(x{e}^{x})$ as $x\\to \\infty $”; “$f=O({x}^{3})$ as $x\\to \\infty $” only." "Suppose we wish to test whether the following statement is true:\n\n$$\nf(x)=O(g(x))\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a.\n$$\n\nTo do this, we can examine the limit\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}.\n$$\n\nWe have the following:\n\n- If the limit is *finite*, the statement is true.\n\n- If the limit is *infinite*, the statement is false.\n\n- If the limit does not exist, the test is inconclusive.\n\nHere, we must consider the polynomial\n\n$$\nf(x)=3{x}^{2}-x.\n$$\n\nNow, let us analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement “$f=O(1)$ as $x\\to \\infty 