)=2{x}^{4}+5{x}^{2}+10.\n$$\n\nNow, let us analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement “$f=O({x}^{5})$ as $x\\to \\infty $” is true. Let $g(x)={x}^{5}.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{2{x}^{4}+5{x}^{2}+10}{{x}^{5}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{2}{x}+\\frac{5}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{10}{{x}^{5}}) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f=O({x}^{5})$ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\n- Statement “$f=O({x}^{2})$ as $x\\to \\infty $” is false. Let $g(x)={x}^{2}.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{2{x}^{4}+5{x}^{2}+10}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(2{x}^{2}+5+\\frac{10}{{x}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f\\ne O({x}^{2})$ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\n- Statement “$f=O(1)$ as $x\\to \\infty $” is false. Let $g(x)=1.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{2{x}^{4}+5{x}^{2}+10}{1} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(2{x}^{4}+5{x}^{2}+10) \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f\\ne O(1)$ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$f=O({x}^{5})$ as $x\\to \\infty $” only." "Suppose we wish to test whether the following statement is true:\n\n$$\nf(x)=O(g(x))\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a.\n$$\n\nTo do this, we can examine the limit\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}.\n$$\n\nWe have the following:\n\n- If the limit is *finite*, the statement is true.\n\n- If the limit is *infinite*, the statement is false.\n\n- If the limit does not exist, the test is inconclusive.\n\nHere, we must consider the polynomial\n\n$$\nf(x)=2{x}^{4}+5{x}^{2}+10.\n$$\n\nNow, let us analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement “$f=O({x}^{5})$ as $x\\to \\infty $” is true. Let $g(x)={x}^{5}.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{2{x}^{4}+5{x}^{2}+10}{{x}^{5}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{2}{x}+\\frac{5}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{10}{{x}^{5}}) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f=O({x}^{5})$ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\n- Statement “$f=O({x}^{2})$ as $x\\to \\infty $” is false. Let $g(x)={x}^{2}.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{2{x}^{4}+5{x}^{2}+10}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }(2{x}^{2}+5+\\frac{10}{{x}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f\\ne O({x}^{2})$ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\n- Statement “$f=O(1)$ as $x\\to \\infty $” is false. Let $g(x)=1.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{2{x}^{4}+5{x}^{2}+10}{1} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }(2{x}^{4}+5{x}^{2}+10) \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f\\ne O(1)$ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$f=O({x}^{5})$ as $x\\to \\infty $” only."] [:db/cas 17592186308717 :question/worked-solution "Suppose we wish to test whether the following statement is true:\n\n$$\nf(x)=O(g(x))\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is either finite or $+\\infty .$ To do this, we can examine the following limit:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}.\n$$\n\nWe have the following:\n\n- If the limit is *finite*, the statement is true.\n\n- If the limit is *infinite*, the statement is false.\n\n- If the limit does not exist, the test is inconclusive.\n\nHere, we must consider the function\n\n$$\nf(x)={e}^{x}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.\n$$\n\nNow, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement “$f=O(1)$ as $x\\to \\infty $” is false. Let $g(x)=1.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }({e}^{x}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x) \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f\\ne O(1)$ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\n- Statement “$f=O(x)$ as $x\\to \\infty $” is false. Let $g(x)=x.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{e}^{x}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince direct evaluation leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty },$ we can evaluate the limit using L'Hopital's rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{e}^{x}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{({e}^{x}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{′}}{(x)^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{({e}^{x}+\\frac{1}{x})}{1} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }({e}^{x}+\\frac{1}{x}) \\\\  & =\\infty +0 \\\\  & =\\infty \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f\\ne O(x)$ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\n- Statement “$f=O({e}^{x})$ as $x\\to \\infty $” is true. Let $g(x)={e}^{x}.$ Then, using L'Hopital's rule where needed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{e}^{x}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{e}^{x}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(1+\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{e}^{x}}) \\\\  & =1+{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{e}^{x}} \\\\  & =1+{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{′}}{({e}^{x})^{′}} \\\\  & =1+{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1/x}{{e}^{x}} \\\\  & =1+{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{x{e}^{x}} \\\\  & =1+0 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f=O({e}^{x})$ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$f=O({e}^{x})$ as $x\\to \\infty $” only." "Suppose we wish to test whether the following statement is true:\n\n$$\nf(x)=O(g(x))\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is either finite or $+\\infty .$ To do this, we can examine the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}.\n$$\n\nWe have the following:\n\n- If the limit is *finite*, the statement is true.\n\n- If the limit is *infinite*, the statement is false.\n\n- If the limit does not exist, the test is inconclusive.\n\nHere, we must consider the function\n\n$$\nf(x)={e}^{x}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.\n$$\n\nNow, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement “$f=O(1)$ as $x\\to \\infty $” is false. Let $g(x)=1.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }({e}^{x}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x) \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f\\ne O(1)$ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\n- Statement “$f=O(x)$ as $x\\to \\infty $” is false. Let $g(x)=x.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{e}^{x}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince direct evaluation leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty },$ we can evaluate the limit using L'Hopital's rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{e}^{x}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{({e}^{x}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{′}}{(x)^{′}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{({e}^{x}+\\frac{1}{x})}{1} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }({e}^{x}+\\frac{1}{x}) \\\\  & =\\infty +0 \\\\  & =\\infty \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f\\ne O(x)$ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\n- Statement “$f=O({e}^{x})$ as $x\\to \\infty $” is true. Let $g(x)={e}^{x}.$ Then, using L'Hopital's rule where needed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{e}^{x}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{e}^{x}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }(1+\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{e}^{x}}) \\\\  & =1+\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{e}^{x}} \\\\  & =1+\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{′}}{({e}^{x})^{′}} \\\\  & =1+\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1/x}{{e}^{x}} \\\\  & =1+\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{x{e}^{x}} \\\\  & =1+0 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f=O({e}^{x})$ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$f=O({e}^{x})$ as $x\\to \\infty $” only."] [:db/cas 17592186311747 :question/worked-solution "Here the limits of the numerator and denominator are ${lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5x)=-\\infty $ and ${lim}_{x\\to {0}^{