0}{0}.$ To find the limit, we apply L'Hopital's rule once again. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{[2x]}^{′}}{{[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)]}^{′}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)} \\\\  & =2\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186311770 :question/worked-solution "When we divide the two functions, we get a constant, and the limit of a constant is just the constant itself:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{p(x)}{q(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(11{x}^{4})}{(5{x}^{4})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{11}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "When we divide the two functions, we get a constant, and the limit of a constant is just the constant itself:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{p(x)}{q(x)} & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(11{x}^{4})}{(5{x}^{4})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{11}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186311771 :question/worked-solution "If we plug in $x=0$ we get the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ so we apply L'Hopital's rule to find the limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{4}^{x}-{9}^{x}-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\operatorname{ln}⁡9)x}{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{[{4}^{x}-{9}^{x}-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\operatorname{ln}⁡9)x]}^{′}}{{[1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)]}^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{4}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-{9}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡9-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\operatorname{ln}⁡9)}{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{4}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-{9}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡9-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\operatorname{ln}⁡9)}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis time, the resulting limit still gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$ To find the limit, we apply L'Hopital's rule once again. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{4}^{x}-{9}^{x}-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\operatorname{ln}⁡9)x}{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)} & =\\frac{1}{3}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{4}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-{9}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡9-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\operatorname{ln}⁡9)}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{[{4}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-{9}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡9-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\operatorname{ln}⁡9)]}^{′}}{{[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)]}^{′}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{4}^{x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡4)^{2}-{9}^{x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡9)^{2}}{3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\cdot \\frac{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡4)^{2}-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡9)^{2}}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}((\\operatorname{ln}⁡4)^{2}-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡9)^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "If we plug in $x=0$ we get the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ so we apply L'Hopital's rule to find the limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{4}^{x}-{9}^{x}-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\operatorname{ln}⁡9)x}{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{[{4}^{x}-{9}^{x}-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\operatorname{ln}⁡9)x]}^{′}}{{[1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)]}^{′}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{4}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-{9}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡9-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\operatorname{ln}⁡9)}{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{4}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-{9}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡9-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\operatorname{ln}⁡9)}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis time, the resulting limit still gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$ To find the limit, we apply L'Hopital's rule once again. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{4}^{x}-{9}^{x}-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\operatorname{ln}⁡9)x}{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)} & =\\frac{1}{3}\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{4}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-{9}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡9-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\operatorname{ln}⁡9)}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{[{4}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-{9}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡9-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\operatorname{ln}⁡9)]}^{′}}{{[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)]}^{′}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{4}^{x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡4)^{2}-{9}^{x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡9)^{2}}{3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\cdot \\frac{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡4)^{2}-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡9)^{2}}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}((\\operatorname{ln}⁡4)^{2}-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡9)^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186311772 :question/worked-solution "If we plug in $x=0$ we get the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ so  we apply L'Hopital's rule to find the limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{ln\\,(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{[ln\\,(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)]}^{′}}{[1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)]^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))}{(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)}{(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis time, the resulting limit still gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$ To find the limit, we apply L'Hopital's rule once again. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{ln\\,(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)} & =-\\frac{2}{3}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)}{(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{[\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)]}^{′}}{{[(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)]}^{′}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)}{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+3(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot \\frac{2}{3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "If we plug in $x=0$ we get the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ so  we apply L'Hopital's rule to find the limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{ln\\,(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{[ln\\,(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)]}^{′}}{[1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)]^{′}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))}{(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)}{(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis time, the resulting limit still gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$ To find the limit, we apply L'Hopital's rule once again. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{ln\\,(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)} & =-\\frac{2}{3}\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)}{(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{[\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)]}^{′}}{{[(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)]}^{′}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)}{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+3(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot \\frac{2}{3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186311773 :question/worked-solution "If we plug in $x=0$ we get the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ so  we apply L'Hopital's rule to find the limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+5x)-5x}{{x}^{2}} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+5x)-5x]}^{′}}{[{x}^{2}]^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{(\\frac{5}{1+5x}-5)}{2x} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{(\\frac{1}{1+5x}-1)}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis time, the resulting limit still gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$ To find the limit, we apply L'Hopital's rule once again. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+5x)-5x}{{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{5}{2}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{(\\frac{1}{1+5x}-1)}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{[(1+5x)^{-1}-1]}^{′}}{[x]^{′}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}{lim}_{x\\to 0}(-5(1+5x)^{-2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}(-5) \\\\  & =-\\frac{25}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "If we plug in $x=0$ we get the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ so  we apply L'Hopital's rule to find the limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+5x)-5x}{{x}^{2}} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+5x)-5x]}^{′}}{[{x}^{2}]^{′}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{(\\frac{5}{1+5x}-5)}{2x} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{(\\frac{1}{1+5x}-1)}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis time, the resulting limit still gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$ To find the limit, we apply L'Hopital's rule once again. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+5x)-5x}{{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{5}{2}\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{(\\frac{1}{1+5x}-1)}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{[(1+5x)^{-1}-1]}^{′}}{[x]^{′}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}\\lim_{x\\to 0}(-5(1+5x)^{-2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}(-5) \\\\  & =-\\frac{25}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186311774 :question/worked-solution "If we plug in $x=0$ we get the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ so we apply L'Hopital's rule to find the limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)+{sin}^{2}⁡(2x)}{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{[1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)+{sin}^{2}⁡(2x)]}^{′}}{{[1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)]}^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis time, the resulting limit still gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$ To find the limit, we apply L'Hopital's rule once again. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)+{sin}^{2}⁡(2x)}{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)} & =\\frac{4}{3}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)]}^{′}}{[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)]^{′}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)+2{cos}^{2}⁡(2x)-2{sin}^{2}⁡(2x)}{3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}\\cdot \\frac{4+2}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "If we plug in $x=0$ we get the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ so we apply L'Hopital's rule to find the limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)+{sin}^{2}⁡(2x)}{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{[1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)+{sin}^{2}⁡(2x)]}^{′}}{{[1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)]}^{′}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis time, the resulting limit still gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$ To find the limit, we apply L'Hopital's rule once again. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)+{sin}^{2}⁡(2x)}{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)} & =\\frac{4}{3}\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)]}^{′}}{[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)]^{′}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)+2{cos}^{2}⁡(2x)-2{sin}^{2}⁡(2x)}{3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}\\cdot \\frac{4+2}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186311775 :question/worked-solution "When we divide the two functions, we get a constant, and the limit of a constant is just the constant itself:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{9{x}^{6}}{(-4{x}^{6})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\left(-\\frac{9}{4}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{9}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "When we divide the two functions, we get a constant, and the limit of a constant is just the constant itself:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & =\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{9{x}^{6}}{(-4{x}^{6})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\left(-\\frac{9}{4}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{9}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186311776 :question/worked-solution "In this case, we have an indeterminate form of type $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty },$ so applying L'Hopital's rule yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+9x-4}{3x+1} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{[2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+9x-4]^{′}}{[3x+1]^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{2}{x}+9)}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{0+9}{3} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "In this case, we have an indeterminate form of type $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty },$ so applying L'Hopital's rule yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+9x-4}{3x+1} & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{[2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+9x-4]^{′}}{[3x+1]^{′}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{2}{x}+9)}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{0+9}{3} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186311777 :question/worked-solution "In this case, we have an indeterminate form of type $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty },$ so applying L'Hopital's rule yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{5{x}^{2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{2x} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{[5{x}^{2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x]^{′}}{[2x]^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(10x+\\frac{1}{x})}{2} \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "In this case, we have an indeterminate form of type $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty },$ so applying L'Hopital's rule yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{5{x}^{2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{2x} & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{[5{x}^{2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x]^{′}}{[2x]^{′}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(10x+\\frac{1}{x})}{2} \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186311778 :question/worked-solution "In this case, we have an indeterminate form of type $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty },$ so applying L'Hopital's rule yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{3x+\\sqrt{x}}{\\sqrt{x}} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{[3x+\\sqrt{x}]^{′}}{[\\sqrt{x}]^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(3+\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}})}{\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(6\\sqrt{x}+1) \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "In this case, we have an indeterminate form of type $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty },$ so applying L'Hopital's rule yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{3x+\\sqrt{x}}{\\sqrt{x}} & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{[3x+\\sqrt{x}]^{′}}{[\\sqrt{x}]^{′}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(3+\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}})}{\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }(6\\sqrt{x}+1) \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186311779 :question/worked-solution "In this case, we have an indeterminate form of type $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty },$ so applying L'Hopital's rule yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{x}^{3/2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{x}^{2}} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{[{x}^{3/2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x]^{′}}{[{x}^{2}]^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{1/2}+\\frac{1}{x}}{2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis time, the resulting limit still gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty }.$ To find the limit, we apply L'Hopital's rule once again. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{x}^{3/2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{x}^{2}} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{[\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{1/2}+\\frac{1}{x}]}^{′}}{[2x]^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\frac{3}{4}{x}^{-1/2}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}}{2} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "In this case, we have an indeterminate form of type $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty },$ so applying L'Hopital's rule yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{x}^{3/2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{x}^{2}} & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{[{x}^{3/2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x]^{′}}{[{x}^{2}]^{′}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{1/2}+\\frac{1}{x}}{2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis time, the resulting limit still gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty }.$ To find the limit, we apply L'Hopital's rule once again. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{x}^{3/2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{x}^{2}} & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{[\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{1/2}+\\frac{1}{x}]}^{′}}{[2x]^{′}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\frac{3}{4}{x}^{-1/2}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}}{2} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186311780 :question/worked-solution "In this case, we have an indeterminate form of type $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty },$ so applying L'Hopital's rule yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{x}^{2}+\\sqrt{x}}{{x}^{2}-5\\sqrt{x}} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{[{x}^{2}+\\sqrt{x}]^{′}}{[{x}^{2}-5\\sqrt{x}]^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{2x+\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}}}{2x-\\frac{5}{2\\sqrt{x}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis time, the resulting limit still gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty }.$ To find the limit, we apply L'Hopital's rule once again. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{x}^{2}+\\sqrt{x}}{{x}^{2}-5\\sqrt{x}} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{[2x+\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}}]}^{′}}{{[2x-\\frac{5}{2\\sqrt{x}}]}^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{[2x+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{-1/2}]}^{′}}{{[2x-\\frac{5}{2}{x}^{-1/2}]}^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{2-\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{-3/2}}{2+\\frac{5}{4}{x}^{-3/2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{2-\\frac{1}{4{x}^{3/2}}}{2+\\frac{5}{4{x}^{3/2}}} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "In this case, we have an indeterminate form of type $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty },$ so applying L'Hopital's rule yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{x}^{2}+\\sqrt{x}}{{x}^{2}-5\\sqrt{x}} & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{[{x}^{2}+\\sqrt{x}]^{′}}{[{x}^{2}-5\\sqrt{x}]^{′}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{2x+\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}}}{2x-\\frac{5}{2\\sqrt{x}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis time, the resulting limit still gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty }.$ To find the limit, we apply L'Hopital's rule once again. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{x}^{2}+\\sqrt{x}}{{x}^{2}-5\\sqrt{x}} & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{[2x+\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}}]}^{′}}{{[2x-\\frac{5}{2\\sqrt{x}}]}^{′}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{[2x+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{-1/2}]}^{′}}{{[2x-\\frac{5}{2}{x}^{-1/2}]}^{′}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{2-\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{-3/2}}{2+\\frac{5}{4}{x}^{-3/2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{2-\\frac{1}{4{x}^{3/2}}}{2+\\frac{5}{4{x}^{3/2}}} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186311781 :question/worked-solution "In this case, we have an indeterminate form of type $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty },$ so applying L'Hopital's rule yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{ln}^{2}⁡x} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{[{x}^{3}]^{′}}{[{ln}^{2}⁡x]^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{\\left(\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x}\\right)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{3{x}^{3}}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis time, the resulting limit still gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty }.$ To find the limit, we apply L'Hopital's rule once again. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{ln}^{2}⁡x} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{[3{x}^{3}]^{′}}{{[2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x]}^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{9{x}^{2}}{\\left(\\frac{2}{x}\\right)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{9{x}^{3}}{2} \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "In this case, we have an indeterminate form of type $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty },$ so applying L'Hopital's rule yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{ln}^{2}⁡x} & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{[{x}^{3}]^{′}}{[{ln}^{2}⁡x]^{′}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{\\left(\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{3{x}^{3}}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis time, the resulting limit still gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty }.$ To find the limit, we apply L'Hopital's rule once again. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{ln}^{2}⁡x} & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{[3{x}^{3}]^{′}}{{[2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x]}^{′}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{9{x}^{2}}{\\left(\\frac{2}{x}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{9{x}^{3}}{2} \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186311782 :question/worked-solution "We identify the leading term and compute its limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(2{x}^{6}-7{x}^{3}+3x-5) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(2{x}^{6}) \\\\  & =2\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }({x}^{6}) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\infty  \\\\  & =\\infty \\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We identify the leading term and compute its limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }(2{x}^{6}-7{x}^{3}+3x-5) & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }(2{x}^{6}) \\\\  & =2\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }({x}^{6}) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\infty  \\\\  & =\\infty \\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186311783 :question/worked-solution "We identify the leading term and compute its limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(-4{x}^{5}+2x-9) & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(-4{x}^{5}) \\\\  & =-4{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }({x}^{5}) \\\\  & =\\infty \\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We identify the leading term and compute its limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }(-4{x}^{5}+2x-9) & =\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }(-4{x}^{5}) \\\\  & =-4\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }({x}^{5}) \\\\  & =\\infty \\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186311784 :question/worked-solution "We identify the leading term and compute its limit. In this case, we raise the main term of the base polynomial to the given power.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to \\infty }(5{t}^{3}+2{)}^{4} & ={lim}_{t\\to \\infty }(5{t}^{3}{)}^{4} \\\\  & ={lim}_{t\\to \\infty }625{t}^{12} \\\\  & =\\infty \\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We identify the leading term and compute its limit. In this case, we raise the main term of the base polynomial to the given power.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to \\infty }(5{t}^{3}+2{)}^{4} & =\\lim_{t\\to \\infty }(5{t}^{3}{)}^{4} \\\\  & =\\lim_{t\\to \\infty }625{t}^{12} \\\\  & =\\infty \\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/