\\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{e}^{7}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186311910 :question/worked-solution "Recall the following special limit: \n\n$$\ne={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}\n$$\n\nNow, we rewrite the given limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{-3n(x+y)} & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{[{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}]}^{-3(x+y)} \\\\  & ={[{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}]}^{-3(x+y)} \\\\  & ={e}^{-3(x+y)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{e}^{3(x+y)}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Recall the following special limit: \n\n$$\ne=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}\n$$\n\nNow, we rewrite the given limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{-3n(x+y)} & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{[{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}]}^{-3(x+y)} \\\\  & ={[\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}]}^{-3(x+y)} \\\\  & ={e}^{-3(x+y)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{e}^{3(x+y)}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186311911 :question/worked-solution "Recall the following special limit: \n\n$$\ne={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}\n$$\n\nNow, we rewrite the given limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{-nxy} & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{[{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}]}^{-xy} \\\\  & ={[{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}]}^{-xy} \\\\  & ={e}^{-xy} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{e}^{xy}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Recall the following special limit: \n\n$$\ne=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}\n$$\n\nNow, we rewrite the given limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{-nxy} & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{[{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}]}^{-xy} \\\\  & ={[\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}]}^{-xy} \\\\  & ={e}^{-xy} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{e}^{xy}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186311912 :question/worked-solution "Recall the following special limit:\n\n$$\n{e}^{u}={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{u}{n})}^{n}\n$$\n\nHence, with $u=x\\sqrt{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n{e}^{x\\sqrt{2}}={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{x\\sqrt{2}}{n})}^{n}.\n$$\n\nNow, we rewrite the given limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{x\\sqrt{2}}{n})}^{n\\sqrt{2}} & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{[{(1+\\frac{x\\sqrt{2}}{n})}^{n}]}^{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & ={[{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{x\\sqrt{2}}{n})}^{n}]}^{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & ={({e}^{x\\sqrt{2}})}^{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & ={e}^{2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Recall the following special limit:\n\n$$\n{e}^{u}=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{u}{n})}^{n}\n$$\n\nHence, with $u=x\\sqrt{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n{e}^{x\\sqrt{2}}=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{x\\sqrt{2}}{n})}^{n}.\n$$\n\nNow, we rewrite the given limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{x\\sqrt{2}}{n})}^{n\\sqrt{2}} & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{[{(1+\\frac{x\\sqrt{2}}{n})}^{n}]}^{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & ={[\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{x\\sqrt{2}}{n})}^{n}]}^{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & ={({e}^{x\\sqrt{2}})}^{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & ={e}^{2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186311940 :question/worked-solution "Using the power and quotient rules, we can rewrite the given limit as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{(3f(x)+2)^{2}}{(f(x)-2)^{3}} & =\\frac{{({lim}_{x\\to 2}(3f(x)+2))}^{2}}{{({lim}_{x\\to 2}(f(x)-2))}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above is true provided that  ${lim}_{x\\to 2}f(x)$ exists.\n\nWe find from the graph that ${lim}_{x\\to 2}f(x)=1.$ We calculate the limits of the numerator and denominator, separately.\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 2}(3f(x)+2)=3(1)+2=5\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 2}(f(x)-2)=1-2=-1\n$$\n\nSo we have\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{(3f(x)+2)^{2}}{(f(x)-2)^{3}} & =\\frac{25}{-1}=-25.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Using the power and quotient rules, we can rewrite the given limit as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 2}\\frac{(3f(x)+2)^{2}}{(f(x)-2)^{3}} & =\\frac{{(\\lim_{x\\to 2}(3f(x)+2))}^{2}}{{(\\lim_{x\\to 2}(f(x)-2))}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above is true provided that  $\\lim_{x\\to 2}f(x)$ exists.\n\nWe find from the graph that $\\lim_{x\\to 2}f(x)=1.$ We calculate the limits of the numerator and denominator, separately.\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 2}(3f(x)+2)=3(1)+2=5\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 2}(f(x)-2)=1-2=-1\n$$\n\nSo we have\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 2}\\frac{(3f(x)+2)^{2}}{(f(x)-2)^{3}} & =\\frac{25}{-1}=-25.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186311941 :question/worked-solution "Applying the root and quotient rules, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 2}\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{x}^{2}-4x+5}} & =\\sqrt[3]{{lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{x}^{2}-4x+5}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{{lim}_{x\\to 2}({x}^{3})}{{lim}_{x\\to 2}({x}^{2}-4x+5)}}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the limits of the numerator and denominator separately. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 2}({x}^{3}) & ={2}^{3} \\\\  & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 2}({x}^{2}-4x+5) & ={2}^{2}-4(2)+5 \\\\  & =4-8+5 \\\\  & =1\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{{lim}_{x\\to 2}({x}^{3})}{{lim}_{x\\to 2}({x}^{2}-4x+5)}} & =\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{8}{1}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{8} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Applying the root and quotient rules, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 2}\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{x}^{2}-4x+5}} & =\\sqrt[3]{\\lim_{x\\to 2}\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{x}^{2}-4x+5}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{\\lim_{x\\to 2}({x}^{3})}{\\lim_{x\\to 2}({x}^{2}-4x+5)}}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the limits of the numerator and denominator separately. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 2}({x}^{3}) & ={2}^{3} \\\\  & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 2}({x}^{2}-4x+5) & ={2}^{2}-4(2)+5 \\\\  & =4-8+5 \\\\  & =1\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{\\lim_{x\\to 2}({x}^{3})}{\\lim_{x\\to 2}({x}^{2}-4x+5)}} & =\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{8}{1}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{8} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186311942 :question/worked-solution "Applying the quotient rule, we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{3x+5}{\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}+2x+5}+2x}=\\frac{{lim}_{x\\to 1}(3x+5)}{{lim}_{x\\to 1}(\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}+2x+5}+2x)}.\n$$\n\nWe calculate the limits of the numerator and denominator separately.\n\nFor the numerator, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 1}(3x+5)=3(1)+5=8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the root rule (continuity) in the denominator, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 1}(\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}+2x+5}+2x) & =\\sqrt[3]{{1}^{2}+2(1)+5}+2(1) \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{1+2+5}+2 \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{8}+2 \\\\  & =2+2 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{3x+5}{\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}+2x+5}+2x} & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Applying the quotient rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 1}\\frac{3x+5}{\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}+2x+5}+2x}=\\frac{\\lim_{x\\to 1}(3x+5)}{\\lim_{x\\to 1}(\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}+2x+5}+2x)}.\n$$\n\nWe calculate the limits of the numerator and denominator separately.\n\nFor the numerator, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 1}(3x+5)=3(1)+5=8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the root rule (continuity) in the denominator, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 1}(\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}+2x+5}+2x) & =\\sqrt[3]{{1}^{2}+2(1)+5}+2(1) \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{1+2+5}+2 \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{8}+2 \\\\  & =2+2 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 1}\\frac{3x+5}{\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}+2x+5}+2x} & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186311944 :question/worked-solution "From the graph, we get that ${lim}_{x\\to \\,1}f(x)=2$ and ${lim}_{x\\to \\,1}g(x)=4.$\n\nApplying the root and the quotient rules, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,1}\\sqrt{\\frac{3f(x)-g(x)}{f(x)}} & =\\sqrt{\\,{lim}_{x\\to \\,1}\\frac{3f(x)-g(x)}{f(x)}\\,} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\,\\frac{{lim}_{x\\to \\,1}(3f(x)-g(x))}{{lim}_{x\\to \\,1}f(x)}\\,}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the limits of the numerator and denominator separately. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,1}(3f(x)-g(x)) & =3(2)-4 \\\\  & =6-4 \\\\  & =2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,1}f(x)=2.\n$$\n\nSo we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,1}\\sqrt{\\frac{3f(x)-g(x)}{f(x)}} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{2}{2}} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "From the graph, we get that $\\lim_{x\\to \\,1}f(x)=2$ and $\\lim_{x\\to \\,1}g(x)=4.$\n\nApplying the root and the quotient rules, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\,1}\\sqrt{\\frac{3f(x)-g(x)}{f(x)}} & =\\sqrt{\\,\\lim_{x\\to \\,1}\\frac{3f(x)-g(x)}{f(x)}\\,} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\,\\frac{\\lim_{x\\to \\,1}(3f(x)-g(x))}{\\lim_{x\\to \\,1}f(x)}\\,}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the limits of the numerator and denominator separately. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\,1}(3f(x)-g(x)) & =3(2)-4 \\\\  & =6-4 \\\\  & =2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,1}f(x)=2.\n$$\n\nSo we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\,1}\\sqrt{\\frac{3f(x)-g(x)}{f(x)}} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{2}{2}} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186311948 :question/worked-solution "If we attempt to evaluate the limit, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 5}\\frac{5-\\sqrt{5x}}{5-x} & =\\frac{5-\\sqrt{5(5)}}{5-5}=\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to first simplify the limit. Since the radical is in the numerator, we can multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the numerator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 5}\\frac{5-\\sqrt{5x}}{5-x} & ={lim}_{x\\to 5}\\frac{(5-\\sqrt{5x})(5+\\sqrt{5x})}{(5-x)(5+\\sqrt{5x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 5}\\frac{{5}^{2}-{(\\sqrt{5x})}^{2}}{(5-x)(5+\\sqrt{5x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 5}\\frac{25-5x}{(5-x)(5+\\sqrt{5x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 5}\\frac{5(5-x)}{(5-x)(5+\\sqrt{5x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 5}\\frac{5\\cancel{(5-x)}}{\\cancel{(5-x)}(5+\\sqrt{5x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 5}\\frac{5}{5+\\sqrt{5x}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{5+\\sqrt{5(5)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{5+\\sqrt{25}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "If we attempt to evaluate the limit, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 5}\\frac{5-\\sqrt{5x}}{5-x} & =\\frac{5-\\sqrt{5(5)}}{5-5}=\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to first simplify the limit. Since the radical is in the numerator, we can multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the numerator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 5}\\frac{5-\\sqrt{5x}}{5-x} & =\\lim_{x\\to 5}\\frac{(5-\\sqrt{5x})(5+\\sqrt{5x})}{(5-x)(5+\\sqrt{5x})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 5}\\frac{{5}^{2}-{(\\sqrt{5x})}^{2}}{(5-x)(5+\\sqrt{5x})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 5}\\frac{25-5x}{(5-x)(5+\\sqrt{5x})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 5}\\frac{5(5-x)}{(5-x)(5+\\sqrt{5x})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 5}\\frac{5\\cancel{(5-x)}}{\\cancel{(5-x)}(5+\\sqrt{5x})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 5}\\frac{5}{5+\\sqrt{5x}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{5+\\sqrt{5(5)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{5+\\sqrt{25}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186311949 :question/worked-solution "If we attempt to evaluate the limit, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to }\\frac{8-\\sqrt{8x}}{x-8} & =\\frac{8-\\sqrt{8(8)}}{8-8}=\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to first simplify the limit. Since the radical is in the numerator, we can multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the numerator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 8}\\frac{8-\\sqrt{8x}}{x-8} & ={lim}_{x\\to 8}\\frac{(8-\\sqrt{8x})(8+\\sqrt{8x})}{(x-8)(8+\\sqrt{8x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 8}\\frac{{8}^{2}-(\\sqrt{8x})^{2}}{(x-8)(8+\\sqrt{8x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 8}\\frac{64-8x}{(x-8)(8+\\sqrt{8x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 8}\\frac{-8(x-8)}{(x-8)(8+\\sqrt{8x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 8}\\frac{-8\\cancel{(x-8)}}{\\cancel{(x-8)}(8+\\sqrt{8x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 8}\\frac{-8}{8+\\sqrt{8x}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{8+\\sqrt{8(8)}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{8+8} \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{16} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "If we attempt to evaluate the limit, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to }\\frac{8-\\sqrt{8x}}{x-8} & =\\frac{8-\\sqrt{8(8)}}{8-8}=\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to first simplify the limit. Since the radical is in the numerator, we can multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the numerator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 8}\\frac{8-\\sqrt{8x}}{x-8} & =\\lim_{x\\to 8}\\frac{(8-\\sqrt{8x})(8+\\sqrt{8x})}{(x-8)(8+\\sqrt{8x})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 8}\\frac{{8}^{2}-(\\sqrt{8x})^{2}}{(x-8)(8+\\sqrt{8x})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 8}\\frac{64-8x}{(x-8)(8+\\sqrt{8x})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 8}\\frac{-8(x-8)}{(x-8)(8+\\sqrt{8x})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 8}\\frac{-8\\cancel{(x-8)}}{\\cancel{(x-8)}(8+\\sqrt{8x})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 8}\\frac{-8}{8+\\sqrt{8x}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{8+\\sqrt{8(8)}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{8+8} \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{16} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186311950 :question/worked-solution "If we attempt to evaluate the limit, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{\\sqrt{x+1}-2}{{x}^{2}-x-6} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3+1}-2}{{3}^{2}-3-6}=\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to first simplify the limit. Since the radical is in the numerator, we can multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the numerator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{\\sqrt{x+1}-2}{{x}^{2}-x-6} & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{(\\sqrt{x+1}-2)(\\sqrt{x+1}+2)}{({x}^{2}-x-6)(\\sqrt{x+1}+2)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{(\\sqrt{x+1})^{2}-{2}^{2}}{(x+2)(x-3)(\\sqrt{x+1}+2)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{(x+1)-4}{(x+2)(x-3)(\\sqrt{x+1}+2)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{x-3}{(x+2)(x-3)(\\sqrt{x+1}+2)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{1}{(x+2)(\\sqrt{x+1}+2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(3+2)(\\sqrt{3+1}+2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5\\cdot 4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{20}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "If we attempt to evaluate the limit, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 3}\\frac{\\sqrt{x+1}-2}{{x}^{2}-x-6} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3+1}-2}{{3}^{2}-3-6}=\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to first simplify the limit. Since the radical is in the numerator, we can multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the numerator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 3}\\frac{\\sqrt{x+1}-2}{{x}^{2}-x-6} & =\\lim_{x\\to 3}\\frac{(\\sqrt{x+1}-2)(\\sqrt{x+1}+2)}{({x}^{2}-x-6)(\\sqrt{x+1}+2)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 3}\\frac{(\\sqrt{x+1})^{2}-{2}^{2}}{(x+2)(x-3)(\\sqrt{x+1}+2)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 3}\\frac{(x+1)-4}{(x+2)(x-3)(\\sqrt{x+1}+2)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 3}\\frac{x-3}{(x+2)(x-3)(\\sqrt{x+1}+2)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 3}\\frac{1}{(x+2)(\\sqrt{x+1}+2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(3+2)(\\sqrt{3+1}+2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5\\cdot 4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{20}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186311952 :question/worked-solution "If we attempt to evaluate the limit by simply evaluating the function at $x=\\frac{1}{4}$ we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (1/4)}\\frac{4x-1}{2x-\\sqrt{x}} & =\\frac{(4\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}-1)}{(2\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}-\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{4}})}=\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to first simplify the limit. Since the radical is in the denominator, we can multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the denominator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (1/4)}\\frac{4x-1}{2x-\\sqrt{x}} & ={lim}_{x\\to (1/4)}\\frac{(4x-1)(2x+\\sqrt{x})}{(2x-\\sqrt{x})(2x+\\sqrt{x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (1/4)}\\frac{(4x-1)(2x+\\sqrt{x})}{(2x)^{2}-(\\sqrt{x})^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (1/4)}\\frac{(4x-1)(2x+\\sqrt{x})}{4{x}^{2}-x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (1/4)}\\frac{(4x-1)(2x+\\sqrt{x})}{x(4x-1)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (1/4)}\\frac{\\cancel{(4x-1)}(2x+\\sqrt{x})}{x\\cancel{(4x-1)}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (1/4)}\\frac{2x+\\sqrt{x}}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{(2\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}+\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{4}})}{\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "If we attempt to evaluate the limit by simply evaluating the function at $x=\\frac{1}{4}$ we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (1/4)}\\frac{4x-1}{2x-\\sqrt{x}} & =\\frac{(4\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}-1)}{(2\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}-\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{4}})}=\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to first simplify the limit. Since the radical is in the denominator, we can multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the denominator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (1/4)}\\frac{4x-1}{2x-\\sqrt{x}} & =\\lim_{x\\to (1/4)}\\frac{(4x-1)(2x+\\sqrt{x})}{(2x-\\sqrt{x})(2x+\\sqrt{x})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (1/4)}\\frac{(4x-1)(2x+\\sqrt{x})}{(2x)^{2}-(\\sqrt{x})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (1/4)}\\frac{(4x-1)(2x+\\sqrt{x})}{4{x}^{2}-x} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (1/4)}\\frac{(4x-1)(2x+\\sqrt{x})}{x(4x-1)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (1/4)}\\frac{\\cancel{(4x-1)}(2x+\\sqrt{x})}{x\\cancel{(4x-1)}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (1/4)}\\frac{2x+\\sqrt{x}}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{(2\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}+\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{4}})}{\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186311953 :question/worked-solution "If we attempt to evaluate the limit by simply evaluating the function at $x=64,$ we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 64}\\frac{x-64}{\\sqrt{4x}-16} & =\\frac{64-64}{\\sqrt{4(64)}-16}=\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to first simplify the limit. Since the radical is in the denominator, we can multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the denominator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 64}\\frac{x-64}{\\sqrt{4x}-16} & ={lim}_{x\\to 64}\\frac{(x-64)(\\sqrt{4x}+16)}{(\\sqrt{4x}-16)(\\sqrt{4x}+16)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 64}\\frac{(x-64)(\\sqrt{4x}+16)}{(\\sqrt{4x})^{2}-{16}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 64}\\frac{(x-64)(\\sqrt{4x}+16)}{4x-256} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 64}\\frac{(x-64)(\\sqrt{4x}+16)}{4(x-64)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 64}\\frac{\\cancel{(x-64)}(\\sqrt{4x}+16)}{4\\cancel{(x-64)}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 64}\\frac{\\sqrt{4x}+16}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{4(64)}+16}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{16+16}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{32}{4} \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "If we attempt to evaluate the limit by simply evaluating the function at $x=64,$ we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 64}\\frac{x-64}{\\sqrt{4x}-16} & =\\frac{64-64}{\\sqrt{4(64)}-16}=\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to first simplify the limit. Since the radical is in the denominator, we can multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the denominator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 64}\\frac{x-64}{\\sqrt{4x}-16} & =\\lim_{x\\to 64}\\frac{(x-64)(\\sqrt{4x}+16)}{(\\sqrt{4x}-16)(\\sqrt{4x}+16)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 64}\\frac{(x-64)(\\sqrt{4x}+16)}{(\\sqrt{4x})^{2}-{16}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 64}\\frac{(x-64)(\\sqrt{4x}+16)}{4x-256} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 64}\\frac{(x-64)(\\sqrt{4x}+16)}{4(x-64)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 64}\\frac{\\cancel{(x-64)}(\\sqrt{4x}+16)}{4\\cancel{(x-64)}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 64}\\frac{\\sqrt{4x}+16}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{4(64)}+16}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{16+16}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{32}{4} \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186311954 :question/worked-solution "If we attempt to evaluate the limit by simply evaluating the function at $x=3$, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{x-3}{3x-\\sqrt{27x}} & =\\frac{3-3}{3(3)-\\sqrt{27(3)}}=\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to first simplify the limit. Since the radical is in the denominator, we can multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the denominator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{x-3}{3x-\\sqrt{27x}} & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{(x-3)(3x+\\sqrt{27x})}{(3x-\\sqrt{27x})(3x+\\sqrt{27x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{(x-3)(3x+\\sqrt{27x})}{(3x)^{2}-(\\sqrt{27x})^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{(x-3)(3x+\\sqrt{27x})}{9{x}^{2}-27x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{(x-3)(3x+\\sqrt{27x})}{9x(x-3)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{\\cancel{(x-3)}(3x+\\sqrt{27x})}{9x\\cancel{(x-3)}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{3x+\\sqrt{27x}}{9x} \\\\  & =\\frac{3(3)+\\sqrt{27(3)}}{9(3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{9+9}{27} \\\\  & =\\frac{18}{27} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "If we attempt to evaluate the limit by simply evaluating the function at $x=3$, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 3}\\frac{x-3}{3x-\\sqrt{27x}} & =\\frac{3-3}{3(3)-\\sqrt{27(3)}}=\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to first simplify the limit. Since the radical is in the denominator, we can multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the denominator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 3}\\frac{x-3}{3x-\\sqrt{27x}} & =\\lim_{x\\to 3}\\frac{(x-3)(3x+\\sqrt{27x})}{(3x-\\sqrt{27x})(3x+\\sqrt{27x})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 3}\\frac{(x-3)(3x+\\sqrt{27x})}{(3x)^{2}-(\\sqrt{27x})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 3}\\frac{(x-3)(3x+\\sqrt{27x})}{9{x}^{2}-27x} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 3}\\frac{(x-3)(3x+\\sqrt{27x})}{9x(x-3)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 3}\\frac{\\cancel{(x-3)}(3x+\\sqrt{27x})}{9x\\cancel{(x-3)}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 3}\\frac{3x+\\sqrt{27x}}{9x} \\\\  & =\\frac{3(3)+\\sqrt{27(3)}}{9(3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{9+9}{27} \\\\  & =\\frac{18}{27} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186311958 :question/worked-solution "If we attempt to evaluate the limit by simply evaluating the function at $z=6,$ we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{z\\to 6}\\frac{z-6}{\\sqrt{3z-2}-4} & =\\frac{6-6}{\\sqrt{3(6)-2}-4}=\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to first simplify the limit. Since the radical is in the denominator, we can multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the denominator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{z\\to 6}\\frac{z-6}{\\sqrt{3z-2}-4} & ={lim}_{z\\to 6}\\frac{(z-6)(\\sqrt{3z-2}+4)}{(\\sqrt{3z-2}-4)(\\sqrt{3z-2}+4)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{z\\to 6}\\frac{(z-6)(\\sqrt{3z-2}+4)}{(\\sqrt{3z-2})^{2}-{4}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{z\\to 6}\\frac{(z-6)(\\sqrt{3z-2}+4)}{(3z-2)-16} \\\\  & ={lim}_{z\\to 6}\\frac{(z-6)(\\sqrt{3z-2}+4)}{3z-18} \\\\  & ={lim}_{z\\to 6}\\frac{(z-6)(\\sqrt{3z-2}+4)}{3(z-6)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{z\\to 6}\\frac{\\sqrt{3z-2}+4}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3(6)-2}+4}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{4+4}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "If we attempt to evaluate the limit by simply evaluating the function at $z=6,$ we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{z\\to 6}\\frac{z-6}{\\sqrt{3z-2}-4} & =\\frac{6-6}{\\sqrt{3(6)-2}-4}=\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to first simplify the limit. Since the radical is in the denominator, we can multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the denominator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{z\\to 6}\\frac{z-6}{\\sqrt{3z-2}-4} & =\\lim_{z\\to 6}\\frac{(z-6)(\\sqrt{3z-2}+4)}{(\\sqrt{3z-2}-4)(\\sqrt{3z-2}+4)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{z\\to 6}\\frac{(z-6)(\\sqrt{3z-2}+4)}{(\\sqrt{3z-2})^{2}-{4}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{z\\to 6}\\frac{(z-6)(\\sqrt{3z-2}+4)}{(3z-2)-16} \\\\  & =\\lim_{z\\to 6}\\frac{(z-6)(\\sqrt{3z-2}+4)}{3z-18} \\\\  & =\\lim_{z\\to 6}\\frac{(z-6)(\\sqrt{3z-2}+4)}{3(z-6)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{z\\to 6}\\frac{\\sqrt{3z-2}+4}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3(6)-2}+4}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{4+4}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186314754 :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(x):$ \n\n![](images/85/85a182aabfe64c27eaafde94e3bdbede9409b02cfd87395cb2fc5543b09c4e05.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(x)$ is $x\\in [-1,1].$   \n\nNow, substituting $x=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$ directly into the limit, we have  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,\\sqrt{3}/2}(2\\sqrt{3}x+6\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(x)) & =2\\sqrt{3}\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)+6\\cdot \\operatorname{arccos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =3+6\\cdot \\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right) \\\\  & =3+\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let's sketch the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(x):$ \n\n![](images/85/85a182aabfe64c27eaafde94e3bdbede9409b02cfd87395cb2fc5543b09c4e05.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(x)$ is $x\\in [-1,1].$   \n\nNow, substituting $x=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$ directly into the limit, we have  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\,\\sqrt{3}/2}(2\\sqrt{3}x+6\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(x)) & =2\\sqrt{3}\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)+6\\cdot \\operatorname{arccos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =3+6\\cdot \\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right) \\\\  & =3+\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186314782 :question/worked-solution "First, we divide both the numerator and the denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{6}.$ Then, by noting that the reciprocals shrink to $0$ in the limit, we can then evaluate the resulting expression.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{2}{4{x}^{6}-5x} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\left(\\frac{2}{{x}^{6}}\\right)}{(\\frac{4{x}^{6}}{{x}^{6}}-\\frac{5x}{{x}^{6}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\left(\\frac{2}{{x}^{6}}\\right)}{(4-\\frac{5}{{x}^{5}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{4-0} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we divide both the numerator and the denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{6}.$ Then, by noting that the reciprocals shrink to $0$ in the limit, we can then evaluate the resulting expression.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{2}{4{x}^{6}-5x} & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\left(\\frac{2}{{x}^{6}}\\right)}{(\\frac{4{x}^{6}}{{x}^{6}}-\\frac{5x}{{x}^{6}})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\left(\\frac{2}{{x}^{6}}\\right)}{(4-\\frac{5}{{x}^{5}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{4-0} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186314783 :question/worked-solution "First, we divide both the numerator and the denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{2}.$ Then, by noting that the reciprocals shrink to $0$ in the limit, we can then evaluate the resulting expression.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{3-x-8{x}^{2}}{k\\,{x}^{2}+7x-4} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{8{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}})}{(\\frac{k{x}^{2}}{\\,{x}^{2}}+\\frac{7x}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{x}-8)}{(k+\\frac{7}{x}-\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{0-0-8}{k+0-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{-8}{k} \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{8}{k} & =\\frac{2}{5} \\\\ 2k & =-40 \\\\ k & =-20.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we divide both the numerator and the denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{2}.$ Then, by noting that the reciprocals shrink to $0$ in the limit, we can then evaluate the resulting expression.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{3-x-8{x}^{2}}{k\\,{x}^{2}+7x-4} & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{8{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}})}{(\\frac{k{x}^{2}}{\\,{x}^{2}}+\\frac{7x}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{x}-8)}{(k+\\frac{7}{x}-\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{0-0-8}{k+0-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{-8}{k} \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{8}{k} & =\\frac{2}{5} \\\\ 2k & =-40 \\\\ k & =-20.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186314786 :question/worked-solution "![](images/24/24f07da42a537ded7916fc2440dcf3ee1ccdfc81c7b027b47e97814fec059f5a.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(x)$ is $x\\in [-1,1].$\n\nNow, substituting $x=\\frac{1}{2}$ directly into the limit, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,\\frac{1}{2}}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(x)) & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "![](images/24/24f07da42a537ded7916fc2440dcf3ee1ccdfc81c7b027b47e97814fec059f5a.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(x)$ is $x\\in [-1,1].$\n\nNow, substituting $x=\\frac{1}{2}$ directly into the limit, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\,\\frac{1}{2}}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(x)) & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186314787 :question/worked-solution "![](images/68/68f1ebd0457e0c127f8ed6c979feba58196aa6bb43d97d211efa9b39bc33f637.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)$ is $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nNow, substituting $x=\\sqrt{3}$ directly into the limit, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,\\sqrt{3}}sin\\,(\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)) & =sin\\,(\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{3})) \\\\  & =sin\\,(\\frac{3}{4}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =sin\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "![](images/68/68f1ebd0457e0c127f8ed6c979feba58196aa6bb43d97d211efa9b39bc33f637.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)$ is $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nNow, substituting $x=\\sqrt{3}$ directly into the limit, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\,\\sqrt{3}}sin\\,(\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)) & =sin\\,(\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{3})) \\\\  & =sin\\,(\\frac{3}{4}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =sin\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186314788 :question/worked-solution "![](images/f5/f5cef3bda76e80edfa467a90823c9b6ad6aca1f8a5e8e3e06dc585258e975e0a.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x+1)$ is $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$  \n\nAs $x$ increases to $\\infty ,$ the graph tends to $y=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,\\infty }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x+1)) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "![](images/f5/f5cef3bda76e80edfa467a90823c9b6ad6aca1f8a5e8e3e06dc585258e975e0a.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x+1)$ is $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$  \n\nAs $x$ increases to $\\infty ,$ the graph tends to $y=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\,\\infty }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x+1)) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186314789 :question/worked-solution "For large negative values of $x,$ we have $|-12x+5|=-12x+5.$ Consequently, for $x\\to -\\infty ,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{|-12x+5|}{4x+3}=\\frac{-12x+5}{4x+3}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we divide both the numerator and the denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{1}.$ Then, by noting that the reciprocals shrink to $0$ in the limit, we can then evaluate the resulting expression.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{-12x+5}{4x+3} & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{-12x}{x}+\\frac{5}{x})}{(\\frac{4x}{x}+\\frac{3}{x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{(-12+\\frac{5}{x})}{(4+\\frac{3}{x})} \\\\  & =\\frac{-12+0}{4+0} \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "For large negative values of $x,$ we have $|-12x+5|=-12x+5.$ Consequently, for $x\\to -\\infty ,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{|-12x+5|}{4x+3}=\\frac{-12x+5}{4x+3}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we divide both the numerator and the denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{1}.$ Then, by noting that the reciprocals shrink to $0$ in the limit, we can then evaluate the resulting expression.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{-12x+5}{4x+3} & =\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{-12x}{x}+\\frac{5}{x})}{(\\frac{4x}{x}+\\frac{3}{x})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{(-12+\\frac{5}{x})}{(4+\\frac{3}{x})} \\\\  & =\\frac{-12+0}{4+0} \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186314797 :question/worked-solution "Since $g(x)$ is right-continuous at $x=10,$ we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {10}^{+}}g(x)=g(10)=5.\n$$\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {10}^{+}}\\frac{g(x)-\\operatorname{log}⁡x}{3g(10)} & =\\frac{{lim}_{x\\to {10}^{+}}g(x)-{lim}_{x\\to {10}^{+}}\\operatorname{log}⁡x}{3g(10)} \\\\  & =\\frac{5-\\operatorname{log}⁡10}{3(5)} \\\\  & =\\frac{5-1}{3(5)} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{15}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Since $g(x)$ is right-continuous at $x=10,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {10}^{+}}g(x)=g(10)=5.\n$$\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {10}^{+}}\\frac{g(x)-\\operatorname{log}⁡x}{3g(10)} & =\\frac{\\lim_{x\\to {10}^{+}}g(x)-\\lim_{x\\to {10}^{+}}\\operatorname{log}⁡x}{3g(10)} \\\\  & =\\frac{5-\\operatorname{log}⁡10}{3(5)} \\\\  & =\\frac{5-1}{3(5)} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{15}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186314799 :question/worked-solution "Since $g(x)$ is left-continuous at $x=5,$ we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {5}^{-}}g(x)=g(5)=5.\n$$\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {5}^{-}}\\frac{g(x)-{x}^{2}}{4x} & =\\frac{{lim}_{x\\to {5}^{-}}g(x)-\\text{ }{lim}_{x\\to {5}^{-}}{x}^{2}}{{lim}_{x\\to {5}^{-}}4x} \\\\  & =\\frac{5-{5}^{2}}{4(5)} \\\\  & =\\frac{5-25}{20} \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Since $g(x)$ is left-continuous at $x=5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {5}^{-}}g(x)=g(5)=5.\n$$\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {5}^{-}}\\frac{g(x)-{x}^{2}}{4x} & =\\frac{\\lim_{x\\to {5}^{-}}g(x)-\\text{ }\\lim_{x\\to {5}^{-}}{x}^{2}}{\\lim_{x\\to {5}^{-}}4x} \\\\  & =\\frac{5-{5}^{2}}{4(5)} \\\\  & =\\frac{5-25}{20} \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186314800 :question/worked-solution "Since $h(x)$ is left-continuous at $x=10,$ we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {10}^{-}}h(x)=h(10)=15.\n$$\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {10}^{-}}(xh(x)-5h(10)) & ={lim}_{x\\to {10}^{-}}x\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to {10}^{-}}h(x)-5\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to {10}^{-}}h(10) \\\\  & =10\\cdot 15-5\\cdot 15 \\\\  & =150-75 \\\\  & =75.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Since $h(x)$ is left-continuous at $x=10,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {10}^{-}}h(x)=h(10)=15.\n$$\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {10}^{-}}(xh(x)-5h(10)) & =\\lim_{x\\to {10}^{-}}x\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to {10}^{-}}h(x)-5\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to {10}^{-}}h(10) \\\\  & =10\\cdot 15-5\\cdot 15 \\\\  & =150-75 \\\\  & =75.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186314802 :question/worked-solution "First, we divide both the numerator and the denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{2}.$ Then, by noting that the reciprocals shrink to $0$ in the limit, we can then evaluate the resulting expression.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{-{x}^{6}+4x}{{x}^{2}+5} & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{(\\frac{-{x}^{6}}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{4x}{{x}^{2}})}{(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{5}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{(-{x}^{4}+\\frac{4}{x})}{(1+\\frac{5}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{(-{x}^{4}+0)}{(1+0)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,-{x}^{4} \\\\  & =-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we divide both the numerator and the denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{2}.$ Then, by noting that the reciprocals shrink to $0$ in the limit, we can then evaluate the resulting expression.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{-{x}^{6}+4x}{{x}^{2}+5} & =\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{(\\frac{-{x}^{6}}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{4x}{{x}^{2}})}{(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{5}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{(-{x}^{4}+\\frac{4}{x})}{(1+\\frac{5}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{(-{x}^{4}+0)}{(1+0)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,-{x}^{4} \\\\  & =-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186314803 :question/worked-solution "First, we divide both the numerator and the denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{3}.$ Then, by noting that the reciprocals shrink to $0$ in the limit, we can then evaluate the resulting expression.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{-{x}^{9}-6{x}^{2}+1}{{x}^{3}+10x} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{(\\frac{-{x}^{9}}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{6{x}^{2}}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}})}{(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{10x}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{(-{x}^{6}-\\frac{6}{x}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}})}{(1+\\frac{10}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\text{ }\\frac{-{x}^{6}-0+0}{1+0} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\,-{x}^{6} \\\\  & =-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we divide both the numerator and the denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{3}.$ Then, by noting that the reciprocals shrink to $0$ in the limit, we can then evaluate the resulting expression.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{-{x}^{9}-6{x}^{2}+1}{{x}^{3}+10x} & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{(\\frac{-{x}^{9}}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{6{x}^{2}}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}})}{(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{10x}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{(-{x}^{6}-\\frac{6}{x}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}})}{(1+\\frac{10}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\text{ }\\frac{-{x}^{6}-0+0}{1+0} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\,-{x}^{6} \\\\  & =-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186314804 :question/worked-solution "First, we divide both the numerator and the denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{2}.$ Then, by noting that the reciprocals shrink to $0$ in the limit, we can then evaluate the resulting expression.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{{x}^{4}-2}{5{x}^{2}-1} & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{(\\frac{{x}^{4}}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}})}{(\\frac{5{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{({x}^{2}-\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}})}{(5-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}-0}{5-0} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{{x}^{2}}{5} \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we divide both the numerator and the denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{2}.$ Then, by noting that the reciprocals shrink to $0$ in the limit, we can then evaluate the resulting expression.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{{x}^{4}-2}{5{x}^{2}-1} & =\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{(\\frac{{x}^{4}}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}})}{(\\frac{5{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{({x}^{2}-\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}})}{(5-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}-0}{5-0} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{{x}^{2}}{5} \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186314805 :question/worked-solution "The horizontal asymptote is $y={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x).$ To find the horizontal asymptote, we apply the usual procedure of dividing the numerator and denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{4}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{6{x}^{4}+6{x}^{2}-2}{2{x}^{4}+3} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{6{x}^{4}}{{x}^{4}}+\\frac{6{x}^{2}}{{x}^{4}}-\\frac{2}{{x}^{4}})}{(\\frac{2{x}^{4}}{{x}^{4}}+\\frac{3}{{x}^{4}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(6+\\frac{6}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{2}{{x}^{4}})}{(2+\\frac{3}{{x}^{4}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{6+0+0}{2} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the horizontal asymptote of $f(x)$ is $y=3.$" "The horizontal asymptote is $y=\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }f(x).$ To find the horizontal asymptote, we apply the usual procedure of dividing the numerator and denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{4}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{6{x}^{4}+6{x}^{2}-2}{2{x}^{4}+3} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{6{x}^{4}}{{x}^{4}}+\\frac{6{x}^{2}}{{x}^{4}}-\\frac{2}{{x}^{4}})}{(\\frac{2{x}^{4}}{{x}^{4}}+\\frac{3}{{x}^{4}})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(6+\\frac{6}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{2}{{x}^{4}})}{(2+\\frac{3}{{x}^{4}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{6+0+0}{2} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the horizontal asymptote of $f(x)$ is $y=3.$"] [:db/cas 17592186314806 :question/worked-solution "For large positive values of $x,$ we have $|3-8x|=8x-3.$ Consequently, for $x\\to \\infty ,$ we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{|3-8x|}{4x+1}={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{8x-3}{4x+1}.\n$$\n\nThe horizontal asymptote for $x>0$ is $y={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x).$ To find the horizontal asymptote, we apply the usual procedure of dividing the numerator and denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is $x.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{8x-3}{4x+1} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{8x}{x}-\\frac{3}{x})}{(\\frac{4x}{x}+\\frac{1}{x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(8-\\frac{3}{x})}{(4+\\frac{1}{x})} \\\\  & =\\frac{8-0}{4+0} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the horizontal asymptote is $y=2.$" "For large positive values of $x,$ we have $|3-8x|=8x-3.$ Consequently, for $x\\to \\infty ,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{|3-8x|}{4x+1}=\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{8x-3}{4x+1}.\n$$\n\nThe horizontal asymptote for $x>0$ is $y=\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }f(x).$ To find the horizontal asymptote, we apply the usual procedure of dividing the numerator and denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is $x.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{8x-3}{4x+1} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{8x}{x}-\\frac{3}{x})}{(\\frac{4x}{x}+\\frac{1}{x})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(8-\\frac{3}{x})}{(4+\\frac{1}{x})} \\\\  & =\\frac{8-0}{4+0} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the horizontal asymptote is $y=2.$"] [:db/cas 17592186314822 :question/worked-solution "Notice that as $x\\to 0,$ both numerator and denominator approach $0.$\n\nSo, if we attempt to evaluate the limit directly, we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{21\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{4x}=\\frac{0}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is an indeterminate form.\n\nInstead, let's recall the following special limit:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x}=1\n$$\n\nRewriting the given limit using the algebra of limits and applying our special limit, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\left(\\frac{21\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{4x}\\right) & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}(-\\frac{21}{4}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{21}{4}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{21}{4}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-\\frac{21}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Notice that as $x\\to 0,$ both numerator and denominator approach $0.$\n\nSo, if we attempt to evaluate the limit directly, we get\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{21\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{4x}=\\frac{0}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is an indeterminate form.\n\nInstead, let's recall the following special limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x}=1\n$$\n\nRewriting the given limit using the algebra of limits and applying our special limit, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,\\left(\\frac{21\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{4x}\\right) & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}(-\\frac{21}{4}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{21}{4}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{21}{4}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-\\frac{21}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186314839 :question/problem "Find the left- and right-sided limits at $x=-2$ of the piecewise function $f(x)$ defined by\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-6x} & \\text{ for }\\,x<-2 \\\\ {e}^{2+x}+3 & \\text{ for }\\,x\\ge -2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n${lim}_{x\\to -{2}^{-}}f(x)=$\n{{field-1}},$\\,$\n\n${lim}_{x\\to -{2}^{+}}f(x)=$\n{{field-2}}" "Find the left- and right-sided limits at $x=-2$ of the piecewise function $f(x)$ defined by\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-6x} & \\text{ for }\\,x<-2 \\\\ {e}^{2+x}+3 & \\text{ for }\\,x\\ge -2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$\\lim_{x\\to -{2}^{-}}f(x)=$\n{{field-1}},$\\,$\n\n$\\lim_{x\\to -{2}^{+}}f(x)=$\n{{field-2}}"] [:db/cas 17592186314839 :question/worked-solution "Approaching from the left, we have $x<-2,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-6x}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -{2}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to -{2}^{-}}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-6x} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}-6(-2)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+12} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApproaching from the right, we have $x\\ge -2,$ so we use the expression $f(x)={e}^{2+x}+3.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -{2}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to -{2}^{+}}{e}^{2+x}+3 \\\\  & ={e}^{2+(-2)}+3 \\\\  & ={e}^{0}+3 \\\\  & =1+3 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -{2}^{-}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to -{2}^{+}}f(x)=4.\n$$" "Approaching from the left, we have $x<-2,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-6x}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to -{2}^{-}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to -{2}^{-}}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-6x} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}-6(-2)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+12} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApproaching from the right, we have $x\\ge -2,$ so we use the expression $f(x)={e}^{2+x}+3.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to -{2}^{+}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to -{2}^{+}}{e}^{2+x}+3 \\\\  & ={e}^{2+(-2)}+3 \\\\  & ={e}^{0}+3 \\\\  & =1+3 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -{2}^{-}}f(x)=\\lim_{x\\to -{2}^{+}}f(x)=4.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186314855 :question/worked-solution "Notice that as $x\\to 0,$ both numerator and denominator approach $0.$\n\nSo, if we attempt to evaluate the limit directly, we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{3{sin}^{2}⁡x}{2{x}^{2}}=\\frac{0}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is an indeterminate form.\n\nInstead, let's recall the following special limit:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x}=1\n$$\n\nRewriting the given limit using the algebra of limits and applying our special limit, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{3{sin}^{2}⁡x}{2{x}^{2}} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}(\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡x}{{x}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to 0}{\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot {[{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x}]}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot {1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Notice that as $x\\to 0,$ both numerator and denominator approach $0.$\n\nSo, if we attempt to evaluate the limit directly, we get\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{3{sin}^{2}⁡x}{2{x}^{2}}=\\frac{0}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is an indeterminate form.\n\nInstead, let's recall the following special limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x}=1\n$$\n\nRewriting the given limit using the algebra of limits and applying our special limit, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{3{sin}^{2}⁡x}{2{x}^{2}} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}(\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡x}{{x}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to 0}{\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot {[\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x}]}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot {1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186314856 :question/worked-solution "Notice that as $x\\to 0,$ both numerator and denominator approach $0.$\n\nSo, if we attempt to evaluate the limit directly, we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{{sin}^{5}⁡x}{4{x}^{4}}=\\frac{0}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is an indeterminate form.\n\nInstead, let's recall the following special limit:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x}=1\n$$\n\nRewriting the given limit using the algebra of limits and applying our special limit, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{{sin}^{5}⁡x}{4{x}^{4}} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}(\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\frac{{sin}^{4}⁡x}{{x}^{4}}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{{sin}^{4}⁡x}{{x}^{4}}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to 0}{\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x}\\right)}^{4}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {[{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x}]}^{4}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {1}^{4}\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Notice that as $x\\to 0,$ both numerator and denominator approach $0.$\n\nSo, if we attempt to evaluate the limit directly, we get\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{{sin}^{5}⁡x}{4{x}^{4}}=\\frac{0}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is an indeterminate form.\n\nInstead, let's recall the following special limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x}=1\n$$\n\nRewriting the given limit using the algebra of limits and applying our special limit, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{{sin}^{5}⁡x}{4{x}^{4}} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}(\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\frac{{sin}^{4}⁡x}{{x}^{4}}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{{sin}^{4}⁡x}{{x}^{4}}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to 0}{\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x}\\right)}^{4}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {[\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x}]}^{4}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {1}^{4}\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186314882 :question/problem "Find the left-sided limit at $x=\\pi $ for the function\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}x+\\pi  & \\text{ for }\\,x<\\pi  \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡x & \\text{ for }\\,x\\ge \\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n${lim}_{x\\to {\\pi }^{-}}f(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" "Find the left-sided limit at $x=\\pi $ for the function\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}x+\\pi  & \\text{ for }\\,x<\\pi  \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡x & \\text{ for }\\,x\\ge \\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$\\lim_{x\\to {\\pi }^{-}}f(x)=$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186314882 :question/worked-solution "Approaching from the left, we have $x<\\pi ,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=x+\\pi .$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {\\pi }^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {\\pi }^{-}}(x+\\pi ) \\\\  & =\\pi +\\pi  \\\\  & =2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Approaching from the left, we have $x<\\pi ,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=x+\\pi .$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {\\pi }^{-}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to {\\pi }^{-}}(x+\\pi ) \\\\  & =\\pi +\\pi  \\\\  & =2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186314883 :question/problem "Find the right-sided limit at $x=8$ for the function\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-3x\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x\\le 8 \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{x}+2\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x>8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n${lim}_{x\\to {8}^{+}}f(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" "Find the right-sided limit at $x=8$ for the function\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-3x\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x\\le 8 \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{x}+2\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x>8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$\\lim_{x\\to {8}^{+}}f(x)=$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186314883 :question/worked-solution "Approaching from the right, we have $x>8,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=\\sqrt[3]{x}+2.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {8}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {8}^{+}}(\\sqrt[3]{x}+2) \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{8}+2 \\\\  & =2+2 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Approaching from the right, we have $x>8,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=\\sqrt[3]{x}+2.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {8}^{+}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to {8}^{+}}(\\sqrt[3]{x}+2) \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{8}+2 \\\\  & =2+2 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186314884 :question/problem "Find ${lim}_{x\\to (-2)^{-}}f(x)$ and ${lim}_{x\\to {4}^{+}}f(x)$ for the function given by\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}(x+2)^{3}+7 & \\text{ for }\\,x<-2 \\\\ \\sqrt{x-4}-1 & \\text{ for }\\,x\\ge 4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n${lim}_{x\\to (-2)^{-}}f(x)=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\,$ and $\\,$\n${lim}_{x\\to {4}^{+}}f(x)=$\n{{field-2}}" "Find $\\lim_{x\\to (-2)^{-}}f(x)$ and $\\lim_{x\\to {4}^{+}}f(x)$ for the function given by\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}(x+2)^{3}+7 & \\text{ for }\\,x<-2 \\\\ \\sqrt{x-4}-1 & \\text{ for }\\,x\\ge 4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$\\lim_{x\\to (-2)^{-}}f(x)=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\,$ and $\\,$\n$\\lim_{x\\to {4}^{+}}f(x)=$\n{{field-2}}"] [:db/cas 17592186314884 :question/worked-solution "Approaching $x=-2$ from the left, we have $x<-2,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=(x+2)^{3}+7.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-2)^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to (-2)^{-}}[(x+2)^{3}+7] \\\\  & =(-2+2)^{3}+7 \\\\  & ={0}^{3}+7 \\\\  & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, approaching $x=4$ from the right, we have $x\\ge 4,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=\\sqrt{x-4}-1.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {4}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {4}^{+}}(\\sqrt{x-4}-1) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4-4}-1 \\\\  & =\\sqrt{0}-1 \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Approaching $x=-2$ from the left, we have $x<-2,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=(x+2)^{3}+7.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (-2)^{-}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to (-2)^{-}}[(x+2)^{3}+7] \\\\  & =(-2+2)^{3}+7 \\\\  & ={0}^{3}+7 \\\\  & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, approaching $x=4$ from the right, we have $x\\ge 4,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=\\sqrt{x-4}-1.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {4}^{+}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to {4}^{+}}(\\sqrt{x-4}-1) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4-4}-1 \\\\  & =\\sqrt{0}-1 \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186314885 :question/problem "Find ${lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}f(x)$ and ${lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x)$ for the function given by\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi (x+1))-5 & \\text{ for }\\,x<-1 \\\\ \\sqrt{x-2}+(x-2)^{2}-3 & \\text{ for }\\,x\\ge 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n${lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}f(x)=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\,$ and $\\,$\n${lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x)=$\n{{field-2}}" "Find $\\lim_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}f(x)$ and $\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x)$ for the function given by\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi (x+1))-5 & \\text{ for }\\,x<-1 \\\\ \\sqrt{x-2}+(x-2)^{2}-3 & \\text{ for }\\,x\\ge 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$\\lim_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}f(x)=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\,$ and $\\,$\n$\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x)=$\n{{field-2}}"] [:db/cas 17592186314885 :question/worked-solution "Approaching $x=-1$ from the left, we have $x<-1,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi (x+1))-5.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}[2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi (x+1))-5] \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi (-1+1))-5 \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)-5 \\\\  & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, approaching $x=2$ from the right, we have $x\\ge 2,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=\\sqrt{x-2}+(x-2)^{2}-3.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}(\\sqrt{x-2}+(x-2)^{2}-3) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2-2}+(2-2)^{2}-3 \\\\  & =\\sqrt{0}+{0}^{2}-3 \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Approaching $x=-1$ from the left, we have $x<-1,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi (x+1))-5.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}[2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi (x+1))-5] \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi (-1+1))-5 \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)-5 \\\\  & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, approaching $x=2$ from the right, we have $x\\ge 2,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=\\sqrt{x-2}+(x-2)^{2}-3.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{+}}(\\sqrt{x-2}+(x-2)^{2}-3) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2-2}+(2-2)^{2}-3 \\\\  & =\\sqrt{0}+{0}^{2}-3 \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186314893 :question/problem "Find ${lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x)$ and ${lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x)$ for the function given by\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}3sin\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{3}x)+2 & \\text{ for }\\,x<0 \\\\ \\sqrt{7x+2}-1 & \\text{ for }\\,x\\ge 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n${lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x)=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\,$ and $\\,$\n${lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x)=$\n{{field-2}}" "Find $\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x)$ and $\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x)$ for the function given by\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}3sin\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{3}x)+2 & \\text{ for }\\,x<0 \\\\ \\sqrt{7x+2}-1 & \\text{ for }\\,x\\ge 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x)=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\,$ and $\\,$\n$\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x)=$\n{{field-2}}"] [:db/cas 17592186314893 :question/worked-solution "Approaching $x=0$ from the left, we have $x<0,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=3sin\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{3}x)+2.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}[3sin\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{3}x)+2] \\\\  & =3sin\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{3}(0))+2 \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)+2 \\\\  & =3\\cdot 0+2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, approaching $x=2$ from the right, we have $x\\ge 2,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=\\sqrt{7x+2}-1.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}(\\sqrt{7x+2}-1) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{7(2)+2}-1 \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16}-1 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Approaching $x=0$ from the left, we have $x<0,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=3sin\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{3}x)+2.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{-}}[3sin\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{3}x)+2] \\\\  & =3sin\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{3}(0))+2 \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)+2 \\\\  & =3\\cdot 0+2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, approaching $x=2$ from the right, we have $x\\ge 2,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=\\sqrt{7x+2}-1.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{+}}(\\sqrt{7x+2}-1) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{7(2)+2}-1 \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16}-1 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186314902 :question/problem "For the piecewise function \n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}a{x}^{2}+2x-3\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x\\le 2 \\\\ 4x+b\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x>2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nit is known that ${lim}_{x\\to 2}f(x)=9.$ Find the values of the constants $a$ and $b.$\n\n$a=$\n{{field-1}}\n$,\\,$\n\n$b=$\n{{field-2}}" "For the piecewise function \n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}a{x}^{2}+2x-3\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x\\le 2 \\\\ 4x+b\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x>2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nit is known that $\\lim_{x\\to 2}f(x)=9.$ Find the values of the constants $a$ and $b.$\n\n$a=$\n{{field-1}}\n$,\\,$\n\n$b=$\n{{field-2}}"] [:db/cas 17592186314902 :question/worked-solution "In order for ${lim}_{x\\to 2}f(x)$ to be equal to $8,$ the left and right-sided limits must both exist and be equal to $8.$\n\n- Approaching from the left, we have $x\\le 2,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=a{x}^{2}+2x-3.$ We get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}(a{x}^{2}+2x-3) \\\\  & =a({2}^{2})+2(2)-3 \\\\  & =4a+1\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the left-sided limit must be equal to $9,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}f(x) & =9 \\\\ 4a+1 & =9 \\\\ 4a & =8 \\\\ a & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Approaching from the right, we have $x>2,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=4x+b.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}(4x+b) \\\\  & =4(2)+b \\\\  & =8+b\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the right-sided limit must be equal to $9,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x) & =9 \\\\ 8+b & =9 \\\\ b & =9-8 \\\\ b & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "In order for $\\lim_{x\\to 2}f(x)$ to be equal to $8,$ the left and right-sided limits must both exist and be equal to $8.$\n\n- Approaching from the left, we have $x\\le 2,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=a{x}^{2}+2x-3.$ We get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{-}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{-}}(a{x}^{2}+2x-3) \\\\  & =a({2}^{2})+2(2)-3 \\\\  & =4a+1\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the left-sided limit must be equal to $9,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{-}}f(x) & =9 \\\\ 4a+1 & =9 \\\\ 4a & =8 \\\\ a & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Approaching from the right, we have $x>2,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=4x+b.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{+}}(4x+b) \\\\  & =4(2)+b \\\\  & =8+b\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the right-sided limit must be equal to $9,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x) & =9 \\\\ 8+b & =9 \\\\ b & =9-8 \\\\ b & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186314916 :question/worked-solution "First, we can simplify the limit using trigonometric identities, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\theta } & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\theta } \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\theta }\\cdot {lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now evaluate the limits separately:\n\n- Consider the first limit. Notice that as $\\theta \\to 0,$ both numerator and denominator approach $0.$ \n\nSo, if we attempt to evaluate the limit directly, we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\theta }=\\frac{0}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is an indeterminate form. \n\nInstead, let's recall the following special limit:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\theta }=1\n$$\n\n- Consider the second limit. Evaluating it directly, we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }=\\frac{0}{1}=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\theta }\\cdot {lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } & =1\\cdot 0=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, \n\n$$\n{lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\theta }=0.\n$$" "First, we can simplify the limit using trigonometric identities, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\theta } & =\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\theta } \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }) \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\theta }\\cdot {lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now evaluate the limits separately:\n\n- Consider the first limit. Notice that as $\\theta \\to 0,$ both numerator and denominator approach $0.$ \n\nSo, if we attempt to evaluate the limit directly, we get\n\n$$\n\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\theta }=\\frac{0}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is an indeterminate form. \n\nInstead, let's recall the following special limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\theta }=1\n$$\n\n- Consider the second limit. Evaluating it directly, we get\n\n$$\n\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }=\\frac{0}{1}=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\theta }\\cdot {lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } & =1\\cdot 0=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, \n\n$$\n\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\theta }=0.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186314917 :question/worked-solution "First, we can simplify the limit using trigonometric identities, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta -1}{3\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta }{3\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\left(\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\right)}{3\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}(\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{1}{3\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{1}{3{cos}^{4}⁡\\theta }) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\theta }\\cdot {lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{1}{3{cos}^{4}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now evaluate the limits separately:\n\n- Consider the first limit. Notice that as $\\theta \\to 0,$ both numerator and denominator approach $0.$ \n\nSo, if we attempt to evaluate the limit directly, we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\theta }=\\frac{0}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is an indeterminate form. \n\nInstead, let's recall the following special limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\theta } & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Consider the second limit. Evaluating it directly, we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{1}{3{cos}^{4}⁡\\theta }=\\frac{1}{3{cos}^{4}⁡0}=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\theta }\\right)\\cdot {lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{1}{3{cos}^{4}⁡\\theta } & =1\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}=\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we can simplify the limit using trigonometric identities, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta -1}{3\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } & =\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta }{3\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\left(\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\right)}{3\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}(\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{1}{3\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }) \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{1}{3{cos}^{4}⁡\\theta }) \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\theta }\\cdot {lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{1}{3{cos}^{4}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now evaluate the limits separately:\n\n- Consider the first limit. Notice that as $\\theta \\to 0,$ both numerator and denominator approach $0.$ \n\nSo, if we attempt to evaluate the limit directly, we get\n\n$$\n\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\theta }=\\frac{0}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is an indeterminate form. \n\nInstead, let's recall the following special limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\theta } & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Consider the second limit. Evaluating it directly, we get\n\n$$\n\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{1}{3{cos}^{4}⁡\\theta }=\\frac{1}{3{cos}^{4}⁡0}=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\theta }\\right)\\cdot {lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{1}{3{cos}^{4}⁡\\theta } & =1\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}=\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186314918 :question/worked-solution "First, we can simplify the limit using trigonometric identities, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\theta \\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta }{4\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta } & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}(\\frac{\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }÷4\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}(\\frac{\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }÷\\frac{4}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}(\\frac{\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{4}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}(\\frac{\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{4}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\frac{\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\cdot {lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now evaluate the limits separately:\n\n- Consider the first limit. Notice that as $\\theta \\to 0,$ both numerator and denominator approach $0.$ \n\nSo, if we attempt to evaluate the limit directly, we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }=\\frac{0}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is an indeterminate form. \n\nRewriting the given limit using the algebra of limits and applying our special limit, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } & =\\frac{1}{({lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\theta })} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Consider the second limit. Evaluating it directly, we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{4}=\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡0}{4}=\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\cdot {lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{4} & =1\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}=\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we can simplify the limit using trigonometric identities, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\theta \\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta }{4\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta } & =\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}(\\frac{\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }÷4\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}(\\frac{\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }÷\\frac{4}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }) \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}(\\frac{\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{4}) \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}(\\frac{\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{4}) \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\frac{\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\cdot {lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now evaluate the limits separately:\n\n- Consider the first limit. Notice that as $\\theta \\to 0,$ both numerator and denominator approach $0.$ \n\nSo, if we attempt to evaluate the limit directly, we get\n\n$$\n\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }=\\frac{0}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is an indeterminate form. \n\nRewriting the given limit using the algebra of limits and applying our special limit, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } & =\\frac{1}{(\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\theta })} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Consider the second limit. Evaluating it directly, we get\n\n$$\n\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{4}=\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡0}{4}=\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\cdot {lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{4} & =1\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}=\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186314922 :question/problem "For the piecewise function \n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}c\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x<2 \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x=2 \\\\ {x}^{3}-3\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x>2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nit is known that ${lim}_{x\\to 2}f(x)$ exists. What is the value of constant $c?$\n\n$c=$\n{{field-1}}" "For the piecewise function \n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}c\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x<2 \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x=2 \\\\ {x}^{3}-3\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x>2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nit is known that $\\lim_{x\\to 2}f(x)$ exists. What is the value of constant $c?$\n\n$c=$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186314922 :question/worked-solution "In order for ${lim}_{x\\to 2}f(x)$ to exist, the left and right-sided limits must both exist and be equal.\n\n- Approaching from the left, we have $x<2,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=c.$ We get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}c \\\\  & =c\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Approaching from the right, we have $x>2,$ so we use the expression $f(x)={x}^{3}-3.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}{x}^{3}-3 \\\\  & ={(2)}^{3}-3 \\\\  & =8-3 \\\\  & =5\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the left and right-sided limits must be equal, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x) \\\\ c & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "In order for $\\lim_{x\\to 2}f(x)$ to exist, the left and right-sided limits must both exist and be equal.\n\n- Approaching from the left, we have $x<2,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=c.$ We get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{-}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{-}}c \\\\  & =c\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Approaching from the right, we have $x>2,$ so we use the expression $f(x)={x}^{3}-3.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{+}}{x}^{3}-3 \\\\  & ={(2)}^{3}-3 \\\\  & =8-3 \\\\  & =5\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the left and right-sided limits must be equal, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{-}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x) \\\\ c & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186314925 :question/problem "For the piecewise function \n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}3{e}^{\\,\\,sin(x-1)}\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x<1 \\\\ p{x}^{3}\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x\\ge 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nit is known that ${lim}_{x\\to 1}f(x)=3.$ What is the value of the constant $p?$\n\n$p=$\n{{field-1}}" "For the piecewise function \n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}3{e}^{\\,\\,sin(x-1)}\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x<1 \\\\ p{x}^{3}\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x\\ge 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nit is known that $\\lim_{x\\to 1}f(x)=3.$ What is the value of the constant $p?$\n\n$p=$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186314925 :question/worked-solution "In order for ${lim}_{x\\to 1}f(x)$ to be equal to $3,$ the left and right-sided limits must both exist and be equal to $3.$\n\n- Approaching from the left, we have $x<1,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=3{e}^{\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-1)}.$ We get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}3{e}^{\\,\\,sin(x-1)} \\\\  & =3{e}^{\\,\\,sin(1-1)} \\\\  & =3{e}^{\\,\\,sin(0)} \\\\  & =3{e}^{0} \\\\  & =3\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Approaching from the right, we have $x>1,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=p{x}^{3}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}(p{x}^{3}) \\\\  & =p\\cdot {1}^{3} \\\\  & =p\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the left and right-sided limits must be equal, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x) \\\\ p & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "In order for $\\lim_{x\\to 1}f(x)$ to be equal to $3,$ the left and right-sided limits must both exist and be equal to $3.$\n\n- Approaching from the left, we have $x<1,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=3{e}^{\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-1)}.$ We get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{-}}3{e}^{\\,\\,sin(x-1)} \\\\  & =3{e}^{\\,\\,sin(1-1)} \\\\  & =3{e}^{\\,\\,sin(0)} \\\\  & =3{e}^{0} \\\\  & =3\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Approaching from the right, we have $x>1,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=p{x}^{3}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{+}}(p{x}^{3}) \\\\  & =p\\cdot {1}^{3} \\\\  & =p\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the left and right-sided limits must be equal, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x) \\\\ p & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186314992 :question/worked-solution "First, we can simplify the limit using trigonometric identities, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{{\\theta }^{2}\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}({\\theta }^{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}(\\frac{{\\theta }^{2}}{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\frac{{\\theta }^{2}}{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\cdot {lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now evaluate the limits separately:\n\n- Consider the first limit. Notice that as $\\theta \\to 0,$ both numerator and denominator approach $0.$ \n\nSo, if we attempt to evaluate the limit directly, we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }=\\frac{0}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is an indeterminate form. \n\nRewriting the given limit using the algebra of limits and applying our special limit, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{{\\theta }^{2}}{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta } & =\\frac{1}{({lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{{\\theta }^{2}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{({lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\theta })}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(1)^{2}} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Consider the second limit. Evaluating it directly, we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\frac{{\\theta }^{2}}{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\cdot {lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =1\\cdot 1=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we can simplify the limit using trigonometric identities, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{{\\theta }^{2}\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } & =\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}({\\theta }^{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }) \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}(\\frac{{\\theta }^{2}}{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\frac{{\\theta }^{2}}{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\cdot {lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now evaluate the limits separately:\n\n- Consider the first limit. Notice that as $\\theta \\to 0,$ both numerator and denominator approach $0.$ \n\nSo, if we attempt to evaluate the limit directly, we get\n\n$$\n\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }=\\frac{0}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is an indeterminate form. \n\nRewriting the given limit using the algebra of limits and applying our special limit, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{{\\theta }^{2}}{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta } & =\\frac{1}{(\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{{\\theta }^{2}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{(\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\theta })}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(1)^{2}} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Consider the second limit. Evaluating it directly, we get\n\n$$\n\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\frac{{\\theta }^{2}}{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\cdot {lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =1\\cdot 1=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186315000 :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(t)$ defined on $[0,\\infty ),$ the Laplace transform of $f,$ denoted $L{f(t)}$ or $F(s),$ is a function of the variable $s$ defined as\n\n$$\nF(s)=L{f(t)}={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nprovided that this integral exists and is finite. \n\nFor the function $f(t)=11,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }11{e}^{-st}\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe calculate our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }11{e}^{-st}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =11{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{e}^{-st}\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo evaluate the last integral, we must consider whether $s\\ne 0$ or $s=0.$\n\n- If $s\\ne 0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & =11{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{e}^{-st}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =11{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{(-\\frac{1}{s}{e}^{-st})|}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{11}{s}({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{-sa}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's evaluate the limit. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{-sa}={\\begin{aligned}0,\\, & s>0, \\\\ \\infty ,\\, & s<0.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- If $s=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(0) & =11{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{e}^{-0t}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =11{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =11{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }t\\,{|}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =11{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(a-0) \\\\  & =11{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }a \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n- For $s>0,$ we have that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }11{e}^{-st}\\text{d}t\\,\\text{is convergent}\n$$\n\n- For $s<0,$ we have that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }11{e}^{-st}\\text{d}t\\,\\text{is divergent}\n$$\n\n- For $s=0,$ we have that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }11{e}^{-st}\\text{d}t\\,\\text{is divergent}\n$$\n\nEvaluating our integral for $s>0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & =-\\frac{11}{s}({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{-sa}-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{11}{s}(0-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Laplace transform of $f(t)=11$ is $F(s)=\\frac{11}{s},$ defined for $s>0.$" "For a function $f(t)$ defined on $[0,\\infty ),$ the Laplace transform of $f,$ denoted $L{f(t)}$ or $F(s),$ is a function of the variable $s$ defined as\n\n$$\nF(s)=L{f(t)}={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nprovided that this integral exists and is finite. \n\nFor the function $f(t)=11,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }11{e}^{-st}\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe calculate our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }11{e}^{-st}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =11\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{e}^{-st}\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo evaluate the last integral, we must consider whether $s\\ne 0$ or $s=0.$\n\n- If $s\\ne 0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & =11\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{e}^{-st}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =11\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{(-\\frac{1}{s}{e}^{-st})|}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{11}{s}(\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{-sa}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's evaluate the limit. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{a\\to \\mathrm{\\infty }}{e}^{-sa}=\\begin{cases}0,\\hspace{1em} & s>0, \\\\ \\mathrm{\\infty },\\hspace{1em} & s<0.\\end{cases}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- If $s=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(0) & =11\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{e}^{-0t}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =11\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =11\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }t\\,{|}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =11\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }(a-0) \\\\  & =11\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }a \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n- For $s>0,$ we have that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }11{e}^{-st}\\text{d}t\\,\\text{is convergent}\n$$\n\n- For $s<0,$ we have that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }11{e}^{-st}\\text{d}t\\,\\text{is divergent}\n$$\n\n- For $s=0,$ we have that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }11{e}^{-st}\\text{d}t\\,\\text{is divergent}\n$$\n\nEvaluating our integral for $s>0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & =-\\frac{11}{s}(\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{-sa}-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{11}{s}(0-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Laplace transform of $f(t)=11$ is $F(s)=\\frac{11}{s},$ defined for $s>0.$"] [:db/cas 17592186315001 :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(t)$ defined on $[0,\\infty ),$ the Laplace transform of $f,$ denoted $L{f(t)}$ or $F(s),$ is a function of the variable $s$ defined as\n\n$$\nF(s)=L{f(t)}={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nprovided that this integral exists and is finite. \n\nFor the function $f(t)=-\\frac{7}{3},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }-\\frac{7}{3}{e}^{-st}\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe calculate our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }-\\frac{7}{3}{e}^{-st}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-\\frac{7}{3}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{e}^{-st}\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo evaluate the last integral, we must consider whether $s\\ne 0$ or $s=0.$\n\n- If $s\\ne 0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & =-\\frac{7}{3}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{e}^{-st}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-\\frac{7}{3}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{(-\\frac{1}{s}{e}^{-st})|}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{3s}({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{-sa}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's evaluate the limit. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{-sa}={\\begin{aligned}0,\\, & s>0, \\\\ \\infty ,\\, & s<0.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- If $s=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(0) & =-\\frac{7}{3}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{e}^{-0t}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-\\frac{7}{3}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-\\frac{7}{3}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }t\\,{|}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{7}{3}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(a-0) \\\\  & =-\\frac{7}{3}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }a \\\\  & =-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n- For $s<0,$ we have that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }-\\frac{7}{3}{e}^{-st}\\text{d}t\\,\\text{is divergent}\n$$\n\n- For $s>0,$ we have that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{{}_{0}}^{\\infty }-\\frac{7}{3}{e}^{-st}\\text{d}t\\,\\text{is convergent}\n$$\n\n- For $s=0,$ we have that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }-\\frac{7}{3}{e}^{-st}\\text{d}t\\,\\text{is divergent}\n$$\n\nEvaluating our integral for $s>0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & =\\frac{7}{3s}({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{-sa}-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{3s}(0-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{7}{3s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Laplace transform of $f(t)=-\\frac{7}{3}$ is $F(s)=-\\frac{7}{3s},$ defined for $s>0.$" "For a function $f(t)$ defined on $[0,\\infty ),$ the Laplace transform of $f,$ denoted $L{f(t)}$ or $F(s),$ is a function of the variable $s$ defined as\n\n$$\nF(s)=L{f(t)}={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nprovided that this integral exists and is finite. \n\nFor the function $f(t)=-\\frac{7}{3},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }-\\frac{7}{3}{e}^{-st}\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe calculate our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }-\\frac{7}{3}{e}^{-st}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-\\frac{7}{3}\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{e}^{-st}\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo evaluate the last integral, we must consider whether $s\\ne 0$ or $s=0.$\n\n- If $s\\ne 0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & =-\\frac{7}{3}\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{e}^{-st}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-\\frac{7}{3}\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{(-\\frac{1}{s}{e}^{-st})|}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{3s}(\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{-sa}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's evaluate the limit. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{a\\to \\mathrm{\\infty }}{e}^{-sa}=\\begin{cases}0,\\hspace{1em} & s>0, \\\\ \\mathrm{\\infty },\\hspace{1em} & s<0.\\end{cases}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- If $s=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(0) & =-\\frac{7}{3}\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{e}^{-0t}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-\\frac{7}{3}\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-\\frac{7}{3}\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }t\\,{|}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{7}{3}\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }(a-0) \\\\  & =-\\frac{7}{3}\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }a \\\\  & =-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n- For $s<0,$ we have that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }-\\frac{7}{3}{e}^{-st}\\text{d}t\\,\\text{is divergent}\n$$\n\n- For $s>0,$ we have that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{{}_{0}}^{\\infty }-\\frac{7}{3}{e}^{-st}\\text{d}t\\,\\text{is convergent}\n$$\n\n- For $s=0,$ we have that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }-\\frac{7}{3}{e}^{-st}\\text{d}t\\,\\text{is divergent}\n$$\n\nEvaluating our integral for $s>0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & =\\frac{7}{3s}(\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{-sa}-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{3s}(0-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{7}{3s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Laplace transform of $f(t)=-\\frac{7}{3}$ is $F(s)=-\\frac{7}{3s},$ defined for $s>0.$"] [:db/cas 17592186315002 :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(t)$ defined on $[0,\\infty ),$ the Laplace transform of $f,$ denoted $L{f(t)}$ or $F(s),$ is a function of the variable $s$ defined as\n\n$$\nF(s)=L{f(t)}={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nprovided that this integral exists and is finite. \n\nFor the function $f(t)={e}^{7t},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\cdot {e}^{7t}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st+7t}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{(7-s)t}\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe calculate our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{(7-s)t}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{e}^{(7-s)t}\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo evaluate the last integral, we must consider whether $s\\ne 7$ or $s=7.$\n\n- If $s\\ne 7,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{e}^{(7-s)t}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{1}{7-s}{e}^{(7-s)t})|}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7-s}({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{(7-s)a}-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s-7}(1-{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{(7-s)a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s-7}-\\frac{1}{s-7}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{(7-s)a}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's evaluate the limit. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{(7-s)a}={\\begin{aligned}0,\\, & s>7, \\\\ \\infty ,\\, & s<7.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- If $s=7,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(7) & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{e}^{(7-7)t}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{e}^{0t}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }t\\,{|}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(a-0) \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }a \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n- For $s\\le 7$ the integral is divergent.\n\n- For $s>7$ the integral is convergent.\n\nEvaluating for $s>7,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & =\\frac{1}{s-7}-\\frac{1}{s-7}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{(7-s)a} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s-7}-\\frac{1}{s-7}\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s-7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Laplace transform of $f(t)={e}^{7t}$ is $F(s)=\\frac{1}{s-7},$ defined for $s>7.$" "For a function $f(t)$ defined on $[0,\\infty ),$ the Laplace transform of $f,$ denoted $L{f(t)}$ or $F(s),$ is a function of the variable $s$ defined as\n\n$$\nF(s)=L{f(t)}={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nprovided that this integral exists and is finite. \n\nFor the function $f(t)={e}^{7t},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\cdot {e}^{7t}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st+7t}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{(7-s)t}\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe calculate our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{(7-s)t}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{e}^{(7-s)t}\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo evaluate the last integral, we must consider whether $s\\ne 7$ or $s=7.$\n\n- If $s\\ne 7,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{e}^{(7-s)t}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{1}{7-s}{e}^{(7-s)t})|}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7-s}(\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{(7-s)a}-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s-7}(1-\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{(7-s)a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s-7}-\\frac{1}{s-7}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{(7-s)a}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's evaluate the limit. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{a\\to \\mathrm{\\infty }}{e}^{(7-s)a}=\\begin{cases}0,\\hspace{1em} & s>7, \\\\ \\mathrm{\\infty },\\hspace{1em} & s<7.\\end{cases}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- If $s=7,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(7) & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{e}^{(7-7)t}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{e}^{0t}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }t\\,{|}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }(a-0) \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }a \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n- For $s\\le 7$ the integral is divergent.\n\n- For $s>7$ the integral is convergent.\n\nEvaluating for $s>7,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & =\\frac{1}{s-7}-\\frac{1}{s-7}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{(7-s)a} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s-7}-\\frac{1}{s-7}\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s-7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Laplace transform of $f(t)={e}^{7t}$ is $F(s)=\\frac{1}{s-7},$ defined for $s>7.$"] [:db/cas 17592186315003 :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(t)$ defined on $[0,\\infty ),$ the Laplace transform of $f,$ denoted $L{f(t)}$ or $F(s),$ is a function of the variable $s$ defined as\n\n$$\nF(s)=L{f(t)}={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nprovided that this integral exists and is finite. \n\nFor the function $f(t)={e}^{-t},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\cdot {e}^{-t}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st-t}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{(-1-s)t}\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe calculate our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{(-1-s)t}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{e}^{(-1-s)t}\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo evaluate the last integral, we must consider whether $s\\ne -1$ or $s=-1.$\n\n- If $s\\ne -1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{e}^{(-1-s)t}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{1}{-1-s}{e}^{(-1-s)t})|}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{-1-s}({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{(-1-s)a}-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s+1}(1-{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{(-1-s)a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s+1}-\\frac{1}{s+1}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{(-1-s)a}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's evaluate the limit. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{(-1-s)a}={\\begin{aligned}0,\\, & s>-1, \\\\ \\infty ,\\, & s<-1.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- If $s=-1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(-1) & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{e}^{(-1-(-1))t}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{e}^{0t}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }t\\,{|}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(a-0) \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }a \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n- For $s\\le -1$ the integral is divergent.\n\n- For $s>-1$ the integral is convergent.\n\nEvaluating for $s>-1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & =\\frac{1}{s+1}-\\frac{1}{s+1}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{(-1-s)a} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s+1}-\\frac{1}{s+1}\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Laplace transform of $f(t)={e}^{-t}$ is $F(s)=\\frac{1}{s+1},$ defined for $s>-1.$" "For a function $f(t)$ defined on $[0,\\infty ),$ the Laplace transform of $f,$ denoted $L{f(t)}$ or $F(s),$ is a function of the variable $s$ defined as\n\n$$\nF(s)=L{f(t)}={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nprovided that this integral exists and is finite. \n\nFor the function $f(t)={e}^{-t},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\cdot {e}^{-t}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st-t}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{(-1-s)t}\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe calculate our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{(-1-s)t}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{e}^{(-1-s)t}\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo evaluate the last integral, we must consider whether $s\\ne -1$ or $s=-1.$\n\n- If $s\\ne -1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{e}^{(-1-s)t}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{1}{-1-s}{e}^{(-1-s)t})|}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{-1-s}(\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{(-1-s)a}-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s+1}(1-\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{(-1-s)a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s+1}-\\frac{1}{s+1}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{(-1-s)a}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's evaluate the limit. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{a\\to \\mathrm{\\infty }}{e}^{(-1-s)a}=\\begin{cases}0,\\hspace{1em} & s>-1, \\\\ \\mathrm{\\infty },\\hspace{1em} & s<-1.\\end{cases}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- If $s=-1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(-1) & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{e}^{(-1-(-1))t}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{e}^{0t}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }t\\,{|}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }(a-0) \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }a \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n- For $s\\le -1$ the integral is divergent.\n\n- For $s>-1$ the integral is convergent.\n\nEvaluating for $s>-1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & =\\frac{1}{s+1}-\\frac{1}{s+1}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{(-1-s)a} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s+1}-\\frac{1}{s+1}\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Laplace transform of $f(t)={e}^{-t}$ is $F(s)=\\frac{1}{s+1},$ defined for $s>-1.$"] [:db/cas 17592186315004 :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(t)$ defined on $[0,\\infty ),$ the Laplace transform of $f,$ denoted $L{f(t)}$ or $F(s),$ is a function of the variable $s$ defined as\n\n$$\nF(s)=L{f(t)}={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nprovided that this integral exists and is finite. \n\nFor the function $f(t)={e}^{t/4},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\cdot {e}^{t/4}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st+t/4}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{(1/4-s)t}\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe calculate our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{(1/4-s)t}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{e}^{(1/4-s)t}\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo evaluate the last integral, we must consider whether $s\\ne \\frac{1}{4}$ or $s=\\frac{1}{4}.$\n\n- If $s\\ne \\frac{1}{4},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{e}^{(1/4-s)t}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{1}{1/4-s}{e}^{(1/4-s)t})|}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1/4-s}({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{(1/4-s)a}-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s-1/4}(1-{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{(1/4-s)a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s-1/4}-\\frac{1}{s-1/4}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{(1/4-s)a}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's evaluate the limit. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{(1/4-s)a}={\\begin{aligned}0,\\, & s>\\frac{1}{4}, \\\\ \\infty ,\\, & s<\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- If $s=\\frac{1}{4},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right) & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{e}^{(1/4-1/4)t}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{e}^{0t}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }t\\,{|}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(a-0) \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }a \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n- For $s>\\frac{1}{4}$ the integral is convergent.\n\n- For $s\\le \\frac{1}{4}$ the integral is divergent.\n\nEvaluating for $s>\\frac{1}{4},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & =\\frac{1}{s-1/4}-\\frac{1}{s-1/4}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{(1/4-s)a} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s-1/4}-\\frac{1}{s-1/4}\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s-1/4} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{4s-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Laplace transform of $f(t)={e}^{t/4}$ is $F(s)=\\frac{4}{4s-1},$ defined for $s>\\frac{1}{4}.$" "For a function $f(t)$ defined on $[0,\\infty ),$ the Laplace transform of $f,$ denoted $L{f(t)}$ or $F(s),$ is a function of the variable $s$ defined as\n\n$$\nF(s)=L{f(t)}={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nprovided that this integral exists and is finite. \n\nFor the function $f(t)={e}^{t/4},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\cdot {e}^{t/4}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st+t/4}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{(1/4-s)t