  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{1}{-1/4-s}{e}^{(-1/4-s)t})|}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{-1/4-s}({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{(-1/4-s)a}-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s+1/4}(1-{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{(-1/4-s)a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s+1/4}-\\frac{1}{s+1/4}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{(-1/4-s)a}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's evaluate the limit. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{(-1/4-s)a}={\\begin{aligned}0,\\, & s>-\\frac{1}{4}, \\\\ \\infty ,\\, & s<-\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- If $s=-\\frac{1}{4},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\left(-\\frac{1}{4}\\right) & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{e}^{(-1/4-(-1/4))t}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{e}^{0t}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }t\\,{|}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(a-0) \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }a \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n- For $s\\le -\\frac{1}{4}$ the integral is divergent.\n\n- For $s>-\\frac{1}{4}$ the integral is convergent.\n\nEvaluating for $s>-\\frac{1}{4},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & =\\frac{1}{s+1/4}-\\frac{1}{s+1/4}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{(-1/4-s)a} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s+1/4}-\\frac{1}{s+1/4}\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s+1/4} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{4s+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Laplace transform of $f(t)={e}^{-t/4}$ is $F(s)=\\frac{4}{4s+1},$ defined for $s>-\\frac{1}{4}.$" "For a function $f(t)$ defined on $[0,\\infty ),$ the Laplace transform of $f,$ denoted $L{f(t)}$ or $F(s),$ is a function of the variable $s$ defined as\n\n$$\nF(s)=L{f(t)}={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nprovided that this integral exists and is finite. \n\nFor the function $f(t)={e}^{-t/4},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\cdot {e}^{-t/4}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st-t/4}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{(-1/4-s)t}\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe calculate our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{(-1/4-s)t}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{e}^{(-1/4-s)t}\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo evaluate the last integral, we must consider whether $s\\ne -\\frac{1}{4}$ or $s=-\\frac{1}{4}.$\n\n- If $s\\ne -\\frac{1}{4},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{e}^{(-1/4-s)t}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{1}{-1/4-s}{e}^{(-1/4-s)t})|}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{-1/4-s}(\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{(-1/4-s)a}-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s+1/4}(1-\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{(-1/4-s)a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s+1/4}-\\frac{1}{s+1/4}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{(-1/4-s)a}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's evaluate the limit. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{a\\to \\mathrm{\\infty }}{e}^{(-1/4-s)a}=\\begin{cases}0,\\hspace{1em} & s>-\\frac{1}{4}, \\\\ \\mathrm{\\infty },\\hspace{1em} & s<-\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{cases}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- If $s=-\\frac{1}{4},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\left(-\\frac{1}{4}\\right) & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{e}^{(-1/4-(-1/4))t}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{e}^{0t}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }t\\,{|}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }(a-0) \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }a \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n- For $s\\le -\\frac{1}{4}$ the integral is divergent.\n\n- For $s>-\\frac{1}{4}$ the integral is convergent.\n\nEvaluating for $s>-\\frac{1}{4},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & =\\frac{1}{s+1/4}-\\frac{1}{s+1/4}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{(-1/4-s)a} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s+1/4}-\\frac{1}{s+1/4}\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s+1/4} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{4s+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Laplace transform of $f(t)={e}^{-t/4}$ is $F(s)=\\frac{4}{4s+1},$ defined for $s>-\\frac{1}{4}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186315007 :question/worked-solution "We apply the definition of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =F(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the function $f(t)={e}^{4\\text{i}t},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}{e}^{4\\text{i}t}\\text{d}t={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{(4\\text{i}-s)t}\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating in the usual way, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{(4\\text{i}-s)t}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{e}^{(4\\text{i}-s)t}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{1}{4\\text{i}-s}{e}^{(4\\text{i}-s)t})|}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4\\text{i}-s}({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{(4\\text{i}-s)a}-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s-4\\text{i}}(1-{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{(4\\text{i}-s)a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s-4\\text{i}}-\\frac{1}{s-4\\text{i}}({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{-sa}\\cdot {e}^{4\\text{i}a}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we recall Euler's formula:\n\n$$\n{e}^{\\text{i}\\theta }=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nF(s)=\\frac{1}{s-4\\text{i}}-\\frac{1}{s-4\\text{i}}[{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{-sa}\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4a)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4a))].\n$$\n\nNow, we have the following cases:\n\n- For $s<0,$ the term ${e}^{-sa}$ increases without bound while $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4a)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4a)$ oscillates rapidly as $a\\to \\infty .$ Therefore, the limit does not exist for $s<0.$\n\n- For $s=0,$ we have simply $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4a)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4a),$ which oscillates rapidly as $a\\to \\infty .$ Therefore, the limit does not exist for $s=0.$\n\n- For $s>0,$ the term ${e}^{-sa}$ decays to zero while $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4a)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4a)$ oscillates rapidly as $a\\to \\infty .$ Therefore, the limit of their product equals zero.\n\nTherefore, for $s>0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & =\\frac{1}{s-4\\text{i}}-\\frac{1}{s-4\\text{i}}[{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{-sa}\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4a)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4a))] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s-4\\text{i}}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s-4\\text{i}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Laplace transform of ${e}^{4\\text{i}t}$ is $F(s)=\\frac{1}{s-4\\text{i}},$ defined for $s>0.$" "We apply the definition of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =F(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the function $f(t)={e}^{4\\text{i}t},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}{e}^{4\\text{i}t}\\text{d}t={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{(4\\text{i}-s)t}\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating in the usual way, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{(4\\text{i}-s)t}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{e}^{(4\\text{i}-s)t}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{1}{4\\text{i}-s}{e}^{(4\\text{i}-s)t})|}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4\\text{i}-s}(\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{(4\\text{i}-s)a}-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s-4\\text{i}}(1-\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{(4\\text{i}-s)a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s-4\\text{i}}-\\frac{1}{s-4\\text{i}}(\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{-sa}\\cdot {e}^{4\\text{i}a}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we recall Euler's formula:\n\n$$\n{e}^{\\text{i}\\theta }=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nF(s)=\\frac{1}{s-4\\text{i}}-\\frac{1}{s-4\\text{i}}[\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{-sa}\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4a)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4a))].\n$$\n\nNow, we have the following cases:\n\n- For $s<0,$ the term ${e}^{-sa}$ increases without bound while $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4a)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4a)$ oscillates rapidly as $a\\to \\infty .$ Therefore, the limit does not exist for $s<0.$\n\n- For $s=0,$ we have simply $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4a)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4a),$ which oscillates rapidly as $a\\to \\infty .$ Therefore, the limit does not exist for $s=0.$\n\n- For $s>0,$ the term ${e}^{-sa}$ decays to zero while $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4a)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4a)$ oscillates rapidly as $a\\to \\infty .$ Therefore, the limit of their product equals zero.\n\nTherefore, for $s>0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & =\\frac{1}{s-4\\text{i}}-\\frac{1}{s-4\\text{i}}[\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{-sa}\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4a)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4a))] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s-4\\text{i}}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s-4\\text{i}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Laplace transform of ${e}^{4\\text{i}t}$ is $F(s)=\\frac{1}{s-4\\text{i}},$ defined for $s>0.$"] [:db/cas 17592186315008 :question/worked-solution "We apply the definition of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =F(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the function $f(t)={e}^{7\\text{i}t},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}{e}^{7\\text{i}t}\\text{d}t={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{(7\\text{i}-s)t}\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating in the usual way, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{(7\\text{i}-s)t}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{e}^{(7\\text{i}-s)t}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{1}{7\\text{i}-s}{e}^{(7\\text{i}-s)t})|}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7\\text{i}-s}({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{(7\\text{i}-s)a}-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s-7\\text{i}}(1-{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{(7\\text{i}-s)a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s-7\\text{i}}-\\frac{1}{s-7\\text{i}}({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{-sa}\\cdot {e}^{7\\text{i}a}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we recall Euler's formula:\n\n$$\n{e}^{\\text{i}\\theta }=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nF(s)=\\frac{1}{s-7\\text{i}}-\\frac{1}{s-7\\text{i}}[{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{-sa}\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7a)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7a))].\n$$\n\nNow, we have the following cases:\n\n- For $s<0,$ the term ${e}^{-sa}$ increases without bound while $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7a)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7a)$ oscillates rapidly as $a\\to \\infty .$ Therefore, the limit does not exist for $s<0.$\n\n- For $s=0,$ we have simply $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7a)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7a),$ which oscillates rapidly as $a\\to \\infty .$ Therefore, the limit does not exist for $s=0.$\n\n- For $s>0,$ the term ${e}^{-sa}$ decays to zero while $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7a)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7a)$ oscillates rapidly as $a\\to \\infty .$ Therefore, the limit of their product equals zero.\n\nTherefore, for $s>0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & =\\frac{1}{s-7\\text{i}}-\\frac{1}{s-7\\text{i}}[{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{-sa}\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7a)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7a))] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s-7\\text{i}}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s-7\\text{i}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Laplace transform of ${e}^{7\\text{i}t}$ is $F(s)=\\frac{1}{s-7\\text{i}},$ defined for $s>0.$" "We apply the definition of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =F(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the function $f(t)={e}^{7\\text{i}t},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}{e}^{7\\text{i}t}\\text{d}t={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{(7\\text{i}-s)t}\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating in the usual way, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{(7\\text{i}-s)t}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{e}^{(7\\text{i}-s)t}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{1}{7\\text{i}-s}{e}^{(7\\text{i}-s)t})|}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7\\text{i}-s}(\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{(7\\text{i}-s)a}-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s-7\\text{i}}(1-\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{(7\\text{i}-s)a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s-7\\text{i}}-\\frac{1}{s-7\\text{i}}(\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{-sa}\\cdot {e}^{7\\text{i}a}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we recall Euler's formula:\n\n$$\n{e}^{\\text{i}\\theta }=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nF(s)=\\frac{1}{s-7\\text{i}}-\\frac{1}{s-7\\text{i}}[\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{-sa}\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7a)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7a))].\n$$\n\nNow, we have the following cases:\n\n- For $s<0,$ the term ${e}^{-sa}$ increases without bound while $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7a)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7a)$ oscillates rapidly as $a\\to \\infty .$ Therefore, the limit does not exist for $s<0.$\n\n- For $s=0,$ we have simply $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7a)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7a),$ which oscillates rapidly as $a\\to \\infty .$ Therefore, the limit does not exist for $s=0.$\n\n- For $s>0,$ the term ${e}^{-sa}$ decays to zero while $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7a)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7a)$ oscillates rapidly as $a\\to \\infty .$ Therefore, the limit of their product equals zero.\n\nTherefore, for $s>0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & =\\frac{1}{s-7\\text{i}}-\\frac{1}{s-7\\text{i}}[\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{-sa}\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7a)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7a))] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s-7\\text{i}}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s-7\\text{i}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Laplace transform of ${e}^{7\\text{i}t}$ is $F(s)=\\frac{1}{s-7\\text{i}},$ defined for $s>0.$"] [:db/cas 17592186315009 :question/worked-solution "We apply the definition of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =F(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the function $f(t)={e}^{-2\\text{i}t},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}{e}^{-2\\text{i}t}\\text{d}t={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{(-2\\text{i}-s)t}\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating in the usual way, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{(-2\\text{i}-s)t}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{e}^{(-2\\text{i}-s)t}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{1}{-2\\text{i}-s}{e}^{(-2\\text{i}-s)t})|}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{-2\\text{i}-s}({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{(-2\\text{i}-s)a}-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s+2\\text{i}}(1-{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{(-2\\text{i}-s)a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s+2\\text{i}}-\\frac{1}{s+2\\text{i}}({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{-sa}\\cdot {e}^{-2\\text{i}a}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we recall Euler's formula:\n\n$$\n{e}^{\\text{i}\\theta }=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nF(s)=\\frac{1}{s+2\\text{i}}-\\frac{1}{s+2\\text{i}}[{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{-sa}\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-2a)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-2a))].\n$$\n\nNow, we have the following cases:\n\n- For $s<0,$ the term ${e}^{-sa}$ increases without bound while $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-2a)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-2a)$ oscillates rapidly as $a\\to \\infty .$ Therefore, the limit does not exist for $s<0.$\n\n- For $s=0,$ we have simply $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-2a)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-2a),$ which oscillates rapidly as $a\\to \\infty .$ Therefore, the limit does not exist for $s=0.$\n\n- For $s>0,$ the term ${e}^{-sa}$ decays to zero while $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-2a)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-2a)$ oscillates rapidly as $a\\to \\infty .$ Therefore, the limit of their product equals zero.\n\nTherefore, for $s>0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & =\\frac{1}{s+2\\text{i}}-\\frac{1}{s+2\\text{i}}[{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{-sa}\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-2a)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-2a))] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s+2\\text{i}}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s+2\\text{i}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Laplace transform of ${e}^{-2\\text{i}t}$ is $F(s)=\\frac{1}{s+2\\text{i}},$ defined for $s>0.$" "We apply the definition of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =F(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the function $f(t)={e}^{-2\\text{i}t},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}{e}^{-2\\text{i}t}\\text{d}t={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{(-2\\text{i}-s)t}\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating in the usual way, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{(-2\\text{i}-s)t}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{e}^{(-2\\text{i}-s)t}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{1}{-2\\text{i}-s}{e}^{(-2\\text{i}-s)t})|}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{-2\\text{i}-s}(\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{(-2\\text{i}-s)a}-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s+2\\text{i}}(1-\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{(-2\\text{i}-s)a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s+2\\text{i}}-\\frac{1}{s+2\\text{i}}(\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{-sa}\\cdot {e}^{-2\\text{i}a}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we recall Euler's formula:\n\n$$\n{e}^{\\text{i}\\theta }=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nF(s)=\\frac{1}{s+2\\text{i}}-\\frac{1}{s+2\\text{i}}[\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{-sa}\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-2a)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-2a))].\n$$\n\nNow, we have the following cases:\n\n- For $s<0,$ the term ${e}^{-sa}$ increases without bound while $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-2a)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-2a)$ oscillates rapidly as $a\\to \\infty .$ Therefore, the limit does not exist for $s<0.$\n\n- For $s=0,$ we have simply $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-2a)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-2a),$ which oscillates rapidly as $a\\to \\infty .$ Therefore, the limit does not exist for $s=0.$\n\n- For $s>0,$ the term ${e}^{-sa}$ decays to zero while $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-2a)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-2a)$ oscillates rapidly as $a\\to \\infty .$ Therefore, the limit of their product equals zero.\n\nTherefore, for $s>0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & =\\frac{1}{s+2\\text{i}}-\\frac{1}{s+2\\text{i}}[\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{-sa}\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-2a)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-2a))] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s+2\\text{i}}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s+2\\text{i}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Laplace transform of ${e}^{-2\\text{i}t}$ is $F(s)=\\frac{1}{s+2\\text{i}},$ defined for $s>0.$"] [:db/cas 17592186315011 :question/worked-solution "We apply the definition of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =F(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the function $f(t)={e}^{-3\\text{i}t/8},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}{e}^{-3\\text{i}t/8}\\text{d}t={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{(-3\\text{i}/8-s)t}\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating in the usual way, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{(-3\\text{i}/8-s)t}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{e}^{(-3\\text{i}/8-s)t}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{1}{-3\\text{i}/8-s}{e}^{(-3\\text{i}/8-s)t})|}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{-3\\text{i}/8-s}({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{(-3\\text{i}/8-s)a}-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s+3\\text{i}/8}(1-{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{(-3\\text{i}/8-s)a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s+3\\text{i}/8}-\\frac{1}{s+3\\text{i}/8}({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{-sa}\\cdot {e}^{-3\\text{i}a/8}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we recall Euler's formula:\n\n$$\n{e}^{\\text{i}\\theta }=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nF(s)=\\frac{1}{s+3\\text{i}/8}-\\frac{1}{s+3\\text{i}/8}[{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{-sa}\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-3a/8)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-3a/8))].\n$$\n\nNow, we have the following cases:\n\n- For $s<0,$ the term ${e}^{-sa}$ increases without bound while $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-3a/8)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-3a/8)$ oscillates rapidly as $a\\to \\infty .$ Therefore, the limit does not exist for $s<0.$\n\n- For $s=0,$ we have simply $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-3a/8)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-3a/8),$ which oscillates rapidly as $a\\to \\infty .$ Therefore, the limit does not exist for $s=0.$\n\n- For $s>0,$ the term ${e}^{-sa}$ decays to zero while $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-3a/8)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-3a/8)$ oscillates rapidly as $a\\to \\infty .$ Therefore, the limit of their product equals zero.\n\nTherefore, for $s>0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & =\\frac{1}{s+3\\text{i}/8}-\\frac{1}{s+3\\text{i}/8}[{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{-sa}\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-3a/8)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-3a/8))] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s+3\\text{i}/8}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s+3\\text{i}/8} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{8s+3\\text{i}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Laplace transform of ${e}^{-3\\text{i}t/8}$ is $F(s)=\\frac{8}{8s+3\\text{i}},$ defined for $s>0.$" "We apply the definition of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =F(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the function $f(t)={e}^{-3\\text{i}t/8},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}{e}^{-3\\text{i}t/8}\\text{d}t={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{(-3\\text{i}/8-s)t}\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating in the usual way, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{(-3\\text{i}/8-s)t}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{e}^{(-3\\text{i}/8-s)t}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{1}{-3\\text{i}/8-s}{e}^{(-3\\text{i}/8-s)t})|}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{-3\\text{i}/8-s}(\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{(-3\\text{i}/8-s)a}-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s+3\\text{i}/8}(1-\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{(-3\\text{i}/8-s)a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s+3\\text{i}/8}-\\frac{1}{s+3\\text{i}/8}(\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{-sa}\\cdot {e}^{-3\\text{i}a/8}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we recall Euler's formula:\n\n$$\n{e}^{\\text{i}\\theta }=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nF(s)=\\frac{1}{s+3\\text{i}/8}-\\frac{1}{s+3\\text{i}/8}[\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{-sa}\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-3a/8)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-3a/8))].\n$$\n\nNow, we have the following cases:\n\n- For $s<0,$ the term ${e}^{-sa}$ increases without bound while $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-3a/8)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-3a/8)$ oscillates rapidly as $a\\to \\infty .$ Therefore, the limit does not exist for $s<0.$\n\n- For $s=0,$ we have simply $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-3a/8)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-3a/8),$ which oscillates rapidly as $a\\to \\infty .$ Therefore, the limit does not exist for $s=0.$\n\n- For $s>0,$ the term ${e}^{-sa}$ decays to zero while $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-3a/8)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-3a/8)$ oscillates rapidly as $a\\to \\infty .$ Therefore, the limit of their product equals zero.\n\nTherefore, for $s>0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & =\\frac{1}{s+3\\text{i}/8}-\\frac{1}{s+3\\text{i}/8}[\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{-sa}\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-3a/8)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-3a/8))] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s+3\\text{i}/8}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s+3\\text{i}/8} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{8s+3\\text{i}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Laplace transform of ${e}^{-3\\text{i}t/8}$ is $F(s)=\\frac{8}{8s+3\\text{i}},$ defined for $s>0.$"] [:db/cas 17592186315012 :question/worked-solution "We apply the definition of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =F(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the function $f(t)={e}^{-\\text{i}t/9},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}{e}^{-\\text{i}t/9}\\text{d}t={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{(-\\text{i}/9-s)t}\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating in the usual way, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{(-\\text{i}/9-s)t}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{e}^{(-\\text{i}/9-s)t}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{1}{-\\text{i}/9-s}{e}^{(-\\text{i}/9-s)t})|}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{-\\text{i}/9-s}({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{(-\\text{i}/9-s)a}-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s+\\text{i}/9}(1-{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{(-\\text{i}/9-s)a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s+\\text{i}/9}-\\frac{1}{s+\\text{i}/9}({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{-sa}\\cdot {e}^{-\\text{i}a/9}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we recall Euler's formula:\n\n$$\n{e}^{\\text{i}\\theta }=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nF(s)=\\frac{1}{s+\\text{i}/9}-\\frac{1}{s+\\text{i}/9}[{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{-sa}\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-a/9)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-a/9))].\n$$\n\nNow, we have the following cases:\n\n- For $s<0,$ the term ${e}^{-sa}$ increases without bound while $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-a/9)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-a/9)$ oscillates rapidly as $a\\to \\infty .$ Therefore, the limit does not exist for $s<0.$\n\n- For $s=0,$ we have simply $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-a/9)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-a/9),$ which oscillates rapidly as $a\\to \\infty .$ Therefore, the limit does not exist for $s=0.$\n\n- For $s>0,$ the term ${e}^{-sa}$ decays to zero while $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-a/9)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-a/9)$ oscillates rapidly as $a\\to \\infty .$ Therefore, the limit of their product equals zero.\n\nTherefore, for $s>0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & =\\frac{1}{s+\\text{i}/9}-\\frac{1}{s+\\text{i}/9}[{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{-sa}\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-a/9)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-a/9))] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s+\\text{i}/9}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s+\\text{i}/9} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{9s+\\text{i}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Laplace transform of ${e}^{-\\text{i}t/9}$ is $F(s)=\\frac{9}{9s+\\text{i}},$ defined for $s>0.$" "We apply the definition of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =F(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the function $f(t)={e}^{-\\text{i}t/9},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}{e}^{-\\text{i}t/9}\\text{d}t={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{(-\\text{i}/9-s)t}\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating in the usual way, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{(-\\text{i}/9-s)t}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{e}^{(-\\text{i}/9-s)t}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{1}{-\\text{i}/9-s}{e}^{(-\\text{i}/9-s)t})|}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{-\\text{i}/9-s}(\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{(-\\text{i}/9-s)a}-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s+\\text{i}/9}(1-\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{(-\\text{i}/9-s)a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s+\\text{i}/9}-\\frac{1}{s+\\text{i}/9}(\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{-sa}\\cdot {e}^{-\\text{i}a/9}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we recall Euler's formula:\n\n$$\n{e}^{\\text{i}\\theta }=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nF(s)=\\frac{1}{s+\\text{i}/9}-\\frac{1}{s+\\text{i}/9}[\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{-sa}\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-a/9)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-a/9))].\n$$\n\nNow, we have the following cases:\n\n- For $s<0,$ the term ${e}^{-sa}$ increases without bound while $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-a/9)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-a/9)$ oscillates rapidly as $a\\to \\infty .$ Therefore, the limit does not exist for $s<0.$\n\n- For $s=0,$ we have simply $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-a/9)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-a/9),$ which oscillates rapidly as $a\\to \\infty .$ Therefore, the limit does not exist for $s=0.$\n\n- For $s>0,$ the term ${e}^{-sa}$ decays to zero while $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-a/9)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-a/9)$ oscillates rapidly as $a\\to \\infty .$ Therefore, the limit of their product equals zero.\n\nTherefore, for $s>0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & =\\frac{1}{s+\\text{i}/9}-\\frac{1}{s+\\text{i}/9}[\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{-sa}\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-a/9)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-a/9))] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s+\\text{i}/9}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s+\\text{i}/9} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{9s+\\text{i}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Laplace transform of ${e}^{-\\text{i}t/9}$ is $F(s)=\\frac{9}{9s+\\text{i}},$ defined for $s>0.$"] [:db/cas 17592186315031 :question/worked-solution "We apply the definition of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =F(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(t){e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that the unit step function is defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t)={\\begin{aligned}0,\\, & t<0 \\\\ 1,\\, & t\\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for the piecewise function \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(5-t)={\\begin{aligned}1,\\, & t\\le 5 \\\\ 0,\\, & t>5\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }u(5-t){e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{5}1\\cdot {e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{5}^{\\infty }0\\cdot {e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{5}{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe compute this integral for $s>0$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{0}^{5}{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{{e}^{-st}}{s}]}_{0}^{5} \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{-5s}}{s}+\\frac{1}{s} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s}(1-{e}^{-5s})\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We apply the definition of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =F(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(t){e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that the unit step function is defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t)=\\begin{cases}0,\\hspace{1em} & t<0 \\\\ 1,\\hspace{1em} & t\\ge 0.\\end{cases}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for the piecewise function \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(5-t)=\\begin{cases}1,\\hspace{1em} & t\\le 5 \\\\ 0,\\hspace{1em} & t>5\\end{cases}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }u(5-t){e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{5}1\\cdot {e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{5}^{\\infty }0\\cdot {e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{5}{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe compute this integral for $s>0$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{0}^{5}{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{{e}^{-st}}{s}]}_{0}^{5} \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{-5s}}{s}+\\frac{1}{s} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s}(1-{e}^{-5s})\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186315035 :question/worked-solution "We apply the definition of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =F(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(t){e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for the piecewise function \n\n$$\nf(t)={\\begin{aligned}-1,\\, & x\\le 4 \\\\ 1\\, & x>4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(t){e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}(-1)\\cdot {e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }1\\cdot {e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{4}{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the improper integral diverges if $s\\le 0.$\n\nFor $s>0,$ we calculate our Laplace transform as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & =-{\\int }_{0}^{4}{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s}{[{e}^{-st}]}_{0}^{4}+{\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-4s}}{s}-\\frac{1}{s}+{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{4}^{a}{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-4s}}{s}-\\frac{1}{s}+{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\left(-\\frac{{e}^{-st}}{s}\\right)|}_{4}^{a} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-4s}}{s}-\\frac{1}{s}-\\frac{1}{s}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }({e}^{-sa}-{e}^{-4s}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-4s}}{s}-\\frac{1}{s}-\\frac{1}{s}(0-{e}^{-4s}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-4s}}{s}-\\frac{1}{s}+\\frac{{e}^{-4s}}{s} \\\\  & =\\frac{2{e}^{-4s}}{s}-\\frac{1}{s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nvalid for $s>0.$" "We apply the definition of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =F(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(t){e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for the piecewise function \n\n$$\nf(t)={\\begin{aligned}-1,\\, & x\\le 4 \\\\ 1\\, & x>4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(t){e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}(-1)\\cdot {e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }1\\cdot {e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{4}{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the improper integral diverges if $s\\le 0.$\n\nFor $s>0,$ we calculate our Laplace transform as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & =-{\\int }_{0}^{4}{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s}{[{e}^{-st}]}_{0}^{4}+{\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-4s}}{s}-\\frac{1}{s}+\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{4}^{a}{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-4s}}{s}-\\frac{1}{s}+\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\left(-\\frac{{e}^{-st}}{s}\\right)|}_{4}^{a} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-4s}}{s}-\\frac{1}{s}-\\frac{1}{s}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }({e}^{-sa}-{e}^{-4s}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-4s}}{s}-\\frac{1}{s}-\\frac{1}{s}(0-{e}^{-4s}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-4s}}{s}-\\frac{1}{s}+\\frac{{e}^{-4s}}{s} \\\\  & =\\frac{2{e}^{-4s}}{s}-\\frac{1}{s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nvalid for $s>0.$"] [:db/cas 17592186315090 :question/worked-solution "We apply the definition of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =F(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(t){e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that the unit step function is defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t)={\\begin{aligned}0,\\, & t<0 \\\\ 1,\\, & t\\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for the piecewise function \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t-3)={\\begin{aligned}0,\\, & t<3 \\\\ 1,\\, & t\\ge 3\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }u(t-3){e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}0\\cdot {e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{3}^{\\infty }1\\cdot {e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that this improper integral diverges if $s\\le 0.$\n\nFor $s>0,$ we calculate this integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{3}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{3}^{a}{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\left(-\\frac{{e}^{-st}}{s}\\right)|}_{3}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{s}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }({e}^{-sa}-{e}^{-3s}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{s}(0-{e}^{-3s}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-3s}}{s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Laplace transform of $f(t)$ is\n\n$$\nL{f(t)}=\\frac{{e}^{-3s}}{s},\\,s>0.\n$$" "We apply the definition of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =F(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(t){e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that the unit step function is defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t)=\\begin{cases}0,\\hspace{1em} & t<0 \\\\ 1,\\hspace{1em} & t\\ge 0.\\end{cases}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for the piecewise function \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t-3)=\\begin{cases}0,\\hspace{1em} & t<3 \\\\ 1,\\hspace{1em} & t\\ge 3\\end{cases}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }u(t-3){e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}0\\cdot {e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{3}^{\\infty }1\\cdot {e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that this improper integral diverges if $s\\le 0.$\n\nFor $s>0,$ we calculate this integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{3}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{3}^{a}{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\left(-\\frac{{e}^{-st}}{s}\\right)|}_{3}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{s}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }({e}^{-sa}-{e}^{-3s}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{s}(0-{e}^{-3s}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-3s}}{s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Laplace transform of $f(t)$ is\n\n$$\nL{f(t)}=\\frac{{e}^{-3s}}{s},\\,s>0.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186315092 :question/worked-solution "We apply the definition of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =F(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(t){e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that the unit step function is defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t)={\\begin{aligned}0,\\, & t<0 \\\\ 1,\\, & t\\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for the piecewise function \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(8-t)={\\begin{aligned}1,\\, & t\\le 8 \\\\ 0,\\, & t>8\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }u(8-t){e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{8}1\\cdot {e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{8}^{\\infty }0\\cdot {e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{8}{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe compute this integral for $s>0$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{0}^{8}{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{{e}^{-st}}{s}]}_{0}^{8} \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{-8s}}{s}+\\frac{1}{s} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s}(1-{e}^{-8s})\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We apply the definition of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =F(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(t){e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that the unit step function is defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t)=\\begin{cases}0,\\hspace{1em} & t<0 \\\\ 1,\\hspace{1em} & t\\ge 0.\\end{cases}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for the piecewise function \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(8-t)=\\begin{cases}1,\\hspace{1em} & t\\le 8 \\\\ 0,\\hspace{1em} & t>8\\end{cases}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }u(8-t){e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{8}1\\cdot {e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{8}^{\\infty }0\\cdot {e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{8}{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe compute this integral for $s>0$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{0}^{8}{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{{e}^{-st}}{s}]}_{0}^{8} \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{-8s}}{s}+\\frac{1}{s} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s}(1-{e}^{-8s})\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186315100 :question/worked-solution "We apply the definition of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =F(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(t){e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for the piecewise function \n\n$$\nf(t)={\\begin{aligned}3,\\, & t\\le 5 \\\\ {e}^{3t}\\, & t>5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(t){e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{5}3\\cdot {e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{5}^{\\infty }{e}^{3t}\\cdot {e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{5}3\\cdot {e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{5}^{\\infty }{e}^{3t-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{5}3{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{5}^{\\infty }{e}^{(3-s)t}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the improper integral diverges if $s\\le 3.$\n\nFor $s>3,$ we calculate our Laplace transform as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{0}^{5}3{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{5}^{\\infty }{e}^{(3-s)t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{s}{[{e}^{-st}]}_{0}^{5}+{\\int }_{5}^{\\infty }{e}^{(3-s)t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{s}-\\frac{3{e}^{-5s}}{s}+{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{5}^{a}{e}^{(3-s)t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{s}-\\frac{3{e}^{-5s}}{s}+{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\left(\\frac{{e}^{(3-s)t}}{3-s}\\right)|}_{5}^{a} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{s}-\\frac{3{e}^{-5s}}{s}+\\frac{1}{3-s}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }({e}^{(3-s)a}-{e}^{5(3-s)}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{s}-\\frac{3{e}^{-5s}}{s}+\\frac{1}{3-s}\\cdot (0-{e}^{5(3-s)}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{s}-\\frac{3{e}^{-5s}}{s}+\\frac{{e}^{5(3-s)}}{s-3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nvalid for $s>3.$" "We apply the definition of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =F(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(t){e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for the piecewise function \n\n$$\nf(t)={\\begin{aligned}3,\\, & t\\le 5 \\\\ {e}^{3t}\\, & t>5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(t){e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{5}3\\cdot {e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{5}^{\\infty }{e}^{3t}\\cdot {e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{5}3\\cdot {e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{5}^{\\infty }{e}^{3t-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{5}3{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{5}^{\\infty }{e}^{(3-s)t}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the improper integral diverges if $s\\le 3.$\n\nFor $s>3,$ we calculate our Laplace transform as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{0}^{5}3{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{5}^{\\infty }{e}^{(3-s)t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{s}{[{e}^{-st}]}_{0}^{5}+{\\int }_{5}^{\\infty }{e}^{(3-s)t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{s}-\\frac{3{e}^{-5s}}{s}+\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{5}^{a}{e}^{(3-s)t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{s}-\\frac{3{e}^{-5s}}{s}+\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\left(\\frac{{e}^{(3-s)t}}{3-s}\\right)|}_{5}^{a} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{s}-\\frac{3{e}^{-5s}}{s}+\\frac{1}{3-s}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }({e}^{(3-s)a}-{e}^{5(3-s)}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{s}-\\frac{3{e}^{-5s}}{s}+\\frac{1}{3-s}\\cdot (0-{e}^{5(3-s)}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{s}-\\frac{3{e}^{-5s}}{s}+\\frac{{e}^{5(3-s)}}{s-3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nvalid for $s>3.$"] [:db/cas 17592186315101 :question/worked-solution "We apply the definition of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =F(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(t){e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for the piecewise function \n\n$$\nf(t)={\\begin{aligned}{e}^{3t},\\, & t\\le 2 \\\\ 2\\, & t>2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(t){e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{3t}\\cdot {e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }2\\cdot {e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{3t-st}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }2{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{(3-s)t}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }2{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the improper integral diverges if $s\\le 0.$\n\nFor $s>0,$ we calculate our Laplace transform as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{(3-s)t}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }2{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3-s}{[{e}^{(3-s)t}]}_{0}^{2}+{\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }2{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2(3-s)}-1}{3-s}+{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{2}^{a}2{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2(3-s)}-1}{3-s}+{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\left(\\frac{-2{e}^{-st}}{s}\\right)|}_{2}^{a} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2(3-s)}-1}{3-s}-\\frac{2}{s}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }({e}^{-as}-{e}^{-2s}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2(3-s)}-1}{3-s}-\\frac{2}{s}\\cdot (0-{e}^{-2s}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2(3-s)}-1}{3-s}+\\frac{2{e}^{-2s}}{s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nvalid for $s>0.$" "We apply the definition of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =F(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(t){e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for the piecewise function \n\n$$\nf(t)={\\begin{aligned}{e}^{3t},\\, & t\\le 2 \\\\ 2\\, & t>2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(t){e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{3t}\\cdot {e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }2\\cdot {e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{3t-st}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }2{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{(3-s)t}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }2{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the improper integral diverges if $s\\le 0.$\n\nFor $s>0,$ we calculate our Laplace transform as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{(3-s)t}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }2{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3-s}{[{e}^{(3-s)t}]}_{0}^{2}+{\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }2{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2(3-s)}-1}{3-s}+\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{2}^{a}2{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2(3-s)}-1}{3-s}+\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\left(\\frac{-2{e}^{-st}}{s}\\right)|}_{2}^{a} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2(3-s)}-1}{3-s}-\\frac{2}{s}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }({e}^{-as}-{e}^{-2s}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2(3-s)}-1}{3-s}-\\frac{2}{s}\\cdot (0-{e}^{-2s}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2(3-s)}-1}{3-s}+\\frac{2{e}^{-2s}}{s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nvalid for $s>0.$"] [:db/cas 17592186315102 :question/worked-solution "We apply the definition of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =F(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(t){e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for the piecewise function \n\n$$\nf(t)={\\begin{aligned}{e}^{5t},\\, & t\\le 1 \\\\ 1\\, & t>1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(t){e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{5t}\\cdot {e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }1\\cdot {e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{5t-st}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{(5-s)t}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the improper integral diverges if $s\\le 0.$\n\nFor $s>0,$ we calculate our Laplace transform as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{(5-s)t}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5-s}{[{e}^{(5-s)t}]}_{0}^{1}+{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{(5-s)}-1}{5-s}+{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{(5-s)}-1}{5-s}+{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\left(\\frac{-{e}^{-st}}{s}\\right)|}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{(5-s)}-1}{5-s}-\\frac{1}{s}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }({e}^{-as}-{e}^{-s}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{(5-s)}-1}{5-s}-\\frac{1}{s}\\cdot (0-{e}^{-s}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{(5-s)}-1}{5-s}+\\frac{{e}^{-s}}{s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nvalid for $s>0.$" "We apply the definition of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =F(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(t){e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for the piecewise function \n\n$$\nf(t)={\\begin{aligned}{e}^{5t},\\, & t\\le 1 \\\\ 1\\, & t>1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(t){e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{5t}\\cdot {e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }1\\cdot {e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{5t-st}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{(5-s)t}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the improper integral diverges if $s\\le 0.$\n\nFor $s>0,$ we calculate our Laplace transform as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{(5-s)t}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5-s}{[{e}^{(5-s)t}]}_{0}^{1}+{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{(5-s)}-1}{5-s}+\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{(5-s)}-1}{5-s}+\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\left(\\frac{-{e}^{-st}}{s}\\right)|}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{(5-s)}-1}{5-s}-\\frac{1}{s}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }({e}^{-as}-{e}^{-s}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{(5-s)}-1}{5-s}-\\frac{1}{s}\\cdot (0-{e}^{-s}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{(5-s)}-1}{5-s}+\\frac{{e}^{-s}}{s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nvalid for $s>0.$"] [:db/cas 17592186315103 :question/worked-solution "We apply the definition of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =F(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(t){e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for the piecewise function \n\n$$\nf(t)={\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t},\\, & t\\le 4 \\\\ 2\\, & t>4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(t){e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}{e}^{t}\\cdot {e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }2\\cdot {e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}{e}^{t-st}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }2{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}{e}^{(1-s)t}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }2{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the improper integral diverges if $s\\le 0.$\n\nFor $s>0,$ we calculate our Laplace transform as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}{e}^{(1-s)t}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }2{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1-s}{[{e}^{(1-s)t}]}_{0}^{4}+{\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }2{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{4(1-s)}-1}{1-s}+{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{4}^{a}2{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{4(1-s)}-1}{1-s}+{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\left(\\frac{-2{e}^{-st}}{s}\\right)|}_{4}^{a} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{4(1-s)}-1}{1-s}-\\frac{2}{s}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }({e}^{-as}-{e}^{-4s}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{4(1-s)}-1}{1-s}-\\frac{2}{s}\\cdot (0-{e}^{-4s}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{4(1-s)}-1}{1-s}+\\frac{2{e}^{-4s}}{s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nvalid for $s>0.$" "We apply the definition of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =F(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(t){e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for the piecewise function \n\n$$\nf(t)={\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t},\\, & t\\le 4 \\\\ 2\\, & t>4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(t){e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}{e}^{t}\\cdot {e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }2\\cdot {e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}{e}^{t-st}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }2{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}{e}^{(1-s)t}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }2{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the improper integral diverges if $s\\le 0.$\n\nFor $s>0,$ we calculate our Laplace transform as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}{e}^{(1-s)t}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }2{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1-s}{[{e}^{(1-s)t}]}_{0}^{4}+{\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }2{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{4(1-s)}-1}{1-s}+\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{4}^{a}2{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{4(1-s)}-1}{1-s}+\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\left(\\frac{-2{e}^{-st}}{s}\\right)|}_{4}^{a} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{4(1-s)}-1}{1-s}-\\frac{2}{s}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }({e}^{-as}-{e}^{-4s}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{4(1-s)}-1}{1-s}-\\frac{2}{s}\\cdot (0-{e}^{-4s}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{4(1-s)}-1}{1-s}+\\frac{2{e}^{-4s}}{s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nvalid for $s>0.$"] [:db/cas 17592186315105 :question/worked-solution "We apply the definition of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =F(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(t){e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for the piecewise function \n\n$$\nf(t)={\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-3t},\\, & t\\le 1 \\\\ 4\\, & t>1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(t){e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{-3t}\\cdot {e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }4\\cdot {e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{-3t-st}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }4\\cdot {e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{-(3+s)t}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }4{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the improper integral diverges if $s\\le 0.$\n\nFor $s>0,$ we calculate our Laplace transform as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{-(3+s)t}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }4{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{-(3+s)}{[{e}^{-(3+s)t}]}_{0}^{1}+{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }4{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1-{e}^{-(3+s)}}{3+s}+{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}4{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1-{e}^{-(3+s)}}{3+s}+{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\left(\\frac{-4{e}^{-st}}{s}\\right)|}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-{e}^{-(3+s)}}{3+s}-\\frac{4}{s}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }({e}^{-as}-{e}^{-s}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1-{e}^{-(3+s)}}{3+s}-\\frac{4}{s}\\cdot (0-{e}^{-s}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1-{e}^{-(3+s)}}{3+s}+\\frac{4{e}^{-s}}{s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nvalid for $s>0.$" "We apply the definition of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =F(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(t){e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for the piecewise function \n\n$$\nf(t)={\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-3t},\\, & t\\le 1 \\\\ 4\\, & t>1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(t){e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{-3t}\\cdot {e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }4\\cdot {e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{-3t-st}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }4\\cdot {e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{-(3+s)t}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }4{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the improper integral diverges if $s\\le 0.$\n\nFor $s>0,$ we calculate our Laplace transform as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{-(3+s)t}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }4{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{-(3+s)}{[{e}^{-(3+s)t}]}_{0}^{1}+{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }4{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1-{e}^{-(3+s)}}{3+s}+\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}4{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1-{e}^{-(3+s)}}{3+s}+\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\left(\\frac{-4{e}^{-st}}{s}\\right)|}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-{e}^{-(3+s)}}{3+s}-\\frac{4}{s}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }({e}^{-as}-{e}^{-s}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1-{e}^{-(3+s)}}{3+s}-\\frac{4}{s}\\cdot (0-{e}^{-s}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1-{e}^{-(3+s)}}{3+s}+\\frac{4{e}^{-s}}{s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nvalid for $s>0.$"] [:db/cas 17592186317223 :question/worked-solution "We're told that $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=3.$ According to the definition of continuity, this means that ${lim}_{x\\to 3}f(x)$ exists and\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to 3}f(x)=f(3)=-3.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}\\frac{x+(f(x))^{3}}{2} & =\\frac{3+(-3)^{3}}{2}=-12.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We're told that $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=3.$ According to the definition of continuity, this means that $\\lim_{x\\to 3}f(x)$ exists and\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {3}^{-}}f(x)=\\lim_{x\\to 3}f(x)=f(3)=-3.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {3}^{-}}\\frac{x+(f(x))^{3}}{2} & =\\frac{3+(-3)^{3}}{2}=-12.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186317224 :question/worked-solution "We're told that $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=6.$ According to the definition of continuity, this means that ${lim}_{x\\to 6}f(x)$ exists and\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {6}^{+}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to 6}f(x)=f(6)=14.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {6}^{+}}\\sqrt{f(x)+{x}^{2}-1} & =\\sqrt{14+{6}^{2}-1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{49} \\\\  & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We're told that $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=6.$ According to the definition of continuity, this means that $\\lim_{x\\to 6}f(x)$ exists and\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {6}^{+}}f(x)=\\lim_{x\\to 6}f(x)=f(6)=14.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {6}^{+}}\\sqrt{f(x)+{x}^{2}-1} & =\\sqrt{14+{6}^{2}-1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{49} \\\\  & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186317225 :question/worked-solution "We're told that $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=-2.$ According to the definition of continuity, this means that ${lim}_{x\\to -2}f(x)$ exists and\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -{2}^{-}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to -2}f(x)=f(-2)=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -{2}^{-}}({x}^{3}+2(f(x))^{2}) & =(-2)^{3}+2(-2)^{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We're told that $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=-2.$ According to the definition of continuity, this means that $\\lim_{x\\to -2}f(x)$ exists and\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -{2}^{-}}f(x)=\\lim_{x\\to -2}f(x)=f(-2)=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to -{2}^{-}}({x}^{3}+2(f(x))^{2}) & =(-2)^{3}+2(-2)^{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186317227 :question/worked-solution "We're told that $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=7.$ According to the definition of continuity, this means that ${lim}_{x\\to 7}f(x)$ exists and\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 7}f(x)=f(7)=5.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 7}\\frac{(f(x)+1)^{2}}{x-f(x)} & =\\frac{(5+1)^{2}}{7-5} \\\\  & =\\frac{36}{2} \\\\  & =18.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We're told that $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=7.$ According to the definition of continuity, this means that $\\lim_{x\\to 7}f(x)$ exists and\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 7}f(x)=f(7)=5.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 7}\\frac{(f(x)+1)^{2}}{x-f(x)} & =\\frac{(5+1)^{2}}{7-5} \\\\  & =\\frac{36}{2} \\\\  & =18.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186317256 :question/problem "$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}x+1, & x\\in [-2,0) \\\\ -2, & x\\in [0,2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above function $F(x)$ is defined on $x\\in [-2,2).$ Let $f(x)$ be the $4$-periodic extension of $F(x)$ to $x\\in R.$\nSelect the correct options below.\n\n| I. | $F(x)$ is continuous on $x\\in (-2,2)$ | {{field-1}} |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| II. | $F(x)$ is piecewise smooth on $x\\in (-2,2)$ | {{field-2}} |\n| III. | ${lim}_{x\\to -{2}^{+}}F(x)={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}F(x)$ | {{field-3}} |\n| IV. | $f(x)$ is piecewise smooth and continuous on $R$ | {{field-4}} |" "$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}x+1, & x\\in [-2,0) \\\\ -2, & x\\in [0,2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above function $F(x)$ is defined on $x\\in [-2,2).$ Let $f(x)$ be the $4$-periodic extension of $F(x)$ to $x\\in R.$\nSelect the correct options below.\n\n| I. | $F(x)$ is continuous on $x\\in (-2,2)$ | {{field-1}} |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| II. | $F(x)$ is piecewise smooth on $x\\in (-2,2)$ | {{field-2}} |\n| III. | $\\lim_{x\\to -{2}^{+}}F(x)=\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{-}}F(x)$ | {{field-3}} |\n| IV. | $f(x)$ is piecewise smooth and continuous on $R$ | {{field-4}} |"] [:db/cas 17592186317271 :question/problem "$$\nF(x)={\\begin{aligned}2x+3, & x\\in [-3,0) \\\\ (x-3)^{2}-3, & x\\in [0,3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above function $F(x)$ is defined on $x\\in [-3,3).$ Let $f(x)$ be the $6$-periodic extension of $F(x)$ to $x\\in R.$\nSelect the correct options below.\n\n| I. | $F(x)$ is continuous on $x\\in (-3,3)$ | {{field-1}} |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| II. | $F(x)$ is piecewise smooth on $x\\in (-3,3)$ | {{field-2}} |\n| III. | ${lim}_{x\\to -{3}^{+}}F(x)={lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}F(x)$ | {{field-3}} |\n| IV. | $f(x)$ is piecewise smooth and continuous on $R$ | {{field-4}} |" "$$\nF(x)={\\begin{aligned}2x+3, & x\\in [-3,0) \\\\ (x-3)^{2}-3, & x\\in [0,3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above function $F(x)$ is defined on $x\\in [-3,3).$ Let $f(x)$ be the $6$-periodic extension of $F(x)$ to $x\\in R.$\nSelect the correct options below.\n\n| I. | $F(x)$ is continuous on $x\\in (-3,3)$ | {{field-1}} |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| II. | $F(x)$ is piecewise smooth on $x\\in (-3,3)$ | {{field-2}} |\n| III. | $\\lim_{x\\to -{3}^{+}}F(x)=\\lim_{x\\to {3}^{-}}F(x)$ | {{field-3}} |\n| IV. | $f(x)$ is piecewise smooth and continuous on $R$ | {{field-4}} |"] [:db/cas 17592186317343 :question/problem "$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}2x+1, & x\\in [-3,0] \\\\ \\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}, & x\\in (0,3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above function $F(x)$ is defined on $x\\in [-3,3).$ Let $f(x)$ be the $6$-periodic extension of $F(x)$ to $x\\in R.$\nSelect the correct options below.\n\n| I. | $F(x)$ is continuous on $x\\in (-3,3)$ | {{field-1}} |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| II. | $F(x)$ is piecewise smooth on $x\\in (-3,3)$ | {{field-2}} |\n| III. | ${lim}_{x\\to -{3}^{+}}F(x)={lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}F(x)$ | {{field-3}} |\n| IV. | $f(x)$ is piecewise smooth and continuous on $R$ | {{field-4}} |" "$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}2x+1, & x\\in [-3,0] \\\\ \\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}, & x\\in (0,3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above function $F(x)$ is defined on $x\\in [-3,3).$ Let $f(x)$ be the $6$-periodic extension of $F(x)$ to $x\\in R.$\nSelect the correct options below.\n\n| I. | $F(x)$ is continuous on $x\\in (-3,3)$ | {{field-1}} |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| II. | $F(x)$ is piecewise smooth on $x\\in (-3,3)$ | {{field-2}} |\n| III. | $\\lim_{x\\to -{3}^{+}}F(x)=\\lim_{x\\to {3}^{-}}F(x)$ | {{field-3}} |\n| IV. | $f(x)$ is piecewise smooth and continuous on $R$ | {{field-4}} |"] [:db/cas 17592186317392 :question/worked-solution "Suppose that $f(x)$ is a $2L$-periodic piecewise smooth function with the corresponding Fourier series\n\n$$\nS(x)=\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x).\n$$\n\nThen, the series converges for all $x\\in R.$\n\n- If $f(x)$ is continuous at $x,$ then the series converges to the value of the function at this point:\n\n$$\nS(x)=f(x)\n$$\n\n- If $f(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x,$ then the series converges to the midpoint between the values of left- and right-sided limits of the function at this point:\n\n$$\nS(x)=\\frac{{f}^{-}(x)+{f}^{+}(x)}{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{-}(x)$ and ${f}^{+}(x)$ are the left- and right-sided limits of the function at this point, respectively.\n\nNotice that the $2\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of our function $F(x)$ is not continuous at $x=\\pi .$ The one-sided limits at this point are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-}(\\pi ) & ={lim}_{x\\to {\\pi }^{-}}f(x)=3 \\\\ {f}^{+}(\\pi ) & ={lim}_{x\\to {\\pi }^{+}}f(x)=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the Fourier series of $f(x)$ converges to \n\n$$\n\\frac{{f}^{-}(\\pi )+{f}^{+}(\\pi )}{2}=\\frac{3+(-1)}{2}=1.\n$$" "Suppose that $f(x)$ is a $2L$-periodic piecewise smooth function with the corresponding Fourier series\n\n$$\nS(x)=\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x).\n$$\n\nThen, the series converges for all $x\\in R.$\n\n- If $f(x)$ is continuous at $x,$ then the series converges to the value of the function at this point:\n\n$$\nS(x)=f(x)\n$$\n\n- If $f(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x,$ then the series converges to the midpoint between the values of left- and right-sided limits of the function at this point:\n\n$$\nS(x)=\\frac{{f}^{-}(x)+{f}^{+}(x)}{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{-}(x)$ and ${f}^{+}(x)$ are the left- and right-sided limits of the function at this point, respectively.\n\nNotice that the $2\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of our function $F(x)$ is not continuous at $x=\\pi .$ The one-sided limits at this point are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-}(\\pi ) & =\\lim_{x\\to {\\pi }^{-}}f(x)=3 \\\\ {f}^{+}(\\pi ) & =\\lim_{x\\to {\\pi }^{+}}f(x)=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the Fourier series of $f(x)$ converges to \n\n$$\n\\frac{{f}^{-}(\\pi )+{f}^{+}(\\pi )}{2}=\\frac{3+(-1)}{2}=1.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186317393 :question/worked-solution "Suppose that $f(x)$ is a $2L$-periodic piecewise smooth function with the corresponding Fourier series\n\n$$\nS(x)=\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x).\n$$\n\nThen, the series converges for all $x\\in R.$\n\n- If $f(x)$ is continuous at $x,$ then the series converges to the value of the function at this point:\n\n$$\nS(x)=f(x)\n$$\n\n- If $f(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x,$ then the series converges to the midpoint between the values of left- and right-sided limits of the function at this point:\n\n$$\nS(x)=\\frac{{f}^{-}(x)+{f}^{+}(x)}{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{-}(x)$ and ${f}^{+}(x)$ are the left- and right-sided limits of the function at this point, respectively.\n\nNotice that the $4$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of our function $F(x)$ is not continuous at $x=-2.$ The one-sided limits at this point are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-}(-2) & ={lim}_{x\\to -{2}^{-}}f(x)=1 \\\\ {f}^{+}(-2) & ={lim}_{x\\to -{2}^{+}}f(x)={e}^{-2+2}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the Fourier series of $f(x)$ converges to \n\n$$\n\\frac{{f}^{-}(-2)+{f}^{+}(-2)}{2}=\\frac{1+1}{2}=1.\n$$" "Suppose that $f(x)$ is a $2L$-periodic piecewise smooth function with the corresponding Fourier series\n\n$$\nS(x)=\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x).\n$$\n\nThen, the series converges for all $x\\in R.$\n\n- If $f(x)$ is continuous at $x,$ then the series converges to the value of the function at this point:\n\n$$\nS(x)=f(x)\n$$\n\n- If $f(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x,$ then the series converges to the midpoint between the values of left- and right-sided limits of the function at this point:\n\n$$\nS(x)=\\frac{{f}^{-}(x)+{f}^{+}(x)}{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{-}(x)$ and ${f}^{+}(x)$ are the left- and right-sided limits of the function at this point, respectively.\n\nNotice that the $4$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of our function $F(x)$ is not continuous at $x=-2.$ The one-sided limits at this point are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-}(-2) & =\\lim_{x\\to -{2}^{-}}f(x)=1 \\\\ {f}^{+}(-2) & =\\lim_{x\\to -{2}^{+}}f(x)={e}^{-2+2}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the Fourier series of $f(x)$ converges to \n\n$$\n\\frac{{f}^{-}(-2)+{f}^{+}(-2)}{2}=\\frac{1+1}{2}=1.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186317467 :question/worked-solution "First, let's attempt to evaluate the limit by direct substitution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (\\pi /2)^{+}}\\operatorname{cot}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡0 \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡0}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{0},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined.\n\nTherefore, to find the limit, let's sketch the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$\n\n![](images/1b/1b564f91cb71a17a1449e1ff723e32f450da6304d9c3ba33cd01e2ff942bc25c.png)\n\nAs $x$ approaches $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ from the right, the values of $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ are positive and grow without bound. Therefore\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (\\pi /2)^{+}}\\operatorname{cot}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})=\\infty .\n$$" "First, let's attempt to evaluate the limit by direct substitution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (\\pi /2)^{+}}\\operatorname{cot}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡0 \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡0}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{0},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined.\n\nTherefore, to find the limit, let's sketch the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$\n\n![](images/1b/1b564f91cb71a17a1449e1ff723e32f450da6304d9c3ba33cd01e2ff942bc25c.png)\n\nAs $x$ approaches $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ from the right, the values of $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ are positive and grow without bound. Therefore\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (\\pi /2)^{+}}\\operatorname{cot}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})=\\infty .\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186317470 :question/worked-solution "Substituting $x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ directly into the limit, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\pi /6}\\frac{\\operatorname{cot}⁡(3x)-\\sqrt{3}}{2} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cot}⁡(3\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6})-\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)-\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)}-\\sqrt{3})}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{0}{1}-\\sqrt{3})}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Substituting $x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ directly into the limit, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\pi /6}\\frac{\\operatorname{cot}⁡(3x)-\\sqrt{3}}{2} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cot}⁡(3\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6})-\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)-\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)}-\\sqrt{3})}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{0}{1}-\\sqrt{3})}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186317512 :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x):$\n\n![](images/ca/ca5e8035505f20619b6707d6d1f2d0b7b2f125a004a0dbd9d448840b0e4ffd99.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)$ is $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nNow, substituting $x=\\sqrt{3}$ directly into the limit, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,\\sqrt{3}}tan\\,(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)-\\frac{\\pi }{6}) & =tan\\,(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{3})-\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let's sketch the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x):$\n\n![](images/ca/ca5e8035505f20619b6707d6d1f2d0b7b2f125a004a0dbd9d448840b0e4ffd99.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)$ is $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nNow, substituting $x=\\sqrt{3}$ directly into the limit, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\,\\sqrt{3}}tan\\,(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)-\\frac{\\pi }{6}) & =tan\\,(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{3})-\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186319070 :question/worked-solution "The definite integral can be defined in terms of the limit of a left Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\n    \n\nwhere $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ and ${x}_{i}=a+iΔx.$\n\nIn the definite integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{2}({x}^{3}+2x+1)\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nthe function is $f(x)={x}^{3}+2x+1,$ the lower bound is $a=0,$ and the upper bound is $b=2.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{2-0}{n}=\\frac{2}{n},\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}({x}^{3}+2x+1)\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[f(0+i\\cdot \\frac{2}{n})\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[f\\left(\\frac{2i}{n}\\right)\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[({(\\,\\frac{2i}{n}\\,)}^{3}+2(\\,\\frac{2i}{n}\\,)+1)\\,\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The definite integral can be defined in terms of the limit of a left Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\n    \n\nwhere $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ and ${x}_{i}=a+iΔx.$\n\nIn the definite integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{2}({x}^{3}+2x+1)\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nthe function is $f(x)={x}^{3}+2x+1,$ the lower bound is $a=0,$ and the upper bound is $b=2.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{2-0}{n}=\\frac{2}{n},\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}({x}^{3}+2x+1)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[f(0+i\\cdot \\frac{2}{n})\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}] \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[f\\left(\\frac{2i}{n}\\right)\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}] \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[({(\\,\\frac{2i}{n}\\,)}^{3}+2(\\,\\frac{2i}{n}\\,)+1)\\,\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186319085 :question/worked-solution "The definite integral can be defined in terms of the limit of a right Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{i}=a+iΔx$ and $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}.$\n\nIn the definite integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\sqrt{x+1}\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nthe function is $f(x)=\\sqrt{x+1},$ the lower bound is $a=0$ and the upper bound is $b=3.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{3-0}{n}=\\frac{3}{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\sqrt{x+1}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}[f(0+i\\cdot \\frac{3}{n})\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}[f\\left(\\frac{3i}{n}\\right)\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}[\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{3i}{n}\\right)+1}\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}[\\,\\sqrt{\\frac{3i}{n}+1}\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The definite integral can be defined in terms of the limit of a right Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{i}=a+iΔx$ and $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}.$\n\nIn the definite integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\sqrt{x+1}\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nthe function is $f(x)=\\sqrt{x+1},$ the lower bound is $a=0$ and the upper bound is $b=3.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{3-0}{n}=\\frac{3}{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\sqrt{x+1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}[f(0+i\\cdot \\frac{3}{n})\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}] \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}[f\\left(\\frac{3i}{n}\\right)\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}] \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}[\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{3i}{n}\\right)+1}\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}] \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}[\\,\\sqrt{\\frac{3i}{n}+1}\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186319126 :question/worked-solution "We proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-2}4{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =4\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{-2}{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[{e}^{x}]}_{a}^{-2} \\\\  & =4\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }({e}^{-2}-{e}^{a}) \\\\  & =4\\cdot (\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}}-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{e}^{a})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y={e}^{x}$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{e}^{x}=0$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{e}^{x}=\\infty $\n\n![](images/4a/4af4a2b7389d3773cc71e843190c6ab111355248a15e6165a8e39e37252061b9.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-2}4{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =4\\cdot (\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}}-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{e}^{a}) \\\\  & =4(\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{{e}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-2}4{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =4\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{-2}{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[{e}^{x}]}_{a}^{-2} \\\\  & =4\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }({e}^{-2}-{e}^{a}) \\\\  & =4\\cdot (\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}}-\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{e}^{a})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y={e}^{x}$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }{e}^{x}=0$\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }{e}^{x}=\\infty $\n\n![](images/4a/4af4a2b7389d3773cc71e843190c6ab111355248a15e6165a8e39e37252061b9.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-2}4{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =4\\cdot (\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}}-\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{e}^{a}) \\\\  & =4(\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{{e}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186319127 :question/worked-solution "We proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}{5}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{1}{5}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{{5}^{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}]}_{a}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[{5}^{x}]}_{a}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(5-{5}^{a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}\\cdot (5-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{5}^{a})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y={5}^{x}$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{5}^{x}=0$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{5}^{x}=\\infty $\n\n![](images/83/83f7d663fa66184b56c4f79d61f6e17012714a3de7512202b3022e91d4325437.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}{5}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}\\cdot (5-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{5}^{a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}\\cdot (5-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}{5}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{1}{5}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{{5}^{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}]}_{a}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[{5}^{x}]}_{a}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(5-{5}^{a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}\\cdot (5-\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{5}^{a})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y={5}^{x}$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }{5}^{x}=0$\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }{5}^{x}=\\infty $\n\n![](images/83/83f7d663fa66184b56c4f79d61f6e17012714a3de7512202b3022e91d4325437.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}{5}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}\\cdot (5-\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{5}^{a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}\\cdot (5-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186319128 :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u={x}^{3}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=3{x}^{2}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u={x}^{2}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to -\\infty $ when $x\\to -\\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u={x}^{3}$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $-\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $-\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}{x}^{2}\\cdot {5}^{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}{5}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}{x}^{2}\\cdot {5}^{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{3}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}{5}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{{5}^{u}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}]}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[{5}^{u}]_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(1-{5}^{a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}(1-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{5}^{a})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, recall the end behavior of the function $y={5}^{a},$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{5}^{a}=0$\n\n- ${lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{5}^{a}=\\infty $\n\n![](images/c0/c0c7699b036ebd8779cd6c3d3143cdcca6312834ee731e144e38488124164f43.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}{x}^{2}\\cdot {5}^{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}(1-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{5}^{a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}(1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we substitute $u={x}^{3}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=3{x}^{2}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u={x}^{2}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to -\\infty $ when $x\\to -\\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u={x}^{3}$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $-\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $-\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}{x}^{2}\\cdot {5}^{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}{5}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}{x}^{2}\\cdot {5}^{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{3}\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}{5}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{{5}^{u}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}]}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }[{5}^{u}]_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }(1-{5}^{a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}(1-\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{5}^{a})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, recall the end behavior of the function $y={5}^{a},$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{5}^{a}=0$\n\n- $\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{5}^{a}=\\infty $\n\n![](images/c0/c0c7699b036ebd8779cd6c3d3143cdcca6312834ee731e144e38488124164f43.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}{x}^{2}\\cdot {5}^{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}(1-\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{5}^{a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}(1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186319155 :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1+(kx)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{k}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(kx)+C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{3}/9}^{\\infty }\\frac{15}{9{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x & =15\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{\\sqrt{3}/9}^{a}\\frac{1}{9{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =15\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{\\sqrt{3}/9}^{a}\\frac{1}{(3x)^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =15\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3x)]}_{\\sqrt{3}/9}^{a} \\\\  & =5\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3a)-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\right)) \\\\  & =5\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3a)-\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =5\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3a)-\\frac{5\\pi }{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3x),$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3x)=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3x)=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n![](images/9f/9f882cb5102270dfe4bef256782c18aaeeca3518f564cd188298040237e30f62.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{3}/9}^{\\infty }\\frac{15}{9{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x & =5\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3a)-\\frac{5\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\frac{5\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1+(kx)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{k}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(kx)+C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{3}/9}^{\\infty }\\frac{15}{9{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x & =15\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{\\sqrt{3}/9}^{a}\\frac{1}{9{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =15\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{\\sqrt{3}/9}^{a}\\frac{1}{(3x)^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =15\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3x)]}_{\\sqrt{3}/9}^{a} \\\\  & =5\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3a)-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\right)) \\\\  & =5\\cdot (\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3a)-\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =5\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3a)-\\frac{5\\pi }{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3x),$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3x)=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3x)=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n![](images/9f/9f882cb5102270dfe4bef256782c18aaeeca3518f564cd188298040237e30f62.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{3}/9}^{\\infty }\\frac{15}{9{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x & =5\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3a)-\\frac{5\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\frac{5\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186319156 :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1+(kx)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{k}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(kx)+C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1/2}^{\\infty }\\frac{18}{4{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x & =18\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1/2}^{a}\\frac{1}{4{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =18\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1/2}^{a}\\frac{1}{(2x)^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =18\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2x)]}_{1/2}^{a} \\\\  & =9\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2a)-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1)) \\\\  & =9\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2a)-\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =9\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2a)-\\frac{9\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2x),$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2x)=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2x)=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n![](images/2a/2ad762128a32c119f1783b88b4d10ae6820a914689296f03eedad206e097a29d.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1/2}^{\\infty }\\frac{18}{4{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x & =9\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2a)-\\frac{9\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =9\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\frac{9\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{9\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1+(kx)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{k}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(kx)+C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1/2}^{\\infty }\\frac{18}{4{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x & =18\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1/2}^{a}\\frac{1}{4{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =18\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1/2}^{a}\\frac{1}{(2x)^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =18\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2x)]}_{1/2}^{a} \\\\  & =9\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2a)-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1)) \\\\  & =9\\cdot (\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2a)-\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =9\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2a)-\\frac{9\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2x),$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2x)=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2x)=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n![](images/2a/2ad762128a32c119f1783b88b4d10ae6820a914689296f03eedad206e097a29d.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1/2}^{\\infty }\\frac{18}{4{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x & =9\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2a)-\\frac{9\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =9\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\frac{9\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{9\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186319158 :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u=\\sqrt{x}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}}\\,⟹\\,2\\,\\text{d}u=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty $ when $x\\to \\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=\\sqrt{x}$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $3$ | $\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $\\sqrt{3}$ | $\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{3}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}(x+1)}\\,\\text{d}x & =2{\\int }_{\\sqrt{3}}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{3}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}(x+1)}\\,\\text{d}x & =2{\\int }_{\\sqrt{3}}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{\\sqrt{3}}^{a}\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u\\,{|}_{\\sqrt{3}}^{a} \\\\  & =2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡a-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{3})) \\\\  & =2\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡a-\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x),$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n![](images/28/28afd0a065525cd225377165c2164a1ecc254b1be8aba60d64324d14430c3be4.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{3}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}(x+1)}\\,\\text{d}x & =2\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡a-\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =2\\cdot (\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we substitute $u=\\sqrt{x}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}}\\,⟹\\,2\\,\\text{d}u=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty $ when $x\\to \\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=\\sqrt{x}$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $3$ | $\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $\\sqrt{3}$ | $\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{3}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}(x+1)}\\,\\text{d}x & =2{\\int }_{\\sqrt{3}}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{3}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}(x+1)}\\,\\text{d}x & =2{\\int }_{\\sqrt{3}}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{\\sqrt{3}}^{a}\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u\\,{|}_{\\sqrt{3}}^{a} \\\\  & =2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡a-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{3})) \\\\  & =2\\cdot (\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡a-\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x),$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n![](images/28/28afd0a065525cd225377165c2164a1ecc254b1be8aba60d64324d14430c3be4.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{3}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}(x+1)}\\,\\text{d}x & =2\\cdot (\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡a-\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =2\\cdot (\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186319184 :question/worked-solution "To apply the ratio test, we define ${a}_{n}=\\frac{{x}^{n}}{{4}^{n}(5n-2)}.$ Then, we calculate the ratio of two successive terms and take the limit as $n\\to \\infty :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{x}^{n+1}}{{4}^{n+1}(5n+3)}\\cdot \\frac{{4}^{n}(5n-2)}{{x}^{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{x(5n-2)}{4(5n+3)}| \\\\  & =\\frac{|x|}{4}{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{5n-2}{5n+3} \\\\  & =\\frac{|x|}{4}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{|x|}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe series converges if $L<1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{|x|}{4}<1\\,⟹\\,|x|<4.\n$$\n\nSo, the radius of convergence is $4,$ and the series converges for any $x$ that lies in the open interval $(-4,4).$\n\nThe ratio test gives no conclusion about the convergence of the series when $L=1.$ So, when $|x|=4,$ we do not know if the series converges. This means that the endpoints $x=-4$ and $x=4$ need to be investigated separately using another test for convergence.\n\n- When $x=4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{x}^{n}}{{4}^{n}(5n-2)} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{4}^{n}}{{4}^{n}(5n-2)} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{5n-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{1}{8}+\\frac{1}{13}+⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo see if this series converges, we use the limit comparison test, with ${a}_{n}=\\frac{1}{5n-2}$ and ${b}_{n}=\\frac{1}{n}.$ We observe that ${a}_{n}$ and ${b}_{n}$ are positive, and\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{{a}_{n}}{{b}_{n}}={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\left(\\frac{1}{5n-2}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{1}{n}\\right)}={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{n}{5n-2}=\\frac{1}{5},\n$$\n\n \nwhich is positive and finite. Hence, the series $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\n$$ either *both* converge or *both* diverge.\nSince $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{n}\n$$ is divergent, the series $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{5n-2}\n$$ is also divergent by the limit comparison test. So, the endpoint $x=4$ is not included in our interval.\n\n- When $x=-4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{x}^{n}}{{4}^{n}(5n-2)} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-4)^{n}}{{4}^{n}(5n-2)} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{5n-2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{1}{8}-\\frac{1}{13}+⋯,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is convergent by the alternating series test. So, the endpoint $x=-4$ is included in our interval.\n\nTherefore, the interval of convergence is $[-4,4),$ or equivalently, $-4\\le x<4.$" "To apply the ratio test, we define ${a}_{n}=\\frac{{x}^{n}}{{4}^{n}(5n-2)}.$ Then, we calculate the ratio of two successive terms and take the limit as $n\\to \\infty :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{x}^{n+1}}{{4}^{n+1}(5n+3)}\\cdot \\frac{{4}^{n}(5n-2)}{{x}^{n}}| \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{x(5n-2)}{4(5n+3)}| \\\\  & =\\frac{|x|}{4}\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{5n-2}{5n+3} \\\\  & =\\frac{|x|}{4}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{|x|}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe series converges if $L<1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{|x|}{4}<1\\,⟹\\,|x|<4.\n$$\n\nSo, the radius of convergence is $4,$ and the series converges for any $x$ that lies in the open interval $(-4,4).$\n\nThe ratio test gives no conclusion about the convergence of the series when $L=1.$ So, when $|x|=4,$ we do not know if the series converges. This means that the endpoints $x=-4$ and $x=4$ need to be investigated separately using another test for convergence.\n\n- When $x=4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{x}^{n}}{{4}^{n}(5n-2)} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{4}^{n}}{{4}^{n}(5n-2)} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{5n-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{1}{8}+\\frac{1}{13}+⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo see if this series converges, we use the limit comparison test, with ${a}_{n}=\\frac{1}{5n-2}$ and ${b}_{n}=\\frac{1}{n}.$ We observe that ${a}_{n}$ and ${b}_{n}$ are positive, and\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{{a}_{n}}{{b}_{n}}=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\left(\\frac{1}{5n-2}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{1}{n}\\right)}=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{n}{5n-2}=\\frac{1}{5},\n$$\n\n \nwhich is positive and finite. Hence, the series $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\n$$ either *both* converge or *both* diverge.\nSince $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{n}\n$$ is divergent, the series $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{5n-2}\n$$ is also divergent by the limit comparison test. So, the endpoint $x=4$ is not included in our interval.\n\n- When $x=-4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{x}^{n}}{{4}^{n}(5n-2)} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-4)^{n}}{{4}^{n}(5n-2)} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{5n-2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{1}{8}-\\frac{1}{13}+⋯,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is convergent by the alternating series test. So, the endpoint $x=-4$ is included in our interval.\n\nTherefore, the interval of convergence is $[-4,4),$ or equivalently, $-4\\le x<4.$"] [:db/cas 17592186319185 :question/worked-solution "To apply the ratio test, we define ${a}_{n}={n}^{3}(x-2)^{n}.$ Then, we calculate the ratio of two successive terms and take the limit as $n\\to \\infty :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{(n+1)^{3}(x-2)^{n+1}}{{n}^{3}(x-2)^{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{(n+1)^{3}(x-2)}{{n}^{3}}| \\\\  & =|x-2|{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\left(\\frac{n+1}{n}\\right)}^{3} \\\\  & =|x-2|\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =|x-2|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe series converges if $L<1.$ In this case, the convergence condition is satisfied if $|x-2|<1.$ Therefore, we conclude that the radius of convergence is $R=1.$" "To apply the ratio test, we define ${a}_{n}={n}^{3}(x-2)^{n}.$ Then, we calculate the ratio of two successive terms and take the limit as $n\\to \\infty :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{(n+1)^{3}(x-2)^{n+1}}{{n}^{3}(x-2)^{n}}| \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{(n+1)^{3}(x-2)}{{n}^{3}}| \\\\  & =|x-2|\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\left(\\frac{n+1}{n}\\right)}^{3} \\\\  & =|x-2|\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =|x-2|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe series converges if $L<1.$ In this case, the convergence condition is satisfied if $|x-2|<1.$ Therefore, we conclude that the radius of convergence is $R=1.$"] [:db/cas 17592186319189 :question/worked-solution "We break up the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}+25}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}+25}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}+25}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nEvaluating the first of these integrals, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}+25} & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}+25}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{25}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{1}{(x/5)^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{25}\\cdot 5{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }({\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{5}\\right)|}_{a}^{0}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡0-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{5}\\right)) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}(0-\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)) \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the integrand is an **even** function. This means that we can immediately deduce that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}+25}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{3\\pi }{10}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since both improper integrals exist, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}+25}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}+25}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}+25}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{10}+\\frac{3\\pi }{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We break up the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}+25}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}+25}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}+25}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nEvaluating the first of these integrals, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}+25} & =\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}+25}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{25}\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{1}{(x/5)^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{25}\\cdot 5\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }({\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{5}\\right)|}_{a}^{0}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡0-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{5}\\right)) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}(0-\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)) \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the integrand is an **even** function. This means that we can immediately deduce that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}+25}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{3\\pi }{10}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since both improper integrals exist, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}+25}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}+25}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}+25}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{10}+\\frac{3\\pi }{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186319192 :question/worked-solution "We break up the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{8{x}^{7}}{{(5+{x}^{8})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{8{x}^{7}}{{(5+{x}^{8})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{8{x}^{7}}{{(5+{x}^{8})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the antiderivative using a substitution. Let  $u=5+{x}^{8}$,  then $\\text{d}u=8{x}^{7}\\text{d}x.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{8{x}^{7}}{{(5+{x}^{8})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{u}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5+{x}^{8}}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now evaluate the first of the improper integrals, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{8{x}^{7}}{{(5+{x}^{8})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{8{x}^{7}}{{(5+{x}^{8})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\left(-\\frac{1}{5+{x}^{8}}\\right)|}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\frac{1}{5+0}-\\frac{1}{5+{a}^{8}}) \\\\  & =-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\frac{1}{5}-\\frac{1}{5+{a}^{8}}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}+0 \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the integrand is an **odd** function. This means that we can immediately deduce that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{8{x}^{7}}{{(5+{x}^{8})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since both improper integrals exist, we conclude that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{8{x}^{7}}{{(5+{x}^{8})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{8{x}^{7}}{{(5+{x}^{8})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{8{x}^{7}}{{(5+{x}^{8})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}+\\frac{1}{5} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We break up the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{8{x}^{7}}{{(5+{x}^{8})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{8{x}^{7}}{{(5+{x}^{8})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{8{x}^{7}}{{(5+{x}^{8})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the antiderivative using a substitution. Let  $u=5+{x}^{8}$,  then $\\text{d}u=8{x}^{7}\\text{d}x.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{8{x}^{7}}{{(5+{x}^{8})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{u}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5+{x}^{8}}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now evaluate the first of the improper integrals, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{8{x}^{7}}{{(5+{x}^{8})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{8{x}^{7}}{{(5+{x}^{8})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\left(-\\frac{1}{5+{x}^{8}}\\right)|}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =-\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\frac{1}{5+0}-\\frac{1}{5+{a}^{8}}) \\\\  & =-\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\frac{1}{5}-\\frac{1}{5+{a}^{8}}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}+0 \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the integrand is an **odd** function. This means that we can immediately deduce that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{8{x}^{7}}{{(5+{x}^{8})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since both improper integrals exist, we conclude that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{8{x}^{7}}{{(5+{x}^{8})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{8{x}^{7}}{{(5+{x}^{8})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{8{x}^{7}}{{(5+{x}^{8})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}+\\frac{1}{5} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186319198 :question/worked-solution "First, let's rewrite the integral using a substitution. Let $u=3x-2.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=3\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{3}\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWe use the table below to change the limits:\n\n| $x$ | $\\frac{2}{3}$ | $22$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $64$ |\n\nCarrying out the change of variable, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2/3}^{22}\\frac{1}{(3x-2)^{1/3}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{64}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{3{u}^{1/3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{64}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{{u}^{1/3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an improper integral of the second kind because the function under the integral is unbounded at the *lower* limit of integration.\n\n$$\nf(u)=\\frac{1}{{u}^{1/3}}\\to \\infty \\,\\text{ as }\\,u\\to {0}^{+}.\n$$\n\nNonetheless, it is possible to calculate the integral by setting the *lower* bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to {0}^{+}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{64}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{{u}^{1/3}} & ={lim}_{a\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{a}^{64}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{{u}^{1/3}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{a}^{64}{u}^{-1/3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {\\frac{3}{2}\\,{u}^{2/3}|}_{a}^{64} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{1}{2}{\\sqrt[3]{{u}^{2}}|}_{a}^{64} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to {0}^{+}}[\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt[3]{{64}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt[3]{{a}^{2}}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 16-0 \\\\  & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{2/3}^{22}\\frac{1}{(3x-2)^{1/3}}\\,\\text{d}x=8.\n$$" "First, let's rewrite the integral using a substitution. Let $u=3x-2.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=3\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{3}\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWe use the table below to change the limits:\n\n| $x$ | $\\frac{2}{3}$ | $22$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $64$ |\n\nCarrying out the change of variable, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2/3}^{22}\\frac{1}{(3x-2)^{1/3}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{64}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{3{u}^{1/3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{64}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{{u}^{1/3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an improper integral of the second kind because the function under the integral is unbounded at the *lower* limit of integration.\n\n$$\nf(u)=\\frac{1}{{u}^{1/3}}\\to \\infty \\,\\text{ as }\\,u\\to {0}^{+}.\n$$\n\nNonetheless, it is possible to calculate the integral by setting the *lower* bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to {0}^{+}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{64}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{{u}^{1/3}} & =\\lim_{a\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{a}^{64}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{{u}^{1/3}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{a}^{64}{u}^{-1/3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {\\frac{3}{2}\\,{u}^{2/3}|}_{a}^{64} \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{1}{2}{\\sqrt[3]{{u}^{2}}|}_{a}^{64} \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to {0}^{+}}[\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt[3]{{64}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt[3]{{a}^{2}}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 16-0 \\\\  & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{2/3}^{22}\\frac{1}{(3x-2)^{1/3}}\\,\\text{d}x=8.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186319214 :question/worked-solution "To determine whether the sequence converges or diverges, we need to determine the limit of the sequence as $n$ approaches infinity:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{-8n+7}{4n}.\n$$\n\nThe dominant term in the denominator is $4n,$ so we divide the numerator and the denominator by $n$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n} & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{-8n+7}{4n} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{-8n}{n}+\\frac{7}{n})}{\\frac{4n}{n}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{-8+\\frac{7}{n}}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{-8+0}{4} \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the sequence converges, and its limit is $-2.$" "To determine whether the sequence converges or diverges, we need to determine the limit of the sequence as $n$ approaches infinity:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{-8n+7}{4n}.\n$$\n\nThe dominant term in the denominator is $4n,$ so we divide the numerator and the denominator by $n$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n} & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{-8n+7}{4n} \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{-8n}{n}+\\frac{7}{n})}{\\frac{4n}{n}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{-8+\\frac{7}{n}}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{-8+0}{4} \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the sequence converges, and its limit is $-2.$"] [:db/cas 17592186319215 :question/worked-solution "To determine whether the sequence converges or diverges, we need to determine the limit of the sequence as $n$ approaches infinity:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{5{n}^{2}+7}{3{n}^{3}-2}.\n$$\n\nThe dominant term in the denominator is $3{n}^{3},$ so we divide the numerator and the denominator by ${n}^{3}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n} & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{5{n}^{2}+7}{3{n}^{3}-2} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{5{n}^{2}}{{n}^{3}}+\\frac{7}{{n}^{3}})}{(\\frac{3{n}^{3}}{{n}^{3}}-\\frac{2}{{n}^{3}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\frac{5}{n}+\\frac{7}{{n}^{3}}}{3-\\frac{2}{{n}^{3}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{0+0}{3-0} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the limit exists, the sequence converges, and its limit is $0.$" "To determine whether the sequence converges or diverges, we need to determine the limit of the sequence as $n$ approaches infinity:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{5{n}^{2}+7}{3{n}^{3}-2}.\n$$\n\nThe dominant term in the denominator is $3{n}^{3},$ so we divide the numerator and the denominator by ${n}^{3}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n} & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{5{n}^{2}+7}{3{n}^{3}-2} \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{5{n}^{2}}{{n}^{3}}+\\frac{7}{{n}^{3}})}{(\\frac{3{n}^{3}}{{n}^{3}}-\\frac{2}{{n}^{3}})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\frac{5}{n}+\\frac{7}{{n}^{3}}}{3-\\frac{2}{{n}^{3}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{0+0}{3-0} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the limit exists, the sequence converges, and its limit is $0.$"] [:db/cas 17592186319216 :question/worked-solution "To determine whether the sequence converges or diverges, we need to determine the limit of the sequence as $n$ approaches infinity:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{2-7{n}^{3}}{4{n}^{4}+n}.\n$$\n\nThe dominant term in the denominator is $4{n}^{4},$ so we divide the numerator and the denominator by ${n}^{4}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n} & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{2-7{n}^{3}}{4{n}^{4}+n} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{2}{{n}^{4}}-\\frac{7{n}^{3}}{{n}^{4}})}{(\\frac{4{n}^{4}}{{n}^{4}}+\\frac{n}{{n}^{4}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\frac{2}{{n}^{4}}-\\frac{7}{n}}{4+\\frac{1}{{n}^{3}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{0-0}{4+0} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the limit exists, the sequence converges, and its limit is $0.$" "To determine whether the sequence converges or diverges, we need to determine the limit of the sequence as $n$ approaches infinity:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{2-7{n}^{3}}{4{n}^{4}+n}.\n$$\n\nThe dominant term in the denominator is $4{n}^{4},$ so we divide the numerator and the denominator by ${n}^{4}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n} & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{2-7{n}^{3}}{4{n}^{4}+n} \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{2}{{n}^{4}}-\\frac{7{n}^{3}}{{n}^{4}})}{(\\frac{4{n}^{4}}{{n}^{4}}+\\frac{n}{{n}^{4}})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\frac{2}{{n}^{4}}-\\frac{7}{n}}{4+\\frac{1}{{n}^{3}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{0-0}{4+0} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the limit exists, the sequence converges, and its limit is $0.$"] [:db/cas 17592186319218 :question/worked-solution "First, we multiply out the numerator and denominator of the sequence and get\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{(5{n}^{2}-4)(5{n}^{2}+4)}{{n}^{2}(3-40{n}^{2})}=\\frac{25{n}^{4}-16}{3{n}^{2}-40{n}^{4}}.\n$$\n\nSo to find the limit, we will need to compute\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{25{n}^{4}-16}{3{n}^{2}-40{n}^{4}}.\n$$\n\nThe dominant term in the denominator is $-40{n}^{4},$ so we divide the numerator and the denominator by ${n}^{4}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n} & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{25{n}^{4}-16}{3{n}^{2}-40{n}^{4}} \\\\  & ={li