heta -1.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}\\theta }=5\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=5\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to -\\infty $ when $\\theta \\to -\\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=5\\theta -1$ is as follows:\n\n| $\\theta $ | $-\\infty $ | $0$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $-\\infty $ | $-1$ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{12}{(5\\theta -1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{12}{5{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u\n$$\n\nFinally, we proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{12}{5{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u & =\\frac{12}{5}\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{-1}{u}^{-2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{12}{5}\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{[-{u}^{-1}]}_{a}^{-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{12}{5}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{1}{u}]}_{a}^{-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{12}{5}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\frac{1}{(-1)}-\\frac{1}{a}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{12}{5}\\cdot (-1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{12}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186320716 :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u={x}^{5}-2.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=5{x}^{4}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=5{x}^{4}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to -\\infty $ when $x\\to -\\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u={x}^{5}-2$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $-\\infty $ | $-1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $-\\infty $ | $-3$ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{20{x}^{4}}{({x}^{5}-2)^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-3}\\frac{4}{{u}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}u\n$$\n\nFinally, we proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-3}\\frac{4}{{u}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}u & =4{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{-3}{u}^{-4}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =4{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{3}{u}^{-3}]}_{a}^{-3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{1}{{u}^{3}}]}_{a}^{-3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\frac{1}{(-3)^{3}}-\\frac{1}{{a}^{3}}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{3}\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{27}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{81}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we substitute $u={x}^{5}-2.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=5{x}^{4}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=5{x}^{4}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to -\\infty $ when $x\\to -\\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u={x}^{5}-2$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $-\\infty $ | $-1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $-\\infty $ | $-3$ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{20{x}^{4}}{({x}^{5}-2)^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-3}\\frac{4}{{u}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}u\n$$\n\nFinally, we proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-3}\\frac{4}{{u}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}u & =4\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{-3}{u}^{-4}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =4\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{3}{u}^{-3}]}_{a}^{-3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{1}{{u}^{3}}]}_{a}^{-3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\frac{1}{(-3)^{3}}-\\frac{1}{{a}^{3}}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{3}\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{27}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{81}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186320717 :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u={x}^{3}-4.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=3{x}^{2}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=3{x}^{2}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to -\\infty $ when $x\\to -\\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u={x}^{3}-4$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $-\\infty $ | $-2$ |\n| --- | --- | --