ng as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{{e}^{3/x-1}}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}{e}^{3u-1}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{{e}^{3u-1}}{3}]}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }({e}^{-1}-{e}^{3a-1}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-1}}{3}-\\frac{1}{3}\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{e}^{3a-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, recall the end behavior of the function $y={e}^{3x-1}$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }{e}^{3x-1}=0$\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }{e}^{3x-1}=\\infty $\n\n![](images/6a/6a5fd63a29d7c8f5fba712c7fa6c20620c9a1438750f3197606e726a85666827.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{{e}^{3/x-1}}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{{e}^{-1}}{3}-\\frac{1}{3}\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{e}^{3a-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-1}}{3}-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3e}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186320868 :question/worked-solution "We break up the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{9+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{4}{9+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{9+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nEvaluating the first of these integrals, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{4}{9+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{4}{9+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }({\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{2x}{3}\\right)|}_{a}^{0}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡0-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{2a}{3}\\right)] \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot [0-\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)] \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the integrand is an **even** function. This means that we can immediately deduce that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{9+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since both improper integrals exist, we conclude that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{9+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{4}{9+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{9+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}+\\frac{\\pi }{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We break up the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{9+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{4}{9+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{9+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nEvaluating the first of these integrals, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{4}{9+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{4}{9+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }({\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{2x}{3}\\right)|}_{a}^{0}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡0-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{2a}{3}\\right)] \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot [0-\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)] \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the integrand is an **even** function. This means that we can immediately deduce that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{9+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since both improper integrals exist, we conclude that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{9+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{4}{9+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{9+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}+\\frac{\\pi }{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186320873 :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int {b}^{\\,px+q}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{p\\operatorname{ln}⁡(b)}{b}^{\\,px+q}+C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{2}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}{2}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{{2}^{-3x}}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[{2}^{-3x}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{2}^{3a}}-\\frac{1}{{2}^{3}})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\frac{1}{{2}^{3x}},$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{{2}^{3x}}=\\infty $\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{2}^{3x}}=0$\n\n![](images/99/998ec723fd7212da4382718750465ce313349ed3f2c0258cc1f68e0a5485d968.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{2}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{2}^{3a}}-\\frac{1}{{2}^{3}}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot (0-\\frac{1}{8}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{24\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int {b}^{\\,px+q}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{p\\operatorname{ln}⁡(b)}{b}^{\\,px+q}+C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{2}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}{2}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{{2}^{-3x}}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[{2}^{-3x}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot (\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{2}^{3a}}-\\frac{1}{{2}^{3}})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\frac{1}{{2}^{3x}},$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{{2}^{3x}}=\\infty $\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{2}^{3x}}=0$\n\n![](images/99/998ec723fd7212da4382718750465ce313349ed3f2c0258cc1f68e0a5485d968.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{2}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot (\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{2}^{3a}}-\\frac{1}{{2}^{3}}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot (0-\\frac{1}{8}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{24\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186320874 :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{{k}^{2}+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{k}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{k}\\right)+C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{6}{{z}^{2}+9}\\,\\text{d}z & =6\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{1}{{z}^{2}+9}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =6\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{1}{{z}^{2}+{3}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =6\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{z}{3}\\right)]}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡0-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{3}\\right)) \\\\  & =2\\cdot (0-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{3}\\right)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{3}\\right),$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{3}\\right)=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\n$$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{3}\\right)=\\frac{\\pi }{2}\n$$\n\n![](images/a4/a4961fa6f4f1aafeba2e8f77ed91e7d034fffd6aa249f13bb8357da11a7cea5e.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{6}{{z}^{2}+9}\\,\\text{d}z & =2\\cdot (0-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{3}\\right)) \\\\  & =2\\cdot (0-\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\  & =\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{{k}^{2}+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{k}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{k}\\right)+C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{6}{{z}^{2}+9}\\,\\text{d}z & =6\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{1}{{z}^{2}+9}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =6\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{1}{{z}^{2}+{3}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =6\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{z}{3}\\right)]}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡0-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{3}\\right)) \\\\  & =2\\cdot (0-\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{3}\\right)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{3}\\right),$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{3}\\right)=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\n$$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{3}\\right)=\\frac{\\pi }{2}\n$$\n\n![](images/a4/a4961fa6f4f1aafeba2e8f77ed91e7d034fffd6aa249f13bb8357da11a7cea5e.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{6}{{z}^{2}+9}\\,\\text{d}z & =2\\cdot (0-\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{3}\\right)) \\\\  & =2\\cdot (0-\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\  & =\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186320875 :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{{k}^{2}+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{k}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{k}\\right)+C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\sqrt{3}}\\frac{12}{{x}^{2}+9}\\,\\text{d}x & =12\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{\\sqrt{3}}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+9}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =12\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{\\sqrt{3}}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{3}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =12\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{3}\\right)]}_{a}^{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\  & =4\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\right)-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{3}\\right)) \\\\  & =4\\cdot (\\frac{\\pi }{6}-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{3}\\right)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{3}\\right),$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{3}\\right)=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\n$$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{3}\\right)=\\frac{\\pi }{2}\n$$\n\n![](images/24/24ca4823d220c3eb31127ed28ab7c75b1297e10ae20a1926d112a234806c411e.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\sqrt{3}}\\frac{12}{{x}^{2}+9}\\,\\text{d}x & =4\\cdot (\\frac{\\pi }{6}-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{3}\\right)) \\\\  & =4\\cdot (\\frac{\\pi }{6}-\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)) \\\\  & =4\\cdot (\\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{2\\pi }{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{8\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{{k}^{2}+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{k}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{k}\\right)+C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\sqrt{3}}\\frac{12}{{x}^{2}+9}\\,\\text{d}x & =12\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{\\sqrt{3}}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+9}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =12\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{\\sqrt{3}}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{3}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =12\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{3}\\right)]}_{a}^{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\  & =4\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\right)-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{3}\\right)) \\\\  & =4\\cdot (\\frac{\\pi }{6}-\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{3}\\right)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{3}\\right),$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{3}\\right)=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\n$$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{3}\\right)=\\frac{\\pi }{2}\n$$\n\n![](images/24/24ca4823d220c3eb31127ed28ab7c75b1297e10ae20a1926d112a234806c411e.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\sqrt{3}}\\frac{12}{{x}^{2}+9}\\,\\text{d}x & =4\\cdot (\\frac{\\pi }{6}-\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{3}\\right)) \\\\  & =4\\cdot (\\frac{\\pi }{6}-\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)) \\\\  & =4\\cdot (\\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{2\\pi }{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{8\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186320876 :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{{k}^{2}+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{k}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{k}\\right)+C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{5}\\frac{6}{{x}^{2}+25}\\,\\text{d}x & =6\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{5}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+25}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =6\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{5}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{5}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =6\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{5}\\right)]}_{a}^{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{5}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1)-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{5}\\right)) \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{5}\\cdot (\\frac{\\pi }{4}-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{5}\\right)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{5}\\right),$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{5}\\right)=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\n$$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{5}\\right)=\\frac{\\pi }{2}\n$$\n\n![](images/f8/f8325b1ea152bd40aaa909202bfce59e621952486041c657c0e27b9192ecec03.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{5}\\frac{6}{{x}^{2}+25}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{6}{5}\\cdot (\\frac{\\pi }{4}-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{5}\\right)) \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{5}\\cdot (\\frac{\\pi }{4}-\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)) \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{5}\\cdot (\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{5}\\cdot \\frac{3\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{9\\pi }{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{{k}^{2}+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{k}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{k}\\right)+C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{5}\\frac{6}{{x}^{2}+25}\\,\\text{d}x & =6\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{5}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+25}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =6\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{5}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{5}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =6\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{5}\\right)]}_{a}^{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{5}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1)-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{5}\\right)) \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{5}\\cdot (\\frac{\\pi }{4}-\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{5}\\right)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{5}\\right),$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{5}\\right)=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\n$$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{5}\\right)=\\frac{\\pi }{2}\n$$\n\n![](images/f8/f8325b1ea152bd40aaa909202bfce59e621952486041c657c0e27b9192ecec03.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{5}\\frac{6}{{x}^{2}+25}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{6}{5}\\cdot (\\frac{\\pi }{4}-\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{5}\\right)) \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{5}\\cdot (\\frac{\\pi }{4}-\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)) \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{5}\\cdot (\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{5}\\cdot \\frac{3\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{9\\pi }{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186320877 :question/worked-solution "We break up the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{6|x|}{{e}^{2{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{6|x|}{{e}^{2{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{6|x|}{{e}^{2{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the antiderivative using a substitution. Let  $u=2{x}^{2}$,  then $\\text{d}u=4x\\,\\text{d}x.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (-6x){e}^{-2{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int -\\frac{3}{2}{e}^{-u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{e}^{-u}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{e}^{-2{x}^{2}}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now evaluate the first of the improper integrals, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{6|x|}{{e}^{2{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}(-6x){e}^{-2{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}(-6x){e}^{-2{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{(\\frac{3}{2}{e}^{-2{x}^{2}})|}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\frac{3}{2}{e}^{0}-\\frac{3}{2}{e}^{-2{a}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the integrand is an **even** function. This means that we can immediately deduce that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{6|x|}{{e}^{2{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since both improper integrals exist, we conclude that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{6|x|}{{e}^{2{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{6|x|}{{e}^{2{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{6|x|}{{e}^{2{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}+\\frac{3}{2} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We break up the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{6|x|}{{e}^{2{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{6|x|}{{e}^{2{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{6|x|}{{e}^{2{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the antiderivative using a substitution. Let  $u=2{x}^{2}$,  then $\\text{d}u=4x\\,\\text{d}x.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (-6x){e}^{-2{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int -\\frac{3}{2}{e}^{-u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{e}^{-u}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{e}^{-2{x}^{2}}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now evaluate the first of the improper integrals, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{6|x|}{{e}^{2{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}(-6x){e}^{-2{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}(-6x){e}^{-2{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{(\\frac{3}{2}{e}^{-2{x}^{2}})|}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\frac{3}{2}{e}^{0}-\\frac{3}{2}{e}^{-2{a}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the integrand is an **even** function. This means that we can immediately deduce that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{6|x|}{{e}^{2{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since both improper integrals exist, we conclude that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{6|x|}{{e}^{2{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{6|x|}{{e}^{2{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{6|x|}{{e}^{2{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}+\\frac{3}{2} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186320878 :question/worked-solution "We find the area of the region bounded by the graph of the curve and the $x$-axis by calculating the integral\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{4+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe break up the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{4+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{3}{4+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{4+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nEvaluating the first of these integrals, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{3}{4+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{3}{4+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{2}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right){|}_{a}^{0}\\,) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡0-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{2}\\right)) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}(0-\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)) \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the integrand is an **even** function. This means that we can immediately deduce that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{4+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since both improper integrals exist, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{4+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{3}{4+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{4+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}+\\frac{3\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We find the area of the region bounded by the graph of the curve and the $x$-axis by calculating the integral\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{4+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe break up the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{4+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{3}{4+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{4+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nEvaluating the first of these integrals, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{3}{4+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{3}{4+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{2}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right){|}_{a}^{0}\\,) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡0-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{2}\\right)) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}(0-\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)) \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the integrand is an **even** function. This means that we can immediately deduce that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{4+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since both improper integrals exist, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{4+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{3}{4+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{4+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}+\\frac{3\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186320879 :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch a graph of the region.\n\n![](images/c2/c2104caf050dd3bb795f29b40ce3047e7c8cea98f7c0a6abede5f92c23a06815.png)\n\nWe find the area of the region bounded by the graph of the curve and the $x$-axis by calculating the integral\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{8{e}^{2x}}{{e}^{4x}+1}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe break up the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{8{e}^{2x}}{{e}^{4x}+1}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{8{e}^{2x}}{{e}^{4x}+1}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{8{e}^{2x}}{{e}^{4x}+1}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the antiderivative using a substitution. Let  $u={e}^{2x}$,  then $\\text{d}u=2{e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{8{e}^{2x}}{{e}^{4x}+1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{8{e}^{2x}}{1+({e}^{2x})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{4}{1+{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u+C \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{arctan}⁡{e}^{2x}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now evaluate the first of the improper integrals, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{8{e}^{2x}}{{e}^{4x}+1}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(4\\operatorname{arctan}⁡{e}^{2x}){|}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(4\\operatorname{arctan}⁡{e}^{0}-4\\operatorname{arctan}⁡{e}^{2a}) \\\\  & =4\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)-0 \\\\  & =\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the integrand $\\frac{8{e}^{2x}}{{e}^{4x}+1}$ is an **even** function. This means that we can immediately deduce that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{8{e}^{2x}}{{e}^{4x}+1}\\,\\text{d}x=\\pi .\n$$\n\nTherefore, since both improper integrals exist, we conclude that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{8{e}^{2x}}{{e}^{4x}+1}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{8{e}^{2x}}{{e}^{4x}+1}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{8{e}^{2x}}{{e}^{4x}+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi +\\pi  \\\\  & =2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let's sketch a graph of the region.\n\n![](images/c2/c2104caf050dd3bb795f29b40ce3047e7c8cea98f7c0a6abede5f92c23a06815.png)\n\nWe find the area of the region bounded by the graph of the curve and the $x$-axis by calculating the integral\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{8{e}^{2x}}{{e}^{4x}+1}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe break up the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{8{e}^{2x}}{{e}^{4x}+1}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{8{e}^{2x}}{{e}^{4x}+1}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{8{e}^{2x}}{{e}^{4x}+1}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the antiderivative using a substitution. Let  $u={e}^{2x}$,  then $\\text{d}u=2{e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{8{e}^{2x}}{{e}^{4x}+1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{8{e}^{2x}}{1+({e}^{2x})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{4}{1+{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u+C \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{arctan}⁡{e}^{2x}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now evaluate the first of the improper integrals, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{8{e}^{2x}}{{e}^{4x}+1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }(4\\operatorname{arctan}⁡{e}^{2x}){|}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }(4\\operatorname{arctan}⁡{e}^{0}-4\\operatorname{arctan}⁡{e}^{2a}) \\\\  & =4\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)-0 \\\\  & =\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the integrand $\\frac{8{e}^{2x}}{{e}^{4x}+1}$ is an **even** function. This means that we can immediately deduce that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{8{e}^{2x}}{{e}^{4x}+1}\\,\\text{d}x=\\pi .\n$$\n\nTherefore, since both improper integrals exist, we conclude that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{8{e}^{2x}}{{e}^{4x}+1}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{8{e}^{2x}}{{e}^{4x}+1}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{8{e}^{2x}}{{e}^{4x}+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi +\\pi  \\\\  & =2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186320880 :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch a graph of the region.\n\n![](images/2b/2b7b85dd8894c73275816a8ee08a8c8a41fb41a327d20ed7bedc489f7e2e55ff.png)\n\nWe find the area of the region bounded by the graph of the curve and the $x$-axis by calculating the integral\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{|x|}{{x}^{4}+9}.\n$$\n\nWe break up the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{|x|}{{x}^{4}+9}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{|x|}{{x}^{4}+9}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{|x|}{{x}^{4}+9}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nWe start by calculating the first of these integrals:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{|x|}{{x}^{4}+9}\\,\\text{d}x=-{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{x}{{x}^{4}+9}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nWe calculate the antiderivative using a substitution. Let $u={x}^{2}$, then $\\text{d}u=2x\\,\\text{d}x.$ Hence, the antiderivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{x}{{x}^{4}+9}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{{u}^{2}+9}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{u}{3}\\right))+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}\\right)+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{|x|}{{x}^{4}+9}\\,\\text{d}x & =-{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{x}{{x}^{4}+9}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{x}{{x}^{4}+9}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{(\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}\\right))|}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{6}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(0)-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{{a}^{2}}{3}\\right)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{6}(0-\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the integrand $\\frac{|x|}{{x}^{4}+9}$ is an **even** function. This means that we can immediately deduce that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{|x|}{{x}^{4}+9}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{\\pi }{12}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since both improper integrals exist, we conclude that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{|x|}{{x}^{4}+9}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{|x|}{{x}^{4}+9}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{|x|}{{x}^{4}+9}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{12}+\\frac{\\pi }{12} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let's sketch a graph of the region.\n\n![](images/2b/2b7b85dd8894c73275816a8ee08a8c8a41fb41a327d20ed7bedc489f7e2e55ff.png)\n\nWe find the area of the region bounded by the graph of the curve and the $x$-axis by calculating the integral\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{|x|}{{x}^{4}+9}.\n$$\n\nWe break up the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{|x|}{{x}^{4}+9}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{|x|}{{x}^{4}+9}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{|x|}{{x}^{4}+9}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nWe start by calculating the first of these integrals:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{|x|}{{x}^{4}+9}\\,\\text{d}x=-{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{x}{{x}^{4}+9}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nWe calculate the antiderivative using a substitution. Let $u={x}^{2}$, then $\\text{d}u=2x\\,\\text{d}x.$ Hence, the antiderivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{x}{{x}^{4}+9}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{{u}^{2}+9}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{u}{3}\\right))+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}\\right)+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{|x|}{{x}^{4}+9}\\,\\text{d}x & =-{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{x}{{x}^{4}+9}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{x}{{x}^{4}+9}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{(\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}\\right))|}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{6}\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(0)-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{{a}^{2}}{3}\\right)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{6}(0-\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the integrand $\\frac{|x|}{{x}^{4}+9}$ is an **even** function. This means that we can immediately deduce that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{|x|}{{x}^{4}+9}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{\\pi }{12}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since both improper integrals exist, we conclude that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{|x|}{{x}^{4}+9}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{|x|}{{x}^{4}+9}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{|x|}{{x}^{4}+9}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{12}+\\frac{\\pi }{12} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186320892 :question/worked-solution "To apply the ratio test, we define ${a}_{n}={n}^{2}(2x-6)^{n}.$ Then, we calculate the ratio of two successive terms and take the limit as $n\\to \\infty :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{(n+1)^{2}(2x-6)^{n+1}}{{n}^{2}(2x-6)^{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{(n+1)^{2}(2x-6)}{{n}^{2}}| \\\\  & =|2x-6|{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\left(\\frac{n+1}{n}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =|2x-6|\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =|2x-6| \\\\  & =2|x-3|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe series converges if $L<1.$ In this case, the convergence condition is satisfied if $|x-3|<\\frac{1}{2}.$ Therefore, we conclude that the radius of convergence is $R=\\frac{1}{2}.$" "To apply the ratio test, we define ${a}_{n}={n}^{2}(2x-6)^{n}.$ Then, we calculate the ratio of two successive terms and take the limit as $n\\to \\infty :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{(n+1)^{2}(2x-6)^{n+1}}{{n}^{2}(2x-6)^{n}}| \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{(n+1)^{2}(2x-6)}{{n}^{2}}| \\\\  & =|2x-6|\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\left(\\frac{n+1}{n}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =|2x-6|\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =|2x-6| \\\\  & =2|x-3|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe series converges if $L<1.$ In this case, the convergence condition is satisfied if $|x-3|<\\frac{1}{2}.$ Therefore, we conclude that the radius of convergence is $R=\\frac{1}{2}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186320893 :question/worked-solution "To apply the ratio test, we define ${a}_{n}=\\frac{{5}^{n}(x-4)^{n}}{n!}.$ Then, we calculate the ratio of two successive terms and take the limit as $n\\to \\infty :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{\\frac{{5}^{n+1}(x-4)^{n+1}}{(n+1)!}}{\\frac{{5}^{n}(x-4)^{n}}{n!}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{5}^{n+1}(x-4)^{n+1}}{(n+1)!}\\cdot \\frac{n!}{{5}^{n}(x-4)^{n}}| \\\\  & =5|x-4|{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{n+1} \\\\  & =5|x-4|\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe series converges if $L<1.$ In this case, the convergence condition is satisfied for all $-\\infty <x<\\infty .$  Therefore, we conclude that the radius of convergence is $R=\\infty .$" "To apply the ratio test, we define ${a}_{n}=\\frac{{5}^{n}(x-4)^{n}}{n!}.$ Then, we calculate the ratio of two successive terms and take the limit as $n\\to \\infty :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{\\frac{{5}^{n+1}(x-4)^{n+1}}{(n+1)!}}{\\frac{{5}^{n}(x-4)^{n}}{n!}}| \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{5}^{n+1}(x-4)^{n+1}}{(n+1)!}\\cdot \\frac{n!}{{5}^{n}(x-4)^{n}}| \\\\  & =5|x-4|\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{n+1} \\\\  & =5|x-4|\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe series converges if $L<1.$ In this case, the convergence condition is satisfied for all $-\\infty <x<\\infty .$  Therefore, we conclude that the radius of convergence is $R=\\infty .$"] [:db/cas 17592186320904 :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u={x}^{4}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=4{x}^{3}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=4{x}^{3}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty $ when $x\\to -\\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u={x}^{4}$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $-\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{{x}^{8}+4}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{\\infty }^{0}\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}u=-{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{{x}^{8}+4}\\,\\text{d}x & =-{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{u}{2}\\right)]}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{2}\\right)-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{2}\\right)]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right),$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)]=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\n$$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)]=\\frac{\\pi }{2}\n$$\n\n![](images/71/7151efaca7e4356e590a437f5d50a2ab6e5646e960b7ffa82b0bbed27bb9db47.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{{x}^{8}+4}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\frac{1}{2}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{2}\\right)] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we substitute $u={x}^{4}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=4{x}^{3}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=4{x}^{3}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty $ when $x\\to -\\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u={x}^{4}$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $-\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{{x}^{8}+4}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{\\infty }^{0}\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}u=-{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{{x}^{8}+4}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{u}{2}\\right)]}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{2}\\right)-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{2}\\right)]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right),$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)]=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\n$$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)]=\\frac{\\pi }{2}\n$$\n\n![](images/71/7151efaca7e4356e590a437f5d50a2ab6e5646e960b7ffa82b0bbed27bb9db47.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{{x}^{8}+4}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{2}\\right)] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186320929 :question/worked-solution "First, we note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }5{x}^{3} & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(5\\cdot {x}^{3}) \\\\  & =5\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe graph $y={x}^{3}$ is power function with an odd positive power and therefore has the property that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{3}=-\\infty .\n$$\n\nMultiplication by the positive constant $5$ does not affect the limit at infinity. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & =5\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{3} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{3} \\\\  & =-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(5{x}^{3})=-\\infty .\n$$" "First, we note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }5{x}^{3} & =\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }(5\\cdot {x}^{3}) \\\\  & =5\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe graph $y={x}^{3}$ is power function with an odd positive power and therefore has the property that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{3}=-\\infty .\n$$\n\nMultiplication by the positive constant $5$ does not affect the limit at infinity. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & =5\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{3} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{3} \\\\  & =-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }(5{x}^{3})=-\\infty .\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186320986 :question/worked-solution "Applying the product rule and the quotient rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{{x}^{2}}{f(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }({x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{f(x)}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{2}\\cdot ({lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{f(x)}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{({lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }f(x))}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the graph, we get that ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }f(x)=3.$ Therefore, the above becomes:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{{x}^{2}}{f(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{2}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe graph $y={x}^{2}$ is an upward parabola, so\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{2}=\\infty .\n$$\n\nMultiplication by the positive constant $\\frac{1}{3}$ does not change the sign of the limit. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{2})={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }({x}^{2})=\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Applying the product rule and the quotient rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{{x}^{2}}{f(x)} & =\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }({x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{f(x)}) \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{2}\\cdot (\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{f(x)}) \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{(\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }f(x))}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the graph, we get that $\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }f(x)=3.$ Therefore, the above becomes:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{{x}^{2}}{f(x)} & =\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{2}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right) \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe graph $y={x}^{2}$ is an upward parabola, so\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{2}=\\infty .\n$$\n\nMultiplication by the positive constant $\\frac{1}{3}$ does not change the sign of the limit. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }(\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{2})=\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }({x}^{2})=\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186321011 :question/worked-solution "The radius of convergence for the Taylor series of ${f}^{′}(x)$ is the same as the Taylor series of $f(x).$ So, to find the radius of convergence of ${f}^{′}(x),$ we need to compute $R.$\n\nTo calculate $R,$ we can use the ratio test. Computing the limit of the absolute value of the ratio of two consecutive terms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{(4(n+1)-1){\\left(\\frac{x}{4}\\right)}^{n+1}}{(4n-1){\\left(\\frac{x}{4}\\right)}^{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{4n+3}{4n-1}||\\frac{x}{4}| \\\\  & =\\frac{|x|}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe series converges when $L<1.$ So, it converges when\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{|x|}{4} & <1 \\\\ |x| & <4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of convergence of the series expansion of ${f}^{′}(x)$ is $R=4.$" "The radius of convergence for the Taylor series of ${f}^{′}(x)$ is the same as the Taylor series of $f(x).$ So, to find the radius of convergence of ${f}^{′}(x),$ we need to compute $R.$\n\nTo calculate $R,$ we can use the ratio test. Computing the limit of the absolute value of the ratio of two consecutive terms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{(4(n+1)-1){\\left(\\frac{x}{4}\\right)}^{n+1}}{(4n-1){\\left(\\frac{x}{4}\\right)}^{n}}| \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{4n+3}{4n-1}||\\frac{x}{4}| \\\\  & =\\frac{|x|}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe series converges when $L<1.$ So, it converges when\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{|x|}{4} & <1 \\\\ |x| & <4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of convergence of the series expansion of ${f}^{′}(x)$ is $R=4.$"] [:db/cas 17592186321070 :question/worked-solution "To apply the ratio test, we define ${a}_{n}=\\frac{{3}^{n}{x}^{n+1}}{(n+4)^{2}}.$ Then, we calculate the ratio of two successive terms and take the limit as $n\\to \\infty :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{3}^{n+1}{x}^{n+2}}{(n+5)^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{(n+4)^{2}}{{3}^{n}{x}^{n+1}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{3x(n+4)^{2}}{(n+5)^{2}}| \\\\  & =3|x|\\cdot {lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{(n+4)^{2}}{(n+5)^{2}} \\\\  & =3|x|\\cdot {lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{{(1+\\frac{4}{n})}^{2}}{{(1+\\frac{5}{n})}^{2}} \\\\  & =3|x|\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =3|x|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe series converges if $L<1.$ In this case, the convergence condition is satisfied if \n\n$$\n3|x|<1\\,⇒\\,|x|<\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the radius of convergence is $R=\\frac{1}{3}.$" "To apply the ratio test, we define ${a}_{n}=\\frac{{3}^{n}{x}^{n+1}}{(n+4)^{2}}.$ Then, we calculate the ratio of two successive terms and take the limit as $n\\to \\infty :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{3}^{n+1}{x}^{n+2}}{(n+5)^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{(n+4)^{2}}{{3}^{n}{x}^{n+1}}| \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{3x(n+4)^{2}}{(n+5)^{2}}| \\\\  & =3|x|\\cdot {lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{(n+4)^{2}}{(n+5)^{2}} \\\\  & =3|x|\\cdot {lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{{(1+\\frac{4}{n})}^{2}}{{(1+\\frac{5}{n})}^{2}} \\\\  & =3|x|\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =3|x|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe series converges if $L<1.$ In this case, the convergence condition is satisfied if \n\n$$\n3|x|<1\\,⇒\\,|x|<\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the radius of convergence is $R=\\frac{1}{3}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186321073 :question/worked-solution "To apply the ratio test, we define ${a}_{n}=\\frac{(-1)^{n}(3x)^{n}}{(n+2)(n+4)}.$ Then, we calculate the ratio of two successive terms and take the limit as $n\\to \\infty :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}(3x)^{n+1}}{(n+3)(n+5)}\\cdot \\frac{(n+2)(n+4)}{(-1)^{n}(3x)^{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{-3x\\,(n+2)(n+4)}{(n+3)(n+5)}| \\\\  & =3|x|\\cdot {lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{(n+2)(n+4)}{(n+3)(n+5)} \\\\  & =3|x|\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =3|x|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe series converges if $L<1.$ In this case, the convergence condition is satisfied if \n\n$$\n3|x|<1\\,⇒\\,|x|<\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the radius of convergence is $R=\\frac{1}{3}.$" "To apply the ratio test, we define ${a}_{n}=\\frac{(-1)^{n}(3x)^{n}}{(n+2)(n+4)}.$ Then, we calculate the ratio of two successive terms and take the limit as $n\\to \\infty :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}(3x)^{n+1}}{(n+3)(n+5)}\\cdot \\frac{(n+2)(n+4)}{(-1)^{n}(3x)^{n}}| \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{-3x\\,(n+2)(n+4)}{(n+3)(n+5)}| \\\\  & =3|x|\\cdot {lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{(n+2)(n+4)}{(n+3)(n+5)} \\\\  & =3|x|\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =3|x|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe series converges if $L<1.$ In this case, the convergence condition is satisfied if \n\n$$\n3|x|<1\\,⇒\\,|x|<\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the radius of convergence is $R=\\frac{1}{3}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186321075 :question/worked-solution "To apply the ratio test we define ${a}_{n}=\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}(2x)^{n}}{(n+4)^{3/2}}.$ Then, we calculate the ratio of two successive terms and take the limit as $n\\to \\infty :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{(-1)^{n+2}(2x)^{n+1}}{(n+5)^{3/2}}\\cdot \\frac{(n+4)^{3/2}}{(-1)^{n+1}(2x)^{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{-2x(n+4)^{3/2}}{(n+5)^{3/2}}| \\\\  & =2|x|\\cdot {lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\left(\\frac{n+4}{n+5}\\right)}^{3/2} \\\\  & =2|x|\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =2|x|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe series converges if $L<1.$ In this case, the convergence condition is satisfied for $2|x|<1,$ or equivalently $|x|<\\frac{1}{2}.$ So, the radius of convergence is $\\frac{1}{2},$ and the series converges for any $x$ that lies in the open interval $(-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2}).$\n\nThe ratio test gives no conclusion about the convergence of the series when $L=1.$ So, when $|x|=\\frac{1}{2},$ we do not know if the series converges. This means that the endpoints $x=-\\frac{1}{2}$ and $x=\\frac{1}{2}$ need to be investigated separately using another test for convergence.\n\n- When $x=\\frac{1}{2}$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}(2x)^{n}}{(n+4)^{3/2}} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}\\cdot (1)^{n}}{(n+4)^{3/2}} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{(n+4)^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{5}^{3/2}}-\\frac{1}{{6}^{3/2}}+\\frac{1}{{7}^{3/2}}-\\frac{1}{{8}^{3/2}}+⋯\\,,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is convergent by the alternating series test.  So the endpoint $x=\\frac{1}{2}$ is included in our interval.\n\n- When $x=-\\frac{1}{2}$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}(2x)^{n}}{(n+4)^{3/2}} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}(-1)^{n}}{(n+4)^{3/2}} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{2n+1}}{(n+4)^{3/2}} \\\\  & =-(\\frac{1}{{5}^{3/2}}+\\frac{1}{{6}^{3/2}}+\\frac{1}{{7}^{3/2}}+\\frac{1}{{8}^{3/2}}+⋯)\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe notice that each term of this series ${a}_{n}=\\frac{1}{(n+4)^{3/2}}$ is smaller than ${b}_{n}=\\frac{1}{{n}^{3/2}}:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{(n+4)^{3/2}}\\le \\frac{1}{{n}^{3/2}}\n$$\n\nSince $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{3/2}}\n$$ is convergent, the series $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{(n+4)^{3/2}}\n$$ is convergent by the comparison test. So the endpoint $x=-\\frac{1}{2}$ is included in our interval.\n\nTherefore, the interval of convergence is $[-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2}],$ or equivalently, $-\\frac{1}{2}\\le x\\le \\frac{1}{2}.$" "To apply the ratio test we define ${a}_{n}=\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}(2x)^{n}}{(n+4)^{3/2}}.$ Then, we calculate the ratio of two successive terms and take the limit as $n\\to \\infty :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{(-1)^{n+2}(2x)^{n+1}}{(n+5)^{3/2}}\\cdot \\frac{(n+4)^{3/2}}{(-1)^{n+1}(2x)^{n}}| \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{-2x(n+4)^{3/2}}{(n+5)^{3/2}}| \\\\  & =2|x|\\cdot {lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\left(\\frac{n+4}{n+5}\\right)}^{3/2} \\\\  & =2|x|\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =2|x|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe series converges if $L<1.$ In this case, the convergence condition is satisfied for $2|x|<1,$ or equivalently $|x|<\\frac{1}{2}.$ So, the radius of convergence is $\\frac{1}{2},$ and the series converges for any $x$ that lies in the open interval $(-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2}).$\n\nThe ratio test gives no conclusion about the convergence of the series when $L=1.$ So, when $|x|=\\frac{1}{2},$ we do not know if the series converges. This means that the endpoints $x=-\\frac{1}{2}$ and $x=\\frac{1}{2}$ need to be investigated separately using another test for convergence.\n\n- When $x=\\frac{1}{2}$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}(2x)^{n}}{(n+4)^{3/2}} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}\\cdot (1)^{n}}{(n+4)^{3/2}} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{(n+4)^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{5}^{3/2}}-\\frac{1}{{6}^{3/2}}+\\frac{1}{{7}^{3/2}}-\\frac{1}{{8}^{3/2}}+⋯\\,,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is convergent by the alternating series test.  So the endpoint $x=\\frac{1}{2}$ is included in our interval.\n\n- When $x=-\\frac{1}{2}$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}(2x)^{n}}{(n+4)^{3/2}} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}(-1)^{n}}{(n+4)^{3/2}} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{2n+1}}{(n+4)^{3/2}} \\\\  & =-(\\frac{1}{{5}^{3/2}}+\\frac{1}{{6}^{3/2}}+\\frac{1}{{7}^{3/2}}+\\frac{1}{{8}^{3/2}}+⋯)\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe notice that each term of this series ${a}_{n}=\\frac{1}{(n+4)^{3/2}}$ is smaller than ${b}_{n}=\\frac{1}{{n}^{3/2}}:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{(n+4)^{3/2}}\\le \\frac{1}{{n}^{3/2}}\n$$\n\nSince $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{3/2}}\n$$ is convergent, the series $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{(n+4)^{3/2}}\n$$ is convergent by the comparison test. So the endpoint $x=-\\frac{1}{2}$ is included in our interval.\n\nTherefore, the interval of convergence is $[-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2}],$ or equivalently, $-\\frac{1}{2}\\le x\\le \\frac{1}{2}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186323682 :question/worked-solution "The horizontal asymptote is $y={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x).$ To find the horizontal asymptote, we apply the usual procedure of dividing the numerator and denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{4}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{x}{2-{x}^{4}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\left(\\frac{x}{{x}^{4}}\\right)}{(\\frac{2}{{x}^{4}}-\\frac{{x}^{4}}{{x}^{4}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\left(\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}\\right)}{(\\frac{2}{{x}^{4}}-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0-1} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the horizontal asymptote of $f(x)$ is $y=0.$" "The horizontal asymptote is $y=\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }f(x).$ To find the horizontal asymptote, we apply the usual procedure of dividing the numerator and denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{4}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{x}{2-{x}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\left(\\frac{x}{{x}^{4}}\\right)}{(\\frac{2}{{x}^{4}}-\\frac{{x}^{4}}{{x}^{4}})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\left(\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}\\right)}{(\\frac{2}{{x}^{4}}-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0-1} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the horizontal asymptote of $f(x)$ is $y=0.$"] [:db/cas 17592186323718 :question/worked-solution "The horizontal asymptote is $y={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x).$ To find the horizontal asymptote, we apply the usual procedure of dividing the numerator and denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{2}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{5{x}^{2}-6}{c{x}^{2}+32x+8} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{5{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{6}{{x}^{2}})}{(\\frac{c{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{32x}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{8}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(5-\\frac{6}{{x}^{2}})}{(c+\\frac{32}{x}+\\frac{8}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{5-0}{c+0+0} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{c}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{c} & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ c & =20.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The horizontal asymptote is $y=\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }f(x).$ To find the horizontal asymptote, we apply the usual procedure of dividing the numerator and denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{2}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{5{x}^{2}-6}{c{x}^{2}+32x+8} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{5{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{6}{{x}^{2}})}{(\\frac{c{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{32x}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{8}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(5-\\frac{6}{{x}^{2}})}{(c+\\frac{32}{x}+\\frac{8}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{5-0}{c+0+0} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{c}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{c} & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ c & =20.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186323719 :question/worked-solution "The horizontal asymptote is $y={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x).$ To find the horizontal asymptote, we apply the usual procedure of dividing the numerator and denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{5}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{9{x}^{5}+3{x}^{2}+11}{12{x}^{5}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{9{x}^{5}}{{x}^{5}}+\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{{x}^{5}}+\\frac{11}{{x}^{5}})}{\\left(\\frac{12{x}^{5}}{{x}^{5}}\\right)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(9+\\frac{3}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{11}{{x}^{5}})}{(12)} \\\\  & =\\frac{9+0+0}{12} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nTherefore, the horizontal asymptote of $f(x)$ is $y=\\frac{3}{4}.$" "The horizontal asymptote is $y=\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }f(x).$ To find the horizontal asymptote, we apply the usual procedure of dividing the numerator and denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{5}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{9{x}^{5}+3{x}^{2}+11}{12{x}^{5}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{9{x}^{5}}{{x}^{5}}+\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{{x}^{5}}+\\frac{11}{{x}^{5}})}{\\left(\\frac{12{x}^{5}}{{x}^{5}}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(9+\\frac{3}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{11}{{x}^{5}})}{(12)} \\\\  & =\\frac{9+0+0}{12} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nTherefore, the horizontal asymptote of $f(x)$ is $y=\\frac{3}{4}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186323720 :question/worked-solution "The horizontal asymptote is $y={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x).$ To find the horizontal asymptote, we apply the usual procedure of dividing the numerator and denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{3}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{14{x}^{3}}{6{x}^{3}+1} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\left(\\frac{14{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}}\\right)}{(\\frac{6{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{14}{(6+\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{14}{6+0} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the horizontal asymptote of $f(x)$ is $y=\\frac{7}{3}.$" "The horizontal asymptote is $y=\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }f(x).$ To find the horizontal asymptote, we apply the usual procedure of dividing the numerator and denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{3}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{14{x}^{3}}{6{x}^{3}+1} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\left(\\frac{14{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}}\\right)}{(\\frac{6{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{14}{(6+\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{14}{6+0} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the horizontal asymptote of $f(x)$ is $y=\\frac{7}{3}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186323721 :question/worked-solution "The horizontal asymptote is $y={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x).$ To find the horizontal asymptote, we apply the usual procedure of dividing the numerator and denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is $x.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{4{x}^{2}-9x+1}{x+4} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{4{x}^{2}}{x}-\\frac{9x}{x}+\\frac{1}{x})}{(\\frac{x}{x}+\\frac{4}{x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(4x-9+\\frac{1}{x})}{(1+\\frac{4}{x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(4x-9+0)}{(1+0)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(4x)-9 \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe limit does not come out to a finite number. Therefore, there is no horizontal asymptote." "The horizontal asymptote is $y=\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }f(x).$ To find the horizontal asymptote, we apply the usual procedure of dividing the numerator and denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is $x.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{4{x}^{2}-9x+1}{x+4} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{4{x}^{2}}{x}-\\frac{9x}{x}+\\frac{1}{x})}{(\\frac{x}{x}+\\frac{4}{x})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(4x-9+\\frac{1}{x})}{(1+\\frac{4}{x})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(4x-9+0)}{(1+0)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }(4x)-9 \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe limit does not come out to a finite number. Therefore, there is no horizontal asymptote."] [:db/cas 17592186323722 :question/worked-solution "The horizontal asymptote is $y={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x).$ To find the horizontal asymptote, we apply the usual procedure of dividing the numerator and denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{3}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{-5{x}^{7}+1}{10{x}^{3}+x+3} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(-\\frac{5{x}^{7}}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}})}{(\\frac{10{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{x}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{3}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(-5{x}^{4}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}})}{(10+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{3}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{-5{x}^{4}+0}{10+0+0} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{x}^{4} \\\\  & =-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe limit does not come out to a finite number. Therefore, there is no horizontal asymptote." "The horizontal asymptote is $y=\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }f(x).$ To find the horizontal asymptote, we apply the usual procedure of dividing the numerator and denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{3}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{-5{x}^{7}+1}{10{x}^{3}+x+3} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(-\\frac{5{x}^{7}}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}})}{(\\frac{10{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{x}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{3}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(-5{x}^{4}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}})}{(10+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{3}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{-5{x}^{4}+0}{10+0+0} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }{x}^{4} \\\\  & =-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe limit does not come out to a finite number. Therefore, there is no horizontal asymptote."] [:db/cas 17592186323723 :question/worked-solution "The horizontal asymptote is $y={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x).$ To find the horizontal asymptote, we apply the usual procedure of dividing the numerator and denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{3}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{24{x}^{3}-1}{2c{x}^{3}-15{x}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{24{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}})}{(\\frac{2c{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{15{x}^{2}}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(24-\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}})}{(2c-\\frac{15}{x})} \\\\  & =\\frac{24-0}{2c-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{12}{c}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{12}{c} & =-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ c & =-36.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The horizontal asymptote is $y=\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }f(x).$ To find the horizontal asymptote, we apply the usual procedure of dividing the numerator and denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{3}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{24{x}^{3}-1}{2c{x}^{3}-15{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{24{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}})}{(\\frac{2c{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{15{x}^{2}}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(24-\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}})}{(2c-\\frac{15}{x})} \\\\  & =\\frac{24-0}{2c-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{12}{c}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{12}{c} & =-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ c & =-36.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186323732 :question/worked-solution "Unfortunately, this limit takes the indeterminate form because the numerator and denominator both evaluate to zero at the given point:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{(x,y,z)\\to (1,-1,1)}\\frac{2{x}^{2}+xy-{y}^{2}}{z{x}^{2}-z{y}^{2}}=\\frac{2\\cdot {1}^{2}+1\\cdot (-1)-(-1)^{2}}{1\\cdot {1}^{2}-1\\cdot (-1)^{2}}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{(x,y,z)\\to (1,-1,1)}\\frac{2{x}^{2}+xy-{y}^{2}}{z{x}^{2}-z{y}^{2}} & ={lim}_{(x,y,z)\\to (1,-1,1)}\\frac{2{x}^{2}+2xy-xy-{y}^{2}}{z({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{(x,y,z)\\to (1,-1,1)}\\frac{2x(x+y)-y(x+y)}{z(x+y)(x-y)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{(x,y,z)\\to (1,-1,1)}\\frac{(2x-y)(x+y)}{z(x+y)(x-y)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{(x,y,z)\\to (1,-1,1)}\\frac{2x-y}{z(x-y)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{(x,y,z)\\to (1,-1,1)}\\frac{2x-y}{z(x-y)} & =\\frac{2(1)-(-1)}{1(1-(-1))} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $\\frac{3}{2}.$" "Unfortunately, this limit takes the indeterminate form because the numerator and denominator both evaluate to zero at the given point:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{(x,y,z)\\to (1,-1,1)}\\frac{2{x}^{2}+xy-{y}^{2}}{z{x}^{2}-z{y}^{2}}=\\frac{2\\cdot {1}^{2}+1\\cdot (-1)-(-1)^{2}}{1\\cdot {1}^{2}-1\\cdot (-1)^{2}}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{(x,y,z)\\to (1,-1,1)}\\frac{2{x}^{2}+xy-{y}^{2}}{z{x}^{2}-z{y}^{2}} & =\\lim_{(x,y,z)\\to (1,-1,1)}\\frac{2{x}^{2}+2xy-xy-{y}^{2}}{z({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{(x,y,z)\\to (1,-1,1)}\\frac{2x(x+y)-y(x+y)}{z(x+y)(x-y)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{(x,y,z)\\to (1,-1,1)}\\frac{(2x-y)(x+y)}{z(x+y)(x-y)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{(x,y,z)\\to (1,-1,1)}\\frac{2x-y}{z(x-y)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{(x,y,z)\\to (1,-1,1)}\\frac{2x-y}{z(x-y)} & =\\frac{2(1)-(-1)}{1(1-(-1))} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $\\frac{3}{2}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186323752 :question/worked-solution "The function \n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=\\frac{{x}^{2}+yz+{z}^{2}}{{x}^{2}-xy+xz-yz}\n$$\n\nis continuous except when the denominator is zero. However, the point $(-1,1,0)$ does not cause the denominator to become zero, so we can calculate the limit by substituting  $(-1,1,0)$ into the function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-1,1,0)}\\frac{{x}^{2}+yz+{z}^{2}}{{x}^{2}-xy+xz-yz} & =\\frac{(-1)^{2}+(1)(0)+(0)^{2}}{(-1)^{2}-(-1)(1)+(-1)(0)-(1)(0)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1+0+0}{1+1+0-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The function \n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=\\frac{{x}^{2}+yz+{z}^{2}}{{x}^{2}-xy+xz-yz}\n$$\n\nis continuous except when the denominator is zero. However, the point $(-1,1,0)$ does not cause the denominator to become zero, so we can calculate the limit by substituting  $(-1,1,0)$ into the function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (-1,1,0)}\\frac{{x}^{2}+yz+{z}^{2}}{{x}^{2}-xy+xz-yz} & =\\frac{(-1)^{2}+(1)(0)+(0)^{2}}{(-1)^{2}-(-1)(1)+(-1)(0)-(1)(0)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1+0+0}{1+1+0-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186323753 :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n{lim}_{(x,y)\\to (1,2)}\\frac{2{x}^{2}+3xy-2{y}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}-xy}=\\frac{2\\cdot {1}^{2}+3\\cdot 1\\cdot 2-2\\cdot {2}^{2}}{2\\cdot {1}^{2}-1\\cdot 2}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by first factoring the denominator, and then canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{(x,y)\\to (1,2)}\\frac{2{x}^{2}+3xy-2{y}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}-xy} & ={lim}_{(x,y)\\to (1,2)}\\frac{2{x}^{2}+4xy-xy-2{y}^{2}}{x(2x-y)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{(x,y)\\to (1,2)}\\frac{2x(x+2y)-y(x+2y)}{x(2x-y)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{(x,y)\\to (1,2)}\\frac{(2x-y)(x+2y)}{x(2x-y)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{(x,y)\\to (1,2)}\\frac{x+2y}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{(x,y)\\to (1,2)}\\frac{x+2y}{x} & =\\frac{1+2\\cdot 2}{1} \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $5.$" "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\lim_{(x,y)\\to (1,2)}\\frac{2{x}^{2}+3xy-2{y}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}-xy}=\\frac{2\\cdot {1}^{2}+3\\cdot 1\\cdot 2-2\\cdot {2}^{2}}{2\\cdot {1}^{2}-1\\cdot 2}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by first factoring the denominator, and then canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{(x,y)\\to (1,2)}\\frac{2{x}^{2}+3xy-2{y}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}-xy} & =\\lim_{(x,y)\\to (1,2)}\\frac{2{x}^{2}+4xy-xy-2{y}^{2}}{x(2x-y)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{(x,y)\\to (1,2)}\\frac{2x(x+2y)-y(x+2y)}{x(2x-y)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{(x,y)\\to (1,2)}\\frac{(2x-y)(x+2y)}{x(2x-y)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{(x,y)\\to (1,2)}\\frac{x+2y}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{(x,y)\\to (1,2)}\\frac{x+2y}{x} & =\\frac{1+2\\cdot 2}{1} \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $5.$"] [:db/cas 17592186323800 :question/worked-solution "To apply the ratio test we define ${a}_{n}=\\frac{{4}^{n}{x}^{n}}{{n}^{2}+4n}.$ Then, we calculate the ratio of two successive terms and take the limit as $n\\to \\infty :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{4}^{n+1}{x}^{n+1}}{(n+1)^{2}+4(n+1)}\\cdot \\frac{{n}^{2}+4n}{{4}^{n}{x}^{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{4x({n}^{2}+4n)}{{n}^{2}+6n+5}| \\\\  & =|4x|\\cdot {lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{{n}^{2}+4n}{{n}^{2}+6n+5} \\\\  & =|4x|\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =|4x|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe series converges if $L<1.$ In this case, the convergence condition is satisfied for $|4x|<1,$ which means $|x|<\\frac{1}{4}.$ So, the radius of convergence is $\\frac{1}{4},$ and the series converges for any $x$ that lies in the open interval $(-\\frac{1}{4},\\frac{1}{4}).$\n\nThe ratio test gives no conclusion about the convergence of the series when $L=1.$ So, when $|x|=\\frac{1}{4},$ we do not know if the series converges. This means that the endpoints $x=-\\frac{1}{4}$ and $x=\\frac{1}{4}$ need to be investigated separately using another test for convergence.\n\n- When $x=\\frac{1}{4}$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{4}^{n}{x}^{n}}{{n}^{2}+4n} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{4}^{n}{(1/4)}^{n}}{{n}^{2}+4n} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}+4n} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1\\cdot 5}+\\frac{1}{2\\cdot 6}+\\frac{1}{3\\cdot 7}+\\frac{1}{4\\cdot 8}+⋯,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is convergent by the direct comparison test with the convergent $p$-series $\\sum \\frac{1}{{n}^{2}}.$ So the endpoint $x=\\frac{1}{4}$ is included in our interval.\n\n- When $x=-\\frac{1}{4}$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{4}^{n}{x}^{n}}{{n}^{2}+4n} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{4}^{n}{(-1/4)}^{n}}{{n}^{2}+4n} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}+4n} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{1\\cdot 5}+\\frac{1}{2\\cdot 6}-\\frac{1}{3\\cdot 7}+\\frac{1}{4\\cdot 8}-⋯,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is convergent by the alternating series test. So the endpoint $x=-\\frac{1}{4}$ is included in our interval.\n\nTherefore, the interval of convergence is $[-\\frac{1}{4},\\frac{1}{4}],$ or equivalently, $-\\frac{1}{4}\\le x\\le \\frac{1}{4}.$" "To apply the ratio test we define ${a}_{n}=\\frac{{4}^{n}{x}^{n}}{{n}^{2}+4n}.$ Then, we calculate the ratio of two successive terms and take the limit as $n\\to \\infty :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{4}^{n+1}{x}^{n+1}}{(n+1)^{2}+4(n+1)}\\cdot \\frac{{n}^{2}+4n}{{4}^{n}{x}^{n}}| \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{4x({n}^{2}+4n)}{{n}^{2}+6n+5}| \\\\  & =|4x|\\cdot {lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{{n}^{2}+4n}{{n}^{2}+6n+5} \\\\  & =|4x|\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =|4x|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe series converges if $L<1.$ In this case, the convergence condition is satisfied for $|4x|<1,$ which means $|x|<\\frac{1}{4}.$ So, the radius of convergence is $\\frac{1}{4},$ and the series converges for any $x$ that lies in the open interval $(-\\frac{1}{4},\\frac{1}{4}).$\n\nThe ratio test gives no conclusion about the convergence of the series when $L=1.$ So, when $|x|=\\frac{1}{4},$ we do not know if the series converges. This means that the endpoints $x=-\\frac{1}{4}$ and $x=\\frac{1}{4}$ need to be investigated separately using another test for convergence.\n\n- When $x=\\frac{1}{4}$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{4}^{n}{x}^{n}}{{n}^{2}+4n} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{4}^{n}{(1/4)}^{n}}{{n}^{2}+4n} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}+4n} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1\\cdot 5}+\\frac{1}{2\\cdot 6}+\\frac{1}{3\\cdot 7}+\\frac{1}{4\\cdot 8}+⋯,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is convergent by the direct comparison test with the convergent $p$-series $\\sum \\frac{1}{{n}^{2}}.$ So the endpoint $x=\\frac{1}{4}$ is included in our interval.\n\n- When $x=-\\frac{1}{4}$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{4}^{n}{x}^{n}}{{n}^{2}+4n} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{4}^{n}{(-1/4)}^{n}}{{n}^{2}+4n} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}+4n} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{1\\cdot 5}+\\frac{1}{2\\cdot 6}-\\frac{1}{3\\cdot 7}+\\frac{1}{4\\cdot 8}-⋯,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is convergent by the alternating series test. So the endpoint $x=-\\frac{1}{4}$ is included in our interval.\n\nTherefore, the interval of convergence is $[-\\frac{1}{4},\\frac{1}{4}],$ or equivalently, $-\\frac{1}{4}\\le x\\le \\frac{1}{4}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186323844 :question/worked-solution "Approaching from the left, we have $x<0,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=\\sqrt{-x}+1.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}(\\sqrt{-x}+1) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{-0}+1 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApproaching from the right, we have $x>0,$ so we use the expression $f(x)={e}^{x}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}{e}^{x} \\\\  & ={e}^{0} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x)=1.\n$$" "Approaching from the left, we have $x<0,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=\\sqrt{-x}+1.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{-}}(\\sqrt{-x}+1) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{-0}+1 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApproaching from the right, we have $x>0,$ so we use the expression $f(x)={e}^{x}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}{e}^{x} \\\\  & ={e}^{0} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x)=\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x)=1.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186323845 :question/problem "For the piecewise function \n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}x\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x<2 \\\\ c\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x\\ge 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nit is known that ${lim}_{x\\to 2}f(x)=2.$ What is the value of the constant $c?$" "For the piecewise function \n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}x\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x<2 \\\\ c\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x\\ge 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nit is known that $\\lim_{x\\to 2}f(x)=2.$ What is the value of the constant $c?$"] [:db/cas 17592186323845 :question/worked-solution "In order for ${lim}_{x\\to 2}f(x)$ to be equal to $2,$ the left and right-sided limits must both exist and be equal to $2.$\n\n- Approaching from the left, we have $x<2,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=x.$ We get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}x \\\\  & =2\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Approaching from the right, we have $x>2,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=c.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}c \\\\  & =c\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the left and right-sided limits must be equal, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}f(x) \\\\ c & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "In order for $\\lim_{x\\to 2}f(x)$ to be equal to $2,$ the left and right-sided limits must both exist and be equal to $2.$\n\n- Approaching from the left, we have $x<2,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=x.$ We get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{-}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{-}}x \\\\  & =2\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Approaching from the right, we have $x>2,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=c.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{+}}c \\\\  & =c\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the left and right-sided limits must be equal, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{-}}f(x) \\\\ c & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186323846 :question/worked-solution "Approaching from the left, we have $x<\\frac{1}{2},$ so we use the expression $f(x)=6x+1.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {(1/2)}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {(1/2)}^{-}}(6x+1) \\\\  & =6\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}+1 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow,  approaching from the right, we have $x>\\frac{1}{2},$ so we use the expression $f(x)=-4{log}_{2}⁡x.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {(1/2)}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {(1/2)}^{+}}-4{log}_{2}⁡x \\\\  & =-4{log}_{2}⁡\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =-4{log}_{2}⁡{2}^{-1} \\\\  & =-4(-1){log}_{2}⁡2 \\\\  & =-4(-1) \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the left- and right-sided limits are equal to $4,$ we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (1/2)}f(x)=4.\n$$" "Approaching from the left, we have $x<\\frac{1}{2},$ so we use the expression $f(x)=6x+1.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {(1/2)}^{-}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to {(1/2)}^{-}}(6x+1) \\\\  & =6\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}+1 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow,  approaching from the right, we have $x>\\frac{1}{2},$ so we use the expression $f(x)=-4{log}_{2}⁡x.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {(1/2)}^{+}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to {(1/2)}^{+}}-4{log}_{2}⁡x \\\\  & =-4{log}_{2}⁡\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =-4{log}_{2}⁡{2}^{-1} \\\\  & =-4(-1){log}_{2}⁡2 \\\\  & =-4(-1) \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the left- and right-sided limits are equal to $4,$ we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (1/2)}f(x)=4.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186323847 :question/worked-solution "Approaching from the left, we have $x<3,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=\\sqrt[3]{3x-1}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}\\sqrt[3]{3x-1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{3\\cdot 3-1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{8} \\\\  & =2\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApproaching from the right, we have $x>3,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x\\pi ).$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {3}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {3}^{+}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x\\pi ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\pi ) \\\\  & =-1\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the two limits are different, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 3}f(x)=\\text{DNE}\n$$" "Approaching from the left, we have $x<3,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=\\sqrt[3]{3x-1}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {3}^{-}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to {3}^{-}}\\sqrt[3]{3x-1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{3\\cdot 3-1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{8} \\\\  & =2\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApproaching from the right, we have $x>3,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x\\pi ).$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {3}^{+}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to {3}^{+}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x\\pi ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\pi ) \\\\  & =-1\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the two limits are different, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 3}f(x)=\\text{DNE}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186323859 :question/worked-solution "Approaching from the left, we have $x<4,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=\\sqrt{x}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {4}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {4}^{-}}\\sqrt{x} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Approaching from the left, we have $x<4,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=\\sqrt{x}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {4}^{-}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to {4}^{-}}\\sqrt{x} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186323899 :question/worked-solution "Substituting $x=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ directly into the limit, we have\n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (\\pi /3)}({tan}^{2}⁡(x)+3{cot}^{2}⁡(x)) & ={tan}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)+3{cot}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right) \\\\  & ={(\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right))}^{2}+3{(\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right))}^{2} \\\\  & ={(\\sqrt{3})}^{2}+3{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =3+3\\left(\\frac{3}{9}\\right) \\\\  & =3+1 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Substituting $x=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ directly into the limit, we have\n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (\\pi /3)}({tan}^{2}⁡(x)+3{cot}^{2}⁡(x)) & ={tan}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)+3{cot}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right) \\\\  & ={(\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right))}^{2}+3{(\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right))}^{2} \\\\  & ={(\\sqrt{3})}^{2}+3{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =3+3\\left(\\frac{3}{9}\\right) \\\\  & =3+1 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186323924 :question/worked-solution "First, we divide both the numerator and the denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{3}.$ Then, by noting that the reciprocals shrink to $0$ in the limit, we can then evaluate the resulting expression.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{-2{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}}{6{x}^{3}-9} & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{(\\frac{-2{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{3}})}{(\\frac{6{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{9}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{(-2+\\frac{1}{x})}{(6-\\frac{9}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2+0}{6-0} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we divide both the numerator and the denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{3}.$ Then, by noting that the reciprocals shrink to $0$ in the limit, we can then evaluate the resulting expression.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{-2{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}}{6{x}^{3}-9} & =\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{(\\frac{-2{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{3}})}{(\\frac{6{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{9}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{(-2+\\frac{1}{x})}{(6-\\frac{9}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2+0}{6-0} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186323933 :question/worked-solution "Note that plugging in $x=1$ gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$ However, we can use L'Hopital's rule to find this limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{{x}^{4}-1}{x-1} & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{[{x}^{4}-1]^{′}}{[x-1]^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{1} \\\\  & =4(1)^{3} \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Note that plugging in $x=1$ gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$ However, we can use L'Hopital's rule to find this limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 1}\\frac{{x}^{4}-1}{x-1} & =\\lim_{x\\to 1}\\frac{[{x}^{4}-1]^{′}}{[x-1]^{′}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 1}\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{1} \\\\  & =4(1)^{3} \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186323934 :question/worked-solution "Note that plugging in $x=2$ gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$ However, we can use L'Hopital's rule to find this limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{5{x}^{2}-3x-14}{{x}^{2}+x-6} & ={lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{[5{x}^{2}-3x-14]^{′}}{[{x}^{2}+x-6]^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{10x-3}{2x+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{10(2)-3}{2(2)+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{17}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Note that plugging in $x=2$ gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$ However, we can use L'Hopital's rule to find this limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 2}\\frac{5{x}^{2}-3x-14}{{x}^{2}+x-6} & =\\lim_{x\\to 2}\\frac{[5{x}^{2}-3x-14]^{′}}{[{x}^{2}+x-6]^{′}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 2}\\frac{10x-3}{2x+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{10(2)-3}{2(2)+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{17}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186323941 :question/worked-solution "In this case, we have an indeterminate form of type $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty },$ so applying  L'Hopital's rule yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{{x}^{2}-1}{x+16} & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{[{x}^{2}-1]^{′}}{[x+16]^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{2x}{1} \\\\  & =-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "In this case, we have an indeterminate form of type $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty },$ so applying  L'Hopital's rule yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{{x}^{2}-1}{x+16} & =\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{[{x}^{2}-1]^{′}}{[x+16]^{′}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{2x}{1} \\\\  & =-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186323942 :question/worked-solution "In this case, we have an indeterminate form of type $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty },$ so applying  L'Hopital's rule yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{2{x}^{3}-6}{{x}^{2}-4} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{[2{x}^{3}-6]^{′}}{[{x}^{2}-4]^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{6{x}^{2}}{2x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }3x \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "In this case, we have an indeterminate form of type $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty },$ so applying  L'Hopital's rule yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{2{x}^{3}-6}{{x}^{2}-4} & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{[2{x}^{3}-6]^{′}}{[{x}^{2}-4]^{′}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{6{x}^{2}}{2x} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }3x \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186329758 :question/worked-solution "If we attempt to evaluate the limit by simply evaluating the function at $x=0$, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{x+5}-\\sqrt{5}} & =\\frac{0}{\\sqrt{0+5}-\\sqrt{5}}=\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to first simplify the limit. Since the radical is in the denominator, we can multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the denominator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{x+5}-\\sqrt{5}} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x(\\sqrt{x+5}+\\sqrt{5})}{(\\sqrt{x+5}-\\sqrt{5})(\\sqrt{x+5}+\\sqrt{5})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x(\\sqrt{x+5}+\\sqrt{5})}{{(\\sqrt{x+5})}^{2}-{(\\sqrt{5})}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x(\\sqrt{x+5}+\\sqrt{5})}{x+5-5} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x(\\sqrt{x+5}+\\sqrt{5})}{x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}(\\sqrt{x+5}+\\sqrt{5}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{0+5}+\\sqrt{5} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "If we attempt to evaluate the limit by simply evaluating the function at $x=0$, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{x+5}-\\sqrt{5}} & =\\frac{0}{\\sqrt{0+5}-\\sqrt{5}}=\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to first simplify the limit. Since the radical is in the denominator, we can multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the denominator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{x+5}-\\sqrt{5}} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x(\\sqrt{x+5}+\\sqrt{5})}{(\\sqrt{x+5}-\\sqrt{5})(\\sqrt{x+5}+\\sqrt{5})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x(\\sqrt{x+5}+\\sqrt{5})}{{(\\sqrt{x+5})}^{2}-{(\\sqrt{5})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x(\\sqrt{x+5}+\\sqrt{5})}{x+5-5} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x(\\sqrt{x+5}+\\sqrt{5})}{x} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}(\\sqrt{x+5}+\\sqrt{5}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{0+5}+\\sqrt{5} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186329759 :question/worked-solution "If we attempt to evaluate the limit, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{\\sqrt{x+1}-2}{{x}^{2}-9} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3+1}-2}{{3}^{2}-9}=\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to first simplify the limit. Since the radical is in the numerator, we can multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the numerator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{\\sqrt{x+1}-2}{{x}^{2}-9} & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{\\sqrt{x+1}-2}{(x-3)(x+3)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{(\\sqrt{x+1}-2)(\\sqrt{x+1}+2)}{(x-3)(x+3)(\\sqrt{x+1}+2)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{{(\\sqrt{x+1})}^{2}-{2}^{2}}{(x-3)(x+3)(\\sqrt{x+1}+2)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{x+1-4}{(x-3)(x+3)(\\sqrt{x+1}+2)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{x-3}{(x-3)(x+3)(\\sqrt{x+1}+2)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{1}{(x+3)(\\sqrt{x+1}+2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(3+3)(\\sqrt{3+1}+2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{24}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "If we attempt to evaluate the limit, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 3}\\frac{\\sqrt{x+1}-2}{{x}^{2}-9} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3+1}-2}{{3}^{2}-9}=\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to first simplify the limit. Since the radical is in the numerator, we can multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the numerator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 3}\\frac{\\sqrt{x+1}-2}{{x}^{2}-9} & =\\lim_{x\\to 3}\\frac{\\sqrt{x+1}-2}{(x-3)(x+3)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 3}\\frac{(\\sqrt{x+1}-2)(\\sqrt{x+1}+2)}{(x-3)(x+3)(\\sqrt{x+1}+2)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 3}\\frac{{(\\sqrt{x+1})}^{2}-{2}^{2}}{(x-3)(x+3)(\\sqrt{x+1}+2)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 3}\\frac{x+1-4}{(x-3)(x+3)(\\sqrt{x+1}+2)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 3}\\frac{x-3}{(x-3)(x+3)(\\sqrt{x+1}+2)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 3}\\frac{1}{(x+3)(\\sqrt{x+1}+2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(3+3)(\\sqrt{3+1}+2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{24}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186329761 :question/worked-solution "If we attempt to evaluate the limit by simply evaluating the function at $x=1$, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}\\frac{x-1}{x-\\sqrt{x}} & =\\frac{1-1}{1-\\sqrt{1}}=\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to first simplify the limit. Since the radical is in the denominator, we can multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the denominator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}\\frac{x-1}{x-\\sqrt{x}} & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}\\frac{(x-1)(x+\\sqrt{x})}{(x-\\sqrt{x})(x+\\sqrt{x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}\\frac{(x-1)(x+\\sqrt{x})}{{x}^{2}-{(\\sqrt{x})}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}\\frac{(x-1)(x+\\sqrt{x})}{{x}^{2}-x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}\\frac{(x-1)(x+\\sqrt{x})}{x(x-1)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}\\frac{x+\\sqrt{x}}{x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}\\frac{1+\\sqrt{1}}{1} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "If we attempt to evaluate the limit by simply evaluating the function at $x=1$, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{-}}\\frac{x-1}{x-\\sqrt{x}} & =\\frac{1-1}{1-\\sqrt{1}}=\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to first simplify the limit. Since the radical is in the denominator, we can multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the denominator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{-}}\\frac{x-1}{x-\\sqrt{x}} & =\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{-}}\\frac{(x-1)(x+\\sqrt{x})}{(x-\\sqrt{x})(x+\\sqrt{x})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{-}}\\frac{(x-1)(x+\\sqrt{x})}{{x}^{2}-{(\\sqrt{x})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{-}}\\frac{(x-1)(x+\\sqrt{x})}{{x}^{2}-x} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{-}}\\frac{(x-1)(x+\\sqrt{x})}{x(x-1)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{-}}\\frac{x+\\sqrt{x}}{x} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{-}}\\frac{1+\\sqrt{1}}{1} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186329795 :question/worked-solution "If we attempt to evaluate the limit by simply evaluating the function at $x=4$, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {4}^{+}}\\frac{x-4}{\\sqrt{3x-3}-3} & =\\frac{4-4}{\\sqrt{3(4)-3}-3}=\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to first simplify the limit. Since the radical is in the denominator, we can multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the denominator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {4}^{+}}\\frac{x-4}{\\sqrt{3x-3}-3} & ={lim}_{x\\to {4}^{+}}\\frac{(x-4)(\\sqrt{3x-3}+3)}{(\\sqrt{3x-3}-3)(\\sqrt{3x-3}+3)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {4}^{+}}\\frac{(x-4)(\\sqrt{3x-3}+3)}{{(\\sqrt{3x-3})}^{2}-{3}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {4}^{+}}\\frac{(x-4)(\\sqrt{3x-3}+3)}{3x-3-9} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {4}^{+}}\\frac{(x-4)(\\sqrt{3x-3}+3)}{3x-12} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {4}^{+}}\\frac{(x-4)(\\sqrt{3x-3}+3)}{3(x-4)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {4}^{+}}\\frac{\\sqrt{3x-3}+3}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3(4)-3}+3}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{3+3}{3} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "If we attempt to evaluate the limit by simply evaluating the function at $x=4$, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {4}^{+}}\\frac{x-4}{\\sqrt{3x-3}-3} & =\\frac{4-4}{\\sqrt{3(4)-3}-3}=\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to first simplify the limit. Since the radical is in the denominator, we can multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the denominator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {4}^{+}}\\frac{x-4}{\\sqrt{3x-3}-3} & =\\lim_{x\\to {4}^{+}}\\frac{(x-4)(\\sqrt{3x-3}+3)}{(\\sqrt{3x-3}-3)(\\sqrt{3x-3}+3)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to {4}^{+}}\\frac{(x-4)(\\sqrt{3x-3}+3)}{{(\\sqrt{3x-3})}^{2}-{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to {4}^{+}}\\frac{(x-4)(\\sqrt{3x-3}+3)}{3x-3-9} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to {4}^{+}}\\frac{(x-4)(\\sqrt{3x-3}+3)}{3x-12} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to {4}^{+}}\\frac{(x-4)(\\sqrt{3x-3}+3)}{3(x-4)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to {4}^{+}}\\frac{\\sqrt{3x-3}+3}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3(4)-3}+3}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{3+3}{3} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186329796 :question/worked-solution "If we attempt to evaluate the limit, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{\\sqrt{x+2}-2}{{x}^{2}-4} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2+2}-2}{{2}^{2}-4}=\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to first simplify the limit. Since the radical is in the numerator, we can multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the numerator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{\\sqrt{x+2}-2}{{x}^{2}-4} & ={lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{\\sqrt{x+2}-2}{(x-2)(x+2)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{(\\sqrt{x+2}-2)(\\sqrt{x+2}+2)}{(x-2)(x+2)(\\sqrt{x+2}+2)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{{(\\sqrt{x+2})}^{2}-{2}^{2}}{(x-2)(x+2)(\\sqrt{x+2}+2)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{x+2-4}{(x-2)(x+2)(\\sqrt{x+2}+2)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{x-2}{(x-2)(x+2)(\\sqrt{x+2}+2)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{1}{(x+2)(\\sqrt{x+2}+2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(2+2)(\\sqrt{2+2}+2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "If we attempt to evaluate the limit, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 2}\\frac{\\sqrt{x+2}-2}{{x}^{2}-4} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2+2}-2}{{2}^{2}-4}=\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to first simplify the limit. Since the radical is in the numerator, we can multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the numerator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 2}\\frac{\\sqrt{x+2}-2}{{x}^{2}-4} & =\\lim_{x\\to 2}\\frac{\\sqrt{x+2}-2}{(x-2)(x+2)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 2}\\frac{(\\sqrt{x+2}-2)(\\sqrt{x+2}+2)}{(x-2)(x+2)(\\sqrt{x+2}+2)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 2}\\frac{{(\\sqrt{x+2})}^{2}-{2}^{2}}{(x-2)(x+2)(\\sqrt{x+2}+2)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 2}\\frac{x+2-4}{(x-2)(x+2)(\\sqrt{x+2}+2)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 2}\\frac{x-2}{(x-2)(x+2)(\\sqrt{x+2}+2)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 2}\\frac{1}{(x+2)(\\sqrt{x+2}+2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(2+2)(\\sqrt{2+2}+2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186329797 :question/worked-solution "If we attempt to evaluate the limit, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (64)^{-}}\\frac{x-8\\sqrt{x}}{x-64} & =\\frac{64-8\\sqrt{64}}{64-64}=\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to first simplify the limit. Since the radical is in the numerator, we can multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the numerator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (64)^{-}}\\frac{x-8\\sqrt{x}}{x-64} & ={lim}_{x\\to (64)^{-}}\\frac{(x-8\\sqrt{x})(x+8\\sqrt{x})}{(x-64)(x+8\\sqrt{x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (64)^{-}}\\frac{{x}^{2}-{(8\\sqrt{x})}^{2}}{(x-64)(x+8\\sqrt{x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (64)^{-}}\\frac{{x}^{2}-64x}{(x-64)(x+8\\sqrt{x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (64)^{-}}\\frac{x(x-64)}{(x-64)(x+8\\sqrt{x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (64)^{-}}\\frac{x}{x+8\\sqrt{x}} \\\\  & =\\frac{64}{64+8\\sqrt{64}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "If we attempt to evaluate the limit, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (64)^{-}}\\frac{x-8\\sqrt{x}}{x-64} & =\\frac{64-8\\sqrt{64}}{64-64}=\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to first simplify the limit. Since the radical is in the numerator, we can multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the numerator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (64)^{-}}\\frac{x-8\\sqrt{x}}{x-64} & =\\lim_{x\\to (64)^{-}}\\frac{(x-8\\sqrt{x})(x+8\\sqrt{x})}{(x-64)(x+8\\sqrt{x})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (64)^{-}}\\frac{{x}^{2}-{(8\\sqrt{x})}^{2}}{(x-64)(x+8\\sqrt{x})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (64)^{-}}\\frac{{x}^{2}-64x}{(x-64)(x+8\\sqrt{x})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (64)^{-}}\\frac{x(x-64)}{(x-64)(x+8\\sqrt{x})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (64)^{-}}\\frac{x}{x+8\\sqrt{x}} \\\\  & =\\frac{64}{64+8\\sqrt{64}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186329798 :question/worked-solution "If we attempt to evaluate the limit, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {25}^{-}}\\frac{2x-10\\sqrt{x}}{x-25} & =\\frac{50-10\\sqrt{25}}{25-25}=\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to first simplify the limit. Since the radical is in the numerator, we can multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the numerator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {25}^{-}}\\frac{2x-10\\sqrt{x}}{x-25} & ={lim}_{x\\to {25}^{-}}\\frac{2(x-5\\sqrt{x})}{x-25} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {25}^{-}}\\frac{2(x-5\\sqrt{x})(x+5\\sqrt{x})}{(x-25)(x+5\\sqrt{x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {25}^{-}}\\frac{2({x}^{2}-{(5\\sqrt{x})}^{2})}{(x-25)(x+5\\sqrt{x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {25}^{-}}\\frac{2({x}^{2}-25x)}{(x-25)(x+5\\sqrt{x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {25}^{-}}\\frac{2x(x-25)}{(x-25)(x+5\\sqrt{x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {25}^{-}}\\frac{2x}{x+5\\sqrt{x}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(25)}{25+5\\sqrt{25}} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "If we attempt to evaluate the limit, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {25}^{-}}\\frac{2x-10\\sqrt{x}}{x-25} & =\\frac{50-10\\sqrt{25}}{25-25}=\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to first simplify the limit. Since the radical is in the numerator, we can multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the numerator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {25}^{-}}\\frac{2x-10\\sqrt{x}}{x-25} & =\\lim_{x\\to {25}^{-}}\\frac{2(x-5\\sqrt{x})}{x-25} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to {25}^{-}}\\frac{2(x-5\\sqrt{x})(x+5\\sqrt{x})}{(x-25)(x+5\\sqrt{x})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to {25}^{-}}\\frac{2({x}^{2}-{(5\\sqrt{x})}^{2})}{(x-25)(x+5\\sqrt{x})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to {25}^{-}}\\frac{2({x}^{2}-25x)}{(x-25)(x+5\\sqrt{x})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to {25}^{-}}\\frac{2x(x-25)}{(x-25)(x+5\\sqrt{x})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to {25}^{-}}\\frac{2x}{x+5\\sqrt{x}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(25)}{25+5\\sqrt{25}} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186329802 :question/worked-solution "First, let's attempt to evaluate the limit by direct substitution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\pi }\\operatorname{cot}⁡(x) & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\pi ) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{-1}{0},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined.\n\nTherefore, to find the limit, let's sketch the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(x).$\n\n![](images/5e/5e9d0e9d944cda9c97555ffdeac5aa245905f800cd99e11f5d172203bad3892d.png)\n\nAs $x$ approaches $\\pi $ from the left, the values of $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(x)$ are negative and decrease without bound. Therefore\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {\\pi }^{-}}\\operatorname{cot}⁡(x)=-\\infty \n$$\n\nHowever, as $x$ approaches $\\pi $ from the right, the values of $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(x)$ are positive and grow without bound. Therefore\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {\\pi }^{+}}\\operatorname{cot}⁡(x)=\\infty \n$$\n\nSince the two limits are not equal, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\pi }\\operatorname{cot}⁡(x)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$" "First, let's attempt to evaluate the limit by direct substitution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\pi }\\operatorname{cot}⁡(x) & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\pi ) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{-1}{0},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined.\n\nTherefore, to find the limit, let's sketch the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(x).$\n\n![](images/5e/5e9d0e9d944cda9c97555ffdeac5aa245905f800cd99e11f5d172203bad3892d.png)\n\nAs $x$ approaches $\\pi $ from the left, the values of $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(x)$ are negative and decrease without bound. Therefore\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {\\pi }^{-}}\\operatorname{cot}⁡(x)=-\\infty \n$$\n\nHowever, as $x$ approaches $\\pi $ from the right, the values of $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(x)$ are positive and grow without bound. Therefore\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {\\pi }^{+}}\\operatorname{cot}⁡(x)=\\infty \n$$\n\nSince the two limits are not equal, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\pi }\\operatorname{cot}⁡(x)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186329826 :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each statement individually. \n\n- Statement “It is continuous and differentiable at $x=1$” is false.  At $x=1$, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}({x}^{3}+1)={1}^{3}+1=2 \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}({x}^{3}+1)={1}^{3}+1=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x)\\ne f(1)=3,\n$$\n\nand so $f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=1$ ($f(x)$ has a removable discontinuity at $x=1$).\n\n- Statement “It is **not** differentiable at $x=1$” is true. Since  $f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=1,$ it is also not differentiable there (because differentiability implies continuity).\n\n- Statement “It is differentiable on the interval $(-1,2)$” is false. Since $f(x)$ is not differentiable at $x=1,$ it is not differentiable on any interval containing this point. Consequently, it is not differentiable on $(-1,2).$\n\nTherefore, only statement “It is **not** differentiable at $x=1$” is true." "Let's analyze each statement individually. \n\n- Statement “It is continuous and differentiable at $x=1$” is false.  At $x=1$, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{+}}({x}^{3}+1)={1}^{3}+1=2 \\\\ \\lim_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{-}}({x}^{3}+1)={1}^{3}+1=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x)=\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x)\\ne f(1)=3,\n$$\n\nand so $f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=1$ ($f(x)$ has a removable discontinuity at $x=1$).\n\n- Statement “It is **not** differentiable at $x=1$” is true. Since  $f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=1,$ it is also not differentiable there (because differentiability implies continuity).\n\n- Statement “It is differentiable on the interval $(-1,2)$” is false. Since $f(x)$ is not differentiable at $x=1,$ it is not differentiable on any interval containing this point. Consequently, it is not differentiable on $(-1,2).$\n\nTherefore, only statement “It is **not** differentiable at $x=1$” is true."] [:db/cas 17592186329840 :question/worked-solution "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nSo, let's check whether the function is continuous on each of the intervals. \n\n- Let first consider the interval  $[-1,1].$ This is a  closed interval, but we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{1}{x}=\\infty \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand so $f(x)$ has an essential discontinuity at $x=0.$ Therefore, $f(x)$ is not continuous on the interval $[-1,1]$, and the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\n- Now consider  $[0.5,1].$ The only discontinuity of the function $f(x)$ is at $x=0,$ which is not in the interval  $[0.5,1].$ Therefore, $f(x)$ is continuous on the interval  $[0.5,1],$ and the extreme value theorem is applicable on this interval.\n\n- Finally, we note that the interval $[-1,0)$ is not closed, so the extreme value theorem is not applicable in this case.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is $[0.5,1]$ only." "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nSo, let's check whether the function is continuous on each of the intervals. \n\n- Let first consider the interval  $[-1,1].$ This is a  closed interval, but we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{1}{x}=\\infty \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand so $f(x)$ has an essential discontinuity at $x=0.$ Therefore, $f(x)$ is not continuous on the interval $[-1,1]$, and the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\n- Now consider  $[0.5,1].$ The only discontinuity of the function $f(x)$ is at $x=0,$ which is not in the interval  $[0.5,1].$ Therefore, $f(x)$ is continuous on the interval  $[0.5,1],$ and the extreme value theorem is applicable on this interval.\n\n- Finally, we note that the interval $[-1,0)$ is not closed, so the extreme value theorem is not applicable in this case.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is $[0.5,1]$ only."] [:db/cas 17592186329853 :question/worked-solution "By inspecting the graph, we can conclude the following:\n\n- Statement “$f(x)$ is defined at $x=-1$” is true. The function $f(x)$ is defined at $x=-1$ and we have $f(-1)=1.$\n\n- Statement “${lim}_{x\\to \\,-1}f(x)$ exists” is false. We get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,-{1}^{-}}f(x) & =2, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to \\,-{1}^{+}}f(x) & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, both left-hand side and right-hand side limits exist but they are not the same. Therefore, ${lim}_{x\\to \\,-1}f(x)$ does not exist.\n\n- Statement “$f(x)$ is continuous at $x=-1$” is false. The function contains a break exactly at $x=-1$ and it's impossible to draw its graph without taking our pencil off of the paper." "By inspecting the graph, we can conclude the following:\n\n- Statement “$f(x)$ is defined at $x=-1$” is true. The function $f(x)$ is defined at $x=-1$ and we have $f(-1)=1.$\n\n- Statement “$\\lim_{x\\to \\,-1}f(x)$ exists” is false. We get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\,-{1}^{-}}f(x) & =2, \\\\ \\lim_{x\\to \\,-{1}^{+}}f(x) & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, both left-hand side and right-hand side limits exist but they are not the same. Therefore, $\\lim_{x\\to \\,-1}f(x)$ does not exist.\n\n- Statement “$f(x)$ is continuous at $x=-1$” is false. The function contains a break exactly at $x=-1$ and it's impossible to draw its graph without taking our pencil off of the paper."] [:db/cas 17592186329854 :question/worked-solution "By inspecting the graph, we can conclude the following:\n\n- Statement “$f(x)$ is defined at $x=1$” is false. From the graph we can see that no value has been assigned to $f(x)$ at $x=1.$\n\n- Statement “${lim}_{x\\to \\,1}f(x)$ exists” is true. Indeed, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}f(x) & =1, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}f(x) & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, \n${lim}_{x\\to \\,1}f(x)=1.$\n\n- Statement “$f(x)$ is continuous at $x=1$” is false. The function is not even defined at $x=1.$ The graph contains a break at $x=1.$ It's impossible to draw the graph of $f(x)$ without taking our pencil off of the paper." "By inspecting the graph, we can conclude the following:\n\n- Statement “$f(x)$ is defined at $x=1$” is false. From the graph we can see that no value has been assigned to $f(x)$ at $x=1.$\n\n- Statement “$\\lim_{x\\to \\,1}f(x)$ exists” is true. Indeed, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}f(x) & =1, \\\\ \\lim_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}f(x) & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, \n$\\lim_{x\\to \\,1}f(x)=1.$\n\n- Statement “$f(x)$ is continuous at $x=1$” is false. The function is not even defined at $x=1.$ The graph contains a break at $x=1.$ It's impossible to draw the graph of $f(x)$ without taking our pencil off of the paper."] [:db/cas 17592186329855 :question/worked-solution "By inspecting the graph, we can conclude the following:\n\n- Statement “$f(x)$ is defined at $x=2$” is true. Indeed, $f(x)$ is defined at $x=2$ and $f(2)=1.$\n\n- Statement ${lim}_{x\\to \\,2}f(x)=f(2)$ is true. Indeed, from the graph we see that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{-}}f(x) & =1, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{+}}f(x) & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, ${lim}_{x\\to \\,2}f(x)=1=f(2).$\n\n- Statement “$f(x)$ is continuous at $x=2$” is true. The graph of $f(x)$ can be drawn without taking our pencil off of the paper in the neighborhood of $x=2.$" "By inspecting the graph, we can conclude the following:\n\n- Statement “$f(x)$ is defined at $x=2$” is true. Indeed, $f(x)$ is defined at $x=2$ and $f(2)=1.$\n\n- Statement $\\lim_{x\\to \\,2}f(x)=f(2)$ is true. Indeed, from the graph we see that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\,{2}^{-}}f(x) & =1, \\\\ \\lim_{x\\to \\,{2}^{+}}f(x) & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $\\lim_{x\\to \\,2}f(x)=1=f(2).$\n\n- Statement “$f(x)$ is continuous at $x=2$” is true. The graph of $f(x)$ can be drawn without taking our pencil off of the paper in the neighborhood of $x=2.$"] [:db/cas 17592186329856 :question/worked-solution "By inspecting the graph, we can conclude the following:\n\n- Statement “$f(x)$ is defined at $x=1$” is true. We have $f(1)=1.$\n\n- Statement ${lim}_{x\\to \\,1}f(x)=f(1)$ is true. Indeed, from the graph we see that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}f(x) & =1, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}f(x) & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, ${lim}_{x\\to \\,1}f(x)=1=f(1).$\n\n- Statement “$f(x)$ is continuous at $x=1$” is true. The graph of $f(x)$ can be drawn without taking our pencil off of the paper in the neighborhood of $x=1.$" "By inspecting the graph, we can conclude the following:\n\n- Statement “$f(x)$ is defined at $x=1$” is true. We have $f(1)=1.$\n\n- Statement $\\lim_{x\\to \\,1}f(x)=f(1)$ is true. Indeed, from the graph we see that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}f(x) & =1, \\\\ \\lim_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}f(x) & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $\\lim_{x\\to \\,1}f(x)=1=f(1).$\n\n- Statement “$f(x)$ is continuous at $x=1$” is true. The graph of $f(x)$ can be drawn without taking our pencil off of the paper in the neighborhood of $x=1.$"] [:db/cas 17592186329862 :question/worked-solution "Let's check the conditions necessary for $f(x)$ to be continuous at $x=2.$\n\n- $f(2)=4,$ so $f(2)$ exists. $✓$\n\n- We calculate the one-sided limits and check if they coincide:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}(-{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =-{2}^{2} \\\\  & =-4, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}(-2x) \\\\  & =-2\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the overall limit exists and ${lim}_{x\\to 2}f(x)=-4$ $✓$\n\n- The limit of the function is not equal to the function value: ${lim}_{x\\to 2}f(x)=-4,$ while $f(2)=4.$ $\\,\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the function $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=2$ because ${lim}_{x\\to 2}f(x)\\ne f(2).$" "Let's check the conditions necessary for $f(x)$ to be continuous at $x=2.$\n\n- $f(2)=4,$ so $f(2)$ exists. $✓$\n\n- We calculate the one-sided limits and check if they coincide:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{-}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{-}}(-{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =-{2}^{2} \\\\  & =-4, \\\\ \\lim_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{+}}(-2x) \\\\  & =-2\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the overall limit exists and $\\lim_{x\\to 2}f(x)=-4$ $✓$\n\n- The limit of the function is not equal to the function value: $\\lim_{x\\to 2}f(x)=-4,$ while $f(2)=4.$ $\\,\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the function $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=2$ because $\\lim_{x\\to 2}f(x)\\ne f(2).$"] [:db/cas 17592186329863 :question/worked-solution "Let's check the conditions necessary for $f(x)$ to be continuous at $x=1.$\n\n- $f(1)=1,$ so $f(1)$ exists. $✓$\n\n- We calculate the one-sided limits and check if they coincide:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}(x) \\\\  & =1, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}(-x) \\\\  & =-(1) \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the one-sided limits exist but are not equal. $\\,\\,\\times $ $\\,\\,\\,$\n\nTherefore, the function $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=1$ because ${lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x)\\ne {lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x).$" "Let's check the conditions necessary for $f(x)$ to be continuous at $x=1.$\n\n- $f(1)=1,$ so $f(1)$ exists. $✓$\n\n- We calculate the one-sided limits and check if they coincide:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{-}}(x) \\\\  & =1, \\\\ \\lim_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{+}}(-x) \\\\  & =-(1) \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the one-sided limits exist but are not equal. $\\,\\,\\times $ $\\,\\,\\,$\n\nTherefore, the function $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=1$ because $\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x)\\ne {lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x).$"] [:db/cas 17592186329865 :question/problem "Given the functions $f(x),g(x)$ and $h(x)$ below, which of them has a jump discontinuity at $x=1?$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={\\begin{aligned}x+4, & x\\le 1 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}+1, & x>1,\\end{aligned} \\\\ g(x) & ={\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+1, & x<1 \\\\ \\sqrt{x+3}, & x\\ge 1,\\end{aligned} \\\\ h(x) & ={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{x+3}, & x\\ne 1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}, & x=1,\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Given the functions $f(x),g(x)$ and $h(x)$ below, which of them has a jump discontinuity at $x=1?$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\begin{cases}x+4, & x\\le 1 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}+1, & x>1,\\end{cases} \\\\ g(x) & =\\begin{cases}{x}^{2}+1, & x<1 \\\\ \\sqrt{x+3}, & x\\ge 1,\\end{cases} \\\\ h(x) & =\\begin{cases}\\frac{1}{x+3}, & x\\ne 1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}, & x=1,\\end{cases}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186329865 :question/worked-solution "For $f(x),$ let's go through our continuity checklist:\n\n- $f(1)=1+4=5$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- Let's now check the left and right limits. We see that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}(x+4)=1+4=5\n$$\n\nwhile\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}(3{x}^{2}+1)=3(1)^{2}+1=4.\n$$\n\nSo both the left and right limits exist and are finite, but they are not equal.$\\,\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x=1.$\n\nNow, for $g(x),$ let's go through our continuity checklist:\n\n- $g(1)=\\sqrt{1+3}=2$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- Let's now check the left and right limits. We see that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}g(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}({x}^{2}+1)=(1)^{2}+1=2, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}g(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}(\\sqrt{x+3})=\\sqrt{1+3}=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the overall limit exists and ${lim}_{x\\to 1}f(x)=2.\\,\\,✓$ $\\,\\,\\,$\n\n- Finally, we check that the value of the limit equals the value of the function: \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,1}g(x)=2=g(1).\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\nTherefore, $g(x)$ is continuous at $x=1.$\n\nFor $h(x),$ let's go through our continuity checklist:\n\n- $h(1)=\\frac{1}{2}$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- Let's now check the left and right limits. We see that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}h(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}\\frac{1}{x+3}=\\frac{1}{4}, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}h(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}\\frac{1}{x+3}=\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the overall limit exists and ${lim}_{x\\to 1}f(x)=\\frac{1}{4}.\\,\\,✓$ $\\,\\,\\,$\n\n- Finally, we check that the value of the limit equals the value of the function: \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,1}h(x)=\\frac{1}{4}\\ne h(1).\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nSo the value of the limit does not equal the value of the function.\n\nTherefore, $h(x)$ has a removable discontinuity at $x=1.$" "For $f(x),$ let's go through our continuity checklist:\n\n- $f(1)=1+4=5$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- Let's now check the left and right limits. We see that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}f(x)=\\lim_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}(x+4)=1+4=5\n$$\n\nwhile\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}f(x)=\\lim_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}(3{x}^{2}+1)=3(1)^{2}+1=4.\n$$\n\nSo both the left and right limits exist and are finite, but they are not equal.$\\,\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x=1.$\n\nNow, for $g(x),$ let's go through our continuity checklist:\n\n- $g(1)=\\sqrt{1+3}=2$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- Let's now check the left and right limits. We see that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}g(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}({x}^{2}+1)=(1)^{2}+1=2, \\\\ \\lim_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}g(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}(\\sqrt{x+3})=\\sqrt{1+3}=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the overall limit exists and $\\lim_{x\\to 1}f(x)=2.\\,\\,✓$ $\\,\\,\\,$\n\n- Finally, we check that the value of the limit equals the value of the function: \n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,1}g(x)=2=g(1).\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\nTherefore, $g(x)$ is continuous at $x=1.$\n\nFor $h(x),$ let's go through our continuity checklist:\n\n- $h(1)=\\frac{1}{2}$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- Let's now check the left and right limits. We see that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}h(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}\\frac{1}{x+3}=\\frac{1}{4}, \\\\ \\lim_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}h(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}\\frac{1}{x+3}=\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the overall limit exists and $\\lim_{x\\to 1}f(x)=\\frac{1}{4}.\\,\\,✓$ $\\,\\,\\,$\n\n- Finally, we check that the value of the limit equals the value of the function: \n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,1}h(x)=\\frac{1}{4}\\ne h(1).\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nSo the value of the limit does not equal the value of the function.\n\nTherefore, $h(x)$ has a removable discontinuity at $x=1.$"] [:db/cas 17592186329866 :question/problem "Given the functions $f(x),g(x)$ and $h(x)$ below, which of them has a jump discontinuity at $x=0?$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-3x, & x\\ne 0 \\\\ 0, & x=0,\\end{aligned} \\\\ g(x) & ={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}, & x\\ne 0 \\\\ 2, & x=0,\\end{aligned} \\\\ h(x) & ={\\begin{aligned}3, & x<0 \\\\ \\sqrt{x+4}, & x\\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Given the functions $f(x),g(x)$ and $h(x)$ below, which of them has a jump discontinuity at $x=0?$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\begin{cases}{x}^{2}-3x, & x\\ne 0 \\\\ 0, & x=0,\\end{cases} \\\\ g(x) & =\\begin{cases}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}, & x\\ne 0 \\\\ 2, & x=0,\\end{cases} \\\\ h(x) & =\\begin{cases}3, & x<0 \\\\ \\sqrt{x+4}, & x\\ge 0.\\end{cases}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186329866 :question/worked-solution "For $y=f(x),$ let's go through our continuity checklist:\n\n- $f(0)=0$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- Let's now check the left and right limits. We see that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{-}}({x}^{2}-3x)={0}^{2}-3(0)=0, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{+}}({x}^{2}-3x)={0}^{2}-3(0)=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the overall limit exists and ${lim}_{x\\to \\,0}f(x)=0.\\,\\,✓$\n\n- Finally, we check that the value of the limit equals the value of the function:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,0}f(x)=0=f(0).\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=0.$\n\nNow, for $y=g(x),$ let's go through our continuity checklist:\n\n- $g(0)=2$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- Let's now check the left and right limits. We see that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{-}}g(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{-}}\\left(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}\\right)=\\frac{1}{{0}^{2}+1}=1, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{+}}g(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{+}}\\left(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}\\right)=\\frac{1}{{0}^{2}+1}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the overall limit exists and ${lim}_{x\\to \\,0}g(x)=1.$\n\n- Finally, we check that the value of the limit equals the value of the function: \n\n$$\ng(0)=2\\ne {lim}_{x\\to \\,0}g(x).\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nSo the value of the limit does not equal the value of the function.\n\nTherefore, $g(x)$ has a removable discontinuity at $x=0.$\n\nFinally, for $y=h(x),$ let's go through our continuity checklist:\n\n- $h(0)=\\sqrt{0+4}=2$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- Let's now check the left and right limits. We see that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{-}}h(x)={lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{-}}(3)=3\n$$\n\nwhile\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{+}}h(x)={lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{+}}(\\sqrt{x+4})=\\sqrt{0+4}=2.\n$$\n\nSo both the left and right limits exist and are finite, but they are not equal.$\\,\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, $h(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x=0.$" "For $y=f(x),$ let's go through our continuity checklist:\n\n- $f(0)=0$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- Let's now check the left and right limits. We see that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\,{0}^{-}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to \\,{0}^{-}}({x}^{2}-3x)={0}^{2}-3(0)=0, \\\\ \\lim_{x\\to \\,{0}^{+}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to \\,{0}^{+}}({x}^{2}-3x)={0}^{2}-3(0)=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the overall limit exists and $\\lim_{x\\to \\,0}f(x)=0.\\,\\,✓$\n\n- Finally, we check that the value of the limit equals the value of the function:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,0}f(x)=0=f(0).\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=0.$\n\nNow, for $y=g(x),$ let's go through our continuity checklist:\n\n- $g(0)=2$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- Let's now check the left and right limits. We see that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\,{0}^{-}}g(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to \\,{0}^{-}}\\left(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}\\right)=\\frac{1}{{0}^{2}+1}=1, \\\\ \\lim_{x\\to \\,{0}^{+}}g(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to \\,{0}^{+}}\\left(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}\\right)=\\frac{1}{{0}^{2}+1}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the overall limit exists and $\\lim_{x\\to \\,0}g(x)=1.$\n\n- Finally, we check that the value of the limit equals the value of the function: \n\n$$\ng(0)=2\\ne {lim}_{x\\to \\,0}g(x).\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nSo the value of the limit does not equal the value of the function.\n\nTherefore, $g(x)$ has a removable discontinuity at $x=0.$\n\nFinally, for $y=h(x),$ let's go through our continuity checklist:\n\n- $h(0)=\\sqrt{0+4}=2$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- Let's now check the left and right limits. We see that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,{0}^{-}}h(x)=\\lim_{x\\to \\,{0}^{-}}(3)=3\n$$\n\nwhile\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,{0}^{+}}h(x)=\\lim_{x\\to \\,{0}^{+}}(\\sqrt{x+4})=\\sqrt{0+4}=2.\n$$\n\nSo both the left and right limits exist and are finite, but they are not equal.$\\,\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, $h(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x=0.$"] [:db/cas 17592186329867 :question/problem "Given the functions $\\,f(x),\\,g(x)\\,$ and $\\,h(x)\\,$ below, which of them has a point discontinuity at $x=-1?$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{3}+3 & x<-1 \\\\ 4, & x=-1 \\\\ 3x+4, & x>-1\\end{aligned} \\\\  \\\\ g(x) & ={\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+1), & x<-1 \\\\ 0, & x=-1 \\\\ {x}^{2}-1, & x>-1\\end{aligned} \\\\  \\\\ h(x) & ={\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+1, & x\\le -1 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+x+1, & x>-1\\end{aligned}\\text{ }\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Given the functions $\\,f(x),\\,g(x)\\,$ and $\\,h(x)\\,$ below, which of them has a point discontinuity at $x=-1?$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\begin{cases}2{x}^{3}+3 & x<-1 \\\\ 4, & x=-1 \\\\ 3x+4, & x>-1\\end{cases} \\\\  \\\\ g(x) & =\\begin{cases}\\operatorname{sin}(x+1), & x<-1 \\\\ 0, & x=-1 \\\\ {x}^{2}-1, & x>-1\\end{cases} \\\\  \\\\ h(x) & =\\begin{cases}{x}^{2}+1, & x\\le -1 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+x+1, & x>-1\\end{cases}\\text{ }\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186329867 :question/worked-solution "Let's check each function, in turn:\n\nLet's check our continuity conditions for $f(x)$ at $x=-1:$\n\n- $f(-1)=4$ exists $✓$\n\n- The left and right-sided limits are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)^{-}}f(x) & =2(-1)^{3}+3=1, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)^{+}}f(x) & =3(-1)+4=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the left and right-sided limits are equal, we conclude\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (-1)}f(x)=1.\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to (-1)}f(x)\\ne f(-1).$ $\\times $\n\nTherefore, the function $f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=-1.$ It's the third condition that failed, which tells us that $x=-1$ is a point discontinuity. \n\nNow, let's check our continuity conditions for $g(x)$ at $x=-1:$\n\n- $g(-1)=0$ exists $✓$\n\n- The left and right-sided limits are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)^{-}}g(x) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-1+1)=0, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)^{+}}g(x) & =(-1)^{2}-1=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the left and right-sided limits are equal, we conclude\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (-1)}g(x)=0.\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to (-1)}g(x)=g(-1)$ $\\,\\,✓$\n\nSo,  the function $g(x)$ is continuous at $x=-1.$\n\nFinally, let's check our continuity conditions for $h(x)$ at $x=-1:$\n\n- $h(-1)=(-1)^{2}+1=2$ exists $✓$\n\n- The left and right-sided limits are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)^{-}}h(x) & =(-1)^{2}+1=2, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)^{+}}h(x) & =2(-1)^{2}+(-1)+1=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the left and right-sided limits are equal, we conclude\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (-1)}h(x)=2.\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to (-1)}h(x)=h(-1)$ $\\,\\,✓$\n\nSo,  the function $h(x)$ is continuous at $x=-1.$\n\nTherefore, only $f(x)$ has a point discontinuity at $x=-1.$" "Let's check each function, in turn:\n\nLet's check our continuity conditions for $f(x)$ at $x=-1:$\n\n- $f(-1)=4$ exists $✓$\n\n- The left and right-sided limits are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\,(-1)^{-}}f(x) & =2(-1)^{3}+3=1, \\\\ \\lim_{x\\to \\,(-1)^{+}}f(x) & =3(-1)+4=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the left and right-sided limits are equal, we conclude\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (-1)}f(x)=1.\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to (-1)}f(x)\\ne f(-1).$ $\\times $\n\nTherefore, the function $f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=-1.$ It's the third condition that failed, which tells us that $x=-1$ is a point discontinuity. \n\nNow, let's check our continuity conditions for $g(x)$ at $x=-1:$\n\n- $g(-1)=0$ exists $✓$\n\n- The left and right-sided limits are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\,(-1)^{-}}g(x) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-1+1)=0, \\\\ \\lim_{x\\to \\,(-1)^{+}}g(x) & =(-1)^{2}-1=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the left and right-sided limits are equal, we conclude\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (-1)}g(x)=0.\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to (-1)}g(x)=g(-1)$ $\\,\\,✓$\n\nSo,  the function $g(x)$ is continuous at $x=-1.$\n\nFinally, let's check our continuity conditions for $h(x)$ at $x=-1:$\n\n- $h(-1)=(-1)^{2}+1=2$ exists $✓$\n\n- The left and right-sided limits are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\,(-1)^{-}}h(x) & =(-1)^{2}+1=2, \\\\ \\lim_{x\\to \\,(-1)^{+}}h(x) & =2(-1)^{2}+(-1)+1=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the left and right-sided limits are equal, we conclude\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (-1)}h(x)=2.\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to (-1)}h(x)=h(-1)$ $\\,\\,✓$\n\nSo,  the function $h(x)$ is continuous at $x=-1.$\n\nTherefore, only $f(x)$ has a point discontinuity at $x=-1.$"] [:db/cas 17592186329869 :question/problem "Given the functions $\\,f(x),\\,g(x)\\,$ and $\\,h(x)\\,$ below, which of them has a point discontinuity at $x=0?$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={\\begin{aligned}{2}^{x}+3 & x<0 \\\\ 4, & x=0 \\\\ 3x+4, & x>0\\end{aligned} \\\\  \\\\ g(x) & ={\\begin{aligned}2(x+1), & x<0 \\\\ 0, & x=0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2, & x>0\\end{aligned} \\\\  \\\\ h(x) & ={\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}+1, & x\\le 0 \\\\ {x}^{5}+12x+1, & x>0\\end{aligned}\\text{ }\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Given the functions $\\,f(x),\\,g(x)\\,$ and $\\,h(x)\\,$ below, which of them has a point discontinuity at $x=0?$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\begin{cases}{2}^{x}+3 & x<0 \\\\ 4, & x=0 \\\\ 3x+4, & x>0\\end{cases} \\\\  \\\\ g(x) & =\\begin{cases}2(x+1), & x<0 \\\\ 0, & x=0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2, & x>0\\end{cases} \\\\  \\\\ h(x) & =\\begin{cases}2{x}^{2}+1, & x\\le 0 \\\\ {x}^{5}+12x+1, & x>0\\end{cases}\\text{ }\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186329869 :question/worked-solution "Let's check each function, in turn:\n\nLet's check our continuity conditions for $f(x)$ at $x=0:$\n\n- $f(0)=4$ exists $✓$\n\n- The left and right-sided limits are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{-}}f(x) & ={2}^{0}+3=4, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{+}}f(x) & =3(0)+4=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the left and right-sided limits are equal, we conclude\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 0}f(x)=4.\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to 0}f(x)=f(0)$ $\\,\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the function $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=0.$\n\nNow, let's check our continuity conditions for $g(x)$ at $x=0:$\n\n- $g(0)=0$ exists $✓$\n\n- The left and right-sided limits are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{-}}g(x) & =2(0+1)=2, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{+}}g(x) & ={0}^{2}+2=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the left and right-sided limits are equal, we conclude\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 0}g(x)=2.\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to 0}g(x)\\ne g(0)$ $\\,\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the function $g(x)$ is not continuous at $x=0.$ It's the third condition that failed, which tells us that $x=0$ is a point discontinuity. \n\nFinally, let's check our continuity conditions for $h(x)$ at $x=0:$\n\n- $h(0)=2(0)^{2}+1=1$ exists $✓$\n\n- The left and right-sided limits are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{-}}h(x) & =2(0)^{2}+1=1, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{+}}h(x) & =(0)^{5}+12(0)+1=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the left and right-sided limits are equal, we conclude\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 0}h(x)=1.\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to 0}h(x)=h(0)$ $\\,\\,✓$\n\nSo, the function $h(x)$ is continuous at $x=0.$\n\nTherefore, only $g(x)$ has a point discontinuity at $x=0.$" "Let's check each function, in turn:\n\nLet's check our continuity conditions for $f(x)$ at $x=0:$\n\n- $f(0)=4$ exists $✓$\n\n- The left and right-sided limits are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\,{0}^{-}}f(x) & ={2}^{0}+3=4, \\\\ \\lim_{x\\to \\,{0}^{+}}f(x) & =3(0)+4=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the left and right-sided limits are equal, we conclude\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 0}f(x)=4.\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to 0}f(x)=f(0)$ $\\,\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the function $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=0.$\n\nNow, let's check our continuity conditions for $g(x)$ at $x=0:$\n\n- $g(0)=0$ exists $✓$\n\n- The left and right-sided limits are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\,{0}^{-}}g(x) & =2(0+1)=2, \\\\ \\lim_{x\\to \\,{0}^{+}}g(x) & ={0}^{2}+2=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the left and right-sided limits are equal, we conclude\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 0}g(x)=2.\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to 0}g(x)\\ne g(0)$ $\\,\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the function $g(x)$ is not continuous at $x=0.$ It's the third condition that failed, which tells us that $x=0$ is a point discontinuity. \n\nFinally, let's check our continuity conditions for $h(x)$ at $x=0:$\n\n- $h(0)=2(0)^{2}+1=1$ exists $✓$\n\n- The left and right-sided limits are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\,{0}^{-}}h(x) & =2(0)^{2}+1=1, \\\\ \\lim_{x\\to \\,{0}^{+}}h(x) & =(0)^{5}+12(0)+1=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the left and right-sided limits are equal, we conclude\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 0}h(x)=1.\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to 0}h(x)=h(0)$ $\\,\\,✓$\n\nSo, the function $h(x)$ is continuous at $x=0.$\n\nTherefore, only $g(x)$ has a point discontinuity at $x=0.$"] [:db/cas 17592186329875 :question/worked-solution "Notice that we can rewrite the first term as\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}{e}^{-x}=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{e}^{x}}.\n$$\n\nBoth the numerator and denominator approach $\\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty .$ However, since ${e}^{x}≫{x}^{2}$ for large values of $x,$ the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator. Consequently, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{e}^{x}}=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }({x}^{2}{e}^{-x}-2) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{e}^{x}}-2 \\\\  & =0-2 \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Notice that we can rewrite the first term as\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}{e}^{-x}=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{e}^{x}}.\n$$\n\nBoth the numerator and denominator approach $\\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty .$ However, since ${e}^{x}≫{x}^{2}$ for large values of $x,$ the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator. Consequently, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{e}^{x}}=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }({x}^{2}{e}^{-x}-2) & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{e}^{x}}-2 \\\\  & =0-2 \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186329876 :question/worked-solution "Notice that we can rewrite the first term as\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5x){e}^{-x}=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5x)}{{e}^{x}}.\n$$\n\nBoth the numerator and denominator approach $\\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty .$ However, since ${e}^{x}≫\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5x)$ for large values of $x,$ the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator. Consequently, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5x)}{{e}^{x}}=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5x){e}^{-x}+5) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5x)}{{e}^{x}}+5) \\\\  & =0+5 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Notice that we can rewrite the first term as\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5x){e}^{-x}=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5x)}{{e}^{x}}.\n$$\n\nBoth the numerator and denominator approach $\\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty .$ However, since ${e}^{x}≫\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5x)$ for large values of $x,$ the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator. Consequently, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5x)}{{e}^{x}}=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5x){e}^{-x}+5) & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5x)}{{e}^{x}}+5) \\\\  & =0+5 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186329878 :question/worked-solution "We're required to determine the left and right continuity properties of $g(x)$ at $x=1.$\n\nLet's start by computing $g(1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(1) & ={1}^{2}-4 \\\\  & =1-4 \\\\  & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement “$g(x)$ is left-continuous at $x=1$” is true. The function is left-continuous at $x=1$ since the left-sided limit is equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}g(x) & =3(1)-6 \\\\  & =3-6 \\\\  & =-3 \\\\  & =g(1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “$g(x)$ is right-continuous at $x=1$” is true. The function is right-continuous at $x=1$ since the right-sided limit is equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}g(x) & ={1}^{2}-4 \\\\  & =1-4 \\\\  & =-3 \\\\  & =g(1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “$g(x)$ is continuous at $x=1$” is true. The function is both left and right-continuous at $x=1,$ which implies that the function is continuous at $x=1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$g(x)$ is left-continuous at $x=1$”; “$g(x)$ is right-continuous at $x=1$”; “$g(x)$ is continuous at $x=1$”." "We're required to determine the left and right continuity properties of $g(x)$ at $x=1.$\n\nLet's start by computing $g(1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(1) & ={1}^{2}-4 \\\\  & =1-4 \\\\  & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement “$g(x)$ is left-continuous at $x=1$” is true. The function is left-continuous at $x=1$ since the left-sided limit is equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{-}}g(x) & =3(1)-6 \\\\  & =3-6 \\\\  & =-3 \\\\  & =g(1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “$g(x)$ is right-continuous at $x=1$” is true. The function is right-continuous at $x=1$ since the right-sided limit is equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{+}}g(x) & ={1}^{2}-4 \\\\  & =1-4 \\\\  & =-3 \\\\  & =g(1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “$g(x)$ is continuous at $x=1$” is true. The function is both left and right-continuous at $x=1,$ which implies that the function is continuous at $x=1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$g(x)$ is left-continuous at $x=1$”; “$g(x)$ is right-continuous at $x=1$”; “$g(x)$ is continuous at $x=1$”."] [:db/cas 17592186329912 :question/worked-solution "For large positive values of $x,$ we have $|7x-4|=7x-4.$ Consequently, for $x\\to \\infty ,$ we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{|7x-4|}{2x-6}={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{7x-4}{2x-6}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we divide both the numerator and the denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{1}.$ Then, by noting that the reciprocals shrink to $0$ in the limit, we can then evaluate the resulting expression.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{7x-4}{2x-6} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{7x}{x}-\\frac{4}{x})}{(\\frac{2x}{x}-\\frac{6}{x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(7-\\frac{4}{x})}{(2-\\frac{6}{x})} \\\\  & =\\frac{7-0}{2-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "For large positive values of $x,$ we have $|7x-4|=7x-4.$ Consequently, for $x\\to \\infty ,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{|7x-4|}{2x-6}=\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{7x-4}{2x-6}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we divide both the numerator and the denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{1}.$ Then, by noting that the reciprocals shrink to $0$ in the limit, we can then evaluate the resulting expression.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{7x-4}{2x-6} & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{7x}{x}-\\frac{4}{x})}{(\\frac{2x}{x}-\\frac{6}{x})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(7-\\frac{4}{x})}{(2-\\frac{6}{x})} \\\\  & =\\frac{7-0}{2-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186329915 :question/worked-solution "For large negative values of $x,$ we have $|3x+6|=-(3x+6).$ Consequently, for $x\\to -\\infty ,$ we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{|3x+6|}{x-4}={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{-(3x+6)}{x-4}.\n$$\n\nThe horizontal asymptote for $x<0$ is $y={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }f(x).$ To find the horizontal asymptote, we apply the usual procedure of dividing the numerator and denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is $x.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{-(3x+6)}{x-4} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{-(\\frac{3x}{x}+\\frac{6}{x})}{(\\frac{x}{x}-\\frac{4}{x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{-(3+\\frac{6}{x})}{(1-\\frac{4}{x})} \\\\  & =\\frac{-(3+0)}{1-0} \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the horizontal asymptote is $y=-3.$" "For large negative values of $x,$ we have $|3x+6|=-(3x+6).$ Consequently, for $x\\to -\\infty ,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{|3x+6|}{x-4}=\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{-(3x+6)}{x-4}.\n$$\n\nThe horizontal asymptote for $x<0$ is $y=\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }f(x).$ To find the horizontal asymptote, we apply the usual procedure of dividing the numerator and denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is $x.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{-(3x+6)}{x-4} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{-(\\frac{3x}{x}+\\frac{6}{x})}{(\\frac{x}{x}-\\frac{4}{x})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{-(3+\\frac{6}{x})}{(1-\\frac{4}{x})} \\\\  & =\\frac{-(3+0)}{1-0} \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the horizontal asymptote is $y=-3.$"] [:db/cas 17592186329929 :question/worked-solution "For large positive values of $x,$ we have $|6x-2|=6x-2.$ Consequently, for $x\\to \\infty ,$ we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{|6x-2|}{8x+5}={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{6x-2}{8x+5}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we divide both the numerator and the denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{1}.$ Then, by noting that the reciprocals shrink to $0$ in the limit, we can then evaluate the resulting expression.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{6x-2}{8x+5} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{6x}{x}-\\frac{2}{x})}{(\\frac{8x}{x}+\\frac{5}{x})} \\\\  & {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(6-\\frac{2}{x})}{(8+\\frac{5}{x})} \\\\  & =\\frac{6-0}{8-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "For large positive values of $x,$ we have $|6x-2|=6x-2.$ Consequently, for $x\\to \\infty ,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{|6x-2|}{8x+5}=\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{6x-2}{8x+5}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we divide both the numerator and the denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{1}.$ Then, by noting that the reciprocals shrink to $0$ in the limit, we can then evaluate the resulting expression.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{6x-2}{8x+5} & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{6x}{x}-\\frac{2}{x})}{(\\frac{8x}{x}+\\frac{5}{x})} \\\\  & \\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(6-\\frac{2}{x})}{(8+\\frac{5}{x})} \\\\  & =\\frac{6-0}{8-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186329932 :question/worked-solution "First, we divide both the numerator and the denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{2}.$ Then, by noting that the reciprocals shrink to $0$ in the limit, we can then evaluate the resulting expression.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}+1} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}}}{\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}}}{1+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{1+0} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we divide both the numerator and the denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{2}.$ Then, by noting that the reciprocals shrink to $0$ in the limit, we can then evaluate the resulting expression.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}+1} & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}}}{\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}}}{1+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{1+0} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186329933 :question/worked-solution "For large positive values of $x,$ we have $|10x+3|=10x+3.$ Consequently, for $x\\to \\infty ,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{|10x+3|}{6-5x}=\\frac{10x+3}{6-5x}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we divide both the numerator and the denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{1}.$ Then, by noting that the reciprocals shrink to $0$ in the limit, we can then evaluate the resulting expression.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{10x+3}{6-5x} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{10x}{x}+\\frac{3}{x})}{(\\frac{6}{x}-\\frac{5x}{x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(10+\\frac{3}{x})}{(\\frac{6}{x}-5)} \\\\  & =\\frac{10+0}{0-5} \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "For large positive values of $x,$ we have $|10x+3|=10x+3.$ Consequently, for $x\\to \\infty ,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{|10x+3|}{6-5x}=\\frac{10x+3}{6-5x}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we divide both the numerator and the denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{1}.$ Then, by noting that the reciprocals shrink to $0$ in the limit, we can then evaluate the resulting expression.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{10x+3}{6-5x} & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{10x}{x}+\\frac{3}{x})}{(\\frac{6}{x}-\\frac{5x}{x})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(10+\\frac{3}{x})}{(\\frac{6}{x}-5)} \\\\  & =\\frac{10+0}{0-5} \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186329940 :question/worked-solution "For sufficiently large negative values of $x,$ we have $|6x-4|=-(6x-4)=-6x+4.$ Consequently, for $x\\to -\\infty ,$ we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{|6x-4|}{3x+4}={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{-6x+4}{3x+4}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we divide both the numerator and the denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{1}.$ Then, by noting that the reciprocals shrink to $0$ in the limit, we can then evaluate the resulting expression.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{-6x+4}{3x+4} & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{-6x}{x}+\\frac{4}{x})}{(\\frac{3x}{x}+\\frac{4}{x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{(-6+\\frac{4}{x})}{(3+\\frac{4}{x})} \\\\  & =\\frac{-6+0}{3+0} \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "For sufficiently large negative values of $x,$ we have $|6x-4|=-(6x-4)=-6x+4.$ Consequently, for $x\\to -\\infty ,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{|6x-4|}{3x+4}=\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{-6x+4}{3x+4}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we divide both the numerator and the denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{1}.$ Then, by noting that the reciprocals shrink to $0$ in the limit, we can then evaluate the resulting expression.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{-6x+4}{3x+4} & =\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{-6x}{x}+\\frac{4}{x})}{(\\frac{3x}{x}+\\frac{4}{x})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{(-6+\\frac{4}{x})}{(3+\\frac{4}{x})} \\\\  & =\\frac{-6+0}{3+0} \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186332671 :question/worked-solution "The definite integral can be defined in terms of the limit of a left Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\n    \n\nwhere $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ and ${x}_{i}=a+iΔx.$\n\nIn the definite integral,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{2}^{6}{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nthe function is $f(x)={x}^{3},$ the lower bound is $a=2,$ and the upper bound is $b=6.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{6-2}{n}=\\frac{4}{n},\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{6}{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[f(2+i\\cdot \\frac{4}{n})\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[f(2+\\frac{4i}{n})\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[{(2+\\frac{4i}{n})}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The definite integral can be defined in terms of the limit of a left Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\n    \n\nwhere $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ and ${x}_{i}=a+iΔx.$\n\nIn the definite integral,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{2}^{6}{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nthe function is $f(x)={x}^{3},$ the lower bound is $a=2,$ and the upper bound is $b=6.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{6-2}{n}=\\frac{4}{n},\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{6}{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[f(2+i\\cdot \\frac{4}{n})\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}] \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[f(2+\\frac{4i}{n})\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}] \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[{(2+\\frac{4i}{n})}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186332672 :question/worked-solution "The definite integral can be defined in terms of the limit of a right Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{i}=a+iΔx$ and $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}.$\n\nIn the definite integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{3}^{4}5{x}^{2}\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nthe function is $f(x)=5{x}^{2},$ the lower bound is $a=3$ and the upper bound is $b=4.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{4-3}{n}=\\frac{1}{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{3}^{4}5{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}[f(3+i\\cdot \\frac{1}{n})\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}[f(3+\\frac{i}{n})\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}[5{(3+\\frac{i}{n})}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The definite integral can be defined in terms of the limit of a right Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{i}=a+iΔx$ and $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}.$\n\nIn the definite integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{3}^{4}5{x}^{2}\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nthe function is $f(x)=5{x}^{2},$ the lower bound is $a=3$ and the upper bound is $b=4.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{4-3}{n}=\\frac{1}{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{3}^{4}5{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}[f(3+i\\cdot \\frac{1}{n})\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}] \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}[f(3+\\frac{i}{n})\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}] \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}[5{(3+\\frac{i}{n})}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186332780 :question/worked-solution "This is an improper integral of the second kind because the function under the integral is unbounded as we approach the *lower* integration limit from any point that lies in the interval $x\\in (-1,1):$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}+1}\\to \\infty \\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to (-1)^{+}\n$$\n\nWe can attempt to evaluate this integral by integrating $f(x)$ over the interval $x\\in [a,1]$ and taking the limit as $a\\to (-1)^{+}:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}+1}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to (-1)^{+}}({\\int }_{a}^{1}\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}+1}\\,\\text{d}x)\\end{aligned}\n$$" "This is an improper integral of the second kind because the function under the integral is unbounded as we approach the *lower* integration limit from any point that lies in the interval $x\\in (-1,1):$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}+1}\\to \\infty \\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to (-1)^{+}\n$$\n\nWe can attempt to evaluate this integral by integrating $f(x)$ over the interval $x\\in [a,1]$ and taking the limit as $a\\to (-1)^{+}:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}+1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\lim_{a\\to (-1)^{+}}({\\int }_{a}^{1}\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}+1}\\,\\text{d}x)\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186332781 :question/worked-solution "This is an improper integral of the second kind because the function under the integral is unbounded at the *lower* limit of integration.\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{4}{\\sqrt[5]{x}}\\to \\infty \\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to {0}^{+}\n$$\n\nNonetheless, it is possible to calculate the integral by setting the *lower* bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to {0}^{+}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{4}{\\sqrt[5]{x}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to {0}^{+}}{\\int }_{a}^{1}\\frac{4}{\\sqrt[5]{x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to {0}^{+}}{\\int }_{a}^{1}4{x}^{-1/5}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to {0}^{+}}5{x}^{4/5}{|}_{a}^{1} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to {0}^{+}}5\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{4}}{|}_{a}^{1} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to {0}^{+}}[5\\cdot \\sqrt[5]{{1}^{4}}-5\\cdot \\sqrt[5]{{a}^{4}}] \\\\  & =5(1)-5(0) \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "This is an improper integral of the second kind because the function under the integral is unbounded at the *lower* limit of integration.\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{4}{\\sqrt[5]{x}}\\to \\infty \\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to {0}^{+}\n$$\n\nNonetheless, it is possible to calculate the integral by setting the *lower* bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to {0}^{+}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{4}{\\sqrt[5]{x}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\lim_{a\\to {0}^{+}}{\\int }_{a}^{1}\\frac{4}{\\sqrt[5]{x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to {0}^{+}}{\\int }_{a}^{1}4{x}^{-1/5}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to {0}^{+}}5{x}^{4/5}{|}_{a}^{1} \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to {0}^{+}}5\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{4}}{|}_{a}^{1} \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to {0}^{+}}[5\\cdot \\sqrt[5]{{1}^{4}}-5\\cdot \\sqrt[5]{{a}^{4}}] \\\\  & =5(1)-5(0) \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186332782 :question/worked-solution "This is an improper integral of the second kind because the function under the integral is unbounded at the *lower* limit of integration. \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{6}{\\sqrt[7]{x}}\\to \\infty \\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to {0}^{+}\n$$\n\nNonetheless, it is possible to calculate the integral by setting the *lower* bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to {0}^{+}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[7]{x}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to {0}^{+}}{\\int }_{a}^{1}\\frac{6}{\\sqrt[7]{x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to {0}^{+}}{\\int }_{a}^{1}6{x}^{-1/7}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to {0}^{+}}7{x}^{6/7}{|}_{a}^{1} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to {0}^{+}}7\\sqrt[7]{{x}^{6}}{|}_{a}^{1} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to {0}^{+}}[7\\cdot \\sqrt[7]{{1}^{6}}-7\\cdot \\sqrt[7]{{a}^{6}}] \\\\  & =7(1)-7(0) \\\\  & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "This is an improper integral of the second kind because the function under the integral is unbounded at the *lower* limit of integration. \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{6}{\\sqrt[7]{x}}\\to \\infty \\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to {0}^{+}\n$$\n\nNonetheless, it is possible to calculate the integral by setting the *lower* bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to {0}^{+}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[7]{x}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\lim_{a\\to {0}^{+}}{\\int }_{a}^{1}\\frac{6}{\\sqrt[7]{x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to {0}^{+}}{\\int }_{a}^{1}6{x}^{-1/7}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to {0}^{+}}7{x}^{6/7}{|}_{a}^{1} \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to {0}^{+}}7\\sqrt[7]{{x}^{6}}{|}_{a}^{1} \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to {0}^{+}}[7\\cdot \\sqrt[7]{{1}^{6}}-7\\cdot \\sqrt[7]{{a}^{6}}] \\\\  & =7(1)-7(0) \\\\  & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186332838 :question/worked-solution "The radius of convergence for the Taylor series of ${f}^{′}(x)$ is the same as the Taylor series of $f(x).$ So, to find the radius of convergence of ${f}^{′}(x),$ we need to compute $R.$\n\nTo calculate $R,$ we can use the ratio test. Computing the limit of the absolute value of the ratio of two consecutive terms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{2}^{n+1}{x}^{n+1}}{{2}^{n}{x}^{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|2x| \\\\  & =2|x|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe series converges when $L<1.$ So, it converges when\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2|x| & <1 \\\\ |x| & <\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of convergence is $R=\\frac{1}{2}.$" "The radius of convergence for the Taylor series of ${f}^{′}(x)$ is the same as the Taylor series of $f(x).$ So, to find the radius of convergence of ${f}^{′}(x),$ we need to compute $R.$\n\nTo calculate $R,$ we can use the ratio test. Computing the limit of the absolute value of the ratio of two consecutive terms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{2}^{n+1}{x}^{n+1}}{{2}^{n}{x}^{n}}| \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }|2x| \\\\  & =2|x|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe series converges when $L<1.$ So, it converges when\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2|x| & <1 \\\\ |x| & <\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of convergence is $R=\\frac{1}{2}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186332840 :question/worked-solution "We use the flowchart below to determine which tests to use for each series.\n\n![](images/3a/3aa7f0243efeab51d00aa9694dd018245f7ca4664cc1a5fdf4e646aebb61536e.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's test each series in turn.\n\n- For the first series, we use the alternating series test. Observe that \n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{1}{n!}\n$$\n\nis positive and decreasing for all $n\\ge 1,$ and ${a}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty .$ So, by the alternating series test, the series\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{n!}\n$$\n\nis also convergent.\n\n- For the second series, we use the limit comparison test. Let \n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{2\\sqrt{n}}{n+2}\\,{b}_{n}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{n}}\n$$\n\nWorking out the necessary limit, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n}}{{b}_{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }(\\frac{2\\sqrt{n}}{n+2}÷\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{n}}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }(\\frac{2\\sqrt{n}}{n+2}\\cdot \\sqrt{n}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\left(\\frac{2n}{n+2}\\right) \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\left(\\frac{2}{(1+\\frac{2}{n})}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{1+0} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $L=2$ is positive and finite. Now, the series\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{n}}\n$$\n\nis divergent by the $p$-series test. So, the series \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2\\sqrt{n}}{n+2}\n$$\n\nis also divergent by the limit comparison test.\n\n- For the final series, we use the $n$th term test for divergence. Let \n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{{n}^{3}}{n+2{n}^{2}}\n$$\n\nThen, we have ${lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}=\\infty ,$ since the polynomial in the numerator grows faster than the polynomial in the denominator. Therefore, by the $n$th term test, the series\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{n}^{3}}{n+2{n}^{2}}\n$$\n\nis divergent.\n\nIn conclusion, only series $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{n!}\n$$ converges." "We use the flowchart below to determine which tests to use for each series.\n\n![](images/3a/3aa7f0243efeab51d00aa9694dd018245f7ca4664cc1a5fdf4e646aebb61536e.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's test each series in turn.\n\n- For the first series, we use the alternating series test. Observe that \n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{1}{n!}\n$$\n\nis positive and decreasing for all $n\\ge 1,$ and ${a}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty .$ So, by the alternating series test, the series\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{n!}\n$$\n\nis also convergent.\n\n- For the second series, we use the limit comparison test. Let \n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{2\\sqrt{n}}{n+2}\\,{b}_{n}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{n}}\n$$\n\nWorking out the necessary limit, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n}}{{b}_{n}}| \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }(\\frac{2\\sqrt{n}}{n+2}÷\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{n}}) \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }(\\frac{2\\sqrt{n}}{n+2}\\cdot \\sqrt{n}) \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\left(\\frac{2n}{n+2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\left(\\frac{2}{(1+\\frac{2}{n})}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{1+0} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $L=2$ is positive and finite. Now, the series\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{n}}\n$$\n\nis divergent by the $p$-series test. So, the series \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2\\sqrt{n}}{n+2}\n$$\n\nis also divergent by the limit comparison test.\n\n- For the final series, we use the $n$th term test for divergence. Let \n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{{n}^{3}}{n+2{n}^{2}}\n$$\n\nThen, we have $\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}=\\infty ,$ since the polynomial in the numerator grows faster than the polynomial in the denominator. Therefore, by the $n$th term test, the series\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{n}^{3}}{n+2{n}^{2}}\n$$\n\nis divergent.\n\nIn conclusion, only series $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{n!}\n$$ converges."] [:db/cas 17592186332842 :question/worked-solution "If we attempt to evaluate the limit, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\sqrt{x+1}-1}{x} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{0+1}-1}{0}=\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to first simplify the limit. Since the radical is in the numerator, we can multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the numerator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\sqrt{x+1}-1}{x} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{(\\sqrt{x+1}-1)(\\sqrt{x+1}+1)}{x(\\sqrt{x+1}+1)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{(\\sqrt{x+1})}^{2}-{1}^{2}}{x(\\sqrt{x+1}+1)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x+1-1}{x(\\sqrt{x+1}+1)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x}{x(\\sqrt{x+1}+1)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x+1}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{0+1}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "If we attempt to evaluate the limit, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\sqrt{x+1}-1}{x} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{0+1}-1}{0}=\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to first simplify the limit. Since the radical is in the numerator, we can multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the numerator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\sqrt{x+1}-1}{x} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{(\\sqrt{x+1}-1)(\\sqrt{x+1}+1)}{x(\\sqrt{x+1}+1)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{(\\sqrt{x+1})}^{2}-{1}^{2}}{x(\\sqrt{x+1}+1)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x+1-1}{x(\\sqrt{x+1}+1)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x}{x(\\sqrt{x+1}+1)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x+1}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{0+1}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186332845 :question/worked-solution "- Statement “It is continuous and differentiable at $x=1$” is false and statement “It is **not** differentiable at $x=1$” is true. Indeed,  let's investigate the continuity of $f(x).$ It is continuous everywhere except, possibly, $x=1.$ At this point, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}(-x+4)=-1+4=3 \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}(x+2)=1+2=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x)\\ne f(1)=0,\n$$\n\nand so $f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=1$ (it has a removable discontinuity there). Hence, it is also not differentiable at this point.\n\n- Statement “It is differentiable on the interval $(-2,2)$” is false. Since $f(x)$ is not differentiable at $x=1,$ it is not differentiable on any interval containing this point. Consequently, it is not differentiable on $(-2,2).$" "- Statement “It is continuous and differentiable at $x=1$” is false and statement “It is **not** differentiable at $x=1$” is true. Indeed,  let's investigate the continuity of $f(x).$ It is continuous everywhere except, possibly, $x=1.$ At this point, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{-}}(-x+4)=-1+4=3 \\\\ \\lim_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{+}}(x+2)=1+2=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x)=\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x)\\ne f(1)=0,\n$$\n\nand so $f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=1$ (it has a removable discontinuity there). Hence, it is also not differentiable at this point.\n\n- Statement “It is differentiable on the interval $(-2,2)$” is false. Since $f(x)$ is not differentiable at $x=1,$ it is not differentiable on any interval containing this point. Consequently, it is not differentiable on $(-2,2).$"] [:db/cas 17592186332946 :question/worked-solution "We find the area of the region bounded by the graph of the curve and the $x$-axis by calculating the integral\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{36+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe break up the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{36+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{4}{36+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{36+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nEvaluating the first of these integrals, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{4}{36+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{4}{36+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{6}{36+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }({\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\frac{x}{6}|}_{a}^{0}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡0-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\frac{a}{6}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\frac{a}{6} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the integrand is an **even** function. This means that we can immediately deduce that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{36+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since both improper integrals exist, we conclude that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{36+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{4}{36+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{36+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}+\\frac{\\pi }{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We find the area of the region bounded by the graph of the curve and the $x$-axis by calculating the integral\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{36+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe break up the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{36+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{4}{36+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{36+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nEvaluating the first of these integrals, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{4}{36+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{4}{36+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{6}{36+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }({\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\frac{x}{6}|}_{a}^{0}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡0-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\frac{a}{6}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\frac{a}{6} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the integrand is an **even** function. This means that we can immediately deduce that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{36+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since both improper integrals exist, we conclude that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{36+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{4}{36+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{36+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}+\\frac{\\pi }{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186332961 :question/worked-solution "We proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{6}^{\\infty }\\frac{\\text{d}z}{z} & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{6}^{a}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{z} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\operatorname{ln}⁡z]}_{6}^{a} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{ln}⁡a-\\operatorname{ln}⁡6) \\\\  & =\\infty -\\operatorname{ln}⁡6 \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the integral is divergent." "We proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{6}^{\\infty }\\frac{\\text{d}z}{z} & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{6}^{a}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{z} \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\operatorname{ln}⁡z]}_{6}^{a} \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{ln}⁡a-\\operatorname{ln}⁡6) \\\\  & =\\infty -\\operatorname{ln}⁡6 \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the integral is divergent."] [:db/cas 17592186332975 :question/worked-solution "We find the area of the region bounded by the graph of the curve and the $x$-axis by calculating the integral\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{{e}^{x}}{{e}^{2x}+1}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe break up the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{{e}^{x}}{{e}^{2x}+1}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{{e}^{x}}{{e}^{2x}+1}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{e}^{x}}{{e}^{2x}+1}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the antiderivative using a substitution. Let  $u={e}^{x}$,  then $\\text{d}u={e}^{x}\\text{d}x.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{e}^{x}}{{e}^{2x}+1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡{e}^{x}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now evaluate the first of the improper integrals, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{{e}^{x}}{{e}^{2x}+1}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡{e}^{x}){|}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡{e}^{0}-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡{e}^{a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the integrand is an **even** function.  Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =\\frac{{e}^{-x}}{{e}^{-2x}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2x}\\cdot {e}^{-x}}{{e}^{2x}\\cdot ({e}^{-2x}+1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{x}}{1+{e}^{2x}} \\\\  & =f(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that we can immediately deduce that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{e}^{x}}{{e}^{2x}+1}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since both improper integrals exist, we conclude that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{{e}^{x}}{{e}^{2x}+1}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{{e}^{x}}{{e}^{2x}+1}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{e}^{x}}{{e}^{2x}+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We find the area of the region bounded by the graph of the curve and the $x$-axis by calculating the integral\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{{e}^{x}}{{e}^{2x}+1}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe break up the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{{e}^{x}}{{e}^{2x}+1}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{{e}^{x}}{{e}^{2x}+1}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{e}^{x}}{{e}^{2x}+1}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the antiderivative using a substitution. Let  $u={e}^{x}$,  then $\\text{d}u={e}^{x}\\text{d}x.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{e}^{x}}{{e}^{2x}+1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡{e}^{x}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now evaluate the first of the improper integrals, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{{e}^{x}}{{e}^{2x}+1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡{e}^{x}){|}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡{e}^{0}-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡{e}^{a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the integrand is an **even** function.  Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =\\frac{{e}^{-x}}{{e}^{-2x}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2x}\\cdot {e}^{-x}}{{e}^{2x}\\cdot ({e}^{-2x}+1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{x}}{1+{e}^{2x}} \\\\  & =f(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that we can immediately deduce that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{e}^{x}}{{e}^{2x}+1}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since both improper integrals exist, we conclude that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{{e}^{x}}{{e}^{2x}+1}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{{e}^{x}}{{e}^{2x}+1}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{e}^{x}}{{e}^{2x}+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186335802 :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}:$\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{2y+x}{3x+y}=\\frac{2\\cdot 0+0}{3\\cdot 0+0}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, the function cannot be simplified by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\nWe attempt to evaluate the limit by approaching $(0,0)$ from different paths.\n\n- Statement “The limit evaluates to $0$ along the path $y=x$” is false. Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=x}\\frac{2y+x}{3x+y} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2x+x}{3x+x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{3x}{4x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{3}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “The limit evaluates to $-\\frac{1}{2}$ along the path $y=-x$” is true. Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=-x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=-x}\\frac{2y+x}{3x+y} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{-2x+x}{3x-x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{-x}{2x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “The limit exists and is equal to $0$” is false. Because the limit evaluates to different results on two different paths, we conclude that the limit does not exist:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{2y+x}{3x+y}=\\text{DNE}\n$$" "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{2y+x}{3x+y}=\\frac{2\\cdot 0+0}{3\\cdot 0+0}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, the function cannot be simplified by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\nWe attempt to evaluate the limit by approaching $(0,0)$ from different paths.\n\n- Statement “The limit evaluates to $0$ along the path $y=x$” is false. Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=x}\\frac{2y+x}{3x+y} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2x+x}{3x+x} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{3x}{4x} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{3}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “The limit evaluates to $-\\frac{1}{2}$ along the path $y=-x$” is true. Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=-x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=-x}\\frac{2y+x}{3x+y} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{-2x+x}{3x-x} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{-x}{2x} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “The limit exists and is equal to $0$” is false. Because the limit evaluates to different results on two different paths, we conclude that the limit does not exist:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{2y+x}{3x+y}=\\text{DNE}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186335841 :question/worked-solution "Substituting $x=\\pi $ directly into the limit, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\pi }{csc}^{2}⁡(\\frac{x}{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{6}) & ={csc}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & ={csc}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{sin}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Substituting $x=\\pi $ directly into the limit, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\pi }{csc}^{2}⁡(\\frac{x}{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{6}) & ={csc}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & ={csc}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{sin}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186335934 :question/worked-solution "When we divide the two functions, we get a constant, and the limit of a constant is just the constant itself:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{p(x)}{q(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{3{x}^{3}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{4}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "When we divide the two functions, we get a constant, and the limit of a constant is just the constant itself:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{p(x)}{q(x)} & =\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{3{x}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{4}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186335942 :question/problem "Find ${lim}_{x\\to 1}f(x)$ for the function given by\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}(x-1)^{3}-2\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x\\le -1 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡x+2\\, & \\text{ for }\\,1\\le x\\le 10.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Find $\\lim_{x\\to 1}f(x)$ for the function given by\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}(x-1)^{3}-2\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x\\le -1 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡x+2\\, & \\text{ for }\\,1\\le x\\le 10.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186335943 :question/worked-solution "First, we divide both the numerator and the denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{3}.$ Then, by noting that the reciprocals shrink to $0$ in the limit, we can then evaluate the resulting expression.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{3{x}^{4}+2}{{x}^{3}-7x} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{(\\frac{3{x}^{4}}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}})}{(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{7x}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{(3x+\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}})}{(1-\\frac{7}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{(3x+0)}{(1-0+0)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\,3x \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we divide both the numerator and the denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{3}.$ Then, by noting that the reciprocals shrink to $0$ in the limit, we can then evaluate the resulting expression.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{3{x}^{4}+2}{{x}^{3}-7x} & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{(\\frac{3{x}^{4}}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}})}{(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{7x}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{(3x+\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}})}{(1-\\frac{7}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{(3x+0)}{(1-0+0)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\,3x \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186335944 :question/worked-solution "First, we divide both the numerator and the denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{2}.$ Then, by noting that the reciprocals shrink to $0$ in the limit, we can then evaluate the resulting expression.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{{x}^{3}+x}{2{x}^{2}+x+4} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}})}{(\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{(x+\\frac{1}{x})}{(2+\\frac{1}{x}+\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{(x+0)}{(2+0+0)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{x}{2} \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we divide both the numerator and the denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{2}.$ Then, by noting that the reciprocals shrink to $0$ in the limit, we can then evaluate the resulting expression.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{{x}^{3}+x}{2{x}^{2}+x+4} & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}})}{(\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{(x+\\frac{1}{x})}{(2+\\frac{1}{x}+\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{(x+0)}{(2+0+0)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{x}{2} \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186335945 :question/worked-solution "First, we divide both the numerator and the denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{3}.$ Then, by noting that the reciprocals shrink to $0$ in the limit, we can then evaluate the resulting expression.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{4{x}^{5}-9}{{x}^{3}+8x} & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{(\\frac{4{x}^{5}}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{9}{{x}^{3}})}{(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{8x}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{(4{x}^{2}-\\frac{9}{{x}^{3}})}{(1+\\frac{8}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{4{x}^{2}-0}{1+0} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }4{x}^{2} \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we divide both the numerator and the denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{3}.$ Then, by noting that the reciprocals shrink to $0$ in the limit, we can then evaluate the resulting expression.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{4{x}^{5}-9}{{x}^{3}+8x} & =\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{(\\frac{4{x}^{5}}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{9}{{x}^{3}})}{(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{8x}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{(4{x}^{2}-\\frac{9}{{x}^{3}})}{(1+\\frac{8}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{4{x}^{2}-0}{1+0} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }4{x}^{2} \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186335946 :question/worked-solution "The horizontal asymptote is $y={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x).$ To find the horizontal asymptote, we apply the usual procedure of dividing the numerator and denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is $x.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{4{x}^{3}+6}{5x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{x}+\\frac{6}{x})}{\\left(\\frac{5x}{x}\\right)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(4{x}^{2}+\\frac{6}{x})}{5} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(4{x}^{2}+0)}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{x}^{2} \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe limit does not come out to a finite number. Therefore, there is no horizontal asymptote." "The horizontal asymptote is $y=\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }f(x).$ To find the horizontal asymptote, we apply the usual procedure of dividing the numerator and denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is $x.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{4{x}^{3}+6}{5x} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{x}+\\frac{6}{x})}{\\left(\\frac{5x}{x}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(4{x}^{2}+\\frac{6}{x})}{5} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(4{x}^{2}+0)}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }{x}^{2} \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe limit does not come out to a finite number. Therefore, there is no horizontal asymptote."] [:db/cas 17592186335947 :question/worked-solution "The horizontal asymptote is $y={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x).$ To find the horizontal asymptote, we apply the usual procedure of dividing the numerator and denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{2}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{-7{x}^{4}+3}{2{x}^{2}-4} \\\\  & {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{-7{x}^{4}}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}})}{(\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(-7{x}^{2}+\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}})}{(2-\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{-7{x}^{2}+0}{2-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{2}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(-{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe limit does not come out to a finite number. Therefore, there is no horizontal asymptote." "The horizontal asymptote is $y=\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }f(x).$ To find the horizontal asymptote, we apply the usual procedure of dividing the numerator and denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{2}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{-7{x}^{4}+3}{2{x}^{2}-4} \\\\  & \\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{-7{x}^{4}}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}})}{(\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(-7{x}^{2}+\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}})}{(2-\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{-7{x}^{2}+0}{2-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{2}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }(-{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe limit does not come out to a finite number. Therefore, there is no horizontal asymptote."] [:db/cas 17592186335948 :question/worked-solution "The horizontal asymptote is $y={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x).$ To find the horizontal asymptote, we apply the usual procedure of dividing the numerator and denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is $x.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{5+7{x}^{3}}{12x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{5}{x}+\\frac{7{x}^{3}}{x})}{\\left(\\frac{12x}{x}\\right)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{5}{x}+7{x}^{2})}{12} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(0+7{x}^{2})}{12} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{12}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{x}^{2} \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe limit does not come out to a finite number. Therefore, there is no horizontal asymptote." "The horizontal asymptote is $y=\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }f(x).$ To find the horizontal asymptote, we apply the usual procedure of dividing the numerator and denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is $x.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{5+7{x}^{3}}{12x} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{5}{x}+\\frac{7{x}^{3}}{x})}{\\left(\\frac{12x}{x}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{5}{x}+7{x}^{2})}{12} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(0+7{x}^{2})}{12} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{12}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }{x}^{2} \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe limit does not come out to a finite number. Therefore, there is no horizontal asymptote."] [:db/cas 17592186335951 :question/worked-solution "Notice that we can rewrite the first term as\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x){e}^{-2x}=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)}{{e}^{2x}}.\n$$\n\nBoth the numerator and denominator approach $\\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty .$ However, since ${e}^{2x}≫\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)$ for large values of $x,$ the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator. Consequently, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)}{{e}^{2x}}=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x){e}^{-2x}+3) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)}{{e}^{2x}}+3) \\\\  & =0+3 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Notice that we can rewrite the first term as\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x){e}^{-2x}=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)}{{e}^{2x}}.\n$$\n\nBoth the numerator and denominator approach $\\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty .$ However, since ${e}^{2x}≫\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)$ for large values of $x,$ the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator. Consequently, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)}{{e}^{2x}}=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x){e}^{-2x}+3) & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)}{{e}^{2x}}+3) \\\\  & =0+3 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186335957 :question/worked-solution "Notice that as $x\\to 0,$ both numerator and denominator approach $0.$\n\nSo, if we attempt to evaluate the limit directly, we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\left(-\\frac{2x}{9\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\right)=\\frac{0}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is an indeterminate form.\n\nInstead, let's recall the following special limit:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x}=1\n$$\n\nRewriting the given limit using the algebra of limits and applying our special limit, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\left(-\\frac{2x}{9\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\right) & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}(-\\frac{2}{9}\\cdot \\frac{x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{9}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{9}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to 0}{\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x}\\right)}^{-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{9}\\cdot {[{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{9}\\cdot {1}^{-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{9}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Notice that as $x\\to 0,$ both numerator and denominator approach $0.$\n\nSo, if we attempt to evaluate the limit directly, we get\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\left(-\\frac{2x}{9\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\right)=\\frac{0}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is an indeterminate form.\n\nInstead, let's recall the following special limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x}=1\n$$\n\nRewriting the given limit using the algebra of limits and applying our special limit, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\left(-\\frac{2x}{9\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\right) & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}(-\\frac{2}{9}\\cdot \\frac{x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{9}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{9}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to 0}{\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x}\\right)}^{-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{9}\\cdot {[\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{9}\\cdot {1}^{-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{9}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186335958 :question/worked-solution "Notice that as $x\\to 0,$ both numerator and denominator approach $0.$\n\nSo, if we attempt to evaluate the limit directly, we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-x}{3x}=\\frac{0}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is an indeterminate form.\n\nInstead, let's recall the following special limit:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x}=1\n$$\n\nRewriting the given limit using the algebra of limits and applying our special limit, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-x}{3x} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}(\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-x}{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-x}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to 0}(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x}-\\frac{x}{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to 0}(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x}-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot [{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x}-{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,1] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (1-1) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Notice that as $x\\to 0,$ both numerator and denominator approach $0.$\n\nSo, if we attempt to evaluate the limit directly, we get\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-x}{3x}=\\frac{0}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is an indeterminate form.\n\nInstead, let's recall the following special limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x}=1\n$$\n\nRewriting the given limit using the algebra of limits and applying our special limit, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-x}{3x} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}(\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-x}{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-x}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to 0}(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x}-\\frac{x}{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to 0}(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x}-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot [\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x}-\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,1] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (1-1) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186335994 :question/worked-solution "To find the value of $(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{2}),$ we need to find the number that is located in row $4$ and position $2$ of Pascal's triangle. \n\n**Important**: When counting the rows and positions in Pascal's triangle, we always start counting from zero! Remember:\n\n- The top row of Pascal's triangle is row $0.$\n\n- The first position of each row (the one of the left) is position number $0.$\n\nThe value located in row $4$ and position $2$ is highlighted below.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} &  &  &  &  & 1 \\\\  &  &  &  & 1 &  & 1 \\\\  &  &  & 1 &  & 2 &  & 1 \\\\  &  & 1 &  & 3 &  & 3 &  & 1 \\\\  & 1 &  & 4 &  & 6 &  & 4 &  & 1 \\\\ ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{2})=6.$" "To find the value of $(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{2}),$ we need to find the number that is located in row $4$ and position $2$ of Pascal's triangle. \n\n**Important**: When counting the rows and positions in Pascal's triangle, we always start counting from zero! Remember:\n\n- The top row of Pascal's triangle is row $0.$\n\n- The first position of each row (the one of the left) is position number $0.$\n\nThe value located in row $4$ and position $2$ is highlighted below.\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{ccccccccccc} &  &  &  &  & 1 &  &  &  &  &  \\\\  &  &  &  & 1 &  & 1 &  &  &  &  \\\\  &  &  & 1 &  & 2 &  & 1 &  &  &  \\\\  &  & 1 &  & 3 &  & 3 &  & 1 &  &  \\\\  & 1 &  & 4 &  & 6 &  & 4 &  & 1 &  \\\\ ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮\\end{array}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{2})=6.$"] [:db/cas 17592186335996 :question/worked-solution "To find $\\left(\\frac{5}{2}\\right),$ we need to find the number that is located in row $5$ and position $2$ of Pascal's triangle. \n\n**Important**: When counting the rows and positions in Pascal's triangle, we always start counting from zero! Remember:\n\n- The top row of Pascal's triangle is row $0.$\n\n- The first position of each row (the one of the left) is position number $0.$\n\nThe location of $\\left(\\frac{5}{2}\\right)$ in Pascal's triangle is highlighted below.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} &  &  &  &  &  &  &  & \\left(\\frac{0}{0}\\right) \\\\  &  &  &  &  &  &  & \\left(\\frac{1}{0}\\right) &  & \\left(\\frac{1}{1}\\right) \\\\  &  &  &  &  &  & \\left(\\frac{2}{0}\\right) &  & \\left(\\frac{2}{1}\\right) &  & \\left(\\frac{2}{2}\\right) \\\\  &  &  &  &  & \\left(\\frac{3}{0}\\right) &  & \\left(\\frac{3}{1}\\right) &  & \\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right) &  & \\left(\\frac{3}{3}\\right) \\\\  &  &  &  & \\left(\\frac{4}{0}\\right) &  & \\left(\\frac{4}{1}\\right) &  & \\left(\\frac{4}{2}\\right) &  & \\left(\\frac{4}{3}\\right) &  & \\left(\\frac{4}{4}\\right) \\\\  &  &  & \\left(\\frac{5}{0}\\right) &  & \\left(\\frac{5}{1}\\right) &  & \\left(\\frac{5}{2}\\right) &  & \\left(\\frac{5}{3}\\right) &  & \\left(\\frac{5}{4}\\right) &  & \\left(\\frac{5}{5}\\right) \\\\  &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, $\\left(\\frac{5}{2}\\right)$ is the sum of the two closest numbers located in the previous row. So,\n\n$$\n\\left(\\frac{5}{2}\\right)=\\left(\\frac{4}{1}\\right)+\\left(\\frac{4}{2}\\right)\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is $\\left(\\frac{4}{2}\\right).$" "To find $\\left(\\frac{5}{2}\\right),$ we need to find the number that is located in row $5$ and position $2$ of Pascal's triangle. \n\n**Important**: When counting the rows and positions in Pascal's triangle, we always start counting from zero! Remember:\n\n- The top row of Pascal's triangle is row $0.$\n\n- The first position of each row (the one of the left) is position number $0.$\n\nThe location of $\\left(\\frac{5}{2}\\right)$ in Pascal's triangle is highlighted below.\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{ccccccccccccccc} &  &  &  &  &  &  &  & \\left(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{0}{0}\\right) &  &  &  &  &  &  \\\\  &  &  &  &  &  &  & \\left(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{1}{0}\\right) &  & \\left(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{1}{1}\\right) &  &  &  &  &  \\\\  &  &  &  &  &  & \\left(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{2}{0}\\right) &  & \\left(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{2}{1}\\right) &  & \\left(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{2}{2}\\right) &  &  &  &  \\\\  &  &  &  &  & \\left(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{3}{0}\\right) &  & \\left(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{3}{1}\\right) &  & \\left(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{3}{2}\\right) &  & \\left(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{3}{3}\\right) &  &  &  \\\\  &  &  &  & \\left(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{0}\\right) &  & \\left(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{1}\\right) &  & \\left(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{2}\\right) &  & \\left(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{3}\\right) &  & \\left(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{4}\\right) &  &  \\\\  &  &  & \\left(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{0}\\right) &  & \\left(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{1}\\right) &  & \\left(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{2}\\right) &  & \\left(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{3}\\right) &  & \\left(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{4}\\right) &  & \\left(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{5}\\right) &  \\\\  &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮\\end{array}\n$$\n\nNow, $\\left(\\frac{5}{2}\\right)$ is the sum of the two closest numbers located in the previous row. So,\n\n$$\n\\left(\\frac{5}{2}\\right)=\\left(\\frac{4}{1}\\right)+\\left(\\frac{4}{2}\\right)\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is $\\left(\\frac{4}{2}\\right).$"] [:db/cas 17592186335998 :question/worked-solution "To find $\\left(\\frac{5}{4}\\right),$ we need to find the number that is located in row $5$ and position $4$ of Pascal's triangle. \n\n**Important**: When counting the rows and positions in Pascal's triangle, we always start counting from zero! Remember:\n\n- The top row of Pascal's triangle is row $0.$\n\n- The first position of each row (the one of the left) is position number $0.$\n\nThe location of $\\left(\\frac{5}{4}\\right)$ in Pascal's triangle is highlighted below.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} &  &  &  &  &  &  &  & \\left(\\frac{0}{0}\\right) \\\\  &  &  &  &  &  &  & \\left(\\frac{1}{0}\\right) &  & \\left(\\frac{1}{1}\\right) \\\\  &  &  &  &  &  & \\left(\\frac{2}{0}\\right) &  & \\left(\\frac{2}{1}\\right) &  & \\left(\\frac{2}{2}\\right) \\\\  &  &  &  &  & \\left(\\frac{3}{0}\\right) &  & \\left(\\frac{3}{1}\\right) &  & \\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right) &  & \\left(\\frac{3}{3}\\right) \\\\  &  &  &  & \\left(\\frac{4}{0}\\right) &  & \\left(\\frac{4}{1}\\right) &  & \\left(\\frac{4}{2}\\right) &  & \\left(\\frac{4}{3}\\right) &  & \\left(\\frac{4}{4}\\right) \\\\  &  &  & \\left(\\frac{5}{0}\\right) &  & \\left(\\frac{5}{1}\\right) &  & \\left(\\frac{5}{2}\\right) &  & \\left(\\frac{5}{3}\\right) &  & \\left(\\frac{5}{4}\\right) &  & \\left(\\frac{5}{5}\\right) \\\\  &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, $\\left(\\frac{5}{4}\\right)$ is the sum of the two closest numbers located in the previous row. So,\n\n$$\n\\left(\\frac{5}{4}\\right)=\\left(\\frac{4}{3}\\right)+\\left(\\frac{4}{4}\\right)\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is $\\left(\\frac{4}{4}\\right).$" "To find $\\left(\\frac{5}{4}\\right),$ we need to find the number that is located in row $5$ and position $4$ of Pascal's triangle. \n\n**Important**: When counting the rows and positions in Pascal's triangle, we always start counting from zero! Remember:\n\n- The top row of Pascal's triangle is row $0.$\n\n- The first position of each row (the one of the left) is position number $0.$\n\nThe location of $\\left(\\frac{5}{4}\\right)$ in Pascal's triangle is highlighted below.\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{ccccccccccccccc} &  &  &  &  &  &  &  & \\left(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{0}{0}\\right) &  &  &  &  &  &  \\\\  &  &  &  &  &  &  & \\left(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{1}{0}\\right) &  & \\left(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{1}{1}\\right) &  &  &  &  &  \\\\  &  &  &  &  &  & \\left(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{2}{0}\\right) &  & \\left(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{2}{1}\\right) &  & \\left(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{2}{2}\\right) &  &  &  &  \\\\  &  &  &  &  & \\left(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{3}{0}\\right) &  & \\left(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{3}{1}\\right) &  & \\left(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{3}{2}\\right) &  & \\left(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{3}{3}\\right) &  &  &  \\\\  &  &  &  & \\left(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{0}\\right) &  & \\left(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{1}\\right) &  & \\left(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{2}\\right) &  & \\left(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{3}\\right) &  & \\left(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{4}\\right) &  &  \\\\  &  &  & \\left(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{0}\\right) &  & \\left(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{1}\\right) &  & \\left(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{2}\\right) &  & \\left(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{3}\\right) &  & \\left(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{4}\\right) &  & \\left(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{5}\\right) &  \\\\  &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮\\end{array}\n$$\n\nNow, $\\left(\\frac{5}{4}\\right)$ is the sum of the two closest numbers located in the previous row. So,\n\n$$\n\\left(\\frac{5}{4}\\right)=\\left(\\frac{4}{3}\\right)+\\left(\\frac{4}{4}\\right)\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is $\\left(\\frac{4}{4}\\right).$"] [:db/cas 17592186338887 :question/worked-solution "We're told that $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=-1.$ According to the definition of continuity, this means that ${lim}_{x\\to (-1)}f(x)$ exists, and\n\n$$\nf(-1)={lim}_{x\\to {(-1)}^{-}}f(x)=8.\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{f(-1)+1} & =\\sqrt{8+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We're told that $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=-1.$ According to the definition of continuity, this means that $\\lim_{x\\to (-1)}f(x)$ exists, and\n\n$$\nf(-1)=\\lim_{x\\to {(-1)}^{-}}f(x)=8.\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{f(-1)+1} & =\\sqrt{8+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186338888 :question/worked-solution "We're told that $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=0.$ According to the definition of continuity, this means that ${lim}_{x\\to 0}f(x)$ exists, and\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x)=f(0)=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}{({(f(x))}^{2}-3)}^{2} & ={({2}^{2}-3)}^{2}={1}^{2}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We're told that $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=0.$ According to the definition of continuity, this means that $\\lim_{x\\to 0}f(x)$ exists, and\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x)=f(0)=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}{({(f(x))}^{2}-3)}^{2} & ={({2}^{2}-3)}^{2}={1}^{2}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186338889 :question/worked-solution "We're told that $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=4.$ According to the definition of continuity, this means that ${lim}_{x\\to 4}f(x)$ exists, and\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 4}f(x)=f(4)=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 4}{({x}^{2}-6f(x))}^{2} & ={({4}^{2}-6(2))}^{2} \\\\  & ={(16-12)}^{2} \\\\  & ={4}^{2} \\\\  & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We're told that $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=4.$ According to the definition of continuity, this means that $\\lim_{x\\to 4}f(x)$ exists, and\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 4}f(x)=f(4)=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 4}{({x}^{2}-6f(x))}^{2} & ={({4}^{2}-6(2))}^{2} \\\\  & ={(16-12)}^{2} \\\\  & ={4}^{2} \\\\  & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186338890 :question/worked-solution "Let's check the conditions necessary for $f(x)$ to be continuous at $x=-2.$\n\n- $f(-2)=6,$ so $f(-2)$ exists. $✓$\n\n- We calculate the one-sided limits and check if they coincide:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-2)^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to (-2)^{-}}({x}^{3}-x) \\\\  & =(-2)^{3}-(-2) \\\\  & =-8+2 \\\\  & =-6, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to (-2)^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to (-2)^{+}}(-{x}^{2}+x) \\\\  & =-(-2)^{2}+(-2) \\\\  & =-4-2 \\\\  & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the overall limit exists and ${lim}_{x\\to (-2)}f(x)=-6$ $✓$\n\n- The limit of the function is not equal to the function value: ${lim}_{x\\to (-2)}f(x)=-6,$ while $f(-2)=6.$ $\\,\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the function $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=-2$ because ${lim}_{x\\to (-2)}f(x)\\ne f(-2).$" "Let's check the conditions necessary for $f(x)$ to be continuous at $x=-2.$\n\n- $f(-2)=6,$ so $f(-2)$ exists. $✓$\n\n- We calculate the one-sided limits and check if they coincide:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (-2)^{-}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to (-2)^{-}}({x}^{3}-x) \\\\  & =(-2)^{3}-(-2) \\\\  & =-8+2 \\\\  & =-6, \\\\ \\lim_{x\\to (-2)^{+}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to (-2)^{+}}(-{x}^{2}+x) \\\\  & =-(-2)^{2}+(-2) \\\\  & =-4-2 \\\\  & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the overall limit exists and $\\lim_{x\\to (-2)}f(x)=-6$ $✓$\n\n- The limit of the function is not equal to the function value: $\\lim_{x\\to (-2)}f(x)=-6,$ while $f(-2)=6.$ $\\,\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the function $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=-2$ because $\\lim_{x\\to (-2)}f(x)\\ne f(-2).$"] [:db/cas 17592186338891 :question/worked-solution "Let's check the conditions necessary for $f(x)$ to be continuous at $x=-1.$\n\n- $f(-1)=2,$ so $f(-1)$ exists. $✓$\n\n- We calculate the one-sided limits and check if they coincide:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}\\left(\\frac{{x}^{2}+1}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{(-1)^{2}+1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1+1}{2} \\\\  & =1, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}\\left(\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}+1}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{(-1)^{2}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{1+1} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the overall limit exists and ${lim}_{x\\to (-1)}f(x)=1$ $✓$\n\n- The limit of the function is not equal to the function value: ${lim}_{x\\to (-1)}f(x)=1,$ while $f(-1)=2.$ $\\,\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the function $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=-1$ because ${lim}_{x\\to (-1)}f(x)\\ne f(-1).$" "Let's check the conditions necessary for $f(x)$ to be continuous at $x=-1.$\n\n- $f(-1)=2,$ so $f(-1)$ exists. $✓$\n\n- We calculate the one-sided limits and check if they coincide:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}\\left(\\frac{{x}^{2}+1}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{(-1)^{2}+1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1+1}{2} \\\\  & =1, \\\\ \\lim_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}\\left(\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}+1}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{(-1)^{2}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{1+1} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the overall limit exists and $\\lim_{x\\to (-1)}f(x)=1$ $✓$\n\n- The limit of the function is not equal to the function value: $\\lim_{x\\to (-1)}f(x)=1,$ while $f(-1)=2.$ $\\,\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the function $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=-1$ because $\\lim_{x\\to (-1)}f(x)\\ne f(-1).$"] [:db/cas 17592186338892 :question/worked-solution "Let's check the conditions necessary for $f(x)$ to be continuous at $x=2.$\n\n- $f(2)=2(2)-3=1,$ so $f(2)$ exists. $✓$\n\n- We calculate the one-sided limits and check if they coincide:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}(-2x+3) \\\\  & =-2(2)+3 \\\\  & =-1, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}(2x-3) \\\\  & =2(2)-3 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the one-sided limits exist but are not equal. Therefore, ${lim}_{x\\to 2}f(x)$ does not exist. $\\times $ $\\,\\,\\,$\n\nTherefore, the function $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=2$ because ${lim}_{x\\to 2}f(x)$ does not exist." "Let's check the conditions necessary for $f(x)$ to be continuous at $x=2.$\n\n- $f(2)=2(2)-3=1,$ so $f(2)$ exists. $✓$\n\n- We calculate the one-sided limits and check if they coincide:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{-}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{-}}(-2x+3) \\\\  & =-2(2)+3 \\\\  & =-1, \\\\ \\lim_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{+}}(2x-3) \\\\  & =2(2)-3 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the one-sided limits exist but are not equal. Therefore, $\\lim_{x\\to 2}f(x)$ does not exist. $\\times $ $\\,\\,\\,$\n\nTherefore, the function $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=2$ because $\\lim_{x\\to 2}f(x)$ does not exist."] [:db/cas 17592186338909 :question/problem "Given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(x) & ={\\begin{aligned}3x, & x\\ne 4 \\\\ 13, & x=4,\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." "Given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(x) & =\\begin{cases}3x, & x\\ne 4 \\\\ 13, & x=4,\\end{cases}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186338909 :question/worked-solution "Let's check our continuity conditions:\n\n- $g(4)=13$ exists$\\,\\,✓$\n\n- The left and right-sided limits are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,{4}^{-}}g(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,{4}^{-}}3x=12, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to \\,{4}^{+}}g(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,{4}^{+}}3x=12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the left and right-sided limits are equal, we conclude that statement ${lim}_{x\\to {4}^{+}}g(x)={lim}_{x\\to {4}^{-}}g(x)$ is true and\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 4}g(x)=12.\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\n- The value of the limit is ${lim}_{x\\to 4}g(x)=12,$ while the value of the function is $g(4)=13.$ So, statement ${lim}_{x\\to 4}g(x)=g(4)$ is false and\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 4}g(x)\\ne g(4)\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nSince the third condition failed, the function has a removable discontinuity at $x=4,$ and statement “$g(x)$ has a removable discontinuity at $x=4$” is true.\n\nIn conclusion, only statements ${lim}_{x\\to {4}^{+}}g(x)={lim}_{x\\to {4}^{-}}g(x)$ and “$g(x)$ has a removable discontinuity at $x=4$” are true." "Let's check our continuity conditions:\n\n- $g(4)=13$ exists$\\,\\,✓$\n\n- The left and right-sided limits are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\,{4}^{-}}g(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to \\,{4}^{-}}3x=12, \\\\ \\lim_{x\\to \\,{4}^{+}}g(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to \\,{4}^{+}}3x=12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the left and right-sided limits are equal, we conclude that statement $\\lim_{x\\to {4}^{+}}g(x)=\\lim_{x\\to {4}^{-}}g(x)$ is true and\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 4}g(x)=12.\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\n- The value of the limit is $\\lim_{x\\to 4}g(x)=12,$ while the value of the function is $g(4)=13.$ So, statement $\\lim_{x\\to 4}g(x)=g(4)$ is false and\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 4}g(x)\\ne g(4)\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nSince the third condition failed, the function has a removable discontinuity at $x=4,$ and statement “$g(x)$ has a removable discontinuity at $x=4$” is true.\n\nIn conclusion, only statements $\\lim_{x\\to {4}^{+}}g(x)=\\lim_{x\\to {4}^{-}}g(x)$ and “$g(x)$ has a removable discontinuity at $x=4$” are true."] [:db/cas 17592186338910 :question/problem "Given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={\\begin{aligned}4x, & x<2 \\\\ 4, & x=2 \\\\ {x}^{2}, & x>2\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements is true?\n\nSelect all that apply." "Given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\begin{cases}4x, & x<2 \\\\ 4, & x=2 \\\\ {x}^{2}, & x>2\\end{cases}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements is true?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186338910 :question/worked-solution "Let's check our continuity conditions:\n\n- $f(2)=4$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, statement “$f(2)$ exists” is true.\n\n- The left and right-sided limits are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{-}}(4x)=8, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{+}}{x}^{2}=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that statement ${lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}f(x)$ is false and\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x)\\ne {lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}f(x).\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nSince the second condition failed, $x=2$ is not a removable discontinuity. So, statement “$f(x)$ has a removable discontinuity at $x=2$” is false.\n\nIn conclusion, only statement “$f(2)$ exists” is true." "Let's check our continuity conditions:\n\n- $f(2)=4$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, statement “$f(2)$ exists” is true.\n\n- The left and right-sided limits are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\,{2}^{-}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to \\,{2}^{-}}(4x)=8, \\\\ \\lim_{x\\to \\,{2}^{+}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to \\,{2}^{+}}{x}^{2}=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that statement $\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x)=\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{-}}f(x)$ is false and\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x)\\ne {lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}f(x).\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nSince the second condition failed, $x=2$ is not a removable discontinuity. So, statement “$f(x)$ has a removable discontinuity at $x=2$” is false.\n\nIn conclusion, only statement “$f(2)$ exists” is true."] [:db/cas 17592186339089 :question/worked-solution "In this case, we have an indeterminate form of type $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty },$ so applying  L'Hopital's rule yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{100x}{{x}^{2}-1} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{[100x]^{′}}{[{x}^{2}-1]^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{100}{2x} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "In this case, we have an indeterminate form of type $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty },$ so applying  L'Hopital's rule yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{100x}{{x}^{2}-1} & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{[100x]^{′}}{[{x}^{2}-1]^{′}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{100}{2x} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186339090 :question/worked-solution "Here the limits of the numerator and denominator are ${lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x=-\\infty $ and ${lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}{e}^{1/x}=\\infty ,$ respectively, so we have the case $\\frac{-\\infty }{\\infty }.$\n\nApplying L'Hopital's rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{e}^{1/x}} & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{[\\operatorname{ln}⁡x]^{′}}{[{e}^{1/x}]^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right)}{{e}^{1/x}\\left(-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\right)} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{{x}^{-1}}{{e}^{1/x}\\cdot {x}^{-2}} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{x}{{e}^{1/x}} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Here the limits of the numerator and denominator are $\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x=-\\infty $ and $\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}{e}^{1/x}=\\infty ,$ respectively, so we have the case $\\frac{-\\infty }{\\infty }.$\n\nApplying L'Hopital's rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{e}^{1/x}} & =\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{[\\operatorname{ln}⁡x]^{′}}{[{e}^{1/x}]^{′}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right)}{{e}^{1/x}\\left(-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\right)} \\\\  & =-\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{{x}^{-1}}{{e}^{1/x}\\cdot {x}^{-2}} \\\\  & =-\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{x}{{e}^{1/x}} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186339091 :question/worked-solution "Here the limits of the numerator and denominator are ${lim}_{x\\to 0}{e}^{2/{x}^{2}}=\\infty $ and ${lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{1}{2{x}^{2}}=\\infty ,$ respectively, so we have the case $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty }.$\n\nApplying L'Hopital's rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{e}^{2/{x}^{2}}}{\\left(\\frac{1}{2{x}^{2}}\\right)} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{[{e}^{2/{x}^{2}}]}^{′}}{{[\\frac{1}{2{x}^{2}}]}^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{e}^{2/{x}^{2}}\\left(-\\frac{4}{{x}^{3}}\\right)}{\\left(-\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}\\right)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{4{e}^{2/{x}^{2}}\\cancel{\\left(-\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}\\right)}}{\\cancel{\\left(-\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}\\right)}} \\\\  & =4{lim}_{x\\to 0}{e}^{2/{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Here the limits of the numerator and denominator are $\\lim_{x\\to 0}{e}^{2/{x}^{2}}=\\infty $ and $\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{1}{2{x}^{2}}=\\infty ,$ respectively, so we have the case $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty }.$\n\nApplying L'Hopital's rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{e}^{2/{x}^{2}}}{\\left(\\frac{1}{2{x}^{2}}\\right)} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{[{e}^{2/{x}^{2}}]}^{′}}{{[\\frac{1}{2{x}^{2}}]}^{′}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{e}^{2/{x}^{2}}\\left(-\\frac{4}{{x}^{3}}\\right)}{\\left(-\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{4{e}^{2/{x}^{2}}\\cancel{\\left(-\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}\\right)}}{\\cancel{\\left(-\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}\\right)}} \\\\  & =4\\lim_{x\\to 0}{e}^{2/{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186339092 :question/worked-solution "Here the limits of the numerator and denominator are ${lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}=\\infty $ and ${lim}_{x\\to 0}(\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}-1)=\\infty ,$ respectively, so we have the case $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty }.$\n\nApplying L'Hopital's rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\left(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\right)}{(\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}-1)} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{[\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}]}^{′}}{{[\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}-1]}^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\left(-\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}\\right)}{\\left(-\\frac{4}{{x}^{5}}\\right)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2{x}^{-3}}{4{x}^{-5}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Here the limits of the numerator and denominator are $\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}=\\infty $ and $\\lim_{x\\to 0}(\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}-1)=\\infty ,$ respectively, so we have the case $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty }.$\n\nApplying L'Hopital's rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\left(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\right)}{(\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}-1)} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{[\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}]}^{′}}{{[\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}-1]}^{′}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\left(-\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}\\right)}{\\left(-\\frac{4}{{x}^{5}}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2{x}^{-3}}{4{x}^{-5}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186339093 :question/worked-solution "Here the limits of the numerator and denominator are ${lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{1}{{x}^{6}}=\\infty $ and ${lim}_{x\\to 0}(\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}+8)=\\infty ,$ respectively, so we have the case $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty }.$\n\nApplying L'Hopital's rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\left(\\frac{1}{{x}^{6}}\\right)}{(\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}+8)} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{[\\frac{1}{{x}^{6}}]}^{′}}{{[\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}+8]}^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\left(-\\frac{6}{{x}^{7}}\\right)}{\\left(-\\frac{4}{{x}^{5}}\\right)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{6{x}^{-7}}{4{x}^{-5}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{3}{2{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Here the limits of the numerator and denominator are $\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{1}{{x}^{6}}=\\infty $ and $\\lim_{x\\to 0}(\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}+8)=\\infty ,$ respectively, so we have the case $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty }.$\n\nApplying L'Hopital's rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\left(\\frac{1}{{x}^{6}}\\right)}{(\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}+8)} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{[\\frac{1}{{x}^{6}}]}^{′}}{{[\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}+8]}^{′}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\left(-\\frac{6}{{x}^{7}}\\right)}{\\left(-\\frac{4}{{x}^{5}}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{6{x}^{-7}}{4{x}^{-5}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{3}{2{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186341907 :question/worked-solution "Since $f(x)$ is right-continuous at $x=-1,$ we must have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}f(x)=f(-1)=-2.\n$$\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}\\,\\frac{1}{3f(x)-10x} & =\\frac{1}{3{lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}f(x)-10{lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3(-2)-10(-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Since $f(x)$ is right-continuous at $x=-1,$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}f(x)=f(-1)=-2.\n$$\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}\\,\\frac{1}{3f(x)-10x} & =\\frac{1}{3\\lim_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}f(x)-10\\lim_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3(-2)-10(-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186341918 :question/worked-solution "The definite integral can be defined in terms of the limit of a left Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{j})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\n    \n\nwhere $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ and ${x}_{j}=a+jΔx.$\n\nIn the definite integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-4}^{-2}-5{x}^{-2}\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nthe function is $f(x)=-5{x}^{-2},$ the lower bound is $a=-4,$ and the upper bound is $b=-2.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{-2-(-4)}{n}=\\frac{2}{n},\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-4}^{-2}-5{x}^{-2}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{j})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}[f(-4+j\\cdot \\frac{2}{n})\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}[f(-4+\\frac{2j}{n})\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}[-5{(-4+\\frac{2j}{n})}^{-2}\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The definite integral can be defined in terms of the limit of a left Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{j})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\n    \n\nwhere $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ and ${x}_{j}=a+jΔx.$\n\nIn the definite integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-4}^{-2}-5{x}^{-2}\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nthe function is $f(x)=-5{x}^{-2},$ the lower bound is $a=-4,$ and the upper bound is $b=-2.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{-2-(-4)}{n}=\\frac{2}{n},\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-4}^{-2}-5{x}^{-2}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{j})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}[f(-4+j\\cdot \\frac{2}{n})\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}] \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}[f(-4+\\frac{2j}{n})\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}] \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}[-5{(-4+\\frac{2j}{n})}^{-2}\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186341919 :question/worked-solution "The definite integral can be defined in terms of the limit of a right Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{m=1}^{n}f({x}_{m})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{m}=a+mΔx$ and $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}.$\n\nIn the definite integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{6}ln⁡(4x-3)\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nthe function is $f(x)=ln⁡(4x-3),$ the lower bound is $a=1$ and the upper bound is $b=6.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{6-1}{n}=\\frac{5}{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{6}ln⁡(4x-3)\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{m=1}^{n}f({x}_{m})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{m=1}^{n}[f(1+m\\cdot \\frac{5}{n})\\cdot \\frac{5}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{m=1}^{n}[f(1+\\frac{5m}{n})\\cdot \\frac{5}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{m=1}^{n}[ln⁡(4(1+\\frac{5m}{n})-3)\\cdot \\frac{5}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{m=1}^{n}[ln⁡(1+\\frac{20m}{n})\\cdot \\frac{5}{n}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The definite integral can be defined in terms of the limit of a right Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{m=1}^{n}f({x}_{m})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{m}=a+mΔx$ and $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}.$\n\nIn the definite integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{6}ln⁡(4x-3)\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nthe function is $f(x)=ln⁡(4x-3),$ the lower bound is $a=1$ and the upper bound is $b=6.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{6-1}{n}=\\frac{5}{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{6}ln⁡(4x-3)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{m=1}^{n}f({x}_{m})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{m=1}^{n}[f(1+m\\cdot \\frac{5}{n})\\cdot \\frac{5}{n}] \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{m=1}^{n}[f(1+\\frac{5m}{n})\\cdot \\frac{5}{n}] \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{m=1}^{n}[ln⁡(4(1+\\frac{5m}{n})-3)\\cdot \\frac{5}{n}] \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{m=1}^{n}[ln⁡(1+\\frac{20m}{n})\\cdot \\frac{5}{n}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186341933 :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each statement individually.\n\n- Statement “It is continuous and differentiable at $x=0$” is false. First, we investigate the continuity of $f(x)$ . It is continuous everywhere except, possibly, $x=0.$ At this point, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}({x}^{3}-1)={0}^{3}-1=-1 \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}({x}^{3}-1)={0}^{3}-1=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x)\\ne f(0)=1,\n$$\n\n \nand so $f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=0.$ Therefore, it's not differentiable there either.\n\n- Statement “It is **not** continuous at $x=0$ but it is differentiable at $x=0$” is false. Since $f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=0,$ it is also not differentiable there.\n\n- Statement “It is **not** differentiable on the interval $x\\in (-1,1)$” is true. Since $f(x)$ is not differentiable at $x=0,$ it is not differentiable on any interval that includes this point. Consequently, it is not differentiable in $x\\in (-1,1).$\n\nTherefore, only statement “It is **not** differentiable on the interval $x\\in (-1,1)$” is true." "Let's analyze each statement individually.\n\n- Statement “It is continuous and differentiable at $x=0$” is false. First, we investigate the continuity of $f(x)$ . It is continuous everywhere except, possibly, $x=0.$ At this point, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}({x}^{3}-1)={0}^{3}-1=-1 \\\\ \\lim_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{-}}({x}^{3}-1)={0}^{3}-1=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x)=\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x)\\ne f(0)=1,\n$$\n\n \nand so $f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=0.$ Therefore, it's not differentiable there either.\n\n- Statement “It is **not** continuous at $x=0$ but it is differentiable at $x=0$” is false. Since $f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=0,$ it is also not differentiable there.\n\n- Statement “It is **not** differentiable on the interval $x\\in (-1,1)$” is true. Since $f(x)$ is not differentiable at $x=0,$ it is not differentiable on any interval that includes this point. Consequently, it is not differentiable in $x\\in (-1,1).$\n\nTherefore, only statement “It is **not** differentiable on the interval $x\\in (-1,1)$” is true."] [:db/cas 17592186341934 :question/worked-solution "- Statement “It is continuous at $x=1$” and “It is **not** differentiable at $x=1$” are true. Indeed, let's investigate the continuity of $f(x).$ It is continuous everywhere except, possibly at  $x=1.$ At this point, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}(x+1)=1+1=2 \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}2=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x)=f(1)=2,\n$$\n\nand so $f(x)$ is  continuous at $x=1$ . However, $f(x)$ is not differentiable at $x=1$ because it has a corner point there: \n\n$\\,$ ${f}^{′}(x)=1\\,$  if  $\\,x<1$ and ${f}^{′}(x)=0\\,$ if $\\,x>1$\n\n- Statement “It is differentiable on the interval $(0,2)$” is false. Since $f(x)$ is not differentiable at $x=1,$ it is not differentiable on any interval containing this point. Consequently, it is not differentiable on $(0,2).$\n\nTherefore, only statements “It is continuous at $x=1$” and “It is **not** differentiable at $x=1$” are true." "- Statement “It is continuous at $x=1$” and “It is **not** differentiable at $x=1$” are true. Indeed, let's investigate the continuity of $f(x).$ It is continuous everywhere except, possibly at  $x=1.$ At this point, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{-}}(x+1)=1+1=2 \\\\ \\lim_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{+}}2=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x)=\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x)=f(1)=2,\n$$\n\nand so $f(x)$ is  continuous at $x=1$ . However, $f(x)$ is not differentiable at $x=1$ because it has a corner point there: \n\n$\\,$ ${f}^{′}(x)=1\\,$  if  $\\,x<1$ and ${f}^{′}(x)=0\\,$ if $\\,x>1$\n\n- Statement “It is differentiable on the interval $(0,2)$” is false. Since $f(x)$ is not differentiable at $x=1,$ it is not differentiable on any interval containing this point. Consequently, it is not differentiable on $(0,2).$\n\nTherefore, only statements “It is continuous at $x=1$” and “It is **not** differentiable at $x=1$” are true."] [:db/cas 17592186341972 :question/worked-solution "We have ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at the critical points $A$ and $B.$ Note the following:\n\n- If $f(x)$ is strictly increasing, then ${f}^{′}(x)>0.$\n\n- If $f(x)$ is strictly decreasing, then ${f}^{′}(x)<0.$\n\nThe information about ${f}^{′}(x)$ can be summarized in the following table:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & (-\\infty ,0) & 0 & (0,3) & 3 & (3,\\infty ) \\\\ f & ↘ & \\text{min} & ↗ & \\text{max} & ↘ \\\\ {f}^{′} & - & 0 & + & 0 & -\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{′}(x)>0$ on the interval $(0,3).$" "We have ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at the critical points $A$ and $B.$ Note the following:\n\n- If $f(x)$ is strictly increasing, then ${f}^{′}(x)>0.$\n\n- If $f(x)$ is strictly decreasing, then ${f}^{′}(x)<0.$\n\nThe information about ${f}^{′}(x)$ can be summarized in the following table:\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{c|c|c|c|c|c}x & (-\\infty ,0) & 0 & (0,3) & 3 & (3,\\infty ) \\\\ \\hline f & ↘ & \\text{min} & ↗ & \\text{max} & ↘ \\\\ \\hline {f}^{′} & - & 0 & + & 0 & -\\end{array}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{′}(x)>0$ on the interval $(0,3).$"] [:db/cas 17592186341990 :question/worked-solution "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nSo, let's check whether the function is continuous on each of the intervals.\n\n- First, consider $[-1,1].$ In this case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}({2}^{-x})={2}^{0}=1, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}(-1)=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the limits from the left and from the right of $x=0$ are not the same. Hence, $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=0.$ Therefore, $f(x)$ is not continuous on the interval $[-1,1],$ and the extreme value theorem is not applicable on this interval.\n\n- Now, consider the interval $[\\frac{1}{2},1].$ In this interval, $f(x)={2}^{-x}$ and so $f(x)$ is continuous on $[\\frac{1}{2},1].$  Therefore, the extreme value theorem is applicable on this interval.\n\n- Finally, the interval $[-1,0)$, is not closed, so the extreme value theorem is not applicable in this case.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is $[\\frac{1}{2},1]$ only." "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nSo, let's check whether the function is continuous on each of the intervals.\n\n- First, consider $[-1,1].$ In this case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}({2}^{-x})={2}^{0}=1, \\\\ \\lim_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}(-1)=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the limits from the left and from the right of $x=0$ are not the same. Hence, $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=0.$ Therefore, $f(x)$ is not continuous on the interval $[-1,1],$ and the extreme value theorem is not applicable on this interval.\n\n- Now, consider the interval $[\\frac{1}{2},1].$ In this interval, $f(x)={2}^{-x}$ and so $f(x)$ is continuous on $[\\frac{1}{2},1].$  Therefore, the extreme value theorem is applicable on this interval.\n\n- Finally, the interval $[-1,0)$, is not closed, so the extreme value theorem is not applicable in this case.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is $[\\frac{1}{2},1]$ only."] [:db/cas 17592186341991 :question/worked-solution "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nSo, let's check whether the function is continuous on each of the intervals.\n\n- First, consider $[0,2].$ In this case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-1)=-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(x)$ has an essential discontinuity at $x=1.$ Therefore, $f(x)$ is not continuous on the interval $[0,2],$ and the extreme value theorem is not applicable on this interval.\n\n- Now, consider the interval $[-1,0].$ In this interval, $f(x)=x-1$ and so $f(x)$ is continuous on $[-1,0].$  Therefore, the extreme value theorem is applicable on this interval.\n\n- Finally, in the interval $[-1,2]$, we also have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-1)=-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nHence, $f(x)$ is not continuous on $[-1,2]$ and therefore  the extreme value theorem is not applicable on this interval.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is $[-1,0]$ only." "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nSo, let's check whether the function is continuous on each of the intervals.\n\n- First, consider $[0,2].$ In this case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{+}}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-1)=-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(x)$ has an essential discontinuity at $x=1.$ Therefore, $f(x)$ is not continuous on the interval $[0,2],$ and the extreme value theorem is not applicable on this interval.\n\n- Now, consider the interval $[-1,0].$ In this interval, $f(x)=x-1$ and so $f(x)$ is continuous on $[-1,0].$  Therefore, the extreme value theorem is applicable on this interval.\n\n- Finally, in the interval $[-1,2]$, we also have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{+}}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-1)=-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nHence, $f(x)$ is not continuous on $[-1,2]$ and therefore  the extreme value theorem is not applicable on this interval.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is $[-1,0]$ only."] [:db/cas 17592186341993 :question/worked-solution "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nSo, let's check whether the function is continuous on each of the intervals. \n\n- First, let's consider $[-1,1].$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2-x}{{2}^{x}+1}=\\frac{2-0}{{2}^{0}+1}=1\\ne f(0).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=0.$ Therefore, $f(x)$ is not continuous on the interval $[-1,1],$ and the extreme value theorem is not applicable on this interval.\n\n- The interval $(0,3]$ is not closed, so the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\n- Finally, consider $[-2,-1].$ The only discontinuity of the function $f(x)$ is at $x=0,$ which is not in the interval  $[-2,-1].$  Therefore, $f(x)$ is continuous on the interval  $[-2,-1],$  and the extreme value theorem is also applicable on this interval.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is $[-2,-1]$ only." "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nSo, let's check whether the function is continuous on each of the intervals. \n\n- First, let's consider $[-1,1].$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 0}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2-x}{{2}^{x}+1}=\\frac{2-0}{{2}^{0}+1}=1\\ne f(0).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=0.$ Therefore, $f(x)$ is not continuous on the interval $[-1,1],$ and the extreme value theorem is not applicable on this interval.\n\n- The interval $(0,3]$ is not closed, so the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\n- Finally, consider $[-2,-1].$ The only discontinuity of the function $f(x)$ is at $x=0,$ which is not in the interval  $[-2,-1].$  Therefore, $f(x)$ is continuous on the interval  $[-2,-1],$  and the extreme value theorem is also applicable on this interval.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is $[-2,-1]$ only."] [:db/cas 17592186341994 :question/worked-solution "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nSo, let's check whether the function is continuous on each of the intervals.\n\n- Consider $[-1,2].$  We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}(2{x}^{3}-1)=2(1)^{3}-1=1, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}{sin}^{2}⁡(x-1)={sin}^{2}⁡(1-1)=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the limits from the left and from the right of $x=1$ are not the same. Hence, $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=1.$ Therefore, $f(x)$ is not continuous on the interval $[-1,2],$ and the extreme value theorem is not applicable on this interval.\n\n- The interval $(1,3]$ is not closed, so the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\n- Finally, consider $[2,\\pi ].$ In this interval, $f(x)={sin}^{2}⁡(x-1)$ and so $f(x)$ is continuous on $[2,\\pi ].$  Therefore, the extreme value theorem is applicable on this interval.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is $[2,\\pi ]$ only." "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nSo, let's check whether the function is continuous on each of the intervals.\n\n- Consider $[-1,2].$  We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to 1}(2{x}^{3}-1)=2(1)^{3}-1=1, \\\\ \\lim_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to 1}{sin}^{2}⁡(x-1)={sin}^{2}⁡(1-1)=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the limits from the left and from the right of $x=1$ are not the same. Hence, $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=1.$ Therefore, $f(x)$ is not continuous on the interval $[-1,2],$ and the extreme value theorem is not applicable on this interval.\n\n- The interval $(1,3]$ is not closed, so the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\n- Finally, consider $[2,\\pi ].$ In this interval, $f(x)={sin}^{2}⁡(x-1)$ and so $f(x)$ is continuous on $[2,\\pi ].$  Therefore, the extreme value theorem is applicable on this interval.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is $[2,\\pi ]$ only."] [:db/cas 17592186342003 :question/worked-solution "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nSo, let's check whether the function is continuous on each of the intervals. \n\n- Let first consider the interval  $[-2,2].$ This is a  closed interval, but we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}\\frac{1}{{2}^{x-1}-1}=\\infty \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand so $f(x)$ has an essential discontinuity at $x=1.$ Therefore, $f(x)$ is not continuous on the interval $[-1,1]$, and the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\n- The interval $[-3,1)$ is not closed, so the extreme value theorem is not applicable in this case.\n\n- Finally consider  $[1.5,2].$ The only discontinuity of the function $f(x)$ is at $x=1,$ which is not in the interval  $[1.5,2].$ Therefore, $f(x)$ is continuous on the interval  $[1.5,2]$, and the extreme value theorem is applicable on this interval.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is $[1.5,2]$ only." "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nSo, let's check whether the function is continuous on each of the intervals. \n\n- Let first consider the interval  $[-2,2].$ This is a  closed interval, but we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{+}}\\frac{1}{{2}^{x-1}-1}=\\infty \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand so $f(x)$ has an essential discontinuity at $x=1.$ Therefore, $f(x)$ is not continuous on the interval $[-1,1]$, and the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\n- The interval $[-3,1)$ is not closed, so the extreme value theorem is not applicable in this case.\n\n- Finally consider  $[1.5,2].$ The only discontinuity of the function $f(x)$ is at $x=1,$ which is not in the interval  $[1.5,2].$ Therefore, $f(x)$ is continuous on the interval  $[1.5,2]$, and the extreme value theorem is applicable on this interval.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is $[1.5,2]$ only."] [:db/cas 17592186342007 :question/worked-solution "Notice that as $x\\to 0,$ both numerator and denominator approach $0.$\n\nSo, if we attempt to evaluate the limit directly, we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\left(-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{4x}\\right)=\\frac{0}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is an indeterminate form.\n\nInstead, let's recall the following special limit:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x}=1\n$$\n\nRewriting the given limit using the algebra of limits and applying our special limit, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\left(-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{4x}\\right) & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}(-\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Notice that as $x\\to 0,$ both numerator and denominator approach $0.$\n\nSo, if we attempt to evaluate the limit directly, we get\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\left(-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{4x}\\right)=\\frac{0}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is an indeterminate form.\n\nInstead, let's recall the following special limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x}=1\n$$\n\nRewriting the given limit using the algebra of limits and applying our special limit, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\left(-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{4x}\\right) & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}(-\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186342010 :question/worked-solution "First, we can simplify the limit using trigonometric identities, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta -1}{{\\theta }^{2}} & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta }{{\\theta }^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\left(\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\right)}{{\\theta }^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}(\\frac{1}{{\\theta }^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{{\\theta }^{2}}\\cdot {lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now evaluate the limits separately:\n\n- Consider the first limit. Notice that as $\\theta \\to 0,$ both numerator and denominator approach $0.$ \n\nSo, if we attempt to evaluate the limit directly, we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{{\\theta }^{2}}=\\frac{0}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is an indeterminate form. \n\nRewriting the given limit using the algebra of limits and applying our special limit, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{{\\theta }^{2}} & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}{\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\theta }\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & ={[{lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\theta }]}^{2} \\\\  & ={1}^{2} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Consider the second limit. Evaluating it directly, we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }=\\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡0}=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{{\\theta }^{2}}\\cdot {lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta } & =1\\cdot 1=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we can simplify the limit using trigonometric identities, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta -1}{{\\theta }^{2}} & =\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta }{{\\theta }^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\left(\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\right)}{{\\theta }^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}(\\frac{1}{{\\theta }^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }) \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{{\\theta }^{2}}\\cdot {lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now evaluate the limits separately:\n\n- Consider the first limit. Notice that as $\\theta \\to 0,$ both numerator and denominator approach $0.$ \n\nSo, if we attempt to evaluate the limit directly, we get\n\n$$\n\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{{\\theta }^{2}}=\\frac{0}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is an indeterminate form. \n\nRewriting the given limit using the algebra of limits and applying our special limit, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{{\\theta }^{2}} & =\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}{\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\theta }\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & ={[\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\theta }]}^{2} \\\\  & ={1}^{2} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Consider the second limit. Evaluating it directly, we get\n\n$$\n\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }=\\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡0}=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{{\\theta }^{2}}\\cdot {lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta } & =1\\cdot 1=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186342011 :question/worked-solution "First, we can simplify the limit using trigonometric identities, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\theta \\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta } & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}(\\theta \\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta ÷\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}(\\frac{\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }÷\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}(\\frac{\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that as $\\theta \\to 0,$ both numerator and denominator approach $0.$\n\nSo, if we attempt to evaluate the limit directly, we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }=\\frac{0}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is an indeterminate form.\n\nInstead, let's recall the following special limit:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\theta }=1\n$$\n\nRewriting the given limit using the algebra of limits and applying our special limit, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}{\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\theta }\\right)}^{-1} \\\\  & ={[{lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\theta }]}^{-1} \\\\  & ={1}^{-1} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we can simplify the limit using trigonometric identities, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\theta \\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta } & =\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}(\\theta \\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta ÷\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}(\\frac{\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }÷\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }) \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}(\\frac{\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }) \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that as $\\theta \\to 0,$ both numerator and denominator approach $0.$\n\nSo, if we attempt to evaluate the limit directly, we get\n\n$$\n\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }=\\frac{0}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is an indeterminate form.\n\nInstead, let's recall the following special limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\theta }=1\n$$\n\nRewriting the given limit using the algebra of limits and applying our special limit, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } & =\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}{\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\theta }\\right)}^{-1} \\\\  & ={[\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\theta }]}^{-1} \\\\  & ={1}^{-1} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186344992 :question/worked-solution "Let the function $f(x,y)$ be defined as\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4-x-y).\n$$\n\nThe function $f(x,y)$ is continuous except when $4-x-y$  is negative or zero. However, at the point $(1,2)$,  the expression  $4-x-y$ is positive, so we can calculate the limit by substituting  $(1,2)$ into the function: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (1,2)}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4-x-y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4-1-2)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let the function $f(x,y)$ be defined as\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4-x-y).\n$$\n\nThe function $f(x,y)$ is continuous except when $4-x-y$  is negative or zero. However, at the point $(1,2)$,  the expression  $4-x-y$ is positive, so we can calculate the limit by substituting  $(1,2)$ into the function: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (1,2)}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4-x-y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4-1-2)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186344993 :question/worked-solution "Let the function $f(x,y)$ be defined as\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\sqrt[3]{xy}.\n$$\n\nThe function $f(x,y)$ is continuous for all values of $x$ and $y.$ So,  we can calculate the limit by substituting  $(-2,4)$ into the function: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-2,4)}\\sqrt[3]{xy}=\\sqrt{(-2)\\cdot 4}=\\sqrt[3]{-8}=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let the function $f(x,y)$ be defined as\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\sqrt[3]{xy}.\n$$\n\nThe function $f(x,y)$ is continuous for all values of $x$ and $y.$ So,  we can calculate the limit by substituting  $(-2,4)$ into the function: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (-2,4)}\\sqrt[3]{xy}=\\sqrt{(-2)\\cdot 4}=\\sqrt[3]{-8}=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186344994 :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (1,1,2)}\\frac{x{z}^{2}-y{z}^{2}}{yz-xz}=\\frac{1\\cdot {2}^{2}-1\\cdot {2}^{2}}{1\\cdot 2-1\\cdot 2}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (1,1,2)}\\frac{x{z}^{2}-y{z}^{2}}{yz-xz} & ={lim}_{x\\to (1,1,2)}\\frac{(x-y){z}^{2}}{(y-x)z} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (1,1,2)}-\\frac{(x-y){z}^{2}}{(x-y)z} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (1,1,2)}(-z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it using direct substitution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (1,1,2)}(-z) & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $-2.$" "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (1,1,2)}\\frac{x{z}^{2}-y{z}^{2}}{yz-xz}=\\frac{1\\cdot {2}^{2}-1\\cdot {2}^{2}}{1\\cdot 2-1\\cdot 2}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (1,1,2)}\\frac{x{z}^{2}-y{z}^{2}}{yz-xz} & =\\lim_{x\\to (1,1,2)}\\frac{(x-y){z}^{2}}{(y-x)z} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (1,1,2)}-\\frac{(x-y){z}^{2}}{(x-y)z} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (1,1,2)}(-z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it using direct substitution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (1,1,2)}(-z) & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $-2.$"] [:db/cas 17592186345000 :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{{x}^{2}+2xy+{y}^{2}}{{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}}=\\frac{(-1)^{2}+2\\cdot (-1)\\cdot 1+{1}^{2}}{{1}^{2}-(-1)^{2}}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{{x}^{2}+2xy+{y}^{2}}{{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}} & ={lim}_{x\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{(x+y)^{2}}{(y-x)(y+x)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{(x+y)(x+y)}{(y-x)(x+y)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{x+y}{y-x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it using direct substitution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{x+y}{y-x} & =\\frac{(-1)+1}{1-(-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{2} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $0.$" "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{{x}^{2}+2xy+{y}^{2}}{{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}}=\\frac{(-1)^{2}+2\\cdot (-1)\\cdot 1+{1}^{2}}{{1}^{2}-(-1)^{2}}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{{x}^{2}+2xy+{y}^{2}}{{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}} & =\\lim_{x\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{(x+y)^{2}}{(y-x)(y+x)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{(x+y)(x+y)}{(y-x)(x+y)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{x+y}{y-x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it using direct substitution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{x+y}{y-x} & =\\frac{(-1)+1}{1-(-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{2} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $0.$"] [:db/cas 17592186345001 :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (3,1)}\\frac{\\sqrt{x}-\\sqrt{y+2}}{x-y-2}=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}-\\sqrt{1+2}}{3-1-2}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by first multiplying the numerator and denominator by the conjugate of the numerator: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (3,1)}\\frac{\\sqrt{x}-\\sqrt{y+2}}{x-y-2} & ={lim}_{x\\to (3,1)}\\frac{(\\sqrt{x}-\\sqrt{y+2})(\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{y+2})}{(x-y-2)(\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{y+2})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (3,1)}\\frac{(\\sqrt{x})^{2}-(\\sqrt{y+2})^{2}}{(x-y-2)(\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{y+2})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (3,1)}\\frac{x-y-2}{(x-y-2)(\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{y+2})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (3,1)}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{y+2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (3,1)}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{y+2}} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}+\\sqrt{1+2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}+\\sqrt{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{3}}.$" "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (3,1)}\\frac{\\sqrt{x}-\\sqrt{y+2}}{x-y-2}=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}-\\sqrt{1+2}}{3-1-2}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by first multiplying the numerator and denominator by the conjugate of the numerator: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (3,1)}\\frac{\\sqrt{x}-\\sqrt{y+2}}{x-y-2} & =\\lim_{x\\to (3,1)}\\frac{(\\sqrt{x}-\\sqrt{y+2})(\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{y+2})}{(x-y-2)(\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{y+2})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (3,1)}\\frac{(\\sqrt{x})^{2}-(\\sqrt{y+2})^{2}}{(x-y-2)(\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{y+2})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (3,1)}\\frac{x-y-2}{(x-y-2)(\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{y+2})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (3,1)}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{y+2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (3,1)}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{y+2}} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}+\\sqrt{1+2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}+\\sqrt{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{3}}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186345002 :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (1,1)}\\frac{\\sqrt{x}-y}{x-{y}^{2}}=\\frac{\\sqrt{1}-1}{1-{1}^{2}}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by first multiplying the numerator and denominator by the conjugate of the numerator: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (1,1)}\\frac{\\sqrt{x}-y}{x-{y}^{2}} & ={lim}_{x\\to (1,1)}\\frac{(\\sqrt{x}-y)(\\sqrt{x}+y)}{(x-{y}^{2})(\\sqrt{x}+y)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (1,1)}\\frac{(\\sqrt{x})^{2}-{y}^{2}}{(x-{y}^{2})(\\sqrt{x}+y)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (1,1)}\\frac{x-{y}^{2}}{(x-{y}^{2})(\\sqrt{x}+y)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (1,1)}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}+y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (1,1)}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}+y} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $\\frac{1}{2}.$" "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (1,1)}\\frac{\\sqrt{x}-y}{x-{y}^{2}}=\\frac{\\sqrt{1}-1}{1-{1}^{2}}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by first multiplying the numerator and denominator by the conjugate of the numerator: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (1,1)}\\frac{\\sqrt{x}-y}{x-{y}^{2}} & =\\lim_{x\\to (1,1)}\\frac{(\\sqrt{x}-y)(\\sqrt{x}+y)}{(x-{y}^{2})(\\sqrt{x}+y)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (1,1)}\\frac{(\\sqrt{x})^{2}-{y}^{2}}{(x-{y}^{2})(\\sqrt{x}+y)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (1,1)}\\frac{x-{y}^{2}}{(x-{y}^{2})(\\sqrt{x}+y)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (1,1)}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}+y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (1,1)}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}+y} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $\\frac{1}{2}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186345003 :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (1,1)}\\frac{{y}^{2}-x}{\\sqrt{x}-y}=\\frac{{1}^{2}-1}{\\sqrt{1}-1}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by first multiplying the numerator and denominator by the conjugate of the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (1,1)}\\frac{{y}^{2}-x}{\\sqrt{x}-y} & ={lim}_{x\\to (1,1)}\\frac{({y}^{2}-x)(\\sqrt{x}+y)}{(\\sqrt{x}-y)(\\sqrt{x}+y)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (1,1)}\\frac{({y}^{2}-x)(\\sqrt{x}+y)}{{(\\sqrt{x})}^{2}-{y}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (1,1)}\\frac{-(x-{y}^{2})(\\sqrt{x}+y)}{x-{y}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (1,1)}-(\\sqrt{x}+y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (1,1)}-(\\sqrt{x}+y) & =-(\\sqrt{1}+1) \\\\  & =-(1+1) \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $-2.$" "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (1,1)}\\frac{{y}^{2}-x}{\\sqrt{x}-y}=\\frac{{1}^{2}-1}{\\sqrt{1}-1}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by first multiplying the numerator and denominator by the conjugate of the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (1,1)}\\frac{{y}^{2}-x}{\\sqrt{x}-y} & =\\lim_{x\\to (1,1)}\\frac{({y}^{2}-x)(\\sqrt{x}+y)}{(\\sqrt{x}-y)(\\sqrt{x}+y)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (1,1)}\\frac{({y}^{2}-x)(\\sqrt{x}+y)}{{(\\sqrt{x})}^{2}-{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (1,1)}\\frac{-(x-{y}^{2})(\\sqrt{x}+y)}{x-{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (1,1)}-(\\sqrt{x}+y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (1,1)}-(\\sqrt{x}+y) & =-(\\sqrt{1}+1) \\\\  & =-(1+1) \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $-2.$"] [:db/cas 17592186345004 :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}:$\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}-y}=\\frac{2\\cdot {0}^{2}}{2\\cdot {0}^{2}-0}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, the function cannot be simplified by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\nWe attempt to evaluate the limit by approaching $(0,0)$ from different paths.\n\n- Statement “The limit evaluates to $0$ along the path $y=x$” is true. Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=x}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}-y} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}-x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{x(2x-1)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2x}{2x-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cdot 0}{2\\cdot 0-1} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “The limit evaluates to $2$ along the path $y={x}^{2}$” is also true. Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y={x}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y={x}^{2}}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}-y} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}-{x}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,2 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “The limit exists and is equal to $2$” is false. Because the limit evaluates to different results on two different paths, we conclude that the limit does not exist:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}-y}=\\text{DNE}\n$$" "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}-y}=\\frac{2\\cdot {0}^{2}}{2\\cdot {0}^{2}-0}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, the function cannot be simplified by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\nWe attempt to evaluate the limit by approaching $(0,0)$ from different paths.\n\n- Statement “The limit evaluates to $0$ along the path $y=x$” is true. Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=x}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}-y} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}-x} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{x(2x-1)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2x}{2x-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cdot 0}{2\\cdot 0-1} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “The limit evaluates to $2$ along the path $y={x}^{2}$” is also true. Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y={x}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y={x}^{2}}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}-y} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}-{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,2 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “The limit exists and is equal to $2$” is false. Because the limit evaluates to different results on two different paths, we conclude that the limit does not exist:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}-y}=\\text{DNE}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186345005 :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}:$\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{2y{x}^{3}}{{x}^{6}+8{y}^{2}}=\\frac{2\\cdot 0\\cdot {0}^{3}}{{0}^{6}+8\\cdot {0}^{2}}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, the function cannot be simplified by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\nLet's attempt to evaluate the limit approaching $(0,0)$ from different paths.\n\n- Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=x}\\,\\frac{2y{x}^{3}}{{x}^{6}+8{y}^{2}} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{4}}{{x}^{6}+8{x}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{4}}{{x}^{2}({x}^{4}+8)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{{x}^{4}+8} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y={x}^{3},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y={x}^{3}}\\,\\frac{2y{x}^{3}}{{x}^{6}+8{y}^{2}} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{6}}{{x}^{6}+8{x}^{6}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{6}}{9{x}^{6}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{2}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause the limit evaluates to different results on two different paths, we conclude that the limit does not exist:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{2y{x}^{3}}{{x}^{6}+8{y}^{2}}=\\text{DNE}\n$$" "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{2y{x}^{3}}{{x}^{6}+8{y}^{2}}=\\frac{2\\cdot 0\\cdot {0}^{3}}{{0}^{6}+8\\cdot {0}^{2}}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, the function cannot be simplified by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\nLet's attempt to evaluate the limit approaching $(0,0)$ from different paths.\n\n- Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=x}\\,\\frac{2y{x}^{3}}{{x}^{6}+8{y}^{2}} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{4}}{{x}^{6}+8{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{4}}{{x}^{2}({x}^{4}+8)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{{x}^{4}+8} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y={x}^{3},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y={x}^{3}}\\,\\frac{2y{x}^{3}}{{x}^{6}+8{y}^{2}} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{6}}{{x}^{6}+8{x}^{6}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{6}}{9{x}^{6}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{2}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause the limit evaluates to different results on two different paths, we conclude that the limit does not exist:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{2y{x}^{3}}{{x}^{6}+8{y}^{2}}=\\text{DNE}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186348055 :question/worked-solution "First, we divide both the numerator and the denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{2}.$ Then, by noting that the reciprocals shrink to $0$ in the limit, we can then evaluate the resulting expression.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{{x}^{3}+1}{2{x}^{2}+x} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})}{(\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{(x+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})}{(2+\\frac{1}{x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{(x+0)}{(2+0)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{x}{2} \\\\  & =-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we divide both the numerator and the denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{2}.$ Then, by noting that the reciprocals shrink to $0$ in the limit, we can then evaluate the resulting expression.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{{x}^{3}+1}{2{x}^{2}+x} & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})}{(\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{(x+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})}{(2+\\frac{1}{x})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{(x+0)}{(2+0)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{x}{2} \\\\  & =-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186348056 :question/worked-solution "The horizontal asymptote is $y={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x).$ To find the horizontal asymptote, we apply the usual procedure of dividing the numerator and denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is $x.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{4{x}^{2}+5}{2x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{4{x}^{2}}{x}+\\frac{5}{x})}{\\left(\\frac{2x}{x}\\right)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(4x+\\frac{5}{x})}{2} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(4x+0)}{2} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }2x \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe limit does not come out to a finite number. Therefore, there is no horizontal asymptote." "The horizontal asymptote is $y=\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }f(x).$ To find the horizontal asymptote, we apply the usual procedure of dividing the numerator and denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is $x.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{4{x}^{2}+5}{2x} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{4{x}^{2}}{x}+\\frac{5}{x})}{\\left(\\frac{2x}{x}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(4x+\\frac{5}{x})}{2} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(4x+0)}{2} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }2x \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe limit does not come out to a finite number. Therefore, there is no horizontal asymptote."] [:db/cas 17592186348057 :question/worked-solution "The horizontal asymptote is $y={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x).$ To find the horizontal asymptote, we apply the usual procedure of dividing the numerator and denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{3}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{x}^{5}+1}{4{x}^{3}-x+1} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{{x}^{5}}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}})}{(\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{x}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{({x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}})}{(4-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{({x}^{2}+0)}{(4-0+0)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{x}^{2} \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe limit does not come out to a finite number. Therefore, there is no horizontal asymptote." "The horizontal asymptote is $y=\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }f(x).$ To find the horizontal asymptote, we apply the usual procedure of dividing the numerator and denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{3}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{x}^{5}+1}{4{x}^{3}-x+1} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{{x}^{5}}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}})}{(\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{x}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{({x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}})}{(4-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{({x}^{2}+0)}{(4-0+0)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }{x}^{2} \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe limit does not come out to a finite number. Therefore, there is no horizontal asymptote."] [:db/cas 17592186348061 :question/worked-solution "Notice that we can rewrite the first term as\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x){e}^{-2x}=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)}{{e}^{2x}}.\n$$\n\nBoth the numerator and denominator approach $\\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty .$ However, since ${e}^{2x}≫\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)$ for large values of $x,$ the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator. Consequently, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)}{{e}^{2x}}=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x){e}^{-2x}) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)}{{e}^{2x}}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Notice that we can rewrite the first term as\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x){e}^{-2x}=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)}{{e}^{2x}}.\n$$\n\nBoth the numerator and denominator approach $\\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty .$ However, since ${e}^{2x}≫\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)$ for large values of $x,$ the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator. Consequently, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)}{{e}^{2x}}=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x){e}^{-2x}) & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)}{{e}^{2x}}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186348062 :question/worked-solution "First, let's calculate ${x}^{2}\\cdot p(x):$\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}\\cdot p(x)={x}^{2}\\cdot {x}^{2}={x}^{4}.\n$$\n\nWhen we divide the two functions ${x}^{2}\\cdot p(x)$ and $q(x),$ we get a constant, and the limit of a constant is just the constant itself:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{x}^{2}\\cdot p(x)}{q(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{x}^{4}}{(-7{x}^{4})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }-\\frac{1}{7} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, let's calculate ${x}^{2}\\cdot p(x):$\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}\\cdot p(x)={x}^{2}\\cdot {x}^{2}={x}^{4}.\n$$\n\nWhen we divide the two functions ${x}^{2}\\cdot p(x)$ and $q(x),$ we get a constant, and the limit of a constant is just the constant itself:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{x}^{2}\\cdot p(x)}{q(x)} & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{x}^{4}}{(-7{x}^{4})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }-\\frac{1}{7} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186348367 :question/worked-solution "We proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\sqrt{1-z}} & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\sqrt{1-z}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[-2\\sqrt{1-z}]}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =-2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\sqrt{1-0}-\\sqrt{1-a}) \\\\  & =-2+2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }\\sqrt{1-a} \\\\  & =-2+2\\cdot \\infty  \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the integral is divergent." "We proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\sqrt{1-z}} & =\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\sqrt{1-z}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{[-2\\sqrt{1-z}]}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =-2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\sqrt{1-0}-\\sqrt{1-a}) \\\\  & =-2+2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }\\sqrt{1-a} \\\\  & =-2+2\\cdot \\infty  \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the integral is divergent."] [:db/cas 17592186351298 :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an improper integral in which both the left and right endpoints are unbounded:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nIf the integrand $f(x)$ is continuous on $(-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ then we can break the integral up into two improper integrals, each with a single unbounded endpoint:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nThen, we can compute each of the two integrals separately and combine their results. \n\n**Watch out!** If either of the two integrals on the right-hand side is divergent, then the integral on the left-hand side is also divergent.\n\nFirst, we calculate the antiderivative using a substitution.  Let $u={x}^{2}$,  then $\\text{d}u=2x\\,\\text{d}x.$ Hence, the antiderivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 2x{e}^{-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {e}^{-u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-{e}^{-u}+C \\\\  & =-{e}^{-{x}^{2}}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's evaluate each integral in turn.\n\n- Statement “$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ is convergent” is true. Computing the required integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }2x{e}^{-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}2x{e}^{-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }({-{e}^{-{x}^{2}}|}_{0}^{a}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(-{e}^{-{a}^{2}}+{e}^{0}) \\\\  & =0+1 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the integral converges.\n\n- Statement “$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ is divergent” is false. Computing the required integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}2x{e}^{-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}2x{e}^{-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }({-{e}^{-{x}^{2}}|}_{a}^{0}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(-{e}^{0}+{e}^{-{a}^{2}}) \\\\  & =-1+0 \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the integral converges.\n\n- Statement “$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ is divergent” is false. The integral in statement “$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ is divergent” can be expressed as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1-1=0.\n$$\n\nSo the integral converges.\n\nIn conclusion, only statement “$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ is convergent” is true." "Suppose we have an improper integral in which both the left and right endpoints are unbounded:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nIf the integrand $f(x)$ is continuous on $(-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ then we can break the integral up into two improper integrals, each with a single unbounded endpoint:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nThen, we can compute each of the two integrals separately and combine their results. \n\n**Watch out!** If either of the two integrals on the right-hand side is divergent, then the integral on the left-hand side is also divergent.\n\nFirst, we calculate the antiderivative using a substitution.  Let $u={x}^{2}$,  then $\\text{d}u=2x\\,\\text{d}x.$ Hence, the antiderivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 2x{e}^{-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {e}^{-u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-{e}^{-u}+C \\\\  & =-{e}^{-{x}^{2}}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's evaluate each integral in turn.\n\n- Statement “$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ is convergent” is true. Computing the required integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }2x{e}^{-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}2x{e}^{-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }({-{e}^{-{x}^{2}}|}_{0}^{a}) \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }(-{e}^{-{a}^{2}}+{e}^{0}) \\\\  & =0+1 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the integral converges.\n\n- Statement “$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ is divergent” is false. Computing the required integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}2x{e}^{-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}2x{e}^{-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }({-{e}^{-{x}^{2}}|}_{a}^{0}) \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }(-{e}^{0}+{e}^{-{a}^{2}}) \\\\  & =-1+0 \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the integral converges.\n\n- Statement “$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ is divergent” is false. The integral in statement “$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ is divergent” can be expressed as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1-1=0.\n$$\n\nSo the integral converges.\n\nIn conclusion, only statement “$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ is convergent” is true."] [:db/cas 17592186351451 :question/worked-solution "This is an improper integral of the second kind because the function under the integral is unbounded as we approach the *lower* integration limit from any point that lies in the interval $x\\in (-2,3):$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}+8}\\to \\infty \\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to (-2)^{+}\n$$\n\nWe can attempt to evaluate this integral by integrating $f(x)$ over the interval $x\\in [a,3]$ and taking the limit as $a\\to (-2)^{+}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-2}^{3}\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}+8}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to (-2)^{+}}({\\int }_{a}^{3}\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}+8}\\,\\text{d}x)\\end{aligned}\n$$" "This is an improper integral of the second kind because the function under the integral is unbounded as we approach the *lower* integration limit from any point that lies in the interval $x\\in (-2,3):$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}+8}\\to \\infty \\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to (-2)^{+}\n$$\n\nWe can attempt to evaluate this integral by integrating $f(x)$ over the interval $x\\in [a,3]$ and taking the limit as $a\\to (-2)^{+}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-2}^{3}\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}+8}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\lim_{a\\to (-2)^{+}}({\\int }_{a}^{3}\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}+8}\\,\\text{d}x)\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186351456 :question/worked-solution "First, let's rewrite the integral using a substitution. Let $u=x-2.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe use the table below to change the limits:\n\n| $x$ | $2$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $2$ |\n\nCarrying out the change of variable, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{4}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{x-2} & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{u}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an improper integral of the second kind because the function under the integral is unbounded at the *lower* limit of integration.\n\n$$\nf(u)=\\frac{1}{u}\\to \\infty \\,\\text{ as }\\,u\\to {0}^{+}.\n$$\n\nNonetheless, it is possible to calculate the integral by setting the *lower* bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to {0}^{+}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{u} & ={lim}_{a\\to {0}^{+}}{\\int }_{a}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{u} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to {0}^{+}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u||}_{a}^{2} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to {0}^{+}}[\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2|-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|a|] \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to {0}^{+}}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\frac{2}{a}| \\\\  & =\\infty \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the integral is divergent." "First, let's rewrite the integral using a substitution. Let $u=x-2.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe use the table below to change the limits:\n\n| $x$ | $2$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $2$ |\n\nCarrying out the change of variable, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{4}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{x-2} & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{u}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an improper integral of the second kind because the function under the integral is unbounded at the *lower* limit of integration.\n\n$$\nf(u)=\\frac{1}{u}\\to \\infty \\,\\text{ as }\\,u\\to {0}^{+}.\n$$\n\nNonetheless, it is possible to calculate the integral by setting the *lower* bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to {0}^{+}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{u} & =\\lim_{a\\to {0}^{+}}{\\int }_{a}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{u} \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to {0}^{+}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u||}_{a}^{2} \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to {0}^{+}}[\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2|-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|a|] \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to {0}^{+}}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\frac{2}{a}| \\\\  & =\\infty \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the integral is divergent."] [:db/cas 17592186351457 :question/worked-solution "To apply the ratio test, we define ${a}_{n}=(-1)^{n+1}{4}^{n}{x}^{n+3}.$ Then, we calculate the ratio of two successive terms and take the limit as $n\\to \\infty :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}{4}^{n+1}{x}^{n+4}}{(-1)^{n}{4}^{n}{x}^{n+3}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|-4x| \\\\  & =4|x|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe series converges if $L<1.$ In this case, the convergence condition is satisfied if \n\n$$\n4|x|<1\\,⇒\\,|x|<\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the radius of convergence is $R=\\frac{1}{4}.$" "To apply the ratio test, we define ${a}_{n}=(-1)^{n+1}{4}^{n}{x}^{n+3}.$ Then, we calculate the ratio of two successive terms and take the limit as $n\\to \\infty :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}{4}^{n+1}{x}^{n+4}}{(-1)^{n}{4}^{n}{x}^{n+3}}| \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }|-4x| \\\\  & =4|x|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe series converges if $L<1.$ In this case, the convergence condition is satisfied if \n\n$$\n4|x|<1\\,⇒\\,|x|<\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the radius of convergence is $R=\\frac{1}{4}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186351458 :question/worked-solution "To apply the ratio test, we define ${a}_{n}=\\frac{(-1)^{n}{x}^{2n}}{{5}^{2(n+1)}}.$ Then, we calculate the ratio of two successive terms and take the limit as $n\\to \\infty :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}{x}^{2(n+1)}}{{5}^{2(n+2)}}\\cdot \\frac{{5}^{2(n+1)}}{(-1)^{n}{x}^{2n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{(-1){x}^{2}}{{5}^{2}}| \\\\  & =\\frac{|{x}^{2}|}{{5}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{5}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe series converges if $L<1.$ In this case, the convergence condition is satisfied if \n\n$$\n{x}^{2}<25\\,⇒\\,|x|<\\sqrt{25}=5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the radius of convergence is $R=5.$" "To apply the ratio test, we define ${a}_{n}=\\frac{(-1)^{n}{x}^{2n}}{{5}^{2(n+1)}}.$ Then, we calculate the ratio of two successive terms and take the limit as $n\\to \\infty :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}{x}^{2(n+1)}}{{5}^{2(n+2)}}\\cdot \\frac{{5}^{2(n+1)}}{(-1)^{n}{x}^{2n}}| \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{(-1){x}^{2}}{{5}^{2}}| \\\\  & =\\frac{|{x}^{2}|}{{5}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{5}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe series converges if $L<1.$ In this case, the convergence condition is satisfied if \n\n$$\n{x}^{2}<25\\,⇒\\,|x|<\\sqrt{25}=5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the radius of convergence is $R=5.$"] [:db/cas 17592186351460 :question/worked-solution "To apply the ratio test, we define ${a}_{n}=\\frac{(-1)^{n}{x}^{2n+1}}{{2}^{n}(2n)!}.$ Then, we calculate the ratio of two successive terms and take the limit as $n\\to \\infty :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}{x}^{2n+3}}{{2}^{n+1}(2n+2)!}\\cdot \\frac{{2}^{n}(2n)!}{(-1)^{n}{x}^{2n+1}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{(-1){x}^{2}}{2(2n+2)(2n+1)}| \\\\  & =|-{x}^{2}|{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{2(2n+2)(2n+1)} \\\\  & =|{x}^{2}|\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe series converges if $L<1.$ In this case, the convergence condition is satisfied for all $-\\infty <x<\\infty .$ Therefore, we conclude that the radius of convergence is $R=\\infty .$" "To apply the ratio test, we define ${a}_{n}=\\frac{(-1)^{n}{x}^{2n+1}}{{2}^{n}(2n)!}.$ Then, we calculate the ratio of two successive terms and take the limit as $n\\to \\infty :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}{x}^{2n+3}}{{2}^{n+1}(2n+2)!}\\cdot \\frac{{2}^{n}(2n)!}{(-1)^{n}{x}^{2n+1}}| \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{(-1){x}^{2}}{2(2n+2)(2n+1)}| \\\\  & =|-{x}^{2}|\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{2(2n+2)(2n+1)} \\\\  & =|{x}^{2}|\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe series converges if $L<1.$ In this case, the convergence condition is satisfied for all $-\\infty <x<\\infty .$ Therefore, we conclude that the radius of convergence is $R=\\infty .$"] [:db/cas 17592186357405 :question/problem "Find $L{f(t)}$ for the piecewise function defined by \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t)={\\begin{aligned}0,\\, & t<1 \\\\ -7,\\, & 1\\le t\\le 3 \\\\ 0,\\, & t>3.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Find $L{f(t)}$ for the piecewise function defined by \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t)=\\begin{cases}0,\\hspace{1em} & t<1 \\\\ -7,\\hspace{1em} & 1\\le t\\le 3 \\\\ 0,\\hspace{1em} & t>3.\\end{cases}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186357405 :question/worked-solution "We apply the definition of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =F(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the piecewise function \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t)={\\begin{aligned}0\\, & \\text{for}\\,\\,t<1 \\\\ -7\\, & \\text{for}\\,\\,1\\le t\\le 3 \\\\ 0\\, & \\text{for}\\,\\,t>3\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\text{d}t={\\int }_{1}^{3}(-7){e}^{-st}\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe calculate this definite integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}(-7){e}^{-st}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\frac{7}{s}{e}^{-st}|}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{s}({e}^{-3s}-{e}^{-s})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Laplace transform of $f(t)$ is\n\n$$\nL{f(t)}=\\frac{7}{s}({e}^{-3s}-{e}^{-s}).\n$$" "We apply the definition of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =F(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the piecewise function \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t)=\\begin{cases}0\\hspace{1em} & \\text{for}\\hspace{0.167em}\\hspace{0.167em}t<1 \\\\ -7\\hspace{1em} & \\text{for}\\hspace{0.167em}\\hspace{0.167em}1\\le t\\le 3 \\\\ 0\\hspace{1em} & \\text{for}\\hspace{0.167em}\\hspace{0.167em}t>3\\end{cases}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\text{d}t={\\int }_{1}^{3}(-7){e}^{-st}\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe calculate this definite integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}(-7){e}^{-st}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\frac{7}{s}{e}^{-st}|}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{s}({e}^{-3s}-{e}^{-s})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Laplace transform of $f(t)$ is\n\n$$\nL{f(t)}=\\frac{7}{s}({e}^{-3s}-{e}^{-s}).\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186362334 :question/worked-solution "To apply the ratio test, we define ${a}_{n}={x}^{n}.$ Then, we calculate the ratio of two successive terms and take the limit as $n\\to \\infty :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{x}^{n+1}}{{x}^{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|x| \\\\  & =|x|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe series converges if $L<1.$ In this case, the convergence condition is satisfied if $|x|<1.$ Therefore, we conclude that the radius of convergence is $R=1.$" "To apply the ratio test, we define ${a}_{n}={x}^{n}.$ Then, we calculate the ratio of two successive terms and take the limit as $n\\to \\infty :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{x}^{n+1}}{{x}^{n}}| \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }|x| \\\\  & =|x|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe series converges if $L<1.$ In this case, the convergence condition is satisfied if $|x|<1.$ Therefore, we conclude that the radius of convergence is $R=1.$"] [:db/cas 17592186362345 :question/worked-solution "To apply the ratio test, we define ${a}_{n}=\\frac{(-1)^{n}{x}^{2n+1}}{(2n+1)!}.$ Then, we calculate the ratio of two successive terms and take the limit as $n\\to \\infty :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}{x}^{2n+3}}{(2n+3)!}\\cdot \\frac{(2n+1)!}{(-1)^{n}{x}^{2n+1}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{(-1){x}^{2}}{(2n+3)(2n+2)}| \\\\  & =|-{x}^{2}|{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{(2n+3)(2n+2)} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe series converges if $L<1.$ In this case, the convergence condition is satisfied for all $-\\infty <x<\\infty .$ Therefore, we conclude that the radius of convergence is $R=\\infty ,$ and the interval of convergence is $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$" "To apply the ratio test, we define ${a}_{n}=\\frac{(-1)^{n}{x}^{2n+1}}{(2n+1)!}.$ Then, we calculate the ratio of two successive terms and take the limit as $n\\to \\infty :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}{x}^{2n+3}}{(2n+3)!}\\cdot \\frac{(2n+1)!}{(-1)^{n}{x}^{2n+1}}| \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{(-1){x}^{2}}{(2n+3)(2n+2)}| \\\\  & =|-{x}^{2}|\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{(2n+3)(2n+2)} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe series converges if $L<1.$ In this case, the convergence condition is satisfied for all $-\\infty <x<\\infty .$ Therefore, we conclude that the radius of convergence is $R=\\infty ,$ and the interval of convergence is $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$"] [:db/cas 17592186362355 :question/worked-solution "The graph $y={x}^{12}$ looks similar to an upward parabola, so\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{x}^{12}=\\infty .\n$$\n\nMultiplication by the positive constant $8$ does not affect the limit at infinity. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(8{x}^{12})={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }({x}^{12})=\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The graph $y={x}^{12}$ looks similar to an upward parabola, so\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }{x}^{12}=\\infty .\n$$\n\nMultiplication by the positive constant $8$ does not affect the limit at infinity. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }(8{x}^{12})=\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }({x}^{12})=\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186362357 :question/worked-solution "We identify the leading term and compute its limit. In this case, we raise the main term of the base polynomial to the given power.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to \\infty }({t}^{2}+1)^{4} & ={lim}_{t\\to \\infty }({t}^{2})^{4} \\\\  & ={lim}_{t\\to \\infty }{t}^{8} \\\\  & =\\infty \\end{aligned}\n$$" "We identify the leading term and compute its limit. In this case, we raise the main term of the base polynomial to the given power.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to \\infty }({t}^{2}+1)^{4} & =\\lim_{t\\to \\infty }({t}^{2})^{4} \\\\  & =\\lim_{t\\to \\infty }{t}^{8} \\\\  & =\\infty \\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186362398 :question/worked-solution "Approaching from the left, we have $x<-1,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=-\\sqrt[3]{x}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to -{1}^{-}}-\\sqrt[3]{x} \\\\  & =-\\sqrt[3]{-1} \\\\  & =-(-1) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApproaching from the right, we have $x>-1,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=x+2.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}(x+2) \\\\  & =-1+2 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the left- and right-sided limits are equal to $1,$ we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -1}f(x)=1.\n$$" "Approaching from the left, we have $x<-1,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=-\\sqrt[3]{x}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to -{1}^{-}}-\\sqrt[3]{x} \\\\  & =-\\sqrt[3]{-1} \\\\  & =-(-1) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApproaching from the right, we have $x>-1,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=x+2.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}(x+2) \\\\  & =-1+2 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the left- and right-sided limits are equal to $1,$ we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -1}f(x)=1.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186362399 :question/problem "For the piecewise function \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}-x\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x\\le -1 \\\\ x+c\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x>-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nit is known that ${lim}_{x\\to -1}f(x)=1.$ What is the value of the constant $c?$" "For the piecewise function \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}-x\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x\\le -1 \\\\ x+c\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x>-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nit is known that $\\lim_{x\\to -1}f(x)=1.$ What is the value of the constant $c?$"] [:db/cas 17592186362399 :question/worked-solution "In order for ${lim}_{x\\to -1}f(x)$ to be equal to $1,$ the left and right-sided limits must both exist and be equal to $1.$\n\n- Approaching from the left, we have $x<-1,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=-x.$ We get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}(-x) \\\\  & =-(-1) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Approaching from the right, we have $x>-1,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=x+c.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}(x+c) \\\\  & =-1+c.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the left and right-sided limits must be equal, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}f(x) \\\\ 1 & =-1+c \\\\ c & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "In order for $\\lim_{x\\to -1}f(x)$ to be equal to $1,$ the left and right-sided limits must both exist and be equal to $1.$\n\n- Approaching from the left, we have $x<-1,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=-x.$ We get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}(-x) \\\\  & =-(-1) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Approaching from the right, we have $x>-1,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=x+c.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}(x+c) \\\\  & =-1+c.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the left and right-sided limits must be equal, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}f(x) \\\\ 1 & =-1+c \\\\ c & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186362400 :question/problem "Find ${lim}_{x\\to (-2)^{-}}f(x)$ and ${lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}f(x)$ for the function given by\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}-x & \\text{ for }\\,x<-2 \\\\ x & \\text{ for }\\,x>3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Find $\\lim_{x\\to (-2)^{-}}f(x)$ and $\\lim_{x\\to {3}^{-}}f(x)$ for the function given by\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}-x & \\text{ for }\\,x<-2 \\\\ x & \\text{ for }\\,x>3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186362400 :question/worked-solution "Approaching $x=-2$ from the left, we have $x<-2,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=-x.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-2)^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to (-2)^{-}}(-x) \\\\  & =-(-2) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the left-sided limit ${lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}f(x)$ does not exist since $f(x)$ is not defined on the interval $x\\in [-2,3].$ \n\nConsequently, \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}f(x)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$" "Approaching $x=-2$ from the left, we have $x<-2,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=-x.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (-2)^{-}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to (-2)^{-}}(-x) \\\\  & =-(-2) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the left-sided limit $\\lim_{x\\to {3}^{-}}f(x)$ does not exist since $f(x)$ is not defined on the interval $x\\in [-2,3].$ \n\nConsequently, \n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {3}^{-}}f(x)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186362401 :question/problem "Find ${lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}f(x)$ and ${lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x)$ for the function given by\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} & \\text{ for }\\,x<-1 \\\\ 3 & \\text{ for }\\,x>1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Find $\\lim_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}f(x)$ and $\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x)$ for the function given by\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} & \\text{ for }\\,x<-1 \\\\ 3 & \\text{ for }\\,x>1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186362401 :question/worked-solution "Approaching $x=-1$ from the left, we have $x<-1,$ so we use the expression $f(x)={x}^{2}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}{x}^{2} \\\\  & =(-1)^{2} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, approaching $x=1$ from the right, we have $x>1,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=3.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}3=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Approaching $x=-1$ from the left, we have $x<-1,$ so we use the expression $f(x)={x}^{2}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}{x}^{2} \\\\  & =(-1)^{2} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, approaching $x=1$ from the right, we have $x>1,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=3.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{+}}3=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186362630 :question/worked-solution "If we attempt to evaluate the limit by simply evaluating the function at $x=3$, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{{x}^{2}-3x}{\\sqrt{x+4}-\\sqrt{7}} & =\\frac{{3}^{2}-3(3)}{\\sqrt{3+4}-\\sqrt{7}}=\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to first simplify the limit. Since the radical is in the denominator, we can multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the denominator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{{x}^{2}-3x}{\\sqrt{x+4}-\\sqrt{7}} & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{x(x-3)}{\\sqrt{x+4}-\\sqrt{7}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{x(x-3)(\\sqrt{x+4}+\\sqrt{7})}{(\\sqrt{x+4}-\\sqrt{7})(\\sqrt{x+4}+\\sqrt{7})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{x(x-3)(\\sqrt{x+4}+\\sqrt{7})}{(\\sqrt{x+4})^{2}-(\\sqrt{7})^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{x(x-3)(\\sqrt{x+4}+\\sqrt{7})}{x+4-7} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{x(x-3)(\\sqrt{x+4}+\\sqrt{7})}{x-3} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}x(\\sqrt{x+4}+\\sqrt{7}) \\\\  & =3(\\sqrt{3+4}+\\sqrt{7}) \\\\  & =3(2\\sqrt{7}) \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "If we attempt to evaluate the limit by simply evaluating the function at $x=3$, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 3}\\frac{{x}^{2}-3x}{\\sqrt{x+4}-\\sqrt{7}} & =\\frac{{3}^{2}-3(3)}{\\sqrt{3+4}-\\sqrt{7}}=\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to first simplify the limit. Since the radical is in the denominator, we can multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the denominator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 3}\\frac{{x}^{2}-3x}{\\sqrt{x+4}-\\sqrt{7}} & =\\lim_{x\\to 3}\\frac{x(x-3)}{\\sqrt{x+4}-\\sqrt{7}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 3}\\frac{x(x-3)(\\sqrt{x+4}+\\sqrt{7})}{(\\sqrt{x+4}-\\sqrt{7})(\\sqrt{x+4}+\\sqrt{7})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 3}\\frac{x(x-3)(\\sqrt{x+4}+\\sqrt{7})}{(\\sqrt{x+4})^{2}-(\\sqrt{7})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 3}\\frac{x(x-3)(\\sqrt{x+4}+\\sqrt{7})}{x+4-7} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 3}\\frac{x(x-3)(\\sqrt{x+4}+\\sqrt{7})}{x-3} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 3}x(\\sqrt{x+4}+\\sqrt{7}) \\\\  & =3(\\sqrt{3+4}+\\sqrt{7}) \\\\  & =3(2\\sqrt{7}) \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186362928 :question/worked-solution "Recall the following special limit: \n\n$$\ne={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}\n$$\n\nNow, we rewrite the given limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{2n} & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{[{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}]}^{2} \\\\  & ={[{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}]}^{2} \\\\  & ={e}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Recall the following special limit: \n\n$$\ne=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}\n$$\n\nNow, we rewrite the given limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{2n} & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{[{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}]}^{2} \\\\  & ={[\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}]}^{2} \\\\  & ={e}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186362935 :question/worked-solution "Recall the following special limit: \n\n$$\ne={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}\n$$\n\nNow, we rewrite the given limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{-20n} & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{[{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}]}^{-20} \\\\  & ={[{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}]}^{-20} \\\\  & ={e}^{-20} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{e}^{20}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Recall the following special limit: \n\n$$\ne=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}\n$$\n\nNow, we rewrite the given limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{-20n} & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{[{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}]}^{-20} \\\\  & ={[\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}]}^{-20} \\\\  & ={e}^{-20} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{e}^{20}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186362948 :question/worked-solution "Recall the following special limit: \n\n$$\ne={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}\n$$\n\nNow, we rewrite the given limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{-n\\theta } & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{[{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}]}^{-\\theta } \\\\  & ={[{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}]}^{-\\theta } \\\\  & ={e}^{-\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Recall the following special limit: \n\n$$\ne=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}\n$$\n\nNow, we rewrite the given limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{-n\\theta } & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{[{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}]}^{-\\theta } \\\\  & ={[\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}]}^{-\\theta } \\\\  & ={e}^{-\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186362955 :question/worked-solution "Recall the following special limit: \n\n$$\n{e}^{y}={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{y}{n})}^{n}\n$$\n\nNow, we rewrite the given limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{y}{n})}^{-4n} & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{[{(1+\\frac{y}{n})}^{n}]}^{-4} \\\\  & ={[{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{y}{n})}^{n}]}^{-4} \\\\  & ={({e}^{y})}^{-4} \\\\  & ={e}^{-4y} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{e}^{4y}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Recall the following special limit: \n\n$$\n{e}^{y}=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{y}{n})}^{n}\n$$\n\nNow, we rewrite the given limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{y}{n})}^{-4n} & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{[{(1+\\frac{y}{n})}^{n}]}^{-4} \\\\  & ={[\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{y}{n})}^{n}]}^{-4} \\\\  & ={({e}^{y})}^{-4} \\\\  & ={e}^{-4y} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{e}^{4y}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186363017 :question/worked-solution "Recall the following special limit: \n\n$$\n{e}^{u}={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{u}{n})}^{n}\n$$\n\nHence, with $u=2y$, we get\n\n$$\n{e}^{2y}={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{2y}{n})}^{n}\n$$\n\nNow, we rewrite the given limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{2y}{n})}^{n/2} & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{[{(1+\\frac{2y}{n})}^{n}]}^{1/2} \\\\  & ={[{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{2y}{n})}^{n}]}^{1/2} \\\\  & ={({e}^{2y})}^{1/2} \\\\  & ={e}^{2y/2} \\\\  & ={e}^{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Recall the following special limit: \n\n$$\n{e}^{u}=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{u}{n})}^{n}\n$$\n\nHence, with $u=2y$, we get\n\n$$\n{e}^{2y}=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{2y}{n})}^{n}\n$$\n\nNow, we rewrite the given limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{2y}{n})}^{n/2} & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{[{(1+\\frac{2y}{n})}^{n}]}^{1/2} \\\\  & ={[\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{2y}{n})}^{n}]}^{1/2} \\\\  & ={({e}^{2y})}^{1/2} \\\\  & ={e}^{2y/2} \\\\  & ={e}^{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186363692 :question/worked-solution "If we attempt to evaluate the limit, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 49}\\frac{\\sqrt{x}-7}{x-49} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{49}-7}{49-49}=\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to first simplify the limit. Since the radical is in the numerator, we can multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the numerator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 49}\\frac{\\sqrt{x}-7}{x-49} & ={lim}_{x\\to 49}\\frac{(\\sqrt{x}-7)(\\sqrt{x}+7)}{(x-49)(\\sqrt{x}+7)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 49}\\frac{(\\sqrt{x})^{2}-{7}^{2}}{(x-49)(\\sqrt{x}+7)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 49}\\frac{x-49}{(x-49)(\\sqrt{x}+7)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 49}\\frac{\\cancel{x-49}}{\\cancel{(x-49)}(\\sqrt{x}+7)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 49}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}+7} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{49}+7} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7+7} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{14}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "If we attempt to evaluate the limit, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 49}\\frac{\\sqrt{x}-7}{x-49} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{49}-7}{49-49}=\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to first simplify the limit. Since the radical is in the numerator, we can multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the numerator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 49}\\frac{\\sqrt{x}-7}{x-49} & =\\lim_{x\\to 49}\\frac{(\\sqrt{x}-7)(\\sqrt{x}+7)}{(x-49)(\\sqrt{x}+7)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 49}\\frac{(\\sqrt{x})^{2}-{7}^{2}}{(x-49)(\\sqrt{x}+7)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 49}\\frac{x-49}{(x-49)(\\sqrt{x}+7)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 49}\\frac{\\cancel{x-49}}{\\cancel{(x-49)}(\\sqrt{x}+7)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 49}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}+7} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{49}+7} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7+7} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{14}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186363695 :question/worked-solution "Recall the following special limit: \n\n$$\ne={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}\n$$\n\nNow, we rewrite the given limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{2n(x+y)} & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{[{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}]}^{2(x+y)} \\\\  & ={[{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}]}^{2(x+y)} \\\\  & ={e}^{2(x+y)}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Recall the following special limit: \n\n$$\ne=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}\n$$\n\nNow, we rewrite the given limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{2n(x+y)} & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{[{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}]}^{2(x+y)} \\\\  & ={[\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}]}^{2(x+y)} \\\\  & ={e}^{2(x+y)}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186363698 :question/worked-solution "Recall the following special limit: \n\n$$\n{e}^{x}={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{x}{n})}^{n}\n$$\n\nNow, we rewrite the given limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{x}{n})}^{3n} & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{[{(1+\\frac{x}{n})}^{n}]}^{3} \\\\  & ={[{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{x}{n})}^{n}]}^{3} \\\\  & ={({e}^{x})}^{3} \\\\  & ={e}^{3x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Recall the following special limit: \n\n$$\n{e}^{x}=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{(1