es\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{s}_{5} & =\\frac{4}{3}[(\\frac{1}{5}-\\frac{1}{6})] \\\\ {s}_{6} & =\\frac{4}{3}[(\\frac{1}{5}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{6}})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{6}}-\\frac{1}{7})] \\\\ {s}_{7} & =\\frac{4}{3}[(\\frac{1}{5}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{6}})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{6}}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{7}})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{7}}-\\frac{1}{8})] \\\\ {s}_{8} & =\\frac{4}{3}[(\\frac{1}{5}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{6}})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{6}}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{7}})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{7}}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{8}})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{8}}-\\frac{1}{9})].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that for each partial sum, we always end up canceling all terms with the exception of the first and last terms. Therefore, the $N$th partial sum is\n\n$$\n{s}_{N}=\\frac{4}{3}[\\frac{1}{5}-\\frac{1}{N+1}].\n$$\n\nFinally, we can determine the sum of the series by taking the limit of ${s}_{N}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=5}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{3n(n+1)} & ={lim}_{N\\to \\infty }{s}_{N} \\\\  & ={lim}_{N\\to \\infty }\\frac{4}{3}[\\frac{1}{5}-\\frac{1}{N+1}] \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}[\\frac{1}{5}-0] \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{15}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, note that the series can be written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{4}{3}{\\sum }_{n=5}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{n(n+1)}.\n$$\n\nUsing partial fractions we can write the series as\n\n$$\n\\frac{4}{3}{\\sum }_{n=5}^{\\infty }(\\frac{1}{n}-\\frac{1}{n+1}).\n$$\n\nComputing the first few partial sums, starting with $n=5,$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{s}_{5} & =\\frac{4}{3}[(\\frac{1}{5}-\\frac{1}{6})] \\\\ {s}_{6} & =\\frac{4}{3}[(\\frac{1}{5}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{6}})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{6}}-\\frac{1}{7})] \\\\ {s}_{7} & =\\frac{4}{3}[(\\frac{1}{5}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{6}})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{6}}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{7}})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{7}}-\\frac{1}{8})] \\\\ {s}_{8} & =\\frac{4}{3}[(\\frac{1}{5}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{6}})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{6}}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{7}})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{7}}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{8}})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{8}}-\\frac{1}{9})].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that for each partial sum, we always end up canceling all terms with the exception of the first and last terms. Therefore, the $N$th partial sum is\n\n$$\n{s}_{N}=\\frac{4}{3}[\\frac{1}{5}-\\frac{1}{N+1}].\n$$\n\nFinally, we can determine the sum of the series by taking the limit of ${s}_{N}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=5}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{3n(n+1)} & =\\lim_{N\\to \\infty }{s}_{N} \\\\  & =\\lim_{N\\to \\infty }\\frac{4}{3}[\\frac{1}{5}-\\frac{1}{N+1}] \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}[\\frac{1}{5}-0] \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{15}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186533995 :question/worked-solution "First, note that the series can be written as\n\n$$\n2{\\sum }_{n=3}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{(n+3)(n+4)}\n$$\n\nWe are also given that\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{(n+3)(n+4)}=\\frac{1}{n+3}-\\frac{1}{n+4}.\n$$\n\nSo, our series can be written as\n\n$$\n2{\\sum }_{n=3}^{\\infty }(\\frac{1}{n+3}-\\frac{1}{n+4}).\n$$\n\nLet's now compute the first few partial sums:\n$$\n\n$$\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{s}_{3} & =2[(\\frac{1}{6}-\\frac{1}{7})] \\\\ {s}_{4} & =2[(\\frac{1}{6}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{7}})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{7}}-\\frac{1}{8})] \\\\ {s}_{5} & =2[(\\frac{1}{6}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{7}})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{7}}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{8}})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{8}}-\\frac{1}{9})] \\\\ {s}_{6} & =2[(\\frac{1}{6}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{7}})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{7}}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{8}})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{8}}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{9}})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{9}}-\\frac{1}{10})]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n$$\n\nNotice that in each partial sum, we always end up canceling all terms except for the first and last terms. Therefore, the $N$th partial sum is\n\n$$\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{s}_{N} & =2(\\frac{1}{6}-\\frac{1}{N+4}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n$$\n\nFinally, we can determine the sum of the series by taking the limit of ${s}_{N}:$\n\n$$\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=3}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{(n+3)(n+4)} & ={lim}_{N\\to \\infty }{s}_{N} \\\\  & ={lim}_{N\\to \\infty }2(\\frac{1}{6}-\\frac{1}{N+4}) \\\\  & =2(\\frac{1}{6}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n" "First, note that the series can be written as\n\n$$\n2{\\sum }_{n=3}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{(n+3)(n+4)}\n$$\n\nWe are also given that\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{(n+3)(n+4)}=\\frac{1}{n+3}-\\frac{1}{n+4}.\n$$\n\nSo, our series can be written as\n\n$$\n2{\\sum }_{n=3}^{\\infty }(\\frac{1}{n+3}-\\frac{1}{n+4}).\n$$\n\nLet's now compute the first few partial sums:\n$$\n\n$$\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{s}_{3} & =2[(\\frac{1}{6}-\\frac{1}{7})] \\\\ {s}_{4} & =2[(\\frac{1}{6}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{7}})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{7}}-\\frac{1}{8})] \\\\ {s}_{5} & =2[(\\frac{1}{6}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{7}})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{7}}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{8}})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{8}}-\\frac{1}{9})] \\\\ {s}_{6} & =2[(\\frac{1}{6}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{7}})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{7}}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{8}})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{8}}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{9}})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{9}}-\\frac{1}{10})]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n$$\n\nNotice that in each partial sum, we always end up canceling all terms except for the first and last terms. Therefore, the $N$th partial sum is\n\n$$\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{s}_{N} & =2(\\frac{1}{6}-\\frac{1}{N+4}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n$$\n\nFinally, we can determine the sum of the series by taking the limit of ${s}_{N}:$\n\n$$\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=3}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{(n+3)(n+4)} & =\\lim_{N\\to \\infty }{s}_{N} \\\\  & =\\lim_{N\\to \\infty }2(\\frac{1}{6}-\\frac{1}{N+4}) \\\\  & =2(\\frac{1}{6}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n"] [:db/cas 17592186534002 :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (7,-2)}\\frac{\\sqrt{2x-y}-4}{16-2x+y} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2(7)-(-2)}-4}{16-2(7)+(-2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{4-4}{16-14-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by first multiplying the numerator and denominator by the conjugate of the numerator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (7,-2)}\\frac{\\sqrt{2x-y}-4}{16-2x+y} & ={lim}_{x\\to (7,-2)}\\frac{(\\sqrt{2x-y}-4)(\\sqrt{2x-y}+4)}{(16-2x+y)(\\sqrt{2x-y}+4)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (7,-2)}\\frac{2x-y-16}{(16-2x+y)(\\sqrt{2x-y}+4)} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{x\\to (7,-2)}\\frac{16-2x+y}{(16-2x+y)(\\sqrt{2x-y}+4)} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{x\\to (7,-2)}\\frac{\\cancel{16-2x+y}}{\\cancel{(16-2x+y)}(\\sqrt{2x-y}+4)} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{x\\to (7,-2)}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2x-y}+4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{lim}_{x\\to (7,-2)}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2x-y}+4} & =-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2(7)-(-2)}+4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{16}+4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4+4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $-\\frac{1}{8}.$" "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (7,-2)}\\frac{\\sqrt{2x-y}-4}{16-2x+y} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2(7)-(-2)}-4}{16-2(7)+(-2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{4-4}{16-14-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by first multiplying the numerator and denominator by the conjugate of the numerator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (7,-2)}\\frac{\\sqrt{2x-y}-4}{16-2x+y} & =\\lim_{x\\to (7,-2)}\\frac{(\\sqrt{2x-y}-4)(\\sqrt{2x-y}+4)}{(16-2x+y)(\\sqrt{2x-y}+4)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (7,-2)}\\frac{2x-y-16}{(16-2x+y)(\\sqrt{2x-y}+4)} \\\\  & =-\\lim_{x\\to (7,-2)}\\frac{16-2x+y}{(16-2x+y)(\\sqrt{2x-y}+4)} \\\\  & =-\\lim_{x\\to (7,-2)}\\frac{\\cancel{16-2x+y}}{\\cancel{(16-2x+y)}(\\sqrt{2x-y}+4)} \\\\  & =-\\lim_{x\\to (7,-2)}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2x-y}+4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\lim_{x\\to (7,-2)}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2x-y}+4} & =-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2(7)-(-2)}+4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{16}+4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4+4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $-\\frac{1}{8}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186534009 :question/worked-solution "We can factor the denominator and use partial fractions to show that\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}-6n+8}=\\frac{1}{(n-4)(n-2)}=\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{1}{n-4}-\\frac{1}{n-2}).\n$$\n\nSo, our series can be written as\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=5}^{\\infty }4\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{1}{n-4}-\\frac{1}{n-2})=2{\\sum }_{n=5}^{\\infty }(\\frac{1}{n-4}-\\frac{1}{n-2}).\n$$\n\nLet's now compute the first few partial sums:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{s}_{5} & =2[(1-\\frac{1}{3})] \\\\ {s}_{6} & =2[(1-\\frac{1}{3})+(\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{4})] \\\\ {s}_{7} & =2[(1-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{3}})+(\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{4})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{3}}-\\frac{1}{5})] \\\\ {s}_{8} & =2[(1-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{3}})+(\\frac{1}{2}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{4}})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{3}}-\\frac{1}{5})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{4}}-\\frac{1}{6})]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe notice that all of the terms cancel except $1,\\frac{1}{2},$ and the $-\\frac{1}{N-3}$ and  $-\\frac{1}{N-2}$ terms. Therefore, the $N$th partial sum is\n\n$$\n{s}_{N}=2(1+\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{N-3}-\\frac{1}{N-2}).\n$$\n\nFinally, we can determine the sum of the series by taking the limit of ${s}_{N}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=5}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{{n}^{2}-6n+8} & ={lim}_{N\\to \\infty }{s}_{N} \\\\  & ={lim}_{N\\to \\infty }2(1+\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{N-3}-\\frac{1}{N-2}) \\\\  & =2(1+\\frac{1}{2}-0-0) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2} \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We can factor the denominator and use partial fractions to show that\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}-6n+8}=\\frac{1}{(n-4)(n-2)}=\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{1}{n-4}-\\frac{1}{n-2}).\n$$\n\nSo, our series can be written as\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=5}^{\\infty }4\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{1}{n-4}-\\frac{1}{n-2})=2{\\sum }_{n=5}^{\\infty }(\\frac{1}{n-4}-\\frac{1}{n-2}).\n$$\n\nLet's now compute the first few partial sums:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{s}_{5} & =2[(1-\\frac{1}{3})] \\\\ {s}_{6} & =2[(1-\\frac{1}{3})+(\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{4})] \\\\ {s}_{7} & =2[(1-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{3}})+(\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{4})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{3}}-\\frac{1}{5})] \\\\ {s}_{8} & =2[(1-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{3}})+(\\frac{1}{2}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{4}})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{3}}-\\frac{1}{5})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{4}}-\\frac{1}{6})]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe notice that all of the terms cancel except $1,\\frac{1}{2},$ and the $-\\frac{1}{N-3}$ and  $-\\frac{1}{N-2}$ terms. Therefore, the $N$th partial sum is\n\n$$\n{s}_{N}=2(1+\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{N-3}-\\frac{1}{N-2}).\n$$\n\nFinally, we can determine the sum of the series by taking the limit of ${s}_{N}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=5}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{{n}^{2}-6n+8} & =\\lim_{N\\to \\infty }{s}_{N} \\\\  & =\\lim_{N\\to \\infty }2(1+\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{N-3}-\\frac{1}{N-2}) \\\\  & =2(1+\\frac{1}{2}-0-0) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2} \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186534016 :question/worked-solution "First, note that the series can be written as\n\n$$\n12{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{(n+3)(n+5)}.\n$$\n\nWe are also given that\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{(n+3)(n+5)}=\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{1}{n+3}-\\frac{1}{n+5}).\n$$\n\nSo, our series can be written as\n\n$$\n12{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{1}{n+3}-\\frac{1}{n+5})=6{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(\\frac{1}{n+3}-\\frac{1}{n+5}).\n$$\n\nLet's now compute the first few partial sums:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{s}_{0} & =6[(\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{1}{5})] \\\\ {s}_{1} & =6[(\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{1}{5})+(\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{1}{6})] \\\\ {s}_{2} & =6[(\\frac{1}{3}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{5}})+(\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{1}{6})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{5}}-\\frac{1}{7})] \\\\ {s}_{3} & =6[(\\frac{1}{3}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{5}})+(\\frac{1}{4}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{6}})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{5}}-\\frac{1}{7})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{6}}-\\frac{1}{8})] \\\\ {s}_{4} & =6[(\\frac{1}{3}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{5}})+(\\frac{1}{4}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{6}})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{5}}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{7}})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{6}}-\\frac{1}{8})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{7}}-\\frac{1}{9})]\\end{aligned}\n$$