 limit of ${s}_{N}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=8}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{{n}^{2}-7n+12} & ={lim}_{N\\to \\infty }{s}_{N} \\\\  & ={lim}_{N\\to \\infty }2(\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{1}{N-3}) \\\\  & =2(\\frac{1}{4}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, factoring the denominator and using partial fractions, we can write the series as\n\n$$\n\\frac{2}{{n}^{2}-7n+12}=\\frac{2}{(n-4)(n-3)}=2(\\frac{1}{n-4}-\\frac{1}{n-3})\n$$\n\nSo, our series can be written as\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=8}^{\\infty }2(\\frac{1}{n-4}-\\frac{1}{n-3})=2{\\sum }_{n=8}^{\\infty }(\\frac{1}{n-4}-\\frac{1}{n-3}).\n$$\n\nLet's now compute the first few partial sums:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{s}_{8} & =2[(\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{1}{5})] \\\\ {s}_{9} & =2[(\\frac{1}{4}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{5}})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{5}}-\\frac{1}{6})] \\\\ {s}_{10} & =2[(\\frac{1}{4}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{5}})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{5}}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{6}})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{6}}-\\frac{1}{7})] \\\\ {s}_{11} & =2[(\\frac{1}{4}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{5}})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{5}}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{6}})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{6}}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{7}})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{7}}-\\frac{1}{8})]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that in each partial sum, we always end up canceling all terms except for the first and last terms. Therefore, the $N$th partial sum is\n\n$$\n{s}_{N}=2(\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{1}{N-3}).\n$$\n\nFinally, we can determine the sum of the series by taking the limit of ${s}_{N}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=8}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{{n}^{2}-7n+12} & =\\lim_{N\\to \\infty }{s}_{N} \\\\  & =\\lim_{N\\to \\infty }2(\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{1}{N-3}) \\\\  & =2(\\frac{1}{4}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186534057 :question/worked-solution "We can factor the denominator and use partial fractions to show that\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}+4n+3}=\\frac{1}{(n+1)(n+3)}=\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{1}{n+1}-\\frac{1}{n+3}).\n$$\n\nSo, our series can be written as\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=4}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{2}(\\fra