}{3{n}^{{n}^{2}}}.\n$$\n\nTo apply the root test, we find the limit of $\\sqrt[n]{{a}_{n}}$ as $n\\to \\infty :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{{a}_{n}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{({a}_{n})}^{1/n} \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\left(\\frac{{n}^{n/2}}{3{n}^{{n}^{2}}}\\right)}^{1/n} \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\sqrt{n}}{\\sqrt[n]{3}\\cdot {n}^{n}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{\\frac{1}{3}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{n}^{n-1/2}} \\\\  & =1\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we used the fact that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{\\frac{1}{3}}=1.\n$$\n\nSince $L=0<1,$ the series converges by the root test."] [:db/cas 17592186535055 :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have the series $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\n$$ such that ${a}_{n}\\ge 0$ for all $n\\ge 1,$ and that $L$ is defined as  \n\n$$\nL={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{{a}_{n}}={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{({a}_{n})}^{1/n}.\n$$\n\n \n\nThen, the root test states the following:\n\n- If $L<1$, the series converges.\n\n- If $L>1$ or $L=\\infty $, the series diverges.\n\n- If $L=1$, the root test is inconclusive.\n\nWe have the sequence ${a}_{n},$ defined as\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{(10n)^{n}n}{(8n+1)^{n}}.\n$$\n\nTo apply the root test, we find the limit of $\\sqrt[n]{{a}_{n}}$ as $n\\to \\infty :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{{a}_{n}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{({a}_{n})}^{1/n} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\left(\\frac{(10n)^{n}n}{(8n+1)^{n}}\\right)}^{1/n} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\left(\\frac{10n\\sqrt[n]{n}}{8n+1}\\right) \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{10n\\sqrt[n]{n}}{n(8+\\frac{1}{n})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{10\\sqrt[n]{n}}{(8+\\frac{1}{n})} \\\\  & =\\frac{10\\cdot 1}{8+0} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we used the fact that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{n}=1.\n$$\n\nSince $L=\\frac{5}{4}>1,$ the series diverges by the root 