\\infty }\\sqrt[n]{{a}_{n}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{({a}_{n})}^{1/n} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\left(\\frac{{6}^{n}}{{5}^{n}{n}^{6}}\\right)}^{1/n} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\left(\\frac{6}{5\\cdot \\sqrt[n]{{n}^{6}}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{5}{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[n]{n}}\\right)}^{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{5}\\cdot {1}^{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we used the fact that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{n}=1.\n$$\n\nSince $L=\\frac{6}{5}>1,$ the series diverges by the root test." "Suppose we have the series $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\n$$ such that ${a}_{n}\\ge 0$ for all $n\\ge 1,$ and that $L$ is defined as  \n\n$$\nL=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{{a}_{n}}=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{({a}_{n})}^{1/n}.\n$$\n\n \n\nThen, the root test states the following:\n\n- If $L<1$, the series converges.\n\n- If $L>1$ or $L=\\infty $, the series diverges.\n\n- If $L=1$, the root test is inconclusive.\n\nWe have the sequence ${a}_{n},$ defined as\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{{6}^{n}}{{5}^{n}{n}^{6}}.\n$$\n\nTo apply the root test, we find the limit of $\\sqrt[n]{{a}_{n}}$ as $n\\to \\infty :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{{a}_{n}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{({a}_{n})}^{1/n} \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\left(\\frac{{6}^{n}}{{5}^{n}{n}^{6}}\\right)}^{1/n} \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\left(\\frac{6}{5\\cdot \\sqrt[n]{{n}^{6}}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{5}\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[n]{n}}\\right)}^{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{5}\\cdot {1}^{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we used the fact that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{n}=1.\n$$\n\nSince $L=\\frac{6}{5}>1,$ the series diverges by the root test."] [:db/cas 17592186535938 :question/worked-solution "We break up the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{4|x|}{{(1+{x}^{2})}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{4|x|}{{(1+{x}^{2})}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{4|x|}{{(1+{x}^{2})}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe start by calculating the second of these integrals:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{4|x|}{{(1+{x}^{2})}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{4x}{{(1+{x}^{2})}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe calculate the antiderivative using a substitution. Let $u=1+{x}^{2}$,  then $\\text{d}u=2x\\text{d}x.$ Hence\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{4x}{{(1+{x}^{2})}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{2}{{u}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{(1+{x}^{2})}^{2}}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{4|x|}{{(1+{x}^{2})}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{4x}{{(1+{x}^{2})}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}\\frac{4x}{{(1+{x}^{2})}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }({-\\frac{1}{{(1+{x}^{2})}^{2}}|}_{0}^{a}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(-\\frac{1}{{(1+{x}^{2})}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{(1+0)^{2}}) \\\\  & =-0+1 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the integrand $\\frac{4|x|}{{(1+{x}^{2})}^{3}}$ is an **even** function. This means that we can immediately deduce that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{4|x|}{{(1+{x}^{2})}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since both improper integrals exist, we conclude that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{4|x|}{{(1+{x}^{2})}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{4|x|}{{(1+{x}^{2})}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{4|x|}{{(1+{x}^{2})}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =1+1 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We break up the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{4|x|}{{(1+{x}^{2})}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{4|x|}{{(1+{x}^{2})}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{4|x|}{{(1+{x}^{2})}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe start by calculating the second of these integrals:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{4|x|}{{(1+{x}^{2})}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{4x}{{(1+{x}^{2})}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe calculate the antiderivative using a substitution. Let $u=1+{x}^{2}$,  then $\\text{d}u=2x\\text{d}x.$ Hence\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{4x}{{(1+{x}^{2})}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{2}{{u}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{(1+{x}^{2})}^{2}}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{4|x|}{{(1+{x}^{2})}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{4x}{{(1+{x}^{2})}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}\\frac{4x}{{(1+{x}^{2})}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }({-\\frac{1}{{(1+{x}^{2})}^{2}}|}_{0}^{a}) \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }(-\\frac{1}{{(1+{x}^{2})}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{(1+0)^{2}}) \\\\  & =-0+1 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the integrand $\\frac{4|x|}{{(1+{x}^{2})}^{3}}$ is an **even** function. This means that we can immediately deduce that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{4|x|}{{(1+{x}^{2})}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since both improper integrals exist, we conclude that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{4|x|}{{(1+{x}^{2})}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{4|x|}{{(1+{x}^{2})}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{4|x|}{{(1+{x}^{2})}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =1+1 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186536823 :question/worked-solution "The definite integral can be defined in terms of the limit of a left Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\n    \n\nwhere $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ and ${x}_{i}=a+iΔx.$\n\nIn the definite integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{7}\\frac{2}{x+3}\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nthe function is $f(x)=\\frac{2}{x+3},$ the lower bound is $a=1,$ and the upper bound is $b=7.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{7-1}{n}=\\frac{6}{n},\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{7}\\frac{2}{x+3}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[f(1+i\\cdot \\frac{6}{n})\\cdot \\frac{6}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[f(1+\\frac{6i}{n})\\cdot \\frac{6}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[\\frac{2}{(1+6i/n)+3}\\cdot \\frac{6}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[\\left(\\frac{2}{6i/n+4}\\right)\\cdot \\frac{6}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[(\\,\\frac{1}{3i/n+2}\\,)\\cdot \\frac{6}{n}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The definite integral can be defined in terms of the limit of a left Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\n    \n\nwhere $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ and ${x}_{i}=a+iΔx.$\n\nIn the definite integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{7}\\frac{2}{x+3}\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nthe function is $f(x)=\\frac{2}{x+3},$ the lower bound is $a=1,$ and the upper bound is $b=7.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{7-1}{n}=\\frac{6}{n},\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{7}\\frac{2}{x+3}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[f(1+i\\cdot \\frac{6}{n})\\cdot \\frac{6}{n}] \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[f(1+\\frac{6i}{n})\\cdot \\frac{6}{n}] \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[\\frac{2}{(1+6i/n)+3}\\cdot \\frac{6}{n}] \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[\\left(\\frac{2}{6i/n+4}\\right)\\cdot \\frac{6}{n}] \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[(\\,\\frac{1}{3i/n+2}\\,)\\cdot \\frac{6}{n}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186536829 :question/worked-solution "The definite integral can be defined in terms of the limit of a right Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{i}=a+iΔx$ and $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}.$\n\nIn the definite integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{2}^{5}8{x}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nthe function is $f(x)=8{x}^{4},$ the lower bound is $a=2$ and the upper bound is $b=5.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{5-2}{n}=\\frac{3}{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{5}8{x}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}[f(2+i\\cdot \\frac{3}{n})\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}[f(2+\\frac{3i}{n})\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}[8{(\\,2+\\frac{3i}{n}\\,)}^{\\,4}\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The definite integral can be defined in terms of the limit of a right Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{i}=a+iΔx$ and $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}.$\n\nIn the definite integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{2}^{5}8{x}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nthe function is $f(x)=8{x}^{4},$ the lower bound is $a=2$ and the upper bound is $b=5.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{5-2}{n}=\\frac{3}{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{5}8{x}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}[f(2+i\\cdot \\frac{3}{n})\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}] \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}[f(2+\\frac{3i}{n})\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}] \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}[8{(\\,2+\\frac{3i}{n}\\,)}^{\\,4}\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186536831 :question/worked-solution "The definite integral can be defined in terms of the limit of a right Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{i}=a+iΔx$ and $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}.$\n\nIn the definite integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-4}^{-1}\\sqrt{5}{x}^{-4}\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nthe function is $f(x)=\\sqrt{5}{x}^{-4},$ the lower bound is $a=-4$ and the upper bound is $b=-1.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{-1-(-4)}{n}=\\frac{3}{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-4}^{-1}\\sqrt{5}{x}^{-4}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}[f(-4+i\\cdot \\frac{3}{n})\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}[f(-4+\\frac{3i}{n})\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}[\\sqrt{5}{(-4+\\frac{3i}{n})}^{-4}\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The definite integral can be defined in terms of the limit of a right Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{i}=a+iΔx$ and $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}.$\n\nIn the definite integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-4}^{-1}\\sqrt{5}{x}^{-4}\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nthe function is $f(x)=\\sqrt{5}{x}^{-4},$ the lower bound is $a=-4$ and the upper bound is $b=-1.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{-1-(-4)}{n}=\\frac{3}{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-4}^{-1}\\sqrt{5}{x}^{-4}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}[f(-4+i\\cdot \\frac{3}{n})\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}] \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}[f(-4+\\frac{3i}{n})\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}] \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}[\\sqrt{5}{(-4+\\frac{3i}{n})}^{-4}\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186536833 :question/worked-solution "To determine whether the sequence converges or diverges, we need to determine the limit of the sequence as $n$ approaches infinity:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{12n+1}{3n}.\n$$\n\nThe dominant term in the denominator is $3n,$ so we divide the numerator and the denominator by $n$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n} & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{12n+1}{3n} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{12n}{n}+\\frac{1}{n})}{\\frac{3n}{n}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{12+\\frac{1}{n}}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{12+0}{3} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the sequence converges, and its limit is $4.$" "To determine whether the sequence converges or diverges, we need to determine the limit of the sequence as $n$ approaches infinity:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{12n+1}{3n}.\n$$\n\nThe dominant term in the denominator is $3n,$ so we divide the numerator and the denominator by $n$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n} & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{12n+1}{3n} \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{12n}{n}+\\frac{1}{n})}{\\frac{3n}{n}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{12+\\frac{1}{n}}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{12+0}{3} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the sequence converges, and its limit is $4.$"] [:db/cas 17592186536837 :question/worked-solution "The definite integral can be defined in terms of the limit of a left Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ and ${x}_{i}=a+iΔx.$\n\nIn the definite integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{4}3{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nthe function is $f(x)=3{x}^{3},$ the lower bound is $a=1,$ and the upper bound is $b=4.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{4-1}{n}=\\frac{3}{n},\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{4}3{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[f(1+i\\cdot \\frac{3}{n})\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[3{(\\,1+\\frac{3i}{n}\\,)}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The definite integral can be defined in terms of the limit of a left Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ and ${x}_{i}=a+iΔx.$\n\nIn the definite integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{4}3{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nthe function is $f(x)=3{x}^{3},$ the lower bound is $a=1,$ and the upper bound is $b=4.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{4-1}{n}=\\frac{3}{n},\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{4}3{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[f(1+i\\cdot \\frac{3}{n})\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}] \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[3{(\\,1+\\frac{3i}{n}\\,)}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186536852 :question/worked-solution "The definite integral can be defined in terms of the limit of a left Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{j})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\n    \n\nwhere $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ and ${x}_{j}=a+jΔx.$\n\nIn the definite integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{2}^{5}2{x}^{1/3}\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nthe function is $f(x)=2{x}^{1/3},$ the lower bound is $a=2,$ and the upper bound is $b=5.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{5-2}{n}=\\frac{3}{n},\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{5}2{x}^{1/3}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{j})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}[f(2+j\\cdot \\frac{3}{n})\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}[f(2+\\frac{3j}{n})\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}[2{(2+\\frac{3j}{n})}^{1/3}\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The definite integral can be defined in terms of the limit of a left Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{j})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\n    \n\nwhere $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ and ${x}_{j}=a+jΔx.$\n\nIn the definite integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{2}^{5}2{x}^{1/3}\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nthe function is $f(x)=2{x}^{1/3},$ the lower bound is $a=2,$ and the upper bound is $b=5.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{5-2}{n}=\\frac{3}{n},\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{5}2{x}^{1/3}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{j})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}[f(2+j\\cdot \\frac{3}{n})\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}] \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}[f(2+\\frac{3j}{n})\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}] \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}[2{(2+\\frac{3j}{n})}^{1/3}\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186536861 :question/worked-solution "The definite integral can be defined in terms of the limit of a right Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{i}=a+iΔx$ and $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}.$\n\nIn the definite integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{4}(x+8)^{1/3}\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nthe function is $f(x)=(x+8)^{1/3},$ the lower bound is $a=1$ and the upper bound is $b=4.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{4-1}{n}=\\frac{3}{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{4}(x+8)^{1/3}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}[f(1+i\\cdot \\frac{3}{n})\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}[f(1+\\frac{3i}{n})\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}[{((1+\\frac{3i}{n})+8)}^{1/3}\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}[{(\\,9+\\frac{3i}{n}\\,)}^{1/3}\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The definite integral can be defined in terms of the limit of a right Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{i}=a+iΔx$ and $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}.$\n\nIn the definite integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{4}(x+8)^{1/3}\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nthe function is $f(x)=(x+8)^{1/3},$ the lower bound is $a=1$ and the upper bound is $b=4.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{4-1}{n}=\\frac{3}{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{4}(x+8)^{1/3}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}[f(1+i\\cdot \\frac{3}{n})\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}] \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}[f(1+\\frac{3i}{n})\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}] \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}[{((1+\\frac{3i}{n})+8)}^{1/3}\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}] \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}[{(\\,9+\\frac{3i}{n}\\,)}^{1/3}\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186536882 :question/worked-solution "First, we multiply out the numerator and denominator of the sequence and get\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{(2{n}^{2}+3)(2{n}^{2}-3)}{2n(5{n}^{3}-1)}=\\frac{4{n}^{4}-9}{10{n}^{4}-2n}.\n$$\n\nSo to find the limit, we will need to compute\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{4{n}^{4}-9}{10{n}^{4}-2n}.\n$$\n\nThe dominant term in the denominator is $10{n}^{4},$ so we divide the numerator and the denominator by ${n}^{4}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{4{n}^{4}-9}{10{n}^{4}-2n} & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{4{n}^{4}}{{n}^{4}}-\\frac{9}{{n}^{4}})}{(\\frac{10{n}^{4}}{{n}^{4}}-\\frac{2n}{{n}^{4}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{(4-\\frac{9}{{n}^{4}})}{(10-\\frac{2}{{n}^{3}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{4-0}{10-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the sequence converges and its limit is $\\frac{2}{5}.$" "First, we multiply out the numerator and denominator of the sequence and get\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{(2{n}^{2}+3)(2{n}^{2}-3)}{2n(5{n}^{3}-1)}=\\frac{4{n}^{4}-9}{10{n}^{4}-2n}.\n$$\n\nSo to find the limit, we will need to compute\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{4{n}^{4}-9}{10{n}^{4}-2n}.\n$$\n\nThe dominant term in the denominator is $10{n}^{4},$ so we divide the numerator and the denominator by ${n}^{4}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{4{n}^{4}-9}{10{n}^{4}-2n} & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{4{n}^{4}}{{n}^{4}}-\\frac{9}{{n}^{4}})}{(\\frac{10{n}^{4}}{{n}^{4}}-\\frac{2n}{{n}^{4}})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{(4-\\frac{9}{{n}^{4}})}{(10-\\frac{2}{{n}^{3}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{4-0}{10-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the sequence converges and its limit is $\\frac{2}{5}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186536898 :question/worked-solution "First, we multiply out the numerator and denominator of the sequence and get\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{(3n-2)(3n+2)}{n(1-9n)}=\\frac{9{n}^{2}-4}{n-9{n}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nSo to find the limit, we will need to compute\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{9{n}^{2}-4}{n-9{n}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nThe dominant term in the denominator is $-9{n}^{2},$ so we divide the numerator and the denominator by ${n}^{2}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{9{n}^{2}-4}{n-9{n}^{2}} & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{9{n}^{2}}{{n}^{2}}-\\frac{4}{{n}^{2}})}{(\\frac{n}{{n}^{2}}-\\frac{9{n}^{2}}{{n}^{2}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{(9-\\frac{4}{{n}^{2}})}{(\\frac{1}{n}-9)} \\\\  & =\\frac{9-0}{0-9} \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the sequence converges and its limit is $-1.$" "First, we multiply out the numerator and denominator of the sequence and get\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{(3n-2)(3n+2)}{n(1-9n)}=\\frac{9{n}^{2}-4}{n-9{n}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nSo to find the limit, we will need to compute\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{9{n}^{2}-4}{n-9{n}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nThe dominant term in the denominator is $-9{n}^{2},$ so we divide the numerator and the denominator by ${n}^{2}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{9{n}^{2}-4}{n-9{n}^{2}} & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{9{n}^{2}}{{n}^{2}}-\\frac{4}{{n}^{2}})}{(\\frac{n}{{n}^{2}}-\\frac{9{n}^{2}}{{n}^{2}})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{(9-\\frac{4}{{n}^{2}})}{(\\frac{1}{n}-9)} \\\\  & =\\frac{9-0}{0-9} \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the sequence converges and its limit is $-1.$"] [:db/cas 17592186537931 :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=2x$, the derivative ${f}^{′}(x)$ can be computed using the definition of the derivative. The first few steps of this calculation are shown below:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{f(x+h)-f(x)}{h} \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{2(x+h)-2x}{h} \\\\  & =…\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinish the calculation, and hence find ${f}^{′}(x).$" "Given that $f(x)=2x$, the derivative ${f}^{′}(x)$ can be computed using the definition of the derivative. The first few steps of this calculation are shown below:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\frac{f(x+h)-f(x)}{h} \\\\  & =\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\frac{2(x+h)-2x}{h} \\\\  & =…\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinish the calculation, and hence find ${f}^{′}(x).$"] [:db/cas 17592186537931 :question/worked-solution "By proceeding with the remaining calculations we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{f(x+h)-f(x)}{h} \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{2(x+h)-2x}{h} \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{2x+2h-2x}{h} \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{2h}{h} \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{′}(x)=2.$" "By proceeding with the remaining calculations we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\frac{f(x+h)-f(x)}{h} \\\\  & =\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\frac{2(x+h)-2x}{h} \\\\  & =\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\frac{2x+2h-2x}{h} \\\\  & =\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\frac{2h}{h} \\\\  & =\\lim_{h\\to 0}2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{′}(x)=2.$"] [:db/cas 17592186537947 :question/worked-solution "The definition of ${f}^{′}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x)={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{f(x+h)-f(x)}{h}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying this to $f(x)=-4{x}^{2}+5x$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\left(\\frac{-4(x+h)^{2}+5(x+h)-(-4{x}^{2}+5x)}{h}\\right) \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\left(\\frac{-4({x}^{2}+2xh+{h}^{2})+5x+5h+4{x}^{2}-5x}{h}\\right) \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\left(\\frac{-4{x}^{2}-8xh-4{h}^{2}+5x+5h+4{x}^{2}-5x}{h}\\right) \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\left(\\frac{-8xh-4{h}^{2}+5h}{h}\\right) \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\left(\\frac{h(-8x-4h+5)}{h}\\right) \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}(-8x-4h+5) \\\\  & =-8x+5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The definition of ${f}^{′}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x)=\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\frac{f(x+h)-f(x)}{h}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying this to $f(x)=-4{x}^{2}+5x$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\left(\\frac{-4(x+h)^{2}+5(x+h)-(-4{x}^{2}+5x)}{h}\\right) \\\\  & =\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\left(\\frac{-4({x}^{2}+2xh+{h}^{2})+5x+5h+4{x}^{2}-5x}{h}\\right) \\\\  & =\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\left(\\frac{-4{x}^{2}-8xh-4{h}^{2}+5x+5h+4{x}^{2}-5x}{h}\\right) \\\\  & =\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\left(\\frac{-8xh-4{h}^{2}+5h}{h}\\right) \\\\  & =\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\left(\\frac{h(-8x-4h+5)}{h}\\right) \\\\  & =\\lim_{h\\to 0}(-8x-4h+5) \\\\  & =-8x+5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186541054 :question/worked-solution "If we attempt to evaluate the limit, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -4}\\frac{{t}^{2}-16}{{t}^{2}+4t} & =\\frac{(-4)^{2}-16}{(-4)^{2}+4(-4)}=\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, to find the limit, we have to factor the numerator and the denominator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{t}^{2}-16}{{t}^{2}+4t} & =\\frac{(t+4)(t-4)}{t(t+4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{t-4}{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to -4}\\frac{{t}^{2}-16}{{t}^{2}+4t} & ={lim}_{t\\to -4}\\frac{t-4}{t} \\\\  & =\\frac{-4-4}{(-4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-8)}{(-4)} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "If we attempt to evaluate the limit, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to -4}\\frac{{t}^{2}-16}{{t}^{2}+4t} & =\\frac{(-4)^{2}-16}{(-4)^{2}+4(-4)}=\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, to find the limit, we have to factor the numerator and the denominator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{t}^{2}-16}{{t}^{2}+4t} & =\\frac{(t+4)(t-4)}{t(t+4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{t-4}{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to -4}\\frac{{t}^{2}-16}{{t}^{2}+4t} & =\\lim_{t\\to -4}\\frac{t-4}{t} \\\\  & =\\frac{-4-4}{(-4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-8)}{(-4)} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186541065 :question/worked-solution "If we attempt to evaluate the limit, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 4}\\frac{{x}^{2}-2x-8}{{x}^{2}+x-20} & =\\frac{{4}^{2}-2(4)-8}{{4}^{2}+4-20}=\\frac{0}{0},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, to find the limit, we have to factor the numerator and the denominator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}-2x-8}{{x}^{2}+x-20} & =\\frac{(x+2)(x-4)}{(x+5)(x-4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{x+2}{x+5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 4}\\frac{{x}^{2}-2x-8}{{x}^{2}+x-20} & ={lim}_{x\\to 4}\\frac{x+2}{x+5} \\\\  & =\\frac{4+2}{4+5} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "If we attempt to evaluate the limit, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 4}\\frac{{x}^{2}-2x-8}{{x}^{2}+x-20} & =\\frac{{4}^{2}-2(4)-8}{{4}^{2}+4-20}=\\frac{0}{0},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, to find the limit, we have to factor the numerator and the denominator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}-2x-8}{{x}^{2}+x-20} & =\\frac{(x+2)(x-4)}{(x+5)(x-4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{x+2}{x+5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 4}\\frac{{x}^{2}-2x-8}{{x}^{2}+x-20} & =\\lim_{x\\to 4}\\frac{x+2}{x+5} \\\\  & =\\frac{4+2}{4+5} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186541105 :question/worked-solution "If we attempt to evaluate the limit, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{{x}^{3}-27}{2x-6} & =\\frac{{3}^{3}-27}{2(3)-6}=\\frac{27-27}{6-6}=\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, to find the limit, we have to factor the numerator and the denominator. \n\nNotice that the numerator is a difference of cubes. Therefore, factoring our expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{3}-27}{2x-6} & =\\frac{{x}^{3}-{3}^{3}}{2(x-3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x-3)({x}^{2}+3x+{3}^{2})}{2(x-3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}+3x+9}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{{x}^{3}-27}{2x-6} & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{{x}^{2}+3x+9}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{{3}^{2}+3(3)+9}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{9+9+9}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{27}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "If we attempt to evaluate the limit, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 3}\\frac{{x}^{3}-27}{2x-6} & =\\frac{{3}^{3}-27}{2(3)-6}=\\frac{27-27}{6-6}=\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, to find the limit, we have to factor the numerator and the denominator. \n\nNotice that the numerator is a difference of cubes. Therefore, factoring our expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{3}-27}{2x-6} & =\\frac{{x}^{3}-{3}^{3}}{2(x-3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x-3)({x}^{2}+3x+{3}^{2})}{2(x-3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}+3x+9}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 3}\\frac{{x}^{3}-27}{2x-6} & =\\lim_{x\\to 3}\\frac{{x}^{2}+3x+9}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{{3}^{2}+3(3)+9}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{9+9+9}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{27}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186541129 :question/worked-solution "If we attempt to evaluate the limit, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 5}\\frac{{x}^{2}-5x}{{x}^{2}-8x+15} & =\\frac{{5}^{2}-5(5)}{{5}^{2}-8(5)+15}=\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, to find the limit, we have to factor the numerator and the denominator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}-5x}{{x}^{2}-8x+15} & =\\frac{x(x-5)}{(x-5)(x-3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{x-3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 5}\\frac{{x}^{2}-5x}{{x}^{2}-8x+15} & ={lim}_{x\\to 5}\\frac{x}{x-3} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{5-3} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "If we attempt to evaluate the limit, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 5}\\frac{{x}^{2}-5x}{{x}^{2}-8x+15} & =\\frac{{5}^{2}-5(5)}{{5}^{2}-8(5)+15}=\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, to find the limit, we have to factor the numerator and the denominator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}-5x}{{x}^{2}-8x+15} & =\\frac{x(x-5)}{(x-5)(x-3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{x-3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 5}\\frac{{x}^{2}-5x}{{x}^{2}-8x+15} & =\\lim_{x\\to 5}\\frac{x}{x-3} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{5-3} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186546967 :question/worked-solution "First, we divide both the numerator and the denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{5}.$ Then, by noting that the reciprocals shrink to $0$ in the limit, we can then evaluate the resulting expression.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{6{x}^{7}-3{x}^{3}-4}{2{x}^{5}+7} & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{(\\frac{6{x}^{7}}{{x}^{5}}-\\frac{3{x}^{3}}{{x}^{5}}-\\frac{4}{{x}^{5}})}{(\\frac{2{x}^{5}}{{x}^{5}}+\\frac{7}{{x}^{5}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{(6{x}^{2}-\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{4}{{x}^{3}})}{(2+\\frac{7}{{x}^{5}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{6{x}^{2}-0-0}{2+0} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }3{x}^{2} \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we divide both the numerator and the denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{5}.$ Then, by noting that the reciprocals shrink to $0$ in the limit, we can then evaluate the resulting expression.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{6{x}^{7}-3{x}^{3}-4}{2{x}^{5}+7} & =\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{(\\frac{6{x}^{7}}{{x}^{5}}-\\frac{3{x}^{3}}{{x}^{5}}-\\frac{4}{{x}^{5}})}{(\\frac{2{x}^{5}}{{x}^{5}}+\\frac{7}{{x}^{5}})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{(6{x}^{2}-\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{4}{{x}^{3}})}{(2+\\frac{7}{{x}^{5}})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{6{x}^{2}-0-0}{2+0} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }3{x}^{2} \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186548008 :question/worked-solution "To apply the ratio test, we define ${a}_{n}=\\frac{(x-2)^{n}}{n!}.$ Then, we calculate the ratio of two successive terms and take the limit as $n\\to \\infty :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{\\frac{(x-2)^{n+1}}{(n+1)!}}{\\frac{(x-2)^{n}}{n!}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{(x-2)^{n+1}}{(n+1)!}\\cdot \\frac{n!}{(x-2)^{n}}| \\\\  & =|x-2|{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{n+1} \\\\  & =|x-2|\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe series converges if $L<1.$ In this case, the convergence condition is satisfied for all $-\\infty <x<\\infty .$  Therefore, we conclude that the radius of convergence is $R=\\infty .$" "To apply the ratio test, we define ${a}_{n}=\\frac{(x-2)^{n}}{n!}.$ Then, we calculate the ratio of two successive terms and take the limit as $n\\to \\infty :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{\\frac{(x-2)^{n+1}}{(n+1)!}}{\\frac{(x-2)^{n}}{n!}}| \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{(x-2)^{n+1}}{(n+1)!}\\cdot \\frac{n!}{(x-2)^{n}}| \\\\  & =|x-2|\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{n+1} \\\\  & =|x-2|\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe series converges if $L<1.$ In this case, the convergence condition is satisfied for all $-\\infty <x<\\infty .$  Therefore, we conclude that the radius of convergence is $R=\\infty .$"] [:db/cas 17592186548015 :question/worked-solution "To apply the ratio test, we define ${a}_{n}=\\frac{(x+3)^{n}}{{2}^{{n}^{2}}}.$ Then, we calculate the ratio of two successive terms and take the limit as $n\\to \\infty :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{\\frac{(x+3)^{n+1}}{{2}^{(n+1)^{2}}}}{\\frac{(x+3)^{n}}{{2}^{{n}^{2}}}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{(x+3)^{n+1}}{{2}^{(n+1)^{2}}}\\cdot \\frac{{2}^{{n}^{2}}}{(x+3)^{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{x+3}{{2}^{(n+1)^{2}-{n}^{2}}}| \\\\  & =|x+3|{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{2}^{2n+1}} \\\\  & =|x+3|\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe series converges if $L<1.$ In this case, the convergence condition is satisfied for all $-\\infty <x<\\infty .$  Therefore, we conclude that the radius of convergence is $R=\\infty .$" "To apply the ratio test, we define ${a}_{n}=\\frac{(x+3)^{n}}{{2}^{{n}^{2}}}.$ Then, we calculate the ratio of two successive terms and take the limit as $n\\to \\infty :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{\\frac{(x+3)^{n+1}}{{2}^{(n+1)^{2}}}}{\\frac{(x+3)^{n}}{{2}^{{n}^{2}}}}| \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{(x+3)^{n+1}}{{2}^{(n+1)^{2}}}\\cdot \\frac{{2}^{{n}^{2}}}{(x+3)^{n}}| \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{x+3}{{2}^{(n+1)^{2}-{n}^{2}}}| \\\\  & =|x+3|\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{2}^{2n+1}} \\\\  & =|x+3|\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe series converges if $L<1.$ In this case, the convergence condition is satisfied for all $-\\infty <x<\\infty .$  Therefore, we conclude that the radius of convergence is $R=\\infty .$"] [:db/cas 17592186548024 :question/worked-solution "To find the radius of convergence, we apply the ratio test.\n\nSo, let's denote ${a}_{n}=\\frac{(-1)^{n}{x}^{4n+2}}{(2n+1)!}.$ Then, we calculate the ratio of two successive terms and take the limit as $n\\to \\infty :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}{x}^{4n+6}}{(2n+3)!}\\cdot \\frac{(2n+1)!}{(-1)^{n}{x}^{4n+2}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|-\\frac{{x}^{4}}{(2n+2)(2n+3)}| \\\\  & ={x}^{4}{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{1}{(2n+2)(2n+3)}| \\\\  & ={x}^{4}\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe series converges if $L<1.$ In this case, the convergence condition is satisfied for all $-\\infty <x<\\infty .$ Therefore, we conclude that the radius of convergence is $R=\\infty .$" "To find the radius of convergence, we apply the ratio test.\n\nSo, let's denote ${a}_{n}=\\frac{(-1)^{n}{x}^{4n+2}}{(2n+1)!}.$ Then, we calculate the ratio of two successive terms and take the limit as $n\\to \\infty :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}{x}^{4n+6}}{(2n+3)!}\\cdot \\frac{(2n+1)!}{(-1)^{n}{x}^{4n+2}}| \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }|-\\frac{{x}^{4}}{(2n+2)(2n+3)}| \\\\  & ={x}^{4}\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{1}{(2n+2)(2n+3)}| \\\\  & ={x}^{4}\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe series converges if $L<1.$ In this case, the convergence condition is satisfied for all $-\\infty <x<\\infty .$ Therefore, we conclude that the radius of convergence is $R=\\infty .$"] [:db/cas 17592186548040 :question/worked-solution "To apply the ratio test we define $$\n\\displaystyle {a}_{n}=\\frac{{3}^{n}}{n+2}\\,{(x+\\frac{1}{4})}^{n}.\n$$Then, we calculate the ratio of two successive terms and take the limit as $n\\to \\infty :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{3}^{n+1}{(x+\\frac{1}{4})}^{n+1}}{n+3}\\cdot \\frac{n+2}{{3}^{n}{(x+\\frac{1}{4})}^{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{3(n+2)}{n+3}(x+\\frac{1}{4})| \\\\  & =|x+\\frac{1}{4}|{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{3(n+2)}{n+3} \\\\  & =|x+\\frac{1}{4}|\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =3|x+\\frac{1}{4}|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe series converges if $L<1.$ In this case, the convergence condition is satisfied if $|x+\\frac{1}{4}|<\\frac{1}{3}.$ Therefore, we conclude that the radius of convergence is $R=\\frac{1}{3}.$" "To apply the ratio test we define $$\n\\displaystyle {a}_{n}=\\frac{{3}^{n}}{n+2}\\,{(x+\\frac{1}{4})}^{n}.\n$$Then, we calculate the ratio of two successive terms and take the limit as $n\\to \\infty :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{3}^{n+1}{(x+\\frac{1}{4})}^{n+1}}{n+3}\\cdot \\frac{n+2}{{3}^{n}{(x+\\frac{1}{4})}^{n}}| \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{3(n+2)}{n+3}(x+\\frac{1}{4})| \\\\  & =|x+\\frac{1}{4}|\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{3(n+2)}{n+3} \\\\  & =|x+\\frac{1}{4}|\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =3|x+\\frac{1}{4}|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe series converges if $L<1.$ In this case, the convergence condition is satisfied if $|x+\\frac{1}{4}|<\\frac{1}{3}.$ Therefore, we conclude that the radius of convergence is $R=\\frac{1}{3}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186548978 :question/problem "Consider the polar curve $r=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta $ for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).$ Given that \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(\\theta )=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta ),\\,{y}^{′}(\\theta )=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\theta ),\n$$\n\nand ${y}^{′}\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\right)=0,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${x}^{′}\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\right)=0$\n\nII. ${lim}_{\\theta \\to (3\\pi /2)^{-}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\infty $\n\nIII. The curve has a vertical tangent at $\\theta =\\frac{3\\pi }{2}$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the following results.*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\theta )\\to {0}^{-}\\,\\text{as}\\,\\theta \\to {\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\right)}^{-} \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\theta )\\to {0}^{+}\\,\\text{as}\\,\\theta \\to {\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\right)}^{+}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Consider the polar curve $r=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta $ for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).$ Given that \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(\\theta )=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta ),\\,{y}^{′}(\\theta )=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\theta ),\n$$\n\nand ${y}^{′}\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\right)=0,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${x}^{′}\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\right)=0$\n\nII. $\\lim_{\\theta \\to (3\\pi /2)^{-}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\infty $\n\nIII. The curve has a vertical tangent at $\\theta =\\frac{3\\pi }{2}$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the following results.*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\theta )\\to {0}^{-}\\,\\text{as}\\,\\theta \\to {\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\right)}^{-} \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\theta )\\to {0}^{+}\\,\\text{as}\\,\\theta \\to {\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\right)}^{+}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186548978 :question/worked-solution "For a given polar curve, there is a vertical tangent at $\\theta ={\\theta }_{0}$ if\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})}{{x}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})}\n$$\n\nis infinite. This means that\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})\\ne 0\\,\\text{and}\\,{x}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})=0.\n$$\n\nHowever, if the curve is continuous at ${\\theta }_{0}$ yet the derivative takes the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}$ at this point, we say that the curve has a vertical tangent at this point if\n\n$$\n{lim}_{\\theta \\to {\\theta }_{0}^{-}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\pm \\infty \\,\\text{and}\\,{lim}_{\\theta \\to {\\theta }_{0}^{+}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\pm \\infty .\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're told that ${y}^{′}\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\right)=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Substituting $\\theta =\\frac{3\\pi }{2}$ into the expression for ${x}^{′}(\\theta ),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\right) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{3\\pi }{2})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\cdot \\frac{3\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\pi )+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\pi ) \\\\  & =-1+1 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Computing the limit as $\\theta \\to {\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\right)}^{-}$ using L'Hopital's rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to (3\\pi /2)^{-}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )} \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to (3\\pi /2)^{-}}\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\theta )}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta )}\\right) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to (3\\pi /2)^{-}}\\left(\\frac{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\theta ))^{′}}{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta ))^{′}}\\right) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to (3\\pi /2)^{-}}\\left(\\frac{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta )}{-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\theta )}\\right) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to (3\\pi /2)^{-}}\\left(\\frac{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta )}{-2(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\theta ))}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the limit in the numerator, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to (3\\pi /2)^{-}}4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta ) & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\cdot \\frac{3\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\pi ) \\\\  & =4\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the first hint, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to (3\\pi /2)^{-}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to (3\\pi /2)^{-}}\\left(\\frac{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta )}{-2(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\theta )}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{-2\\cdot {0}^{-}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{{0}^{+}} \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Using the calculations from part II, and the second hint, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to (3\\pi /2)^{+}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to (3\\pi /2)^{+}}\\left(\\frac{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta )}{-2(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\theta )}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{-2\\cdot {0}^{+}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{{0}^{-}} \\\\  & =-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the limits of our derivative are both infinite, there is a vertical tangent to the curve at $\\theta =\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.$\n\n![](images/f9/f9a4fc8fa61dac84d3dbc4bc48f86e2b702f7854b1fd1d3b3e5f4303fe7769a7.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" "For a given polar curve, there is a vertical tangent at $\\theta ={\\theta }_{0}$ if\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})}{{x}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})}\n$$\n\nis infinite. This means that\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})\\ne 0\\,\\text{and}\\,{x}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})=0.\n$$\n\nHowever, if the curve is continuous at ${\\theta }_{0}$ yet the derivative takes the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}$ at this point, we say that the curve has a vertical tangent at this point if\n\n$$\n\\lim_{\\theta \\to {\\theta }_{0}^{-}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\pm \\infty \\,\\text{and}\\,\\lim_{\\theta \\to {\\theta }_{0}^{+}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\pm \\infty .\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're told that ${y}^{′}\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\right)=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Substituting $\\theta =\\frac{3\\pi }{2}$ into the expression for ${x}^{′}(\\theta ),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\right) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{3\\pi }{2})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\cdot \\frac{3\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\pi )+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\pi ) \\\\  & =-1+1 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Computing the limit as $\\theta \\to {\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\right)}^{-}$ using L'Hopital's rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\theta \\to (3\\pi /2)^{-}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\lim_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\theta \\to (3\\pi /2)^{-}}\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\theta )}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta )}\\right) \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\theta \\to (3\\pi /2)^{-}}\\left(\\frac{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\theta ))^{′}}{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta ))^{′}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\theta \\to (3\\pi /2)^{-}}\\left(\\frac{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta )}{-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\theta )}\\right) \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\theta \\to (3\\pi /2)^{-}}\\left(\\frac{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta )}{-2(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\theta ))}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the limit in the numerator, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\theta \\to (3\\pi /2)^{-}}4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta ) & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\cdot \\frac{3\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\pi ) \\\\  & =4\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the first hint, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\theta \\to (3\\pi /2)^{-}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\lim_{\\theta \\to (3\\pi /2)^{-}}\\left(\\frac{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta )}{-2(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\theta )}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{-2\\cdot {0}^{-}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{{0}^{+}} \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Using the calculations from part II, and the second hint, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\theta \\to (3\\pi /2)^{+}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\lim_{\\theta \\to (3\\pi /2)^{+}}\\left(\\frac{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta )}{-2(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\theta )}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{-2\\cdot {0}^{+}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{{0}^{-}} \\\\  & =-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the limits of our derivative are both infinite, there is a vertical tangent to the curve at $\\theta =\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.$\n\n![](images/f9/f9a4fc8fa61dac84d3dbc4bc48f86e2b702f7854b1fd1d3b3e5f4303fe7769a7.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""] [:db/cas 17592186548979 :question/worked-solution "For a given polar curve, there is a vertical tangent at $\\theta ={\\theta }_{0}$ if\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})}{{x}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})}\n$$\n\nis infinite. This means that\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})\\ne 0\\,\\text{and}\\,{x}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})=0.\n$$\n\nHowever, if the curve is continuous at ${\\theta }_{0}$ yet the derivative takes the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}$ at this point, we say that the curve has a vertical tangent at this point if\n\n$$\n{lim}_{\\theta \\to {\\theta }_{0}^{-}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\pm \\infty \\,\\text{and}\\,{lim}_{\\theta \\to {\\theta }_{0}^{+}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\pm \\infty .\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're told that ${x}^{′}(0)=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Substituting $\\theta =0$ into the expression for ${y}^{′}(\\theta ),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(0) & ={tan}^{2}⁡(0)\\operatorname{sec}⁡(0)({cos}^{2}⁡(0)+2) \\\\  & =0\\cdot 1\\cdot ({1}^{2}+2) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Computing the limit as $\\theta \\to {0}^{+},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )} \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to {0}^{+}}\\left(\\frac{{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta ({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +2)}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta (\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta )}\\right) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to {0}^{+}}\\left(\\frac{{tan}^{\\cancel{2}}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta ({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +2)}{\\cancel{\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta )}\\right) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to {0}^{+}}\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta ({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +2)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡(0)\\operatorname{sec}⁡(0)({cos}^{2}⁡(0)+2)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)+\\operatorname{sec}⁡(0)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0\\cdot 1\\cdot ({1}^{2}+2)}{1+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{2} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the right limit of our derivative is not infinite, the curve does *not* have a vertical tangent at $\\theta =0.$ In fact, it is a horizontal tangent.\n\n![](images/49/498b937eba18870a36ee6f09bb99cf7dde38eb95c4cc2aa6edcb46080d0fcf04.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "For a given polar curve, there is a vertical tangent at $\\theta ={\\theta }_{0}$ if\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})}{{x}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})}\n$$\n\nis infinite. This means that\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})\\ne 0\\,\\text{and}\\,{x}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})=0.\n$$\n\nHowever, if the curve is continuous at ${\\theta }_{0}$ yet the derivative takes the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}$ at this point, we say that the curve has a vertical tangent at this point if\n\n$$\n\\lim_{\\theta \\to {\\theta }_{0}^{-}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\pm \\infty \\,\\text{and}\\,\\lim_{\\theta \\to {\\theta }_{0}^{+}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\pm \\infty .\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're told that ${x}^{′}(0)=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Substituting $\\theta =0$ into the expression for ${y}^{′}(\\theta ),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(0) & ={tan}^{2}⁡(0)\\operatorname{sec}⁡(0)({cos}^{2}⁡(0)+2) \\\\  & =0\\cdot 1\\cdo