rac{0}{0}$ at this point, we say that the curve has a horizontal tangent at this point if\n\n$$\n{lim}_{\\theta \\to {\\theta }_{0}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=0.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're told that ${x}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Substituting $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ into the expression for ${y}^{′}(\\theta ),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)({cos}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)-3{sin}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Computing the limit as $\\theta \\to \\frac{\\pi }{2},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi /2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi /2}\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )} \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi /2}\\left(\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta ({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -3{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}{-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {cos}^{3}⁡\\theta }\\right) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi /2}\\left(\\frac{\\cancel{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -3{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}{-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {cos}^{\\cancel{3}}⁡\\theta }\\right) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi /2}\\left(\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -3{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)-3{sin}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)}{-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-3}{0} \\\\  & \\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the limit in part II does *not* equal to $0,$ there curve does *not* have a horizontal tangent at $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ In fact, it is a vertical tangent.\n\n![](images/8b/8b68110c25bfa464c0971a396ec50908c0c0622cbdc92bf42239b984695655ca.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer 