torname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-3}{0} \\\\  & \\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the limit in part II does *not* equal to $0,$ there curve does *not* have a horizontal tangent at $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ In fact, it is a vertical tangent.\n\n![](images/8b/8b68110c25bfa464c0971a396ec50908c0c0622cbdc92bf42239b984695655ca.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""] [:db/cas 17592186549007 :question/worked-solution "When a polar curve is differentiable at a point ${\\theta }_{0}$, there is a horizontal tangent at ${\\theta }_{0}$ if\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})}{{x}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})}=0\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{x}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nHowever, if the curve is continuous at ${\\theta }_{0}$ yet the derivative ${y}^{′}(x)$ takes the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}$ at this point, we say that the curve has a horizontal tangent at this point if\n\n$$\n{lim}_{\\theta \\to {\\theta }_{0}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=0.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're told that ${x}^{′}(\\pi )=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Substituting $\\theta =\\pi $ into the expression for ${y}^{′}(\\theta ),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\pi ) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\pi ) \\\\  & =-1+1 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Computing the limit using L'Hopital's rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi }\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi }\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )} \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi }\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )}\\right) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi }\\left(\\frac{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ))^{′}}{(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ))^{′}}\\right) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi }\\left(\\frac{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )}{-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\pi )}{-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi -2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi )} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{-1} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since the limit in part II equals $0,$ there is a horizontal tangent at $\\theta =0.$\n\n![](images/d1/d1308067e18807f159aee95433ea645be71abd153aa03888ef8092cf6fe226bd.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" "When a polar curve is differentiable at a point ${\\theta }_{0}$, there is a horizontal tangent at ${\\theta }_{0}$ if\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})}{{x}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})}=0\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{x}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nHowever, if the curve is continuous at ${\\theta }_{0}$ yet the derivative ${y}^{′}(x)$ takes the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}$ at this point, we say that the curve has a horizontal tangent at this point if\n\n$$\n\\lim_{\\theta \\to {\\theta }_{0}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=0.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're told that ${x}^{′}(\\pi )=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Substituting $\\theta =\\pi $ into the expression for ${y}^{′}(\\theta ),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\pi ) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\pi ) \\\\  & =-1+1 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Computing the limit using L'Hopital's rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\theta \\to \\pi }\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\lim_{\\theta \\to \\pi }\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\theta \\to \\pi }\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )}\\right) \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\theta \\to \\pi }\\left(\\frac{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ))^{′}}{(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ))^{′}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\theta \\to \\pi }\\left(\\frac{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )}{-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\pi )}{-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi -2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi )} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{-1} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since the limit in part II equals $0,$ there is a horizontal tangent at $\\theta =0.$\n\n![](images/d1/d1308067e18807f159aee95433ea645be71abd153aa03888ef8092cf6fe226bd.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""] [:db/cas 17592186549008 :question/worked-solution "When a polar curve is differentiable at a point ${\\theta }_{0}$, there is a horizontal tangent at ${\\theta }_{0}$ if\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})}{{x}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})}=0\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{x}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nHowever, if the curve is continuous at ${\\theta }_{0}$ yet the derivative ${y}^{′}(x)$ takes the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}$ at this point, we say that the curve has a horizontal tangent at this point if\n\n$$\n{lim}_{\\theta \\to {\\theta }_{0}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=0.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're told that ${x}^{′}(0)=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Substituting $\\theta =0$ into the expression for ${y}^{′}(\\theta ),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(0) & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot 0)-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0) \\\\  & =3-3 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Computing the limit using L'Hopital's rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )} \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\left(\\frac{3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )}{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )}\\right) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\left(\\frac{(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta ))^{′}}{(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ))^{′}}\\right) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\left(\\frac{-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot 0)-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)}{3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot 0)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{-3} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, there is a horizontal tangent at $\\theta =0.$\n\n![](images/9e/9ef9840644e37fbb660ede7d574d8814b9ce7ce3bd0ff3790b5c5d21420f3e70.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" "When a polar curve is differentiable at a point ${\\theta }_{0}$, there is a horizontal tangent at ${\\theta }_{0}$ if\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})}{{x}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})}=0\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{x}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nHowever, if the curve is continuous at ${\\theta }_{0}$ yet the derivative ${y}^{′}(x)$ takes the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}$ at this point, we say that the curve has a horizontal tangent at this point if\n\n$$\n\\lim_{\\theta \\to {\\theta }_{0}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=0.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're told that ${x}^{′}(0)=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Substituting $\\theta =0$ into the expression for ${y}^{′}(\\theta ),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(0) & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot 0)-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0) \\\\  & =3-3 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Computing the limit using L'Hopital's rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\left(\\frac{3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )}{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )}\\right) \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\left(\\frac{(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta ))^{′}}{(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ))^{′}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\left(\\frac{-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot 0)-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)}{3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot 0)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{-3} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, there is a horizontal tangent at $\\theta =0.$\n\n![](images/9e/9ef9840644e37fbb660ede7d574d8814b9ce7ce3bd0ff3790b5c5d21420f3e70.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""] [:db/cas 17592186549021 :question/worked-solution "When a polar curve is differentiable at a point ${\\theta }_{0}$, there is a horizontal tangent at ${\\theta }_{0}$ if\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})}{{x}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})}=0\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{x}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nHowever, if the curve is continuous at ${\\theta }_{0}$ yet the derivative ${y}^{′}(x)$ takes the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}$ at this point, we say that the curve has a horizontal tangent at this point if\n\n$$\n{lim}_{\\theta \\to {\\theta }_{0}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=0.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're told that ${y}^{′}(0)=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Substituting $\\theta =0$ into the expression for ${x}^{′}(\\theta ),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(0) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot 0)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot 0) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Computing the limit, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )} \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\left(\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta (3{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}\\right) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\left(\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta (3{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}\\right) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\left(\\frac{{sin}^{\\cancel{2}}⁡\\theta (3{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}{2\\cancel{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}\\right) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (3{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)(3{cos}^{2}⁡(0)-{sin}^{2}⁡(0))}{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot 0)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0\\cdot (3\\cdot 1-0)}{2\\cdot 1\\cdot 1} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{2} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since the limit in part II equals $0,$ there curve has a horizontal tangent at $\\theta =0.$\n\n![](images/a4/a48593a3bbcc61afaf80de619be505cb6dac58c559e03a0eab4553bd5a1e5013.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" "When a polar curve is differentiable at a point ${\\theta }_{0}$, there is a horizontal tangent at ${\\theta }_{0}$ if\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})}{{x}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})}=0\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{x}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nHowever, if the curve is continuous at ${\\theta }_{0}$ yet the derivative ${y}^{′}(x)$ takes the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}$ at this point, we say that the curve has a horizontal tangent at this point if\n\n$$\n\\lim_{\\theta \\to {\\theta }_{0}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=0.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're told that ${y}^{′}(0)=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Substituting $\\theta =0$ into the expression for ${x}^{′}(\\theta ),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(0) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot 0)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot 0) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Computing the limit, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\left(\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta (3{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}\\right) \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\left(\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta (3{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}\\right) \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\left(\\frac{{sin}^{\\cancel{2}}⁡\\theta (3{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}{2\\cancel{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}\\right) \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (3{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)(3{cos}^{2}⁡(0)-{sin}^{2}⁡(0))}{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot 0)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0\\cdot (3\\cdot 1-0)}{2\\cdot 1\\cdot 1} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{2} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since the limit in part II equals $0,$ there curve has a horizontal tangent at $\\theta =0.$\n\n![](images/a4/a48593a3bbcc61afaf80de619be505cb6dac58c559e03a0eab4553bd5a1e5013.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""] [:db/cas 17592186549022 :question/worked-solution "We find the area of the region bounded by the graph of the curve and the $x$-axis by calculating the integral\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }5{e}^{-|x|+1}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe break up the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }5{e}^{-|x|+1}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}5{e}^{-|x|+1}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }5{e}^{-|x|+1}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nEvaluating the first of these integrals, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}5{e}^{-|x|+1}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}5{e}^{x+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}5{e}^{x+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =5{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }({e}^{x+1}{|}_{a}^{0}\\,) \\\\  & =5{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }({e}^{0+1}-{e}^{a+1}) \\\\  & =5(e-0) \\\\  & =5e.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the integrand $5{e}^{-|x|+1}$ is an **even** function. This means that we can immediately deduce that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }5{e}^{-|x|+1}\\,\\text{d}x=5e.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since both improper integrals exist, we conclude that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }5{e}^{-|x|+1}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}5{e}^{-|x|+1}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }5{e}^{-|x|+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =5e+5e \\\\  & =10e.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We find the area of the region bounded by the graph of the curve and the $x$-axis by calculating the integral\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }5{e}^{-|x|+1}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe break up the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }5{e}^{-|x|+1}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}5{e}^{-|x|+1}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }5{e}^{-|x|+1}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nEvaluating the first of these integrals, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}5{e}^{-|x|+1}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}5{e}^{x+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}5{e}^{x+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =5\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }({e}^{x+1}{|}_{a}^{0}\\,) \\\\  & =5\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }({e}^{0+1}-{e}^{a+1}) \\\\  & =5(e-0) \\\\  & =5e.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the integrand $5{e}^{-|x|+1}$ is an **even** function. This means that we can immediately deduce that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }5{e}^{-|x|+1}\\,\\text{d}x=5e.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since both improper integrals exist, we conclude that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }5{e}^{-|x|+1}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}5{e}^{-|x|+1}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }5{e}^{-|x|+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =5e+5e \\\\  & =10e.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186549025 :question/worked-solution "For a given polar curve, there is a vertical tangent at $\\theta ={\\theta }_{0}$ if\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})}{{x}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})}\n$$\n\nis infinite. This means that\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})\\ne 0\\,\\text{and}\\,{x}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})=0.\n$$\n\nHowever, if the curve is continuous at ${\\theta }_{0}$ yet the derivative takes the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}$ at this point, we say that the curve has a vertical tangent at this point if\n\n$$\n{lim}_{\\theta \\to {\\theta }_{0}^{-}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\pm \\infty \\,\\text{and}\\,{lim}_{\\theta \\to {\\theta }_{0}^{+}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\pm \\infty .\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're told that ${y}^{′}(0)=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Substituting $\\theta =0$ into the expression for ${x}^{′}(\\theta ),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(0) & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot 0)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\cdot 0) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Computing the limit as $\\theta \\to {0}^{-},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to {0}^{-}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to {0}^{-}}\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )} \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to {0}^{-}}\\left(\\frac{16{sin}^{3}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta (3{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -1)}{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\theta )}\\right) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to {0}^{-}}\\left(\\frac{16{sin}^{3}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta (3{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -1)}{6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\theta )}\\right) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to {0}^{-}}\\left(\\frac{16{sin}^{3}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta (3{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -1)}{12\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}\\right) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to {0}^{-}}\\left(\\frac{16{sin}^{3}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta (3{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -1)}{24{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}\\right) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to {0}^{-}}\\left(\\frac{16{sin}^{\\cancel{3}}⁡\\theta \\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }(3{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -1)}{24\\cancel{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{cos}^{\\cancel{2}}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}\\right) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to {0}^{-}}\\left(\\frac{16\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (3{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -1)}{24\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{16\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)(3{cos}^{2}⁡(0)-1)}{24\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot 0)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{24} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the right limit of our derivative is not infinite, the curve does *not* have a vertical tangent at $\\theta =0.$ In fact, it is a horizontal tangent.\n\n![](images/4a/4a525b9a7a04f228bb45dc8c0c1cb916d1e98d8907e5fb1411ea52eb964c96ef.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "For a given polar curve, there is a vertical tangent at $\\theta ={\\theta }_{0}$ if\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})}{{x}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})}\n$$\n\nis infinite. This means that\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})\\ne 0\\,\\text{and}\\,{x}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})=0.\n$$\n\nHowever, if the curve is continuous at ${\\theta }_{0}$ yet the derivative takes the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}$ at this point, we say that the curve has a vertical tangent at this point if\n\n$$\n\\lim_{\\theta \\to {\\theta }_{0}^{-}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\pm \\infty \\,\\text{and}\\,\\lim_{\\theta \\to {\\theta }_{0}^{+}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\pm \\infty .\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're told that ${y}^{′}(0)=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Substituting $\\theta =0$ into the expression for ${x}^{′}(\\theta ),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(0) & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot 0)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\cdot 0) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Computing the limit as $\\theta \\to {0}^{-},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\theta \\to {0}^{-}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\lim_{\\theta \\to {0}^{-}}\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\theta \\to {0}^{-}}\\left(\\frac{16{sin}^{3}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta (3{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -1)}{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\theta )}\\right) \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\theta \\to {0}^{-}}\\left(\\frac{16{sin}^{3}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta (3{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -1)}{6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\theta )}\\right) \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\theta \\to {0}^{-}}\\left(\\frac{16{sin}^{3}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta (3{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -1)}{12\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}\\right) \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\theta \\to {0}^{-}}\\left(\\frac{16{sin}^{3}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta (3{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -1)}{24{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}\\right) \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\theta \\to {0}^{-}}\\left(\\frac{16{sin}^{\\cancel{3}}⁡\\theta \\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }(3{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -1)}{24\\cancel{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{cos}^{\\cancel{2}}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}\\right) \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\theta \\to {0}^{-}}\\left(\\frac{16\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (3{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -1)}{24\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{16\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)(3{cos}^{2}⁡(0)-1)}{24\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot 0)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{24} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the right limit of our derivative is not infinite, the curve does *not* have a vertical tangent at $\\theta =0.$ In fact, it is a horizontal tangent.\n\n![](images/4a/4a525b9a7a04f228bb45dc8c0c1cb916d1e98d8907e5fb1411ea52eb964c96ef.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""] [:db/cas 17592186549031 :question/problem "Consider the polar curve $r=1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).$ Given that \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(\\theta )=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta ),\\,{y}^{′}(\\theta )=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ),\n$$\n\nand ${y}^{′}\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\right)=0,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${x}^{′}\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\right)=0$\n\nII. ${lim}_{\\theta \\to (3\\pi /2)^{-}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\infty $\n\nIII. The curve has a vertical tangent at $\\theta =\\frac{3\\pi }{2}$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the following results.*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )\\to {0}^{-}\\,\\text{as}\\,\\theta \\to {\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\right)}^{-} \\\\ -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )\\to {0}^{+}\\,\\text{as}\\,\\theta \\to {\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\right)}^{+}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Consider the polar curve $r=1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).$ Given that \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(\\theta )=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta ),\\,{y}^{′}(\\theta )=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ),\n$$\n\nand ${y}^{′}\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\right)=0,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${x}^{′}\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\right)=0$\n\nII. $\\lim_{\\theta \\to (3\\pi /2)^{-}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\infty $\n\nIII. The curve has a vertical tangent at $\\theta =\\frac{3\\pi }{2}$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the following results.*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )\\to {0}^{-}\\,\\text{as}\\,\\theta \\to {\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\right)}^{-} \\\\ -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )\\to {0}^{+}\\,\\text{as}\\,\\theta \\to {\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\right)}^{+}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186549031 :question/worked-solution "For a given polar curve, there is a vertical tangent at $\\theta ={\\theta }_{0}$ if\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})}{{x}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})}\n$$\n\nis infinite. This means that\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})\\ne 0\\,\\text{and}\\,{x}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})=0.\n$$\n\nHowever, if the curve is continuous at ${\\theta }_{0}$ yet the derivative takes the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}$ at this point, we say that the curve has a vertical tangent at this point if\n\n$$\n{lim}_{\\theta \\to {\\theta }_{0}^{-}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\pm \\infty \\,\\text{and}\\,{lim}_{\\theta \\to {\\theta }_{0}^{+}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\pm \\infty .\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're told that ${y}^{′}\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\right)=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Substituting $\\theta =\\frac{3\\pi }{2}$ into the expression for ${x}^{′}(\\theta ),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\right) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{3\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-1-(-1) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Computing the limit as $\\theta \\to {\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\right)}^{-}$ using L'Hopital's rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to (3\\pi /2)^{-}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to (3\\pi /2)^{-}}\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )} \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to (3\\pi /2)^{-}}\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )}\\right) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to (3\\pi /2)^{-}}\\left(\\frac{{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ))}^{′}}{{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta ))}^{′}}\\right) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to (3\\pi /2)^{-}}\\left(\\frac{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}{-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the limit in the numerator, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to (3\\pi /2)^{-}}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\right)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{3\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\right)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\pi ) \\\\  & =-(-1)+2\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =1-2 \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the first hint, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to (3\\pi /2)^{-}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to (3\\pi /2)^{-}}\\left(\\frac{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}{-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{-1}{{0}^{-}} \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Using the calculations from part II, and the second hint, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to (3\\pi /2)^{+}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to (3\\pi /2)^{+}}\\left(\\frac{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}{-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{-1}{{0}^{+}} \\\\  & =-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the limits of our derivative are both infinite, there is a vertical tangent to the curve at $\\theta =\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.$\n\n![](images/f4/f4462c7c41e7d6d5d10acec7627ac54f73477569356e22857659f5110847302d.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" "For a given polar curve, there is a vertical tangent at $\\theta ={\\theta }_{0}$ if\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})}{{x}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})}\n$$\n\nis infinite. This means that\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})\\ne 0\\,\\text{and}\\,{x}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})=0.\n$$\n\nHowever, if the curve is continuous at ${\\theta }_{0}$ yet the derivative takes the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}$ at this point, we say that the curve has a vertical tangent at this point if\n\n$$\n\\lim_{\\theta \\to {\\theta }_{0}^{-}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\pm \\infty \\,\\text{and}\\,\\lim_{\\theta \\to {\\theta }_{0}^{+}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\pm \\infty .\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're told that ${y}^{′}\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\right)=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Substituting $\\theta =\\frac{3\\pi }{2}$ into the expression for ${x}^{′}(\\theta ),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\right) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{3\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-1-(-1) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Computing the limit as $\\theta \\to {\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\right)}^{-}$ using L'Hopital's rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\theta \\to (3\\pi /2)^{-}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\lim_{\\theta \\to (3\\pi /2)^{-}}\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\theta \\to (3\\pi /2)^{-}}\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )}\\right) \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\theta \\to (3\\pi /2)^{-}}\\left(\\frac{{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ))}^{′}}{{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta ))}^{′}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\theta \\to (3\\pi /2)^{-}}\\left(\\frac{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}{-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the limit in the numerator, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\theta \\to (3\\pi /2)^{-}}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\right)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{3\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\right)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\pi ) \\\\  & =-(-1)+2\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =1-2 \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the first hint, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\theta \\to (3\\pi /2)^{-}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\lim_{\\theta \\to (3\\pi /2)^{-}}\\left(\\frac{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}{-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{-1}{{0}^{-}} \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Using the calculations from part II, and the second hint, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\theta \\to (3\\pi /2)^{+}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\lim_{\\theta \\to (3\\pi /2)^{+}}\\left(\\frac{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}{-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{-1}{{0}^{+}} \\\\  & =-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the limits of our derivative are both infinite, there is a vertical tangent to the curve at $\\theta =\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.$\n\n![](images/f4/f4462c7c41e7d6d5d10acec7627ac54f73477569356e22857659f5110847302d.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""] [:db/cas 17592186549058 :question/worked-solution "We find the area of the region bounded by the graph of the curve and the $x$-axis by calculating the integral\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }|x{|}^{3}{e}^{-{x}^{4}}.\n$$\n\nWe break up the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }|x{|}^{3}{e}^{-{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}|x{|}^{3}{e}^{-{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }|x{|}^{3}{e}^{-{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nWe start by calculating the first of these integrals:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}|x{|}^{3}{e}^{-{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x=-{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}{x}^{3}{e}^{-{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nWe calculate the antiderivative using a substitution. Let $u=-{x}^{4}$,  then $\\text{d}u=-4{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Hence, the antiderivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {x}^{3}{e}^{-{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\int {e}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{u}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{-{x}^{4}}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}|x{|}^{3}{e}^{-{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x & =-{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}{x}^{3}{e}^{-{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}{x}^{3}{e}^{-{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{(-\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{-{x}^{4}})|}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }({e}^{0}-{e}^{-{a}^{4}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the integrand $|x{|}^{3}{e}^{-{x}^{4}}$ is an **even** function. This means that we can immediately deduce that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }|x{|}^{3}{e}^{-{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since both improper integrals exist, we conclude that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }|x{|}^{3}{e}^{-{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}|x{|}^{3}{e}^{-{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }|x{|}^{3}{e}^{-{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We find the area of the region bounded by the graph of the curve and the $x$-axis by calculating the integral\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }|x{|}^{3}{e}^{-{x}^{4}}.\n$$\n\nWe break up the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }|x{|}^{3}{e}^{-{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}|x{|}^{3}{e}^{-{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }|x{|}^{3}{e}^{-{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nWe start by calculating the first of these integrals:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}|x{|}^{3}{e}^{-{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x=-{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}{x}^{3}{e}^{-{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nWe calculate the antiderivative using a substitution. Let $u=-{x}^{4}$,  then $\\text{d}u=-4{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Hence, the antiderivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {x}^{3}{e}^{-{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\int {e}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{u}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{-{x}^{4}}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}|x{|}^{3}{e}^{-{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x & =-{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}{x}^{3}{e}^{-{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}{x}^{3}{e}^{-{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{(-\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{-{x}^{4}})|}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }({e}^{0}-{e}^{-{a}^{4}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the integrand $|x{|}^{3}{e}^{-{x}^{4}}$ is an **even** function. This means that we can immediately deduce that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }|x{|}^{3}{e}^{-{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since both improper integrals exist, we conclude that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }|x{|}^{3}{e}^{-{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}|x{|}^{3}{e}^{-{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }|x{|}^{3}{e}^{-{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186551285 :question/problem "$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}-5x, & x\\in [-2,0) \\\\ \\frac{5}{2}{x}^{2}, & x\\in [0,2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above function $F(x)$ is defined on $x\\in [-2,2).$ Let $f(x)$ be the $4$-periodic extension of $F(x)$ to $x\\in R.$\nSelect the correct options below.\n\n| I. | $F(x)$ is continuous on $x\\in (-2,2)$ | {{field-1}} |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| II. | $F(x)$ is piecewise smooth on $x\\in (-2,2)$ | {{field-2}} |\n| III. | ${lim}_{x\\to -{2}^{+}}F(x)={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}F(x)$ | {{field-3}} |\n| IV. | $f(x)$ is piecewise smooth and continuous on $R$ | {{field-4}} |" "$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}-5x, & x\\in [-2,0) \\\\ \\frac{5}{2}{x}^{2}, & x\\in [0,2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above function $F(x)$ is defined on $x\\in [-2,2).$ Let $f(x)$ be the $4$-periodic extension of $F(x)$ to $x\\in R.$\nSelect the correct options below.\n\n| I. | $F(x)$ is continuous on $x\\in (-2,2)$ | {{field-1}} |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| II. | $F(x)$ is piecewise smooth on $x\\in (-2,2)$ | {{field-2}} |\n| III. | $\\lim_{x\\to -{2}^{+}}F(x)=\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{-}}F(x)$ | {{field-3}} |\n| IV. | $f(x)$ is piecewise smooth and continuous on $R$ | {{field-4}} |"] [:db/cas 17592186551347 :question/problem "$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}-2x+1, & x\\in [-3,0) \\\\ {x}^{2}+1, & x\\in [0,3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above function $F(x)$ is defined on $x\\in [-3,3).$ Let $f(x)$ be the $6$-periodic extension of $F(x)$ to $x\\in R.$\nSelect the correct options below.\n\n| I. | $F(x)$ is continuous on $x\\in (-3,3)$ | {{field-1}} |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| II. | $F(x)$ is piecewise smooth on $x\\in (-3,3)$ | {{field-2}} |\n| III. | ${lim}_{x\\to -{3}^{+}}F(x)={lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}F(x)$ | {{field-3}} |\n| IV. | $f(x)$ is piecewise smooth and continuous on $R$ | {{field-4}} |" "$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}-2x+1, & x\\in [-3,0) \\\\ {x}^{2}+1, & x\\in [0,3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above function $F(x)$ is defined on $x\\in [-3,3).$ Let $f(x)$ be the $6$-periodic extension of $F(x)$ to $x\\in R.$\nSelect the correct options below.\n\n| I. | $F(x)$ is continuous on $x\\in (-3,3)$ | {{field-1}} |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| II. | $F(x)$ is piecewise smooth on $x\\in (-3,3)$ | {{field-2}} |\n| III. | $\\lim_{x\\to -{3}^{+}}F(x)=\\lim_{x\\to {3}^{-}}F(x)$ | {{field-3}} |\n| IV. | $f(x)$ is piecewise smooth and continuous on $R$ | {{field-4}} |"] [:db/cas 17592186551357 :question/worked-solution "The definite integral can be defined in terms of the limit of a left Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\n    \n\nwhere $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ and ${x}_{i}=a+iΔx.$\n\nIn the definite integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{2}^{4}(x+1)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nthe function is $f(x)=(x+1)^{2},$ the lower bound is $a=2,$ and the upper bound is $b=4.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{4-2}{n}=\\frac{2}{n},\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{4}(x+1)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[f(2+i\\cdot \\frac{2}{n})\\,\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[f(2+\\frac{2i}{n})\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[{(2+\\frac{2i}{n}+1)}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[{(3+\\frac{2i}{n})}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The definite integral can be defined in terms of the limit of a left Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\n    \n\nwhere $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ and ${x}_{i}=a+iΔx.$\n\nIn the definite integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{2}^{4}(x+1)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nthe function is $f(x)=(x+1)^{2},$ the lower bound is $a=2,$ and the upper bound is $b=4.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{4-2}{n}=\\frac{2}{n},\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{4}(x+1)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[f(2+i\\cdot \\frac{2}{n})\\,\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}] \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[f(2+\\frac{2i}{n})\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}] \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[{(2+\\frac{2i}{n}+1)}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}] \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[{(3+\\frac{2i}{n})}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186552420 :question/worked-solution "Applying the quotient rule for limits, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -5}\\frac{4x}{x+1} & =\\frac{{lim}_{x\\to -5}4x}{{lim}_{x\\to -5}(x+1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{4(-5)}{-5+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{-20}{-4} \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Applying the quotient rule for limits, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to -5}\\frac{4x}{x+1} & =\\frac{\\lim_{x\\to -5}4x}{\\lim_{x\\to -5}(x+1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{4(-5)}{-5+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{-20}{-4} \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186552439 :question/worked-solution "According to the sum and constant multiple rules, we can compute the required limit as follows:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)}(5f(x)+6g(x)+3{x}^{3})=5{lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)}f(x)+6{lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)}g(x)+3{lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)}{x}^{3}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe above equation assumes that ${lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)}f(x)$ and ${lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)}g(x)$ both exist.\n\nFrom the graph, we see ${lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)}f(x)=0,$ and from part 3, we found ${lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)}g(x)=1.$ So, we have  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)}(5f(x)+6g(x)+3{x}^{3}) & =5{lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)}f(x)+6{lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)}g(x)+3{lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)}{x}^{3} \\\\  & =5\\cdot 0+6\\cdot 1+3\\cdot (-1)^{3} \\\\  & =0+6-3 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "According to the sum and constant multiple rules, we can compute the required limit as follows:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,(-1)}(5f(x)+6g(x)+3{x}^{3})=5\\lim_{x\\to \\,(-1)}f(x)+6\\lim_{x\\to \\,(-1)}g(x)+3\\lim_{x\\to \\,(-1)}{x}^{3}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe above equation assumes that $\\lim_{x\\to \\,(-1)}f(x)$ and $\\lim_{x\\to \\,(-1)}g(x)$ both exist.\n\nFrom the graph, we see $\\lim_{x\\to \\,(-1)}f(x)=0,$ and from part 3, we found $\\lim_{x\\to \\,(-1)}g(x)=1.$ So, we have  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\,(-1)}(5f(x)+6g(x)+3{x}^{3}) & =5\\lim_{x\\to \\,(-1)}f(x)+6\\lim_{x\\to \\,(-1)}g(x)+3\\lim_{x\\to \\,(-1)}{x}^{3} \\\\  & =5\\cdot 0+6\\cdot 1+3\\cdot (-1)^{3} \\\\  & =0+6-3 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186552447 :question/worked-solution "From the graph, we see ${lim}_{x\\to \\,1}f(x)=-1.$\n\nWe factor the constant out of the limit and evaluate the remaining limit, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 1}Cf(x) & =8 \\\\ C\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to 1}f(x) & =8 \\\\ C\\cdot (-1) & =8 \\\\ C & =-8\\end{aligned}\n$$" "From the graph, we see $\\lim_{x\\to \\,1}f(x)=-1.$\n\nWe factor the constant out of the limit and evaluate the remaining limit, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 1}Cf(x) & =8 \\\\ C\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to 1}f(x) & =8 \\\\ C\\cdot (-1) & =8 \\\\ C & =-8\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186552511 :question/worked-solution "Applying the quotient rule for limits, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -1}\\frac{{x}^{2}-4}{1-{x}^{3}} & =\\frac{{lim}_{x\\to -1}({x}^{2}-4)}{{lim}_{x\\to -1}(1-{x}^{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-1)^{2}-4}{1-(-1)^{3}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Applying the quotient rule for limits, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to -1}\\frac{{x}^{2}-4}{1-{x}^{3}} & =\\frac{\\lim_{x\\to -1}({x}^{2}-4)}{\\lim_{x\\to -1}(1-{x}^{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-1)^{2}-4}{1-(-1)^{3}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186555764 :question/worked-solution "If $X\\in (a,b)$ is a continuous random variable with probability density function ${f}_{X}$ and $Y=u(X),$ where the function $u(x)$ is strictly monotonic, then according to the change-of-variables method, the probability density function of $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}({u}^{-1}(y))\\cdot |({u}^{-1})^{′}(y)|, & y\\text{ is between }u(a)\\text{ and }u(b) \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $Y=2X.$ The transformation $u(x)=2x$ is strictly monotonically *increasing*, so the change-of-variables method can be used.\n\nFirst, we find ${u}^{-1}(y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(y) & =2y \\\\ y & =2{u}^{-1}(y) \\\\ {u}^{-1}(y) & =\\frac{y}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we find $({u}^{-1})^{′}(y):$\n\n$$\n({u}^{-1})^{′}(y)=\\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nAccording to the change-of-variables method, for nonzero ${f}_{Y},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={f}_{X}({u}^{-1}(y))\\cdot |({u}^{-1})^{′}(y)| \\\\  & ={f}_{X}\\left(\\frac{y}{2}\\right)\\cdot |\\frac{1}{2}| \\\\  & ={e}^{1-y/2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{1-y/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${f}_{X}$ is nonzero on $x\\ge 1,$ the bounds are $a=1$ and $b\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\nu(1)=2,\\,{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }u(x)=\\infty .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability distribution of $Y$ is\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{1-y/2}, & y\\ge 2 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "If $X\\in (a,b)$ is a continuous random variable with probability density function ${f}_{X}$ and $Y=u(X),$ where the function $u(x)$ is strictly monotonic, then according to the change-of-variables method, the probability density function of $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}({u}^{-1}(y))\\cdot |({u}^{-1})^{′}(y)|, & y\\text{ is between }u(a)\\text{ and }u(b) \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $Y=2X.$ The transformation $u(x)=2x$ is strictly monotonically *increasing*, so the change-of-variables method can be used.\n\nFirst, we find ${u}^{-1}(y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(y) & =2y \\\\ y & =2{u}^{-1}(y) \\\\ {u}^{-1}(y) & =\\frac{y}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we find $({u}^{-1})^{′}(y):$\n\n$$\n({u}^{-1})^{′}(y)=\\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nAccording to the change-of-variables method, for nonzero ${f}_{Y},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={f}_{X}({u}^{-1}(y))\\cdot |({u}^{-1})^{′}(y)| \\\\  & ={f}_{X}\\left(\\frac{y}{2}\\right)\\cdot |\\frac{1}{2}| \\\\  & ={e}^{1-y/2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{1-y/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${f}_{X}$ is nonzero on $x\\ge 1,$ the bounds are $a=1$ and $b\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\nu(1)=2,\\,\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }u(x)=\\infty .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability distribution of $Y$ is\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{1-y/2}, & y\\ge 2 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186558489 :question/worked-solution "The derivative ${f}^{′}(x)$ is given by the limit \n\n$$\n{lim}_{h\\to 0}\\left(\\frac{f(x+h)-f(x)}{h}\\right).\n$$\n\nSince $f(x)=-7{x}^{3}$,  we have that $f(x+h)=-7(x+h)^{3}.$ Hence,  we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\left(\\frac{-7(x+h)^{3}-(-7{x}^{3})}{h}\\right) \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\left(\\frac{-7(x+h)^{3}+7{x}^{3}}{h}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The derivative ${f}^{′}(x)$ is given by the limit \n\n$$\n\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\left(\\frac{f(x+h)-f(x)}{h}\\right).\n$$\n\nSince $f(x)=-7{x}^{3}$,  we have that $f(x+h)=-7(x+h)^{3}.$ Hence,  we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\left(\\frac{-7(x+h)^{3}-(-7{x}^{3})}{h}\\right) \\\\  & =\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\left(\\frac{-7(x+h)^{3}+7{x}^{3}}{h}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186558508 :question/worked-solution "The definition of ${f}^{′}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x)={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{f(x+h)-f(x)}{h}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying this to $f(x)=-5{x}^{2}+2$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\left(\\frac{-5(x+h)^{2}+2-(-5{x}^{2}+2)}{h}\\right) \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\left(\\frac{-5({x}^{2}+2xh+{h}^{2})+2+5{x}^{2}-2)}{h}\\right) \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\left(\\frac{-5{x}^{2}-10xh-5{h}^{2}+2+5{x}^{2}-2}{h}\\right) \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\left(\\frac{-10xh-5{h}^{2}}{h}\\right) \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{h(-10x-5h)}{h} \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}(-10x-5h) \\\\  & =-10x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The definition of ${f}^{′}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x)=\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\frac{f(x+h)-f(x)}{h}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying this to $f(x)=-5{x}^{2}+2$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\left(\\frac{-5(x+h)^{2}+2-(-5{x}^{2}+2)}{h}\\right) \\\\  & =\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\left(\\frac{-5({x}^{2}+2xh+{h}^{2})+2+5{x}^{2}-2)}{h}\\right) \\\\  & =\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\left(\\frac{-5{x}^{2}-10xh-5{h}^{2}+2+5{x}^{2}-2}{h}\\right) \\\\  & =\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\left(\\frac{-10xh-5{h}^{2}}{h}\\right) \\\\  & =\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\frac{h(-10x-5h)}{h} \\\\  & =\\lim_{h\\to 0}(-10x-5h) \\\\  & =-10x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186562447 :question/worked-solution "First, we divide the two functions, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{p(x)}{q(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(-2{x}^{4})}{\\left(-\\frac{8}{{x}^{6}}\\right)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{x}^{10}}{4} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {x}^{10}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{x}^{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe graph $y={x}^{10}$ looks similar to an upward parabola, so\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{x}^{10}=\\infty .\n$$\n\nMultiplication by the positive constant $\\frac{1}{4}$ does not affect the limit at infinity So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{x}^{10}={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }({x}^{10})=\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we divide the two functions, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{p(x)}{q(x)} & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(-2{x}^{4})}{\\left(-\\frac{8}{{x}^{6}}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{x}^{10}}{4} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {x}^{10}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{x}^{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe graph $y={x}^{10}$ looks similar to an upward parabola, so\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }{x}^{10}=\\infty .\n$$\n\nMultiplication by the positive constant $\\frac{1}{4}$ does not affect the limit at infinity So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{x}^{10}=\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }({x}^{10})=\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186562466 :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the powers and multiply the two functions, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }({p}^{3}(x)\\cdot {q}^{2}(x)) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }({(p(x))}^{3}\\cdot {(q(x))}^{2}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }({(-3{x}^{3})}^{3}\\cdot {(-3{x}^{3})}^{2}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(-27{x}^{9}\\cdot 9{x}^{6}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }((-27\\cdot 9){x}^{9+6}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(-243\\cdot {x}^{15}) \\\\  & =-243\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{x}^{15}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe graph $y={x}^{15}$ is power function with an odd positive power and therefore has the property that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{x}^{15}=\\infty .\n$$\n\nMultiplication by the negative constant $-243$ changes the sign of the limit. \nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & =-243\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{x}^{15} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{x}^{15} \\\\  & =-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(-243{x}^{15})=-\\infty .\n$$" "First, we calculate the powers and multiply the two functions, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }({p}^{3}(x)\\cdot {q}^{2}(x)) & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }({(p(x))}^{3}\\cdot {(q(x))}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }({(-3{x}^{3})}^{3}\\cdot {(-3{x}^{3})}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }(-27{x}^{9}\\cdot 9{x}^{6}) \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }((-27\\cdot 9){x}^{9+6}) \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }(-243\\cdot {x}^{15}) \\\\  & =-243\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{x}^{15}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe graph $y={x}^{15}$ is power function with an odd positive power and therefore has the property that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }{x}^{15}=\\infty .\n$$\n\nMultiplication by the negative constant $-243$ changes the sign of the limit. \nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & =-243\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{x}^{15} \\\\  & =-\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }{x}^{15} \\\\  & =-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }(-243{x}^{15})=-\\infty .\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186562478 :question/worked-solution "First, let's calculate ${x}^{2}\\cdot q(x):$\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}\\cdot q(x)={x}^{2}\\cdot (15{x}^{7})=15{x}^{9}\n$$\n\nWhen we divide the two functions ${x}^{2}\\cdot q(x)$ and $p(x),$ we get a constant, and the limit of a constant is just the constant itself:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{x}^{2}\\cdot q(x)}{p(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{15{x}^{9}}{6{x}^{9}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{15}{6} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{5}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, let's calculate ${x}^{2}\\cdot q(x):$\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}\\cdot q(x)={x}^{2}\\cdot (15{x}^{7})=15{x}^{9}\n$$\n\nWhen we divide the two functions ${x}^{2}\\cdot q(x)$ and $p(x),$ we get a constant, and the limit of a constant is just the constant itself:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{x}^{2}\\cdot q(x)}{p(x)} & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{15{x}^{9}}{6{x}^{9}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{15}{6} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{5}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186562488 :question/worked-solution "When we divide the two functions, we get a constant, and the limit of a constant is just the constant itself:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{u(x)}{v(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{14{x}^{11}}{(-7{x}^{11})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(-2) \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$" "When we divide the two functions, we get a constant, and the limit of a constant is just the constant itself:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{u(x)}{v(x)} & =\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{14{x}^{11}}{(-7{x}^{11})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }(-2) \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186562499 :question/worked-solution "The graph $y={x}^{8}$ looks similar to an upward parabola, so\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{8}=\\infty .\n$$\n\nMultiplication by the positive constant $5$ does not affect the limit at infinity. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(5{x}^{8})={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }({x}^{8})=\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The graph $y={x}^{8}$ looks similar to an upward parabola, so\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{8}=\\infty .\n$$\n\nMultiplication by the positive constant $5$ does not affect the limit at infinity. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }(5{x}^{8})=\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }({x}^{8})=\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186562505 :question/worked-solution "First, we multiply the two functions, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(p(x)\\cdot q(x)) & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(3{x}^{2}\\cdot 5{x}^{7}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }((3\\cdot 5){x}^{2+7}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(15\\cdot {x}^{9}) \\\\  & =15\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{9}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe graph $y={x}^{9}$ is power function with an odd positive power and therefore has the property that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{9}=-\\infty .\n$$\n\nMultiplication by the positive constant $15$ does not affect the limit at infinity. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}15\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{9}={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{9}=-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(15{x}^{9})=-\\infty .\n$$" "First, we multiply the two functions, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }(p(x)\\cdot q(x)) & =\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }(3{x}^{2}\\cdot 5{x}^{7}) \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }((3\\cdot 5){x}^{2+7}) \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }(15\\cdot {x}^{9}) \\\\  & =15\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{9}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe graph $y={x}^{9}$ is power function with an odd positive power and therefore has the property that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{9}=-\\infty .\n$$\n\nMultiplication by the positive constant $15$ does not affect the limit at infinity. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}15\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{9}=\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{9}=-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }(15{x}^{9})=-\\infty .\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186563647 :question/worked-solution "Using the  addition rule for limits, and the fact that both functions are continuous, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 5}[f(t)+3g(t)] & ={lim}_{t\\to 5}f(t)+{lim}_{t\\to 5}[3g(t)] \\\\  & ={lim}_{t\\to 5}f(t)+3{lim}_{t\\to 5}g(t) \\\\  & =f(5)+3g(5) \\\\  & =f(5)+3\\underbrace{⟨-1,1,-2⟩}_{g(5)} \\\\  & =f(5)+⟨-3,3,-6⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, we are told that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{t\\to 5}[f(t)+3g(t)]=⟨1,3,1⟩.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we obtain the following equation which we can solve for $f(5):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 5}[f(t)+3g(t)] & =⟨1,3,1⟩ \\\\ f(5)+⟨-3,3,-6⟩ & =⟨1,3,1⟩ \\\\ f(5) & =⟨1,3,1⟩-⟨-3,3,-6⟩ \\\\ f(5) & =⟨4,0,7⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Using the  addition rule for limits, and the fact that both functions are continuous, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to 5}[f(t)+3g(t)] & =\\lim_{t\\to 5}f(t)+\\lim_{t\\to 5}[3g(t)] \\\\  & =\\lim_{t\\to 5}f(t)+3\\lim_{t\\to 5}g(t) \\\\  & =f(5)+3g(5) \\\\  & =f(5)+3\\underbrace{⟨-1,1,-2⟩}_{g(5)} \\\\  & =f(5)+⟨-3,3,-6⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, we are told that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{t\\to 5}[f(t)+3g(t)]=⟨1,3,1⟩.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we obtain the following equation which we can solve for $f(5):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to 5}[f(t)+3g(t)] & =⟨1,3,1⟩ \\\\ f(5)+⟨-3,3,-6⟩ & =⟨1,3,1⟩ \\\\ f(5) & =⟨1,3,1⟩-⟨-3,3,-6⟩ \\\\ f(5) & =⟨4,0,7⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186563649 :question/problem "Find the limit ${lim}_{t\\to 0}[f(t)\\cdot g(t)]$ given that $f(t)$ and $g(t)$ are defined as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t) & =⟨\\frac{{t}^{2}-2t+2}{t+1},\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)}{t},\\,t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩ \\\\ g(t) & =⟨\\frac{{e}^{2t}-1}{t},\\,1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,t+1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Find the limit $\\lim_{t\\to 0}[f(t)\\cdot g(t)]$ given that $f(t)$ and $g(t)$ are defined as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t) & =⟨\\frac{{t}^{2}-2t+2}{t+1},\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)}{t},\\,t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩ \\\\ g(t) & =⟨\\frac{{e}^{2t}-1}{t},\\,1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,t+1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186563649 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the limit of each function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 0}f(t) & =⟨{lim}_{t\\to 0}\\frac{{t}^{2}-2t+2}{t+1},\\,{lim}_{t\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)}{t},\\,{lim}_{t\\to 0}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{lim}_{t\\to 0}\\frac{{t}^{2}-2t+2}{t+1},\\,2{lim}_{t\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)}{(2t)},\\,{lim}_{t\\to 0}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{{0}^{2}-2\\cdot 0+2}{0+1},\\,2\\cdot 1,\\,0\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,2,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that for the second component of ${lim}_{t\\to 0}f(t)$ we used a special limit ${lim}_{u\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡u}{u}=1.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 0}g(t) & =⟨{lim}_{t\\to 0}\\frac{{e}^{2t}-1}{t},\\,{lim}_{t\\to 0}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,{lim}_{t\\to 0}(t+1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{lim}_{t\\to 0}\\frac{({e}^{2t}-1)^{′}}{(t)^{′}},\\,{lim}_{t\\to 0}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,{lim}_{t\\to 0}(t+1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{lim}_{t\\to 0}\\frac{2{e}^{2t}}{1},\\,{lim}_{t\\to 0}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,{lim}_{t\\to 0}(t+1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2{e}^{0},\\,1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡0,\\,0+1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,0,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that for the first component of ${lim}_{t\\to 0}g(t)$ we used L'Hopital's Rule.\n\nNow, we use properties of limits to calculate the final result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 0}[f(t)\\cdot g(t)] & ={lim}_{t\\to 0}f(t)\\cdot {lim}_{t\\to 0}g(t) \\\\  & =⟨2,\\,2,\\,0⟩\\cdot ⟨2,0,1⟩ \\\\  & =2\\cdot 2+2\\cdot 0+0\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we find the limit of each function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to 0}f(t) & =⟨\\lim_{t\\to 0}\\frac{{t}^{2}-2t+2}{t+1},\\,\\lim_{t\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)}{t},\\,\\lim_{t\\to 0}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\lim_{t\\to 0}\\frac{{t}^{2}-2t+2}{t+1},\\,2\\lim_{t\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)}{(2t)},\\,\\lim_{t\\to 0}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{{0}^{2}-2\\cdot 0+2}{0+1},\\,2\\cdot 1,\\,0\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,2,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that for the second component of $\\lim_{t\\to 0}f(t)$ we used a special limit $\\lim_{u\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡u}{u}=1.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to 0}g(t) & =⟨\\lim_{t\\to 0}\\frac{{e}^{2t}-1}{t},\\,\\lim_{t\\to 0}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\lim_{t\\to 0}(t+1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\lim_{t\\to 0}\\frac{({e}^{2t}-1)^{′}}{(t)^{′}},\\,\\lim_{t\\to 0}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\lim_{t\\to 0}(t+1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\lim_{t\\to 0}\\frac{2{e}^{2t}}{1},\\,\\lim_{t\\to 0}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\lim_{t\\to 0}(t+1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2{e}^{0},\\,1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡0,\\,0+1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,0,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that for the first component of $\\lim_{t\\to 0}g(t)$ we used L'Hopital's Rule.\n\nNow, we use properties of limits to calculate the final result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to 0}[f(t)\\cdot g(t)] & =\\lim_{t\\to 0}f(t)\\cdot {lim}_{t\\to 0}g(t) \\\\  & =⟨2,\\,2,\\,0⟩\\cdot ⟨2,0,1⟩ \\\\  & =2\\cdot 2+2\\cdot 0+0\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186563652 :question/worked-solution "Using the constant multiple and the addition rule for limits, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to -7}[\\frac{f(t)}{2}+3g(t)] & ={lim}_{t\\to -7}\\frac{f(t)}{2}+{lim}_{t\\to -7}3g(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{lim}_{t\\to -7}f(t)+3{lim}_{t\\to -7}g(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the fact that both functions are continuous, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{lim}_{t\\to -7}f(t)+3{lim}_{t\\to -7}g(t) & =\\frac{1}{2}f(-7)+3g(-7) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}⟨6,0,-2⟩+3⟨2,-1,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3,0,-1⟩+⟨6,-3,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨9,-3,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Using the constant multiple and the addition rule for limits, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to -7}[\\frac{f(t)}{2}+3g(t)] & =\\lim_{t\\to -7}\\frac{f(t)}{2}+\\lim_{t\\to -7}3g(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\lim_{t\\to -7}f(t)+3\\lim_{t\\to -7}g(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the fact that both functions are continuous, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}\\lim_{t\\to -7}f(t)+3\\lim_{t\\to -7}g(t) & =\\frac{1}{2}f(-7)+3g(-7) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}⟨6,0,-2⟩+3⟨2,-1,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3,0,-1⟩+⟨6,-3,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨9,-3,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186563660 :question/worked-solution "Applying the sum and root rules, we get\n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to -1}(\\sqrt[7]{(2t+1)^{7}}-\\sqrt[3]{({t}^{2}+t+1)^{6}}) & ={lim}_{t\\to -1}\\sqrt[7]{(2t+1)^{7}}-{lim}_{t\\to -1}\\sqrt[3]{({t}^{2}+t+1)^{6}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt[7]{{({lim}_{t\\to -1}(2t+1))}^{7}}-\\sqrt[3]{{({lim}_{t\\to -1}({t}^{2}+t+1))}^{6}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt[7]{(2(-1)+1)^{7}}-\\sqrt[3]{(((-1)^{2}+(-1)+1))^{6}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt[7]{(-2+1)^{7}}-\\sqrt[3]{((1-1+1))^{6}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt[7]{(-1)^{7}}-\\sqrt[3]{(1)^{6}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt[7]{-1}-\\sqrt[3]{1} \\\\  & =-1-1 \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Applying the sum and root rules, we get\n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to -1}(\\sqrt[7]{(2t+1)^{7}}-\\sqrt[3]{({t}^{2}+t+1)^{6}}) & =\\lim_{t\\to -1}\\sqrt[7]{(2t+1)^{7}}-\\lim_{t\\to -1}\\sqrt[3]{({t}^{2}+t+1)^{6}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt[7]{{(\\lim_{t\\to -1}(2t+1))}^{7}}-\\sqrt[3]{{(\\lim_{t\\to -1}({t}^{2}+t+1))}^{6}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt[7]{(2(-1)+1)^{7}}-\\sqrt[3]{(((-1)^{2}+(-1)+1))^{6}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt[7]{(-2+1)^{7}}-\\sqrt[3]{((1-1+1))^{6}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt[7]{(-1)^{7}}-\\sqrt[3]{(1)^{6}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt[7]{-1}-\\sqrt[3]{1} \\\\  & =-1-1 \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186563661 :question/worked-solution "First, recall that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{t\\to 2}f(t)=⟨{lim}_{t\\to 2}{e}^{t-2},\\,{lim}_{t\\to 2}\\sqrt{6-t},\\,{lim}_{t\\to 2}\\frac{3}{t+2}⟩.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the limits one by one.\n\n- For the first component, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 2}{e}^{t-2} & ={e}^{2-2} \\\\  & ={e}^{0} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the second component, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 2}\\sqrt{6-t} & =\\sqrt{6-2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the third component, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 2}\\frac{3}{t+2} & =\\frac{3}{2+2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, ${lim}_{t\\to 2}f(t)=⟨1,2,\\frac{3}{4}⟩.$" "First, recall that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{t\\to 2}f(t)=⟨\\lim_{t\\to 2}{e}^{t-2},\\,\\lim_{t\\to 2}\\sqrt{6-t},\\,\\lim_{t\\to 2}\\frac{3}{t+2}⟩.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the limits one by one.\n\n- For the first component, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to 2}{e}^{t-2} & ={e}^{2-2} \\\\  & ={e}^{0} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the second component, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to 2}\\sqrt{6-t} & =\\sqrt{6-2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the third component, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to 2}\\frac{3}{t+2} & =\\frac{3}{2+2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $\\lim_{t\\to 2}f(t)=⟨1,2,\\frac{3}{4}⟩.$"] [:db/cas 17592186563662 :question/worked-solution "First, recall that:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 1}f(t) & =({lim}_{t\\to 1}\\frac{4+t}{5})\\,i+({lim}_{t\\to 1}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-1)}{{t}^{2}-1})\\,j+({lim}_{t\\to 1}\\sqrt{9t})\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the limits one-by-one.\n\n- For the first component, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 1}\\frac{4+t}{5} & =\\frac{4+1}{5}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The second component can be found using a special limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 1}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-1)}{{t}^{2}-1} & ={lim}_{t\\to 1}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-1)}{(t-1)(t+1)} \\\\  & =({lim}_{t\\to 1}\\frac{1}{t+1})\\cdot ({lim}_{t\\to 1}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-1)}{t-1}) \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{1}{1+1}\\right)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the third component, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 1}\\sqrt{9t} & =\\sqrt{9(1)}=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, ${lim}_{t\\to 1}f(t)=i+\\frac{1}{2}\\,j+3\\,k.$\n\nFinally, the limit of $‖f(t)‖$ as $t$ approaches $1$ is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 1}‖f(t)‖ & =‖{lim}_{t\\to 1}f(t)‖ \\\\  & =‖i+\\frac{1}{2}\\,j+3\\,k‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{2}+{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{4}+9} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{41}{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{41}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, recall that:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to 1}f(t) & =(\\lim_{t\\to 1}\\frac{4+t}{5})\\,i+(\\lim_{t\\to 1}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-1)}{{t}^{2}-1})\\,j+(\\lim_{t\\to 1}\\sqrt{9t})\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the limits one-by-one.\n\n- For the first component, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to 1}\\frac{4+t}{5} & =\\frac{4+1}{5}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The second component can be found using a special limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to 1}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-1)}{{t}^{2}-1} & =\\lim_{t\\to 1}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-1)}{(t-1)(t+1)} \\\\  & =(\\lim_{t\\to 1}\\frac{1}{t+1})\\cdot (\\lim_{t\\to 1}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-1)}{t-1}) \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{1}{1+1}\\right)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the third component, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to 1}\\sqrt{9t} & =\\sqrt{9(1)}=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $\\lim_{t\\to 1}f(t)=i+\\frac{1}{2}\\,j+3\\,k.$\n\nFinally, the limit of $‖f(t)‖$ as $t$ approaches $1$ is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to 1}‖f(t)‖ & =‖\\lim_{t\\to 1}f(t)‖ \\\\  & =‖i+\\frac{1}{2}\\,j+3\\,k‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{2}+{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{4}+9} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{41}{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{41}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186563663 :question/problem "Find ${lim}_{t\\to 0}[f(t)+4g(t)]$ given that $f(t)$ and $g(t)$ are defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}3{e}^{t} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{t}^{2}+9}{t+3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,g(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}t-2 \\\\ \\frac{3t-2}{{t}^{2}-1} \\\\ {2}^{t}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Find $\\lim_{t\\to 0}[f(t)+4g(t)]$ given that $f(t)$ and $g(t)$ are defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}3{e}^{t} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{t}^{2}+9}{t+3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,g(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}t-2 \\\\ \\frac{3t-2}{{t}^{2}-1} \\\\ {2}^{t}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186563663 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the limit of each function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 0}f(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}{lim}_{t\\to 0}3{e}^{t} \\\\ {lim}_{t\\to 0}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t \\\\ {lim}_{t\\to 0}\\frac{{t}^{2}+9}{t+3}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}3{e}^{0} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡0 \\\\ \\frac{{0}^{2}+9}{0+3}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {lim}_{t\\to 0}g(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}{lim}_{t\\to 0}\\,(t-2) \\\\ {lim}_{t\\to 0}\\frac{3t-2}{{t}^{2}-1} \\\\ {lim}_{t\\to 0}{2}^{t}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0-2 \\\\ \\frac{3(0)-2}{{0}^{2}-1} \\\\ {2}^{0}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use properties of limits to calculate the final result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 0}[f(t)+4g(t)] & ={lim}_{t\\to 0}f(t)+4{lim}_{t\\to 0}g(t) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}+4\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-8 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we find the limit of each function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to 0}f(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\lim_{t\\to 0}3{e}^{t} \\\\ \\lim_{t\\to 0}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t \\\\ \\lim_{t\\to 0}\\frac{{t}^{2}+9}{t+3}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}3{e}^{0} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡0 \\\\ \\frac{{0}^{2}+9}{0+3}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\lim_{t\\to 0}g(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\lim_{t\\to 0}\\,(t-2) \\\\ \\lim_{t\\to 0}\\frac{3t-2}{{t}^{2}-1} \\\\ \\lim_{t\\to 0}{2}^{t}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0-2 \\\\ \\frac{3(0)-2}{{0}^{2}-1} \\\\ {2}^{0}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use properties of limits to calculate the final result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to 0}[f(t)+4g(t)] & =\\lim_{t\\to 0}f(t)+4\\lim_{t\\to 0}g(t) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}+4\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-8 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186563664 :question/problem "Find the limit ${lim}_{t\\to 1}[f(t)\\cdot g(t)]$ given that $f(t)$ and $g(t)$ are defined as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡t}{t-1} \\\\ \\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi t}{t} \\\\ \\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi t}{t}\\end{bmatrix},\\,g(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{t}^{2}-2t+1}{t+1} \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡t+t}{t+1} \\\\ \\frac{{e}^{t+1}}{t+1}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Find the limit $\\lim_{t\\to 1}[f(t)\\cdot g(t)]$ given that $f(t)$ and $g(t)$ are defined as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡t}{t-1} \\\\ \\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi t}{t} \\\\ \\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi t}{t}\\end{bmatrix},\\,g(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{t}^{2}-2t+1}{t+1} \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡t+t}{t+1} \\\\ \\frac{{e}^{t+1}}{t+1}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186563664 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the limit of each function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 1}f(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}{lim}_{t\\to 1}\\,\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡t}{t-1}\\right) \\\\ {lim}_{t\\to 1}\\,\\left(\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi t}{t}\\right) \\\\ {lim}_{t\\to 1}\\,\\left(\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi t}{t}\\right)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}{lim}_{t\\to 1}\\,\\left(\\frac{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)^{′}}{(t-1)^{′}}\\right) \\\\ {lim}_{t\\to 1}\\,\\left(\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi t}{t}\\right) \\\\ {lim}_{t\\to 1}\\,\\left(\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi t}{t}\\right)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}{lim}_{t\\to 1}\\,\\left(\\frac{1}{t}\\right) \\\\ \\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi }{1} \\\\ \\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi }{1}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that for the first component of ${lim}_{t\\to 1}f(t)$ we used L'Hopital's Rule.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 1}g(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}{lim}_{t\\to 1}\\,\\frac{{t}^{2}-2t+1}{t+1} \\\\ {lim}_{t\\to 1}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡t+t}{t+1} \\\\ {lim}_{t\\to 1}\\,\\frac{{e}^{t+1}}{t+1}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{1}^{2}-2\\cdot 1+1}{1+1} \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡1+1}{1+1} \\\\ \\frac{{e}^{1+1}}{1+1}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{{e}^{2}}{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use properties of limits to calculate the final result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 1}[f(t)\\cdot g(t)] & ={lim}_{t\\to 1}f(t)\\cdot {lim}_{t\\to 1}g(t) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{{e}^{2}}{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot 0+(-2)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}+0\\cdot \\frac{{e}^{2}}{2} \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we find the limit of each function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to 1}f(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\lim_{t\\to 1}\\,\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡t}{t-1}\\right) \\\\ \\lim_{t\\to 1}\\,\\left(\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi t}{t}\\right) \\\\ \\lim_{t\\to 1}\\,\\left(\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi t}{t}\\right)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\lim_{t\\to 1}\\,\\left(\\frac{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)^{′}}{(t-1)^{′}}\\right) \\\\ \\lim_{t\\to 1}\\,\\left(\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi t}{t}\\right) \\\\ \\lim_{t\\to 1}\\,\\left(\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi t}{t}\\right)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\lim_{t\\to 1}\\,\\left(\\frac{1}{t}\\right) \\\\ \\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi }{1} \\\\ \\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi }{1}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that for the first component of $\\lim_{t\\to 1}f(t)$ we used L'Hopital's Rule.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to 1}g(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\lim_{t\\to 1}\\,\\frac{{t}^{2}-2t+1}{t+1} \\\\ \\lim_{t\\to 1}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡t+t}{t+1} \\\\ \\lim_{t\\to 1}\\,\\frac{{e}^{t+1}}{t+1}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{1}^{2}-2\\cdot 1+1}{1+1} \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡1+1}{1+1} \\\\ \\frac{{e}^{1+1}}{1+1}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{{e}^{2}}{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use properties of limits to calculate the final result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to 1}[f(t)\\cdot g(t)] & =\\lim_{t\\to 1}f(t)\\cdot {lim}_{t\\to 1}g(t) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{{e}^{2}}{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot 0+(-2)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}+0\\cdot \\frac{{e}^{2}}{2} \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186563667 :question/problem "Calculate ${lim}_{s\\to 0}f(s)$, if $$\n\\displaystyle f(s)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{s+8} \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡s}{s} \\\\ (1+2s)^{1/(2s)}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" "Calculate $\\lim_{s\\to 0}f(s)$, if $$\n\\displaystyle f(s)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{s+8} \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡s}{s} \\\\ (1+2s)^{1/(2s)}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186563667 :question/worked-solution "First, recall that:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{s\\to 0}f(s)=\\begin{bmatrix}{lim}_{s\\to 0}\\sqrt{s+8} \\\\ {lim}_{s\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡s}{s} \\\\ {lim}_{s\\to 0}(1+2s)^{1/(2s)}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the limits one-by-one.\n\n- For the first component, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{s\\to 0}\\sqrt{s+8} & =\\sqrt{0+8} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{8} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{3}} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The second component can be found using L'Hopital's Rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{s\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡s}{s} & ={lim}_{s\\to 0}\\frac{{(\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡s)}^{′}}{{s}^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{s\\to 0}\\frac{\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{s}^{2}}}}{1} \\\\  & ={lim}_{s\\to 0}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{s}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{0}^{2}}} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The third component can be found using a special limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{s\\to 0}(1+2s)^{1/(2s)} & ={lim}_{u\\to 0}(1+u)^{1/u}=e\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $$\n\\displaystyle {lim}_{s\\to 0}f(s)=\\begin{bmatrix}2\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 1 \\\\ e\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" "First, recall that:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{s\\to 0}f(s)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\lim_{s\\to 0}\\sqrt{s+8} \\\\ \\lim_{s\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡s}{s} \\\\ \\lim_{s\\to 0}(1+2s)^{1/(2s)}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the limits one-by-one.\n\n- For the first component, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{s\\to 0}\\sqrt{s+8} & =\\sqrt{0+8} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{8} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{3}} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The second component can be found using L'Hopital's Rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{s\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡s}{s} & =\\lim_{s\\to 0}\\frac{{(\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡s)}^{′}}{{s}^{′}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{s\\to 0}\\frac{\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{s}^{2}}}}{1} \\\\  & =\\lim_{s\\to 0}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{s}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{0}^{2}}} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The third component can be found using a special limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{s\\to 0}(1+2s)^{1/(2s)} & =\\lim_{u\\to 0}(1+u)^{1/u}=e\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $$\n\\displaystyle {lim}_{s\\to 0}f(s)=\\begin{bmatrix}2\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 1 \\\\ e\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186563676 :question/worked-solution "From the graph, we get that ${lim}_{x\\to \\,2}f(x)=3.$\n\nFrom the product rule, we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,2}(x-1)^{2}\\,(f(x)-2)^{3}={lim}_{x\\to \\,2}(x-1)^{2}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\,2}(f(x)-2)^{3}.\n$$\n\nWe calculate the limits of each factor, separately.\n\nApplying the power rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,2}(x-1)^{2} & ={({lim}_{x\\to \\,2}(x-1))}^{2} \\\\  & =(2-1)^{2} \\\\  & ={1}^{2} \\\\  & =1\\,,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,2}(f(x)-2)^{3} & ={({lim}_{x\\to \\,2}(f(x)-2))}^{3} \\\\  & =(3-2)^{3} \\\\  & ={1}^{3} \\\\  & =1\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,2}(x-1)^{2}\\,(f(x)-2)^{3} & =(1)\\cdot (1) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "From the graph, we get that $\\lim_{x\\to \\,2}f(x)=3.$\n\nFrom the product rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,2}(x-1)^{2}\\,(f(x)-2)^{3}=\\lim_{x\\to \\,2}(x-1)^{2}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\,2}(f(x)-2)^{3}.\n$$\n\nWe calculate the limits of each factor, separately.\n\nApplying the power rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\,2}(x-1)^{2} & ={(\\lim_{x\\to \\,2}(x-1))}^{2} \\\\  & =(2-1)^{2} \\\\  & ={1}^{2} \\\\  & =1\\,,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\,2}(f(x)-2)^{3} & ={(\\lim_{x\\to \\,2}(f(x)-2))}^{3} \\\\  & =(3-2)^{3} \\\\  & ={1}^{3} \\\\  & =1\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\,2}(x-1)^{2}\\,(f(x)-2)^{3} & =(1)\\cdot (1) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186563690 :question/worked-solution "Using the  addition rule for limits, and the fact that both functions are continuous, we get:  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 0}[f(t)-g(t)] & ={lim}_{t\\to 0}f(t)+{lim}_{t\\to 0}[-g(t)] \\\\  & ={lim}_{t\\to 0}f(t)-{lim}_{t\\to 0}g(t) \\\\  & =f(0)-g(0) \\\\  & =f(0)-\\underbrace{⟨-1,0,1⟩}_{g(0)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, we are told that:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{t\\to 0}[f(t)-g(t)]=⟨-2,-3,0⟩\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we obtain the following equation which we can solve for $f(0):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 0}[f(t)-g(t)] & =⟨-2,-3,0⟩ \\\\ ⟨-2,-3,0⟩ & =f(0)-⟨-1,0,1⟩ \\\\ ⟨-2,-3,0⟩+⟨-1,0,1⟩ & =f(0) \\\\ f(0) & =⟨-3,-3,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Using the  addition rule for limits, and the fact that both functions are continuous, we get:  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to 0}[f(t)-g(t)] & =\\lim_{t\\to 0}f(t)+\\lim_{t\\to 0}[-g(t)] \\\\  & =\\lim_{t\\to 0}f(t)-\\lim_{t\\to 0}g(t) \\\\  & =f(0)-g(0) \\\\  & =f(0)-\\underbrace{⟨-1,0,1⟩}_{g(0)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, we are told that:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{t\\to 0}[f(t)-g(t)]=⟨-2,-3,0⟩\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we obtain the following equation which we can solve for $f(0):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to 0}[f(t)-g(t)] & =⟨-2,-3,0⟩ \\\\ ⟨-2,-3,0⟩ & =f(0)-⟨-1,0,1⟩ \\\\ ⟨-2,-3,0⟩+⟨-1,0,1⟩ & =f(0) \\\\ f(0) & =⟨-3,-3,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186563691 :question/worked-solution "Using the scalar function multiple rule for limits, and the fact that both functions are continuous, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 0}[u(t)f(t)] & ={lim}_{t\\to 0}u(t)\\cdot {lim}_{t\\to 0}f(t) \\\\  & =u(0)f(0) \\\\  & =3f(0)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, we are told that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{t\\to 0}[u(t)f(t)]=⟨-18,-21,6⟩\\,.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following equation which we can solve for $f(0):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 0}[u(t)f(t)] & =⟨-18,-21,6⟩ \\\\ 3f(0) & =⟨-18,-21,6⟩ \\\\ f(0) & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨-18,-21,6⟩ \\\\ f(0) & =⟨-6,-7,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Using the scalar function multiple rule for limits, and the fact that both functions are continuous, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to 0}[u(t)f(t)] & =\\lim_{t\\to 0}u(t)\\cdot {lim}_{t\\to 0}f(t) \\\\  & =u(0)f(0) \\\\  & =3f(0)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, we are told that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{t\\to 0}[u(t)f(t)]=⟨-18,-21,6⟩\\,.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following equation which we can solve for $f(0):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to 0}[u(t)f(t)] & =⟨-18,-21,6⟩ \\\\ 3f(0) & =⟨-18,-21,6⟩ \\\\ f(0) & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨-18,-21,6⟩ \\\\ f(0) & =⟨-6,-7,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186563693 :question/problem "Calculate ${lim}_{s\\to 1}‖f(s)‖,$ if $$\n\\displaystyle f(s)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{5-s} \\\\ \\frac{{e}^{1-s}}{s} \\\\ (1+5s)^{1-s}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" "Calculate $\\lim_{s\\to 1}‖f(s)‖,$ if $$\n\\displaystyle f(s)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{5-s} \\\\ \\frac{{e}^{1-s}}{s} \\\\ (1+5s)^{1-s}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186563693 :question/worked-solution "First, recall that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{s\\to 1}f(s)=\\begin{bmatrix}{lim}_{s\\to 1}\\sqrt{5-s} \\\\ {lim}_{s\\to 1}\\frac{{e}^{1-s}}{1-s} \\\\ {lim}_{s\\to 1}\\,(1+5s)^{1/(2s)}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the limits one by one.\n\n- For the first component, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{s\\to 1}\\sqrt{5-s} & =\\sqrt{5-1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the second component, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{s\\to 1}\\frac{{e}^{1-s}}{s}=\\frac{1}{1}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the third component, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{s\\to 1}\\,(1+5s)^{1-s} & =(1+5\\cdot 1)^{1-1}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $$\n\\displaystyle {lim}_{s\\to 1}f(s)=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the limit of $‖f(s)‖$ as $s$ approaches $1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{s\\to 1}‖f(s)‖ & =‖{lim}_{s\\to 1}f(s)‖ \\\\  & =‖\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, recall that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{s\\to 1}f(s)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\lim_{s\\to 1}\\sqrt{5-s} \\\\ \\lim_{s\\to 1}\\frac{{e}^{1-s}}{1-s} \\\\ \\lim_{s\\to 1}\\,(1+5s)^{1/(2s)}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the limits one by one.\n\n- For the first component, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{s\\to 1}\\sqrt{5-s} & =\\sqrt{5-1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the second component, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{s\\to 1}\\frac{{e}^{1-s}}{s}=\\frac{1}{1}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the third component, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{s\\to 1}\\,(1+5s)^{1-s} & =(1+5\\cdot 1)^{1-1}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $$\n\\displaystyle {lim}_{s\\to 1}f(s)=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the limit of $‖f(s)‖$ as $s$ approaches $1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{s\\to 1}‖f(s)‖ & =‖\\lim_{s\\to 1}f(s)‖ \\\\  & =‖\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186563706 :question/problem "Find ${lim}_{t\\to \\infty }[g(t)+2f(t)]$ given that $f(t)$ and $g(t)$ are defined as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t) & =\\frac{{t}^{2}}{1-{t}^{2}}\\,i+(1+\\frac{1}{t})\\,j+{t}^{-2}\\,k \\\\ g(t) & =2\\,i+{e}^{-t}\\,j+{(1+\\frac{1}{t})}^{t}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Find $\\lim_{t\\to \\infty }[g(t)+2f(t)]$ given that $f(t)$ and $g(t)$ are defined as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t) & =\\frac{{t}^{2}}{1-{t}^{2}}\\,i+(1+\\frac{1}{t})\\,j+{t}^{-2}\\,k \\\\ g(t) & =2\\,i+{e}^{-t}\\,j+{(1+\\frac{1}{t})}^{t}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186563706 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the limit of each function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to \\infty }f(t) & =({lim}_{t\\to \\infty }\\frac{{t}^{2}}{1-{t}^{2}})\\,i+({lim}_{t\\to \\infty }(1+\\frac{1}{t}))\\,j-({lim}_{t\\to \\infty }{t}^{-2})\\,k, \\\\ {lim}_{t\\to \\infty }g(t) & =({lim}_{t\\to \\infty }2)\\,i-({lim}_{t\\to \\infty }{e}^{-t})\\,j+({lim}_{t\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{t})}^{t})\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's find the limits one-by-one.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {lim}_{t\\to \\infty }\\frac{{t}^{2}}{1-{t}^{2}}={lim}_{t\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}-1}=-1 \\\\  & {lim}_{t\\to \\infty }(1+\\frac{1}{t})=1+{lim}_{t\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{t}=1 \\\\  & {lim}_{t\\to \\infty }{t}^{-2}={lim}_{t\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}=0 \\\\  & {lim}_{t\\to \\infty }2=2 \\\\  & {lim}_{t\\to \\infty }{e}^{-t}=0 \\\\  & {lim}_{t\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{t})}^{t}=e\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to \\infty }f(t) & =-1\\,i+1\\,j+0\\,k \\\\  & =-i+j, \\\\ {lim}_{t\\to \\infty }g(t) & =2\\,i+0\\,j+e\\,k \\\\  & =2\\,i+e\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use properties of limits to calculate the final result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to \\infty }[g(t)+2f(t)] & ={lim}_{t\\to \\infty }g(t)+2{lim}_{t\\to \\infty }f(t) \\\\  & =(2\\,i+e\\,k)+2(-i+j) \\\\  & =2j+e\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we find the limit of each function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to \\infty }f(t) & =(\\lim_{t\\to \\infty }\\frac{{t}^{2}}{1-{t}^{2}})\\,i+(\\lim_{t\\to \\infty }(1+\\frac{1}{t}))\\,j-(\\lim_{t\\to \\infty }{t}^{-2})\\,k, \\\\ \\lim_{t\\to \\infty }g(t) & =(\\lim_{t\\to \\infty }2)\\,i-(\\lim_{t\\to \\infty }{e}^{-t})\\,j+(\\lim_{t\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{t})}^{t})\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's find the limits one-by-one.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\lim_{t\\to \\infty }\\frac{{t}^{2}}{1-{t}^{2}}=\\lim_{t\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}-1}=-1 \\\\  & \\lim_{t\\to \\infty }(1+\\frac{1}{t})=1+\\lim_{t\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{t}=1 \\\\  & \\lim_{t\\to \\infty }{t}^{-2}=\\lim_{t\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}=0 \\\\  & \\lim_{t\\to \\infty }2=2 \\\\  & \\lim_{t\\to \\infty }{e}^{-t}=0 \\\\  & \\lim_{t\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{t})}^{t}=e\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to \\infty }f(t) & =-1\\,i+1\\,j+0\\,k \\\\  & =-i+j, \\\\ \\lim_{t\\to \\infty }g(t) & =2\\,i+0\\,j+e\\,k \\\\  & =2\\,i+e\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use properties of limits to calculate the final result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to \\infty }[g(t)+2f(t)] & =\\lim_{t\\to \\infty }g(t)+2\\lim_{t\\to \\infty }f(t) \\\\  & =(2\\,i+e\\,k)+2(-i+j) \\\\  & =2j+e\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186563712 :question/problem "Calculate ${lim}_{t\\to 3}‖f(t)‖$, if $$\n\\displaystyle f(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}{t}^{2}-3t \\\\ \\frac{1}{{t}^{2}-8} \\\\ 5-2t\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" "Calculate $\\lim_{t\\to 3}‖f(t)‖$, if $$\n\\displaystyle f(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}{t}^{2}-3t \\\\ \\frac{1}{{t}^{2}-8} \\\\ 5-2t\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186563712 :question/worked-solution "First, recall that:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{t\\to 3}f(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}{lim}_{t\\to 3}({t}^{2}-3t) \\\\ {lim}_{t\\to 3}\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}-8} \\\\ {lim}_{t\\to 3}(5-2t)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the limits one-by-one.\n\n- For the first component, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 3}({t}^{2}-3t) & ={3}^{2}-3(3)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the second component, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 3}\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}-8} & =\\frac{1}{{3}^{2}-8}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the third component, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 3}(5-2t) & =5-2(3)=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $$\n\\displaystyle {lim}_{t\\to 3}f(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the limit of $‖f(t)‖$ as $t$ approaches $3$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 3}‖f(t)‖ & =‖{lim}_{t\\to 3}f(t)‖ \\\\  & =‖\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, recall that:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{t\\to 3}f(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\lim_{t\\to 3}({t}^{2}-3t) \\\\ \\lim_{t\\to 3}\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}-8} \\\\ \\lim_{t\\to 3}(5-2t)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the limits one-by-one.\n\n- For the first component, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to 3}({t}^{2}-3t) & ={3}^{2}-3(3)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the second component, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to 3}\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}-8} & =\\frac{1}{{3}^{2}-8}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the third component, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to 3}(5-2t) & =5-2(3)=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $$\n\\displaystyle {lim}_{t\\to 3}f(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the limit of $‖f(t)‖$ as $t$ approaches $3$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to 3}‖f(t)‖ & =‖\\lim_{t\\to 3}f(t)‖ \\\\  & =‖\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186563714 :question/worked-solution "First, recall that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{t\\to 0}f(t)=({lim}_{t\\to 0}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)i+({lim}_{t\\to 0}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)j+({lim}_{t\\to 0}(t+2)^{2})k.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the limits one-by-one.\n\n- For the first component, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 0}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡0=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the second component, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 0}\\operatorname{cos}⁡0=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the third component, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 0}\\,(t+2)^{2} & =(0+2)^{2}=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, ${lim}_{t\\to 0}f(t)=j+4\\,k.$" "First, recall that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{t\\to 0}f(t)=(\\lim_{t\\to 0}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)i+(\\lim_{t\\to 0}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)j+(\\lim_{t\\to 0}(t+2)^{2})k.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the limits one-by-one.\n\n- For the first component, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to 0}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡0=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the second component, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to 0}\\operatorname{cos}⁡0=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the third component, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to 0}\\,(t+2)^{2} & =(0+2)^{2}=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $\\lim_{t\\to 0}f(t)=j+4\\,k.$"] [:db/cas 17592186563716 :question/problem "Calculate ${lim}_{s\\to 0}‖f(s)‖$, if $$\n\\displaystyle f(s)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{s}{(s+1)^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡s}{{s}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡s}{2{s}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\\,.\n$$" "Calculate $\\lim_{s\\to 0}‖f(s)‖$, if $$\n\\displaystyle f(s)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{s}{(s+1)^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡s}{{s}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡s}{2{s}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\\,.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186563716 :question/worked-solution "First, recall that:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{s\\to 0}f(s) & =\\begin{bmatrix}{lim}_{s\\to 0}\\frac{s}{(s+1)^{2}} \\\\ {lim}_{s\\to 0}\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡s}{{s}^{2}} \\\\ {lim}_{s\\to 0}\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡s}{2{s}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the limits one-by-one.\n\n- For the first component, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{s\\to 0}\\frac{s}{(s+1)^{2}} & =\\frac{0}{(0+1)^{2}}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The second component can be found using a special limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{s\\to 0}\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡s}{{s}^{2}} & ={lim}_{s\\to 0}{\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡s}{s}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & ={({lim}_{s\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡s}{s})}^{2} \\\\  & ={1}^{2} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The third component can be found using L'Hopital's Rule twice:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{s\\to 0}\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡s}{2{s}^{2}} & ={lim}_{s\\to 0}\\frac{{(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡s)}^{″}}{{(2{s}^{2})}^{″}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{s\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡s}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡0}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $$\n\\displaystyle {lim}_{s\\to 0}f(s)=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1/4\\end{bmatrix}\\,.\n$$\n\nFinally, the limit of $‖f(s)‖$ as $s$ approaches $0$ is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{s\\to 0}‖f(s)‖ & =‖{lim}_{s\\to 0}f(s)‖ \\\\  & =‖\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1/4\\end{bmatrix}‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{17}{16}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, recall that:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{s\\to 0}f(s) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\lim_{s\\to 0}\\frac{s}{(s+1)^{2}} \\\\ \\lim_{s\\to 0}\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡s}{{s}^{2}} \\\\ \\lim_{s\\to 0}\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡s}{2{s}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the limits one-by-one.\n\n- For the first component, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{s\\to 0}\\frac{s}{(s+1)^{2}} & =\\frac{0}{(0+1)^{2}}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The second component can be found using a special limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{s\\to 0}\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡s}{{s}^{2}} & =\\lim_{s\\to 0}{\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡s}{s}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & ={(\\lim_{s\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡s}{s})}^{2} \\\\  & ={1}^{2} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The third component can be found using L'Hopital's Rule twice:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{s\\to 0}\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡s}{2{s}^{2}} & =\\lim_{s\\to 0}\\frac{{(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡s)}^{″}}{{(2{s}^{2})}^{″}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{s\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡s}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡0}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $$\n\\displaystyle {lim}_{s\\to 0}f(s)=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1/4\\end{bmatrix}\\,.\n$$\n\nFinally, the limit of $‖f(s)‖$ as $s$ approaches $0$ is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{s\\to 0}‖f(s)‖ & =‖\\lim_{s\\to 0}f(s)‖ \\\\  & =‖\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1/4\\end{bmatrix}‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{17}{16}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186563727 :question/problem "Find ${lim}_{t\\to 0}[f(t)+g(t)]$ given that $f(t)$ and $g(t)$ are defined as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}{t}^{2} \\\\ {e}^{t} \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{t}\\end{bmatrix},\\,g(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 1 \\\\ \\frac{2t-1}{{t}^{2}+1}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Find $\\lim_{t\\to 0}[f(t)+g(t)]$ given that $f(t)$ and $g(t)$ are defined as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}{t}^{2} \\\\ {e}^{t} \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{t}\\end{bmatrix},\\,g(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 1 \\\\ \\frac{2t-1}{{t}^{2}+1}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186563727 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the limit of each function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 0}f(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}{lim}_{t\\to 0}{t}^{2} \\\\ {lim}_{t\\to 0}{e}^{t} \\\\ {lim}_{t\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{t}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}{0}^{2} \\\\ {e}^{0} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the third component is a special limit.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 0}g(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}{lim}_{t\\to 0}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ {lim}_{t\\to 0}1 \\\\ {lim}_{t\\to 0}\\frac{2t-1}{{t}^{2}+1}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ \\frac{2(0)-1}{{0}^{2}+1})\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use properties of limits to calculate the final result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 0}[f(t)+g(t)] & ={lim}_{t\\to 0}f(t)+{lim}_{t\\to 0}g(t) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we find the limit of each function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to 0}f(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\lim_{t\\to 0}{t}^{2} \\\\ \\lim_{t\\to 0}{e}^{t} \\\\ \\lim_{t\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{t}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}{0}^{2} \\\\ {e}^{0} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the third component is a special limit.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to 0}g(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\lim_{t\\to 0}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\lim_{t\\to 0}1 \\\\ \\lim_{t\\to 0}\\frac{2t-1}{{t}^{2}+1}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ \\frac{2(0)-1}{{0}^{2}+1})\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use properties of limits to calculate the final result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to 0}[f(t)+g(t)] & =\\lim_{t\\to 0}f(t)+\\lim_{t\\to 0}g(t) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186563729 :question/problem "Find ${lim}_{t\\to 2}[f(t)\\times g(t)]$ given that $f(t)$ and $g(t)$ are defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t) & =⟨6-{t}^{3},\\,{t}^{2}-6,\\,\\frac{16}{{t}^{3}}⟩, \\\\ g(t) & =⟨5-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3-t),\\,\\sqrt{6-t},\\,2t⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Find $\\lim_{t\\to 2}[f(t)\\times g(t)]$ given that $f(t)$ and $g(t)$ are defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t) & =⟨6-{t}^{3},\\,{t}^{2}-6,\\,\\frac{16}{{t}^{3}}⟩, \\\\ g(t) & =⟨5-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3-t),\\,\\sqrt{6-t},\\,2t⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186563729 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the limit of each function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 2}f(t) & =⟨{lim}_{t\\to 2}\\,(6-{t}^{3}),\\,{lim}_{t\\to 2}\\,({t}^{2}-6),\\,{lim}_{t\\to 2}\\frac{16}{{t}^{3}}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨6-{2}^{3},{2}^{2}-6,\\frac{16}{{2}^{3}}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2,-2,2⟩ \\\\ {lim}_{t\\to 2}g(t) & =⟨{lim}_{t\\to 2}\\,(5-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3-t)),\\,{lim}_{t\\to 2}\\,\\sqrt{6-t},\\,{lim}_{t\\to 2}\\,2t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨5-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3-2),\\sqrt{6-2},2(2)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨5,2,4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use properties of limits to calculate the final result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 2}[f(t)\\times g(t)] & ={lim}_{t\\to 2}f(t)\\times {lim}_{t\\to 2}g(t) \\\\  & =⟨-2,-2,2⟩\\times ⟨5,2,4⟩ \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ -2 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 5 & 2 & 4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 2 \\\\ 5 & 4\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & -2 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =((-2)\\cdot 4-2\\cdot 2)\\,i-((-2)\\cdot 4-5\\cdot 2)\\,j+((-2)\\cdot 2-5\\cdot (-2))\\,k \\\\  & =-12\\,i+18\\,j+6\\,k \\\\  & =⟨-12,18,6⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we find the limit of each function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to 2}f(t) & =⟨\\lim_{t\\to 2}\\,(6-{t}^{3}),\\,\\lim_{t\\to 2}\\,({t}^{2}-6),\\,\\lim_{t\\to 2}\\frac{16}{{t}^{3}}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨6-{2}^{3},{2}^{2}-6,\\frac{16}{{2}^{3}}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2,-2,2⟩ \\\\ \\lim_{t\\to 2}g(t) & =⟨\\lim_{t\\to 2}\\,(5-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3-t)),\\,\\lim_{t\\to 2}\\,\\sqrt{6-t},\\,\\lim_{t\\to 2}\\,2t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨5-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3-2),\\sqrt{6-2},2(2)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨5,2,4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use properties of limits to calculate the final result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to 2}[f(t)\\times g(t)] & =\\lim_{t\\to 2}f(t)\\times {lim}_{t\\to 2}g(t) \\\\  & =⟨-2,-2,2⟩\\times ⟨5,2,4⟩ \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ -2 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 5 & 2 & 4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 2 \\\\ 5 & 4\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & -2 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =((-2)\\cdot 4-2\\cdot 2)\\,i-((-2)\\cdot 4-5\\cdot 2)\\,j+((-2)\\cdot 2-5\\cdot (-2))\\,k \\\\  & =-12\\,i+18\\,j+6\\,k \\\\  & =⟨-12,18,6⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186563738 :question/worked-solution "First, recall that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{s\\to 2}f(s)=⟨{lim}_{s\\to 2}\\frac{5}{{e}^{s-2}},\\,{lim}_{s\\to 2}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4-2s),\\,{lim}_{s\\to 2}\\,({s}^{3}-5)⟩.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the limits one by one.\n\n- For the first component, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{s\\to 2}\\frac{5}{{e}^{s-2}} & =\\frac{5}{{e}^{2-2}}=5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the first component, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{s\\to 2}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4-2s) & =3{lim}_{s\\to 2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4-2s) \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4-2\\cdot 2) \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡0 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the third component, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{s\\to 2}\\,({s}^{3}-5) & ={2}^{3}-5=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, ${lim}_{s\\to 2}f(s)=⟨5,3,3⟩.$" "First, recall that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{s\\to 2}f(s)=⟨\\lim_{s\\to 2}\\frac{5}{{e}^{s-2}},\\,\\lim_{s\\to 2}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4-2s),\\,\\lim_{s\\to 2}\\,({s}^{3}-5)⟩.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the limits one by one.\n\n- For the first component, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{s\\to 2}\\frac{5}{{e}^{s-2}} & =\\frac{5}{{e}^{2-2}}=5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the first component, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{s\\to 2}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4-2s) & =3\\lim_{s\\to 2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4-2s) \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4-2\\cdot 2) \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡0 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the third component, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{s\\to 2}\\,({s}^{3}-5) & ={2}^{3}-5=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $\\lim_{s\\to 2}f(s)=⟨5,3,3⟩.$"] [:db/cas 17592186563742 :question/worked-solution "Applying the quotient rule, we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{3x}{\\sqrt[3]{2{x}^{4}-5}}=\\frac{{lim}_{x\\to 2}3x}{{lim}_{x\\to 2}\\sqrt[3]{2{x}^{4}-5}}\n$$\n\nWe calculate the limits of the numerator and denominator, separately.\n\nFor the numerator, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 2}3x & =3{lim}_{x\\to 2}x \\\\  & =3\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =6\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the root rule in the denominator, we have\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 2}\\sqrt[3]{2{x}^{4}-5} & =\\sqrt[3]{{lim}_{x\\to 2}(2{x}^{4}-5)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{2(2)^{4}-5)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{27} \\\\  & =3\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{3x}{\\sqrt[3]{2{x}^{4}-5}} & =\\frac{6}{3}=2\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Applying the quotient rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 2}\\frac{3x}{\\sqrt[3]{2{x}^{4}-5}}=\\frac{\\lim_{x\\to 2}3x}{\\lim_{x\\to 2}\\sqrt[3]{2{x}^{4}-5}}\n$$\n\nWe calculate the limits of the numerator and denominator, separately.\n\nFor the numerator, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 2}3x & =3\\lim_{x\\to 2}x \\\\  & =3\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =6\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the root rule in the denominator, we have\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 2}\\sqrt[3]{2{x}^{4}-5} & =\\sqrt[3]{\\lim_{x\\to 2}(2{x}^{4}-5)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{2(2)^{4}-5)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{27} \\\\  & =3\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 1}\\frac{3x}{\\sqrt[3]{2{x}^{4}-5}} & =\\frac{6}{3}=2\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186563751 :question/problem "Find the limit ${lim}_{t\\to 0}[f(t)\\cdot g(t)]$ given that $f(t)$ and $g(t)$ are defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}3{e}^{t} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{t}^{2}+9}{t+3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,g(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}t-2 \\\\ \\frac{3t-2}{{t}^{2}-1} \\\\ {2}^{t}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Find the limit $\\lim_{t\\to 0}[f(t)\\cdot g(t)]$ given that $f(t)$ and $g(t)$ are defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}3{e}^{t} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{t}^{2}+9}{t+3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,g(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}t-2 \\\\ \\frac{3t-2}{{t}^{2}-1} \\\\ {2}^{t}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186563751 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the limit of each function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 0}f(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}{lim}_{t\\to 0}3{e}^{t} \\\\ {lim}_{t\\to 0}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t \\\\ {lim}_{t\\to 0}\\frac{{t}^{2}+9}{t+3}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}3{e}^{0} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡0 \\\\ \\frac{{0}^{2}+9}{0+3}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {lim}_{t\\to 0}g(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}{lim}_{t\\to 0}\\,(t-2) \\\\ {lim}_{t\\to 0}\\frac{3t-2}{{t}^{2}-1} \\\\ {lim}_{t\\to 0}{2}^{t}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0-2 \\\\ \\frac{3(0)-2}{{0}^{2}-1} \\\\ {2}^{0}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use properties of limits to calculate the final result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 0}[f(t)\\cdot g(t)] & ={lim}_{t\\to 0}f(t)\\cdot {lim}_{t\\to 0}g(t) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =3(-2)+0(2)+3(1) \\\\  & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we find the limit of each function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to 0}f(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\lim_{t\\to 0}3{e}^{t} \\\\ \\lim_{t\\to 0}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t \\\\ \\lim_{t\\to 0}\\frac{{t}^{2}+9}{t+3}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}3{e}^{0} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡0 \\\\ \\frac{{0}^{2}+9}{0+3}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\lim_{t\\to 0}g(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\lim_{t\\to 0}\\,(t-2) \\\\ \\lim_{t\\to 0}\\frac{3t-2}{{t}^{2}-1} \\\\ \\lim_{t\\to 0}{2}^{t}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0-2 \\\\ \\frac{3(0)-2}{{0}^{2}-1} \\\\ {2}^{0}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use properties of limits to calculate the final result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to 0}[f(t)\\cdot g(t)] & =\\lim_{t\\to 0}f(t)\\cdot {lim}_{t\\to 0}g(t) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =3(-2)+0(2)+3(1) \\\\  & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186563752 :question/worked-solution "First, recall that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{t\\to 0}f(t)=({lim}_{t\\to 0}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)i+({lim}_{t\\to 0}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)j+({lim}_{t\\to 0}(t+2)^{2})k.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the limits one by one.\n\n- For the first component, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 0}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡0=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the second component, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 0}\\operatorname{cos}⁡0=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the third component, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 0}\\,(t+2)^{2} & =(0+2)^{2}=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, ${lim}_{t\\to 0}f(t)=j+4\\,k.$\n\nFinally, the limit of $‖f(t)‖$ as $t$ approaches $0$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 0}‖f(t)‖ & =‖{lim}_{t\\to 0}f(t)‖ \\\\  & =‖j+4\\,k‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{4}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{17}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, recall that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{t\\to 0}f(t)=(\\lim_{t\\to 0}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)i+(\\lim_{t\\to 0}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)j+(\\lim_{t\\to 0}(t+2)^{2})k.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the limits one by one.\n\n- For the first component, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to 0}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡0=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the second component, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to 0}\\operatorname{cos}⁡0=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the third component, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to 0}\\,(t+2)^{2} & =(0+2)^{2}=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $\\lim_{t\\to 0}f(t)=j+4\\,k.$\n\nFinally, the limit of $‖f(t)‖$ as $t$ approaches $0$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to 0}‖f(t)‖ & =‖\\lim_{t\\to 0}f(t)‖ \\\\  & =‖j+4\\,k‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{4}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{17}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186563754 :question/problem "Calculate ${lim}_{s\\to 1}f(s),$ if $$\n\\displaystyle f(s)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{5-s} \\\\ \\frac{{e}^{1-s}}{s} \\\\ (1+5s)^{1-s}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" "Calculate $\\lim_{s\\to 1}f(s),$ if $$\n\\displaystyle f(s)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{5-s} \\\\ \\frac{{e}^{1-s}}{s} \\\\ (1+5s)^{1-s}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186563754 :question/worked-solution "First, recall that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{s\\to 1}f(s)=\\begin{bmatrix}{lim}_{s\\to 1}\\sqrt{5-s} \\\\ {lim}_{s\\to 1}\\frac{{e}^{1-s}}{s} \\\\ {lim}_{s\\to 1}\\,(1+5s)^{1/(2s)}.\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the limits one by one.\n\n- For the first component, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{s\\to 1}\\sqrt{5-s} & =\\sqrt{5-1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the second component, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{s\\to 1}\\frac{{e}^{1-s}}{s}=\\frac{{e}^{1-1}}{1}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the third component, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{s\\to 1}\\,(1+5s)^{1-s} & =(1+5\\cdot 1)^{1-1} \\\\  & ={6}^{0} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $$\n\\displaystyle {lim}_{s\\to 1}f(s)=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" "First, recall that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{s\\to 1}f(s)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\lim_{s\\to 1}\\sqrt{5-s} \\\\ \\lim_{s\\to 1}\\frac{{e}^{1-s}}{s} \\\\ \\lim_{s\\to 1}\\,(1+5s)^{1/(2s)}.\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the limits one by one.\n\n- For the first component, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{s\\to 1}\\sqrt{5-s} & =\\sqrt{5-1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the second component, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{s\\to 1}\\frac{{e}^{1-s}}{s}=\\frac{{e}^{1-1}}{1}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the third component, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{s\\to 1}\\,(1+5s)^{1-s} & =(1+5\\cdot 1)^{1-1} \\\\  & ={6}^{0} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $$\n\\displaystyle {lim}_{s\\to 1}f(s)=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186564486 :question/worked-solution "The function ${log}_{2}⁡(4x)$ is well defined for $x>0,$ and so to find this limit we substitute $x=4$ into the above expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 4}{log}_{2}⁡(4x) & ={log}_{2}⁡(4\\cdot 4) \\\\  & ={log}_{2}⁡(16) \\\\  & ={log}_{2}⁡({2}^{4}) \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The function ${log}_{2}⁡(4x)$ is well defined for $x>0,$ and so to find this limit we substitute $x=4$ into the above expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 4}{log}_{2}⁡(4x) & ={log}_{2}⁡(4\\cdot 4) \\\\  & ={log}_{2}⁡(16) \\\\  & ={log}_{2}⁡({2}^{4}) \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186564525 :question/worked-solution "The function $\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(2x+8)}{2-x}$ is well defined for $x>-4,$ and $x\\ne 2$ and so to find this limit we substitute $x=1$ into the above expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(2x+8)}{2-x} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(2(1)+8)}{2-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(10)}{1} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{log}⁡({10}^{1}) \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The function $\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(2x+8)}{2-x}$ is well defined for $x>-4,$ and $x\\ne 2$ and so to find this limit we substitute $x=1$ into the above expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 1}\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(2x+8)}{2-x} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(2(1)+8)}{2-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(10)}{1} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{log}⁡({10}^{1}) \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186565411 :question/worked-solution "First, we use the algebra of limits to pull out the constant factor of $-3:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{-3}{{x}^{8}} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }[-3\\cdot \\frac{1}{{x}^{8}}] \\\\  & =-3\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{x}^{8}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the fact that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\left(\\frac{1}{{x}^{8}}\\right)=0,\n$$\n\nwe can evaluate our limit as follows:\n\n$$\n-3\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{x}^{8}}=-3\\cdot 0=0\n$$" "First, we use the algebra of limits to pull out the constant factor of $-3:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{-3}{{x}^{8}} & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }[-3\\cdot \\frac{1}{{x}^{8}}] \\\\  & =-3\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{x}^{8}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the fact that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\left(\\frac{1}{{x}^{8}}\\right)=0,\n$$\n\nwe can evaluate our limit as follows:\n\n$$\n-3\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{x}^{8}}=-3\\cdot 0=0\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186565412 :question/worked-solution "From the diagram, ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }g(x)=-2.$\n\nNow, rewrite the target limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{g(x)}{{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}-7} & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }g(x)\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}-7}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor any non-constant polynomial $P(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\pm \\infty }\\left(\\frac{1}{P(x)}\\right)=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since ${x}^{3}$ is a non-constant polynomial, we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}-7}=0.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate the original limit as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }g(x)\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}-7}=-2\\cdot 0=0\\end{aligned}\n$$" "From the diagram, $\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }g(x)=-2.$\n\nNow, rewrite the target limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{g(x)}{{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}-7} & =\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }g(x)\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}-7}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor any non-constant polynomial $P(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\pm \\infty }\\left(\\frac{1}{P(x)}\\right)=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since ${x}^{3}$ is a non-constant polynomial, we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}-7}=0.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate the original limit as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }g(x)\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}-7}=-2\\cdot 0=0\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186565415 :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the limit using the algebra of limits,  as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{7}{3x} & =\\frac{7}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{1}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the fact that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right)=-\\infty ,\\,{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right)=\\infty .\n$$\n\nWe conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\frac{7}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{1}{x}=\\text{DNE}.\n$$" "First, we rewrite the limit using the algebra of limits,  as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{7}{3x} & =\\frac{7}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{1}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the fact that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{-}}\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right)=-\\infty ,\\,\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right)=\\infty .\n$$\n\nWe conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\frac{7}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{1}{x}=\\text{DNE}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186565424 :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (3,1,2)}\\,\\frac{y-x+z}{\\sqrt{x-z}-\\sqrt{y}}=\\frac{1-3+2}{\\sqrt{3-2}-\\sqrt{1}}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by first multiplying the numerator and denominator by the conjugate of the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (3,1,2)}\\,\\frac{y-x+z}{\\sqrt{x-z}-\\sqrt{y}} & ={lim}_{x\\to (3,1,2)}\\,\\frac{(y-x+z)(\\sqrt{x-z}+\\sqrt{y})}{(\\sqrt{x-z}-\\sqrt{y})(\\sqrt{x-z}+\\sqrt{y})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (3,1,2)}\\,\\frac{(y-x+z)(\\sqrt{x-z}+y)}{{(\\sqrt{x-z})}^{2}-{(\\sqrt{y})}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (3,1,2)}\\,\\frac{(y-x+z)(\\sqrt{x-z}+y)}{x-z-y} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (3,1,2)}\\,\\frac{-(x-z-y)(\\sqrt{x-z}+y)}{x-z-y} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (3,1,2)}-(\\sqrt{x-z}+y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (3,1,2)}-(\\sqrt{x-z}+y) & =-(\\sqrt{3-2}+1) \\\\  & =-(1+1) \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $-2.$" "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (3,1,2)}\\,\\frac{y-x+z}{\\sqrt{x-z}-\\sqrt{y}}=\\frac{1-3+2}{\\sqrt{3-2}-\\sqrt{1}}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by first multiplying the numerator and denominator by the conjugate of the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (3,1,2)}\\,\\frac{y-x+z}{\\sqrt{x-z}-\\sqrt{y}} & =\\lim_{x\\to (3,1,2)}\\,\\frac{(y-x+z)(\\sqrt{x-z}+\\sqrt{y})}{(\\sqrt{x-z}-\\sqrt{y})(\\sqrt{x-z}+\\sqrt{y})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (3,1,2)}\\,\\frac{(y-x+z)(\\sqrt{x-z}+y)}{{(\\sqrt{x-z})}^{2}-{(\\sqrt{y})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (3,1,2)}\\,\\frac{(y-x+z)(\\sqrt{x-z}+y)}{x-z-y} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (3,1,2)}\\,\\frac{-(x-z-y)(\\sqrt{x-z}+y)}{x-z-y} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (3,1,2)}-(\\sqrt{x-z}+y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (3,1,2)}-(\\sqrt{x-z}+y) & =-(\\sqrt{3-2}+1) \\\\  & =-(1+1) \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $-2.$"] [:db/cas 17592186565425 :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}:$\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{2{y}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}=\\frac{2\\cdot {0}^{2}}{2\\cdot {0}^{2}-{0}^{2}}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, the function cannot be simplified by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\nLet's attempt to evaluate the limit approaching $(0,0)$ from different paths.\n\n- Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=x}\\,\\frac{2{y}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}-{x}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the line $y=0$ (the $x$-axis),  we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=0}\\,\\frac{2{y}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{2\\cdot {0}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}-{0}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause the limit evaluates to different results on two different paths, we conclude that the limit does not exist:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{2{y}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}=\\text{DNE}\n$$" "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{2{y}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}=\\frac{2\\cdot {0}^{2}}{2\\cdot {0}^{2}-{0}^{2}}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, the function cannot be simplified by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\nLet's attempt to evaluate the limit approaching $(0,0)$ from different paths.\n\n- Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=x}\\,\\frac{2{y}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}-{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the line $y=0$ (the $x$-axis),  we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=0}\\,\\frac{2{y}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{2\\cdot {0}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}-{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause the limit evaluates to different results on two different paths, we conclude that the limit does not exist:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{2{y}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}=\\text{DNE}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186565432 :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}:$\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{xy}{2{x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}}=\\frac{0\\cdot 0}{2\\cdot {0}^{2}+3\\cdot {0}^{2}}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, the function cannot be simplified by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\nLet's attempt to evaluate the limit approaching $(0,0)$ from different paths.\n\n- Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=x}\\,\\frac{xy}{2{x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}+3{x}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}}{5{x}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{1}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the line $y=-x$,  we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=-x}\\,\\frac{xy}{2{x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{-{x}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}+3{x}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{-{x}^{2}}{5{x}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\left(-\\frac{1}{5}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause the limit evaluates to different results on two different paths, we conclude that the limit does not exist:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{xy}{2{x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}}=\\text{DNE}\n$$" "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{xy}{2{x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}}=\\frac{0\\cdot 0}{2\\cdot {0}^{2}+3\\cdot {0}^{2}}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, the function cannot be simplified by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\nLet's attempt to evaluate the limit approaching $(0,0)$ from different paths.\n\n- Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=x}\\,\\frac{xy}{2{x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}+3{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}}{5{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{1}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the line $y=-x$,  we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=-x}\\,\\frac{xy}{2{x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{-{x}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}+3{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{-{x}^{2}}{5{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,\\left(-\\frac{1}{5}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause the limit evaluates to different results on two different paths, we conclude that the limit does not exist:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{xy}{2{x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}}=\\text{DNE}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186565438 :question/worked-solution "Let the function $f(x,y)$ be defined as\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=2x-y+3.\n$$\n\nThe function $f(x,y)$ is continuous for all values of $x$ and $y.$ So, we can calculate the limit by substituting $(-2,0)$ into the function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-2,0)}(2x-y+3) & =2(-2)-0+3 \\\\  & =-4-0+3 \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let the function $f(x,y)$ be defined as\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=2x-y+3.\n$$\n\nThe function $f(x,y)$ is continuous for all values of $x$ and $y.$ So, we can calculate the limit by substituting $(-2,0)$ into the function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (-2,0)}(2x-y+3) & =2(-2)-0+3 \\\\  & =-4-0+3 \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186565443 :question/worked-solution "First, we simplify the limit:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{9}{12{x}^{4}}={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{3}{4{x}^{4}}\n$$\n\nThen, we use the algebra of limits to pull out the constant factor of $\\frac{3}{4}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{3}{4{x}^{4}} & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{3}{4}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{x}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe recall the graph of the function $y=\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}$\n\n![](images/fa/fa92c457186fbdf71552ec79c782af7983e6fa09009e7f0efdd02b8f74e3ab98.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to ({0}^{+})}\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}=\\infty \n$$\n\nThe positive factor of $\\frac{3}{4}$ does not change the sign of the limit. Therefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{4}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}=\\infty .\n$$" "First, we simplify the limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{9}{12{x}^{4}}=\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{3}{4{x}^{4}}\n$$\n\nThen, we use the algebra of limits to pull out the constant factor of $\\frac{3}{4}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{3}{4{x}^{4}} & =\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{3}{4}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{x}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe recall the graph of the function $y=\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}$\n\n![](images/fa/fa92c457186fbdf71552ec79c782af7983e6fa09009e7f0efdd02b8f74e3ab98.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to ({0}^{+})}\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}=\\infty \n$$\n\nThe positive factor of $\\frac{3}{4}$ does not change the sign of the limit. Therefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{4}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}=\\infty .\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186565463 :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (9,1,3)}\\frac{x-{y}^{2}{z}^{2}}{\\sqrt{x}-yz} & =\\frac{9-({1}^{2})({3}^{2})}{\\sqrt{9}-1(3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{9-9}{3-3} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by first multiplying the numerator and denominator by the conjugate of the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (9,1,3)}\\frac{x-{y}^{2}{z}^{2}}{\\sqrt{x}-yz} & ={lim}_{x\\to (9,1,3)}\\frac{(x-{y}^{2}{z}^{2})(\\sqrt{x}+yz)}{(\\sqrt{x}-yz)(\\sqrt{x}+yz)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (9,1,3)}\\frac{(x-{y}^{2}{z}^{2})(\\sqrt{x}+yz)}{x-{y}^{2}{z}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (9,1,3)}\\frac{\\cancel{(x-{y}^{2}{z}^{2})}(\\sqrt{x}+yz)}{\\cancel{x-{y}^{2}{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (9,1,3)}(\\sqrt{x}+yz)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (9,1,3)}(\\sqrt{x}+yz) & =\\sqrt{9}+1(3) \\\\  & =3+3 \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $6.$" "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (9,1,3)}\\frac{x-{y}^{2}{z}^{2}}{\\sqrt{x}-yz} & =\\frac{9-({1}^{2})({3}^{2})}{\\sqrt{9}-1(3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{9-9}{3-3} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by first multiplying the numerator and denominator by the conjugate of the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (9,1,3)}\\frac{x-{y}^{2}{z}^{2}}{\\sqrt{x}-yz} & =\\lim_{x\\to (9,1,3)}\\frac{(x-{y}^{2}{z}^{2})(\\sqrt{x}+yz)}{(\\sqrt{x}-yz)(\\sqrt{x}+yz)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (9,1,3)}\\frac{(x-{y}^{2}{z}^{2})(\\sqrt{x}+yz)}{x-{y}^{2}{z}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (9,1,3)}\\frac{\\cancel{(x-{y}^{2}{z}^{2})}(\\sqrt{x}+yz)}{\\cancel{x-{y}^{2}{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (9,1,3)}(\\sqrt{x}+yz)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (9,1,3)}(\\sqrt{x}+yz) & =\\sqrt{9}+1(3) \\\\  & =3+3 \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $6.$"] [:db/cas 17592186565476 :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}\\left(\\frac{7}{9{x}^{7}}\\right) & =\\frac{7}{9}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}\\frac{1}{{x}^{7}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the fact that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}\\frac{1}{{x}^{7}}=-\\infty .\n$$\n\nThe positive factor of $\\frac{7}{9}$ does not change the sign of the limit. Therefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{7}{9}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}\\frac{1}{{x}^{7}}=-\\infty .\n$$" "First, we rewrite the limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{-}}\\left(\\frac{7}{9{x}^{7}}\\right) & =\\frac{7}{9}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}\\frac{1}{{x}^{7}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the fact that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{-}}\\frac{1}{{x}^{7}}=-\\infty .\n$$\n\nThe positive factor of $\\frac{7}{9}$ does not change the sign of the limit. Therefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{7}{9}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}\\frac{1}{{x}^{7}}=-\\infty .\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186565477 :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n{lim}_{(x,y)\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{2{x}^{2}+3xy+{y}^{2}}{{x}^{3}+xy+{x}^{2}y+{y}^{2}}=\\frac{2\\cdot (-1)^{2}+3\\cdot (-1)\\cdot 1+{1}^{2}}{(-1)^{3}+(-1)\\cdot 1+(-1)^{2}\\cdot 1+{1}^{2}}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by factoring the numerator and denominator and then canceling common factors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{(x,y)\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{2{x}^{2}+3xy+{y}^{2}}{{x}^{3}+xy+{x}^{2}y+{y}^{2}} & ={lim}_{(x,y)\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{2{x}^{2}+2xy+xy+{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}(x+y)+y(x+y)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{(x,y)\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{2x(x+y)+y(x+y)}{({x}^{2}+y)(x+y)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{(x,y)\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{(2x+y)(x+y)}{({x}^{2}+y)(x+y)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{(x,y)\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{2x+y}{{x}^{2}+y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{(x,y)\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{2x+y}{{x}^{2}+y} & =\\frac{2(-1)+(1)}{(-1)^{2}+1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $-\\frac{1}{2}.$" "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\lim_{(x,y)\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{2{x}^{2}+3xy+{y}^{2}}{{x}^{3}+xy+{x}^{2}y+{y}^{2}}=\\frac{2\\cdot (-1)^{2}+3\\cdot (-1)\\cdot 1+{1}^{2}}{(-1)^{3}+(-1)\\cdot 1+(-1)^{2}\\cdot 1+{1}^{2}}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by factoring the numerator and denominator and then canceling common factors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{(x,y)\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{2{x}^{2}+3xy+{y}^{2}}{{x}^{3}+xy+{x}^{2}y+{y}^{2}} & =\\lim_{(x,y)\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{2{x}^{2}+2xy+xy+{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}(x+y)+y(x+y)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{(x,y)\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{2x(x+y)+y(x+y)}{({x}^{2}+y)(x+y)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{(x,y)\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{(2x+y)(x+y)}{({x}^{2}+y)(x+y)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{(x,y)\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{2x+y}{{x}^{2}+y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{(x,y)\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{2x+y}{{x}^{2}+y} & =\\frac{2(-1)+(1)}{(-1)^{2}+1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $-\\frac{1}{2}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186565484 :question/worked-solution "Applying the product rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{g(x)}{{x}^{2}} & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(g(x)\\cdot \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }g(x)\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the graph, we get that ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }g(x)=-2.$ Therefore, the above becomes:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{g(x)}{{x}^{2}}=-2\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\n$$\n\nNow, using the fact that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\left(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\right)=0,\n$$\n\nwe can evaluate our limit as follows:\n\n$$\n-2\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}=-2\\cdot 0=0\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{g(x)}{{x}^{2}}=0.\n$$" "Applying the product rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{g(x)}{{x}^{2}} & =\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }(g(x)\\cdot \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }g(x)\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the graph, we get that $\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }g(x)=-2.$ Therefore, the above becomes:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{g(x)}{{x}^{2}}=-2\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\n$$\n\nNow, using the fact that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\left(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\right)=0,\n$$\n\nwe can evaluate our limit as follows:\n\n$$\n-2\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}=-2\\cdot 0=0\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{g(x)}{{x}^{2}}=0.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186565485 :question/worked-solution "First, we use the algebra of limits to pull out the constant factor of $-7:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\left(-\\frac{7}{{x}^{5}}\\right) & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(-7\\cdot \\frac{1}{{x}^{5}}) \\\\  & =-7\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{{x}^{5}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the fact that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{{x}^{5}}=0,\n$$\n\nwe can evaluate our limit as follows:\n\n$$\n-7\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{{x}^{5}}=-7\\cdot 0=0\n$$" "First, we use the algebra of limits to pull out the constant factor of $-7:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\left(-\\frac{7}{{x}^{5}}\\right) & =\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }(-7\\cdot \\frac{1}{{x}^{5}}) \\\\  & =-7\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{{x}^{5}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the fact that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{{x}^{5}}=0,\n$$\n\nwe can evaluate our limit as follows:\n\n$$\n-7\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{{x}^{5}}=-7\\cdot 0=0\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186569964 :question/worked-solution "For a given polar curve, there is a vertical tangent at $\\theta ={\\theta }_{0}$ if\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})}{{x}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})}\n$$\n\nis infinite. This means that\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})\\ne 0\\,\\text{and}\\,{x}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})=0.\n$$\n\nHowever, if the curve is continuous at ${\\theta }_{0}$ yet the derivative takes the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}$ at this point, we say that the curve has a vertical tangent at this point if\n\n$$\n{lim}_{\\theta \\to {\\theta }_{0}^{-}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\pm \\infty \\,\\text{and}\\,{lim}_{\\theta \\to {\\theta }_{0}^{+}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\pm \\infty .\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're told that ${y}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Substituting $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ into the expression for ${x}^{′}(\\theta ),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) & =-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right){cos}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-3\\cdot 1\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Computing the limit as $\\theta \\to {\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)}^{+},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{+}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{+}}\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )} \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{+}}\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}{-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\right) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{+}}\\left(\\frac{\\cancel{cos}\\theta ({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}{-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {cos}^{\\cancel{2}}⁡\\theta }\\right) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{+}}\\left(\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\right) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{+}}\\left(\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)-2{sin}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)}{-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{0-2\\cdot 1}{-3\\cdot 1\\cdot {0}^{-}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2}{{0}^{+}} \\\\  & =-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Using the calculations from part II, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}\\left(\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)-2{sin}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)}{-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{0-2\\cdot 1}{-3\\cdot 1\\cdot {0}^{+}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2}{{0}^{-}} \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the limits of our derivative are both infinite, there is a vertical tangent to the curve at $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\n![](images/50/50637a502a56fc242ba5d8e2fda59ddc899295d4b14f7ae692869536b95ef661.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" "For a given polar curve, there is a vertical tangent at $\\theta ={\\theta }_{0}$ if\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})}{{x}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})}\n$$\n\nis infinite. This means that\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})\\ne 0\\,\\text{and}\\,{x}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})=0.\n$$\n\nHowever, if the curve is continuous at ${\\theta }_{0}$ yet the derivative takes the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}$ at this point, we say that the curve has a vertical tangent at this point if\n\n$$\n\\lim_{\\theta \\to {\\theta }_{0}^{-}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\pm \\infty \\,\\text{and}\\,\\lim_{\\theta \\to {\\theta }_{0}^{+}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\pm \\infty .\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're told that ${y}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Substituting $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ into the expression for ${x}^{′}(\\theta ),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) & =-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right){cos}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-3\\cdot 1\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Computing the limit as $\\theta \\to {\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)}^{+},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{+}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\lim_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{+}}\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{+}}\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}{-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\right) \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{+}}\\left(\\frac{\\cancel{cos}\\theta ({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}{-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {cos}^{\\cancel{2}}⁡\\theta }\\right) \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{+}}\\left(\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\right) \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{+}}\\left(\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)-2{sin}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)}{-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{0-2\\cdot 1}{-3\\cdot 1\\cdot {0}^{-}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2}{{0}^{+}} \\\\  & =-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Using the calculations from part II, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\lim_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}\\left(\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)-2{sin}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)}{-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{0-2\\cdot 1}{-3\\cdot 1\\cdot {0}^{+}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2}{{0}^{-}} \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the limits of our derivative are both infinite, there is a vertical tangent to the curve at $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\n![](images/50/50637a502a56fc242ba5d8e2fda59ddc899295d4b14f7ae692869536b95ef661.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""] [:db/cas 17592186569981 :question/worked-solution "When a polar curve is differentiable at a point ${\\theta }_{0}$, there is a horizontal tangent at ${\\theta }_{0}$ if\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})}{{x}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})}=0\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{x}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nHowever, if the curve is continuous at ${\\theta }_{0}$ yet the derivative ${y}^{′}(x)$ takes the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}$ at this point, we say that the curve has a horizontal tangent at this point if\n\n$$\n{lim}_{\\theta \\to {\\theta }_{0}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=0.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're told that ${x}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Substituting $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ into the expression for ${y}^{′}(\\theta ),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right){cos}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)(5{cos}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)-3) \\\\  & =1\\cdot 0\\cdot (5\\cdot 0-3) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Computing the limit as $\\theta \\to \\frac{\\pi }{2},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi /2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi /2}\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )} \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi /2}\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,(5{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -3)}{{cos}^{3}⁡\\theta \\,(5{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -4)}\\right) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi /2}\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {\\cancel{cos}}^{2}\\theta \\,(5{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -3)}{{cos}^{\\cancel{3}}⁡\\theta \\,(5{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -4)}\\right) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi /2}\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,(5{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -3)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,(5{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -4)}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\cdot (5\\cdot 0-3)}{0\\cdot (5\\cdot 0-4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-3}{0} \\\\  & \\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the limit in part II does *not* equal to $0,$ there curve does *not* have a horizontal tangent at $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ In fact, it is a vertical tangent.\n\n![](images/d7/d7881ba860bcc32301a162eb200ea9075b06b63c780be74c14aedbcb1654eaaa.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "When a polar curve is differentiable at a point ${\\theta }_{0}$, there is a horizontal tangent at ${\\theta }_{0}$ if\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})}{{x}^